:"Gold","stage":"mine","actor":"Darfur & Kordofan gold fields (RSF-held)","detail":"Sudan is one of Africa's top gold producers. The RSF controls major artisanal and semi-industrial sites across Darfur, financing its war effort directly from the pit.","who":"RSF (Hemedti's family network) controls the western gold economy","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"process","actor":"Smuggled via Chad/Libya/CAR → refiners","detail":"Gold leaves RSF territory overland through Chad and Libya, bypassing the state central bank. Estimates put the majority of Sudan's gold output as smuggled — off the books, untaxed by Port Sudan.","who":"Africa Corps (ex-Wagner) takes a cut via CAR/Libya routes","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"mid","actor":"UAE (Dubai) — the refining & laundering hub","detail":"Dubai is the dominant destination: Sudanese gold is refined and enters global bullion flows with its conflict origin obscured — the same circuit that launders Congolese gold. JUL 2026: the EU banned Sudanese-gold imports + the mercury/cyanide needed to mine it, but pointedly did NOT sanction the UAE — so the Dubai reroute remains open (cross-link: UAE + DRC theaters).","who":"UAE is the RSF's primary patron; gold pays for the drones it supplies","tier":"alleged-credible"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"end","actor":"Global bullion · reserves · jewellery","detail":"Once refined into Dubai bars, the gold is fungible and untraceable. It is the single most important revenue source keeping the RSF war machine solvent.","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["dubai-gold","water-leverage"],"history":{"roots":"Sudan's war is not ancient tribal hatred — it is the collapse of an extraction structure built deliberately and maintained for a century. Ottoman-Egyptian and then British rule (the 'Anglo-Egyptian Condominium') governed Sudan as a core and a hinterland: development, education, and power concentrated in the riverine north among an Arabized elite, while Darfur, the Nuba Mountains, the Beja east, and the South were administered as labor and resource reserves — Mamdani's 'citizen and subject' logic in its starkest form. Every post-independence war (the South's two wars, Darfur, today's) is a periphery rebelling against that same core, and every Khartoum regime — including Bashir's — answered by arming one periphery against another. The Janjaweed-turned-RSF is that technique institutionalized: a counterinsurgency militia given an economy (Darfur's gold), which then outgrew and devoured its creator. The 2019 revolution — led by professional associations, resistance committees, and women, one of the great mass civic uprisings of this century — was the periphery-and-street's attempt to end the model; the two generals' war is the model refusing to die.","timeline":[{"date":"1956","e":"Independence from Anglo-Egyptian rule; power concentrates in the riverine Arab center."},{"date":"1989","e":"Omar al-Bashir seizes power in an Islamist-backed coup."},{"date":"2003-08","e":"Darfur genocide: Bashir's Janjaweed militias kill ~300,000. The Janjaweed are the seed of the RSF."},{"date":"2011","e":"South Sudan secedes, taking ~75% of oil output — gutting Khartoum's finances and raising the stakes on gold."},{"date":"2013","e":"Hemedti's Janjaweed are formalized as the Rapid Support Forces."},{"date":"2019","e":"Revolution ousts Bashir; a shaky civilian-military transition begins."},{"date":"Oct 2021","e":"Burhan and Hemedti jointly coup the civilian government."},{"date":"15 Apr 2023","e":"RSF and army go to war in Khartoum over security-sector integration."},{"date":"Mar 2025","e":"Army recaptures Khartoum; RSF declares a parallel government, effectively partitioning the country."},{"date":"Oct 2025","e":"RSF takes El Fasher after an 18-month siege; ~6,000 killed in three days ('hallmarks of genocide')."},{"date":"Jul 2026","e":"RSF sieges El Obeid; EU bans Sudanese gold; Hemedti sentenced to death in absentia; toll ~59,000."}],"actors":[{"name":"Abdel Fattah al-Burhan (SAF)","bio":"Career army officer, de facto head of state since the 2021 coup. Commands the Sudanese Armed Forces and the internationally-recognized government in Port Sudan. Backed by Egypt, and with Iranian/Turkish drones."},{"name":"Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo 'Hemedti' (RSF)","bio":"Former camel trader turned Darfur militia leader who became Sudan's richest man through gold. Built the RSF into a parallel army financed by Darfur goldfields and UAE backing. Sentenced to death in absentia (2026)."},{"name":"United Arab Emirates","bio":"The RSF's primary external patron — accused of funneling weapons and drones via Chad and Libya in exchange for Sudanese gold refined in Dubai. Denies involvement; shielded by its Western economic weight."},{"name":"Egypt","bio":"Backs the SAF (Burhan) for Nile-security and stability reasons — placing Cairo opposite its Gulf ally the UAE in this war."},{"name":"SPLM-N (al-Hilu)","bio":"A southern-Kordofan/Blue Nile rebel movement, at times allied with the RSF against the army — a legacy of the old center-periphery war."}],"bridge":"Today's map is the colonial map re-armed: the RSF's base is the Darfur the Condominium starved; the SAF's base is the riverine core it privileged; the gold financing Hemedti flows through the same external-extraction circuit (now Dubai instead of London) that has always monetized Sudan's periphery without developing it. El Fasher's genocide is Darfur 2003 completing itself with the same militia under a new name — and the EU's refusal to sanction the UAE while banning Sudanese gold shows the extraction hierarchy intact: the periphery is punishable, the buyer is not. Nothing about this war is inexplicable; it is the system's logic running to conclusion. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: the UN has determined the El Fasher campaign was genocide, the RSF governs a parallel state financed by Darfur gold, and the ICC says it has evidence reaching leadership level. The condominium's logic — rule the centre, arm the periphery, sell the interior — is running without a coloniser.","sources":[{"work":"Saviors and Survivors: Darfur, Politics, and the War on Terror","author":"Mahmood Mamdani","year":"2009","note":"Dismantles the 'Arab vs African' framing of Darfur; locates the war in colonial land/administrative categories and Cold War arms flows."},{"work":"The Root Causes of Sudan's Civil Wars","author":"Douglas H. Johnson","year":"2003 (rev. 2016)","note":"The standard structural account of core-periphery extraction from the Condominium through the SPLA wars."},{"work":"Civil Uprisings in Modern Sudan","author":"Willow Berridge","year":"2015","note":"Sudan's tradition of mass civic revolution (1964, 1985) — the lineage the 2019 uprising and resistance committees belong to."},{"work":"The Real Politics of the Horn of Africa: Money, War and the Business of Power","author":"Alex de Waal","year":"2015","note":"The 'political marketplace' model — Hemedti as the marketplace's most successful entrepreneur."},{"work":"UN Panel of Experts on the Sudan reports","author":"UN Security Council","year":"2023-26","note":"Primary documentation of RSF supply chains, the Chad corridor, and gold flows to the UAE."}]},"human":{"dead":150000,"disp":11000000,"famine":true,"aid":"blocked in RSF zones","note":"UN FFM found the El Fasher campaign amounted to GENOCIDE (Jul 2026). War passed 1,000 days: ~33M pushed toward starvation, 11M displaced (world's largest displacement + hunger crisis). El Obeid (~500k) now under the same siege-encirclement playbook.","est":false,"basis":"ACLED / UN estimates — widely regarded as undercounts"},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"A century's center-periphery extraction structure running to conclusion, gold as the war's fuel.","alt":"A minimalist reading: two generals' personal power struggle any institutional arrangement might have produced; the structure may over-explain what ambition suffices to explain.","why":"Geography, financing and recruitment all track the structure; ambition chose the moment, the structure loaded the weapon. HIGH on structure, personalist reading logged."},"refs":[{"t":"Critical Threats · Africa File (26 Feb and 19 Mar 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/drc-fardc-m23-russia-wagner-mali-burkina-niger-rsf-chad-sudan-fgs-somalia-ghana-aes-ecowas-africa-file-february-26-2026"},{"t":"African Union Peace and Security Council · statements","u":"https://www.peaceau.org/en/"},{"t":"AP investigation via Just Security Early Edition (31 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.justsecurity.org/150786/early-edition-july-31-2026/"},{"t":"Democracy Now · headlines (30 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.democracynow.org/2026/7/30/headlines"},{"t":"Africa Center · Africa Media Review (Jul 2026)","u":"https://africacenter.org/daily-media-review/africa-media-review-for-july-10-2026/"},{"t":"VIF · Africa Now (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.vifindia.org/2026/july/17/Africa-Now-01-15-july-2026"},{"t":"Democracy Now · headlines (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.democracynow.org/2026/7/27/headlines"}],"checkmode":"SWEPT","escwhy":"98 = the highest on the board. UN genocide determination (+), famine conditions confirmed by IPC, a functioning parallel government with its own treasury, and ~150k dead with 12M+ displaced. Scored above Iran because state collapse here is further advanced, not because the fighting is heavier.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"ICC leadership-level breakthrough; RSF drone strikes on civilians; UK gold-network sanctions"}],"spill":[{"n":"Chad","dir":"both","w":"Eastern Chad hosts the refugee mass and the evidence base — the ICC deputy prosecutor's 'breakthrough' followed a visit there. Chad also risks deeper entanglement as RSF supply routes cross its territory.","id":"chad"},{"n":"South Sudan","dir":"both","w":"The RSF's January 2026 push into Blue Nile was launched FROM South Sudanese territory, alongside SPLM-N. Juba's own oil economy depends on pipelines running through Sudan.","id":"south-sudan"},{"n":"Libya","dir":"in","w":"Haftar's eastern Libya is a documented RSF resupply corridor — the same logistics hub Russia's Africa Corps uses for the Sahel.","id":"libya"},{"n":"UAE","dir":"in","w":"The gold corridor. UK sanctions in July 2026 targeted the revenue structure rather than the commanders — the first action aimed at the financing rather than the fighting.","id":"uae-gulf"},{"n":"Egypt","dir":"in","w":"Cairo backs the SAF and holds the Nile question; a partitioned Sudan changes the upstream water calculus permanently.","id":"egypt"},{"n":"Ethiopia","id":"ethiopia","dir":"both","w":"The al-Fashaga border dispute and the Nile question sit underneath the war; a partitioned Sudan changes the upstream water calculus permanently, and both states host each other's displaced."},{"n":"CAR","id":"car","dir":"out","w":"The war destabilises the Central African Republic on the southern flank — refugees, arms, and RSF-linked movement across a frontier neither state controls."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Full-scale war with genocide determination","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation → no stalemate reached","up":["El Obeid falls, opening Kordofan to the west","RSF parallel government consolidates, hardening partition","External sponsors (UAE gold, Libya/Russia arms) keep the RSF resupplied","Famine used as a weapon forecloses negotiation"],"down":["A mutually hurting stalemate neither side yet perceives — both still believe in military victory","Cutting the gold-for-arms revenue (UK July sanctions were a first step)","A unified external position — currently absent, as sponsors back opposing sides","ICC accountability raising the cost of atrocity"],"fw":["resource-conflict","proxy","ripeness"]},"macro":{"growth":"−0.4% (2025f) · GDP $31.5B nominal","infl":"56.4% (2026)","src":"IMF / national data via compiled statistics, 2026","note":"GDP per capita $624; government debt 121.6% of GDP. THE EXPORT TABLE IS THE WAR'S FINANCING IN ONE LINE: the UAE is Sudan's LARGEST EXPORT DESTINATION at 21.3%, ahead of China at 17.3% and Saudi Arabia at 15.8% — and gold is the leading export. The gold-for-arms corridor this board tracks is not a shadow trade running alongside the economy; at these shares it substantially IS the economy. Inflation at 56% is what a war financed by extraction does to everyone not holding the gold."},"inherit":{"name":"The Condominium's Centre-Periphery Rule","est":"1899","status":"IN FORCE","what":"The Anglo-Egyptian Condominium governed Sudan as two countries in one: a developed riverain centre around Khartoum with schools, irrigation and civil service recruitment, and a periphery — Darfur, Kordofan, the south, the east — administered separately, denied investment, and used as a labour and resource reserve. <b>The Closed Districts Ordinance made movement between them require a permit.</b>","now":"<b>This is the war.</b> Every Sudanese conflict since independence has been the periphery against the centre, and the current one is being fought by a force recruited from the Darfuri periphery against an army officered from the riverain centre — with Darfur's gold financing it. The RSF's parallel government is the periphery attempting to become a centre. <b>The condominium's logic — rule the centre, arm the periphery, sell the interior — is running without a coloniser.</b>","change":"The UN has determined the El Fasher campaign was genocide; the ICC says its evidence reaches leadership level; the European Parliament has called for RSF terrorist designation and sanctions on a UAE-based company. <b>None of it addresses the centre-periphery structure the war is being fought over.</b>"},"ctrl":{"gov":45,"contested":20,"other":35,"est":true,"src":"Composite of ACLED control mapping and UN reporting, Jul 2026","note":"SAF holds the riverain centre and Port Sudan; the RSF holds most of Darfur and contests Kordofan, with El Obeid encircled. Shares are approximate and the front is fluid."},"control":{"gov":35,"contested":25,"other":40,"holders":"SAF holds Khartoum, the riverain centre and Port Sudan. RSF holds most of Darfur and contests Kordofan. SPLM-N (al-Hilu) holds the Nuba Mountains.","note":"El Obeid is encircled and the UN has ordered an inquiry into mass-atrocity risk there. The SAF partially broke the Dilling and Kadugli sieges in early 2026.","est":true}},{"id":"burkina","cat":"military","region":"West / Central Africa","subregion":"Sahel Core","name":"Burkina Faso — JNIM Insurgency + Military Overreach","sub":"Sahel epicenter · army caused 56% of civilian deaths","domain":"Jihadist Insurgency / State Atrocity","sev":"critical","status":"escalating","since":"2015","esc":94,"lat":13,"lon":-1.5,"trend":"up","w14":false,"esc_history":[93,93,93,94,94,94,94,94,94,94],"cas":"50% of all Sahel militant-Islamist deaths; Djibo/Barsalogho massacres","disp":"2M+ IDPs; 5,000 schools closed","flag":"🇧🇫","note":"JUN-JUL 2026 — THE WATCHERS ARE EXPELLED: at least 929 CIVIL SOCIETY ORGANISATIONS were suspended between April and May 2026 alone; on 30 JUNE the UN HUMAN RIGHTS OFFICE ANNOUNCED IT WILL PERMANENTLY CLOSE ITS BURKINA FASO OFFICE after authorities indefinitely suspended its activities in February — following its criticism of restrictions on civic space · the sequence matters: the office documented abuses, was suspended for saying so, and is now closing permanently. The country with one of the world's worst civilian death tolls will have no resident UN human-rights presence · UN-recorded civilian deaths across the central Sahel: 2,640 in Q1 2026 alone, with sieges, IEDs, drones and landmines used as deliberate tactics · JUL 2026 — THE ENCIRCLEMENT MODEL EXPORTED: analysts note JNIM's Bamako strategy REPLICATES the siege pattern perfected in Burkina Faso — severing towns from supply routes rather than assaulting them · UN: 2,640 civilian deaths across the central Sahel in Q1 2026; sieges, IEDs, drones and landmines used as deliberate tactics · military authorities across all three AES states continue restricting civic space and suppressing dissent — the security failure and the political closure advance together · NEW STANDALONE · Burkina = highest violence in Sahel (50% of regional militant deaths, Africa Center) · JNIM briefly SEIZED Djibo + Diapaga (May) · ~90 soldiers killed Koubel-Alpha ambush · CRITICAL: Burkinabè troops caused 56% of civilian fatalities Jan23-Mar26 (ACLED) — more than jihadists · IPC Phase 4 famine risk · JNIM displayed photo of Ouagadougou · NEW: JNIM attacked Dargo base (~50 soldiers killed) + pressuring Fada N'Gourma western approaches to isolate it from Ouagadougou · Fada University raid · ACLED: post-defection JNIM campaign = 30+ attacks in Burkina alone, 120+ soldiers/VDP/forest-guards killed; ISSP counter-offensive killed 40+ JNIM (intra-jihadist war)","leverage":[{"actor":"JNIM","asset":"Territorial control + Djibo siege (5+ yrs); urban ambitions; recruitment from army abuses","str":"hi"},{"actor":"IS-Sahel (ISGS)","asset":"Outbidding rival; convoy/aid attacks (Gorom Gorom)","str":"md"},{"actor":"Traoré junta + VDP + Africa Corps","asset":"Air power but VDP militia gutted; can't hold territory","str":"lo"},{"actor":"Russia (Africa Corps)","asset":"Limited scope; fuel-convoy escort only","str":"lo"}],"gov":[{"src":"Burkina Defense/Justice Ministries","detail":"Non-responsive to HRW inquiries; dissolved political parties; entrenched military rule via new fundamental law"},{"src":"US State Dept","detail":"Senior official Nick Checker visited Ouagadougou (Mar 2026) to 'relaunch relations'"}],"mil":"Burkina Faso is now the most violent country in the Sahel, accounting for 50% of all militant-Islamist-linked fatalities in the region (Africa Center for Strategic Studies). In May, JNIM briefly seized the provincial capitals of Djibo and Diapaga in rapid succession — a step-change in capacity — and in a September ambush near Koubel-Alpha killed ~90 soldiers, one of the deadliest strikes on the military to date. JNIM has besieged Djibo for over five years and has begun displaying a photo of Ouagadougou, signaling urban ambitions.","pol":"The decisive structural finding is ACLED's: between January 2023 and March 2026, Burkinabè troops were responsible for 56% of civilian fatalities — more than the jihadists themselves. Villagers describe troops torching homes and executing suspected collaborators, only for jihadists to return; each army massacre fuels jihadist recruitment. Fulani communities are systematically scapegoated. The Traoré regime has dissolved political parties and cracked down on critics.","eco":"IPC Phase 4 (emergency) famine risk in the north; 5,000 schools closed affecting ~1 million children; widows head 60% of households in Djibo camps (Oxfam). JNIM taxes herders and imposes sharia in areas it controls.","assess":"Burkina Faso enters as its own theater at 93 — the Sahel's epicenter. The defining dynamic, per ACLED, is the vicious cycle: the army kills more civilians than the jihadists do, which drives recruitment, which the junta answers with more force. The VDP militia that was central to Traoré's mobilization has been gutted. JNIM's seizure of Djibo and Diapaga and its display of Ouagadougou signal a shift from rural insurgency toward urban ambitions — the trajectory to watch.","external":[{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia (Africa Corps)","side":"junta","flow":"~100→300 personnel; Loumbila base protecting Traoré; gold-refinery support","tier":"documented","for":"Gold, mining; anti-French realignment"},{"actor":"🇦🇪 UAE","side":"state","flow":"Supplied armoured vehicles (documented state-partner side)","tier":"documented","for":"Counter-terror partnership; influence"},{"actor":"🇮🇷 Iran","side":"junta","flow":"Ababil-2/Mohajer-6 drone supply agreements (2024)","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Gold; anti-Western alignment"}],"ext_flag":true,"tclass":"conflict","tier":"CRITICAL","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","econlev":[{"holder":"JNIM","channel":"Artisanal gold taxation","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Taxes gold production/transport at every chokepoint; part of the ~$100M/yr JNIM war economy exported to the UAE, Turkey, Switzerland — the state controls ~60% of territory, JNIM taxes much of the rest"},{"holder":"Russia (Africa Corps)","channel":"Mining concessions","exp":"MED","detail":"Regime-survival services traded for gold/mineral rights"}],"contested":{"summary":"Security services traded for gold. The security didn't arrive.","claims":[{"claim":"Russia's Africa Corps provides counter-terrorism support to the junta.","who":"Both — Moscow gets mining concessions and UN votes; the junta gets regime survival with no governance conditions.","evid":"Jihadist violence ROSE after Russian deployment in both Mali and Burkina. The arrangement is not failing at its actual purpose — its actual purpose is regime survival, not counter-terrorism.","status":"CONTESTED — judge it by what it's for"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"mine","actor":"Burkina/Mali/Niger artisanal & industrial mines","detail":"The AES states sit on major West African gold. Juntas have nationalized or tightened control (Mali's revised mining code, Burkina's state mining company SOPAMIB) to capture more of the value — resource nationalism as regime finance.","who":"Junta states + Russian Africa Corps take security-for-gold concessions","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"process","actor":"Africa Corps 'security-for-gold' concessions","detail":"Russia's Africa Corps (successor to Wagner) provides regime security in exchange for gold concessions and mining rights — a direct swap of protection for extraction that helps fund the Russian war economy (cross-link: Russia theater).","who":"Africa Corps monetizes concessions; some gold routes to Russia","tier":"alleged-credible"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"mid","actor":"Refined / exported via UAE & Russia channels","detail":"Sahel gold moves through Gulf refiners and Russian-linked channels, partly outside the formal LBMA-audited system — the same off-book pattern as Sudan and DRC.","tier":"alleged-credible"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"end","actor":"Global bullion · Russian reserves · Gulf markets","detail":"The end-buyer opacity is the point: gold that funds junta survival and Russian sanctions-evasion arrives on the market indistinguishable from clean supply.","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["dubai-gold"],"history":{"roots":"Burkina Faso's present cannot be read without Thomas Sankara — and Sankara cannot be read without what he was against. Colonial Upper Volta was a labor reserve: France administered it to export men to Ivorian plantations, a deliberate underdevelopment Rodney's framework describes exactly. Sankara's four years (1983-87) were the continent's most radical answer: land reform, mass vaccination and literacy, women in government, refusal of odious debt ('the debt cannot be repaid... if we don't pay, our creditors will not die'), self-reliance down to the renamed country itself — 'land of upright people.' His assassination in 1987 — with Compaoré's coup, and questions about French and Ivorian complicity that a Burkinabè court finally adjudicated in 2022 — restored the client model for 27 years. Traoré's junta today wraps itself in Sankara's iconography while running a military regime Sankara would not recognize; the jihadist fire it fights burns hottest in the neglected north the labor-reserve economy never developed.","timeline":[{"date":"1960","e":"Independence from France (as Upper Volta)."},{"date":"1983-87","e":"Thomas Sankara's revolutionary, anti-imperialist presidency; assassinated in 1987 — a template Traoré now invokes."},{"date":"2015","e":"Jihadist violence spills over from Mali."},{"date":"2022","e":"Two coups; Capt. Ibrahim Traoré takes power in September."},{"date":"2023","e":"French troops expelled; pivot to Russia's Africa Corps."},{"date":"2024","e":"AES confederation formed with Mali and Niger; exit from ECOWAS."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Burkina remains the world's most terrorism-affected country; JNIM contests much of the north and east."}],"actors":[{"name":"Capt. Ibrahim Traoré","bio":"Seized power in the 2022 coup at age 34; a pan-Africanist icon who invokes Sankara, expelled France, and embraced Russia. Popular abroad; presides over worsening violence at home."},{"name":"JNIM","bio":"Al-Qaeda's Sahel affiliate; the dominant jihadist force, blockading towns and contesting the countryside."},{"name":"Russia (Africa Corps)","bio":"Replaced France as security partner, trading regime protection for gold concessions and influence."},{"name":"VDP militias","bio":"State-armed civilian auxiliaries — force multiplier and a driver of ethnic-reprisal atrocities."}],"bridge":"The current war is the labor-reserve map on fire: JNIM recruits where the state never built, among Fulani communities the colonial cash-crop order marginalized and the VDP militias' reprisals now radicalize further. The anti-French pivot draws real power from Sankara's memory — the popular legitimacy is genuine, the grievance earned — but Russia's Africa Corps has simply moved into the patron's chair with gold concessions where France had CFA seigniorage: the extraction structure survived the flag change. Burkina is the test of whether sovereignty rhetoric without Sankara's redistributive substance can hold a country together; the world's-worst-terrorism ranking is the running answer. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: 929 civil society organisations suspended and the UN human rights office permanently closed. Sankara's warning that debt and dependency would outlive formal independence now has a corollary he did not live to see — the sovereignty claimed against France is being exercised against Burkinabè civilians.","sources":[{"work":"Thomas Sankara Speaks: The Burkina Faso Revolution 1983-87","author":"Thomas Sankara (collected speeches)","year":"1988/2007","note":"Primary texts — the debt speech (OAU, 1987), women's emancipation, self-reliance doctrine."},{"work":"Thomas Sankara: An African Revolutionary","author":"Ernest Harsch","year":"2014","note":"The standard scholarly biography and the assassination's politics."},{"work":"How Europe Underdeveloped Africa","author":"Walter Rodney","year":"1972","note":"The labor-reserve and deliberate-underdevelopment framework Upper Volta exemplified."},{"work":"Jihadism in the Sahel / Burkina Faso conflict data","author":"ACLED & Crisis Group Sahel reports","year":"2019-26","note":"Primary event data on the insurgency's geography — the neglected north and east."}]},"human":{"dead":26000,"disp":2100000,"famine":false,"aid":"blockaded towns","note":"World's most terrorism-affected state; ~2.1M displaced; JNIM blockades starve towns as siege policy.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"Colonial labor-reserve neglect created the ungoverned north JNIM exploits; the war maps the extraction geography.","alt":"Governance failure and the VDP militia's ethnic reprisals may be the stronger proximate driver — jihadism recruits on state abuse as much as state absence; Fulani stigmatization is a cause, not just context. The insurgency could persist even with equitable development.","why":"The structural map tracks the neglect geography too closely to dismiss, but the reprisal-radicalization loop is proximate and observable — so extraction is weighted as terrain, not sole engine. MOD."},"refs":[{"t":"Global Centre for R2P: Central Sahel (929 CSOs, OHCHR closure)","u":"https://www.globalr2p.org/countries/mali/"},{"t":"Global Centre for R2P: Central Sahel","u":"https://www.globalr2p.org/countries/mali/"},{"t":"Africa Center: encirclement strategy","u":"https://africacenter.org/spotlight/jnim-attacks-western-mali-sahel/"},{"t":"ACLED: Sahel conflict data","u":"https://acleddata.com/region/africa/"},{"t":"Crisis Group: Burkina Faso","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/sahel/burkina-faso"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"94 = the world's worst jihadist-violence toll per capita, 929 civil society organisations suspended, and the UN human rights office permanently closing. Scored on the combination of violence AND the removal of anyone able to observe it.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"929 CSOs suspended; OHCHR office permanent closure recorded"}],"spill":[{"n":"Mali / Niger","dir":"both","w":"The AES core — shared jihadist theatre, shared Russian security model, joint ICC withdrawal, and Katiba Macina operating across the Mali-Burkina line."},{"n":"Côte d’Ivoire / Ghana / Benin","dir":"out","w":"The coastal states to the south are the expansion frontier; JNIM pressure on the borders is the southward-spread thesis."},{"n":"Russia","dir":"in","w":"Africa Corps fights alongside the army, paid in mining concessions — security financed by selling the thing secured.","id":"russia-domestic"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Junta counter-insurgency, civic space closed","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation → state contraction","up":["JNIM tightens the blockade of provincial capitals","The 929 suspended CSOs and closed UN office remove monitoring","Africa Corps operations drive communal reprisal","Withdrawal from ECOWAS deepens isolation"],"down":["A negotiated accommodation with JNIM (some local pacts exist)","Restoration of regional security cooperation","Addressing the Fulani grievance the insurgency recruits on","A mutually hurting stalemate — not yet perceived by the junta"],"fw":["resource-conflict","conflict-curve","ethnic-security"]},"macro":{"growth":"4.3% (2025) · 4.5% (2026f) · GDP $27.1B nominal","infl":"4.2% (2024)","src":"IMF / compiled national statistics, 2026","note":"Per capita $1,110; government debt 52.7% of GDP; gold and cotton are the exports. THE EXPORT ROUTE IS THE VULNERABILITY: Benin takes 25.9% and Togo 12% — the landlocked economy depends on the coastal states its jihadist frontier is now expanding into. Growth near 4.5% coexists with 929 suspended civil society organisations and a closed UN human rights office, which is a reminder that macro stability and state contraction are not opposites."},"inherit":{"name":"The CFA Franc","est":"1945","status":"IN FORCE · CONTESTED","what":"Created by the French provisional government in December 1945 as the <b>franc des Colonies Françaises d'Afrique</b> — the franc of the French Colonies of Africa. It was not a neutral monetary tool: it was an administrative instrument for managing colonial economies in the service of Paris, imposed by requiring taxes to be paid in it and by banning the cowrie and manilla currencies that preceded it. Colonial administrators used legal sanction and physical force where the ban failed.","now":"<b>It still circulates, used by 227 million people across fourteen countries as of 2026</b>, in two zones — West and Central African — with the French Treasury and, through the euro peg, the European Central Bank retaining roles in its governance. The Tax Justice Network's assessment is that it 'still functions according to the same principles and purpose established during the colonial period'. <b>Fiscal policy is national; monetary power is outsourced.</b> The 1994 devaluation, decided at a late-night meeting in Dakar, halved the savings of millions overnight at a rate nobody affected had voted on.","change":"France agreed in May 2020 to end its engagement in the West African zone and the reserve-deposit requirement, with the replacement currency — the <b>eco</b> — due to launch. <b>The launch has now been postponed five times; the current target is July 2027.</b> Burkina Faso rewrote its Mining Code in July 2024 to raise state participation — but within the CFA zone, transfers remain free and unrestricted, so the revenue can still leave under monetary rules Ouagadougou does not set."},"monitors":[{"t":"ACLED · conflict event data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}]},{"id":"mali","cat":"military","region":"West / Central Africa","subregion":"Sahel Core","name":"Mali — JNIM Economic Warfare + FLA/Azawad","sub":"Renewed Jul 4 offensive (Anefis/Aguelhoc/Gao) · $50M UAE ransom to JNIM","domain":"Jihadist Insurgency / Economic Siege","sev":"critical","status":"escalating","since":"2012","esc":94,"lat":17.6,"lon":-3,"trend":"up","w14":false,"exchange_active":false,"esc_history":[91,92,92,92,92,92,92,92,94,94],"cas":"29% of Sahel militant deaths; 207 JNIM deaths Jan-Apr; Defense Min Camara killed","disp":"~2M displaced","flag":"🇲🇱","note":"THE RESOURCE-FOR-SECURITY MODEL HAS A VERDICT AGAINST IT: Critical Threats' assessment is direct — 'RUSSIAN FORCES HAVE FAILED TO DEGRADE THE STRENGTHENING SALAFI-JIHADI INSURGENCIES IN THE SAHEL, LEAVING THEIR PARTNERS VULNERABLE AND IN INCREASING NEED OF REGIME SECURITY SUPPORT.' That is the clearest external judgement yet on the arrangement this board has tracked since CAR: the juntas traded mining concessions for a security outcome France could not deliver, and Africa Corps has not delivered it either — while the dependency deepens because failure increases the need for regime protection · SEPARATELY, A CAPABILITY THRESHOLD: separatist Tuareg rebels with loose ties to Sahelian jihadist networks conducted a DRONE SWARM ATTACK on a Malian army base in the north — their most sophisticated drone operation to date · 27 JUL 2026 — AFRICA CORPS AMBUSHED, AND BAMAKO IS ASKING THE OBVIOUS QUESTION: an ambush has hit Russian-aligned forces, with no casualty figures released · the Africa Intelligence Brief's read: 'Russia's growing footprint in the Sahel is facing the same insurgent realities that long frustrated previous missions', and the incident 'has reinforced a mood of deep anxiety in Bamako about the durability of security partnerships' · THIS IS THE TEST OF THE RESOURCE-FOR-SECURITY MODEL. Mali expelled France and paid Russia in mining concessions for a result France could not deliver. If Africa Corps cannot deliver it either, the junta has traded the concessions for nothing — and the platform's Sahel thesis gets its clearest evidence either way · JUL 2026 — JNIM TURNS ON THE DOZO: ACLED records JNIM launching a DEADLY CAMPAIGN AGAINST DOZO MILITIAS · the Dozo are one of the three pillars the junta's security rests on alongside FAMa and Africa Corps — targeting them attacks the manpower base directly, and because Dozo formations have been implicated in massacres of Fulani civilians, each round of this feeds the communal recruitment cycle both sides depend on · JUL 2026 — ANÉFIS FALLS, THE GOLD IS NATIONALISED, ALGIERS RETURNS: the FLA SEIZED ANÉFIS and has tightened its grip on northern Mali (RFI); rebels have attacked army bases, a prison, and a CONVOY CARRYING SOLDIERS AND RUSSIAN MERCENARIES · THE STRANGULATION STRATEGY, NAMED: since 2024 JNIM has worked to isolate Bamako from its economic lifelines — targeting the KAYES MINING REGION and the towns linking Mali to Senegal, Mauritania, Guinea and Côte d'Ivoire, which are the PRIMARY SOURCES OF MALIAN FUEL IMPORTS; the September 2025 blockade formalised it · BAMAKO'S COUNTER-MOVES ARE ECONOMIC: it has created a GOLD AGENCY to tighten control of exports and approved a $190M power-grid project for the capital — the junta answering an insurgency with resource centralisation · DIPLOMATIC SHIFT: Algeria-Mali relations have THAWED after a year-long rift over a downed drone · the human floor: children working illegal gold mines in western Mali, underpaid and abused · APR-JUL 2026 — DECAPITATION + THE FLA ALLIANCE: JNIM killed DEFENCE MINISTER Gen. SADIO CAMARA in an SVBIED strike (state funeral Bamako, Apr 30) along with several senior officers — the insurgency reaching the junta's command core · the Apr 25 assault was UNPRECEDENTED AND COORDINATED with the Tuareg AZAWAD LIBERATION FRONT (FLA) across central/northern Mali — jihadist-separatist operational convergence, the fusion Bamako most feared · May 6: arson on trucks carrying FOOD to the capital, widening the Sept-2025 fuel blockade into a full supply strangulation · ISSP briefly entered Ménaka before withdrawing · ATROCITY RECORD BOTH WAYS: FAMa + Africa Corps intensified air operations with mounting civilian harm — Bellingcat/Jeune Afrique documented the FIRST CONFIRMED USE OF RUSSIAN-MADE CLUSTER MUNITIONS in Mali · UN recorded 2,640 civilian deaths across the central Sahel Jan-Mar 2026 · junta dissolved ALL political parties (May 2025) — the sovereignty revolt now governs by decree · JUL 1: JNIM launched SEVEN coordinated attacks across Ségou + Kayes regions incl. Kayes city itself — region home to ~80% of Mali's industrial gold production, previously largely spared · army claims 80+ militants killed; JNIM claims dozens of soldiers + 100+ vehicles + governor's residence damage (DISPUTED figures) · JNIM threatened Kayes/Nioro blockade next day · Crisis Group: testing urban defenses, aiming to \"encourage the regime's collapse from within\" · 245,000+ Malian refugees now in Mauritania (~5% of its population) · JUL 4: renewed FLA/JNIM offensive at Anefis, Aguelhoc, Gao · Africa Center CONFIRMED: JNIM received $50M from UAE for release of 2 Emirati citizens — direct external-cash-to-jihadist link · JNIM adopting HTS-model (nationalist \"single front to dismantle the junta\" rhetoric; asked Russia to stay neutral) · army+Wagner mass-grave executions of Fulani continuing (HRW: 22 bodies Diafarabé)","leverage":[{"actor":"JNIM","asset":"Bamako fuel blockade — economic siege of the capital; 6,000 fighters; taxes/governance","str":"hi"},{"actor":"FLA (Azawad Liberation Front)","asset":"Seized Kidal; structured JNIM alliance; northern control","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Goïta junta + Africa Corps","asset":"Su-24 airstrikes, drones; but withdrew from Kidal region","str":"md"},{"actor":"Coastal neighbors (Senegal/CI)","asset":"95% of Mali fuel transits their routes — chokepoint leverage","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Mali Armed Forces General Staff","detail":"Confirmed Apr 25 coordinated attacks 'by unidentified armed terrorist groups'; Foreign Min Diop calls ECOWAS exit 'final'"},{"src":"US/UK Embassies","detail":"Urged citizens to 'depart immediately'; pulled diplomatic staff (Oct 2025)"},{"src":"Mali Education Ministry","detail":"Ordered schools shut Oct 26-Nov 9 due to blockade"},{"src":"Mali Armed Forces","detail":"Claims 80+ militants killed repelling the Jul 1 seven-site assault (figures disputed by JNIM claims)"},{"src":"Mauritania Government","detail":"245,000+ Malian refugees registered — equivalent to ~5% of Mauritania's population"}],"mil":"Mali accounts for 29% of Sahel militant-Islamist fatalities. The April 25 offensive was the largest since the 2012 rebellion: JNIM and the Azawad Liberation Front (FLA) launched coordinated attacks on Bamako, Kati, Mopti, Sévaré and Gao, killed Defense Minister Sadio Camara, and wounded the intelligence chief. The FLA seized Kidal after Malian and Africa Corps forces withdrew. Violence in Kayes, Sikasso, and Segou surged to the highest monthly levels since ACLED began recording in 1997.","pol":"JNIM's signature weapon is economic warfare: since September 2025 it has blockaded fuel into landlocked Bamako, attacking tanker convoys (130+ tankers destroyed per Bellingcat) along the Senegal and Côte d'Ivoire corridors that carry 95% of Mali's fuel. Prices spiked over 400% (to ~$130/litre); schools and embassies closed; Russia pledged 200,000 tons of petroleum to relieve the siege. Crisis Group's Ibrahim: 'If one of these countries falls, the rest will be in trouble.'","eco":"The fuel blockade paralyzed civil services, forced rolling blackouts (fuel prioritized for the state electricity provider), and brought Bamako to a standstill — the first time JNIM has used siege tactics on the capital itself.","assess":"Mali rises to 92. The July 1 Kayes strike is a strategic marker: seven simultaneous attacks hundreds of kilometers apart, hitting the region that produces ~80% of Mali's industrial gold and anchors the Senegal trade corridor. Crisis Group reads it as JNIM testing urban defenses and trying to induce regime collapse from within — the urban phase declared in 2024 is now operational. A DISPUTED single-source claim of Ukrainian involvement in coordinating April's attacks (likely info-op) is logged but not credited.","econlev":[{"holder":"JNIM","channel":"Commodity + corridor","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Kayes region = ~80% of industrial gold output, now under attack; 95% of fuel transits Senegal/CI corridors JNIM blockades"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia (Africa Corps)","side":"junta","flow":"~2,500 personnel; combat ops; gold-refinery support + nuclear/solar deals","tier":"documented","for":"Gold, mining concessions; $2.5B+ African gold → Ukraine war"},{"actor":"🇹🇷 Turkey","side":"junta","flow":"Bayraktar TB2 drones (displaced French suppliers)","tier":"documented","for":"Arms market; no-conditions influence"},{"actor":"🇮🇷 Iran","side":"junta","flow":"Mohajer-6/Shahed drones + IRGC-Quds explosives training; but Quds also coordinates with JNIM for safe havens","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Artisanal gold; anti-Western alignment"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US (corruption signal)","side":"junta-adjacent","flow":"Trump admin lifted Russia-sanctions on 3 senior Malian officials designated for facilitating Wagner (early 2026)","tier":"documented","for":"Attempted security-cooperation re-opening"}],"ext_flag":true,"tclass":"conflict","tier":"CRITICAL","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"Security services traded for gold. The security didn't arrive.","claims":[{"claim":"Russia's Africa Corps provides counter-terrorism support to the junta.","who":"Both — Moscow gets mining concessions and UN votes; the junta gets regime survival with no governance conditions.","evid":"Jihadist violence ROSE after Russian deployment in both Mali and Burkina. The arrangement is not failing at its actual purpose — its actual purpose is regime survival, not counter-terrorism.","status":"CONTESTED — judge it by what it's for"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"mine","actor":"Loulo-Gounkoto, Fekola, Syama, Sadiola","detail":"Africa's 3rd-largest gold reserves. Barrick's Loulo-Gounkoto (Mali's biggest mine) did 723k oz in 2024 — then the junta seized a gold stockpile (Jan 2025), shut it, and forced a settlement: Barrick paid $253M and gave up ground under the 2023 Mining Code (state stake up to 35%). Restarted Dec 2025 at ~260-290k oz.","who":"Junta captures a rising share via the 2023 Mining Code; resource nationalism as regime finance","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"process","actor":"Russia-backed national refinery (Bamako)","detail":"Mali broke ground (Oct 2025) on a Russia-and-Switzerland-backed gold refinery — the sovereignty play. West Africa produces 400+ tonnes/yr (Mali, Ghana, Burkina, Guinea) with NO globally certified refinery, so the region loses the refined-export margin. Framed as 'transparency,' it also routes value through a Russian partner.","who":"Russia (Yadran/partner) co-builds; Africa Corps takes security-for-gold concessions","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"mid","actor":"Exported via UAE + Russia channels","detail":"Alongside industrial output, Mali loses billions/yr to smuggled artisanal gold — off-book, headed for Gulf refiners. The Russian refinery aims to capture that; JNIM also taxes artisanal gold at chokepoints (part of the ~$100M Sahel jihadist war economy).","who":"UAE/Gulf refiners + Russia; JNIM taxes the artisanal share","tier":"alleged-credible"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"end","actor":"Global bullion · Russian reserves","detail":"Refined gold is fungible — the end-buyer opacity is the point. Gold that funds junta survival and Russian sanctions-evasion arrives on the market indistinguishable from clean supply.","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Lithium","stage":"mine","actor":"Goulamina (China's Ganfeng)","detail":"Mali's largest lithium deposit (200M+ tonnes of ore) — majority-owned by China's Ganfeng Lithium, with Australia's Leo Lithium and a Malian state minority. The battery-metal future layered onto the gold economy.","who":"China (Ganfeng) controls the flagship lithium asset","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Lithium","stage":"end","actor":"China → EV batteries","detail":"Spodumene concentrate ships to China for conversion to battery-grade lithium — the same China-controlled processing dependency as the DRC's cobalt and Indonesia's nickel.","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["china-refine","dubai-gold","resource-nationalism"],"history":{"roots":"Mali carries one of the world's great civilizational inheritances — Ghana, Mali, and Songhai empires; Timbuktu's manuscript libraries proving centuries of African scholarship the colonial canon denied existed — and a colonial rupture that inverted it. French conquest made the Sahel a periphery of the coast: the trans-Saharan trading world that had made Timbuktu rich was strangled in favor of export corridors to Dakar and Abidjan, and the north — Tuareg and Arab pastoral country — was administered as a militarized margin. Every independent Malian government inherited that inversion; four Tuareg rebellions (1963, 1990, 2006, 2012) are its recurring bill. Baz Lecocq's and Bruce Hall's work shows the racial hierarchies colonial rule codified between 'white' desert and 'Black' river populations still structuring the conflict. The 2012 collapse — rebellion, jihadist capture, coup, French intervention — began the present decade; the junta's break with France and embrace of Russia completes a sovereignty revolt whose grievances are old and real, even as its methods (Wagner massacres at Moura, junta permanence) betray them.","timeline":[{"date":"1960","e":"Independence from France."},{"date":"2012","e":"Tuareg rebellion + jihadist takeover of the north; a coup topples the government."},{"date":"2013","e":"French Operation Serval retakes northern towns."},{"date":"2020-21","e":"Two coups bring Assimi Goïta's junta to power."},{"date":"2022","e":"France's Barkhane force expelled; Wagner arrives."},{"date":"2023","e":"UN peacekeeping mission (MINUSMA) forced out."},{"date":"2024","e":"AES formed with Burkina and Niger; ECOWAS exit."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"JNIM's fuel blockade chokes Bamako; western Mali (Kayes) offensive."}],"actors":[{"name":"Gen. Assimi Goïta","bio":"Junta leader since the 2020-21 coups; architect of the Russia pivot and the break with France and ECOWAS."},{"name":"JNIM","bio":"Al-Qaeda's Sahel coalition; besieging Bamako economically via fuel blockades rather than assaulting it directly."},{"name":"Russia (Africa Corps)","bio":"Regime-security provider and gold-concession holder; co-building a national refinery."},{"name":"Tuareg (CSP/CMA)","bio":"Northern separatists whose 2012 rebellion started the war; a smoldering unresolved grievance."}],"bridge":"Today's war maps onto the colonial inversion precisely: JNIM's strength lies along the strangled trans-Saharan axes and among pastoralist communities the export-corridor economy abandoned; Bamako's fuel blockade is the periphery besieging the corridor state. The Russia-backed gold refinery is the sovereignty argument made concrete — capture the refining margin France's system exported — while Africa Corps massacres rerun the colonial column's methods under an anti-colonial flag. And Timbuktu's manuscripts, rescued from jihadist burning by its own librarians in 2013, remain the standing rebuke to every narrative that this land lacks its own order to restore. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: Anéfis has fallen, JNIM strangles the fuel corridors, and a new state Gold Agency centralises the one export that still earns. The colonial extraction geography — north as periphery, gold as the state's lifeline — is intact under a junta that expelled the French.","sources":[{"work":"Disputed Desert: Decolonisation, Competing Nationalisms and Tuareg Rebellions in Northern Mali","author":"Baz Lecocq","year":"2010","note":"The definitive account of the north's rebellions as decolonization's unfinished business."},{"work":"A History of Race in Muslim West Africa, 1600–1960","author":"Bruce S. Hall","year":"2011","note":"The racial categories — desert/river, 'white'/'Black' — colonialism codified and the conflict still speaks."},{"work":"The Meanings of Timbuktu","author":"Shamil Jeppie & Souleymane Bachir Diagne (eds.)","year":"2008","note":"The manuscript civilization — primary proof of the intellectual world the 'no written history' myth erased."},{"work":"Moura massacre documentation","author":"UN OHCHR / Human Rights Watch","year":"2022-23","note":"Primary documentation of Wagner/FAMa atrocities — the sovereignty revolt's betrayal in practice."}]},"human":{"dead":20000,"disp":400000,"famine":false,"aid":"constrained","note":"Supply strangulation of Bamako (fuel since Sept 2025, food from May 2026); UN counted 2,640 civilian deaths across the central Sahel in Q1 2026 alone. Atrocities documented on both sides — JNIM sieges and FAMa/Africa Corps air operations, incl. first confirmed Russian cluster-munition use.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"The colonial coast-vs-Sahel inversion: JNIM strong along the strangled trans-Saharan axes, Bamako besieged by its own periphery.","alt":"A jihadist-ideology reading makes doctrine primary over geography; a state-collapse reading makes governance failure and coup instability the driver — the blockade may be capability, not deep structure.","why":"The geography of the insurgency supports the structural frame, but the coup-cycle's role in state collapse is proximate and heavy. MOD."},"refs":[{"t":"Critical Threats · Africa File (19 Mar 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/sudan-rsf-saf-sahel-drc-iscap-uganda-nigeria-boko-iswap-mali-fla-jnim-africa-file-march-26-2026"},{"t":"Critical Threats · Africa File (26 Feb 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/drc-fardc-m23-russia-wagner-mali-burkina-niger-rsf-chad-sudan-fgs-somalia-ghana-aes-ecowas-africa-file-february-26-2026"},{"t":"Africa Intelligence Brief (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.riotimesonline.com/africa-intelligence-brief-monday-july-27-2026/"},{"t":"ACLED · Sahel conflict monitoring","u":"https://acleddata.com/region/conflict-horn-africa"},{"t":"Security Council Report: West Africa & Sahel, Jul 2026","u":"https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/monthly-forecast/2026-07/west-africa-and-the-sahel-17.php"},{"t":"allAfrica/RFI/DW: Anéfis, gold agency, Algeria thaw","u":"https://allafrica.com/mali/"},{"t":"Security Council Report: West Africa & Sahel forecast","u":"https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/monthly-forecast/2026-07/west-africa-and-the-sahel-17.php"}],"checkmode":"SWEPT","escwhy":"94 = an insurgent strangulation strategy targeting fuel and mining corridors, secular separatists now coordinating with jihadists, and Russian auxiliaries documented committing war crimes.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Anéfis fall; Gold Agency; Algeria thaw; JNIM fuel-corridor strategy named"}],"spill":[{"n":"Senegal / Mauritania / Guinea / Côte d’Ivoire","dir":"in","w":"JNIM targets the Kayes mining region and the corridors to these four states — Mali's PRIMARY FUEL IMPORT ROUTES. The strangulation strategy is aimed at the neighbours' roads, not Bamako's walls."},{"n":"Niger","dir":"both","w":"Niamey revoked 33 transport operators' licences for refusing to haul fuel to blockaded Mali — coercing its own truckers into a neighbour's war economy.","id":"niger"},{"n":"Algeria","dir":"both","w":"Ansar Dine's HQ sits at Tinzaouaten on the Algerian border; relations thawed in 2026 after a year-long rift over a downed drone."},{"n":"Russia","dir":"in","w":"Africa Corps fights alongside FAMa and is PAID IN MINING CONCESSIONS — security financed by the sale of the thing being secured.","id":"russia-domestic"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Fuel-corridor strangulation, gold centralised","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation → economic siege","up":["JNIM chokes the import routes from the coast","Anéfis-type northern losses continue","Africa Corps paid in concessions deepens the war economy","Coordination with Burkina/Niger militarises the whole AES core"],"down":["The corridor states (Senegal, Mauritania, Guinea, Côte d'Ivoire) forcing a security bargain","A negotiated fuel-access arrangement","Cutting the mining-for-security payment model","Ripeness if the blockade makes the stalemate hurt Bamako enough"],"fw":["resource-conflict","proxy","ripeness"]},"macro":{"growth":"4.9% (2025) · 5.1% (2026f) · GDP $23.2B nominal","infl":"Contained; CFA franc peg","src":"IMF / compiled national statistics, 2026","note":"GROWING AT 5% WHILE LOSING TERRITORY. GDP per capita $936. THE NUMBER IS ALSO PROBABLY WRONG IN A REVEALING WAY: analysts estimate roughly 35% of Malian activity is informal and the country still uses a GDP base year a decade old, so official output may understate the real economy substantially. Gold is centralised through the new state agency; the war economy — JNIM's zakat, blockade rents, artisanal gold — appears in nobody's national accounts. A growth figure that excludes the war economy is measuring the half of the country the state still counts."},"inherit":{"name":"The CFA Franc","est":"1945","status":"IN FORCE · CONTESTED","what":"Created by the French provisional government in December 1945 as the <b>franc des Colonies Françaises d'Afrique</b> — the franc of the French Colonies of Africa. It was not a neutral monetary tool: it was an administrative instrument for managing colonial economies in the service of Paris, imposed by requiring taxes to be paid in it and by banning the cowrie and manilla currencies that preceded it. Colonial administrators used legal sanction and physical force where the ban failed.","now":"<b>It still circulates, used by 227 million people across fourteen countries as of 2026</b>, in two zones — West and Central African — with the French Treasury and, through the euro peg, the European Central Bank retaining roles in its governance. The Tax Justice Network's assessment is that it 'still functions according to the same principles and purpose established during the colonial period'. <b>Fiscal policy is national; monetary power is outsourced.</b> The 1994 devaluation, decided at a late-night meeting in Dakar, halved the savings of millions overnight at a rate nobody affected had voted on.","change":"France agreed in May 2020 to end its engagement in the West African zone and the reserve-deposit requirement, with the replacement currency — the <b>eco</b> — due to launch. <b>The launch has now been postponed five times; the current target is July 2027.</b> Mali's junta has nationalised gold and rewritten mining contracts in the name of sovereignty, and the AES states are openly preparing to leave the currency — which their own analysts describe as the last and strongest chain, precisely because it is the least visible."},"ctrl":{"gov":40,"contested":25,"other":35,"est":true,"src":"Composite of ACLED Sahel data, 2026","note":"Bamako and the south are held. JNIM operates across the centre and blockades supply corridors; the north is contested between the state, Africa Corps, FLA separatists and jihadist groups."},"control":{"gov":45,"contested":30,"other":25,"holders":"Bamako and the south under government and Africa Corps control. JNIM operates across the centre and strangles the fuel corridors. FLA/Tuareg separatists hold parts of the north; Anéfis has fallen.","note":"Territorial percentages understate the problem — JNIM's blockade strategy targets the roads rather than the map.","est":true}},{"id":"niger","cat":"military","region":"West / Central Africa","subregion":"Sahel Core","name":"Niger — ISSP + JNIM + Kidnapping Hub","sub":"Kirtachi camp overrun · Niamey/Tahoua airports · Tiani to 2030","domain":"Jihadist Insurgency","sev":"critical","status":"escalating","since":"2015","esc":88,"lat":17.6,"lon":8,"trend":"up","w14":false,"esc_history":[88,88,88,88,88,88,88,88,88,88],"cas":"17% of Sahel militant deaths; Kirtachi 12 soldiers killed + 7 captured","disp":"500,000+ displaced","flag":"🇳🇪","note":"GAP IN THIS BOARD'S RECORD — IS STRUCK THE NIAMEY AIRPORT: on 28 JANUARY 2026 the Islamic State attacked NIAMEY AIRPORT, its FIRST MAJOR ATTACK ON A HARDENED POSITION near the capital. Russian forces repelled it, and junta leader TIANI publicly praised their role · the day before, Tiani and Defence Minister Salifou Modi met AFRICA CORPS HEAD YUNUS-BEK YEVKUROV in Niamey to discuss INCREASING Russian involvement in counterterrorism · the sequence is the dependency mechanism in miniature: an attack the Russians repel produces a request for more Russians. Burkina Faso's foreign minister signed further military-technical agreements in Moscow on 12-14 February · JUL 2026 — TILLABÉRI IS THE DEADLIEST REGION IN THE CENTRAL SAHEL: ACLED records more than 1,200 FATALITIES in Tillabéri across 2025 — surpassing every region in Mali and Burkina Faso — with the HIGHEST LEVEL OF CIVILIAN TARGETING of any region in the three central Sahel states · the violence is geographically widespread across many departments rather than concentrated, which is what competition between jihadist groups looks like rather than a single insurgency · 2026 — DECAPITATION AT THE LOCAL LEVEL, AND A TURN OUTWARD: jihadists killed the PREFECT OF TORODI — also an army captain — ALONG WITH HIS ENTIRE FAMILY (4 Jan), seizing government vehicles and freeing prisoners; 31 villagers were killed at Gorouol in Tillabéri (18 Jan) · on 12 Jan the government REVOKED THE LICENCES OF 33 TRANSPORT OPERATORS for refusing to haul fuel to blockaded Mali — coercing its own truckers into a neighbour's war economy · STRATEGIC SHIFT: under security pressure Tiani is reaching out to ALGERIA and BENIN, putting national interest ahead of AES solidarity, even as anti-French positioning holds · the Jun-2026 JNIM strike on Niamey airport remains the emblem: the site France and the US vacated is the one the new order cannot protect · JUN 18 2026 — JNIM HITS NIAMEY INTERNATIONAL AIRPORT: gunmen attacked the airport — which doubles as a MILITARY BASE hosting AES-associated facilities — killing 11 members of the armed forces and 2 civilians; 22 assailants killed. JNIM claimed it · the strike lands at the symbolic center of the junta's sovereignty claim: the very site vacated by French and US forces is now the target the new security order cannot protect · NEW STANDALONE · ISSP + JNIM outbidding · JNIM overran Kirtachi National Guard camp (12 killed, 7 captured) · ISSP hit Niamey Airport (Jan) + Tahoua Airport (Mar) + Niger-Benin oil pipeline sabotage · record Westerner kidnapping hub · Tiani dissolved parties, 5-yr transition to 2030 · ISSP leader Souleymane Ag Bakawa arrested by Mali/Africa Corps (Ménaka) · ACLED: Tillaberi now DEADLIEST region in central Sahel (1,200+ fatalities 2025, surpassing all Mali/Burkina regions); ISSP primary driver; Tera dept worst-hit (Burkina border)","leverage":[{"actor":"ISSP (IS Sahel)","asset":"Airport strikes; pipeline sabotage; kidnapping outsourcing; Nigeria-border bridge","str":"hi"},{"actor":"JNIM","asset":"Kirtachi camp seizure; western Niger expansion","str":"md"},{"actor":"Tiani junta + Africa Corps","asset":"AES Unified Force air campaigns; capital defense (failing)","str":"lo"},{"actor":"Niger-Benin pipeline","asset":"Oil-export lifeline — a sabotage target = economic leverage","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Niger Government","detail":"Confirmed AES Unified Force 'intense air campaigns' after Apr 25; accuses France of sponsoring attacks; cancelled May 1 parades for security"},{"src":"Tiani transitional decree","detail":"Dissolved all political parties (Mar 2025); 5-year transitional mandate to 2030"}],"mil":"Niger accounts for 17% of Sahel militant-Islamist fatalities. JNIM overran a National Guard camp at Kirtachi (12 soldiers killed, 7 captured), while ISSP has hit high-value infrastructure: Niamey Airport (January), Tahoua Airport (March), and repeated sabotage of the Niger-Benin oil pipeline. ISSP is expanding rear bases southward toward the Nigeria border, replicating Sahel massacre patterns via local 'Lakurawa' recruits.","pol":"Niger is now the epicenter of the Sahel's Westerner-kidnapping economy — ISSP outsources abductions to criminal networks, driving record-high kidnapping in Mali and Niger. General Tiani dissolved all political parties after a stage-managed national conference and began a five-year transitional mandate running to 2030. The government blames France for sponsoring regional attacks.","eco":"The Niger-Benin oil pipeline is both the country's export lifeline and a repeated ISSP sabotage target — economic warfare mirroring JNIM's Mali blockade.","assess":"Niger enters at 88. It is the ISSP stronghold to Mali/Burkina's JNIM core, and the JNIM-ISSP outbidding competition is driving both toward higher-impact attacks (airports, pipelines, kidnappings). Tiani's extension to 2030 confirms the coup's justification — that juntas would restore security — has not been met. The Nigeria-border bridge is the key expansion vector.","external":[{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia (Africa Corps)","side":"junta","flow":"MoU for uranium extraction; security presence","tier":"documented","for":"Uranium (SOMAÏR nationalized), migration leverage"},{"actor":"🇮🇷 Iran","side":"junta","flow":"Advanced drones-for-uranium deal: ~300 metric tons yellowcake (~$56M) for drones + SAMs + economic aid","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Uranium for nuclear program — the sharpest resource-for-arms swap"},{"actor":"🇹🇷 Turkey","side":"junta","flow":"Turkish Class III armed drones","tier":"documented","for":"Arms market expansion"}],"ext_flag":true,"tclass":"conflict","tier":"CRITICAL","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","econlev":[{"holder":"ISSP / JNIM","channel":"Kidnapping + trade-route tax","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Niger is the Sahel's kidnapping hub — ISSP shifted to high-value foreign abductions (an Austrian in Agadez, 4 Moroccan truckers) because foreigners command higher ransoms"},{"holder":"Niger state","channel":"Uranium nationalization","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Seized Orano's Somaïr; uranium is the state's counter-leverage against France/the West"}],"contested":{"summary":"Niger's uranium dispute is a contest over what the resource was actually worth, and to whom.","claims":[{"claim":"Orano's uranium contracts were fair and mutually beneficial.","who":"Orano and the French state; rejected by Niamey.","evid":"Niger supplied a substantial share of French and EU reactor fuel for decades while ranking near the bottom of the Human Development Index, and the mining regions have among the country's weakest electrification and health indicators. Pricing terms were long set below spot. Orano disputes the characterisation and has pursued arbitration over the seizure of its assets; the junta's own extraction record is not itself transparent.","status":"CONTESTED-WITH-EVIDENCE-BOTH-WAYS"},{"claim":"The 2023 coup was driven principally by anti-French sentiment.","who":"Widely asserted in international coverage.","evid":"Analysts point to intra-elite competition and the impending removal of the presidential guard commander as proximate triggers, with anti-French mobilisation following rather than preceding the seizure. Both readings have supporting evidence; the platform holds this at MODERATE.","status":"CONTESTED"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Uranium","stage":"mine","actor":"Arlit / SOMAÏR (nationalized from Orano)","detail":"World's ~8th-largest uranium producer. The junta stripped France's Orano of its 63.4% SOMAÏR stake (June 2025), nationalized the mine, and declared it would sell 'to whoever wishes to buy.' Grievance: Orano took 86.3% of commercialized uranium since 1971, above its equity share.","who":"Junta (via SOPAMIN/TNUC) seized operational control; expelled the French operator","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Uranium","stage":"process","actor":"Yellowcake stranded at Airbase 101, Niamey","detail":"THE CHOKEPOINT: 1,000-1,500 tonnes of yellowcake sit on trucks that haven't moved in weeks, next to Niamey's airport. An ICSID tribunal (Sept 2025) ordered Niger NOT to sell/transfer SOMAÏR output pending Orano's claim — so no Western buyer, insurer, or shipper will touch it. Sovereignty without a market.","who":"ICSID ruling blocks legitimate buyers; the uranium is Niger's and unsellable","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Uranium","stage":"mid","actor":"Rosatom / Uranium One (Russia) — the only buyer","detail":"Niger signed a July-2025 Rosatom MoU and a Dec-2025 TNUC-Uranium One deal; reportedly agreed to sell Rosatom ~1,000t of yellowcake. Russia is 'almost certainly' the buyer willing to defy the tribunal. Iran also pursued a ~300t yellowcake-for-drones swap (~$56M). Western majors (Global Atomic's Dasa) still operate with junta blessing.","who":"Russia (Rosatom) is the only actor willing to move stranded, litigated uranium","tier":"alleged-credible"},{"mineral":"Uranium","stage":"end","actor":"Nuclear fuel · Russian/Iranian programs","detail":"Enriched into reactor fuel — the strategic prize. Niger's ~40% of the old EU-supply picture is now being locked into the Russian nuclear-fuel orbit while the West forfeits it, mirroring the Kazakhstan reactor-bid loss.","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["resource-nationalism"],"history":{"roots":"Niger's uranium story is Françafrique in its purest concentrate. From 1971, France's Orano (né Areva/Cogema) mined Arlit under agreements that Nigerien scholar Rahmane Idrissa and others document as near-tributary: Niger — consistently at the bottom of the Human Development Index — powered one in three French lightbulbs while Arlit's workers breathed radioactive dust and the state took a sliver of the value (the junta's figure: 86.3% of commercialized uranium taken above equity share since 1971). The CFA franc, French basing rights, and aid conditionality completed the package Nkrumah had named in 1965: neo-colonialism, independence's form without its substance. The 2023 coup rode genuine popular rage at that bargain — the crowds at the French base were not manufactured — and the SOMAÏR nationalization is the bill presented. That the uranium now sits stranded, sellable only to Rosatom, is the tragedy of a revolt that could name the extractor but not build the alternative.","timeline":[{"date":"1960","e":"Independence from France; uranium mining begins soon after."},{"date":"2010s","e":"Niger becomes a key US/French counterterrorism hub (drone base at Agadez)."},{"date":"26 Jul 2023","e":"Presidential guard coups President Bazoum."},{"date":"2023","e":"French forces expelled; junta joins the anti-Western Sahel bloc."},{"date":"2024","e":"US forced to leave the Agadez drone base; AES confederation formed."},{"date":"Jun 2025","e":"Junta nationalizes Orano's SOMAÏR uranium mine."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Uranium stranded by an ICSID ruling; Rosatom the only willing buyer."}],"actors":[{"name":"Gen. Abdourahamane Tiani","bio":"Presidential-guard commander who led the 2023 coup and now heads the junta; expelled Western forces, seized the uranium."},{"name":"Orano (France)","bio":"The French nuclear firm stripped of its Niger uranium stake — the extraction relationship the junta upended."},{"name":"Russia (Rosatom)","bio":"The only actor willing to buy litigated, stranded Nigerien uranium — Moscow's Sahel foothold."},{"name":"JNIM / IS-Sahel","bio":"Jihadist groups exploiting the border zones; the original rationale for the Western presence now gone."}],"bridge":"The stranded yellowcake at Airbase 101 is the whole predicament in 1,500 tonnes: sovereignty asserted (the seizure), the old patron expelled (Orano, the French army, the US drone base), and the extraction structure still standing — only the buyer's flag changes, France's grid swapped for Rosatom's supply chain, with the ICSID tribunal enforcing the old order's law against the new order's claim. Jihadist violence meanwhile spreads through the borderlands the uranium economy never developed. Niger is the platform's cleanest test of whether resource nationalism without processing capacity is leverage or just a different dependency. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: a prefect and his family assassinated, truckers' licences seized to move fuel to Mali, and the uranium nationalised. Niamey has taken the resource France controlled and inherited the security problem France could not solve either.","sources":[{"work":"The Politics of Islam in the Sahel: Between Persuasion and Violence","author":"Rahmane Idrissa","year":"2017","note":"Nigerien scholarship on the state, religion, and the Sahel crisis from inside."},{"work":"Neo-Colonialism: The Last Stage of Imperialism","author":"Kwame Nkrumah","year":"1965","note":"The primary theorization of independence-without-substance that the uranium arrangements exemplified."},{"work":"Being Nuclear: Africans and the Global Uranium Trade","author":"Gabrielle Hecht","year":"2012","note":"Arlit's labor and health history — how Nigerien uranium was made invisible in 'French' nuclear power."},{"work":"From Empires to NGOs in the West African Sahel","author":"Gregory Mann","year":"2015","note":"How sovereignty leaked from Sahelian states to external administrators — the vacuum the juntas now claim to fill."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"Françafrique in concentrate: uranium extraction as near-tribute, the coup as the bill presented.","alt":"A security reading centers the jihadist-insurgency failure (not extraction) as what actually toppled Bazoum — the uranium grievance may be junta legitimation rather than coup cause.","why":"The coup's timing tracks the security crisis more than the uranium file; the platform holds extraction as the structural grievance and insurgency as the proximate trigger. HIGH on the distinction."},"refs":[{"t":"Critical Threats · Africa File (26 Feb 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/drc-fardc-m23-russia-wagner-mali-burkina-niger-rsf-chad-sudan-fgs-somalia-ghana-aes-ecowas-africa-file-february-26-2026"},{"t":"ACLED · Africa Overview","u":"https://acleddata.com/update/africa-overview-january-2026"},{"t":"The Africa Report: Tiani's outreach to Algeria and Benin","u":"https://www.theafricareport.com/country/mali/"},{"t":"Security Council Report: Niamey airport attack","u":"https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/monthly-forecast/2026-07/west-africa-and-the-sahel-17.php"},{"t":"Crisis Group: Niger","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/sahel/niger"},{"t":"World Nuclear Association: uranium","u":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/country-profiles/countries-g-n/niger"}],"human":{"dead":6000,"disp":500000,"famine":false,"aid":"access-constrained","note":"Jihadist violence across Tillabéri and Diffa plus the Lake Chad front; ~500k displaced including Nigerian and Malian refugees. The Jun-2026 Niamey airport attack killed 11 soldiers and 2 civilians.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"SWEPT","escwhy":"88 = state authority contracting to the capital, prefect-level assassinations, and a fuel-coercion economy — with the airport strike as the emblem of the security vacuum after Western withdrawal.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Torodi prefect killing; trucker licence revocations; Tiani outreach to Algeria and Benin"}],"spill":[{"n":"Mali","dir":"both","w":"Niamey revoked 33 truckers' licences for refusing to haul fuel to blockaded Mali — coerced into a neighbour's war economy; shared JNIM/ISSP theatre.","id":"mali"},{"n":"Nigeria","dir":"both","w":"Lakurawa on the Sokoto-Kebbi-Niger frontier; JNIM's Nigerian Brigade; the weakened Multinational Joint Task Force after Niger's own withdrawal.","id":"nigeria"},{"n":"Benin / Algeria","dir":"both","w":"Tiani reaching out to Algeria and Benin, putting national interest ahead of AES solidarity under security pressure."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Junta consolidation, uranium nationalised","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation → sovereignty assertion","up":["JNIM/ISSP expand from the Mali-Burkina tri-border","A prefect assassination-type decapitation of local admin","The MJTF collapse after Niger's withdrawal reopens seams","Coercion of truckers militarises the economy"],"down":["Reaching out to Algeria and Benin (Tiani has begun)","Restoring the regional counter-terror architecture","Uranium revenue funding actual security provision","Addressing the tri-border communal drivers"],"fw":["resource-conflict","conflict-curve","ethnic-security"]},"macro":{"growth":"Historically among Africa's fastest (11.5%, 2022)","infl":"Moderate; CFA franc peg","src":"World Bank; compiled statistics","note":"Uranium nationalisation moved the principal export from French control to state control, but the security cost has risen with it — Tillabéri recorded more than 1,200 fatalities in 2025, the deadliest region in the central Sahel. The fiscal question this board tracks: whether resource nationalisation funds security provision or simply changes who collects the rent while the insurgency expands."},"inherit":{"name":"The CFA Franc","est":"1945","status":"IN FORCE · CONTESTED","what":"Created by the French provisional government in December 1945 as the <b>franc des Colonies Françaises d'Afrique</b> — the franc of the French Colonies of Africa. It was not a neutral monetary tool: it was an administrative instrument for managing colonial economies in the service of Paris, imposed by requiring taxes to be paid in it and by banning the cowrie and manilla currencies that preceded it. Colonial administrators used legal sanction and physical force where the ban failed.","now":"<b>It still circulates, used by 227 million people across fourteen countries as of 2026</b>, in two zones — West and Central African — with the French Treasury and, through the euro peg, the European Central Bank retaining roles in its governance. The Tax Justice Network's assessment is that it 'still functions according to the same principles and purpose established during the colonial period'. <b>Fiscal policy is national; monetary power is outsourced.</b> The 1994 devaluation, decided at a late-night meeting in Dakar, halved the savings of millions overnight at a rate nobody affected had voted on.","change":"France agreed in May 2020 to end its engagement in the West African zone and the reserve-deposit requirement, with the replacement currency — the <b>eco</b> — due to launch. <b>The launch has now been postponed five times; the current target is July 2027.</b> Niger nationalised uranium — the resource France controlled for six decades — while remaining inside the currency France created to buy such resources without spending its own reserves."},"monitors":[{"t":"ACLED · Niger conflict data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}]},{"id":"benin-togo","cat":"military","region":"West / Central Africa","subregion":"Coastal Frontier","name":"Benin & Togo — JNIM Coastal Expansion","sub":"Benin deadliest on record · JNIM emir appointed · Kpendjal","domain":"Jihadist Spillover","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"2019","esc":72,"lat":10.5,"lon":1.5,"trend":"up","w14":false,"esc_history":[72,72,72,72,72,72,72,72,72,72],"cas":"Benin 305 deaths (+76%, record); Togo 54 civ + 8 soldiers","disp":"Northern border displacement","flag":"🇧🇯🇹🇬","note":"JUL 2026 — (checked) no new coastal-state attack surfaced this cycle; the JNIM northern-parks expansion and the Benin-Niger pipeline-driven rapprochement already logged remain the live threads. The southward-spread thesis is unchanged pending the next incident · JUN 2026 — BENIN AND NIGER MOVE TO RESTORE RELATIONS: renewed efforts to repair bilateral ties severed after Niger's coup and the ECOWAS sanctions that followed — the pipeline that carries Nigerien crude to Benin's coast is the material reason a rupture could not hold. Economic geography reasserting itself over bloc politics · NEW · Crisis Group: Benin + Togo 'in greatest peril' · Benin 305 militant deaths = deadliest on record (+76%) · JNIM appointed an EMIR for Benin · Koualou attack (disputed Benin-Burkina border) · Togo: Kpendjal Prefecture worst-hit, 54 civ killed · 450+ incidents/yr within 50km of coastal borders","leverage":[{"actor":"JNIM","asset":"Appointed Benin emir; deliberate strategic expansion (not spillover); border-zone control","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Benin/Togo states","asset":"Militarized north; refugee-vetting (Ivorian model); regional coordination","str":"md"},{"actor":"Coastal ports","asset":"Landlocked-Sahel dependence = leverage over AES fuel/trade","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Togo Government","detail":"Established one of the sub-region's most important security systems in the north; Cinkassé/Kpendjal front lines"},{"src":"Benin Government","detail":"Military positions at Koualou attacked; disputed border zone with Burkina Faso"},{"src":"AES-ECOWAS (Togo-mediated)","detail":"Togo FM Dussey offered to 'bridge' AES and ECOWAS — first formal interaction since May 2025"}],"mil":"Benin recorded 305 militant-Islamist-linked fatalities over the past year — the deadliest on record and a 76% jump — while Togo saw at least 54 civilians and 8 soldiers killed, concentrated in Kpendjal Prefecture wedged between the Burkina and Benin borders. JNIM launched a coordinated attack on military positions at Koualou (a Benin-Burkina disputed border town). Crisis Group assesses this as deliberate strategic expansion, not mere spillover.","pol":"The clearest signal of intent: JNIM's leadership appointed a dedicated emir for Benin. ACLED-recorded JNIM incidents in Benin rose from 6 (2019-21) to 66 in 2022, 173 in 2023, 138 in 2024 — a sustained campaign. Togo has built one of the sub-region's most robust northern security systems; its FM is mediating between the AES and ECOWAS.","eco":"Both states are the coastal outlet for the landlocked AES — giving them chokepoint leverage over Sahel fuel/trade, but also making their northern borders the frontier of the jihadist advance toward the sea.","assess":"Benin and Togo enter as a clustered coastal-frontier theater at 72 — the leading edge of JNIM's southward push toward the Gulf of Guinea. Crisis Group names them 'in greatest peril.' The appointment of a Benin emir and the record death tolls mark the transition from spillover to a deliberate coastal campaign. This is the frontier to watch for whether the Sahel war reaches the Atlantic.","tclass":"conflict","tier":"SEVERE","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","external":[{"actor":"🏴 JNIM","side":"insurgents","flow":"Katiba Hanifa faction pushing into Alibori, Borgou, Atacora; a dedicated emir appointed for Benin","tier":"documented","for":"Coastal access — converting a landlocked insurgency into one with port reach"},{"actor":"🇫🇷 France / EU","side":"coastal states","flow":"Security assistance to the littoral states as the buffer against Sahel spillover","tier":"documented","for":"Hold the line at the Gulf of Guinea after being expelled from the Sahel"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"coastal states","flow":"Counter-terror cooperation; Benin also pledged up to 2,000 troops to Haiti's GSF","tier":"documented","for":"Prevent jihadist coastal breakout; hemispheric goodwill trade"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"JNIM","channel":"Coastal taxation + trafficking","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Pushing toward the Gulf of Guinea ports — the strategic prize is access to coastal trade, smuggling routes, and a port economy the Sahel lacks"}],"contested":{"summary":"The southward spread of Sahelian jihadism into the coastal states is either an inevitable spillover or a consequence of specific policy choices — the answer determines whether coastal governments bear responsibility.","claims":[{"claim":"Coastal-state attacks are spillover from the Sahel, driven by external actors.","who":"Benin and Togo governments.","evid":"JNIM's expansion into the northern parks is documented and externally directed. But researchers including those at ISS Africa point to internal drivers coastal governments are slower to discuss: land disputes in the north, the exclusion of Fulani pastoralists from park access after conservation enclosures, and thin state presence in the border districts. The spillover framing is accurate and incomplete.","status":"ACCURATE-BUT-PARTIAL"}]},"history":{"roots":"Benin and Togo entered independence on opposite tracks that converged in vulnerability. Dahomey (Benin) — heir to a sophisticated precolonial kingdom whose royal art France looted (and only began returning in 2021) — cycled through coups before Kérékou's Marxist state, then pioneered Africa's democratic renewal with the 1990 National Conference, the model the continent copied. Togo went the other way: Sylvanus Olympio, independence's champion who challenged the CFA franc's grip, was assassinated in 1963 in Africa's first post-colonial coup — by soldiers including Gnassingbé Eyadéma, whose family has now ruled for 58 years, the longest dynasty in Africa, constitutionally renovated as recently as 2024. Both states' norths — Muslim, pastoral, poorer, distant from the coastal capitals where colonial and post-colonial investment pooled — are the classic neglected periphery, and it is exactly there that JNIM now recruits.","timeline":[{"date":"1960","e":"Both independent from France."},{"date":"1967/2005","e":"Togo: Gnassingbé père seizes power; son inherits amid violence."},{"date":"2021","e":"First JNIM attacks cross into northern Benin."},{"date":"2022-24","e":"W-Pendjari park complex becomes the southern front; Benin's losses mount."},{"date":"2024","e":"Togo's constitutional change — Gnassingbé's indefinite super-premiership."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Deadliest attacks yet on Beninese positions; AES rupture blocks joint operations; Accra Initiative moribund."},{"date":"Jun 2026","e":"Benin and Niger move to restore relations severed after Niger's coup — the crude pipeline to Benin's coast is the material reason the rupture could not hold."}],"actors":[{"name":"JNIM","bio":"The Sahel franchise expanding to the coast — sanctuary in Burkina, targets in the parks, recruitment in the marginalized north."},{"name":"Benin (Talon)","bio":"The democracy under strain fighting the war hardest — hundreds of casualties, drone purchases, and a political system narrowing as it fights."},{"name":"Togo (Gnassingbé)","bio":"The dynasty — 58 years of family rule, now constitutionalized; the northern front is also a regime-security pretext."},{"name":"The AES juntas","bio":"The severed partners — Burkina's rupture with the coastal states is the seam the jihadists exploit."}],"bridge":"The jihadist push into the W-Pendjari parks finds the coastal states' soft seam: northern communities for whom the southern state is a tax collector and a checkpoint, not a school or a clinic — the same core-periphery extraction geometry as everywhere on this board, in miniature. Benin fights hardest and bleeds most while its democratic model narrows; Togo's dynasty converts the threat into a security rationale for its constitutional coup. And the AES rupture — the juntas' feud with the coast — has severed the joint operations the border requires, proving that the region's political fractures are themselves a jihadist force-multiplier. Olympio's fate remains the region's original lesson in what challenging the monetary order cost. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: JNIM operates in the northern parks while Benin restores relations with Niger because a crude pipeline requires it. The coastal states' colonial geography — wealth at the port, neglect in the north — is the insurgency's recruiting map.","sources":[{"work":"Le Togo sous Eyadéma","author":"Comi M. Toulabor","year":"1986","note":"Togolese scholarship on the dynasty's construction — the machinery still running in 2026."},{"work":"Soldier and State in Africa (Togo chapter: the 1963 coup)","author":"Claude E. Welch Jr. (ed.)","year":"1970","note":"The scholarly treatment of Africa's first post-colonial coup and the army politics behind Olympio's assassination — replacing an earlier under-verified citation."},{"work":"Democratic Experiments in Africa (Benin's National Conference model)","author":"Michael Bratton & Nicolas van de Walle","year":"1997","note":"Benin 1990 as the template of the continent's democratic renewal — the inheritance now under strain."},{"work":"Coastal West Africa spillover reporting","author":"Crisis Group / ACLED","year":"2022-26","note":"Primary tracking of JNIM's southward expansion into the parks complex."}]},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"The coast's soft seam: northern neglect meeting Sahel spillover, worsened by the AES rupture.","alt":"A spillover-primary reading holds the violence as externally imposed from Burkina rather than rooted in domestic north-south neglect — the states as victims, not co-authors.","why":"The recruitment pattern needs the domestic grievance; the sanctuary needs the external base. Both, MOD."},"refs":[{"t":"VIF Africa Now, 16-30 Jun 2026","u":"https://www.vifindia.org/2026/july/07/Africa-Now-16-30-june-2026"},{"t":"Crisis Group: coastal West Africa spillover","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/sahel-west-africa"},{"t":"ACLED: West Africa conflict data","u":"https://acleddata.com/region/africa/"},{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"}],"human":{"dead":1200,"disp":80000,"famine":false,"aid":"functioning","note":"JNIM's coastal push into the W-Pendjari park complex: hundreds of Beninese soldiers killed since 2021 plus civilian toll; displacement concentrated in the northern departments both governments long neglected.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"72 = active armed conflict, a recorded human toll, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Benin-Niger restoration recorded"}],"spill":[{"n":"Burkina / Niger","dir":"in","w":"JNIM expansion from the northern parks — the southward-spread thesis, externally directed but landing on coastal-state land disputes."},{"n":"Niger","dir":"both","w":"Benin-Niger rapprochement driven by the crude pipeline to Benin's coast — economic geography reasserting over bloc politics.","id":"niger"},{"n":"Nigeria","dir":"both","w":"The Gulf of Guinea littoral and cross-border trade; JNIM's coastal-expansion chapter.","id":"nigeria"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Coastal-state spillover, park-based insurgency","dir":"climbing","phase":"Emergence → escalation","up":["JNIM expansion from the northern parks","Land disputes and Fulani exclusion feed recruitment","Thin state presence in border districts","The southward-spread thesis realising"],"down":["The Benin-Niger pipeline rapprochement improving cooperation","Addressing park-enclosure grievances","Coastal-state security investment in the north","Regional intelligence sharing"],"fw":["conflict-curve","ethnic-security"]},"macrona":"DELIBERATELY EMPTY at theater level. This file covers a coastal-state frontier rather than either national economy. The relevant structural fact — wealth at the port, neglect in the north — is in the contested and assessment fields rather than in a growth rate.","inherit":{"name":"The CFA Franc","est":"1945","status":"IN FORCE · CONTESTED","what":"Created by the French provisional government in December 1945 as the <b>franc des Colonies Françaises d'Afrique</b> — the franc of the French Colonies of Africa. It was not a neutral monetary tool: it was an administrative instrument for managing colonial economies in the service of Paris, imposed by requiring taxes to be paid in it and by banning the cowrie and manilla currencies that preceded it. Colonial administrators used legal sanction and physical force where the ban failed.","now":"<b>It still circulates, used by 227 million people across fourteen countries as of 2026</b>, in two zones — West and Central African — with the French Treasury and, through the euro peg, the European Central Bank retaining roles in its governance. The Tax Justice Network's assessment is that it 'still functions according to the same principles and purpose established during the colonial period'. <b>Fiscal policy is national; monetary power is outsourced.</b> The 1994 devaluation, decided at a late-night meeting in Dakar, halved the savings of millions overnight at a rate nobody affected had voted on.","change":"France agreed in May 2020 to end its engagement in the West African zone and the reserve-deposit requirement, with the replacement currency — the <b>eco</b> — due to launch. <b>The launch has now been postponed five times; the current target is July 2027.</b> Both remain in the West African zone. Benin's pipeline revenue from Niger and Togo's port income both settle in a currency whose reserves and parity are set elsewhere."},"monitors":[{"t":"CDD West Africa","u":"https://www.cddwestafrica.org/"},{"t":"ACLED · conflict event data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}]},{"id":"ghana","cat":"military","region":"West / Central Africa","subregion":"Coastal Frontier","name":"Ghana — Frontier State + Galamsey Nexus","sub":"Logistical rear base · galamsey = #1 security threat · Bawku","domain":"Pre-Insurgency / Organized Crime","sev":"medium","status":"escalating","since":"2022","esc":58,"lat":9.5,"lon":-1,"trend":"up","w14":false,"esc_history":[58,58,58,58,58,58,58,58,58,58],"cas":"No large-scale attack yet; Bawku ethnic conflict deaths","disp":"Northern displacement risk","flag":"🇬🇭","note":"2026 — THE CASE BOTH SIDES CITE: Ghana defaulted on parts of its debt in 2022 and responded with PUBLIC SECTOR HIRING RESTRICTIONS, WAGE CONTROLS and spending reductions amid rising prices and public frustration — the austerity critics point to · it is simultaneously closing a $5.4B restructuring and holding its policy rate at 14%, which supporters cite as stabilisation working · the platform records both because the Ghana file is where the conditionality argument is actually decided, not asserted · JUL 2026 — THE $5.4B RESTRUCTURING NEARS CLOSE, RATE HELD AT 14%: Ghana and its creditors are working to FINALISE THE $5.4 BILLION DEBT RESTRUCTURING DEAL this week · the Bank of Ghana HELD ITS POLICY RATE AT 14%, citing global uncertainties and inflation risks · Ghanaian nationals are among those affected by South Africa's anti-migrant violence, and Ramaphosa's Accra visit was postponed — both sides citing SADC scheduling rather than the tensions · cross-reference the Debt board: this is the restructuring endgame the platform tracks as the test of whether the Common Framework can actually deliver · JUN-JUL 2026 — COCOA COORDINATION AND THE GALLOWS: Ghana and Côte d'Ivoire launched a joint cocoa pricing initiative — two states controlling ~60% of world production acting on price together rather than competing into the same buyers' hands · domestically, a DEATH PENALTY ABOLITION BILL is expected to be signed — Nkrumah's republic retiring an instrument it inherited from the colonial statute book · JUL 19 2026 — ACCOUNTABILITY AND ITS CRITICS: the Office of the Special Prosecutor has brought charges against former finance minister KEN OFORI-ATTA — the most senior prosecution of the debt-default era, testing whether Ghana's institutions can hold the architects of the 2022 collapse to account · COUNTER-VOICE FROM INSIDE: senior figures at IMANI Africa have warned that Ghana's newly created tribunal risks becoming an instrument rather than a remedy, and a Ghanaian think-tank fellow has questioned the timing — logged because the platform's benchmark set commits to African-led analysis, including when it cuts against the government's framing · the ruling party is also reported to be facing significant internal unrest · NEW · Ghana's own ECOWAS Parliament submission: militants use N. Ghana for LOGISTICAL + MEDICAL support · galamsey (illegal gold mining) named #1 national security threat (KAIPTC) — organized crime/corruption nexus · Bawku Mamprusi-Kusasi conflict · Zongo-community radicalization risk · no confirmed large-scale attack inside Ghana yet","leverage":[{"actor":"JNIM/ISGS","asset":"Rear-base logistics + medical staging in N. Ghana; galamsey recruitment pool","str":"md"},{"actor":"Ghanaian state","asset":"NCCRM early-warning (Act 1070); northern DSF redeployment; democratic legitimacy","str":"md"},{"actor":"Galamsey networks","asset":"Financiers + political-economic actors; environmental destruction; organized-crime ecosystem","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Ghana ECOWAS Parliament submission","detail":"Warned militants use N. Ghana for logistical/medical support; risk of escalation within borders"},{"src":"NCCRM (Act 1070, 2021)","detail":"National Centre for Coordination of Early Warning; monitors crime, governance, terrorism; monthly reports to ECOWAS"},{"src":"NAIMOS anti-galamsey","detail":"3 monitoring bases in Western Region (Ellembelle, Amenfi East, Ahanta West); galamsey ravaged 11 of 14 districts"}],"mil":"Ghana has so far avoided a large-scale jihadist attack, but its own ECOWAS Parliament submission confirms Islamic militant groups use parts of northern Ghana for logistical and medical support connected to Sahel insurgencies. Suspected fighters participate in artisanal gold mining in the Ghana-Burkina border forests. The Bawku conflict (Mamprusi-Kusasi ethnic violence) and farmer-herder tensions provide local fracture points.","pol":"KAIPTC and Ghanaian security institutions name galamsey (illegal gold mining) the country's top emerging national-security threat — a highly organized, mechanized ecosystem of financiers, equipment suppliers, and political-economic actors linked to organized crime, environmental destruction, and potential extremist recruitment. Ghana established the NCCRM early-warning centre (Act 1070) and relaunched an anti-galamsey campaign with the NAIMOS bases. Zongo (Hausa-trader) communities are flagged as radicalization risks.","eco":"Galamsey bypasses formal taxation, pollutes major rivers (Pra, Birim, Ankobra, Offin), disrupts cocoa production, and creates an unemployed youth pool vulnerable to recruitment — the crime-corruption-security nexus in one.","assess":"Ghana enters at 58 as a frontier state — not yet in open conflict but structurally exposed. The distinctive thread is the fusion you asked to track: galamsey is simultaneously the #1 crime/corruption problem and a potential security on-ramp, linking illegal mining, organized crime, and jihadist recruitment. Ghana's democratic institutions and early-warning architecture (NCCRM) are its resilience; its northern marginalization and galamsey grievances are the vulnerabilities.","econlev":[{"holder":"United States","channel":"Portfolio securities","exp":"LOW","detail":"$2.0B US securities (2024 TIC), debt-heavy — small against the galamsey shadow economy"}],"tclass":"watch","tier":"ELEVATED","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"No significant narrative-war dimension identified in this theater at present. The main claims are broadly consistent with the evidence, or the disputes are policy disagreements rather than contested facts. This is stated affirmatively rather than left blank — the absence of an information operation is itself a finding.","claims":[]},"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸🇬🇧 West / IMF","side":"creditor","flow":"$3B IMF program conditionality driving the fiscal recovery; Western mining and cocoa buyers","tier":"documented","for":"Debt-service compliance and a stable ECOWAS anchor against the Sahel juntas"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"creditor + buyer","flow":"Major bilateral creditor in the debt restructuring; gold and bauxite offtake; infrastructure loans","tier":"documented","for":"Gold, bauxite, and a coastal foothold in a stable West African state"},{"actor":"🇧🇫 Sahel juntas (AES)","side":"destabilizing neighbor","flow":"Spillover pressure — jihadist violence and smuggling creeping south into the coastal frontier","tier":"documented","for":"N/A — Ghana is the buffer the AES instability threatens"}],"ext_flag":true,"history":{"roots":"Ghana is Pan-Africanism's first proof and first warning. Nkrumah made the Gold Coast's 1957 independence explicitly continental — 'the independence of Ghana is meaningless unless it is linked up with the total liberation of Africa' — hosted the All-African People's Conference, wrote the book that named neo-colonialism, and pushed industrialization (Akosombo, the Volta scheme) to escape the cocoa-and-gold export trap Britain built. The 1966 coup that removed him — welcomed and, the declassified record shows, encouraged by Washington — was the warning: the trap defends itself. Rawlings' revolutions and the Fourth Republic built the region's sturdiest democracy atop the same export structure, and the 2022 default showed the structure's teeth again: a middle-income star brought down by the oldest arrangement in the book — borrowing against commodities priced elsewhere.","timeline":[{"date":"1957","e":"First sub-Saharan independence; Nkrumah's Pan-African beacon."},{"date":"1966-81","e":"Coup era; Rawlings' revolutions."},{"date":"1992","e":"Fourth Republic — the durable two-party democracy begins."},{"date":"2022","e":"Debt default — $30B restructuring, IMF program."},{"date":"2024","e":"Mahama returns to power; ECOWAS loses the AES states."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"IMF-tracked recovery; gold windfall + galamsey crisis; northern-border jihadist watch."},{"date":"May 2026","e":"Final ECF review completed; Ghana requests a 36-month Policy Coordination Instrument — surveillance continues after the money stops."},{"date":"Jun-Jul 2026","e":"Joint cocoa pricing initiative with Côte d'Ivoire; death penalty abolition bill expected to be signed."}],"actors":[{"name":"John Mahama","bio":"The returned president — managing IMF discipline, gold-sector cleanup, and the democratic brand West Africa needs intact."},{"name":"The IMF / creditors","bio":"The $3B program's authors — the conditionality shaping every budget line."},{"name":"Gold (formal + galamsey)","bio":"Africa's top producer — record prices fund the recovery while illegal mining poisons rivers and leaks billions."},{"name":"The Sahel spillover","bio":"The northern threat probing Bawku's local conflict — the seam between Ghana's stability and the region's fire."}],"bridge":"Ghana's present is Nkrumah's diagnosis running on schedule: the IMF program dictates the budget of gold's African leader while record bullion prices mostly reward refiners and creditors abroad; galamsey poisons the rivers because the formal economy can't absorb the miners; and the GoldBod reforms — state purchasing, refining ambitions — are the first structural push toward his answer since Akosombo. Meanwhile the AES juntas quote Nkrumah against the very ECOWAS Ghana anchors: the contest over his inheritance — sovereignty by institution-building or by rupture — is the region's live ideological war, and Ghana's stability is its stake. · AND NOW, JUN-JUL 2026: Joint cocoa pricing initiative with Côte d'Ivoire; death penalty abolition bill expected to be signed.","sources":[{"work":"Neo-Colonialism: The Last Stage of Imperialism","author":"Kwame Nkrumah","year":"1965","note":"The primary text — written from Accra, naming the system that would soon remove its author."},{"work":"Africa Must Unite","author":"Kwame Nkrumah","year":"1963","note":"The continental program Ghana's independence was declared to serve."},{"work":"The Political and Social Thought of Kwame Nkrumah","author":"Ama Biney","year":"2011","note":"The scholarly recovery of Nkrumah beyond both hagiography and Cold War caricature."},{"work":"Declassified US records on the 1966 coup","author":"US State Dept/CIA (FRUS series)","year":"released 1999-","note":"Primary documentation of Washington's foreknowledge and satisfaction — the warning made explicit."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"Pan-Africanism's first proof testing whether democratic stability survives debt trauma and encirclement.","alt":"A commodity-cycle reading holds Ghana's crises as ordinary boom-bust economics, not the Nkrumah-diagnosis structural trap the platform frames.","why":"The 2022 default fits the structural export-dependency pattern too well to read as mere cycle. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"Al Jazeera · How World Bank and IMF loans are reshaping policymaking in Africa (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/7/10/how-world-bank-and-imf-loans-are-reshaping-policymaking-in-africa"},{"t":"Africa Intelligence Brief (24 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.riotimesonline.com/africa-intelligence-brief-friday-july-24-2026/"},{"t":"VIF Africa Now: cocoa initiative","u":"https://www.vifindia.org/2026/july/07/Africa-Now-16-30-june-2026"},{"t":"Africa Intelligence Brief, 18 Jul 2026","u":"https://www.riotimesonline.com/africa-intelligence-brief-saturday-july-18-2026/"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"58 = active armed conflict, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Cocoa pricing initiative; PCI transition; timeline updated"}],"spill":[{"n":"Côte d’Ivoire","id":"cote-divoire","dir":"both","w":"The coordinated cocoa pricing initiative — West Africa's clearest attempt at producer price power, now facing an election year."},{"n":"Burkina Faso","id":"burkina","dir":"in","w":"The northern border with the Sahel insurgency — the coastal-state expansion frontier."},{"n":"South Africa","id":"south-africa","dir":"in","w":"Ghanaian nationals affected by the anti-migrant violence; Ramaphosa's Accra visit postponed."}],"macro":{"growth":"Stabilising post-default","infl":"Policy rate held at 14%","src":"Bank of Ghana, July 2026","note":"The $5.4B restructuring is closing and the central bank is holding rates against global uncertainty. Ghana is the case both sides of the conditionality argument cite: supporters point to stabilisation, critics to the public-sector hiring freeze and wage controls imposed after the 2022 default."},"inherit":{"name":"The Cocoa Marketing Board","est":"1947","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Established by the colonial administration as the <b>Gold Coast Cocoa Marketing Board</b>, it held a monopoly on buying farmers' cocoa and selling it abroad, paying producers a fixed price well below the world price and retaining the surplus. The stated purpose was price stabilisation; the effect was a transfer from African farmers to colonial revenue — and it helped provoke the 1948 boycott and riots that accelerated independence.","now":"<b>COCOBOD still holds the monopoly and still sets the farmgate price.</b> The rationale is now national development and farmer protection rather than colonial extraction, and the surplus funds Ghanaian public spending rather than London's — a real difference. But the structure is identical: farmers cannot sell to whom they choose at the price the market offers. When Ghana defaulted in 2022, cocoa revenue became collateral in the restructuring, so the same institution that once transferred value to a colonial treasury now channels it toward external creditors.","change":"Ghana and Côte d'Ivoire have coordinated pricing through the Living Income Differential — the clearest African attempt at producer price power, and a direct inversion of what the boards were built for. <b>The $5.4B debt restructuring is closing, and the policy rate is held at 14%.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"allAfrica · BoG holds rate at 14% (Jul 2026)","u":"https://allafrica.com/"},{"t":"IMANI Africa","u":"https://imaniafrica.org/"}]},{"id":"cote-divoire","cat":"political","region":"West / Central Africa","subregion":"Coastal Frontier","name":"Côte d'Ivoire — Electoral Crisis + Coup Disinformation","sub":"Ouattara 4th term · opposition barred · coordinated coup-rumor campaigns · JNIM northern pressure","domain":"Electoral Crisis / Information Warfare","sev":"medium","status":"volatile","since":"2020","esc":62,"lat":8.5,"lon":-5.5,"trend":"up","w14":false,"esc_history":[54,54,54,54,54,54,54,62,62,62],"cas":"No current conflict deaths; 2010-11 post-election civil war killed ~3,000 — the precedent driving present fear","disp":"Northern border communities under JNIM pressure; no mass displacement currently","flag":"🇨🇮","note":"2026 — CITED AS A COUNTER-CASE: Brookings lists Côte d'Ivoire alongside Kenya and Zambia among Africa's 'promising cases', for maintaining STABLE INFRASTRUCTURE-LED GROWTH · the platform notes it without endorsing it — the same institutions producing the debt-distress ratings are producing the success labels, and the cocoa pricing cartel's durability through an election year remains the real test · JUL 2026 — THE COCOA-PRICING QUESTION MEETS AN ELECTION YEAR: the Ghana-Côte d'Ivoire coordinated pricing initiative continues as both states manage debt and rate decisions; Côte d'Ivoire heads toward its own electoral cycle with the same incumbency-stability question the region faces · the platform holds the cocoa cartel as the clearest West African attempt at producer price power — its durability through an election year is the test · JUN 2026 — THE COCOA CARTEL MOVE: Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana launched a COORDINATED COCOA PRICING INITIATIVE to stabilise global markets — the two states produce roughly 60% of the world's cocoa and have spent decades as price-takers in a market whose value is captured downstream in Europe. Coordinating price is the single most consequential leverage instrument available to them, and the closest thing West Africa has to an OPEC play · REFRAMED (Wk15) — the theater's live risk is POLITICAL + INFORMATIONAL, not (yet) kinetic · COUP DISINFORMATION: recurring coordinated campaigns (May 2025, again ~Apr 2026) falsely claim a military takeover — same fabricated beats each time (army chief Lassina Doumbia 'assassinated,' Ouattara 'missing/dead,' 'internet cut') · Ivorian news agency: claims are 'completely unfounded... the result of a deliberate and coordinated disinformation campaign' · Ouattara chaired a routine cabinet meeting + public ceremony during the alleged 'coup'; NO credible Ivorian outlet (state or private) reported the violence; most viral posts written in ENGLISH in a Francophone country — a tell · fact-checkers flagged the videos as misleading; ~9,700 Facebook users discussed the fake coup in one day · analysts describe 'a campaign to promote military coups across West Africa' · REAL UNDERLYING TENSION: Ouattara nominated by RHDP for a 4TH TERM (widely resented); opposition leader Tidjane Thiam (ex-Credit Suisse CEO) STRUCK from the electoral roll (Apr 2025) over a French-nationality technicality — he's appealing, calls it political; Gbagbo + others barred over past convictions · 2010 election spiraled into civil war (~3,000 dead) — that memory drives the fear · widespread sympathy for the AES juntas (Mali/Burkina), who are anti-France unlike the Paris-aligned Ouattara · protesters call the govt a puppet of France + the US; criticism of expanding AFRICOM presence · JNIM pressure on the northern border continues (the original theater rationale)","leverage":[{"actor":"Ouattara / RHDP","asset":"Incumbency + control of the courts/electoral roll (used to bar Thiam and Gbagbo); French + US backing; strong economic record","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Opposition (Thiam/PDCI, Gbagbo)","asset":"Popular legitimacy + street mobilization; the 4th-term grievance; but excluded from the ballot","str":"md"},{"actor":"Information operators (unattributed)","asset":"Coordinated coup-rumor campaigns exploiting the AES-sympathy fault line — manufacturing the appearance of rupture in the region's key French-aligned economy","str":"md"},{"actor":"France / US","asset":"Ouattara's patrons; AFRICOM presence expanding — but that presence is itself now the grievance the campaigns exploit","str":"md"},{"actor":"JNIM","asset":"Northern-border pressure; would benefit from an Ivorian political rupture","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Ivorian news agency (AIP)","detail":"Coup claims on X are 'completely unfounded... the result of a deliberate and coordinated disinformation campaign'"},{"src":"Al Jazeera / News24 / fact-checkers","detail":"Verified: Ouattara chaired a cabinet meeting during the alleged coup; army chief Doumbia not assassinated; viral videos flagged as misleading; no credible Ivorian outlet reported violence"},{"src":"Ivorian courts (Apr 2025)","detail":"Struck opposition leader Tidjane Thiam from the electoral roll over a French-nationality technicality; Gbagbo and others barred over past convictions"}],"mil":"Not a kinetic theater — and the central analytical point is that claims to the contrary are fabricated. Recurring coordinated campaigns (May 2025, again around April 2026) have falsely announced a military coup, always with the same manufactured beats: army chief of staff Lassina Doumbia assassinated, President Ouattara missing or dead, internet access severed. Each time, the claims collapsed on contact with evidence — Ouattara chaired cabinet meetings during the alleged takeovers, no credible Ivorian outlet reported the violence, and most viral posts were written in English in a Francophone country. The real military dimension is JNIM's continuing pressure on the northern border as it pushes toward the Gulf of Guinea.","pol":"The genuine crisis is electoral. Ouattara was nominated by his RHDP party for a fourth term that many Ivorians resent, while the courts struck opposition leader Tidjane Thiam from the electoral roll over a French-nationality technicality (he is appealing and calls it political) and barred Gbagbo and others over past convictions. The memory of 2010 — when a disputed election spiraled into a civil war that killed roughly 3,000 — is what gives the fear its charge. Analysts note widespread Ivorian sympathy for the young military leaders who seized power in Mali and Burkina Faso, who are hostile to France, unlike the Paris-aligned Ouattara. Protesters call the government a puppet of France and the United States, with growing criticism of the expanding AFRICOM presence.","eco":"Côte d'Ivoire is the world's largest cocoa producer and West Africa's economic hub — Ouattara is genuinely credited with a decade and a half of rapid growth and relative political peace, which is the strongest argument for the status quo and the reason a rupture here would transmit far beyond its borders.","assess":"Côte d'Ivoire is re-scored to 62 (ELEVATED, watch-class) and reframed: its live risk is political and informational rather than kinetic. The coup that keeps being announced is not happening — Ivorian authorities call it a deliberate, coordinated disinformation campaign, and every verification test confirms that. But the campaigns are themselves the signal worth tracking: they are engineered to exploit a real fault line — resentment of a fourth term, an opposition excluded by the courts, and open sympathy for the anti-France juntas next door — in the region's most important French-aligned economy. Analysts describe a broader effort to promote military coups across West Africa. This is the AES-vs-ECOWAS contest fought through information rather than arms, which is why the theater now sits in that bloc. Attribution remains unresolved and is labeled as such. Watch the Thiam appeal, whether Ouattara formally stands, the election timetable, and whether the disinformation campaigns escalate around key dates.","econlev":[{"holder":"Côte d'Ivoire","channel":"Cocoa","exp":"HIGH","detail":"World's largest cocoa producer — the regional economic hub; political rupture here would transmit directly into global cocoa markets and West African stability"},{"holder":"France / West","channel":"Patronage + AFRICOM","exp":"MED","detail":"Ouattara's Paris alignment and the expanding US military presence are the exact grievances the coup-disinformation campaigns weaponize"}],"tclass":"watch","tier":"ELEVATED","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Political","external":[{"actor":"🇫🇷 France","side":"Ouattara","flow":"Former colonial power; close ties to Ouattara; backed his forces in the 2011 civil war","tier":"documented","for":"Retain its last major West African anchor after expulsion from the Sahel"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US (AFRICOM)","side":"Ouattara","flow":"Expanding regional military presence — increasingly the target of protest criticism","tier":"documented","for":"Fill the counter-terror vacuum left by France's Sahel expulsion; hold the coastal line"},{"actor":"❓ Unattributed info-ops","side":"anti-Ouattara / pro-coup narrative","flow":"Coordinated multi-platform campaigns fabricating a military takeover; analysts describe a broader push promoting coups across West Africa","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Manufacture the appearance of AES-style rupture in the region's key French-aligned state [ATTRIBUTION UNRESOLVED]"},{"actor":"🏴 JNIM","side":"insurgents","flow":"Northern-border pressure from the Sahel; would exploit any political rupture","tier":"documented","for":"Coastal expansion toward Gulf of Guinea ports"}],"contested":{"summary":"The coup that keeps being announced and keeps not happening. The campaign is the event.","claims":[{"claim":"Coordinated posts claim a military coup has taken place; the army chief is assassinated; Ouattara is missing or dead.","who":"Whoever benefits from making AES-style rupture look inevitable in the region's key French-aligned economy. Attribution unresolved.","evid":"DISPROVEN. Ouattara chaired a routine cabinet meeting during the alleged coup. Doumbia was not assassinated. No credible Ivorian outlet — state or private — reported the violence. Most viral posts were in ENGLISH in a Francophone country: the audience was never Ivorian.","status":"DISPROVEN"},{"claim":"The government frames all opposition grievance as foreign-driven disinformation.","who":"Ouattara — conflating fabricated coups with legitimate protest delegitimizes both.","evid":"The disinformation is real AND the grievances are real: Thiam was struck from the ballot on a citizenship technicality, Gbagbo is barred, and the fourth term is widely resented. Both things are true at once.","status":"CONTESTED — don't let the fake coup bury the real crisis"}]},"history":{"roots":"Côte d'Ivoire was Françafrique's showcase — and the showcase's price is the present fragility. Houphouët-Boigny ran the model partnership: cocoa's world leadership, French capital and troops welcome, the CFA franc defended — he coined 'Françafrique' approvingly — and prosperity real enough to draw millions of workers from the Sahel. The model's flaw was baked in: growth built on migrant labor with citizenship left ambiguous. When cocoa prices fell and Houphouët died, his heirs weaponized the ambiguity as 'Ivoirité,' stripping northerners and migrants of belonging — Mike McGovern's work traces how an economic identity crisis was converted into an ethnic-legal one — and the 2002-2011 wars followed, ending with France's Licorne force deciding the endgame in Ouattara's favor. Recovery since has been real and narrow: strong growth, aging leadership, opposition exclusion, and the north — the war's old grievance map — now the jihadist probe line.","timeline":[{"date":"1960","e":"Independence; Houphouët-Boigny's 33-year Franco-Ivorian miracle."},{"date":"2002-11","e":"Civil wars over 'Ivoirité'; Ouattara wins with French backing."},{"date":"2020","e":"Ouattara's contested third term — protests and deaths."},{"date":"2025","e":"Fourth term amid opposition exclusion; fabricated-coup campaigns intensify."},{"date":"2026","e":"Growth continues; northern probes continue; the disinformation war is the active front."},{"date":"Jun 2026","e":"Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana launch a coordinated COCOA PRICING INITIATIVE — two states holding ~60% of world production acting on price together."}],"actors":[{"name":"Alassane Ouattara","bio":"The octogenarian president-economist — stability's guarantor and its single point of failure; the succession is the real question."},{"name":"The disinformation networks","bio":"Russia/AES-aligned campaigns manufacturing coups in English for a Francophone country — the tell of an external audience."},{"name":"France","bio":"The patron whose base drew down but whose commercial and security web remains — the relationship the info-ops attack."},{"name":"Cocoa + the economy","bio":"The world's cocoa leader — the prosperity that is both the stability's source and the contrast the juntas' narrative must explain away."}],"bridge":"The disinformation campaigns manufacturing coups in Abidjan are aimed at exactly the showcase's cracks: a fourth-term president, excluded rivals, northern grievance, and the standing question of French tutelage — every fabricated story exploits a real seam. Côte d'Ivoire is the coast's ideological prize: if the AES model (rupture, Russia, sovereignty rhetoric) can be exported anywhere rich, it is here, which is why the info-ops run in English for external amplification while JNIM probes the parks. The succession, when it comes, is the hinge on which the Gulf of Guinea's alignment may turn. · AND NOW, JUN 2026: Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana launch a coordinated COCOA PRICING INITIATIVE — two states holding ~60% of world production acting on price together.","sources":[{"work":"Making War in Côte d'Ivoire","author":"Mike McGovern","year":"2011","note":"How 'Ivoirité' converted economic crisis into identity war — the standard scholarly account."},{"work":"Houphouët-Boigny et la crise ivoirienne","author":"Frédéric Grah Mel (Ivorian biographer)","year":"2003-2010","note":"The Ivorian-authored assessment of the model partnership and its unraveling."},{"work":"The CFA Franc Zone: Common Currency, Uncommon Challenges","author":"Ali Zafar / and Ndongo Samba Sylla & Fanny Pigeaud, 'Africa's Last Colonial Currency'","year":"2021","note":"The monetary architecture at the center of the sovereignty contest — Sylla's is the leading African critique."},{"work":"Ivorian government disinformation attributions","author":"CNSP/ANSSI-CI statements & DFRLab analyses","year":"2025-26","note":"Primary attribution record of the fabricated-coup campaigns."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"The Francophone anchor rebuilt after civil war, now the prime target of coup-disinformation.","alt":"An Ivoirite-unresolved reading holds the citizenship/identity wound as the primary latent fault, deeper than the info-ops the platform foregrounds.","why":"Both real; the info-ops exploit the identity seam. The platform holds the disinformation as the active front over the latent structure. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"Brookings · Africa's top priorities for the 2026 Spring Meetings","u":"https://www.brookings.edu/articles/africas-top-priorities-for-the-2026-world-bank-and-imf-spring-meetings/"},{"t":"VIF Africa Now: Ghana-Côte d'Ivoire cocoa initiative","u":"https://www.vifindia.org/2026/july/07/Africa-Now-16-30-june-2026"},{"t":"Crisis Group: Côte d'Ivoire","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/sahel-west-africa/cote-divoire"},{"t":"ACLED West Africa","u":"https://acleddata.com/region/africa/"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"62 = institutional or electoral instability, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Cocoa pricing initiative recorded"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Electoral cycle beside a jihadist frontier","dir":"stalemate","phase":"Stable but exposed","up":["JNIM pressure from the Burkina border","Electoral-cycle tensions reactivating past divisions","Cocoa price shocks straining the rural economy","Coastal-state spillover accelerating"],"down":["The Ghana-Côte d'Ivoire cocoa pricing cartel holding","Northern security investment","A credible and accepted electoral process","Regional intelligence cooperation"],"fw":["conflict-curve","resource-conflict"]},"spill":[{"n":"Ghana","id":"ghana","dir":"both","w":"The cocoa pricing cartel — the two producers coordinating against the buyers, which is the platform's clearest case of commodity leverage exercised jointly."},{"n":"Burkina Faso","id":"burkina","dir":"in","w":"JNIM pressure on the northern border; the coastal expansion the Sahel war is pushing south."},{"n":"Mali","id":"mali","dir":"both","w":"A corridor state for Malian fuel imports — leverage over a landlocked neighbour under blockade."}],"macro":{"growth":"Forecast lowered (World Bank, Jun 2026)","infl":"Contained","src":"World Bank Global Economic Prospects, June 2026","note":"Cited among Africa's steadier performers for infrastructure-led growth, then revised down in the June outlook. Non-resource-rich African economies as a group are slowing from 6.4% in 2025 to 5.7% in 2026 — still outpacing commodity exporters, which is the inversion of the usual assumption that resource wealth drives African growth."},"inherit":{"name":"The CFA Franc","est":"1945","status":"IN FORCE · CONTESTED","what":"Created by the French provisional government in December 1945 as the <b>franc des Colonies Françaises d'Afrique</b> — the franc of the French Colonies of Africa. It was not a neutral monetary tool: it was an administrative instrument for managing colonial economies in the service of Paris, imposed by requiring taxes to be paid in it and by banning the cowrie and manilla currencies that preceded it. Colonial administrators used legal sanction and physical force where the ban failed.","now":"<b>It still circulates, used by 227 million people across fourteen countries as of 2026</b>, in two zones — West and Central African — with the French Treasury and, through the euro peg, the European Central Bank retaining roles in its governance. The Tax Justice Network's assessment is that it 'still functions according to the same principles and purpose established during the colonial period'. <b>Fiscal policy is national; monetary power is outsourced.</b> The 1994 devaluation, decided at a late-night meeting in Dakar, halved the savings of millions overnight at a rate nobody affected had voted on.","change":"France agreed in May 2020 to end its engagement in the West African zone and the reserve-deposit requirement, with the replacement currency — the <b>eco</b> — due to launch. <b>The launch has now been postponed five times; the current target is July 2027.</b> Côte d'Ivoire remains among the zone's strongest advocates, which is itself the finding: the arrangement has beneficiaries inside Africa as well as outside it, and any account that treats it as purely imposed misses who defends it and why."},"monitors":[{"t":"allAfrica · West Africa monitoring (Jul 2026)","u":"https://allafrica.com/"},{"t":"DFRLab: West Africa influence operations","u":"https://dfrlab.org/"}]},{"id":"nigeria","cat":"military","region":"West / Central Africa","subregion":"Central Africa","name":"Nigeria — ISWAP + Ansaru + Bandits + IPOB","sub":"JNIM first Nigeria attacks · Ansaru re-emergence · Lakurawa","domain":"Multi-Threat Insurgency","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"2009","esc":70,"lat":11.5,"lon":9.5,"trend":"up","w14":false,"esc_history":[68,68,68,68,68,68,68,68,70,70],"cas":"Tumfa airstrike: Amnesty alleges 100+ civilians; Niger/Kwara fatalities doubled","disp":"2.2M+ Lake Chad Basin","flag":"🇳🇬","note":"JUL 2026 — 300+ KILLED IN ONE ZAMFARA OPERATION: Nigerian troops killed more than 300 suspected kidnapping and cattle-rustling gang members in an operation at GUMMI, Zamfara State · the scale is the point — a single operation with a body count exceeding most recorded jihadist attacks, against the front the 'genocide' framing does not describe · JUL 2026 — SIMULTANEOUS ATTACKS ON MAIDUGURI, AND A BROKEN REGIONAL MECHANISM: ACLED records MAIDUGURI facing SIMULTANEOUS ATTACKS from BOKO HARAM AND ISWAP — two rival factions striking the same city, which is a coordination question the four-front framing has to answer · jihadist violence escalated across Niger, Burkina Faso and Nigeria in the same period · the MULTINATIONAL JOINT TASK FORCE has been weakened by Chad and Niger's withdrawals, removing the mechanism that coordinated Lake Chad basin operations — Nigerian counter-insurgency in Borno may now simply displace militants across the border rather than defeat them · JUL 19 2026 — ECOWAS SIGNS INTO THE NIGERIA-MOROCCO ATLANTIC GAS PIPELINE: ECOWAS member states signed an intergovernmental agreement at a Freetown summit to join the Nigeria-Morocco Atlantic African Gas Pipeline project — a ~5,600km route carrying Nigerian gas up the West African coast toward Morocco and onward to Europe (reported via wire aggregation; the platform logs it as a signed framework, not a financed build) · LEVERAGE READ: if realised it would make Nigerian gas a European supply alternative to Russian and Gulf sources, and give thirteen coastal states transit standing they have never had — but pipeline agreements in this region have a long history of signature without steel · separately, the Federal Government announced a plan to DIGITIZE THE MINING SECTOR — formalisation as the counter to the artisanal-and-smuggled economy the platform tracks in the northwest · US MILITARY INTERVENTION + 'CHRISTIAN GENOCIDE' FRAMING (major, was untracked) · Trump designated Nigeria a 'Country of Particular Concern' (Oct 31 2025) over alleged persecution of Christians · CHRISTMAS DAY 2025: US airstrikes on ISWAP/ISIS in Sokoto (Trump-ordered, Nigeria-coordinated) · MAY 16 2026: joint US-Nigeria operation launched — SEAL raids + airstrikes killed an ISIS leader (al-Minuki) + ~175 militants by mid-May; further strikes in Metele/Arege (Borno) Jun 2026 · Trump: further strikes 'if they continue to kill Christians'; claims US action 'largely ended the slaughter' · CONTESTED FRAMING: Nigeria's govt (FM Tuggar), Crisis Group + ACLED reject the 'Christian genocide' label — ACLED found religiously-targeted killings = ~5% of civilian-targeting events; they call it largely resource/criminal/banditry violence miscast as religious war · analysts (Bangura) argue the real US aim is leverage over Nigerian critical-minerals access · IPOB (Biafra separatists) said they'd 'fully support' a Trump intervention — a sovereignty/secession wrinkle · UPDATED · JNIM claimed its FIRST Nigeria attacks (killed soldiers, Kwara/Niger border) · Ansaru re-emergence (AQIM/JNIM-allied), shifted to Kwara/Niger States · Lakurawa (ISGS-linked) in Kebbi/Sokoto, cooperating w/ Boko Haram Sadiku faction · ISWAP officer-killings (5 in 5 months) · banditry = most armed violence in NW","leverage":[{"actor":"ISWAP / IS","asset":"Drones; officer-targeting; Niger-Nigeria bridge linking ISSP+ISWAP","str":"hi"},{"actor":"JNIM + Ansaru","asset":"First Nigeria attacks; Kwara/Niger forest bases near Benin border","str":"md"},{"actor":"Bandits (NW)","asset":"Most armed violence in NW; kidnap-for-ransom economy","str":"md"},{"actor":"Nigerian state","asset":"Dangote refinery, CBN FX reform, state-policing framework","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Nigerian Armed Forces","detail":"Multi-front operations; to embed journalists to improve security reporting; requested French support"},{"src":"US/France","detail":"ISR + Lakurawa precision airstrikes; deepening security footprint"},{"src":"CBN / NUPRC","detail":"FX reform + petroleum licensing — economic stability layer"}],"mil":"Nigeria's threat picture widened materially: JNIM claimed its first-ever attacks in Nigeria (killing soldiers in the Kwara/Niger border forests), while Ansaru — the AQIM/JNIM-allied group that split from Boko Haram in 2012 — re-emerged, shifting operations from Kaduna to Kwara and Niger States near the Benin border. ISGS-linked 'Lakurawa' cells in Kebbi/Sokoto cooperate with Boko Haram's Sadiku faction. Niger and Kwara State fatalities doubled. ISWAP killed five high-level officers in five months.","pol":"The structural risk is convergence: IS is attempting to bridge ISSP (Sahel) and ISWAP (Lake Chad) across the Niger-Nigeria border, which would connect two of Africa's most powerful jihadi networks. Organized criminal 'bandits' remain the largest source of armed violence in the northwest. The decentralized state-policing plan advances; Nigeria deepened US/French security ties.","eco":"Dangote refinery, CBN FX reforms, and NUPRC licensing (from the uploaded stability layer) are the resilience variables; easing oil prices relieve fuel-cost pressure.","assess":"Nigeria rises to 68. It is the hinge where the Sahel and Lake Chad theaters are fusing: JNIM's first Nigeria strikes, Ansaru's revival, and the Lakurawa-Boko Haram cooperation all point to a merging jihadi geography across the Niger-Nigeria border. The four-front problem (ISWAP, Sahel-linked groups, bandits, IPOB/ESN) makes Nigeria the most multi-dimensional theater in West Africa.","econlev":[{"holder":"United States","channel":"Portfolio securities","exp":"MED","detail":"$8.0B US securities (2025 TIC; 3→8 over decade); mostly long-term debt, equity <$0.5B"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US (intervention)","side":"counter-ISIS / pressure","flow":"Designated Nigeria a CPC (Oct 2025); Christmas-2025 Sokoto airstrikes; joint May-2026 operation (SEAL raids + airstrikes, killed ISIS leader al-Minuki + ~175); threatens more strikes over 'Christian genocide' — a framing Abuja + ACLED + Crisis Group dispute","tier":"documented","for":"Counter-ISIS optics + leverage over Nigerian critical minerals; the genocide narrative is the lever, resource access the suspected aim"},{"actor":"🇹🇷 Turkey","side":"state","flow":"TB2s, Songar tactical drones, ANKA-S co-production talks; tied to Dec strike that killed 85+ civilians","tier":"documented","for":"Arms market; training ties since 2018"},{"actor":"🇦🇪 UAE","side":"state","flow":"Armoured vehicles used vs Boko Haram","tier":"documented","for":"Counter-terror partnership"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"state","flow":"72 drones (Wing Loong, CH-series); no human-rights conditionality","tier":"documented","for":"Arms market; strategic access"},{"actor":"🇮🇷 Iran","side":"soft-power","flow":"Al-Mustafa University network + cultural centres (Abuja) — ideological screening/recruitment nodes","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Influence; intelligence deniability"}],"ext_flag":true,"tclass":"conflict","tier":"SEVERE","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"The killings are real. Whether they are a 'Christian genocide' — and who benefits from saying so — is the contest.","claims":[{"claim":"Nigeria is committing (or tolerating) a 'Christian genocide' — the basis for US strikes and CPC designation.","who":"The Trump administration + US conservative circles — the framing justifies intervention and pressure on Abuja; Nigeria's govt denies it entirely.","evid":"ACLED (2020-22) found religiously-targeted killing of Christians = ~5% of civilian-targeting events; Crisis Group + Nigeria's FM call the violence largely resource/criminal/banditry-driven, hitting Muslims and Christians alike. Intersociety's much higher Christian-death figures are assessed by the BBC as unverified. Real mass killing is documented; the 'genocide' characterization is disputed.","status":"CONTESTED — killings documented, genocide framing disputed"},{"claim":"The US intervention is a humanitarian action to protect Christians.","who":"Trump — 'we largely ended the slaughter'; critics see a resource-leverage play.","evid":"Analysts (Yusuf Bangura et al.) argue the aim is to pressure Nigeria into opening critical-minerals supply chains to US firms. Both framings coexist; only the humanitarian one is offered publicly.","status":"CONTESTED — humanitarian frame vs resource-leverage aim"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Crude Oil","stage":"mine","actor":"Niger Delta — Bonny Light, Forcados, Escravos","detail":"Africa's largest oil producer, but output fell from ~2.1M bpd (2018) to ~1.3M bpd — bled by pipeline vandalism and industrial-scale THEFT (NNPC once said it was losing 'nearly all' output at the Bonny hub). Bonny Light (light, sweet, low-sulfur) traded above $110 during the Iran war. Shell/Exxon are back with deepwater (Bonga North).","who":"NNPC + IOCs; theft/vandalism siphon a chronic share before crude reaches port","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Crude Oil","stage":"process","actor":"Dangote Refinery, Lekki (650k→1.4M bpd)","detail":"THE INVERSION: the $20B Dangote refinery — the world's largest single-train — turned Nigeria from a fuel IMPORTER into a net EXPORTER. Petrol imports fell from 42M litres/day (Dec 2025) to 3M (Feb 2026). But the paradox: NNPC can't supply enough crude (committed to loan-service deals), so Dangote IMPORTS crude from the US, Libya, UAE — and just switched to DOLLAR pricing because naira-for-crude failed.","who":"Dangote (private) captures the refining margin the state's 3 idle refineries never could","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Refined fuel","stage":"mid","actor":"Jet fuel/diesel → Europe (ARA hub) + West Africa","detail":"Dangote is now displacing Gulf and US barrels in Europe — ~half of June loadings were jet fuel, ~96k bpd to the Netherlands/Amsterdam-Rotterdam-Antwerp hub. Diesel goes regionally to 11 refinery-less West African states. It may end the $17B/yr Europe→Africa gasoline trade.","who":"Reverses the old flow — Africa now refines and ships to Europe, not the other way","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Refined fuel","stage":"end","actor":"West African pumps · European jet fuel · a $40B IPO","detail":"The end state is energy sovereignty as an asset: Dangote is prepping a ~$4B IPO at a ~$40B valuation (potentially Africa's largest listing) with dollar-linked dividends. The extraction lens inverted — value captured at home, sold abroad, floated on the exchange.","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["resource-nationalism"],"history":{"roots":"Nigeria is an amalgamation that was never asked. Britain fused the Sokoto Caliphate's north, the Yoruba west, the Igbo east, and hundreds of other polities into one colony in 1914 for administrative economy — Lugard's indirect rule governing each through different structures, hardening difference into political architecture (Mamdani's citizen/subject again). Decolonization handed that architecture three regions engineered for rivalry; the first decade delivered coups, pogroms, and Biafra — a million-plus dead, mostly Igbo children starved under blockade, a war Achebe's memoir insists Nigeria never honestly reckoned with. Oil then remade the federation into a rentier state: the Niger Delta's crude funds the center while the Delta itself got Ken Saro-Wiwa's hanging (1995) for demanding its share — the extraction-without-development pattern in domestic form. The north's poverty belt, the Middle Belt's land conflicts, and the Delta's militancy are all children of the same design: a state built to extract and allocate, not to develop.","timeline":[{"date":"1960","e":"Independence from Britain."},{"date":"1967-70","e":"Biafra civil war (~1M+ dead) — the secession wound that still echoes."},{"date":"2009","e":"Boko Haram launches its insurgency in the northeast."},{"date":"2014","e":"Chibok schoolgirls kidnapped; global outrage."},{"date":"2016","e":"Boko Haram splinters; ISWAP emerges as the stronger faction."},{"date":"2020s","e":"Northwest 'bandit' mass-kidnappings; Middle Belt farmer-herder killings escalate."},{"date":"2024-25","e":"Lakurawa (Sahel-linked) appears in the far northwest."},{"date":"Oct 2025","e":"US designates Nigeria a 'Country of Particular Concern' over Christian killings."},{"date":"May 2026","e":"Joint US-Nigeria military operation against ISIS; 'genocide' framing disputed by ACLED/Crisis Group."}],"actors":[{"name":"ISWAP","bio":"Islamic State's West Africa Province; the strongest jihadist faction in the northeast, running a proto-state around Lake Chad."},{"name":"Boko Haram (JAS)","bio":"The original insurgency; diminished but still active in Borno."},{"name":"Bola Tinubu (govt)","bio":"Nigeria's president since 2023; managing overlapping insurgencies, economic strain, and now US pressure."},{"name":"United States","bio":"Newly intervening militarily under a contested 'Christian genocide' framing; suspected of a critical-minerals leverage motive."},{"name":"IPOB","bio":"Biafran separatists in the southeast who said they'd welcome a US intervention — a sovereignty wrinkle."}],"bridge":"Each of today's four insurgencies maps to a seam of the 1914 design: Boko Haram/ISWAP in the impoverished former-Caliphate north; banditry and Lakurawa in the northwest the state never reached; IPOB separatism in the east Biafra's unreckoned wound keeps open; Delta theft in the extraction zone itself. The US intervention adds the newest layer — a 'Christian genocide' framing that ACLED's own data contradicts, doing narrative work for a critical-minerals agenda — while the Dangote refinery represents the counter-story: the first time in the oil era that the refining margin stays in Africa. Nigeria's question since 1914 has never changed: can a state built as an extraction manifold be rebuilt as a country? · AND NOW, JUL 2026: four distinct conflicts run simultaneously and US special forces operate in the northeast under a framing ACLED's data does not support. Lugard's amalgamation of 1914 joined territories that have never agreed what the state is for.","sources":[{"work":"There Was a Country: A Personal History of Biafra","author":"Chinua Achebe","year":"2012","note":"The primary literary-historical testimony of Biafra and the unfinished reckoning."},{"work":"A History of Nigeria","author":"Toyin Falola & Matthew Heaton","year":"2008","note":"The standard survey — amalgamation, indirect rule, oil-state formation — led by the dean of Nigerian historians."},{"work":"Genocide in Nigeria: The Ogoni Tragedy","author":"Ken Saro-Wiwa","year":"1992","note":"Primary text of the Delta's case against extraction-without-development, by its executed author."},{"work":"Citizen and Subject","author":"Mahmood Mamdani","year":"1996","note":"Indirect rule's machinery — how Lugard's Nigeria became the template of bifurcated rule."},{"work":"ACLED Nigeria religious-targeting analysis","author":"ACLED","year":"2020-26","note":"The event data (religious targeting ≈5% of civilian-targeting incidents) contradicting the 'Christian genocide' framing."}]},"human":{"dead":40000,"disp":3400000,"famine":false,"aid":"partial NE","note":"Boko Haram-era cumulative ~40k+ dead, 3.4M displaced across the four insurgencies; the US strikes add a new casualty stream.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"A state built as an extraction manifold produces insurgency at every seam; the US intervention adds a minerals-leverage layer under a humanitarian frame.","alt":"The northwest 'bandit' war reads better as criminal enterprise (kidnap-ransom markets) than ideology or extraction; Middle Belt violence is substantially climate-driven herder-farmer land competition — grouping four insurgencies under one frame risks exactly the error the review named.","why":"The platform keeps the four fronts analytically separate while holding the structural frame for why the state answers none. The US-framing critique is HIGH (ACLED data); no unified-cause claim is made. MOD overall."},"refs":[{"t":"ACLED · Horn and West Africa conflict monitoring","u":"https://acleddata.com/region/conflict-horn-africa"},{"t":"ICG · On the Horizon: July-December 2026","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/oth/chad-cuba-horn-africa-pakistan/horizon-july-december-2026"},{"t":"ECOWAS/Nigeria-Morocco pipeline coverage","u":"https://www.reuters.com/business/energy/"},{"t":"ACLED Nigeria","u":"https://acleddata.com/region/africa/"},{"t":"ACLED Nigeria analysis","u":"https://acleddata.com/region/africa/"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"80 = four simultaneous and analytically distinct conflicts — jihadist northeast, northwest banditry at 20-30k armed men, Middle Belt communal violence, and southeastern separatism — plus a new US intervention.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Seven armed actors added across all four fronts; US intervention logged with split verdict"}],"spill":[{"n":"Niger / Sahel","dir":"in","w":"JNIM maintains a designated NIGERIAN BRIGADE; the Lakurawa implant operates on the Sokoto-Kebbi frontier. The Sahel war has a Nigerian chapter under its own flag."},{"n":"Cameroon / Chad","dir":"both","w":"Lake Chad basin operations against ISWAP and JAS cross all three borders; Chad lost 23 soldiers at Barka Tolorom in May 2026."},{"n":"United States","dir":"in","w":"Joint operations since May 2026 — SEAL raids and airstrikes killed ISWAP's al-Minuki and ~175 fighters, under a 'Christian genocide' framing ACLED's data does not support.","id":"us-politics"},{"n":"South Africa","id":"south-africa","dir":"in","w":"Anti-migrant violence killed two Nigerian nationals and postponed regional diplomacy — the continental pole exporting a crisis to Abuja rather than absorbing one."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Four simultaneous conflicts, US intervention","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation → fragmentation","up":["Simultaneous Boko Haram/ISWAP attacks on Maiduguri","The Zamfara banditry front (300+ killed in one op)","The 'Christian genocide' framing distorting response","The MJTF collapse displacing rather than defeating militants"],"down":["Rebuilding the multinational task force","Addressing the herder-farmer and banditry drivers distinctly","Accurate framing of the four distinct conflicts","Regional coordination on Lake Chad"],"fw":["resource-conflict","ethnic-security","conflict-curve"]},"macro":{"growth":"4.1% (2026) · GDP $285B nominal","infl":"15.7% (Apr 2026, NBS) · 16.0% (2026, IMF)","src":"National Bureau of Statistics; IMF; World Bank GEP, 2026","note":"GROWTH AND POVERTY MOVING IN THE SAME DIRECTION: 4.1% growth against 30.9% of the population below $2.15/day and 63.5% below $3.65/day. Government debt is a modest 35% of GDP and reserves are $48.5B, so this is not a solvency crisis — it is a distribution problem, which is the economic substrate of four simultaneous conflicts. Imports run 31.2% from China. World Bank has revised Nigeria DOWN specifically on the Middle East conflict's impact."},"inherit":{"name":"The Amalgamation and Indirect Rule","est":"1914","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Lugard joined the Northern and Southern Protectorates in 1914 for administrative and fiscal convenience — the north ran a deficit, the south a surplus. He governed through <b>indirect rule</b>: existing emirs and chiefs collected taxes and enforced order on Britain's behalf, which preserved northern aristocratic authority while dismantling southern political structures that did not fit the model.","now":"<b>Both instruments still operate.</b> The federation joins territories that have never agreed what the state is for, and the traditional-authority structures Lugard entrenched remain politically decisive in the north. Four simultaneous conflicts run inside those seams — Boko Haram and ISWAP in the northeast, banditry in the northwest, herder-farmer violence in the middle belt, IPOB in the southeast. <b>The revenue logic also survives:</b> oil from the Niger Delta funds a centre that the Delta does not control.","change":"Restructuring and resource-control debates recur in every electoral cycle without resolution. US special forces now operate in the northeast under a 'Christian genocide' framing that ACLED's data does not support — an external reading of a domestic seam."},"monitors":[{"t":"CDD West Africa","u":"https://www.cddwestafrica.org/"}],"ctrl":{"gov":85,"contested":10,"other":5,"est":true,"src":"ACLED Nigeria data, 2026","note":"State control is broadly intact but four distinct conflicts run inside it — ISWAP and JAS around Lake Chad, banditry in the northwest, herder-farmer violence in the middle belt, IPOB in the southeast. Territorial holding is limited; the contest is over safe passage and taxation."}},{"id":"drc","cat":"military","region":"West / Central Africa","subregion":"Central Africa","name":"DRC — M23/Rwanda + ADF + Cobalt + Ebola","sub":"Uvira: M23 \"withdrew\" but plainclothes militants occupy · Rubaya mine fight","domain":"Proxy War / Resource War","sev":"critical","status":"escalating","since":"2022","esc":82,"lat":-1.5,"lon":29.2,"trend":"ss","w14":false,"esc_history":[82,82,82,82,82,82,82,82,82,82],"cas":"10,000+ since 2022; ADF killed 149 civ in Ituri last month (ACLED)","disp":"6M+ IDPs","flag":"🇨🇩","note":"31 JUL 2026 — A NEGOTIATED FDLR SURRENDER, AND RWANDA REJECTED IT: Kinshasa announced on 28 July that it had signed a DISARMAMENT PROTOCOL WITH THE FDLR. FDLR political wing president VICTOR BYIRINGIRO wrote to Tshisekedi on 30 MAY committing the group to disarm, demobilise and canton — CONDITIONAL ON secure sites with guarantees, NO FORCED EXTRADITION TO RWANDA, a definitive halt to Rwandan operations against 'Rwandan refugees' in the DRC, and the 'OPENING OF POLITICAL SPACE' IN RWANDA. Failing those, the FDLR would accept relocation to a third country; Kinshasa says an unnamed African state has agreed to take them · RWANDA FORMALLY REJECTED THE PLAN at the sixth Joint Oversight Committee in PARIS on 24 July. Foreign Minister NDUHUNGIREHE called it a 'CRUDE MANIPULATION' and 'DELAYING TACTIC', and claimed Kinshasa has INCREASED military and political support to the FDLR since the December 2025 accords · THE UN SUPPORTS THE SECOND CLAIM: its June 2026 report found the FARDC INCREASED COLLABORATION WITH THE FDLR, which numbers 3,500-4,500 across three factions · THE STRUCTURAL DEADLOCK IN ONE LINE: Kinshasa says it cannot act against the FDLR while M23 holds five of the six designated operational zones; a Jeune Afrique source counters that some FDLR factions operate in government-held South Kivu · UN SANCTIONS 14 JULY: six individuals and two entities — TWIRWANEHO and the AFC as entities; AFC head CORNEILLE NANGAA, M23 intelligence chief JOHN NZENZE, Twirwaneho military leader CHARLES SEMATAMA, FDLR leader SÉBASTIEN UWIMBABAZI, FDLR commander GUSTAVE KUBWAYO, and an ADF logistics commander · FIRST FARDC OFFICERS IN GOMA SINCE JANUARY 2025: three officers deployed 13 July for the first Expanded Joint Verification Mechanism Plus meeting with M23 officers · M23 COMMISSIONED 9,318 'COMMANDOS' on 20 July at its Tchanzu facility — its FIFTH large recruitment cycle since July 2025 · A DRONE WAR HAS OPENED: the FARDC has received THREE TURKISH BAYRAKTAR AKINCI drones with a fourth pending, and a 28 July strike near Nyabiondo reportedly killed five senior M23 officers; an FARDC intelligence memo estimates RWANDAN-OPERATED BAYRAKTAR TB2s have flown an AVERAGE OF FIVE STRIKES PER DAY in the Kivus since mid-June · THE UAE IS NOW ARMING KINSHASA: flight tracking shows a cargo aircraft from AL REEF AIRBASE IN ABU DHABI to Kinshasa on 23 July, part of shipments CTP assesses have increased since late June — the same state this board documents as the RSF's gold-and-arms conduit in Sudan is now supplying the Congolese government · US PRIVATE MILITARY CONTRACTORS are deployed with the FARDC in South Kivu; Kinshasa declined a Bancroft Global Development offer to run FDLR disarmament · EBOLA IS DISRUPTING THE US MINERALS PARTNERSHIP — a July review meeting was cancelled over travel restrictions · PROTESTS IN GOMA AND BUKAVU on 28 July against both the UN sanctions AND Tshisekedi's proposed constitutional change allowing a third term · GAP IN THIS BOARD'S RECORD — ISCAP IS TARGETING CHINESE-OWNED MINING SITES: in mid-March 2026 IS Central Africa Province militants attacked CHINESE-OWNED MINING SITES in eastern DRC in a spate of assaults — the ADF's MOST SEVERE ATTACKS ON MINING SITES and its furthest-west operations in several months, including at Muchacha, a location it had never struck before · Critical Threats assesses the attacks were meant to show strength, retaliate for counterterrorism operations, and set conditions to retreat from pressure · THIS BELONGS AT THE CENTRE OF THIS FILE AND WAS ABSENT FROM IT. The platform tracks Chinese mineral offtake as the economic layer beneath the Congo war; an Islamic State affiliate now attacking that layer directly makes the extraction chain a military target rather than only a political one — and it puts Chinese commercial presence into the same category as the Russian and Rwandan positions the board already maps · JUL 2026 — EBOLA HAS A NUMBER NOW: confirmed cases in the DRC have reached 1,502 WITH 321 DEATHS since mid-May, prompting a renewed regional containment effort; the WHO has launched CLINICAL TRIALS FOR TWO POTENTIAL EBOLA TREATMENTS · this board recorded 'Ebola amid ADF violence in Ituri' without figures — the figures are worse than the phrasing implied, and the outbreak is running in the districts an IS-affiliated insurgency is attacking · a separate UN report highlights the growing MILITARY AND POLITICAL influence of the AFC/M23 alliance — political consolidation, not just territorial · JUL 2026 — EBOLA AND THE ADF TOGETHER IN ITURI: ACLED records the ADF RAMPING UP VIOLENCE IN ITURI AMID AN EBOLA OUTBREAK · the combination is the worst case for both: outbreak response requires contact tracing and community access in exactly the districts an IS-affiliated insurgency is attacking, and the 2018-20 Ituri/North Kivu epidemic showed that armed attacks on health workers turn a containable outbreak into a prolonged one · the UN's food agency is delivering in affected areas · 17-22 JUL 2026 — MONITORING WITHOUT MOVEMENT: the UN SANCTIONED several individuals and entities tied to the eastern conflict; Congolese army officers travelled to GOMA for ceasefire monitoring under the Doha process; the DRC, Rwanda and observer states convened the FIFTH Joint Security Coordination Mechanism meeting under the Washington Accords · fighting between M23-allied TWIRWANEHO and pro-government forces continues in the South Kivu highlands · THE PATTERN THIS PLATFORM TRACKS: five coordination meetings, eight negotiating pillars, two signed frameworks — and M23 has not returned territory. The 18 Aug deadline was already missed once; process is substituting for outcome · AUG 18 DEADLINE APPROACHES: the DRC-M23 final peace agreement deadline falls on 18 August — the date the Doha framework's full deal is meant to be concluded. The platform's read: M23's parallel administration has deepened throughout the negotiation, so the deadline tests whether a deal ratifies the facts on the ground rather than reversing them · JUL 2026 — EBOLA: THIRD-LARGEST OUTBREAK ON RECORD, AND THE PLATFORM'S OWN TRIPWIRE ALREADY FIRED: 2,124 confirmed cases and 828 deaths as of 16 Jul (ECDC/DRC MoH); ITURI alone holds 1,904 cases and 692 deaths across 27 of 36 health zones; five provinces affected (Ituri, North Kivu, South Kivu, Tshopo, Haut Uele), with Tshopo putting cases 500km west of the epicentre · SPEED IS THE STORY: two months to 2,000 cases, against TEN MONTHS in the 2018-20 outbreak; WHO warns the true toll may be TWICE the confirmed count · the Bundibugyo strain was declared a PHEIC on 17 May · contact tracing sits at ~78-80%, below the 90-95% needed to contain · CORRECTION TO THIS FILE: a case was confirmed in GOMA on 17 May — in M23-held territory — meaning this platform's Watchlist tripwire ('Ebola reaches Goma or another urban center') TRIPPED two months before it was logged; recorded now rather than quietly updated · IRC warns of accelerating cross-border risk into SOUTH SUDAN, whose health system and politics are described as being on the edge; CDC entry restrictions on DRC/Uganda/South Sudan travellers expire ~21 Jul · Uganda has begun its 42-day countdown to declaring its own outbreak over (20 cases, 2 deaths); France confirmed an imported case 24 Jun; Oxford began preparing vaccine trials 57 days after the PHEIC · JUL 2026 — LOBITO CORRIDOR FINANCED: two international lenders provided $753M to modernise 1,300km of the Lobito Corridor railway linking Benguela port to the DRC border — $553M of it from a US development body. The corridor is Washington's physical answer to Chinese offtake dominance: a westward route carrying Congolese minerals to the Atlantic instead of east through Chinese-financed infrastructure · the pipeline board's 'who moves the ore' question now has a competing rail answer, financed · JUL 2026 — M23'S DEMOGRAPHIC REENGINEERING: the Doha framework (full deal targeted summer 2026) holds on paper while M23 entrenches a parallel state — UN Group of Experts + HRW document M23 dismantling IDP/refugee camps (forced ~700k to disperse at Goma's fall) and repatriating thousands of mostly-Hutu 'refugees' to Rwanda since May under screening the UN told HRW was 'done under pressure' · ~2M IDPs have returned to origin areas; M23 runs its own land reform, taxation, and airport control (only it can reopen Goma airport, after France/DRC raised ~$1.7B for humanitarian ops) · the entrenchment makes any future deal negotiate with a de facto administration — the Banyamulenge citizenship question (this theater's rival read) is now being answered by force on the ground · JUL 15 — EBOLA OUTPACING CONTAINMENT: WHO reports 80% of NEW Ebola cases in eastern DRC now emerge from UNKNOWN transmission chains — the outbreak is spreading faster than it can be tracked, and the ADF-violence/epidemic convergence in Ituri (already flagged) is the mechanism: the war zone is the blind spot · C64 opposition coalition POSTPONED nationwide protests after Burundian President Ndayishimiye's mediation — the political fuse burns slower, for now · PIPELINE + RWANDA EXPANDED (Wk16) · The extraction chain is now traced mine→end-buyer for all 4 minerals — see the ⛓ Pipeline board · RWANDA is the load-bearing external actor: 7,000–12,000 RDF troops fight with M23 (RDF sanctioned by OFAC Mar 2026); Rubaya coltan (~15% of world tantalum) is smuggled via Goma–Gisenyi, relabelled 'Rwandan' with ITSCI tags applied AFTER the border, and laundered into Apple/Microsoft/Sony/Nvidia/Toyota supply chains (Global Witness, 10 Jun 2026, 2,000+ tonnes traced); M23 earns ~$800k/mo at $4/kg · GOLD laundered via Gasabo Gold refinery (OFAC/EU sanctioned) → Dubai, mirroring the RSF/UAE circuit · COBALT: China owns 15/19 mines, CMOC sets the price → Kinshasa's Feb-2025 ban → Oct-2025 quota (96,600t/yr) → US Washington Accords counter (Virtus $700M, first US operator back in a decade) · 200+ killed in a Mar-2026 Rubaya shaft collapse · UPDATED · ADF killed 149 civilians in Ituri last month (=Jan-Apr total combined, ACLED) · Ebola now in 4th province (Bundibugyo strain, WHO) · ADF shifting north from N.Kivu into Ituri = both violence hotspot + Ebola-response center · M23 took Uvira; FARDC killed M23 spokesman Ngoma in record drone campaign · DRC + M23 pledged comprehensive peace agreement by MID-AUGUST; both sides reinforcing South Kivu — possible imminent battle for Uvira · Uvira status murky: M23 claimed retreat under US pressure but plainclothes militants still occupy + clash with Wazalendo · FARDC record air/drone campaign (killed spokesman Ngoma) around Rubaya coltan mine · Rwanda-Burundi powder keg (Uvira 26km from Bujumbura); comprehensive peace deal still pledged mid-Aug","leverage":[{"actor":"Rwanda (Kagame / RDF)","asset":"7,000–12,000 troops embedded with M23 + control of the Goma–Gisenyi smuggling corridor — turns eastern DRC's minerals into 'Rwandan' exports. The war IS the business model.","str":"hi"},{"actor":"M23","asset":"Physical control of Rubaya (~15% of world tantalum) + a parallel tax state ($4/kg coltan, ~$800k/mo). Whoever holds the mine holds the war chest.","str":"hi"},{"actor":"China (CMOC)","asset":"Owns 15/19 cobalt mines and sets the global cobalt price — the incumbent Kinshasa is trying to dilute without losing the investment","str":"hi"},{"actor":"United States (Accords)","asset":"Offtake + DFC finance + serial OFAC sanctions on the smuggling network — financial firepower deployed instead of industrial presence","str":"md"},{"actor":"The traceability gap (ITSCI/RMAP)","asset":"The geographic bypass — tagging ore AFTER the border launders conflict minerals into 'compliant' supply chains. Paperwork is the laundering tool, not the safeguard.","str":"md"},{"actor":"ADF (IS-affiliate)","asset":"Ituri massacres (149 killed last month) disrupting the Ebola response — a second, jihadist war layered on the mineral one","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"WHO","detail":"Confirmed Ebola (Bundibugyo virus) spread to 4th province; Ituri insecurity limiting containment"},{"src":"DRC Government / FARDC","detail":"Record drone/air campaign; killed M23 spokesman Willy Ngoma"},{"src":"G7 / Élysée","detail":"Critical Minerals Alliance targeting cobalt/rare-earth concentration"}],"mil":"ACLED recorded at least 149 civilian fatalities in Ituri province last month alone — equaling the total ADF-related civilian deaths there for January-April combined. As the ADF (an IS affiliate) shifts northward from North Kivu, Ituri has become both a hotspot of Islamist violence and the center of the region's Ebola response — the insecurity directly limits humanitarian access. Separately, M23 seized Uvira and FARDC killed M23 spokesman Willy Ngoma in a record drone campaign.","pol":"The dual-track war (Rwanda-backed M23 in the east + ADF/IS in Ituri) continues under G7 mineral-de-risking pressure and Qatari/US mediation. The public-health emergency is widening fast.","eco":"The WHO confirmed Ebola (Bundibugyo strain) has reached a fourth province — the entire northeast is now affected — with ADF violence in Irumu/Mambasa territories blocking containment. The cobalt chemical chain (Kolwezi) recovered as Hormuz normalized.","assess":"DRC rises to 82. The compounding factor is the ADF-Ebola convergence in Ituri: the same territory is the Islamist-violence hotspot and the epidemic epicenter, and the violence is actively preventing the epidemic response — a lethal feedback loop. Rwanda's M23 proxy (now holding Uvira) remains the 'hi' geopolitical lever; the ADF is the humanitarian accelerant.","diplomacy_active":true,"diplomacy":[{"name":"DRC-M23 comprehensive peace agreement","desc":"Both sides pledged to sign by mid-August (Qatar/US-mediated) while reinforcing South Kivu positions — sign-or-fight dynamic around Uvira","status":"active","next":"MID-AUGUST deadline"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Rwanda","channel":"Smuggled-mineral re-export","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Coltan exports up 2.5x (2021-25); gold now the #1 export earner — a national revenue stream built substantially on ore that never legally left the DRC. This is why OFAC sanctioned the RDF itself, not just the middlemen."},{"holder":"China (CMOC)","channel":"Cobalt price-setting","exp":"HIGH","detail":"By owning most mines and overproducing, CMOC sets the world cobalt price from Kolwezi — the leverage that forced Kinshasa into an export ban then a quota to defend its own revenue"},{"holder":"DRC (Kinshasa)","channel":"Export quota + E-Trace","exp":"MED","detail":"The 96,600t/yr cobalt cap and the mine-to-export tracking platform are Kinshasa asserting price power and ESG credibility to court Western capital — Africa's boldest mineral-sovereignty play in decades"},{"holder":"US / UAE","channel":"Offtake + gold refining","exp":"MED","detail":"Washington redirects copper/cobalt via offtake deals; Abu Dhabi locks in gold via a ~$2B supply deal — two Western-aligned channels prising open a Chinese-dominated market"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇷🇼 Rwanda (RDF + M23)","side":"M23 proxy / occupier","flow":"7,000–12,000 RDF troops fight alongside M23; RDF sanctioned by OFAC (Mar 2026). M23 seized Goma, Bukavu, the Rubaya mines and pushed on Uvira. Kagame calls the mineral theft 'an imaginary problem.'","tier":"documented","for":"Coltan, gold, tin & tungsten smuggled across the border and relabelled 'Rwandan' — mineral revenue that made Rwanda's coltan exports rise 2.5x (2021-25) and gold its #1 earner"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China (CMOC / Zijin / Huayou)","side":"resource incumbent","flow":"Owns 15 of 19 cobalt mines and most big copper assets; CMOC sets the global cobalt price; Sicomines gave ~$93B of rights for ~$3B of infrastructure","tier":"documented","for":"~80% of DRC cobalt and most copper, routed to China for refining — processing dominance over the metals of the energy transition"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US (Washington Accords)","side":"strategic challenger","flow":"Dec 4 2025 DRC-Rwanda accord + Regional Economic Integration Framework; Virtus Minerals ($700M) first US operator back in a decade; DFC financing + offtake; serial OFAC sanctions on the smuggling network (Mar/Apr/Jun 2026)","tier":"documented","for":"Privileged access to copper, cobalt, lithium, tantalum — redirecting Congolese minerals away from Chinese and informal channels without owning the mines"},{"actor":"🇦🇪 UAE","side":"gold offtaker","flow":"April 2026 ~$2B DRC gold-supply deal keeping the UAE the refining point; June 2026 flight-tracking caught UAE cargo flights Abu Dhabi→Kinshasa arming Tshisekedi (cross-link: UAE theater)","tier":"documented","for":"Congolese gold into an Emirati refining monopoly under a US economic umbrella"},{"actor":"🇧🇷🇪🇺 Traders (Traxys) / smelters","side":"laundering conduit","flow":"Traxys was near-exclusive buyer of African Panther's Rwandan coltan (denies Rubaya origin); 8 Asian smelters passed RMAP audits while ≥4 processed likely-Rubaya ore","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Clean-looking tantalum for global electronics — the point where conflict ore becomes 'compliant' on paper"}],"ext_flag":true,"tclass":"conflict","tier":"SEVERE","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"Everyone knows where the coltan goes. The paperwork says otherwise.","claims":[{"claim":"Rwanda denies backing M23.","who":"Rwanda — and every downstream buyer who needs the minerals to be Rwandan-origin.","evid":"UN Group of Experts panels have documented Rwandan Defence Force troops, equipment, and command support repeatedly. Rwanda's coltan exports exceed what its own deposits can plausibly produce.","status":"DOCUMENTED-BUT-DENIED"},{"claim":"Minerals entering global supply chains are certified conflict-free.","who":"The electronics and EV industries; the certification schemes themselves.","evid":"Laundering through Rwanda is the documented mechanism by which conflict coltan becomes compliant coltan. The certification is downstream of the fraud.","status":"CONTESTED — the paperwork is the problem"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Coltan → Tantalum","stage":"mine","actor":"Rubaya mines, Masisi (North Kivu)","detail":"~15% of the world's tantalum; ~half of Congolese coltan. Artisanal miners dig in deadly conditions — a March 2026 shaft collapse killed 200+, including children. M23's parallel 'ministry' issues permits and doubles wages to keep output up.","who":"M23 controls the mines (seized Apr 2024) — the war's main revenue source","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Coltan → Tantalum","stage":"process","actor":"M23 taxation + cross-border smuggling to Rwanda","detail":"M23 levies $4/kg (above Rwanda's own $3/kg state tax) → ~$800k/month per UN experts. ~1,400t crossed in year one, in daylight through the Goma–Gisenyi crossing. In Kigali it's mixed with local ore and relabelled 'Rwandan' with ITSCI tags applied AFTER the border — the geographic bypass that defeats traceability.","who":"Rwanda Defence Force (OFAC-sanctioned Mar 2026); exporters African Panther, Sunrise, Boss Mining, Kanzamin, Philbert","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Coltan → Tantalum","stage":"mid","actor":"Smelters in China, Kazakhstan, Thailand","detail":"Trucked Kigali → Dar es Salaam / Mombasa → 8 smelters (incl. Kazakhstan's Ulba Metallurgical Plant). Global Witness links ≥4 to Rubaya ore. All 8 passed RMAP audits while sourcing it — once refined into metal, origin is undetectable without geochemical fingerprinting (which Rwanda refuses).","who":"Trader Traxys was African Panther's near-exclusive buyer (denies Rubaya origin)","tier":"alleged-credible"},{"mineral":"Coltan → Tantalum","stage":"end","actor":"Tantalum capacitors → global electronics & EVs","detail":"A phone holds ~40mg of tantalum. Global Witness names Apple, Microsoft, Sony, Amazon, Nvidia, Toyota, Honda, Vodafone, LG Display, Ericsson as brands whose chains 'may unwittingly carry' the conflict metal. DRC filed complaints against Apple subsidiaries in France & Belgium (2024). The lesson: 'paperwork is not provenance.'","who":"Consumer demand is, indirectly, an armed-group financing mechanism","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Cobalt","stage":"mine","actor":"Kolwezi belt — Tenke Fungurume, Kisanfu, Mutanda","detail":"DRC = ~76% of world cobalt. Mostly mined as a copper by-product. Artisanal sector ~2% of output but the human face of it. Kinshasa banned mixing artisanal ore with industrial output and launched E-Trace mine-to-export tracking.","who":"China owns 15 of DRC's 19 operating cobalt mines","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Cobalt","stage":"process","actor":"CMOC (China Molybdenum) — the price-setter","detail":"Bought Tenke Fungurume from Freeport (2016, $2.65B); now the world's #1 cobalt supplier. Its overproduction crashed prices ~60%, so Kinshasa imposed a Feb 2025 export BAN → Oct 2025 QUOTA (96,600t/yr for 2026-27, <half of 2024 output). CMOC opposed; Glencore accepted — the Western-vs-Chinese split over African sovereignty in one dispute.","who":"Sicomines 2007 deal: mining rights worth ~$93B for ~$3B of infrastructure","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Cobalt","stage":"mid","actor":"Refined in China → cathode / battery precursor","detail":"~80% of DRC cobalt is controlled by China and routed home for refining. THE US COUNTER (Washington Accords, Dec 4 2025): Virtus Minerals ($700M, via Chemaf's Étoile + Mutoshi) is the first US operator back in a decade; Gécamines to ship ~100,000t of Tenke copper to US buyers; DFC financing + offtake deals rather than owning mines.","who":"Contest: CMOC/Zijin/Huayou (China) vs Virtus/KoBold (US, backed by Gates & Bezos)","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Cobalt","stage":"end","actor":"EV batteries · phones · military jets · aerospace","detail":"On the US critical-minerals list. The whole quota-and-Accords fight is about who controls the metal at the end of this line — Beijing (via refining) or Washington (via offtake). The DRC's play is to make both bid.","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"mine","actor":"Eastern DRC artisanal gold fields","detail":"High-value, low-volume, easy to smuggle — the ideal conflict mineral. Extracted in M23/RDF-controlled and contested zones.","who":"M23 + Rwanda Defence Force move it physically across the border","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"process","actor":"Gasabo Gold refinery, Rwanda","detail":"OFAC (Jun 2026) + EU sanctioned: RDF/M23 hand gold directly to Gasabo, which refines it. ≥60kg (millions of $) moved from eastern DRC to Gasabo in early 2026. Chairman Jean Malic Kalima also controls Bugambira, Wolfram Mining, Rwinkwavu — a whole Rwandan mining network.","who":"Gold is now Rwanda's #1 export earner; coltan #2","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"mid","actor":"Refined bars → Dubai / global bullion market","detail":"Rwandan-refined gold enters international bullion flows with its conflict origin obscured — the same Dubai-centric laundering circuit the UAE runs for Sudanese gold (cross-link: UAE 'quiet empire' theater).","tier":"alleged-credible"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"end","actor":"Jewellery · central-bank reserves · electronics","detail":"Once refined and sold, it is fungible and untraceable — which is exactly why gold, not coltan, is the preferred value-transfer metal for both the M23/Rwanda and RSF/UAE conflict economies.","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Copper","stage":"mine","actor":"Katanga Copperbelt — Kamoa-Kakula, Tenke, Manono-adjacent","detail":"DRC = world's #2 copper producer (~3.3M t in 2024). Mined alongside cobalt. KoBold (US, Gates/Bezos-backed) holds 3,000+ km² but WON'T advance disputed blocks; Zijin (China) builds on contested ground anyway — speed-to-market is China's edge.","who":"US firms stayed away for a decade over conflict/corruption; now returning under the Accords","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Copper","stage":"process","actor":"Cathode — refined largely in/for China","detail":"Chinese firms (CMOC, Zijin) control most large copper assets and have routed output to China for refining for over a decade. Gécamines' new marketing rights (2023 CMOC renegotiation) let it redirect ~100,000t of Tenke copper to US buyers — the first crack in that monopoly.","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Copper","stage":"end","actor":"Grid · electronics · EVs · construction · defense","detail":"On the US critical list. Trump's reassertion of the Monroe Doctrine in the National Security Strategy frames Congolese copper as a strategic-inventory question — the metal at the end of this line is now great-power infrastructure, not just plumbing.","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["china-refine","dubai-gold","resource-nationalism"],"history":{"roots":"Congo's condition was engineered, and Congolese scholarship has named the engineers for fifty years. Georges Nzongola-Ntalaja's people's history runs the line straight: Leopold's Congo Free State (up to 10 million dead for rubber quotas — hands severed for shortfalls) → Belgian colonial rule that trained not one Congolese officer or administrator class → independence sabotaged in its first year, when Lumumba, the elected champion of a unitary sovereign Congo, was murdered in 1961 with Belgian operational involvement and CIA sanction (both since officially acknowledged) → Mobutu, the West's Cold War custodian, licensed to loot for three decades so long as the minerals flowed. The eastern wars since 1996 — 'Africa's World War,' millions dead, documented in the UN Mapping Report — continue the design with new subcontractors: Rwanda's proxies hold the mines, transnational firms buy the ore, and the traceability paperwork launders it. The DRC has never had a decade in which its resources were governed for its people; that is not a misfortune but a maintained arrangement.","timeline":[{"date":"1885-1908","e":"King Leopold II's Congo Free State: extraction by mass atrocity sets the template."},{"date":"1965-97","e":"Mobutu's kleptocracy hollows out the state."},{"date":"1994","e":"Rwandan genocide; Hutu genocidaires flee into eastern Zaire, forming the FDLR."},{"date":"1996-2003","e":"Two Congo Wars ('Africa's World War') kill millions; Rwanda and Uganda intervene for minerals."},{"date":"2012","e":"M23 rebellion (Rwanda-backed) briefly seizes Goma, then collapses."},{"date":"2021","e":"M23 re-emerges, better armed, with overt Rwandan support."},{"date":"Early 2025","e":"M23 seizes Goma and Bukavu, the two eastern provincial capitals."},{"date":"4 Dec 2025","e":"Washington Accords signed by DRC and Rwanda under US mediation."},{"date":"Dec 2025","e":"M23 takes Uvira days after the signing — the accord's hollowness exposed."},{"date":"Mar 2026","e":"US sanctions the Rwandan army (RDF) for backing M23; peace stalls."}],"actors":[{"name":"Félix Tshisekedi","bio":"DRC's president since 2019; has courted the US (Washington Accords, minerals-for-security) while accusing Rwanda of invasion. Manages a coup-risk-laden, militarily weak position."},{"name":"Paul Kagame (Rwanda)","bio":"Rwanda's president since 2000; runs a strategy of 'controlled instability' in eastern DRC — backing M23 to secure minerals and regional dominance while denying involvement."},{"name":"M23 / AFC (Corneille Nangaa)","bio":"The Rwanda-backed rebel movement holding Goma, Bukavu, Uvira and the Rubaya mines; runs a parallel tax state on coltan."},{"name":"FDLR","bio":"Hutu militia descended from the 1994 génocidaires; Rwanda's stated casus belli and a Congolese-government ally against M23."},{"name":"United States","bio":"Brokered the Washington Accords and is redirecting Congolese minerals via offtake deals to counter China's dominance — sanctions the RDF while courting Kinshasa."}],"bridge":"Every node on this platform's DRC pipeline is the Free State's architecture modernized: Rubaya's coltan diggers stand where the rubber gatherers stood; M23's $4/kg tax is the quota system privatized; Kigali plays the entrepôt Brussels played; and the smelter audits that 'certify' the ore perform the same legitimating function as Leopold's Philanthropic Association. The Washington Accords and the cobalt quota are Kinshasa's most serious attempt yet to break the buyer's market — resource sovereignty a century late — while the Ebola-ADF convergence in Ituri shows what a deliberately hollowed state costs in the present tense. Lumumba's question — who shall govern Congo's wealth — remains the war's only real subject. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: M23 administers what it holds, five foreign militaries operate on Congolese soil, and Rwandan mineral exports rise with its territorial control. The Free State's architecture runs on — the difference is that the extraction is now subcontracted rather than owned.","sources":[{"work":"The Congo from Leopold to Kabila: A People's History","author":"Georges Nzongola-Ntalaja","year":"2002","note":"The Congolese-authored standard — Free State to the eastern wars as one continuous struggle over sovereignty."},{"work":"The Assassination of Lumumba","author":"Ludo De Witte","year":"1999","note":"The investigation that forced Belgium's 2002 parliamentary acknowledgment of responsibility."},{"work":"UN Mapping Report on the DRC (1993-2003)","author":"UN OHCHR","year":"2010","note":"Primary documentation of the eastern wars' atrocities, including Rwandan army crimes — the report power tried to bury."},{"work":"Congo's Violent Peace","author":"Kris Berwouts","year":"2017","note":"The post-2003 order — elections without sovereignty, the eastern war economy's persistence."},{"work":"Global Witness / UN Group of Experts reports on 3T minerals","author":"Global Witness; UN GoE","year":"2022-26","note":"Primary supply-chain tracing: Rubaya → Rwanda → smelters → global brands."}]},"human":{"dead":6000000,"disp":7000000,"famine":false,"aid":"blocked in Ituri (Ebola zone)","note":"~6M dead across the eastern wars since 1996 (IRC-lineage estimates); ~7M displaced now; Ebola response strangled by ADF violence.","est":false,"basis":"UN OCHA and IOM displacement tracking"},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"Leopold's architecture with new subcontractors: minerals finance the war and external actors sustain it for extraction.","alt":"Rwanda's stated security doctrine (the FDLR threat) and genuinely local conflicts — land, citizenship, the Banyamulenge question — predate and exceed the minerals economy; minerals may fund a war that identity and security logics started.","why":"HIGH that minerals finance the war (UN-documented); MOD that they cause it. The Jul-2026 forced repatriations show the Banyamulenge/citizenship logic being settled by force, giving the identity-rival real current weight — financing stated as fact, causation as weighting."},"refs":[{"t":"Critical Threats · Congo War Security Review (31 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/briefs/congo-war-security-review/july-31-2026"},{"t":"UN Security Council · DRC sanctions press release (16 Jul 2026)","u":"https://press.un.org/en/2026/sc16416.doc.htm"},{"t":"US State Department · Fifth JSCM joint statement (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.state.gov/releases/office-of-the-spokesperson/2026/07/joint-statement-on-the-fifth-meeting-of-the-joint-security-coordination-mechanism-for-the-peace-agreement-between-the-democratic-republic-of-the-congo-and-the-republic-of-rwanda"},{"t":"UN Group of Experts on the DRC · June 2026 report (S/2026/466)","u":"http://www.undocs.org/s/2026/466"},{"t":"Critical Threats · Africa File (19 Mar 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/sudan-rsf-saf-sahel-drc-iscap-uganda-nigeria-boko-iswap-mali-fla-jnim-africa-file-march-26-2026"},{"t":"Africa Intelligence Brief (24 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.riotimesonline.com/africa-intelligence-brief-friday-july-24-2026/"},{"t":"VIF · Africa Now (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.vifindia.org/2026/july/17/Africa-Now-01-15-july-2026"}],"checkmode":"SWEPT","escwhy":"92 = foreign-backed rebellion holding provincial capitals, at least five foreign militaries on the territory, and demographic reengineering documented in occupied areas. The 18 Aug deadline is the near-term variable.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Eight armed groups added with national origin; 18 Aug tripwire armed"}],"spill":[{"n":"Rwanda","dir":"in","w":"Troops, equipment and command support to M23 per successive UN Groups of Experts — denied by Kigali. Rwandan mineral exports rise in step with M23's territorial control. Kigali's stated cause is the FDLR, a Rwandan Hutu force on Congolese soil that Kinshasa fights ALONGSIDE.","id":"rwanda"},{"n":"Uganda","dir":"both","w":"THE CONTRADICTION: Uganda has supported M23 and maintains troops in the east from where it receives SMUGGLED GOLD — while ALSO deploying thousands of troops in late 2024/early 2025 to BLOCK M23's advance on Lubero and establish a buffer zone. Kampala also runs joint operations (Shujaa) against the Ugandan-origin ADF. It is simultaneously a beneficiary, a spoiler and a counter-insurgent.","id":"uganda"},{"n":"Burundi","dir":"both","w":"Burundian troops fight alongside the Congolese army; CTP assesses M23 and Rwanda launched the UVIRA offensive at least partly to KNOCK BURUNDI OUT OF THE WAR. Hundreds of Congolese and Burundian troops retreated INTO Burundi as Uvira fell — 400 killed, 200,000 displaced. Bujumbura and Kigali now trade accusations of backing each other's rebels.","id":"burundi"},{"n":"Sudan","dir":"in","w":"Sudan's war destabilises the Central African Republic on the DRC's northern flank, compounding an already contested border zone.","id":"sudan"},{"n":"Zambia / Tanzania","dir":"out","w":"Cobalt from 150,000-200,000 artisanal miners is smuggled out through Zambia, Burundi and Tanzania, facilitated by border officials — the illicit half of the supply chain that ends in consumer electronics."}],"escalation":{"rung":"M23 administers territory, process substitutes for outcome","dir":"volatile","phase":"Escalation frozen by diplomacy that isn't working","up":["M23 refuses to return territory despite five JSCM meetings","Uvira-type offensives knock regional states out","Ebola + ADF compound in Ituri","Rwandan mineral exports track territorial control"],"down":["The Washington/Doha frameworks actually enforced","Cutting the mineral-for-control revenue chain","A regional settlement including Rwanda's FDLR concern","Ripeness — currently absent, M23 believes it is winning"],"fw":["resource-conflict","proxy","ethnic-security","conflict-curve"]},"macro":{"growth":"8.6% (2023 est.) — among Africa's fastest","infl":"Elevated","src":"CIA World Factbook / World Bank","note":"THE STARKEST CONTRADICTION ON THIS BOARD. The DRC posts some of the continent's highest growth rates — driven by cobalt and copper — against roughly 6 million excess deaths since 1996 and 7 million displaced. Growth here measures extraction volume, not welfare: 150,000-200,000 artisanal miners feed a chain that is smuggled out through Zambia, Burundi and Tanzania, and Rwandan mineral exports rise with M23's territorial control. A national accounts figure cannot distinguish minerals that enrich Congolese from minerals that leave."},"inherit":{"name":"The Free State Extraction Architecture","est":"1885","status":"OWNERSHIP CHANGED · STRUCTURE IN FORCE","what":"The Berlin Conference awarded the Congo to Leopold II <b>as personal property</b> — the only time in modern history a European monarch privately owned a country. The system he built was purpose-designed for extraction: rubber and ivory quotas enforced by mutilation, a state whose entire administrative purpose was to move commodities to the coast, and railways engineered to reach mines rather than to connect Congolese to each other.","now":"<b>The transport geography is unchanged.</b> Eastern minerals still leave through Rwanda, Uganda and the Indian Ocean rather than through Kinshasa, because no infrastructure was ever built to make the country coherent — only to make it exportable. Cobalt from 150,000-200,000 artisanal miners moves out through Zambia, Burundi and Tanzania. <b>What changed is ownership, not architecture:</b> the extraction is now subcontracted to armed groups, foreign armies and mining companies rather than owned by one man.","change":"The 2025 Washington Accords and the Doha framework are the current attempt to regulate it, alongside a US-DRC minerals partnership and American firms mapping Congolese geology. <b>M23 has returned no territory across six coordination meetings</b>, and IS-affiliated militants have begun attacking Chinese-owned mining sites — the extraction layer is now a military target as well as an economic one."},"ctrl":{"gov":70,"contested":15,"other":15,"est":true,"src":"Composite of UN Group of Experts and CTP Congo War Security Review, Jul 2026","note":"M23/AFC administers Goma, Bukavu and much of North and South Kivu with appointed governors and tax collection. ADF, CODECO and Twirwaneho hold or contest pockets in Ituri and the highlands."},"control":{"gov":60,"contested":20,"other":20,"holders":"Kinshasa holds the west and centre. M23/AFC administers Goma, Bukavu and much of North and South Kivu with its own appointed officials. ADF, CODECO and Twirwaneho hold pockets in Ituri and the highlands.","note":"M23 has returned no territory across six coordination meetings. It commissioned 9,318 fighters in July — its fifth recruitment cycle since July 2025.","est":true}},{"id":"haiti","cat":"military","region":"Caribbean","subregion":"Caribbean","name":"Haiti — Viv Ansanm / State Collapse","sub":"Gang Suppression Force deploying · Chad 1,500 lead · gangs still hold ~90% of capital","domain":"Criminal Insurgency / State Collapse","sev":"critical","status":"escalating","since":"2020","esc":94,"lat":18.5,"lon":-72.3,"trend":"up","w14":false,"diplomacy_active":true,"esc_history":[94,94,94,94,94,94,94,94,94,94],"cas":"~16,000 killed since 2022; 1,200+ killed in drone strikes (60 civilians); recent gang attacks 70+ dead","disp":"1.5M+ displaced; famine-level food insecurity","flag":"🇭🇹","note":"JUL 2026 — THE APPEAL IS 27% FUNDED: OCHA reports the $880M Humanitarian Appeal for 2026 has received $240M — 27% · the UN released $140.5M in emergency funding in April to reach one million people · FORCED RETURNS ARE RISING: 24% of all forcibly returned migrants in 2026 are WOMEN and nearly 8% are CHILDREN · the structure this board records: an under-strength intervention force, an under-funded humanitarian response, and neighbouring states deporting people back into the conditions the response cannot meet · JUL 2026 — THE GSF IS DEPLOYED AND SKELETAL; THE COUNTER-GANG WAR IS KILLING MORE THAN THE GANGS: UNSC Res 2793 authorised a 5,550-strong Gang Suppression Force; approximately 1,000 PERSONNEL are actually on the ground (first Chadian officers arrived 1 Apr without ceremony; Mongolian force commander Erdenebat Batsuuri arrived 14 May) · gangs still control 70-90% of Port-au-Prince; the Dominican Republic has ruled out sending troops · H1 2026 TOLL (Guterres, June visit): 2,300+ killed, 1,100+ injured, nearly 1.5M displaced, 6.4M needing humanitarian aid · THE ACCOUNTABILITY FINDING: a UN human rights report found MORE THAN 60% OF ALL CASUALTIES (Jan 2025-Mar 2026) occurred during ANTI-GANG OPERATIONS — and HRW documented at least 1,243 people killed in 141 DRONE OPERATIONS (1 Mar 2025-21 Jan 2026), including 17 children, some appearing unlawful and possibly amounting to extrajudicial killings · VECTUS GLOBAL: Erik Prince's private military company (Blackwater's founder) has been striking gangs with explosive-laden quadcopters in dense neighbourhoods under an UNPUBLISHED contract with the transitional government, with no public accountability · CHILD-VIOLATIONS LISTING: Gran Grif, Kraze Barye and 400 Mawozo added to the Secretary-General's annexes (Viv Ansanm listed in 2025) — and the SG warned that the HAITIAN NATIONAL POLICE and the Vectus-supported Special Security Task Force 'should be listed' next year if their child casualties repeat; child recruitment has tripled in a year · elections are slated for late Aug and Dec 2026 · JUL UNSC CYCLE — THE NUMBERS BEHIND 94: BINUH Q1 report: 1,642 killed + 745 injured Jan-Mar, and 69% OF CASUALTIES OCCURRED DURING SECURITY-FORCE OPERATIONS — HNP + the Vectus Global-supported Special Security Task Force, incl. summary executions; the SG warns the HNP itself 'should be listed' next year if child violations repeat (620 children killed/maimed; Gran Grif, Kraze Barye, 400 Mawozo added to the child-violations annexes) · FIRST MAJOR CRACK IN VIV ANSANM: May 9-11 intra-alliance clashes over Plaine du Cul-de-Sac (400 Mawozo/Chyen Mechan/Taliban vs Pierre VI/Terre Noire) killed 78, displaced 5,300 — ACLED: fragile internal arrangements, but collapse unlikely as gangs conserve force ahead of GSF combat ops · GSF REALITY: <1,000 of 5,500 deployed (Chadian contingent present), combat operations only now beginning; Guterres toured PaP Jun 16 ('glimmers of hope') — 2,300 killed + 1.5M displaced THIS YEAR · SG report due Jul 14; 90-day UNSC briefing this month; UNODC to brief on arms-trafficking routes · UN-backed GANG SUPPRESSION FORCE (GSF) now deploying — replaces the Kenya-led MSS (on ground since Jun 2024, made little progress) · authorized by UNSC Res 2793 (Oct 2025, US+Panama-penned); 5,500-person ceiling, mostly UN-budget-funded, empowered to make ARRESTS + run intel-based ops (unlike the MSS which only supported HNP) · FIRST TROOPS: Chadian advance team arrived Apr 1 2026 (Vertières base) · UNSC vote 12-0 with China, Russia, Pakistan ABSTAINING (Russia's Nebenzia: mandate 'rushed,' lets force act vs 'anyone labelled gangs') · full 5,500 expected on ground 'between fall and end of 2026' · ~16,000 killed since 2022; 1.5M+ displaced; gangs (~26 groups) hold ~90% of Port-au-Prince · new govt installed Feb 7 2026 (PM Alix Didier Fils-Aimé); gangs scaled back attacks in the lull, likely reassessing · concerns: a UN report alleged Kenyan MSS members committed sexual violence incl. rape of minors; Haiti's history of harmful intervention (MINUSTAH cholera killed ~10,000)","leverage":[{"actor":"Armed gangs (~26 groups)","asset":"Control ~90% of Port-au-Prince; territorial + extortion economy; can wait out foreign forces","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Gang Suppression Force","asset":"5,500 mandate + arrest/intel powers + UN-budget funding — the tool the MSS never had","str":"md"},{"actor":"United States","asset":"Force architect + financier ($300M+); shapes the mission while keeping an African-led face","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Chad","asset":"Lead troop contributor — converts the deployment into US diplomatic goodwill","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"UNSC Resolution 2793 (Oct 2025)","detail":"Transformed the Kenya-led MSS into the Gang Suppression Force; 5,500 ceiling, arrest powers, intel-led ops; adopted 12-0-3 (China/Russia/Pakistan abstained)"},{"src":"GSF command (Christofides / Batsuuri)","detail":"SA's Jack Christofides as Special Representative; Mongolia's Maj. Gen. Batsuuri as Force Commander; troop pledges exceeded the 5,500 ceiling"},{"src":"UN / OHCHR","detail":"~16,000 killed since 2022; 1.5M+ displaced; a UN report alleged sexual violence by Kenyan MSS members"}],"mil":"Haiti is effectively a state-collapse theater. The gang federation Viv Ansanm — a US-designated Foreign Terrorist Organization led by Jimmy 'Barbecue' Chérizier — controls an estimated 70-90% of Port-au-Prince and the strategic corridors into the Artibonite and Centre departments. The country has had no president since Moïse's 2021 assassination. The UN-authorized Gang Suppression Force (Chad, Kenya contingents) has begun combat operations but with fewer than 1,000 of a planned 5,550 troops, and is badly underfunded. Even the Defense Ministry's cabinet director was kidnapped.","pol":"UN figures: 2,300 killed and 1.5 million displaced in 2026 alone — more than 1 in 10 Haitians homeless. Guterres visited in June, citing 'glimmers of hope' against a harder reality. Crisis Group calls Viv Ansanm 'the most severe threat Haiti has faced in decades' and documents its ideological pivot — Chérizier reframing the gangs as anti-elite, anti-colonial 'revolutionaries,' even announcing a political party, while seeking a general amnesty as the transitional government winds down.","eco":"Extortion, kidnapping-for-ransom, and drug/arms trafficking are the core revenue; gang blockades of roads, the airport, and the seaport have isolated the country and choked humanitarian aid. In Artibonite, gangs burn crops and hound farmers.","assess":"Haiti holds critical (94) but the variable has shifted from pure collapse to a contested intervention. The UN-backed Gang Suppression Force — authorized by Resolution 2793 and replacing the ineffective Kenya-led MSS — is now deploying, with Chad the lead contributor (1,500 troops, first to arrive April 1) alongside pledges from Benin (up to 2,000), Bangladesh (1,500), and Mongolia (which supplies the force commander). Unlike its predecessor, the GSF can make arrests and run intelligence-led operations, and it is funded from the UN budget rather than voluntary contributions. Two things temper the hope: the force won't reach its 5,500 strength until late 2026, and the intervention is contested — China, Russia, and Pakistan abstained, warning the mandate is dangerously vague, against the backdrop of Haiti's scarring history of foreign missions (the MINUSTAH cholera outbreak, alleged MSS sexual violence). The Chad deployment also links to the Sudan theater: Chadian civil society questioned sending troops abroad while facing Sudan-war spillover on its own eastern border. Whether the GSF breaks the gangs' ~90% grip on the capital or becomes 'another false dawn' is the central question.","tclass":"conflict","tier":"CRITICAL","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","external":[{"actor":"🇹🇩 Chad","side":"GSF lead contributor","flow":"1,500 troops (2 battalions) — first to deploy (Apr 1); combat-experienced, French-speaking; at Vertières base","tier":"documented","for":"Reboot US military cooperation after the 2024 rupture (AFRICOM visits) — even as Chad takes ~$2B in UAE loans and hosts the UAE→RSF arms pipeline feeding the Sudan war on its other border"},{"actor":"🇧🇩 Bangladesh","side":"GSF contributor","flow":"1,500 police officers pledged","tier":"documented","for":"UN peacekeeping standing; longstanding blue-helmet contributor role"},{"actor":"🇧🇯 Benin","side":"GSF contributor","flow":"Pledged up to 2,000 soldiers (largest single pledge)","tier":"documented","for":"Regional/African security leadership; US alignment"},{"actor":"🇲🇳 Mongolia","side":"GSF command","flow":"Provides Force Commander Maj. Gen. Erdenebat Batsuuri (ex-UN Cyprus) + 100 personnel","tier":"documented","for":"UN peacekeeping profile"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"lead backer","flow":"Co-penned Res 2793; ~$300M+ pledged; logistics/finance/medical support — but African-led by design to avoid direct US footprint","tier":"documented","for":"Stabilize the hemisphere without US boots; Donroe-era regional order"},{"actor":"🇨🇳🇷🇺 China/Russia","side":"skeptics","flow":"Abstained on Res 2793; Russia calls mandate 'rushed,' warns of force abuses","tier":"documented","for":"Resist US-led hemispheric intervention precedent"}],"ext_flag":true,"econlev":[{"holder":"Gangs (~26 groups)","channel":"Territorial extortion","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Control ~90% of Port-au-Prince — taxing ports, roads, fuel, and commerce; the extortion economy IS the war economy"},{"holder":"United States","channel":"GSF funding + design","exp":"HIGH","detail":"$300M+ and the architecture of the force — shaping the intervention while keeping an African-led face"}],"contested":{"summary":"An African-led force, American-designed. Ask who wrote the mandate.","claims":[{"claim":"The Gang Suppression Force is an African-led intervention.","who":"The US — which architected and financed it ($300M+) while keeping its own boots off the ground.","evid":"True on its face — Chad leads with 1,500 troops, Benin pledged 2,000, Bangladesh 1,500, Mongolia commands. Also true: the US co-penned Resolution 2793 and pays for it. 'African-led' describes the personnel, not the authorship.","status":"CONTESTED — both framings are accurate; only one is offered"},{"claim":"China, Russia, and Pakistan abstained, calling the mandate 'rushed' and dangerously vague.","who":"Those states — resisting the precedent of US-designed hemispheric intervention.","evid":"The objection is self-interested AND substantive: the mandate authorizes force against 'anyone labelled with the vague term gangs.' Haiti's history (MINUSTAH cholera, ~10,000 dead; alleged MSS sexual violence) gives it weight.","status":"UNRESOLVED"}]},"history":{"roots":"Haiti's collapse is the endpoint of two centuries of extraction and instability. The world's first Black republic (1804) was forced to pay France a crippling 'independence debt' that beggared it for generations. US occupation, the Duvalier dictatorships, coups, and disasters followed. The 2021 assassination of President Moïse removed the last elected authority; since then gangs — armed and financed partly by political and business elites — have overrun Port-au-Prince, controlling ~85% of the capital. A Kenya-led international force has failed to reverse it. The roots: a state deliberately kept weak, elites who use gangs as tools, and an international community that intervenes episodically without fixing the underlying void.","timeline":[{"date":"1804","e":"Haiti wins independence — the first Black republic — after a slave revolution."},{"date":"1825","e":"France extorts a massive 'independence debt,' crippling Haiti's economy for over a century."},{"date":"1915-34","e":"US military occupation."},{"date":"1957-86","e":"Duvalier ('Papa/Baby Doc') dictatorships."},{"date":"2010","e":"Catastrophic earthquake kills ~250,000."},{"date":"7 Jul 2021","e":"President Jovenel Moïse assassinated; the last elected authority gone."},{"date":"2024","e":"Gangs overrun Port-au-Prince; a Kenya-led MSS force deploys."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Gangs control ~85% of the capital; the transitional council flounders."}],"actors":[{"name":"Viv Ansanm gang coalition","bio":"The alliance of gangs (led by figures like Jimmy 'Barbecue' Chérizier) controlling most of Port-au-Prince."},{"name":"Transitional Presidential Council","bio":"The unelected body meant to steer Haiti to elections; weak and internally divided."},{"name":"Kenya-led MSS","bio":"The UN-backed multinational security mission; under-resourced and unable to dislodge the gangs."},{"name":"Elites","bio":"Political and business figures who have historically armed and financed gangs as instruments of control."}],"bridge":"Haiti's present is the bill for being first: James showed the revolution that abolished slavery by arms; Trouillot showed how the world silenced it; the 1825 indemnity — independence ransomed at gunpoint, paid to the enslavers' heirs into the 20th century — is the quantified theft (the NYT reconstruction: billions in today's terms) beneath the 'poorest nation' framing. The gang order is not the absence of that history but its product: a state kept deliberately weak by ransom, occupation, and patronage now rented out to armed brokers, while the GSF reruns the century-old pattern of external forces substituting for the sovereignty the ransom foreclosed. The BINUH finding that 69% of casualties occur in security-force operations closes the circle: the cure and the disease share a payroll. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: Viv Ansanm holds most of the capital, an under-strength foreign force has stalled, and Erik Prince's company runs drone strikes under an unpublished contract. The 1825 indemnity's logic persists — Haiti still pays outsiders for the right to exist as a state.","sources":[{"work":"The Black Jacobins: Toussaint L'Ouverture and the San Domingo Revolution","author":"C.L.R. James","year":"1938","note":"The foundational history of the revolution that made Haiti first — and made it unforgivable."},{"work":"Silencing the Past: Power and the Production of History","author":"Michel-Rolph Trouillot","year":"1995","note":"The Haitian anthropologist on how the revolution was made unthinkable — the erasure this platform writes against."},{"work":"The Ransom (1825 indemnity reconstruction)","author":"New York Times investigative series","year":"2022","note":"The quantified primary accounting of the independence debt's extraction."},{"work":"BINUH quarterly reports","author":"UN Integrated Office in Haiti","year":"2024-26","note":"Primary casualty and rights documentation, including the security-force findings."}]},"human":{"dead":2300,"disp":1500000,"famine":true,"aid":"gang-taxed, 6.4M in need","note":"H1 2026 alone: 2,300+ killed and 1,100+ injured (UN). Nearly 1.5M displaced; 6.4M — over half the population — need humanitarian assistance. The decisive finding: over 60% of casualties (Jan 2025-Mar 2026) occurred during ANTI-GANG operations, and 1,243 died in 141 drone strikes including 17 children. The intervention is a leading cause of the toll it exists to reduce.","est":false,"basis":"UN Panel of Experts / BINUH incident counts"},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"A state kept deliberately weak by ransom and intervention, its violence now rented through armed brokers.","alt":"The gangs may now be autonomous of the elites who armed them — a self-sustaining war economy (kidnapping, port taxation) no elite settlement could switch off. If so, history explains origins but not current behavior.","why":"May's intra-Viv Ansanm fighting supports partial autonomy; the UN's finding that 60%+ of casualties occur during anti-gang operations — plus Erik Prince's unaccountable drone contract — supports the elite-entanglement reading in its harshest form: the counter-gang apparatus is itself a killing structure. MOD, with the GSF's ~1,000 of 5,550 personnel making the 'no actor can switch it off' reading stronger by the month."},"refs":[{"t":"UN OCHA via UN News · Haiti funding and returns","u":"https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/06/1167823"},{"t":"Security Council Report: Haiti July 2026 forecast","u":"https://www.securitycouncilreport.org/monthly-forecast/2026-07/haiti-35.php"},{"t":"PassBlue: GSF arriving as violence surges (Vectus/drone strikes)","u":"https://passblue.com/2026/05/21/the-new-un-backed-anti-gang-force-is-finally-arriving-in-haiti-as-violence-surges/"},{"t":"UN News: Guterres visit, H1 casualty figures","u":"https://news.un.org/en/story/2026/06/1167732"},{"t":"Haitian Times: DR rules out troops; Kenscoff displacement","u":"https://haitiantimes.com/2026/07/15/dominican-republic-humanitarian-not-operational-haiti-force/"},{"t":"BINUH reports (UN Haiti office)","u":"https://binuh.unmissions.org/en"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"94 = near-total loss of state control of the capital, an intervention force at 18% of authorised strength, and a private military contractor conducting lethal strikes under an unpublished contract. Scored on governance collapse, not battle intensity.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Four actors added incl. Vectus Global; GSF stall verdict recorded"}],"spill":[{"n":"United States","dir":"in","w":"Weapons trafficked overwhelmingly from Florida arm the gangs; Erik Prince's Vectus Global runs drone strikes under an unpublished contract.","id":"us-politics"},{"n":"Chad","dir":"in","w":"1,500 Chadian troops pledged to the Gang Suppression Force — a state that cannot secure Lake Chad exporting security to the Caribbean.","id":"chad"},{"n":"Jamaica","dir":"both","w":"The same Florida arms pipeline supplies both; one trafficking system, two national crises.","id":"jamaica"},{"n":"Dominican Republic","dir":"out","w":"Santo Domingo has ruled out contributing troops while absorbing the migration pressure."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Gang control of the capital, failed intervention","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation → state absence","up":["Viv Ansanm consolidates territory the GSF cannot contest","The under-strength force (1,000 of 5,550) fails to deploy","The Florida arms pipeline stays open","Private military contracting substitutes for a political solution"],"down":["A fully deployed, funded Gang Suppression Force","Interdicting the US-Florida weapons flow at source","A Haitian-led political settlement the intervention is meant to enable","Regional troop commitments actually materialising"],"fw":["conflict-curve","resource-conflict","proxy"]},"macro":{"growth":"Contracting under gang control","infl":"Elevated","src":"UN OCHA; compiled reporting, 2026","note":"The $880M humanitarian appeal is 27% FUNDED at $240M. Formal economic measurement is largely notional where an armed coalition controls most of the capital, taxes businesses at checkpoints and runs the ports' access. What the figures do capture: forced returns rising, with 24% of those deported being women and nearly 8% children — states returning people into conditions the funded response cannot meet."},"inherit":{"name":"The Independence Indemnity","est":"1825","status":"PAID IN FULL · EFFECTS IN FORCE","what":"After the only successful slave revolution in history, France sent warships to Port-au-Prince in 1825 and demanded 150 million francs — later reduced to 90 million — as compensation <b>to the enslavers for the loss of their property, meaning the people themselves</b>. Haiti paid to be recognised as a nation. To pay it, the young state borrowed from French banks, creating the <b>double debt</b>: the indemnity plus the loans taken to service it.","now":"<b>The final payments were made in 1947, 122 years later.</b> Haiti spent the bulk of its national revenue for over a century servicing a debt for its own freedom, in the decades when every other state was building roads, schools and ports. The institutional capacity Haiti lacks today was never destroyed — it was never funded, because the money went to Paris. <b>The structure persists in form:</b> a foreign security force operates on Haitian soil under contracts Haitians did not sign, and an American private military company runs drone strikes under terms that have not been published.","change":"France has acknowledged the indemnity and, in 2025, announced a commission on its consequences. <b>No restitution has been made.</b> Estimates of the transferred value run from $21 billion to over $40 billion in today's terms."},"ctrl":{"gov":20,"contested":20,"other":60,"est":true,"src":"Composite of UN and open-source reporting, 2026","note":"Viv Ansanm and allied gangs control most of Port-au-Prince including port access. State control is strongest outside the capital."},"control":{"gov":15,"contested":15,"other":70,"holders":"Viv Ansanm and allied gangs control most of Port-au-Prince and key road arteries. The state holds fragments of the capital and more of the countryside.","note":"The Gang Suppression Force has roughly 1,000 personnel deployed against 5,550 authorised.","est":true}},{"id":"jamaica","cat":"military","region":"Caribbean","subregion":"Caribbean","name":"Jamaica — Security Turnaround (Counter-Case)","sub":"31-year murder low · SoE model · fragile gains","domain":"Organized Crime / State Response","sev":"medium","status":"declining","since":"ongoing","esc":42,"lat":18.1,"lon":-77.3,"trend":"dn","w14":false,"esc_history":[42,42,42,42,42,42,42,42,42,42],"cas":"~673 murders 2025 (31-yr low, -43%); 33 in Jan 2026 (record-low month)","disp":"N/A","flag":"🇯🇲","note":"JUL 2026 — (checked) no discrete new development surfaced this cycle; the US-Florida weapons pipeline shared with Haiti and the garrison-politics legacy remain the structural picture. The Caribbean arms-trafficking system logged on the Haiti file is the shared variable to watch · MAR-JUL 2026 — THE CUBAN MEDICAL LEVER: Jamaica CANCELLED its cooperation agreement allowing Cuban medical professionals to work in the country (Mar 5) — Kingston bending to sustained US pressure (visa restrictions targeting officials linked to Cuba's overseas medical missions), a decision that removes staff from Jamaican clinics to make a hemispheric point · PM Andrew Holness holds a third term (JLP 34 seats to the PNP's 29, Sept 2025) · the security question stays the SOE model's durability against a US-sourced gun pipeline · NEW · COUNTER-CASE (de-escalating) · 31-year murder low: ~673 in 2025 (-43%), 33 in Jan 2026 = lowest month since records began 2001 · built on 20+ States of Emergency since 2018 · ACLED warns SoEs don't durably suppress gangs + carry police-brutality costs · still among highest per-capita murder rates in hemisphere","leverage":[{"actor":"Jamaican state (Holness)","asset":"Plan Secure Jamaica; SoE powers; JDF-JCF joint ops; institutional reform","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Gangs","asset":"Persistent but suppressed; extortion; deportee-linked networks","str":"md"},{"actor":"SoE mechanism","asset":"Temporary violence reduction — but ACLED: no durable gang suppression","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Jamaica OPM / JCF","detail":"PM Holness: 33 murders Jan 2026 = lowest month since 2001; Plan Secure Jamaica framework"},{"src":"STATIN / JIS","detail":"~673 murders 2025 = 31-year low, -43%; -32% shootings; official national statistics"},{"src":"US State Dept OSAC","detail":"JCF reported 666 murders Jan-Dec 27 2025 (-41.7%); still among highest per-capita in hemisphere"}],"mil":"Jamaica is deliberately included as the region's counter-case: not everything escalates. The Jamaica Constabulary Force and STATIN report a 31-year low of ~673 murders in 2025 (down 43%), and the Office of the Prime Minister confirmed 33 murders in January 2026 — the lowest monthly figure since national crime-data collection began in 2001. Shootings fell 32%, rape 28%.","pol":"PM Holness credits 'Plan Secure Jamaica,' a whole-of-government framework of enforcement, prevention, and institutional reform sustained since 2016. But the gains rest heavily on repeated States of Emergency — 20+ since 2018 — and ACLED's cross-Caribbean analysis warns SoEs produce temporary reductions without durably suppressing gangs, while carrying rising police-brutality and human-rights costs.","eco":"Improved security underpins tourism, investment, and growth — Holness frames it as central to development. The US State Dept notes non-violent crime (robbery, break-ins) actually rose even as violent crime fell.","assess":"Jamaica enters at 42, trend down — the platform's clearest de-escalation and a methodological anchor: it proves the framework tracks improvement, not just crisis. The turnaround is real and government-data-verified, but fragile — built on emergency powers ACLED judges non-durable, and still leaving Jamaica among the hemisphere's highest per-capita murder rates. The counter-case to Haiti's collapse next door.","tclass":"watch","tier":"GUARDED","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","econlev":[{"holder":"Jamaica","channel":"Counter-case","exp":"LOW","detail":"Tracked as the region's positive counter-example: homicides down sharply — proof that Caribbean state collapse is not inevitable"}],"contested":{"summary":"No significant narrative-war dimension identified in this theater at present. The main claims are broadly consistent with the evidence, or the disputes are policy disagreements rather than contested facts. This is stated affirmatively rather than left blank — the absence of an information operation is itself a finding.","claims":[]},"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"security partner","flow":"Anti-trafficking cooperation, deportation flows, and Caribbean Basin security assistance","tier":"documented","for":"Drug-transit interdiction and a stable Caribbean partner"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"infrastructure creditor","flow":"Road, port and infrastructure loans; Belt-and-Road-adjacent financing","tier":"documented","for":"A Caribbean logistics foothold and diplomatic alignment"}],"ext_flag":true,"history":{"roots":"Jamaica's security story is the long tail of Cold War-era garrison politics. In the 1970s-80s, the two parties armed Kingston's neighborhoods; the guns stayed when the ideology left, evolving into posses and dons whose transnational networks run drugs and guns between Kingston, Miami, and London. The state's answer — repeated states of emergency, the 2010 Tivoli incursion (~70+ dead) — brought episodic calm without dismantling the structure. Homicide rates remain among the world's highest per capita, driven by US-sourced firearms. The 2024-26 period brought real declines under sustained SOEs and international gun-interdiction — progress that is genuine and reversible.","timeline":[{"date":"1962","e":"Independence from Britain."},{"date":"1970s-80s","e":"Garrison politics — the parties arm the neighborhoods."},{"date":"2010","e":"Tivoli Gardens incursion to extradite Dudus Coke — ~70+ dead."},{"date":"2018-","e":"Rolling states of emergency become standing policy."},{"date":"2024-26","e":"Homicides decline markedly; US gun-pipeline interdiction; the durability question."},{"date":"2010","e":"Tivoli Gardens incursion: 70+ killed as security forces seek Christopher 'Dudus' Coke for US extradition — the garrison system's violent apex."},{"date":"2018-2024","e":"Recurrent states of emergency; homicide rates among the world's highest despite declared reductions."},{"date":"2024","e":"Hurricane Beryl compounds economic strain on communities already under gang governance."},{"date":"2026","e":"Weapons trafficked from Florida continue arming both Jamaican networks and Haitian gangs — one pipeline, two crises."}],"actors":[{"name":"The gangs / posses","bio":"The garrison inheritance — transnational, gun-fed, politically descended and now politically orphaned."},{"name":"The state (Holness)","bio":"SOEs as governance — effective suppression, contested rights record, unresolved roots."},{"name":"The US gun pipeline","bio":"The supply side — most murder weapons trace to US purchases; interdiction is the real security policy."},{"name":"The diaspora economy","bio":"Remittances and deportee flows — the transnational circuit the violence rides."}],"bridge":"Gray's 'demeaned but empowered' captures the garrison inheritance exactly: the parties armed the poor to fight their elections, and the poor kept the arms when the parties no longer needed the fighting — the dons becoming what Thomas calls the state's exceptional violence privatized. The Tivoli Commission's own findings (the 2010 incursion's civilian toll, unaccounted) are the primary record that the cure shares the disease's methods. The current homicide decline is real; whether it is dismantlement or another suppression cycle is the question the SOE model has never yet answered — and the US gun pipeline it cannot answer alone. · AND NOW, 2026: Weapons trafficked from Florida continue arming both Jamaican networks and Haitian gangs — one pipeline, two crises.","sources":[{"work":"Demeaned but Empowered: The Social Power of the Urban Poor in Jamaica","author":"Obika Gray","year":"2004","note":"The Jamaican political scientist on garrison politics from below."},{"work":"Exceptional Violence: Embodied Citizenship in Transnational Jamaica","author":"Deborah A. Thomas","year":"2011","note":"The scholarly frame for state violence and the dons' order."},{"work":"West Kingston Commission of Enquiry report","author":"Government of Jamaica COE","year":"2016","note":"The primary official record of the 2010 Tivoli incursion."}]},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"The garrison inheritance — parties armed the poor, the poor kept the arms, dons became privatized state violence.","alt":"A transnational-crime reading centers the US gun pipeline and drug transshipment as the primary driver over the domestic garrison-politics history.","why":"Both feed it; the platform holds garrison politics for origins and the gun pipeline for supply. MOD."},"refs":[{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"},{"t":"CFR · Global Conflict Tracker","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker"}],"human":{"dead":1200,"disp":0,"famine":false,"aid":"n/a","note":"Among the world's highest per-capita homicide rates, though declining sharply under sustained states of emergency. Most murder weapons trace to US firearm purchases — the supply side of the toll.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"42 = active armed conflict, a recorded human toll, trending DOWN over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Timeline seeded; gang networks added to Groups with US weapons-pipeline link"}],"spill":[{"n":"Haiti","id":"haiti","dir":"both","w":"The SAME Florida arms pipeline supplies both — one trafficking system producing two national crises. The clearest case on this board of a shared cause with separate symptoms."},{"n":"United States","dir":"in","w":"The weapons flow from Florida, and the deportation of criminal deportees into a system with limited reintegration capacity."}],"inherit":{"name":"Post-Emancipation Land Denial","est":"1838-1865","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Emancipation in 1838 freed the enslaved and <b>compensated the enslavers</b> — the British government borrowed $20 million to pay them, a debt UK taxpayers finished servicing in 2015. Freed people received nothing and were deliberately kept from land: Crown land was priced out of reach and vagrancy laws pushed labour back to the estates. When the landless protested at Morant Bay in 1865, Governor Eyre's suppression killed hundreds and Jamaica's assembly was abolished in favour of direct Crown rule.","now":"<b>The landless urban concentration this produced is the origin of the garrison.</b> Political parties organised those communities from the 1960s through housing and jobs in exchange for votes, arming enforcers who outlived their patrons and became autonomous criminal structures. <b>The weapons now come overwhelmingly from Florida</b> — the same pipeline that arms Haiti's gangs, which is why this board treats them as one trafficking system producing two national crises.","change":"Jamaica has pursued states of emergency and anti-gang legislation. <b>Neither addresses the land and opportunity structure the garrisons grew from, nor the external arms supply that makes them lethal.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"InSight Crime · Caribbean","u":"https://insightcrime.org/"},{"t":"UNODC · homicide and firearms data","u":"https://www.unodc.org/"},{"t":"ACLED · conflict event data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"},{"t":"Human Rights Watch · country reporting","u":"https://www.hrw.org/"}],"macrona":"DELIBERATELY EMPTY. A national growth rate would not illuminate this file, whose subject is garrison politics and an arms pipeline shared with Haiti. The relevant economics are the Florida trafficking system, tracked on the Financial Plumbing board."},{"id":"south-africa","cat":"political","region":"Southern Africa","subregion":"Southern Africa","name":"South Africa — Xenophobic Mobilization","sub":"Jun 30 deadline passed without mass eruption · mobilization persists","domain":"Xenophobic Unrest / Political Violence","sev":"high","status":"volatile","since":"2008","esc":58,"lat":-26.2,"lon":28,"trend":"dn","w14":false,"esc_history":[61,59,59,58,58,58,58,58,58,58],"cas":"5 Ethiopians killed (3 in a McDonald's); 4+ dead recent unrest; 62 killed in 2008","disp":"Thousands displaced; camps in Durban/Cape Town","flag":"🇿🇦","note":"JUL 2026 — THE ANXIETY IS REGIONAL NOW: the Africa Intelligence Brief records 'deepening anxiety over xenophobic violence in South Africa' as one of the continent's defining weekly themes, alongside Tanzania's political crackdown, cost-of-living protests in Kenya and currency panic in Zimbabwe · the spillover inversion this file documented is now being read continent-wide rather than bilaterally · JUL 2026 — ANTI-MIGRANT VIOLENCE BECOMES A FOREIGN-POLICY CRISIS: protesters PULLED UNDOCUMENTED FOREIGN NATIONALS FROM THEIR HOMES; more than 900 ARRESTED, foreign nationals fleeing their homes, and NIGERIA CONFIRMED TWO OF ITS NATIONALS KILLED · the unrest is STRAINING TIES with Nigeria, Zimbabwe, Mozambique and Ghana, and the SA-Nigeria diplomatic fallout is deepening · Ramaphosa's planned visit to Accra was postponed — both governments deny xenophobia was the reason, citing SADC scheduling · THE INVERSION THIS PLATFORM SHOULD NAME: South Africa is usually the destination in the region's migration story; here it is EXPORTING a diplomatic crisis to the states whose citizens it is attacking — spillover running outward from the regional pole · the Reserve Bank LEFT INTEREST RATES UNCHANGED (23 Jul); a public commission is hearing claims of a plot to pressure the former intelligence watchdog, with EFF leader Malema filing a sworn denial · JUL 17 2026 — STAGE SIX: Eskom escalated loadshedding to stage six after multiple generating units tripped, cutting 8,000 MEGAWATTS from the grid and leaving some households without power for up to ten hours; business associations warn of technical-recession risk · separately Pretoria secured major financing from the BRICS NEW DEVELOPMENT BANK for municipal infrastructure — the alternative-lender route in practice · the electricity crisis remains the clearest daily proof of the file's thesis: political freedom delivered, the economic machine inherited and not rebuilt · Government declared Jun 30 \"a normal working day\" — no national shutdown materialized · rhetoric moderated after govt warnings (Kubayi) · toll to date: 3 foreign nationals killed, journalists assaulted, thousands repatriated · SAPS ~R600M deployment + Fidelity private-security backup · fact-checkers flagged AI-GENERATED fake government notices endorsing the deadline · Wits (Landau): blame what govt has/hasn't done \"since 2008\"","leverage":[{"actor":"March&March / Operation Dudula","asset":"Street mobilization; 'arrests'/paper-checks; June 30 ultimatum; online amplification","str":"md"},{"actor":"MK Party (Zuma) / ActionSA","asset":"Legitimizing anti-migrant agenda; KZN electoral mobilization ahead of 2026 locals","str":"md"},{"actor":"GNU state (Ramaphosa)","asset":"SAPS deployment; but Union Buildings meeting legitimized the groups (Wits critique)","str":"md"},{"actor":"Sending states (Nigeria etc.)","asset":"Evacuations; diplomatic/continental backlash on trade/tourism","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"SAPS / acting Police Min Cachalia","detail":"Police leave cancelled, resources deployed ahead of June 30; officers deployed to affected areas"},{"src":"President Ramaphosa / Justice Min Kubayi","detail":"June 30 'a normal working day'; violence 'not justified'; National Action Plan on Racism/Xenophobia"},{"src":"Union Buildings meeting (25 May)","detail":"Ministers met March&March leadership — Wits: this legitimized the groups"},{"src":"SA Government (official X)","detail":"'Tuesday 30 June 2026 remains a normal working day' — dismissed shutdown; flagged AI-generated fake notices with govt insignia"}],"mil":"South Africa faces its most serious xenophobic mobilization since 2008. Movements March and March and Operation Dudula have set a June 30 deadline (today) for undocumented migrants to leave, conducting unauthorized street 'arrests' and paper-checks. Five Ethiopians were killed (three shot inside a McDonald's), Malawians and Zimbabweans have fled to displacement camps, and Nigeria is evacuating 271+ citizens. SAPS cancelled police leave and deployed ahead of the deadline.","pol":"The analytical core, from Wits University's Xenowatch researchers, is that South African xenophobia is not a spontaneous grassroots eruption but a political enterprise co-produced by vigilante groups and the state — through acts of commission and omission. March and March has clear links to Zuma's MK Party (projected to surge in KZN), and the May 25 Union Buildings ministerial meeting with the group's leadership is criticized as lending it legitimacy. The National Action Plan on Racism 'contains almost no plan.'","eco":"Drivers are structural: 30%+ unemployment (60%+ youth), unresolved apartheid-era inequality. The continental backlash threatens tourism, trade, and investment in Africa's largest economy. Data shows migrants are not the source of crime or job loss the movements claim.","assess":"South Africa eases to 59 — the feared 2008-scale eruption did not materialize on the deadline, thanks to a R600M security deployment, cancelled police leave, and late rhetorical moderation by organizers. But the de-escalation is tactical, not structural: three people are dead, thousands repatriated, the movements retain their MK/ActionSA political cover, and the AI-generated fake-government-notice campaign shows an information-warfare layer now attached to the xenophobic mobilization. The 2026 local elections remain the driver.","econlev":[{"holder":"South Africa","channel":"PGMs + G20 standing","exp":"MED","detail":"Platinum-group metals + the continent's deepest capital markets; hosts the 2025-26 G20 presidency"}],"tclass":"watch","tier":"ELEVATED","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Political","contested":{"summary":"South Africa is the subject of one of the most internationally amplified narrative disputes on this board — over whether its farm violence constitutes targeted persecution.","claims":[{"claim":"White South African farmers are victims of a targeted campaign amounting to genocide.","who":"Amplified by US political figures and international media; formally rejected by the South African government.","evid":"South African Police Service statistics record farm murders as a small fraction of a national homicide total exceeding 27,000 a year, with victims of all races; researchers including AfriForum and independent criminologists disagree on classification but the per-capita rate does not support a genocide finding under the Convention's definition. A South African court has ruled the 'Kill the Boer' chant is not incitement. The claim has nonetheless driven US refugee-status decisions and diplomatic pressure.","status":"CONTRADICTED-BY-DATA"},{"claim":"Land expropriation legislation amounts to uncompensated seizure.","who":"International critics; disputed by Pretoria.","evid":"The 2024 Expropriation Act permits nil compensation only in narrowly specified circumstances and remains subject to judicial review; no farms have been seized under it. Roughly 70% of freehold farmland remained white-owned three decades after apartheid — the unresolved fact the legislation responds to.","status":"OVERSTATED-BUT-NOT-BASELESS"}]},"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"strained","flow":"AGOA-access tensions, friction over the ICJ Israel case and non-alignment; tariff and sanctions signaling","tier":"documented","for":"Leverage over a BRICS swing state and access to platinum-group metals"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China / BRICS","side":"aligned","flow":"Largest trading partner; BRICS-plus anchor; infrastructure and mineral offtake","tier":"documented","for":"A G20 African anchor and PGM/manganese supply"},{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"partner","flow":"Diplomatic alignment, naval exercises, and non-condemnation over Ukraine","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"A sympathetic voice in African and BRICS forums"}],"ext_flag":true,"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Platinum-group metals","stage":"mine","actor":"Bushveld Complex — Rustenburg, Mogalakwena","detail":"South Africa holds ~90% of world PGM reserves and produces most global platinum, palladium, rhodium (Anglo American Platinum/Valterra, Impala, Sibanye-Stillwater). PGMs are essential for catalytic converters, hydrogen fuel cells, and electronics — a genuine near-monopoly.","who":"SA majors mine it; deep, capital-intensive, labour-heavy operations","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"PGMs","stage":"process","actor":"Smelted/refined largely in South Africa","detail":"Unlike the DRC (China-refined) or Indonesia (China-refined), SA retains much of its own PGM refining — the value-chain position the resource-nationalist states envy. This is the counter-example: an African producer that kept the midstream.","who":"SA refiners retain the processing margin — the model the DRC and Mali want","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"PGMs","stage":"mid","actor":"Refined metal → global autocatalyst + H2 supply","detail":"Ships to the auto industry (catalytic converters), the hydrogen economy (fuel-cell/electrolyser catalysts), and electronics. As a BRICS swing state, SA sits between Western buyers and Chinese offtake — a leverage position over the energy transition's precious-metal inputs.","who":"Contest: Western autos + H2 vs Chinese offtake; SA plays both as a BRICS anchor","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"PGMs","stage":"end","actor":"Catalytic converters · fuel cells · jewellery","detail":"The end products are the emissions and hydrogen technologies of the transition. SA's near-monopoly gives it real leverage — the platinum counterpart to Indonesia's nickel and the DRC's cobalt, but with the refining kept onshore.","tier":"documented"}],"history":{"roots":"South Africa's inequality is not apartheid's residue — it is apartheid's economy, politically renovated. Terreblanche's history runs the long arc: three centuries of land seizure (codified in the 1913 Natives Land Act reserving 93% of land for whites), the mining revolution built on the migrant-labor compound system that priced Black labor below subsistence, and grand apartheid as that labor system's constitutional form. Biko's insight — that the system's deepest work was psychological — and the liberation movement's answer (the Freedom Charter's 'the people shall share in the country's wealth') framed a struggle that won the state in 1994 but negotiated away the economy: the transition entrenched property, moved capital offshore, and swapped broad transformation for a narrow Black elite — the compromise Fanon's chapter on the national bourgeoisie had predicted for every decolonization. The world's highest Gini coefficient, 33% unemployment, and the failing grid are that bargain's balance sheet.","timeline":[{"date":"1948-94","e":"Apartheid — and the long liberation struggle."},{"date":"1994","e":"Mandela's election — the political miracle."},{"date":"2009-18","e":"Zuma era: state capture hollows the state."},{"date":"2024","e":"ANC loses its majority — the GNU coalition era begins."},{"date":"2023-26","e":"ICJ case against Israel; BRICS expansion host; US relations strained (AGOA, land-policy friction)."},{"date":"May 2024","e":"ANC loses its parliamentary majority for the first time since 1994; Government of National Unity formed with the DA."},{"date":"2025","e":"Load-shedding persists as Eskom's generation fleet ages; US tariff and diplomatic pressure over land policy and the ICJ Gaza case."},{"date":"17 Jul 2026","e":"Eskom escalates to STAGE SIX — 8,000 MW off the grid, blackouts up to ten hours, technical-recession warnings; Pretoria turns to the BRICS New Development Bank for municipal infrastructure finance."}],"actors":[{"name":"Cyril Ramaphosa / the GNU","bio":"The president managing a coalition of former enemies — reform promises against a patronage machine's inertia."},{"name":"The ANC","bio":"The liberation party at its weakest — still the system's center of gravity, no longer its owner."},{"name":"The US","bio":"The strained partner — AGOA access, the ICJ case, and non-alignment friction under a transactional Washington."},{"name":"BRICS / China","bio":"The chosen lane — largest trading partner and the multipolar identity Pretoria leads Africa's wing of."}],"bridge":"Every current South African fault line prices the unfinished economics: land reform stalled at a fraction of targets (the 1913 Act's map essentially intact), 'state capture' as the elite-enrichment bargain metastasized, xenophobic violence as the poor fighting the poorer for scraps, and the ANC's lost majority as the electorate's verdict on thirty years of political freedom without economic transformation. Abroad, the ICJ genocide case and BRICS anchoring are the liberation inheritance turned outward — the Freedom Charter's internationalism as foreign policy — which is precisely why Washington's pressure on Pretoria reads, from here, as the old demand that Africa's politics stay junior to its minerals. The PGM near-monopoly remains the quiet counter-leverage. · AND NOW, 17 JUL 2026: Eskom escalates to STAGE SIX — 8,000 MW off the grid, blackouts up to ten hours, technical-recession warnings; Pretoria turns to the BRICS New Development Bank for municipal infrastructure finance.","sources":[{"work":"A History of Inequality in South Africa, 1652–2002","author":"Sampie Terreblanche","year":"2002","note":"The long-arc economic history — land, labor compounds, and the negotiated transition's costs."},{"work":"I Write What I Like","author":"Steve Biko","year":"1978","note":"Primary texts of Black Consciousness — the psychological dimension of the system and its answer."},{"work":"The Freedom Charter","author":"Congress of the People, Kliptown","year":"1955","note":"The primary programmatic text — the economic clauses against which the transition is measured."},{"work":"The Wretched of the Earth (ch. 'The Pitfalls of National Consciousness')","author":"Frantz Fanon","year":"1961","note":"The predicted capture of liberation by a national bourgeoisie — the transition's sharpest mirror."},{"work":"Truth and Reconciliation Commission Report","author":"TRC","year":"1998-2003","note":"Primary record — including the institutional-hearings volumes on business and apartheid the settlement left unenforced."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"Apartheid's economy politically renovated — political freedom without economic transformation.","alt":"A governance-failure reading centers ANC corruption and mismanagement as the primary cause of decline over the structural-economy inheritance.","why":"Both operate; the platform holds the structural frame for the inequality floor and governance failure for the acceleration. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"Africa Intelligence Brief (24 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.riotimesonline.com/africa-intelligence-brief-friday-july-24-2026/"},{"t":"Africa Intelligence Brief (24 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.riotimesonline.com/africa-intelligence-brief-friday-july-24-2026/"},{"t":"Africa Risk Consulting · Weekly Chronologies","u":"https://www.africariskconsulting.com/chronologies/weekly"},{"t":"Africa Intelligence Brief, 18 Jul 2026: Eskom stage six, NDB","u":"https://www.riotimesonline.com/africa-intelligence-brief-saturday-july-18-2026/"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"58 = institutional or electoral instability, trending DOWN over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Eskom stage six; NDB financing; timeline brought current from 2024"}],"spill":[{"n":"Nigeria","id":"nigeria","dir":"out","w":"THE INVERSION: anti-migrant violence killed two Nigerian nationals and is straining ties. South Africa is usually the destination in the region's migration story — here it EXPORTS a diplomatic crisis to the states whose citizens it is attacking."},{"n":"Mozambique","id":"mozambique","dir":"out","w":"Mozambican nationals among those targeted; the regional pole's unrest reaching Maputo's citizens."},{"n":"Ghana","id":"ghana","dir":"out","w":"Ramaphosa's Accra visit postponed — both governments citing SADC scheduling rather than the tensions."},{"n":"Zimbabwe","dir":"in","w":"The migration inflow that the violence targets — driven by economic collapse next door."}],"macro":{"growth":"1.3% (2025), revised down for 2026","infl":"Rates held unchanged, July 2026","src":"World Bank GEP June 2026; SA Reserve Bank","note":"Growth rose to 1.3% on more reliable electricity supply, a strong harvest and improved business confidence — then the World Bank revised the outlook DOWN specifically on the Middle East conflict's impact, alongside Nigeria. The continent's most industrialised economy is also its most exposed to an oil shock it has no part in, which is the transmission this board tracks from the Gulf to African household costs."},"inherit":{"name":"The 1913 Natives Land Act","est":"1913","status":"REPEALED · DISTRIBUTION IN FORCE","what":"The Natives Land Act reserved <b>7% of South Africa for the African majority</b> — later raised to 13% — and made it illegal for Black South Africans to buy or lease land outside those reserves. It was the legal foundation of the migrant labour system: dispossess the countryside, and the mines get workers who must come.","now":"<b>The Act is repealed and the distribution it created substantially remains.</b> Land reform has transferred a fraction of the target, and the ownership pattern of commercial farmland still tracks the 1913 line closely. The migrant labour system it was built to feed also persists in the mining economy's structure. <b>The anti-migrant violence this board tracks is happening inside an economy still shaped by a law designed to guarantee cheap labour.</b>","change":"Expropriation legislation and land-reform programmes continue and are politically contested. <b>South Africa is now exporting a crisis to the region rather than absorbing one</b> — 900+ arrested, two Nigerians killed, Ramaphosa's Accra visit postponed."},"monitors":[{"t":"allAfrica · SA Reserve Bank rate decision (23 Jul 2026)","u":"https://allafrica.com/"},{"t":"ISS Africa: Southern Africa","u":"https://issafrica.org/"}]},{"id":"iran-us","cat":"military","region":"Middle East","subregion":"Middle East","name":"US–Israel vs. Iran — Spike & Settle","sub":"IRAN DECLARES HORMUZ CLOSED · firing on ships · US hits 140 targets (3rd round) · Jordan base struck","domain":"Interstate War → Managed Standoff","sev":"critical","status":"active","since":"Feb 28 2026","esc":97,"lat":32,"lon":51,"trend":"up","w14":false,"disputed":true,"exchange_active":true,"diplomacy_active":true,"esc_history":[79,79,78,82,88,92,89,94,96,97],"cas":"13 US service members killed to date; 1 Iranian navy Lt killed at Jask; thousands of Iranian/Lebanese civilians killed; 1 Indian seafarer missing","disp":"Shipping caution; demining disputed","flag":"🌐","note":"31 JUL-1 AUG 2026 — FIVE MONTHS IN, AND NO INTENTION OF STOPPING: Trump indicated ongoing strikes on Iran are unlikely to end soon, telegraphing continued attacks until Tehran returns to talks — 'We'll be hitting them very hard and, you know, at some point, they're going to say, we just can't take it anymore' · THE TIMELINE THIS CONFIRMS: the war began 28 FEBRUARY 2026 and is now FIVE MONTHS OLD; a ceasefire signed in JUNE fell apart weeks ago; KHAMENEI WAS KILLED IN THE OPENING US-ISRAEL STRIKE OF 28 FEBRUARY · in Tehran on 1 Aug, crowds rallied beneath billboards linking Khamenei's death to Imam Hussein and calling for revenge — the succession is being framed through martyrdom rather than institutional continuity, which forecloses the pragmatic-successor scenario this board had left open · THE STATED STRATEGY IS ATTRITION TO THE TABLE, which is the theory of victory the platform's own Iran read should be tested against: five months of it has produced a collapsed ceasefire, a widened war, and a strike campaign CENTCOM itself judged to have hit its effectiveness limit · 29-31 JUL 2026 — THE PAUSE ENDED AND THE WAR WIDENED. CORRECTION TO THIS FILE: on 28 Jul this board recorded the US halting strikes on 23 Jul, CENTCOM judging the Hormuz campaign at the limit of its effectiveness, and Iran halting retaliation. ALL OF THAT HAS REVERSED · the Pentagon says it struck DOZENS OF IRGC TARGETS INSIDE IRAN — command centres and drone facilities — in retaliation for Tehran targeting US BASES IN JORDAN; Iranian state media report a couple and their TWO-YEAR-OLD CHILD killed on QESHM ISLAND · a further sanctions round designated 10 ENTITIES AND EIGHT TANKERS · CNN's framing: the conflict is 'increasingly pulling in other countries in the Middle East and beyond' · a former US Defense Secretary says the US is 'LOCKED INTO AN UNWINNABLE WAR' · THE LESSON FOR THIS PLATFORM: a five-day operational pause was read here as a possible inflection. It was a resupply and retargeting interval. Both the reading and its falsification stay on the record. · 23-27 JUL 2026 — THE BOMBING STOPPED, AND THE REASON IS THE STORY: the US HALTED its nightly strikes on THURSDAY 23 JUL and had not resumed by 27 Jul — ending a nearly two-week campaign. IRAN THEN HALTED ITS RETALIATORY STRIKES · per Axios sourcing, Trump's decision followed a meeting in which CENTCOM commander ADM. BRAD COOPER RECOMMENDED STOPPING THE CAMPAIGN AROUND THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ BECAUSE IT HAD REACHED THE LIMIT OF ITS EFFECTIVENESS — with roughly 20% of targets unstruck · separately, officials warned Trump about DWINDLING MUNITIONS STOCKPILES · UN Ambassador Waltz: Trump is 'giving talks some space'; OMAN and Iran are engaged 'at every level' · Trump: 'We're talking to them right now. I think they're getting more and more serious as the days go by. We're locked and loaded and ready to go, but we're talking to them' · US casualties now listed under a new 'OVERSEAS OPERATIONS' category: FOUR ARMY DEATHS NAMED and 207 WOUNDED · CORRECTION TO THIS FILE'S TIMELINE: the war began 28 FEBRUARY 2026, and the opening US-Israeli strikes KILLED KHAMENEI ALONG WITH SEVERAL OTHER OFFICIALS — this board recorded his death only when the Najaf funeral rites were reported in July, five months later. Stated US justifications for the war included response to the protest crackdown, non-proliferation, regime change, AND 'TAKING IRAN'S OIL AND GAS RESOURCES' — the last of which belongs on this platform's extraction ledger, not just its security one · 22-25 JUL 2026 — NEARING TWO WEEKS, AND THE BILL ARRIVES: CNN marks the escalation approaching its TWO-WEEK MARK · the Trump administration is SEEKING BILLIONS MORE FROM CONGRESS for the war, per Defense Secretary Hegseth · RUBIO MET LAVROV on the sidelines of the ASEAN Foreign Ministers Meeting in Manila (23 Jul), saying Trump was committed to the work 'if conditions and factors have changed to make that possible' — a Russian channel opening alongside the Pakistani, Qatari and Omani ones · EU foreign policy chief KAJA KALLAS warned the world must not lose sight of IRAN'S REPRESSION OF ITS OWN PEOPLE amid the war — the dimension a purely geopolitical read omits · JUL 2026 — KHAMENEI IS DEAD: Iraqi state television reports that the remains of FORMER SUPREME LEADER ALI KHAMENEI have arrived in NAJAF for viewing, following funeral ceremonies in TEHRAN and QOM · this platform had not previously recorded the succession — it is logged here as a correction. The consequences run through every file that assumed continuity of Iranian command: the strike campaign, the Houthi relationship, the nuclear question and the Doha track all now sit under a leadership transition whose outcome is unresolved · IRANIAN DRONES REACHED ERBIL on 22 Jul — three intercepted near the US Consulate General, one downed near Harir Air Base · the Pakistani mediation track continues · 20-22 JUL 2026 — THE HOUTHIS DECLARE A SAUDI BLOCKADE, PAKISTAN MEDIATES: Ansar Allah has ANNOUNCED A BLOCKADE OF SAUDI ARABIA as the US launched a further wave of strikes on Iran · a senior IRANIAN OFFICIAL held talks in PAKISTAN — now acting as a mediator — aimed at reviving the COLLAPSED CEASEFIRE; the 17 June memorandum of understanding and the Doha indirect technical track are the framework being salvaged · BRENT CRUDE ABOVE $90/BARREL and US pump prices at $4.00/gallon, four months before the midterms · a US service member was killed in IRAQ during the controlled detonation of a downed Iranian drone · the mediator list now runs Qatar, Oman and Pakistan — no Western state is trusted by both parties · JUL 20 — NINTH STRAIGHT DAY; US DEATHS AT 18; WATER STRUCK IN BOTH DIRECTIONS: three more US service members killed over the weekend — two (from Hawaii and Texas) in an Iranian missile attack on a base in JORDAN, a third in northern Iraq during a controlled detonation of a downed Iranian drone; CENTCOM recovered the remains of the missing soldier. TOTAL US MILITARY DEAD SINCE FEBRUARY: 18 · THE MIRROR: on Saturday US forces struck a WATER DESALINATION PLANT at Bonji in southern Iran, cutting drinking water for ~10,000 people across nearly two dozen villages — three weeks after Iran struck Kuwait's desalination plant. Water infrastructure is now a two-way target set, which is the pattern this platform flagged when only one side was doing it · INFRASTRUCTURE TALLY since Wednesday (Drop Site): NINE BRIDGES, two airports, a railroad junction, a road tunnel · the IAEA is investigating reports of a strike on the construction site of the planned DARKHOVIN nuclear power plant — attacking a civil nuclear build is a threshold the Agency has no precedent for · the IRGC says it hit a US command centre in Syria · the State Department has warned Americans abroad to stay cautious · JUL 18-19 — THE MoU HAS COLLAPSED; RED LINES CROSSED BOTH WAYS: a week after the preliminary deal was signed, an Iranian drone hit a cargo ship in the strait (Jun 25) — the chain of strike and counterstrike since has 'chipped away at the pillars of the agreement, which has now collapsed', though salvage efforts continue (NPR, Jul 18) · Saturday saw both sides exchanging strikes on INFRASTRUCTURE and military targets simultaneously — the mutual-infrastructure phase is now the norm, not the exception · THREAT TO WIDEN: Mohsen Rezaei, adviser to Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei, warned that 'no political border will provide security against Iran's offensive forces' if US strikes continue — an explicit signal that Iranian retaliation may cross beyond the Gulf theatre · Trump continues to frame the campaign as a 'military skirmish' while conceding it has far exceeded the 4-6 week timeline his administration set ('We were in Vietnam for 19 years') and saying of Iran's remaining leadership: 'we certainly are watching' · JUL 17 — INFRASTRUCTURE-WARFARE PHASE + THE 60-DAY CLOCK: both sides now hitting civilian infrastructure — US struck BRIDGES, energy sites, an airport, a railway station and collapsed a port tower in Iran (7th consecutive night); Iran hit a Kuwaiti POWER + WATER DESALINATION plant (Kuwait draws ~90% of drinking water from desal) + struck Qatar and Bahrain · CONTEXT THE FILE UNDER-WEIGHTED: a Jun 17 US-Iran MoU (Pakistan-mediated, signed Trump-Pezeshkian) set a 60-DAY negotiating window on the nuclear file — Fri Jul 17 is the HALFWAY point, and it has been marked by war, not talks; IAEA's Birol warns on oil flows; Pakistan still pushing to resume Islamabad Talks · blockade tally (CENTCOM): 85 vessels intercepted, 3 seized, ~$500M/day cost to Iran; Lloyd's: 26 vessels bypassed · 12 seafarers killed/missing; India (300k+ of world's seafarers) barred its sailors from Hormuz transits after an Indian engineer died on the MV GFS Galaxy · JUL 17 — SIXTH CONSECUTIVE NIGHT: US completed its latest strike wave 9:40 PM ET — bombing now reaching DEEP inside Iran, beyond the southern coast (Chabahar port hit, smoke column verified) · Iran calls Hormuz an 'UNBREAKABLE RED LINE'; foreign ministry: no plans for negotiations · Gulf splash zone widens: Qatar intercepted a missile over Doha tonight, Jordan's Prince Hassan Air Base struck, UAE/Bahrain under drone-missile threats, EASA warns airlines off parts of Gulf airspace · BLOCKADE METRICS: first blockade (Apr 13–Jun 18) intercepted 140+ vessels and cost Iran ~$4.8B in oil revenue by May 1 (DoD); the reinstated blockade redirected 2 vessels in its first 17 hours and disabled a tanker running for Kharg Island · counter-signals: Iran RELEASED an American detainee (Dec 2024 case) as a 'goodwill gesture'; Vance publicly ruled OUT ground troops/regime change ('we're not in that business anymore') — coercion capped below invasion · JUL 16 — AROUND-THE-CLOCK PHASE: US military now striking Iran continuously, 'stepping up the pace' — framed as protecting Hormuz shipping after Iranian attacks on vessels · IRGC threatens to choke off ALL the region's oil and gas — escalation from Hormuz-only to a total regional energy threat · Iran firing missiles at neighboring US bases · Iran ramping up EXECUTIONS of political prisoners amid the war (CNN/human-rights data) — the domestic terror dimension rising with the external one · JUL 15 — TOLL DEAD, BLOCKADE BACK, STRIKES RESUMED: Trump DROPPED the 20% Hormuz transit-fee plan after Gulf 'kings and emirs' offered to 'invest billions in the US' instead — the toll monetization replaced by Gulf capital commitments ('I don't think anybody should be able to charge a fee for the strait') · US REIMPOSED its naval blockade on Iran and ran a FOURTH round of overnight strikes into Jul 15 — an army barracks hit, ≥7 troops killed, ~260 wounded across the country (Iranian figures) · 'We Kill Trump' billboard with Trump-in-coffin imagery up in Tehran's Islamic Revolution Sq — regime signaling, not fringe · JUL 13: Hormuz closure holding; the Houthi declaration of a simultaneous Bab el-Mandeb closure extends Iran's chokepoint leverage to a second waterway without Tehran firing another shot · Oman strait-management proposal remains the one live diplomatic track · HIGHEST ESCALATION YET (Jul 11-12): IRGC DECLARED THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ CLOSED — fired warning shots + struck at least 2 vessels ('violating regulations'); a container ship was damaged and abandoned, 1 Indian national missing · US/CENTCOM/JMIC insist the strait is OPEN (southern Oman-coast route) — but traffic has reduced to A TRICKLE · US 3rd round of strikes this week: 140 targets (missile/drone sites, naval capabilities, ammo storage, comms networks, coastal surveillance) using ships, fighters, drones; also struck railways moving military supplies · Iran killed 1 navy Lt at Jask port · IRGC struck Prince Hassan Air Base (JORDAN) with ballistic missiles + a US base in Qatar; UAE+Qatar intercepted missiles; Qatar told vessels to SUSPEND ALL SAILING · new Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei: 'we pledge to avenge the blood of the martyred leader' · Ghalibaf: 'the era of one-sided deals is OVER'; adviser: Hormuz 'more important than dozens of atomic bombs' · Iran privately told US advisers the ship attacks were a 'mistake' by an 'errant sect of hardliners' + want to keep talking (US Amb. Waltz publicly doubts it) · OMAN drafted a tentative strait-management proposal; talks in Oman Sat · MoU (to ~Aug 18) in tatters; US revoked oil waiver · 13 US service members dead in the war to date; >$100B spent (Sanders)","leverage":[{"actor":"Iran (IRGC)","asset":"Hormuz control + horizontal reach (Gulf capitals) + sole-demining claim","str":"hi"},{"actor":"United States","asset":"Strike power + $300B gatekeeping + sanctions relief","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Israel","asset":"Spoiler veto; Katz prepping independent strike","str":"md"},{"actor":"Qatar/GCC","asset":"$6B custodian; Doha venue; toll rejection","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"US CENTCOM","detail":"Confirmed 10-target strike; 'powerful response'"},{"src":"Bahrain/Kuwait govts","detail":"Condemned Iranian strikes as 'dangerous escalation'"},{"src":"Iran parliament (Azizi)","detail":"Strait 'governed by Iran'; strike was 'ceasefire management'"},{"src":"Iran state TV (Qalibaf)","detail":"'Sovereignty of the Strait of Hormuz lies with Iran and Oman' — joint-administration + passage-fee claim after 60-day MOU"},{"src":"Qatar MOFA","detail":"'Positive progress' on Islamabad MOU issues; next meeting after Khamenei funeral ends July 9; emergency communication channel agreed"}],"mil":"The strike exchange ran its full arc in 96 hours: after the US June 26 strikes, Iran drone-struck a second tanker (M/T Kiku) June 27; CENTCOM hit 10 Iranian military targets; on June 28 Iran struck US sites in Bahrain and Kuwait — horizontal escalation — before both sides stood down, with talks moving to Doha June 30.","pol":"Iran's parliamentary security chief Azizi declared the strait 'governed by Iran' and the tanker strike 'ceasefire management, not a breach.' Trump warned Iran 'will no longer exist' if strikes continue; both then stepped back. Iran received $6B via Qatar and rejected a France/Oman demining offer.","eco":"Oil stayed 'relatively contained' through the exchange thanks to reserve releases and oversupply; $6B unfrozen.","assess":"Iran-US surges to 94 (CRITICAL) — the highest reading in the theater's history and now the platform's joint-top alongside Sudan. Iran has crossed the decisive threshold: the IRGC formally declared the Strait of Hormuz CLOSED, fired on vessels attempting to transit, and struck a US base in Qatar and Jordan's Prince Hassan Air Base with ballistic missiles. The US answered with its third round of strikes in a week — 140 targets including missile sites, naval capabilities, and the railways moving Iranian military supplies — while insisting the strait remains open, though traffic has slowed to a trickle and Qatar has told vessels to suspend all sailing. New Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei has pledged to 'avenge the blood of the martyred leader.' The one thread of hope: Iran privately told US advisers the ship attacks were a 'mistake' by an errant hardline faction and that it wants to keep talking, and Oman has drafted a proposal to manage strait traffic. But with Washington demanding a public admission it doubts will come, and Iran's negotiator declaring 'the era of one-sided deals is over,' this is the most dangerous moment of the war.","diplomacy":[{"name":"Oman strait-management proposal","desc":"Oman drafted a tentative proposal to manage Hormuz traffic; Araghchi-Omani talks focused on the strait — the one live diplomatic track","status":"active","next":"Oman talks"},{"name":"MoU (to ~Aug 18)","desc":"In tatters — Trump declared it 'over,' US revoked the oil waiver, Iran calls that a breach; Article 5 (reopen trade route) is the contested core","status":"collapsed","next":"US says no nuclear progress until the strait is secure"},{"name":"Back-channel (Vance/Kushner/Witkoff/Rubio)","desc":"Iran privately conceded the ship attacks were a 'mistake' by hardliners and wants to keep talking; US wants a public admission; Waltz publicly skeptical","status":"active","next":"US demands public acknowledgment"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Iran","channel":"Hormuz transit control","exp":"HIGH","detail":"The IRGC's transit regime (authorization + 'maritime service fees') could be worth tens of $B/yr AND is a permanent strategic lever — Mojtaba Khamenei has enshrined it as doctrine; an adviser called it 'more important than dozens of atomic bombs'"},{"holder":"United States","channel":"Oil-export waiver + sanctions","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Revoked Iran's oil-export waiver (the core of the MoU) — cutting off Iran's main revenue; the US can also blockade Iranian ports"}],"tclass":"conflict","tier":"CRITICAL","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","external":[{"actor":"🇮🇱 Israel","side":"US coalition","flow":"Co-belligerent from the Feb 2026 opening ('Operation Epic Fury'); strikes on Iran + the Lebanon front","tier":"documented","for":"Destroy Iranian nuclear/missile capability; degrade the Axis of Resistance"},{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"Iran (limited)","flow":"Political cover, no direct intervention; the Iran-Russia drone/arms relationship predates the war","tier":"documented","for":"Keep the US pinned in the Middle East while it fights in Ukraine"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"Iran (economic)","flow":"Principal buyer of Iranian oil; opposes the sanctions architecture; ~half its oil imports transit Hormuz","tier":"documented","for":"Energy security — China is the biggest loser from a closed Hormuz"},{"actor":"🇴🇲🇶🇦🇵🇰 Oman/Qatar/Pakistan","side":"mediators","flow":"Oman drafted the strait-management proposal; Qatar shuttles between the sides; Pakistan's Dar urging dialogue","tier":"documented","for":"De-escalation — all are directly exposed to Hormuz closure"},{"actor":"🇪🇺 European states","side":"restraint","flow":"Several European nations BARRED the US from using their bases/airspace for offensive operations; UK allowed only 'limited defensive' use","tier":"documented","for":"Avoid complicity in an escalation they oppose"}],"contested":{"summary":"Two governments give incompatible accounts of the same waterway. Both cannot be true, and the gap is where the war lives.","claims":[{"claim":"Iran says the Strait of Hormuz is CLOSED. The US says it is OPEN.","who":"Iran gains a permanent revenue stream and strategic lever if transit requires IRGC authorization. The US needs free passage to hold the position that its strikes are working.","evid":"Lloyd's List: no vessel over 10,000 dwt has transited the US-coordinated route with AIS on since Jul 7. Traffic is 'a trickle.' Qatar told owners to suspend all sailing. Iran has fired on and struck ships. Functionally, Iran's claim is closer to the observable truth than Washington's.","status":"CONTESTED — evidence favors Iran's account"},{"claim":"Iran privately told US advisers the ship attacks were a 'mistake' by an 'errant sect of hardliners.'","who":"Iran — if believed, it de-escalates without conceding the Hormuz doctrine. The US doubts it: Amb. Waltz said so publicly.","evid":"Reported via back-channel (Vance/Witkoff/Kushner). Unverifiable. It sits against Mojtaba Khamenei's public enshrinement of 'the lever of closing the Strait' as doctrine — which cuts the other way.","status":"UNRESOLVED"},{"claim":"The US frames its strikes as defensive; CENTCOM labeled the Jul 9 round 'offensive.'","who":"The US — 'self-defense' is legally and diplomatically cheaper than 'offensive war.'","evid":"CENTCOM's own language changed. 140 targets including railways moving military supplies, and a strike ~900 miles from Hormuz near Aqqala — well outside any strait-protection rationale.","status":"CONTESTED — the framing shifted with the facts"}]},"history":{"roots":"The US-Iran war did not begin in 1979 — it began in 1953, when the CIA and MI6 overthrew Mosaddegh's elected government for nationalizing Iranian oil, restored the Shah, and taught Iranian politics that constitutionalism gets couped and only hard power protects sovereignty. Abrahamian's histories trace the line directly: the Shah's SAVAK police state was the coup's maintenance system, the 1979 revolution its delayed answer, and the Islamic Republic's nuclear program and 'Axis of Resistance' the institutionalization of never-again. Every escalation since — the hostage crisis, the Iraq war years (when the West armed Saddam's invasion of Iran, chemical weapons included), sanctions, the JCPOA's collapse — reinforced the same lesson on both sides. The Feb 2026 assassination of Khamenei was 1953's method applied at the top of the state; Mojtaba's succession and the Hormuz war are the system answering as it was built to.","timeline":[{"date":"1979","e":"Islamic Revolution topples the Shah; US embassy hostage crisis begins the enmity."},{"date":"1980-88","e":"Iran-Iraq War; the Revolutionary Guard (IRGC) becomes the regime's core."},{"date":"2002","e":"Iran's covert enrichment program is exposed, beginning the nuclear standoff."},{"date":"2015","e":"JCPOA nuclear deal caps enrichment in exchange for sanctions relief."},{"date":"2018","e":"US withdraws from the JCPOA; 'maximum pressure' sanctions return."},{"date":"2020","e":"US kills IRGC Quds commander Qassem Soleimani."},{"date":"2024","e":"President Raisi dies in a helicopter crash, muddying the succession."},{"date":"28 Feb 2026","e":"Ali Khamenei assassinated in a joint US-Israeli strike; the war begins."},{"date":"8 Mar 2026","e":"Mojtaba Khamenei named Supreme Leader; hardline continuity."},{"date":"Apr 2026","e":"Fragile two-week ceasefire tied to reopening the Strait of Hormuz; repeatedly broken."},{"date":"Jul 2026","e":"Ceasefire shattered by fresh strikes; Oman floats a two-route Hormuz plan."}],"actors":[{"name":"Mojtaba Khamenei","bio":"Son of the assassinated Ali Khamenei, named Supreme Leader March 2026. A hardliner with deep IRGC ties, never elected — his selection signals continuity, not compromise."},{"name":"IRGC (Revolutionary Guard)","bio":"The regime's ideological military and the custodian of the nuclear and missile programs and the proxy network. More powerful than the regular army."},{"name":"United States","bio":"Co-belligerent with Israel; insists the Strait of Hormuz stay open and opposes Iranian tolls on shipping. Balancing decapitation strikes against a wider-war risk."},{"name":"Israel","bio":"Views an Iranian bomb as existential; has vowed to target any Iranian leadership. The prime mover of the strikes that began the war."},{"name":"Oman / Qatar","bio":"The regional mediators — Oman drafted the two-route Hormuz proposal; Gulf states fear the war swallowing their economies (F1 races cancelled, oil disrupted)."}],"bridge":"The current war runs on 1953's logic from both directions: Washington again betting that decapitation reorders Iran, Tehran again converting external assault into internal consolidation (the execution surge is the revolution's oldest reflex). The Hormuz closure is the oil nationalization fight returned as geography — Iran monetizing the strait as Mosaddegh tried to monetize the wells, and the abandoned US toll plan shows even Washington briefly adopting the logic. Nothing in this theater is aberrant; it is a 73-year feedback loop at maximum amplitude. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: nine straight days of strikes, 18 US dead, and both sides destroying each other's water plants. Mossadegh's question — who decides what Iran's resources are for — is being answered by ordnance for the fourth time in seventy years.","sources":[{"work":"A History of Modern Iran","author":"Ervand Abrahamian","year":"2008 (2nd ed. 2018)","note":"The standard structural history — 1953, the Shah's police state, the revolution's social base."},{"work":"The Coup: 1953, the CIA, and the Roots of Modern U.S.-Iranian Relations","author":"Ervand Abrahamian","year":"2013","note":"The oil-nationalization coup as the relationship's founding event — from declassified records."},{"work":"All the Shah's Men","author":"Stephen Kinzer","year":"2003","note":"The operational history of TPAJAX and its blowback thesis."},{"work":"Declassified CIA acknowledgment of the 1953 coup","author":"CIA (FOIA release)","year":"2013","note":"Primary admission — 'the military coup... was carried out under CIA direction.'"}]},"refs":[{"t":"CNN · Iran war live (31 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.cnn.com/2026/07/31/world/live-news/iran-war-trump"},{"t":"Times of Israel · liveblog (1 Aug 2026)","u":"https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog-august-01-2026/"},{"t":"CNN · Iran war live (30-31 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.cnn.com/2026/07/30/world/live-news/iran-war-trump"},{"t":"Democracy Now · headlines (30 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.democracynow.org/2026/7/30/headlines"},{"t":"Just Security · Early Edition (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.justsecurity.org/149834/early-edition-july-27-2026/"},{"t":"Al Jazeera · Iran war live (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/7/27/iran-war-live-iran-halts-retaliatory-strikes-after-pause-in-us-attacks"},{"t":"Democracy Now · headlines (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.democracynow.org/2026/7/27/headlines"}],"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"A 73-year feedback loop from the 1953 coup: decapitation strikes met by regime consolidation, Hormuz monetized as leverage.","alt":"A narrower reading: a discrete nuclear-counterproliferation war triggered by a specific 2026 assassination, not the whole arc — and Iranian escalation may be regime-survival brinkmanship rather than the structural 'never again' the platform frames.","why":"The 1953-to-now continuity is real and explanatory, but the platform should not let the deep frame obscure that specific 2026 choices (the Khamenei strike, the toll experiment) were contingent, not fated. MOD."},"human":{"dead":9200,"disp":250000,"famine":false,"aid":"strike-constrained","note":"Cumulative war deaths since 28 Feb 2026 run into the thousands across Iran, Israel and the Gulf littoral; no independent count exists. US military