Iran/Doha, Lebanon, Sudan/Boulos), each with cross-pressures — most sharply the UAE arms-sale fork, where the lever most effective on Sudan is the partnership needed for Iran's $300B.","eco":"Oil stayed contained; the $300B funding question and post-IEEPA tariff cliff loom.","assess":"US foreign policy holds at 63 — striking Iran while running three mediation tracks. The central tension: US leverage over the UAE is the most effective unused lever on Sudan, yet the UAE is needed for the Iran coalition. Volatile stays right.","tclass":"watch","tier":"ELEVATED","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Political","econlev":[{"holder":"United States","channel":"Sanctions + dollar system","exp":"HIGH","detail":"The master lever behind most theaters on this board — oil waivers, secondary sanctions, SWIFT access, tariff threats"}],"contested":{"summary":"The information environment is itself the theater: who gets to say what the war, the law, and the election mean.","claims":[{"claim":"The Iran war is constitutional and 'largely ended' threats — congressional authorization is unnecessary.","who":"The administration — Article II maximalism; the Senate's 50-46 NDAA block and withdrawal resolution are the institutional counter.","evid":"No AUMF covers Iran; the War Powers fight is now live with budget leverage. Whether 'success' claims match ground truth (blockade reimposed, strikes ongoing) is itself contested.","status":"CONTESTED — war-powers constitutional dispute, unresolved"},{"claim":"US strikes in Nigeria answer a 'Christian genocide.'","who":"The administration + advocacy networks; Nigeria's government, ACLED and Crisis Group dispute the characterization.","evid":"Mass killings documented; religious-targeting is ~5% of civilian-targeting events (ACLED). Analysts read the framing as leverage for critical-minerals access (cross-link: Nigeria theater).","status":"CONTESTED — humanitarian frame vs resource-leverage aim"}]},"history":{"roots":"The US theater tracks the superpower's internal contest over what its power is for. The second Trump term operationalized a doctrine the platform tags 'Donroe': hemispheric assertion (Venezuela, cartels, Panama), transactional alliances, tariff statecraft, and executive maximalism at home — mass deportations, National Guard deployments, institutional purges. The counterweights — courts, a blocking Senate minority, states, streets — fight a running battle over each instrument. The Iran war sharpened it into a constitutional question: strikes without authorization, answered by a 50-46 defeat of the defense bill. The world's variable is no longer what America wants, but which America shows up.","timeline":[{"date":"2016/2020","e":"Trump's first win; the contested 2020 aftermath and Jan 6."},{"date":"2024","e":"Trump returns with unified control."},{"date":"2025","e":"Deportation surge; Guard deployments; tariff statecraft; Venezuela operation."},{"date":"Feb-Mar 2026","e":"The Iran war begins without authorization — the war-powers clash ignites."},{"date":"Jul 2026","e":"Senate blocks the $1.15T NDAA 50-46 over the war; ICC pressure campaign; the institutional contest deepens."}],"actors":[{"name":"The administration","bio":"Article II maximalism as doctrine — war, trade, and enforcement run from the White House, courts permitting or not."},{"name":"Congress (the blocking minority)","bio":"The NDAA defeat shows the remaining veto — money as the last war power."},{"name":"The courts","bio":"The contested referee — injunctions, emergency dockets, and the question of compliance."},{"name":"The states + streets","bio":"The federalism front — sanctuary fights, Guard federalization battles, mass protest as counterweight."}],"bridge":"Schlesinger named the arc a half-century ago: the imperial presidency grows in war and emergency, and Congress's power of the purse is the last constraint standing when authorization dies. The 50-46 NDAA block is that constraint exercised — the first time budget leverage has met this presidency's Article II maximalism head-on — while the ICC campaign, the deportation architecture, and the Guard federalization fights test Levitsky-Ziblatt's forbearance thesis in real time: the rules survive only while both sides decline to use them fully. The platform tracks this as a theater because every other theater's American variable is decided here. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: Senate blocks the $1.15T NDAA 50-46 over the war; ICC pressure campaign; the institutional contest deepens.","sources":[{"work":"The Imperial Presidency","author":"Arthur M. Schlesinger Jr.","year":"1973","note":"The classic arc of executive war power — written after the last unauthorized war fight."},{"work":"How Democracies Die","author":"Steven Levitsky & Daniel Ziblatt","year":"2018","note":"The forbearance-and-guardrails frame the current contest tests."},{"work":"War Powers Resolution + Senate NDAA vote record","author":"US Congress","year":"1973 / Jul 2026","note":"The primary legal text and the live vote applying its logic through the purse."}]},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"The superpower's internal contest over what its power is for — Article II maximalism versus the purse and the courts.","alt":"A normal-polarization reading holds this as cyclical partisan conflict, not a structural constitutional crisis — the guardrails-dying framing may overstate.","why":"The 50-46 NDAA block and the war-powers fight are real institutional stress; whether they're crisis or cycle is genuinely contested. MOD, explicitly."},"refs":[{"t":"Britannica: $95B package clears hurdle","u":"https://www.britannica.com/event/2026-Iran-war"},{"t":"AP/Reuters: $200B request opposition","u":"https://www.aol.com/articles/pentagon-seeks-200-billion-additional-131216211.html"},{"t":"IMF · country data and Article IV reports","u":"https://www.imf.org/en/Countries"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"64 = institutional or electoral instability, broadly static over the