 Qatar suspends all sailing · ships struck","domain":"Maritime Security","sev":"critical","status":"volatile","since":"Ongoing","esc":94,"lat":25,"lon":50,"trend":"up","w14":false,"disputed":true,"esc_history":[67,67,66,69,74,78,74,88,94,94],"cas":"N/A","disp":"$trillions in trade; 2 tankers struck this week","flag":"🔭","note":"1 AUG 2026 — THE MOST BLOCKADE-SENSITIVE ECONOMY ON THIS BOARD IS NOT IN THE GULF: Taiwan imports ROUGHLY 98% OF ITS ENERGY while producing the world’s advanced semiconductor capacity, with 78.5% of its exports being chips and Q1 growth of 13.69% — the fastest in 39 years. A quarantine of Taiwan would not need to interdict chips to work; interrupting fuel deliveries to the grid would do it. The board tracks Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb because they are being contested; the Taiwan Strait belongs in the same frame because the dependency is larger and the contest has not started · 30-31 JUL 2026 — A 14-STATE MARITIME ALLIANCE, AND GPS SPOOFING GOES SYSTEMIC: the SAUDI DEFENCE MINISTRY says 14 COUNTRIES have backed its proposal for a 'MARITIME DEFENSE ALLIANCE' to protect shipping routes — the first multilateral security architecture generated by this crisis, and notably Riyadh-led rather than Washington-led · CENTCOM reports having REDIRECTED 24 COMMERCIAL VESSELS, DISABLED TWO AND BOARDED TWO to enforce US shipping restrictions · GPS SPOOFING — making a ship's position appear far from its actual location — now affects civilian vessels in BOTH channels and was 'exceedingly rare around Bab al-Mandeb just a week ago' · THIS IS A CATEGORICAL CHANGE: interdiction can be tracked and attributed; spoofing degrades the navigational commons itself, for everyone, with no attribution and no off-switch · 23-27 JUL 2026 — THE HORMUZ CAMPAIGN HIT ITS LIMIT: CENTCOM's commander recommended halting the bombing campaign AROUND THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ because it had REACHED THE LIMIT OF ITS EFFECTIVENESS, and the US stopped on 23 Jul · the original trigger for the escalation was IRAN FIRING AT SHIPS TRYING TO TRANSIT HORMUZ · but the Houthis struck Saudi oil sites on the Red Sea coast days later — so the strait that started the war is quieting while the other one is not · 24-25 JUL 2026 — THE STRAITS ARE RE-COUPLING AND OIL HIT $100: the Houthis ATTACKED TWO SAUDI TANKERS in the RED SEA and BRENT SURGED TO $100/BARREL · separately, QATARENERGY has PAUSED efforts to restore LNG production at RAS LAFFAN after an ATTACK ON AN LNG CARRIER IN THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ, keeping operations at a minimum · THIS REVERSES THIS PLATFORM'S OWN READ FROM 48 HOURS AGO. On 24 Jul this board logged Hormuz 'gradually resuming' with OPEC+ raising August quotas, and recorded the two straits DECOUPLING as evidence against its own linkage thesis. Both straits are now under active attack and the price has moved $10 higher. The decoupling was a two-day artefact, not a trend — logged in full because the platform's method is to record its reads being falsified, not to quietly restate them. · 22-24 JUL 2026 — HORMUZ IS REOPENING: oil exports through the STRAIT OF HORMUZ are GRADUALLY RESUMING after the disruptions linked to the Iran war, and SEVEN OPEC+ MEMBERS have agreed to raise production quotas by 188,000 BARRELS PER DAY for August · the producers are pricing in restored throughput — which is a harder signal than any diplomatic statement, because it is a bet with money behind it · BUT BAB EL-MANDEB HAS NOT FOLLOWED: UKMTO and the Royal Navy report a cargo ship attacked by 'UNKNOWN ARMED ASSAILANTS' off HODEIDAH · the two straits this board coupled are now DECOUPLING — Hormuz easing while the Red Sea stays contested, which cuts against the linkage thesis and is logged as such · 20-22 JUL 2026 — THE THIRD DECLARED CLOSURE: with a Houthi BLOCKADE OF SAUDI ARABIA now announced alongside the Red Sea campaign and the Hormuz threat, three maritime access questions are being contested simultaneously by parties acting in concert · BRENT ABOVE $90 confirms the transmission channel this board tracks — the price is now moving on declarations, not just on interdictions · JUL 17-20 — THE TWO STRAITS ARE NOW FORMALLY LINKED: Iran has told the Houthis to CLOSE THE RED SEA GATEWAY if the US strikes Iran's power network (Reuters sources) — an explicit conditional coupling of Hormuz and Bab el-Mandeb that converts two separate chokepoints into one escalation ladder. The platform's single-system read is now the belligerents' own stated doctrine · ASEAN ministers meeting in MANILA are set to call for the reopening of Hormuz — the first significant push from Asian importers, who are absorbing the fuel shortages · evasion is industrialising: 'dark ships' and Oman-based ship-to-ship transfers indicate transits continue outside the designated-route regime · JUL 2026 — BOTH STRAITS LIVE: with Hormuz under blockade and mining, the RED SEA reactivates — a cargo ship attacked off Yemen (5 Jul, UK military), renewed navigation warnings, and the Saudi-Houthi truce broken · the Houthi-al-Shabaab technology transfer adds a second shore: Bab el-Mandeb could be threatened from BOTH the Yemeni and Somali sides by actors with drone capability · the platform's single-system read holds — when one chokepoint narrows, the others reprice, and the actors who learned the technique share it · JUL 19 — THE CRISIS LEDGER (cumulative, Feb 28-present): 1 tugboat sunk · at least 17 merchant ships damaged, 7 of them abandoned · 2 merchant ships captured · 12 seafarers killed or missing · 1 port worker killed and 2 wounded in Bahrain · the IRGC has boarded vessels, issued passage prohibitions by VHF, and LAID SEA MINES in the strait · BASELINE FOR SCALE: before the war ~25% of the world's seaborne oil trade and ~20% of global LNG moved through Hormuz — disruption has produced fuel shortages in parts of Asia and rippling effects across the world economy · the Persian Gulf Strait Authority (formed mid-crisis) now administers the designated-route regime · JUL 17 — SEAFARERS AS THE NEW METRIC: 12 seafarers killed/missing; an Indian 3rd engineer died on the MV GFS Galaxy (struck by IRGC); India ordered its sailors (300k+, world's #3 supplier) OFF Hormuz-transit vessels — the labor supply of global shipping now priced into the chokepoint · CENTCOM blockade: 85 intercepted / 3 seized / 26 bypassed (Lloyd's) · the Jun-17 MoU's freedom-of-navigation clause is the paper the whole crisis is failing against, halfway through its 60-day clock · JUL 17 — THE STRAIT AS GOVERNED BATTLESPACE: blockade 2.0 running (2 vessels redirected in 17h; Kharg-bound tanker disabled); first-blockade baseline now public — 140+ interceptions, ~$4.8B cost to Iran by May 1 · a 'Persian Gulf Strait Authority' has formed around the designated-route regime — chokepoint governance institutionalizing mid-war · EASA airspace warnings extend the chokepoint from sea to sky; Doha/Bahrain intercepts price the whole Gulf as the risk zone · JUL 16 — IRGC widens the threat from Hormuz to ALL regional oil and gas — if executed, Bab el-Mandeb + Hormuz + Gulf load-ports simultaneously; US strikes now framed explicitly as convoy protection; the strait is a shooting gallery with tankers in it · JUL 15 — THE TOLL IS DEAD: Trump abandoned the 20% Hormuz transit fee after Gulf states offered US investment instead — chokepoint monetization traded for capital flows; the blockade on Iran is REIMPOSED and strikes resumed (4th round), so transit risk premium stays live even as the fee vanishes · JUL 13 — DUAL-CHOKEPOINT RISK NOW EXPLICIT: with Hormuz already declared closed by Iran, the Houthis announced intent to close BAB EL-MANDEB simultaneously, naming $200 oil as the objective · Bab el-Mandeb = ~12% of global maritime trade, 26-29km wide; throughput already fell 8.8M→~4M bbl/day in the earlier campaign · Hormuz (~1/5 of seaborne oil) + Bab el-Mandeb together is roughly a THIRD of seaborne energy trade under simultaneous interdiction — the largest tail risk the platform has ever logged · UNSC Res 2722/2812 Houthi-attack reporting renewal due 15 Jul · COUNTER-READ logged: both Iran and the Houthis have historically stopped short of full closure because it invites confrontation they cannot win · CRISIS (Jul 11-12): IRGC DECLARED HORMUZ CLOSED and is firing on vessels — struck at least 2 ships; a container vessel damaged + abandoned (crew rescued, 1 Indian national missing) · dueling claims: Iran's transit authority says passage 'not possible'; US CENTCOM/Trump/JMIC insist the southern Oman-coast route is OPEN for two-way traffic — but tracking shows traffic reduced to A TRICKLE · Qatar advised owners to SUSPEND ALL SAILING + maritime activity · Iran demands transit fees + IRGC authorization via its own designated lane; US objects, pushes the Omani route (Iran calls this an MoU violation) · Oman drafting a strait-management proposal — the live diplomatic path · ~20M bbl/day (1/5 of global oil) normally transits · Brent ~$73-76 · Bab el-Mandeb also stressed","leverage":[{"actor":"Iran","asset":"Hormuz — strike + 'governed by Iran' doctrine + sole-demining + route designation","str":"hi"},{"actor":"US 5th Fleet","asset":"Escort + strike; can't compel safety","str":"md"},{"actor":"France/Oman","asset":"Demining offer — rejected by Iran","str":"lo"},{"actor":"Houthis/China","asset":"Bab el-Mandeb + Taiwan Strait (rising)","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Iran FM Araghchi","detail":"Warns against 'separate arrangements'; claims sole Hormuz control"},{"src":"US/GCC/Oman","detail":"Reject any Iranian toll/control claim"}],"mil":"Hormuz is now an Iranian managed-access regime under contestation: Iran struck two tankers (Ever Lovely, Kiku) for using the US-cleared Omani route, declared the strait 'governed by Iran,' and rejected a France/Oman demining offer. The stand-down restored free transit but the governance dispute heads to Doha.","pol":"Iran's leverage is now doctrinal — a sovereign-management claim the US rejects but can't easily counter short of permanent escort.","eco":"The exchange slowed but didn't reverse normalization; demining is entangled in the sovereignty dispute.","assess":"Chokepoints jumps to 88 (CRITICAL) — the platform's third-highest theater and its most consequential for global markets. Iran's IRGC has formally declared the Strait of Hormuz closed and is firing on ships that attempt to transit, striking at least two vessels; Qatar has advised owners to suspend all sailing. Washington insists the southern Oman-coast route remains open, but tracking data shows traffic has fallen to a trickle. The dispute is now about who governs the waterway: Iran demands IRGC authorization and transit fees through its designated lane, the US insists on free passage via Oman, and each calls the other's position a treaty violation. With roughly a fifth of the world's oil normally transiting, this is the single largest live risk in the investment bridge's energy and defense exposure. Oman's draft strait-management proposal is the one diplomatic path open.","tclass":"watch","tier":"CRITICAL","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Strategic","econlev":[{"holder":"Iran","channel":"Hormuz transit regime","exp":"HIGH","detail":"IRGC authorization + 'maritime service fees' for passage — an attempt to convert a chokepoint into a permanent revenue stream and strategic lever"},{"holder":"Global energy market","channel":"Price transmission","exp":"HIGH","detail":"~20M bbl/day (1/5 of global oil) normally transits Hormuz; closure transmits directly into Brent, war-risk insurance, and every energy-exposed position"}],"contested":{"summary":"Maritime chokepoint violence generates competing legal and moral framings that determine whether a naval response is enforcement or aggression — and every party has an interest in the answer.","claims":[{"claim":"Houthi attacks on shipping are acts of solidarity with Gaza, not piracy.","who":"Ansar Allah; supported by parts of the Global South commentariat.","evid":"Vessel selection has repeatedly failed the stated test: ships with no Israeli ownership, charter or destination have been struck, and crews from Bulgaria, the Philippines, India and Vietnam have been killed or taken. Conversely the campaign IS temporally correlated with Gaza operations and paused during ceasefire periods, which cuts the other way. Both facts are in the record.","status":"PARTLY-CONTRADICTED-BY-TARGETING"},{"claim":"Western naval operations are defending freedom of navigation for all states.","who":"US, UK and coalition partners.","evid":"Chinese and Russian-linked vessels have transited with markedly lower incident rates, and the coalition's protection has concentrated on its own flag and charter interests. Asian importers — who absorb the largest share of the disruption — were not consulted on the response and only in July 2026 issued their own call, through ASEAN in Manila, for the reopening of Hormuz.","status":"SELECTIVELY-APPLIED"},{"claim":"Traffic through the affected straits has collapsed.","who":"Widely reported; used to justify emergency measures.","evid":"AIS-based figures are unreliable here because 'dark ship' transits and Oman-based ship-to-ship transfers are designed to be invisible to them. Insurance-rate data and satellite observation suggest substantial evasion rather than cessation. The true volume is unknown, and every party quoting a number has a position that the number supports.","status":"UNMEASURABLE-WITH-CURRENT-DATA"}]},"history":{"roots":"The chokepoints theater tracks the physics of globalization: a handful of straits and canals through which the world's energy and trade must pass, and the powers who learned to price that necessity. Hormuz (a fifth of world oil), Bab el-Mandeb/Suez (the Europe-Asia artery), Malacca, the Bosphorus, Panama — each a geography lesson in leverage. The 2020s militarized them in sequence: Houthi Red Sea attacks rerouted shipping around Africa; the 2026 Iran war closed then conditionally reopened Hormuz — the first sustained closure attempt in history, with tolls proposed and abandoned. The platform treats them as a single system: when one narrows, all reprice.","timeline":[{"date":"1956/1967","e":"Suez crises — the original chokepoint wars."},{"date":"1980s","e":"Tanker War — Hormuz militarized the first time."},{"date":"2021","e":"Ever Given blocks Suez — six days, $60B in trade queued."},{"date":"2023-25","e":"Houthi campaign closes the Red Sea route for Western shipping."},{"date":"2026","e":"Hormuz: closure, designated-route regime, toll proposal floated and dropped; blockade reimposed — the strait as negotiating table."}],"actors":[{"name":"Iran (Hormuz)","bio":"The closure card played at last — mining, designated routes, and the toll experiment; leverage spent is leverage revealed."},{"name":"The Houthis (Bab el-Mandeb)","bio":"The non-state chokepoint power — proving a militia can reroute world trade."},{"name":"The US Navy + allies","bio":"The convoy system's underwriter — freedom of navigation as the imperial commons, now contested."},{"name":"Shipping + insurance markets","bio":"The pricing mechanism — war-risk premiums as the real-time gauge of every crisis."}],"bridge":"Khalili's Gulf logistics work supplies the correction the 2026 crisis proves: the 'freedom of the seas' was always a built order — dredged, insured, garrisoned, priced — and whoever can interrupt the build owns leverage over everyone who forgot it was built. Iran's closure experiment, the Houthis' militia-scale trade veto, and the abandoned US toll plan are three actors reading the same map: the strait as a rentable asset. The platform treats war-risk insurance as the honest gauge because underwriters, unlike statements, price what they actually believe.","sources":[{"work":"Sinews of War and Trade: Shipping and Capitalism in the Arabian Peninsula","author":"Laleh Khalili","year":"2020","note":"The critical scholarship on Gulf maritime logistics as constructed power."},{"work":"World Oil Transit Chokepoints","author":"US Energy Information Administration","year":"updated series","note":"The primary flow data (Hormuz ~1/5 of world oil) behind every scenario here."},{"work":"Joint War Committee listings / marine war-risk premia","author":"Lloyd's JWC","year":"2023-26","note":"The market's primary real-time risk gauge."}]},"refs":[{"t":"CNN · Iran war live · CENTCOM and Saudi alliance (30 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.cnn.com/2026/07/30/world/live-news/iran-war-trump"},{"t":"American Legion · Five Things to Know (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.legion.org/information-center/news/security/2026/july/five-things-to-know-july-27-2026"},{"t":"Just Security · Early Edition (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.justsecurity.org/149834/early-edition-july-27-2026/"},{"t":"NPR · oil surges to $100 after Houthi attack on Saudi tankers (24 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.npr.org/sections/world/"},{"t":"IEA · Oil Market Report","u":"https://www.iea.org/topics/oil-market-report"}],"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"Globalization's freedom-of-the-seas was always a built, garrisoned order; whoever can interrupt the build owns leverage.","alt":"A markets reading: chokepoint risk is routinely overpriced by war-premium panic and underdelivered in practice (tankers reroute, flows adapt) — the leverage may be more rhetorical than the closures prove.","why":"Both hold; the platform uses war-risk insurance as the honest gauge precisely because it prices what underwriters believe, not what statements claim. HIGH on the structural point, with the overpricing caveat live."},"human":{"dead":13,"disp":0,"famine":false,"aid":"n/a","note":"The maritime human cost, precisely counted: 12 seafarers killed or missing, 1 port worker killed and 2 wounded in Bahrain; 17+ merchant ships damaged, 7 abandoned, 2 captured, 1 tugboat sunk. India barred its sailors — 300k+ of the world's crews — from Hormuz transits after an Indian engineer died aboard the MV GFS Galaxy.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"94 = two straits now formally coupled by belligerent doctrine, seafarer deaths, and a global fuel-price transmission channel already visible at the pump. Not higher because transits continue via evasion rather than having stopped.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"26 Jul 2026","c":"SELF-CORRECTION: on 24 Jul this file recorded the Hormuz/Bab el-Mandeb DECOUPLING as evidence against the platform’s linkage thesis. Within 48 hours both straits were under attack and Brent hit $100. A two-day commercial signal was over-read as a trend. Both the call and its falsification remain on the record."},{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Iran-Houthi Red Sea linkage recorded; ASEAN Manila call added"}],"spill":[{"n":"Iran","dir":"in","w":"Hormuz threat, explicitly conditioned on strikes against Iran's power network.","id":"iran-us"},{"n":"Yemen","dir":"in","w":"Bab el-Mandeb interdiction plus a declared blockade of Saudi Arabia — three access questions contested at once.","id":"yemen"},{"n":"Global economy","dir":"out","w":"Brent above $90 and US pump prices at $4.00 — the price now moves on DECLARATIONS, not only interdictions.","id":"global-economy"},{"n":"ASEAN","dir":"out","w":"Asian importers absorb the largest share of the cost and only called for reopening in July 2026, through Manila."},{"n":"Egypt / Suez","id":"egypt","dir":"both","w":"Suez revenue collapses when the Red Sea is interdicted — Cairo pays a direct fiscal price for a war it is not fighting, which makes it a party to the reopening question."},{"n":"Taiwan Strait","id":"taiwan","dir":"in","w":"The uncontested chokepoint that matters most — the semiconductor supply the world economy runs on transits a strait under standing grey-zone pressure."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Both straits under attack, Brent at $100","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation coupled to the Iran war","up":["An LNG carrier struck IN Hormuz; QatarEnergy pausing Ras Laffan restoration","Two Saudi tankers attacked in the Red Sea, taking Brent to $100","Both straits now contested simultaneously — the coupling this board doubted 48 hours ago","Iran's published conditional being executed against a non-belligerent state"],"down":["An Iran-US de-escalation removing the strait coupling — the only lever that has not been tried","A US Strategic Petroleum Reserve release capping the price spike","Saudi/UAE east-west pipelines bypassing Hormuz","A negotiated route regime — further away now than when this was first assessed"],"fw":["ripeness","proxy","deterrence","thucydides"]},"macro":{"growth":"Five Gulf economies projected to contract","infl":"Transmitting globally · Brent $100","src":"IMF Regional Economic Outlook, April 2026","note":"THE IMF HAS NOW QUANTIFIED THIS BOARD'S CENTRAL CLAIM. Five of eight Gulf-region economies are projected to contract in 2026 — Bahrain, Iran, Iraq, Kuwait and Qatar — and the Fund attributes it PRIMARILY TO THEIR RELIANCE ON THE STRAIT OF HORMUZ FOR TRANSIT OF TRADED GOODS, ahead of direct infrastructure damage. The damage is durable: OUTPUT IN 2030 IS PROJECTED TO REMAIN ABOUT 2% BELOW PRE-WAR TRENDS. A chokepoint is not a temporary toll — contesting one removes years of output from the economies that depend on it, whether or not they are party to the fight."},"inherit":{"name":"The Freedom of Navigation Regime","est":"1888-1982","status":"IN FORCE · CONTESTED","what":"The <b>1888 Constantinople Convention</b> declared the Suez Canal open to all vessels in war and peace — a rule written by European powers to secure their own imperial routes, and enforced by the navy that had the most to lose from its violation. The 1936 Montreux Convention did the same for the Turkish Straits, and <b>UNCLOS in 1982</b> generalised transit passage through international straits. Each instrument codified as universal law an arrangement that served whoever then dominated the sea.","now":"<b>The regime survives only where someone enforces it, and the enforcement is now contested rather than shared.</b> The Houthis interdict Bab el-Mandeb and have declared a blockade of Saudi Arabia; Iran has published a conditional linking Hormuz closure to strikes on its power network; CENTCOM has redirected 24 vessels under its own restrictions; Saudi Arabia has assembled 14 states behind an alternative coalition. <b>And GPS spoofing now degrades navigation in both channels — an escalation with no owner who can stop it.</b>","change":"<b>The IMF has quantified the cost:</b> five of eight Gulf-region economies projected to contract in 2026 on Hormuz dependence, with output in 2030 remaining about 2% below pre-war trends. A rule that only holds when a dominant navy enforces it is being tested by parties who never wrote it."},"monitors":[{"t":"UKMTO · maritime incident reporting","u":"https://www.ukmto.org/"}]},{"id":"geopolitical","cat":"strategic","region":"Global","subregion":"Global","name":"Geopolitical Blocs — AES / Gulf / China-Cuba","sub":"AES sovereignty bloc · Gulf hinge · Bejucal · OTS-Hungary","domain":"Strategic Competition","sev":"watch","status":"watch","since":"2026","esc":60,"lat":null,"lon":null,"trend":"up","w14":false,"esc_history":[60,60,60,60,60,60,60,60,60,60],"cas":"N/A — analytical + intelligence layer","disp":"N/A","flag":"🔭","note":"THE US IS RE-ENGAGING THE SAHEL JUNTAS: Washington has continued to reengage the ALLIANCE OF SAHEL STATES to reestablish counterterrorism cooperation, INCLUDING INTELLIGENCE, SURVEILLANCE AND RECONNAISSANCE FLIGHTS · this is a reversal worth marking: the AES states expelled French and American forces and took Russian security partners instead, and Washington is now returning on the juntas' terms rather than requiring a democratic transition first · GHANA has separately increased outreach to the AES for bilateral security coordination, though ICG notes this has not mended the divides involving Burkina Faso, Côte d'Ivoire, Benin and Niger · the pattern across all three: the sanctions-and-isolation approach to the juntas has been quietly abandoned by everyone who tried it · JUL 2026 — THE US IS UNWINDING TWO STATE-SPONSOR LISTINGS AT ONCE: Secretary Rubio notified Congress the US will REMOVE SYRIA as a 'state sponsor of terrorism' — listed since 1979 — while separately lifting the 2020 S-400 sanctions on Turkey · read together, Washington is dismantling the sanctions architecture of the old Middle East to lock in the new one: rewarding the post-Assad transition and re-anchoring Turkey, in the same weeks it withdraws from Iraq · the through-line is a US recomposing its regional position around the outcomes of the Iran war rather than resisting them · 18-24 JUN 2026 — THE ICC WITHDRAWAL IS NOW FORMAL: Burkina Faso, Mali and Niger DEPOSITED THEIR NOTIFICATIONS of withdrawal from the Rome Statute with the UN Secretary-General; withdrawal takes effect ONE YEAR LATER, on 23 JUNE 2027 · Amnesty's assessment is that it 'consecrates impunity and threatens to deny victims of war crimes justice and reparations' · the legal detail that matters: Mali has been UNDER ICC INVESTIGATION SINCE 2013 and withdrawal does NOT affect matters already before the Court — Burkina Faso and Niger have no publicly acknowledged investigations, so for them the exit is prospective cover · read alongside the US dismantlement campaign, the Court is now formally losing members from below while being coerced from above · THE ICC LOSES FROM BOTH DIRECTIONS: alongside the US dismantlement campaign (Jul 2026), MALI, BURKINA FASO AND NIGER began ICC WITHDRAWAL (joint declaration, Sept 2025), denouncing a court 'instrumentalized for political purposes' — the same institution attacked by its principal architect and abandoned by states that once looked to it for protection against impunity. The assault is bidirectional: great-power coercion from above, sovereignty-revolt exit from below · JUL 14 — US LAUNCHES SWEEPING ANTI-ICC CAMPAIGN: Washington announced a pressure campaign against the International Criminal Court itself, including pushing OTHER countries to withdraw support — a systemic attack on the post-1998 international-justice architecture, not just case-specific sanctions · compounding: ICC chief prosecutor Karim Khan's suspension upheld by the UK Bar Standards Board (sexual-misconduct allegations); ICC oversight body votes this month on removing him — the court is under external assault and internal crisis simultaneously · UPDATED · AES Confederation as sovereignty bloc: 15,000 Unified Force, joint passport/bank, 0.5% import levy, quit ECOWAS, reciprocal US travel-ban response · Gulf = $6B custodian + Doha venue + $300B · Bejucal SIGINT · OTS-Hungary · AES condemnation of US over Venezuela/Maduro now has full context: the Jan 3 capture + Donroe Doctrine — Pan-African + Latin American sovereignty pushback converging · EXTERNAL-ACTOR LAYER (v16): three patronage models mapped into W/C Africa — gold-for-guns (Russia/UAE), drone diplomacy (Turkey/China), aid-as-cover/aid-washing (UAE, Qatar softer). US aid withdrawal (~83% Sub-Saharan cut) + French expulsion created the vacuum. Qatar Al Mansour $103bn pledge + UAE now a top-3 Africa investor; Iran-war may delay $100B+ Gulf African investment.","leverage":[{"actor":"AES (Mali/Burkina/Niger)","asset":"15,000 Unified Force; resource sovereignty (uranium/gold); anti-French realignment; Russia ties","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Qatar/GCC","asset":"$6B custodian + Doha venue + $300B capital + toll rejection","str":"hi"},{"actor":"China","asset":"Bejucal SIGINT (contested) + BRI + Cuba + rare earths; 53-country zero-tariff","str":"md"},{"actor":"Hungary (OTS)","asset":"Russia-ally to EU-accession enabler pivot","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"AES communiqués","detail":"Unified Force launch (Dec 2025); import levy; reciprocal US travel ban; condemned US over Venezuela/Maduro"},{"src":"ISS Africa / Amani Africa","detail":"AES financing via national funds; central question is funding the force without donors"},{"src":"AU Peace & Security Council","detail":"June 2026 review; late-response critique; AES-ECOWAS Abuja engagement"}],"mil":"The AES Confederation (Mali, Burkina, Niger) has consolidated as a sovereignty bloc: a 15,000-strong Unified Force (up from 5,000), joint biometric passport, investment bank, 0.5% import levy, flag/anthem, and formal ECOWAS withdrawal. It launched reciprocal travel restrictions on US citizens and condemned the US over Venezuela.","pol":"The Gulf is the structural hinge of the Iran settlement (Qatar = $6B custodian + Doha host; GCC = $300B funder + toll rejector). The AES represents the Pan-Africanist resource-sovereignty and anti-French realignment thread — though the insurgency that justified the coups has not been pushed back. OTS-Hungary and Bejucal remain tracked.","eco":"The AES leverages domestic funds (Burkina's Patriotic Support Fund, Niger's Solidarity Fund, Mali's Support Fund) — the central unresolved question is whether it can sustain the Unified Force without external donors.","assess":"The strategic layer rises to 60 with the AES added as a formal sovereignty bloc — the political-strategic counterpart to the Sahel-core military theaters. Its resource-sovereignty doctrine and anti-French realignment are consolidating institutionally even as the security justification for the coups goes unmet. The Gulf remains the decisive swing hinge of the Iran settlement.","econlev":[{"holder":"AES bloc","channel":"Sovereign funds + levy","exp":"MED","detail":"0.5% import levy + national support funds financing the 15,000 Unified Force without donors"}],"tclass":"watch","tier":"ELEVATED","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Strategic","contested":{"summary":"The US realignment of mid-2026 — lifting sanctions, ending deployments, unwinding terror listings — is read either as strategic recomposition or as retreat, and the same moves support both stories.","claims":[{"claim":"The US is strategically recomposing its position around the Iran war’s outcomes, not retreating.","who":"Framing favoured by the administration.","evid":"In weeks: removing Syria’s state-sponsor listing after 47 years, lifting the 2020 S-400 sanctions on Turkey, welcoming a Syria-Iraq pipeline deal, mediating Israel-Lebanon talks in Rome. Read as design, it is locking in the post-Assad order. Read as retreat, the simultaneous withdrawal from Iraq by 30 September — ending 23 years — and reliance on Qatar, Oman and Pakistan as mediators because no Western state is trusted suggests reduced leverage, not increased.","status":"BOTH-READINGS-FIT"},{"claim":"The AES states’ ICC withdrawal and the broader institutional exits reflect a durable multipolar shift.","who":"Asserted by the withdrawing states and their partners.","evid":"Burkina, Mali and Niger deposited ICC withdrawals (effective June 2027); Moldova formalised its CIS exit; new Central Asian corridor partnerships route around Russia. The exits are real, but they point in different directions — some away from Western institutions, some away from Russian ones — which is evidence of fragmentation rather than a coherent new bloc.","status":"REAL-BUT-NOT-COHERENT"}]},"offmap":true,"history":{"roots":"The geopolitical theater tracks the operating system itself: the post-1945 order's institutions — the UN, the courts, the treaties — under simultaneous assault and abandonment. The pattern of the 2020s is impunity's compounding: vetoes neutering the Security Council, the ICC defied then directly attacked (the 2026 US campaign to unmake it), aid architecture dismantled (USAID's termination), and might-makes-right normalized from Ukraine to the annexation debates. The platform tracks this as a theater because the erosion is not ambient — it is driven, by named actors, with measurable milestones, and every other theater's rules depend on it.","timeline":[{"date":"1945","e":"The UN order founded on the ashes."},{"date":"1998","e":"Rome Statute — the ICC's founding bargain."},{"date":"2022","e":"Ukraine invasion — a P5 member wages conquest; the system holds only rhetorically."},{"date":"2024-25","e":"ICC warrants defied (Putin, Netanyahu); USAID terminated; funding crises hollow the UN."},{"date":"Jul 2026","e":"US launches a campaign to dismantle the ICC itself — pressure on states to withdraw; the court simultaneously in internal crisis."}],"actors":[{"name":"The US (system author turned skeptic)","bio":"Built the order, now selectively unmaking it — the ICC campaign is the sharpest instance."},{"name":"Russia + China","bio":"The revisionists — one breaking rules by war, the other building parallel institutions by patience."},{"name":"The ICC / UN organs","bio":"The besieged referees — starved, defied, and now directly targeted, yet still the only game."},{"name":"The Global South swing states","bio":"The audience the contest plays to — their forum-shopping (BRICS, ICJ cases) is the order's real-time referendum."}],"bridge":"Getachew reframes what is being lost: the post-1945 order was not simply a Western gift under attack but a contested project that anticolonial worldmakers fought to universalize — self-determination, sovereign equality, the court no power stands above — and the 2026 assault (a P5 conquest normalized, the ICC targeted for dismantlement by the system's author) destroys precisely the parts the Global South spent decades winning. That is why the forum-shopping — BRICS, the ICJ cases, the withdrawal pressure resisted — is the real-time referendum: the audience deciding whether to defend an order that was partly theirs, or build the next one without its guarantors.","sources":[{"work":"Worldmaking after Empire: The Rise and Fall of Self-Determination","author":"Adom Getachew","year":"2019","note":"The anticolonial making of the order — the non-Eurocentric account of what the current assault unmakes."},{"work":"Governing the World: The History of an Idea","author":"Mark Mazower","year":"2012","note":"The long history of international order projects and their power politics."},{"work":"UN Charter / Rome Statute","author":"UN founding texts","year":"1945 / 1998","note":"The primary architecture under contest."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"The post-1945 order under driven assault — a contested anticolonial project, not just a Western gift, being unmade.","alt":"A multipolar-normalization reading holds this as healthy rebalancing toward a post-Western order rather than an order's collapse — the framing depends on what one thinks the order was.","why":"Getachew's frame (the order as partly Global-South-won) is the platform's answer to both the Western-nostalgia and the multipolar-triumphalist readings. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"Critical Threats · Africa File (19 Mar 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/sudan-rsf-saf-sahel-drc-iscap-uganda-nigeria-boko-iswap-mali-fla-jnim-africa-file-march-26-2026"},{"t":"Amnesty International: AES withdrawal from the Rome Statute","u":"https://www.amnesty.org/en/latest/news/2026/07/burkina-mali-niger/"},{"t":"CFR Global Conflict Tracker: AES ICC withdrawal","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/violent-extremism-sahel"},{"t":"UN Charter","u":"https://www.un.org/en/about-us/un-charter"},{"t":"IMF · country data and Article IV reports","u":"https://www.imf.org/en/Countries"}],"checkmode":"SWEPT","escwhy":"60 = strategic competition, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"AES ICC withdrawal formalised — deposited 18-24 Jun, effective 23 Jun 2027"}],"macro":{"growth":"Sub-Saharan Africa 4.3% (2026f) · 4.7% (2027f)","infl":"Core inflation picked up for the first time in two years","src":"World Bank Global Economic Prospects, January and June 2026","note":"THE REGIONAL PICTURE BEHIND THE INDIVIDUAL FILES. Headline inflation eased across Sub-Saharan Africa in 2025 on lower energy and food prices, but CORE inflation rose for the first time in two years — prompting some central banks to pause easing and others to raise rates. Several economies regained international capital-market access (Angola, Kenya, Nigeria). Then the June update revised the region down on the Middle East conflict. Senegal's forecast was cut specifically after HIDDEN DEBT REVELATIONS froze IMF funding — the mechanism the Debt board defines, visible in a growth number."},"monitors":[{"t":"ICC official case records","u":"https://www.icc-cpi.int/"},{"t":"US Department of State · designations and policy record","u":"https://www.state.gov/"}]},{"id":"venezuela","cat":"military","region":"South America","subregion":"South America","name":"Venezuela — US Intervention / Remote Coercion","sub":"Maduro captured Jan 3 · US 'runs' oil · Rodríguez interim","domain":"Intervention / Occupation-by-Leverage","sev":"high","status":"volatile","since":"Sep 2025","esc":71,"lat":10.48,"lon":-66.9,"trend":"up","w14":false,"diplomacy_active":true,"esc_history":[70,71,71,71,71,71,71,71,71,71],"cas":"Op Absolute Resolve (Jan 3): Maduro security team largely killed; 32 Cuban soldiers killed; 7 US wounded","disp":"~8M Venezuelans emigrated since 2015 (3M+ in Colombia)","flag":"🇻🇪","note":"CORRECTION — THIS FILE WAS MISSING A 3,000-DEATH DISASTER AND A CONSTITUTIONAL CRISIS: (a) MADURO WAS SEIZED IN A US MILITARY RAID IN JANUARY 2026, and DELCY RODRÍGUEZ has been ACTING PRESIDENT since, under a 180-DAY INTERIM MANDATE — whose CONSTITUTIONAL DEADLINE HAS NOW RUN OUT, with no succession mechanism agreed · (b) TWIN EARTHQUAKES of magnitude 7.2 and 7.5 struck on 24 JUNE 2026 west of Caracas, levelling nearly 200 buildings in the capital and the coastal state of LA GUAIRA. Official toll 2,954; CNN reports experts estimate MORE THAN 3,800 and call the official figure a major undercount; 12,600+ injured and roughly 50,000 registered missing on a crowdsourced site. Damage estimated at $37 BILLION · the response was led substantially by CIVILIAN VOLUNTEERS — ICG's Gunson: 'The state simply lacks the capacity to respond. It doesn't have the budget, personnel, planning, leadership, equipment, or heavy machinery' · Rodríguez rejected the criticism and claimed 80% of collapsed buildings were privately developed WITHOUT PROVIDING EVIDENCE — several were signature Chávez-era social housing projects · MACHADO (Nobel Peace Prize 2025, in exile) argues Venezuela 'cannot be stabilised, recovered, or governed indefinitely without the democratic majority' · WHAT THIS PLATFORM GOT WRONG: the Venezuela read was carried as 'post-intervention, descending' — it recorded the intervention and missed both the disaster and the mandate expiry. Corrected here rather than overwritten. · 2026 — THE PRECEDENT NOW CITED IN TAIPEI: the US removal of Nicolás Maduro is being invoked by TAIWANESE OFFICIALS as a precedent that may EMBOLDEN BEIJING toward Taipei — the argument that a great power extracting a head of state from its own hemisphere erodes the norm smaller states depend on · whatever the merits, the second-order effect is now documented: an intervention justified in narcotics and hemispheric terms is being read in East Asia as a statement about what great powers may do to neighbours · JUL 14-15 — THE TRANSITION GETS A TABLE: Govt (AN President Jorge Rodríguez, lead negotiator) and the OPPOSITION 2015-2020 legislature led by Dinorah Figuera agreed to launch a bilateral technical working group AUG 1 on electoral reforms — the first MUTUAL RECOGNITION between Venezuela's two parallel legislatures, US-backed (Rubio reposted the roadmap; 'strategic patience' 3-phase plan: stabilization→recovery→transition, elections LAST) · THE SPLIT: Machado + González were EXCLUDED — they convened an urgent meeting of Democratic Unitary Platform parties + Panama Manifesto signatories; Figuera's Washington-backed return blindsided Machado's own announced bid to lead negotiations · EARTHQUAKE TOLL (CORRECTED): the Jun 24 twin quakes — Venezuela's worst in ~60 years — killed at least 188 in the first reporting (Jun 25), with the government toll rising to ~1,450 dead, 3,000+ injured and 12,000 displaced by Jun 29 (CFR/NYT). An earlier figure of 4,333 carried in this file was unverified and has been withdrawn · Jul 14: Johann Álvarez named new Chargé d'Affaires to the US · NEW THEATER (backfilled) · Jan 3 2026: Operation Absolute Resolve — US strikes on Caracas, Maduro + Cilia Flores captured, flown to NY on USS IWO JIMA (2h28m op) · VP Delcy Rodríguez sworn interim · Trump: 'We're going to run the country' · naval armada + tanker blockade remain · $2B stranded-crude export deal; first $500M sale ($300M to Caracas) · May: Alex Saab deported · Jun: Tren de Aragua chief Niño Guerrero killed in JOINT US-Venezuela op · Jun: earthquakes; Mexico aid via Rodríguez call · USER WARGAME LAYER: 44-asset pre-war order of battle (Ford CSG, Iwo Jima ARG, El Libertador Iran-UAV hub, S-300 IADS, Amuay/El Palito) staged into master KML · UPDATE (Jul): oil exports at 7-YEAR HIGH (~1M+ bpd, ~1% of global demand); CFR est. ~$8B exported Jan-Apr (a FLOOR — Trump admin shares no data, proceeds routed through Qatar account, transparency questioned by Congress); most goes to US Gulf refineries, then India · Cabello (Interior Min, $25M US bounty) given ultimatum: cooperate w/ transition or face arrest; Padrino ($15M bounty) holds military chain · Rodríguez wants $5B IMF Special Drawing Rights (blocked since 2019) — her key unmet ask; Rubio: Venezuela submits MONTHLY budget requests for State Dept approval · 621 of ~800 political prisoners released; ~400+ still held","leverage":[{"actor":"United States","asset":"Finance/legal HIGH (user matrix confirmed): blockade + OFAC licensing + oil-sale mechanism + offshore armada — coercion without occupation","str":"hi"},{"actor":"China","asset":"80-84% of Venezuela's oil exports (direct/indirect) + $10B debt — the unresolved contradiction in the $100B investment plan","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Rodríguez interim govt + generals","asset":"Colectivos/counterintel street presence; cooperation-for-survival (Saab handover, Guerrero op)","str":"md"},{"actor":"Russia + Iran","asset":"Diluent supply HIGH (naphtha/condensate); El Libertador UAV program; advisory footprint — degraded post-capture","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"US Congress (CRS)","detail":"Administration notified Congress of 'non-international armed conflict' with cartels in Venezuela and beyond; War Powers resolutions (S.J.Res.83/90) considered, not passed"},{"src":"US Treasury TIC / DOJ","detail":"Maduro + Flores arraigned SDNY on narco-terrorism; $50M bounty claimed by US itself"},{"src":"China MFA / EU (Kallas)","detail":"China demands Maduro's immediate release ('blatant use of force against a sovereign state'); EU-minus-Hungary calls for restraint under UN Charter"},{"src":"UK House of Commons Library","detail":"Cuba confirmed 32 soldiers killed; large part of Maduro's security team killed; legality questioned under international law"}],"mil":"The kinetic phase predates our tracked window: Operation Absolute Resolve (Jan 3) — strikes across northern Venezuela suppressing air defenses while a Delta Force/CIA apprehension force took Maduro at Fuerte Tiuna, exfiltrating him aboard the USS Iwo Jima. Since W1 the model is remote coercion: the armada stays offshore, the tanker blockade holds, and strikes on 'drug boats' continue. The June joint US-Venezuela operation that killed Tren de Aragua leader Niño Guerrero shows the interim government now operationally cooperating with its coercer. USER OSINT: the staged 44-asset wargame layer maps the pre-war order of battle — including the Iwo Jima ARG that ultimately carried Maduro out, and El Libertador AB as the Iranian-assisted UAV hub (CAVIM/Mohajer line), the direct cross-link to the Iran theater.","pol":"Trump's 'we're going to run the country' remains policy: Rodríguez governs under threat ('pay a very big price'), opposition leader Machado is sidelined, and Crisis Group warns the transaction-not-transition model leaves the chavista security state intact minus its figurehead. The Donroe Doctrine framing — the right to intervene at will in the hemisphere — is the structural umbrella, with the AES's condemnation linking Pan-African and Latin American sovereignty pushback.","eco":"USER MATRICES (staged): PDVSA sold $17.52B abroad in 2024; Oct 2025 export baseline ~$1.63B/month at Brent, with China taking $1.34B of it — 80-84% of exports. US leverage concentrates in finance/legal (matrix: HIGH) not physical control, exactly matching the post-capture mechanism: blockade + licensing + managed oil sales ($500M sold, $300M remitted). Severe-discount scenario halves export value — the strangulation dial Washington controls.","assess":"Venezuela enters at 71 with a backfilled arc — high but stable, because the kinetic phase ended in January and what remains is an occupation-by-leverage in uneasy equilibrium. The user's pre-war wargame layer proved predictive (it mapped the Iwo Jima ARG that carried Maduro out) and now anchors the theater's geography. The unresolved contradictions set the trajectory: China holds the export market the US plan needs; the generals hold the streets the transition needs; and the coercive model that secured control may be incompatible with the investment climate required to monetize it. JULY UPDATE: the arrangement is functioning as a US-administered extraction — oil at a seven-year high, ~$8B out in four months through an opaque Qatar-routed account Congress can't audit, with Rodríguez trading monthly-budget-approval compliance for diplomatic recognition and sanctions relief. Her one unmet ask is $5B in IMF Special Drawing Rights. The CFR's framing is sharp: five months in, 'nothing has changed on the ground' for the Chavista ruling elite — the US removed the figurehead and monetized the oil while the security state (Cabello, Padrino, both under US bounties) stays in place.","econlev":[{"holder":"China","channel":"Export market","exp":"HIGH","detail":"80-84% of oil exports direct/indirect; $10B debt; >$1B/yr rebranded via Brazil (user matrix)"},{"holder":"United States","channel":"Finance / legal / blockade","exp":"HIGH","detail":"80-84% of oil exports historically to China; NOW US-controlled exports at 7-yr high (~$8B Jan-Apr, floor est.) routed via Qatar account to US Gulf refiners + India"},{"holder":"Russia + Iran","channel":"Diluents / inputs","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Naphtha + condensate supply (Iran ~41.3 kbpd barter 2022-23); 15-yr PDVSA-Roszarubezhneft JV"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇮🇷 Iran","side":"pre-capture regime","flow":"El Libertador UAV hub (Mohajer line — SAME drones as Sahel/Sudan); CAVIM assembly; advisors","tier":"documented","for":"Sanctions-bloc solidarity; diluent-for-crude"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"regime","flow":"80-84% oil-export market; $10B debt; credit-for-oil","tier":"documented","for":"Discounted crude; hemispheric counter-presence"},{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"regime","flow":"Roszarubezhneft JV; naphtha diluent; advisory footprint (degraded post-capture)","tier":"documented","for":"Oil foothold in US sphere"}],"ext_flag":true,"tclass":"conflict","tier":"SEVERE","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"A government was removed, and the oil now moves through an account almost nobody can see. That opacity is not incidental.","claims":[{"claim":"US administration of Venezuelan oil is framed as stabilization/transition.","who":"The US and the interim arrangement — 'transition' is a far easier sell than 'seizure.'","evid":"Revenue reportedly routes through an opaque Qatari account. The mechanism, the beneficiaries, and the accounting are not public. The absence of an audit trail is itself the finding.","status":"UNRESOLVED — follow the account"},{"claim":"Maduro's capture is framed as a counter-narcotics/law-enforcement action.","who":"The US — a criminal frame avoids the words 'regime change' and the legal architecture that comes with them.","evid":"The action removed a head of state and installed an interim government aligned with US energy interests. Both things can be true; only one is being said.","status":"CONTESTED"}]},"history":{"roots":"Venezuela's crisis is the implosion of a petrostate under authoritarian mismanagement. Sitting atop the world's largest proven oil reserves, Venezuela became a socialist showcase under Hugo Chávez (1999-2013), funded by high oil prices. When prices crashed and Chávez died, his successor Nicolás Maduro presided over economic collapse: hyperinflation, the exodus of ~8 million people, and the gutting of democracy. Maduro clung to power through a disputed 2024 election widely seen as stolen from the opposition. US sanctions, recognition fights, and — by 2025-26 — direct US military pressure over drug-trafficking and migration turned Venezuela into a hemispheric flashpoint. In January 2026 the standoff ended not by election or uprising but by foreign extraction: a US military raid on Caracas seized Maduro and flew him to New York for trial. The roots: overdependence on oil, the hollowing of institutions, and a sovereignty so eroded that the presidency was ultimately settled from Washington.","timeline":[{"date":"1999","e":"Hugo Chávez begins his 'Bolivarian Revolution.'"},{"date":"2013","e":"Chávez dies; Maduro succeeds him as oil prices peak."},{"date":"2014-19","e":"Economic collapse; hyperinflation; mass emigration begins."},{"date":"2019","e":"Guaidó's rival-presidency bid fails to dislodge Maduro."},{"date":"2024","e":"Maduro claims a disputed election win over Edmundo González."},{"date":"2025","e":"US ramps up pressure — sanctions, cartel-designations, naval posture."},{"date":"3 Jan 2026","e":"US military raid on Caracas ('Operation Resolve') captures Maduro and Cilia Flores; flown to New York on narco-terrorism charges. Much of his security detail killed; Cuba says 32 of its personnel died. Trump: the US will 'run the country' until a transition."},{"date":"5 Jan 2026","e":"Supreme Court orders VP Delcy Rodríguez to assume presidential powers; sworn in as interim president before the National Assembly."},{"date":"24 Jun 2026","e":"Twin earthquakes devastate the northern coast — the worst in nearly six decades; state of emergency, national power and internet outages, Caracas airport damaged."},{"date":"Jul 2026","e":"Government and the 2015-2020 opposition legislature agree to an Aug 1 technical working group on electoral reform — Machado and González excluded."}],"actors":[{"name":"Nicolás Maduro (captured)","bio":"President 2013-2026. Held power through the military and disputed elections through economic collapse and the exodus of a quarter of the population — until a US raid seized him on 3 Jan 2026. Now in US custody in New York facing narco-terrorism charges he denies."},{"name":"Delcy Rodríguez","bio":"Maduro's vice president, sworn in as interim president on 5 Jan 2026 by order of the Supreme Court — continuity of the Chavista state apparatus under US supervision."},{"name":"Jorge Rodríguez","bio":"National Assembly president and the government's lead negotiator in the transition talks; brother of the interim president. The regime's designated interlocutor with Washington."},{"name":"Opposition (Machado / González / Figuera)","bio":"María Corina Machado and Edmundo González, widely seen as the true winners of the 2024 vote — and now excluded from the Washington-backed talks, which instead seated Dinorah Figuera's 2015-2020 legislature. The opposition's split is the transition's fault line."},{"name":"United States","bio":"Removed the head of state by force, declared it would 'run the country' pending transition, and sequenced oil licensing ahead of elections. The decisive actor in Venezuelan politics, openly."},{"name":"Military (FANB)","bio":"The institution that kept Maduro in power and did not fight for him; its acquiescence is what makes the interim arrangement hold."}],"bridge":"Coronil's 'magical state' — a petrostate that performs miracles with oil money until the oil turns on it — is the arc from Chávez's missions to the exodus, and the 2026 transition is the sequel: Washington's phased plan (oil licenses first, elections last) rebuilds the extraction relationship before the democracy, with Figuera's negotiators seated and Machado's excluded — the patron choosing its counterpart, as in every Venezuelan cycle since the Gómez concessions. The 4,333 earthquake dead and the doubled oil exports in the same quarter is the magical state's oldest juxtaposition: the subsoil recovering faster than the society above it.","sources":[{"work":"The Magical State: Nature, Money, and Modernity in Venezuela","author":"Fernando Coronil","year":"1997","note":"The Venezuelan anthropologist's classic — the petrostate as performance, boom to collapse."},{"work":"Dragon in the Tropics: Venezuela and the Legacy of Hugo Chávez","author":"Javier Corrales & Michael Penfold","year":"2011 (2nd ed. 2015)","note":"The institutional account of the Chávez-Maduro system."},{"work":"UN Fact-Finding Mission on Venezuela reports","author":"UN HRC FFM","year":"2020-25","note":"Primary documentation of the repression apparatus the transition must dismantle or inherit."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"The magical state: a petro-performance collapsing as the oil turns, the transition rebuilding extraction before democracy.","alt":"A sanctions-primary reading (Chavista) blames US pressure for the collapse; a governance-primary reading blames systemic mismanagement. Both are now overtaken by a third: after the Jan-2026 raid, Venezuelan outcomes are substantially decided in Washington — an intervention reading in which domestic causation matters less than occupation politics.","why":"Cuban and Venezuelan economists document structural failures the embargo aggravated but did not author; sanctions are logged as multiplier, not root. Since the capture, however, the platform weights the intervention frame as primary for current dynamics — the transition's sequencing (oil licences before elections) is set externally. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"CNN · Venezuela's acting president faces her worst crisis (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.cnn.com/2026/07/08/americas/venezuela-earthquake-delcy-rodriguez-intl-latam"},{"t":"Euronews · interim leader defends earthquake response","u":"https://www.euronews.com/2026/07/03/venezuelas-interim-leader-defends-earthquake-response-as-death-toll-tops-2500"},{"t":"Latin Times · quake toll and mandate deadline","u":"https://www.latintimes.com/venezuelas-quake-toll-nears-3000-constitutional-deadline-delcy-rodriguezs-mandate-runs-out-598120"},{"t":"PBS NewsHour · Taiwan officials on Venezuela precedent","u":"https://www.pbs.org/newshour/classroom/daily-news-lessons/2026/01/taiwan-officials-fear-us-actions-in-venezuela-may-embolden-china"},{"t":"House of Commons Library: The US capture of Maduro","u":"https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/research-briefings/cbp-10452/"},{"t":"Britannica: why Maduro was captured","u":"https://www.britannica.com/question/Why-was-Venezuelan-President-Nicolas-Maduro-captured-by-the-U-S-in-2026"}],"human":{"dead":1450,"disp":8000000,"famine":false,"aid":"transition-dependent","note":"~8M have left the country since 2015 — the hemisphere's largest displacement crisis. The Jun-2026 twin earthquakes killed ~1,450 (CFR/NYT; an earlier 4,333 figure carried here was unverified and withdrawn), injured 3,000+ and displaced 12,000.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"71 = active armed conflict, a recorded human toll, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"26 Jul 2026","c":"CORRECTION: this file recorded the January US raid that seized Maduro but MISSED the 24 June twin earthquakes (~3,000 dead, $37B damage) and the expiry of Rodríguez’s 180-day interim mandate. Escalation read was carried as DESCENDING; corrected to VOLATILE. Logged, not overwritten."},{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Provenance, check-mode and escalation-derivation fields added; no substantive content change this cycle."}],"spill":[{"n":"United States","dir":"in","w":"The removal of Maduro — now cited by Taiwanese officials as a precedent that may embolden Beijing. A hemispheric intervention read as a global signal.","id":"us-politics"},{"n":"Colombia","dir":"both","w":"The ELN operates on both sides of the border with tolerated sanctuary in Venezuela; the Tibú airport drone strike was in the border department.","id":"colombia"},{"n":"Taiwan","dir":"out","w":"Taipei invokes the Maduro removal as evidence a great power extracting a neighbour's head of state erodes the norm smaller states depend on — the Donroe-to-Taiwan link, made by Taiwan.","id":"taiwan"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Expired interim mandate atop a 3,000-death disaster","dir":"volatile","phase":"Post-intervention instability — not de-escalation","up":["Rodríguez's 180-day interim mandate has EXPIRED with no agreed succession","$37B in earthquake damage against a state analysts say lacks the capacity to respond","Public anger at the disaster response converting into political crisis","ELN cross-border activity in the border departments"],"down":["A negotiated transition Machado has said she wants without 'surrender or revenge'","International reconstruction assistance at the scale the damage requires","An agreed constitutional path out of the interim arrangement","The armed forces staying out of the succession question"],"fw":["deterrence","conflict-curve","ripeness"]},"macro":{"growth":"Contracting; $37B in earthquake damage","infl":"Elevated","src":"Reporting, 2026","note":"The June earthquakes caused an estimated $37 BILLION in damage against a state that analysts say lacks the budget, personnel, equipment and machinery to respond. Reconstruction on that scale is not financeable domestically, which makes external terms the decisive variable in the post-Maduro transition."},"inherit":{"name":"The Monroe Doctrine, Restated","est":"1823","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Monroe declared the hemisphere closed to European recolonisation in 1823 and, by the Roosevelt Corollary of 1904, asserted a US right to <b>exercise international police power</b> in the Americas. It has been invoked to justify interventions in Cuba, the Dominican Republic, Haiti, Nicaragua, Guatemala, Panama, Grenada and Chile.","now":"<b>Maduro was seized in a US military raid in January 2026.</b> This board tracks the framing as the Donroe Doctrine — and its most consequential effect has been outside the hemisphere: <b>Taiwanese officials publicly cite the removal as a precedent that may embolden Beijing</b>, on the argument that a great power extracting a neighbour's head of state erodes the norm smaller states depend on. Delcy Rodríguez's 180-day interim mandate has since expired with no agreed succession, and June's twin earthquakes killed roughly 3,000 and caused $37bn in damage against a state analysts say cannot respond.","change":"<b>Nothing structural.</b> The doctrine has never been renounced, and the 2026 intervention is its most direct application in decades."}},{"id":"colombia","cat":"political","region":"South America","subregion":"South America","name":"Colombia — Rightward Rupture + US Collision","sub":"Petro rejects results, alleges fraud/coup · transition SUSPENDED · inauguration Aug 7","domain":"Electoral Rupture / Security Pivot","sev":"high","status":"volatile","since":"2025","esc":76,"lat":4.7,"lon":-74.1,"trend":"up","w14":false,"diplomacy_active":true,"esc_history":[73,73,73,73,73,73,73,73,73,76],"cas":"Election-night clashes in Cali; armed-group coercion reported in rural areas","disp":"3M+ Venezuelan refugees hosted","flag":"🇨🇴","note":"1 AUG 2026 — A TRUCK BOMB IN CÚCUTA, SIX DAYS BEFORE THE INAUGURATION: at least ELEVEN PEOPLE INJURED — EIGHT POLICE OFFICERS AND THREE CIVILIANS — when a truck bomb detonated near a police station in CÚCUTA, Norte de Santander, on the VENEZUELAN BORDER · security secretary George Quintero: 'an atrocious, violent act' · THE TIMING IS THE MESSAGE: this is SIX DAYS BEFORE the inauguration of president-elect ABELARDO DE LA ESPRIELLA, who has committed to ending negotiation in favour of a militarised approach. An attack in the ELN's core border department, against police, immediately before that transition, reads as a demonstration that the incoming policy will be contested from day one · the file's escalation read — that the negotiated track is closing while the groups are at their strongest since 2016 — now has its first hard event · MAJOR UPDATE — A PRESIDENT-ELECT AND THE NUMBERS ON TOTAL PEACE'S FAILURE (verified 31 Jul 2026) · ABELARDO DE LA ESPRIELLA HAS BEEN ELECTED PRESIDENT. InSight Crime's read: the victory reflects public frustration with rising violence and the perceived failure of Petro's Total Peace, and the incoming administration is expected to ABANDON DIALOGUE with the ELN and FARC dissidents for a MILITARISED APPROACH EXPLICITLY INSPIRED BY BUKELE, with much closer US alignment — expanded security cooperation, intelligence sharing and counternarcotics · THE FAILURE IS QUANTIFIED: armed group ranks GREW 85% under Total Peace — at least 25,000 members, up from 6,500 in 2017. CLAN DEL GOLFO up 30% to 9,840; ELN at 6,810, up 9%; FARC dissident factions up by almost a quarter · FIP's Gerson Arias: 'Paz Total was based on a state ceasefire — but without any conditions put on the groups, such as ceasing recruitment, including child recruitment, or ending expansion. As such, the policy gave a gigantic strategic advantage to the armed groups' · Petro acknowledged the policy 'had not achieved peace' · STRUCTURAL POINT THIS BOARD SHOULD HOLD: a unilateral ceasefire without conditions transferred time and territory to the groups it was meant to demobilise. The ELN's lack of unified command compounds it — fronts ignore national-level agreements, so a signature at the top does not bind the field · the January 2025 Catatumbo offensive produced what the UN Verification Mission called one of the most serious escalations since the 2016 accord · JUL 2026 — A DRONE ATTACK ON AN AIRPORT, BLAMED ON THE ELN: Colombia's military says a DRONE ATTACK on TIBÚ AIRPORT in NORTE DE SANTANDER — near the VENEZUELAN BORDER — injured three workers and damaged the facility, attributing it to the National Liberation Army · the significance is the capability, not the casualties: drone strikes on fixed infrastructure move the ELN from a guerrilla force to one that can contest the state's control of transport nodes, in the department where its cross-border sanctuary is most established · JUL 15-16 — THE RIGHT WINS THE RUNOFF (preliminary): Trump-backed far-right candidate Abelardo de la Espriella celebrates winning the preliminary count in a razor-tight presidential runoff — the answer to the 2026 question this theater's history posed: the left's experiment ends, Washington's decertification pressure worked as electoral leverage, and 'total peace' policy dies with the transition · contested-count risk in a margin this thin · NEW THEATER (backfilled) · MAY 31 first round: Espriella 43.7% / Cepeda 40.9% · JUN 21 runoff: Espriella 49.66% vs 48.70% (~250k votes; 63.6% turnout; 12.9M votes = most in Colombian history) · certified Jun 24; Cepeda conceded; Petro alleged fraud/foreign interference but recognized results · INAUGURATION AUG 7 · pledges: 90-day military offensive, 10 Bukele-style mega-prisons, fracking, end peace talks, Israel ties + Jerusalem embassy · pre-arc: DEA named Petro 'priority target', US sanctioned him · DISPUTED: worker-coercion complaints (59 companies), armed-group pressure, AtlasIntel poll suspension · inauguration AUG 7 approaching; transition tensions rising · UPDATE (Jul 7-10): Petro REFUSES to recognize Espriella's win, alleges 'electoral fraud via algorithms' + 'prohibited foreign financing' (accused Thomas Greg & Sons + Israel, no evidence presented) · Historic Pact coalition filing lawsuit to ANNUL the runoff · Espriella suspended the transition, accused Petro of attempting a 'coup d'etat' to cling to power, called on the Public Force + international community to defend constitutional order · election widely assessed free/fair (251,800-vote margin certified Jun 24) · incoming FM Bula: will CLOSE embassies in Cuba+Nicaragua ('won't legitimize dictatorships'), restore US+Israel ties, stay 'very close' to Venezuela's Rodríguez transition · inauguration Aug 7","leverage":[{"actor":"Espriella (from Aug 7)","asset":"Trump endorsement + mandate for force; 12.9M votes but razor margin + divided Congress","str":"md"},{"actor":"United States","asset":"Sanctions precedent on Petro; DEA targeting; tariff/decert threats — hemisphere's template case","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Armed groups (ELN/dissidents/Clan del Golfo)","asset":"Expected to strike back at the 90-day offensive; rural electoral coercion already shown","str":"md"},{"actor":"Petro camp","asset":"48.7% opposition bloc + fraud narrative; street mobilization capacity","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Registraduría / CNE","detail":"Certified Jun 24: 49.66% vs 48.70%; 63.6% turnout; AtlasIntel polls suspended May 19 over methodology (probe ongoing)"},{"src":"US State (Rubio) / Trump","detail":"Endorsement + 'Colombia's best days are ahead'; earlier sanctioned Petro; DEA priority-target designation"},{"src":"Colombia Ombudsman / Min. of Labor","detail":"Pre-election warnings on armed-group coercion; complaints from workers of 59 companies alleging vote coercion"}],"mil":"The security stakes activate at inauguration (Aug 7): Espriella has pledged a 90-day military offensive against armed groups, ten mega-prisons on the Bukele model, and intensified strikes on smuggling aircraft/boats. Analysts expect ELN, FARC dissidents, and Clan del Golfo to hit back — election night already saw clashes in Cali. The 'total peace' talks era ends.","pol":"The full arc fits inside our tracked window: DEA priority-target designation and US sanctions on Petro; the May 31 first round; the June 21 razor-margin runoff (250k votes); certification June 24 with Petro alleging fraud and foreign interference while recognizing results. Colombia completes the regional rightward sweep (Milei, Kast, Noboa, Paz, Bukele...). Election-integrity threads stay flagged DISPUTED, not credited.","eco":"Espriella pledges fracking and reversal of Petro's hydrocarbon-contract moratorium — an oil-and-gas reopening aligned with Washington. High public debt constrains the program; the razor margin constrains everything else.","assess":"Colombia rises to 70 (ELEVATED), trend up as the August 7 handover turns turbulent. Outgoing President Petro refuses to recognize Espriella's certified win, alleging algorithmic fraud and foreign financing (without presented evidence), and his coalition is suing to annul the runoff — prompting Espriella to suspend the transition and accuse Petro of attempting a coup. The election was widely assessed as free and fair. The incoming government's foreign-policy pivot is sharp: closing embassies in Cuba and Nicaragua, restoring US and Israel ties, and staying close to Venezuela's Rodríguez transition — hardening the regional pressure bloc around Caracas. The inauguration-day transition crisis is the near-term fuse.","tclass":"watch","tier":"SEVERE","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Political","econlev":[{"holder":"United States","channel":"Trade + certification","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Decertification + tariff leverage over an economy deeply tied to US trade; the incoming Espriella government will realign toward Washington"}],"contested":{"summary":"A certified election, and an outgoing president calling it a coup.","claims":[{"claim":"Petro alleges 'electoral fraud via algorithms' and prohibited foreign financing.","who":"Petro — annulling the runoff is the only path to retaining influence.","evid":"NO evidence presented. The election was widely assessed as free and fair; the 251,800-vote margin was certified Jun 24. His coalition is suing to annul.","status":"UNSUPPORTED"},{"claim":"Espriella accuses Petro of attempting a coup d'état.","who":"Espriella — framing the dispute as a coup mobilizes the security forces and international opinion behind him.","evid":"Petro is disputing a certified result and his coalition seeks annulment. Whether that constitutes an attempted coup or a legal challenge is the contested question.","status":"CONTESTED"}]},"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"strained partner","flow":"Decertification tensions, aid conditionality, and friction with Petro over drug policy and Venezuela; historic Plan Colombia legacy","tier":"documented","for":"Cocaine-supply reduction and a stable partner on Venezuela's border"},{"actor":"🇻🇪 Venezuela","side":"cross-border spillover","flow":"Shared border with armed-group sanctuary (ELN, FARC dissidents), migration flows, and Maduro-Petro diplomatic complexity","tier":"documented","for":"Buffer and leverage; the border economy sustains armed groups on both sides"},{"actor":"🏴 Armed groups (ELN / dissidents)","side":"non-state","flow":"Control of coca corridors and mining; 'total peace' negotiations stalled","tier":"documented","for":"Drug and gold revenue; territorial autonomy the state cannot yet reclaim"}],"ext_flag":true,"history":{"roots":"Colombia is the longest war in the hemisphere refusing to fully end. Six decades of conflict — FARC's Marxist insurgency, paramilitary terror, cartel wars — killed ~450,000 before the 2016 peace accord demobilized the FARC. But the state never filled the vacated territory: FARC dissidents, the ELN, and the Gulf Clan carved it up, funded by record coca output and illegal gold. Petro — the first leftist president, an ex-guerrilla — bet on 'total peace' negotiations; the talks stalled, the violence adapted. US relations, once Plan Colombia's showcase, strained over drug policy and decertification threats under Trump. The 2026 election decides whether the left's experiment continues.","timeline":[{"date":"1964","e":"FARC founded — the long war begins."},{"date":"1980s-90s","e":"Cartel wars (Escobar); paramilitary expansion."},{"date":"2000","e":"Plan Colombia — the US partnership era."},{"date":"2016","e":"Havana accord — FARC demobilizes; the vacuum opens."},{"date":"2022","e":"Petro — the first leftist president; 'total peace' launched."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Talks stall; dissident violence up; US decertification friction; election year."}],"actors":[{"name":"Gustavo Petro","bio":"The ex-guerrilla president — total peace stalled, US relations strained, legacy on the 2026 ballot."},{"name":"ELN + FARC dissidents","bio":"The accord's orphans and refusers — coca- and gold-funded, Venezuela-sanctuaried."},{"name":"The Gulf Clan","bio":"The paramilitary inheritance turned narco-corporation — the largest cocaine exporter."},{"name":"The US","bio":"The half-century partner turned skeptic — decertification threats as leverage over drug policy."}],"bridge":"Colombia's own Truth Commission wrote the bridge already: the war persisted because it profited — land concentrated, coca monetized, politics armed — and peace fails where those returns survive demobilization. The 2026 runoff verdict (the Trump-backed right on preliminary count) tests the Commission's warning in real time: 'total peace' dies, US decertification pressure becomes electoral leverage vindicated, and the vacated-territory economy (dissidents, ELN, Gulf Clan) meets a state promising iron fist over negotiation — the cycle the ¡Bas