ine":false,"aid":"functioning","note":"Six decades of war: ~450k killed (Truth Commission-lineage estimates) and one of the world's largest internally displaced populations (~7M registered victims). Post-accord violence continues against social leaders and ex-combatants.","est":false,"basis":"Counted — Colombia's Truth Commission (Comisión de la Verdad) documented approximately 450,000 conflict deaths for 1985-2018, roughly 80% of them civilians. The figure is cumulative for the armed conflict and does not cover 2019-2026, so it understates the current total."},"checkmode":"SWEPT","escwhy":"76 = active multi-actor armed conflict (base ~70) raised for armed group strength up 85% under a collapsed peace policy, an incoming administration expected to abandon negotiation entirely, and ELN capability now including drone strikes on fixed infrastructure. Not higher because there is no front line and the state holds the cities; not lower because the negotiated track is closing as the groups are at their strongest since 2016. · NOTE ON THE TRAJECTORY CHART: the step in this theater's history is a CORRECTION TO THIS BOARD'S READ, not a real-world escalation — the board carried 73 through the election of a president committed to ending the peace policy; the step to 76 is the correction. The flat line before it is what the platform actually recorded, and it is left visible rather than smoothed.","log":[{"v":"v60","d":"31 Jul 2026","c":"MAJOR UPDATE: file lacked the election of Abelardo de la Espriella and the quantified failure of Total Peace (ranks +85% to 25,000). Escalation raised 73 → 76 and the read rebuilt — the previous down-lever \"renewed Total Peace negotiations\" is void, since the incoming administration is expected to abandon them."},{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Three actors added (ELN, FARC dissidents, Clan del Golfo)"}],"spill":[{"n":"Venezuela","dir":"both","w":"ELN and FARC-dissident sanctuary across the border; the Tibú airport drone attack struck Norte de Santander near the frontier.","id":"venezuela"},{"n":"United States","dir":"in","w":"Counter-narcotics pressure and the wider hemispheric posture that produced the Venezuela intervention next door.","id":"us-politics"},{"n":"Ecuador / Pacific","dir":"out","w":"Cocaine trafficking corridors run south and to the Pacific; the war economy the Truth Commission says sustains the conflict crosses borders by design."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Total Peace failed; a militarised administration incoming","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation — policy reversal ahead","up":["The incoming de la Espriella administration abandoning dialogue for a Bukele-style militarised approach","Armed group ranks up 85% under the ceasefires now ending — 25,000 members against 6,500 in 2017","ELN fronts that ignore national-level agreements, so no signature binds the field","Venezuelan sanctuary and the post-Maduro transition on the border"],"down":["Localised agreements that worked — Comuneros del Sur handed over weapons for a legal mining district","Judicial submission processes for criminal structures rather than political negotiation","Rural development substituting for coca income","US security cooperation actually targeting the trafficking economy rather than the fighters"],"fw":["ripeness","resource-conflict","conflict-curve"]},"corrdelta":"This period's change is a CORRECTION to this board's read, not movement in the theater.","macro":{"growth":"2.6% (2026f) · fourth straight year below potential · GDP $539.5B","infl":"6.23% forecast — target not reached until 2030","src":"Fedesarrollo; Banco de la República Monetary Policy Report Jan 2026; OECD Economic Outlook 2026/1","note":"THE INCOMING PRESIDENT INHERITS A FISCAL EMERGENCY, WHICH CONSTRAINS THE MILITARISED SECURITY POLICY HE CAMPAIGNED ON. Analysts describe a 'perfect storm': credit rating downgrades, BanRep hiking 200bp to 11.25% with 11.50% expected, a 23.7% MINIMUM WAGE INCREASE among the largest in Colombian history, Constitutional Court suspension of emergency tax measures, LOSS OF THE IMF SAFETY NET, and inflation stuck above target until 2030 · THE FISCAL RULE WAS SUSPENDED and the deficit reached 6.2% OF GDP, pushing public debt above 61% and keeping Colombia below investment grade · 33% remain in poverty with a Gini of 53.9 · the point for this board: de la Espriella has promised a Bukele-style security build-out to a state that has lost its IMF backstop, cannot borrow cheaply, and is fighting armed groups whose ranks grew 85% while it was not fighting them."},"inherit":{"name":"Land Concentration and La Violencia","est":"1500s-1958","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Spanish <b>encomienda</b> and <b>hacienda</b> tenure concentrated land in few hands and set a pattern independence never broke. The 1928 army massacre of striking United Fruit workers at Ciénaga made the alignment explicit — the state's force serving foreign agricultural capital. <b>La Violencia</b> then killed some 200,000 between 1948 and 1958 in a partisan war that displaced peasants en masse, and the National Front pact that ended it excluded everyone outside the two parties, closing the political route just as the land question sharpened.","now":"<b>Colombia's Gini remains 53.9 and land distribution is among the most unequal in the hemisphere.</b> Every armed group on this file taxes or contests rural territory the state has never effectively administered. <b>Total Peace's failure is quantified: ranks grew 85% to 25,000 while a unilateral state ceasefire ran without conditions on recruitment or expansion.</b> The Truth Commission documented roughly 450,000 conflict deaths for 1985-2018, about 80% civilians.","change":"The 2016 FARC accord included rural reform provisions that have been substantially underimplemented. <b>President-elect de la Espriella is expected to abandon negotiation entirely for a militarised approach</b> — inheriting a suspended fiscal rule, a 6.2% deficit and no IMF backstop."},"monitors":[{"t":"Colombia Truth Commission final report","u":"https://www.comisiondelaverdad.co/"}]},{"id":"mexico","cat":"political","region":"North America","subregion":"North America","name":"Mexico — Cartel-FTO Confrontation / Sovereignty Squeeze","sub":"Sinaloa governor indicted · 'hitting land' threat · USMCA lever","domain":"Coercive Diplomacy / Cartel War","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"Jan 2026","esc":62,"lat":19.43,"lon":-99.13,"trend":"up","w14":false,"esc_history":[61,62,62,62,62,62,62,62,62,62],"cas":"Cartel violence ongoing; no US kinetic strikes on Mexican soil to date","disp":"Record-low border migration flows","flag":"🇲🇽","note":"25 JUL 2026 — THIRD REVIEW ROUND CONCLUDES, WHICH COMPLICATES THE NON-RENEWAL FRAMING: Mexico and the US concluded a THIRD ROUND of USMCA REVIEW TALKS, advancing discussions on economic security, labour, agriculture, metals, electronic payments and the automotive sector · read against the USTR's statement that the agreement WILL NOT BE RENEWED: the US position is annual review and amendment rather than termination. This board recorded non-renewal as a rupture; the mechanism is closer to converting a treaty into a rolling negotiation — still a structural downgrade in predictability, but not a cliff · JUL 2026 — (carry) the USMCA non-renewal and the Sinaloa civil war remain the two live variables; no further material this cycle beyond the trade decision already logged · JUL 2026 — USMCA WILL NOT BE RENEWED: US Trade Representative Jamieson Greer has stated the United States WILL NOT RENEW the USMCA agreement · this is the single largest economic variable on Mexico's board — roughly 80% of Mexican exports go to the US under its terms. Non-renewal converts a rules-based framework into bilateral leverage exercised annually, and it lands while US pressure over cartel designation is already live · NEW THEATER (backfilled from W1) · APR 29 (=our W1): DOJ indicted 10 Mexican officials incl. SITTING Sinaloa Gov. Rubén Rocha Moya (AMLO ally, Morena), Sen. Inzunza, Culiacán mayor — extradition demanded · Nat'l Drug Control Strategy 2026: cartels = FTOs, fentanyl = WMD, extraterritorial-ops authority · Trump post-Maduro: 'We are going to start hitting land... The cartels are running Mexico' · Sheinbaum: charges 'political'; first public criticism of Amb. Johnson in 14 months; ~70% approval · cooperation continues: 10,000 troops to border, 90+ extraditions · Jun: Sheinbaum rejects Espriella labeling Mexican cartels military targets; warns vs Mexico as Nov-2026-midterm rhetoric · UPDATE (Jul): Sheinbaum publicly rebuffed Colombia President-elect Espriella's designation of Mexican cartels as military targets in Colombian territory ('each sovereign nation manages its own internal security'); warned again against Mexico as US Nov-2026-midterm rhetoric · skipped Trump's 'Shield of the Americas' Doral summit (with Lula + Petro) — the 3 big left-led economies absent · Americas Counter-Cartel Coalition (A3C, Hegseth/SOUTHCOM Miami) advancing around her · still NO US strikes on Mexican soil; Rocha Moya extradition decision still pending","leverage":[{"actor":"United States","asset":"FTO/WMD legal architecture + extraterritorial authority + tariff/USMCA-nonrenewal threat + indictment-extradition pressure","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Sheinbaum government","asset":"Migration cooperation (Trump's signature win depends on it) + 80% of exports to US = mutual dependence + 70% approval","str":"md"},{"actor":"Cartels (Sinaloa/CJNG)","asset":"Reduced fentanyl shipments under pressure — adaptive, not defeated; state-capture depth the indictments expose","str":"md"},{"actor":"Morena party dynamics","asset":"Rocha Moya = AMLO ally; extradition decision risks splintering the ruling party","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"US DOJ / DEA (Cole)","detail":"Apr 29: 10 officials charged incl. sitting Sinaloa governor — 'No one is above the law'; extradition requested"},{"src":"National Drug Control Strategy 2026","detail":"Cartels designated FTOs; fentanyl classified WMD; measurable compliance benchmarks; expanded extraterritorial authority"},{"src":"Sheinbaum govt","detail":"Rejected charges as political; 10,000 security forces deployed to border; 90+ extraditions delivered; record-low migration flows"}],"mil":"No US strikes on Mexican soil yet — but the architecture is built: FTO designations enable military targeting authorities, fentanyl-as-WMD reframes trafficking as a weapons issue, and Congress has been notified of a 'non-international armed conflict' with cartels. Trump's post-Maduro statement — 'we are going to start hitting land... the cartels are running Mexico' — is the declared trajectory. Mexico's counter: 10,000 troops to the border and visible enforcement wins that reduce the pretext.","pol":"The April 29 indictment of a sitting governor (and AMLO ally) converted the pressure campaign into a domestic crisis for Sheinbaum: extraditing Rocha Moya risks splintering Morena; refusing risks tariffs, USMCA non-renewal, or worse. Her balancing act — cooperation on migration/extraditions while defending sovereignty rhetorically — has held approval near 70%, but the US strategy's compliance benchmarks are designed to force choices. She has also pushed back regionally, rejecting Espriella's 'military target' framing of Mexican cartels and warning against Mexico as US midterm rhetoric.","eco":"The US takes ~80% of Mexico's exports; USMCA review is the standing guillotine. Compliance benchmarks now reach into Mexico's financial sector. Cartel adaptation (reduced fentanyl shipments) shows economic pressure registering inside illicit markets too.","assess":"Mexico enters at 62, trend up. This is the hemisphere's most consequential slow-burn: the Venezuela precedent plus the FTO/WMD legal stack means the distance from indictments to kinetic action is shorter than it has ever been, while mutual dependence (migration for Trump, exports for Mexico) is the stabilizer neither side can afford to break. The Rocha Moya extradition decision is the fuse to watch — it forces Sheinbaum to choose between her party and her northern flank. JULY UPDATE: Sheinbaum is holding the line — no US strikes have hit Mexican soil, she skipped Trump's Doral 'Shield of the Americas' summit alongside Lula and Petro (the three big left-led economies conspicuously absent), and she rebuffed Colombia's president-elect for declaring Mexican cartels military targets. Her 'each nation manages its own security' sovereignty framing holds at ~70% approval, but the A3C counter-cartel coalition is consolidating around her and the Rocha Moya extradition fuse is still unlit.","tclass":"watch","tier":"ELEVATED","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Political","econlev":[{"holder":"United States","channel":"Trade (USMCA) + tariffs","exp":"HIGH","detail":"~80% of Mexican exports go to the US — the ultimate asymmetric lever, now tied explicitly to cartel enforcement"}],"contested":{"summary":"Mexico's violence is the subject of a live framing contest with direct policy consequences: whether cartels are criminal enterprises or terrorist organisations.","claims":[{"claim":"Mexican cartels should be designated foreign terrorist organisations, justifying unilateral US action.","who":"US political figures; opposed by the Mexican government.","evid":"Designation carries legal consequences enabling military options and exposing anyone transacting with designated groups to sanctions. Analysts note cartels lack the political-ideological objective the FTO framework assumes — they seek profit and impunity, not governance change. Mexico frames unilateral action as a sovereignty violation and points to US demand and US-sourced weapons as the upstream drivers.","status":"FRAMING-CONTEST"},{"claim":"Homicide reductions reported by the government reflect real declines.","who":"Mexican federal authorities.","evid":"Disappearances — over 100,000 registered — are counted separately from homicides, and bodies recovered from clandestine graves may not enter homicide statistics for the year of death. Analysts caution that the two series must be read together.","status":"METHODOLOGICALLY-CONTESTED"}]},"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"pressure + partner","flow":"Tariff threats, fentanyl-and-migration leverage, cartel terrorist-designations, and USMCA dependence; possible unilateral strike rhetoric on cartel targets","tier":"documented","for":"Border control, drug-flow reduction, and nearshoring supply chains away from China"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"economic","flow":"Investment and use of Mexico as a tariff-arbitrage assembly platform into the US market","tier":"documented","for":"A back door into North American supply chains around US-China trade barriers"},{"actor":"🏴 Cartels (CJNG/Sinaloa)","side":"non-state power","flow":"Territorial control, fentanyl production, and state-capture of institutions — the de facto external-actor problem inside Mexico's own borders","tier":"documented","for":"Drug revenue and impunity; the state's monopoly on force is the contested asset"}],"ext_flag":true,"history":{"roots":"Mexico's security crisis is the cartel wars' third decade meeting a transformed state. The 2006 militarization ('Calderón's war') fragmented the cartels into warring factions — CJNG and the Sinaloa network chief among them — that diversified from drugs into extortion, fuel theft, and territorial governance. Fentanyl made them Washington's problem; ~30,000 homicides a year made them Mexico's. Morena's dominance (AMLO, now Sheinbaum) rebuilt the state's social legitimacy while militarizing security further. Trump's second term weaponized the relationship: tariffs, terrorist designations, strike threats — sovereignty versus supply-chain integration, with USMCA as the hostage.","timeline":[{"date":"2006","e":"Calderón militarizes the drug war — the fragmentation begins."},{"date":"2014","e":"Ayotzinapa disappearances — the impunity emblem."},{"date":"2019","e":"Culiacanazo — the state releases El Chapo's son under fire."},{"date":"2024","e":"Sheinbaum elected; Sinaloa civil war erupts (Mayo Zambada's betrayal-capture)."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Cartels designated terrorist organizations; tariff-and-strike pressure; record extraditions as bargaining chips."}],"actors":[{"name":"Claudia Sheinbaum","bio":"The president holding sovereignty's line — cooperating on cartels and migration while refusing US unilateralism, with USMCA leverage cutting both ways."},{"name":"CJNG + the Sinaloa factions","bio":"The warring networks — fentanyl's manufacturers, territory's governors, and now designated terrorists."},{"name":"The US (Trump)","bio":"The pressure engine — tariffs, designations, strike talk; the border is the leverage and the flashpoint."},{"name":"The military (SEDENA)","bio":"The institution that won the state's security portfolio — airports, trains, customs — power without accountability."}],"bridge":"Trejo and Ley's finding decodes the present: cartel violence in Mexico tracks political transition — fragmentation of protection rackets when parties alternate — which is why militarization keeps winning budgets and losing wars. The Trump-era layer (designations, tariffs, strike talk) imports the hemisphere's oldest asymmetry into the relationship's newest instrument: supply-chain integration as hostage. Sheinbaum's line — cooperation without subordination — is the sovereignty position every Mexican government since 1848 has had to hold with the leverage it has; the extraditions-as-bargaining-chips show what that looks like priced. · AND NOW, 2025-26: Cartels designated terrorist organizations; tariff-and-strike pressure; record extraditions as bargaining chips.","sources":[{"work":"Votes, Drugs, and Violence: The Political Logic of Criminal Wars in Mexico","author":"Guillermo Trejo & Sandra Ley","year":"2020","note":"The scholarly standard — why democratization fragmented the cartel order into war."},{"work":"GIEI Ayotzinapa reports","author":"Interdisciplinary Group of Independent Experts","year":"2015-23","note":"The primary forensic record of the impunity emblem."},{"work":"El narco: Inside Mexico's Criminal Insurgency","author":"Ioan Grillo","year":"2011","note":"The narrative history of the drug war's construction."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"Criminal-market fragmentation under political transition (Trejo-Ley) — the platform's read here is already criminal-incentives, not extraction.","alt":"A state-capture reading — cartels as instruments of political-protection networks rather than autonomous markets — would invert cause and effect; some scholarship supports partial capture.","why":"Fragmentation has the strongest empirical base; capture is logged where documented (Ayotzinapa). HIGH, held because this theater never leaned on the extraction lens."},"refs":[{"t":"Anadolu · third USMCA review round (25 Jul 2026)","u":"https://aa.com.tr/en/world/morning-briefing-july-25-2026/4008806"},{"t":"Crisis Group: Mexico","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/latin-america-caribbean/mexico"},{"t":"InSight Crime · Mexico organised crime","u":"https://insightcrime.org/mexico-organized-crime-news/"},{"t":"Office of the US Trade Representative · USMCA","u":"https://ustr.gov/trade-agreements/free-trade-agreements/united-states-mexico-canada-agreement"}],"human":{"dead":400000,"disp":400000,"famine":false,"aid":"n/a","note":"~400k homicides since the 2006 militarization plus 100k+ officially registered disappeared — a casualty scale exceeding most declared wars, classified as criminal violence rather than armed conflict.","est":false,"basis":"Counted — cumulative homicides since the 2006 militarisation of drug enforcement, per INEGI national statistics. Not all are cartel-attributable, and disappearances (over 100,000 registered) are tracked separately and not included here."},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"62 = institutional or electoral instability, a recorded human toll, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"CJNG/Sinaloa split added; contested framing entry on FTO designation"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Cartel fragmentation meets trade rupture","dir":"volatile","phase":"Escalation with an economic shock incoming","up":["USMCA non-renewal converting rules into annual leverage","Sinaloa's internal war fragmenting into more factions","US cartel-designation pressure enabling unilateral action","Fentanyl enforcement militarising the border"],"down":["A successor trade framework restoring predictability","Bilateral security cooperation on precursor chemicals","Cartel fragmentation reducing any single group's reach","Judicial and institutional reform"],"fw":["resource-conflict","deterrence","conflict-curve"]},"spill":[{"n":"United States","dir":"both","w":"The USTR will NOT RENEW USMCA — roughly 80% of Mexican exports move under its terms. Converting a rules-based framework into annually-exercised leverage, while cartel-designation pressure is already live."},{"n":"Colombia","id":"colombia","dir":"in","w":"The cocaine supply chain that terminates at the US border; precursor and trafficking routes."},{"n":"Central America","dir":"both","w":"The migration corridor and the cartel franchise networks running south."}],"inherit":{"name":"Guadalupe Hidalgo","est":"1848","status":"IN FORCE","what":"The 1848 Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo ended the US-Mexican War with Mexico ceding <b>roughly half its territory</b> — present-day California, Nevada, Utah, most of Arizona and New Mexico, and parts of Colorado and Wyoming. The treaty guaranteed property and civil rights to Mexicans who stayed; those guarantees were substantially not honoured. The border it created runs 3,145 kilometres through a region that had been one economy.","now":"<b>The asymmetry that treaty established is the structure of the relationship.</b> Roughly 80% of Mexican exports go north, which is why the USTR's decision not to renew USMCA is the largest single economic variable on this file — converting a rules-based framework into leverage exercised annually. <b>Weapons flow south, drugs and labour flow north, and enforcement pressure runs one direction only.</b> US cartel-designation pressure now raises the question of unilateral action on Mexican soil.","change":"A third USMCA review round concluded in July 2026. <b>The mechanism is annual review rather than termination — a structural downgrade in predictability, not a cliff.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"Small Arms Survey: firearms flows","u":"https://www.smallarmssurvey.org/"},{"t":"INEGI · Mexican national statistics","u":"https://www.inegi.org.mx/"}],"macrona":"NOT YET SOURCED. The load-bearing economic fact is on the card already: roughly 80% of exports go to the US under a framework the USTR will not renew. A full macro block awaits sourced national figures rather than an estimate."},{"id":"cuba","cat":"political","region":"Caribbean","subregion":"Caribbean","name":"Cuba — Post-Intervention Shock + Strangulation","sub":"Island-wide grid COLLAPSE Jul 6 (7th in 18mo) · US seeks Díaz-Canel ouster · \"Cuba is next\"","domain":"Economic Strangulation / Regime Pressure","sev":"medium","status":"escalating","since":"Jan 2026","esc":66,"lat":23.1,"lon":-82.4,"trend":"ss","w14":false,"esc_history":[63,63,63,66,66,66,66,66,66,66],"cas":"32 Cuban soldiers killed in Jan 3 Caracas op; sanctions-linked excess-death risk (Lancet: 564k/yr globally from sanctions)","disp":"Emigration wave amid 15+ hr blackouts, ~100k surgery backlog, food spoilage","flag":"🇨🇺","note":"JUL 2026 — THIRD NATIONAL GRID COLLAPSE OF THE YEAR: Unión Eléctrica de Cuba reported TOTAL GRID DISCONNECTION, the island's third nationwide outage in 2026 · the failure is now periodic rather than exceptional — an infrastructure state that cannot guarantee the most basic public service, which is the material context for both the medical-mission economy and the emigration wave · MAR 2026 — THE MEDICAL-DIPLOMACY REVENUE CRACKS: Jamaica cancelled its Cuban medical-cooperation agreement (Mar 5) under US visa pressure — the overseas medical missions are among Havana's largest remaining hard-currency earners, and Washington is now dismantling them client by client rather than by embargo alone · the leverage read: sanctions failed to end the system; unbundling its revenue streams may do what the embargo could not · NEW THEATER (backfilled) · JUL 6: total national grid collapse — 3rd this year, 7th in 18 months (Nuevitas Unit 6 cascade) · US cut Cuba's oil when it captured Maduro (Jan) + forced acting Venezuela govt to halt shipments; Mexico also halted a shipment under US tariff threat · only ONE oil tanker (Russian Anatoli Kolodkin, Matanzas) docked all year · May: Trump exec order sanctioning Cuba energy/defense/mining/finance sectors · US NOW SEEKS DÍAZ-CANEL OUSTER (NYT/AP) + regime change 'by end of year'; talks ongoing, Cuba released 2,000+ prisoners as goodwill · Trump: 'Cuba is next... I'll have the honor of taking Cuba' · Axios: US intel claims Cuba acquired 300+ Russia/Iran attack drones since 2023, 'discussed plans to attack US' — Cuba calls it fraudulent pretext for military action · Díaz-Canel: 'genocidal energy blockade,' calls for 'war of the entire nation' · UN: ~100,000 pending surgeries backlog, humanitarian 'collapse' risk; blackouts up to 15+ hrs/day · Bejucal SIGINT (China) cross-link retained · UPDATE (Jul 6-10): another full national grid collapse Jul 6 (derelict grid, millions dark) · Cuba's legislature passed 100+ market-friendly reforms (private business + private investment stakes) — major concessions under US pressure; US dismissed as 'superficial smoke signals' + imposed MORE sanctions (military-owned firm + a Castra family member) · Communist Party endorsed Raúl Castro's grandson (Raúl Guillermo Rodríguez Castro) as intermediary for US talks · Colombia's incoming govt to close its Havana embassy · strangulation trajectory holding","leverage":[{"actor":"United States","asset":"Venezuela-oil cutoff + embargo + 'next' threat — strangulation without strikes","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Cuban state","asset":"Security apparatus intact; emigration as pressure valve; martyrdom narrative from the 32","str":"md"},{"actor":"China","asset":"Bejucal SIGINT + economic lifeline potential — hemispheric counter-presence","str":"md"},{"actor":"Russia","asset":"Symbolic solidarity; limited material capacity while consumed by Ukraine","str":"lo"}],"gov":[{"src":"Cuban Government (official)","detail":"Confirmed 32 soldiers killed 'carrying out missions' at Venezuela's request; Díaz-Canel condemned 'state terrorism'"},{"src":"US policy (Brookings/CRS synthesis)","detail":"Oil-to-Cuba interdiction intended to immiserate and spur uprising against the 'Castroite regime'"},{"src":"US Executive Order (May 2026)","detail":"Authorizes sanctioning entities in Cuba's energy, defense, mining, and financial sectors"}],"mil":"No kinetic theater — Cuba's 32 dead in Caracas were the spillover. The pressure is systemic: the Venezuelan oil lifeline is being interdicted deliberately, the island's grid is failing (the user's staged 52D layer independently maps the utility crisis and the short-lived Matanzas fuel relief), and Trump has placed Cuba on the 'could be next' list.","pol":"Díaz-Canel's 'state terrorism' framing and the Raúl Castro tribute converted the 32 dead into regime legitimacy fuel — martyrdom as cohesion. The bet on both sides is endurance: Washington bets immiseration produces uprising; Havana bets six decades of siege-adaptation outlasts one administration. The Bejucal SIGINT relationship with China is the strategic wildcard that could convert strangulation into confrontation.","eco":"Blackouts, fuel shortage, and a tourism collapse compound the oil cutoff. Emigration remains the escape valve that paradoxically stabilizes the regime by exporting discontent.","assess":"Cuba rises to 66, now escalating rather than stalemate. The July 6 island-wide grid collapse — the third total blackout of the year and seventh in 18 months — makes the strangulation strategy's endpoint concrete: with only one oil tanker (Russian) docked all year, the Soviet-era grid is failing outright. Washington has moved from pressure to explicit regime change, openly seeking Díaz-Canel's ouster and, per Trump, 'taking Cuba' next after Venezuela and Iran. The Axios drone-attack allegation — which Cuba calls a fraudulent pretext for military action — echoes the exact information-operation pattern that preceded the Venezuela intervention, making Cuba the clearest candidate for the next Donroe-Doctrine escalation. The same Mohajer/Shahed drone line alleged here runs through the Venezuela wargame layer and the Sahel theaters — one sanctions-bloc weapons ecosystem spanning three continents.","econlev":[{"holder":"United States","channel":"Energy interdiction","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Cut Venezuelan oil lifeline + pressured Mexico to halt shipments; only 1 tanker docked all year; May exec order sanctions energy/mining/finance sectors — grid collapse as coercion"},{"holder":"Russia","channel":"Oil lifeline","exp":"MED","detail":"Anatoli Kolodkin (Matanzas) sole 2026 shipment; symbolic more than sufficient"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"pressure/regime-change","flow":"Oil blockade (cut Venezuela + pressured Mexico to halt shipments); May exec order sanctioning energy/defense/mining/finance; explicit push for Díaz-Canel ouster","tier":"documented","for":"Regime change 'by end of year'; hemispheric Donroe consolidation"},{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"regime lifeline","flow":"Anatoli Kolodkin tanker (Matanzas) — the ONLY oil shipment to dock all year; reaffirmed commitment; alleged attack-drone supplier (Axios)","tier":"documented","for":"Caribbean foothold in US sphere; anti-US solidarity"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"regime","flow":"Bejucal SIGINT station (contested); economic-lifeline potential","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Signals-intelligence reach into US; hemispheric counter-presence"},{"actor":"🇮🇷 Iran","side":"regime","flow":"Alleged co-supplier of the 300+ attack drones (Axios/US intel) — same Mohajer/Shahed line as Venezuela/Sahel","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Sanctions-bloc solidarity; asymmetric reach"}],"ext_flag":true,"tclass":"watch","tier":"ELEVATED","hist_real":true,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk4 (2026)","domain_tag":"Political","contested":{"summary":"Havana's crisis is real; the fight is over whether its cause is the embargo or the system — and what the Russia/Iran hardware means.","claims":[{"claim":"Cuba hosts 300+ Russian/Iranian attack drones and growing SIGINT facilities aimed at the US.","who":"US intelligence (Axios reporting); Havana denies entirely.","evid":"The Bejucal SIGINT complex is documented history; the drone claim rests on US intel leaks not yet independently verified. Cuba's denial is categorical. Logged, not asserted.","status":"ALLEGED-CREDIBLE vs categorical denial"},{"claim":"The collapse (blackouts, exodus) is caused by the US embargo alone.","who":"Havana — the six-decade external-cause frame.","evid":"The embargo demonstrably constrains the economy AND the system's own structural failures (state-sector paralysis, failed monetary reform) are documented by Cuban economists. Single-cause narratives on either side don't survive the data.","status":"CONTESTED — embargo vs system, both load-bearing"}]},"history":{"roots":"Cuba is the revolution that outlived its patron and now outlives its own economy. Castro's 1959 revolution, the missile crisis, and six decades of US embargo made it the hemisphere's defining standoff. The USSR's collapse brought the first starvation-era 'Special Period'; the 2020s brought the second: the tourism collapse, a failed monetary reform, the island's largest protests (2021), a million-person exodus (~10% of the population), and a grid that now fails island-wide. Russia and Iran court the wreckage — fuel, and allegedly drones and SIGINT — returning Cuba to its Cold War function: leverage 90 miles from Florida, priced in desperation.","timeline":[{"date":"1959","e":"Revolution; Castro takes Havana."},{"date":"1962","e":"Missile crisis — the closest nuclear call."},{"date":"1991","e":"Soviet collapse; the first Special Period."},{"date":"2021","e":"Largest protests since the revolution."},{"date":"2021-25","e":"~1M emigrate; grid collapses repeatedly; monetary reform fails."},{"date":"2026","e":"Russian fuel lifeline; alleged drone/SIGINT buildout (US intel; Havana denies)."}],"actors":[{"name":"The regime (Díaz-Canel)","bio":"The post-Castro management — administering scarcity with the same security state, minus the revolutionary legitimacy."},{"name":"Russia / Iran","bio":"The suitors — fuel and hardware for position; the alleged drone/SIGINT axis is the new leverage play."},{"name":"The US","bio":"The embargo's author and the exodus's destination — maximum pressure meets migration blowback."},{"name":"The exodus","bio":"~10% of the population gone in four years — the demographic verdict on the system."}],"bridge":"Ferrer's long history makes the present legible: Cuba and the United States were made in each other's shadow for two centuries, and the current wreckage — exodus, blackouts, Russian fuel, alleged Iranian drones — is the standoff's late style, with Havana again renting its geography to Washington's rivals because geography is the asset the embargo cannot reach. The exodus is the demographic verdict no narrative survives: a tenth of the population chose exit over voice. The platform logs the drone/SIGINT claims at their tier — alleged, denied — because ninety miles' worth of history shows exactly how intelligence claims about Cuba have been both true and manufactured. · AND NOW, 2026: Russian fuel lifeline; alleged drone/SIGINT buildout (US intel; Havana denies).","sources":[{"work":"Cuba: An American History","author":"Ada Ferrer","year":"2021","note":"The Pulitzer-winning entangled history by the Cuban-born scholar — two centuries of mutual making."},{"work":"Cuba: Between Reform and Revolution","author":"Louis A. Pérez Jr.","year":"5th ed. 2015","note":"The standard survey of the republic and revolution."},{"work":"Cuban economists' independent analyses (e.g., Pedro Monreal)","author":"Cuban economic scholarship","year":"2020-26","note":"The internal diagnosis of the monetary-reform failure and state-sector paralysis."}]},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"The revolution outliving its economy, renting geography to Washington's rivals because geography is what the embargo can't reach.","alt":"An embargo-primary reading (Havana's) blames US sanctions alone; a system-primary reading blames the model — the platform weights the latter.","why":"Cuban economists document internal failures the embargo aggravates; the drone/SIGINT claims stay at alleged tier. MOD."},"refs":[{"t":"Reuters · Cuba coverage","u":"https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/"},{"t":"IMF · country data and Article IV reports","u":"https://www.imf.org/en/Countries"},{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"}],"human":{"dead":0,"disp":1000000,"famine":false,"aid":"restricted","note":"~1M departures in four years — roughly a tenth of the population — amid grid collapse and scarcity. Recorded as displacement rather than casualties: the exodus is the measurable human cost of the system's failure."},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"66 = institutional or electoral instability, broadly static over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Provenance, check-mode and escalation-derivation fields added; no substantive content change this cycle."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Infrastructure collapse, third national blackout","dir":"climbing","phase":"Systemic degradation, not armed conflict","up":["Repeated total grid failures becoming permanent","Emigration draining the working-age population","US pressure tightening on the medical-mission economy","No fuel or capital to repair generation"],"down":["Energy investment from non-US partners","Remittance and travel liberalisation","Economic reform expanding the private sector","Regional energy supply arrangements"],"fw":["resource-conflict","conflict-curve"]},"spill":[{"n":"United States","dir":"in","w":"The embargo, the pressure on the medical-mission economy, and the migration policy that shapes the exodus."},{"n":"Venezuela","id":"venezuela","dir":"in","w":"Subsidised oil was the lifeline; the post-Maduro transition puts that arrangement in question just as the grid fails."}],"inherit":{"name":"The Platt Amendment and the Embargo","est":"1901 / 1962","status":"REPEALED / IN FORCE","what":"Cuba won independence from Spain in 1898 with American intervention, and the <b>Platt Amendment of 1901</b> was written into its constitution as a condition of US withdrawal: Washington retained the right to intervene militarily, to veto Cuban treaties and debt, and to a permanent naval base at Guantánamo. Cuba was sovereign on paper and constitutionally subordinate in fact.","now":"<b>Platt was abrogated in 1934; the base lease was not, and the US still holds Guantánamo against Cuban objection.</b> The embargo imposed in 1962 remains the longest-running economic sanctions regime in modern history, and its effects are the material context for everything on this file — including a power grid that has now failed nationally three times in 2026 and an emigration wave draining the working-age population.","change":"Periodic normalisation attempts have been reversed. <b>Venezuela's subsidised oil was the lifeline, and the post-Maduro transition puts that arrangement in question exactly as the grid fails.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"CFR · Cuba backgrounders","u":"https://www.cfr.org/"},{"t":"UN Statistics and country data · Cuba","u":"https://data.un.org/"},{"t":"World Bank · country data and Global Economic Prospects","u":"https://data.worldbank.org/"}],"macrona":"NOT YET SOURCED. Reliable Cuban macro data is genuinely difficult — dual-currency legacy effects and limited independent verification make published figures contested. The material facts here are physical rather than statistical: three total grid failures in 2026 and an emigration wave draining the working-age population."},{"id":"myanmar","cat":"military","region":"Southeast Asia","subregion":"Southeast Asia","tclass":"conflict","name":"Myanmar — Civil War + Rare-Earth Supply War","sub":"KIA controls ~94% of China's heavy-REE feedstock · junta offensives on Kachin mines","domain":"Civil War / Resource Chokehold","sev":"critical","status":"active","since":"2021 coup","esc":88,"lat":25.38,"lon":97.4,"trend":"up","esc_history":[87,88,88,88,88,88,88,88,88,88],"cas":"~3.5M displaced since 2021 coup; ongoing airstrikes (Ponnagyun market strike killed ~20)","disp":"3.5M+ internally displaced","flag":"🇲🇲","note":"JUL 6 2026 — THE BEIJING LIFELINE: Min Aung Hlaing signed memoranda on ALL FOUR of Xi's Global Initiatives — analysts assess Chinese political, financial and military support has become CENTRAL TO THE REGIME'S SURVIVAL; in exchange the junta pledged border security, Belt-and-Road acceleration, and protection for Chinese personnel · ANALYTICAL CONSEQUENCE (against this file's own framing): the 'junta is losing' read must be qualified — it is losing territory while gaining a patron, and Beijing's hedge between Naypyidaw and the ethnic armies now tilts toward the centre to protect its corridors and mineral supply · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · SE Asia) · civil war since 2021 coup now fused with a rare-earth supply war · KIA (Kachin Independence Army) seized the Irrawaddy-basin REE belt in 2024 — controls mines supplying ~94% of China's heavy rare-earth imports (dysprosium/terbium for EVs, wind, missiles, robotics) · junta launched renewed offensives (Chipwi/Pangwa) to retake the mining belts · China plays BOTH sides — backs junta + maintains KIA ties for mineral-flow stability over ideology; closed border to pressure KIA into peace talks, KIA counter-closed crossings, halting exports · India (via Pax Silica) + US now courting KIA to diversify off China · Dec 2025-Jan 2026 junta 'sham elections' returned USDP (339/420 seats); Rakhine/Ponnagyun airstrikes continue","leverage":[{"actor":"KIA / KIO","asset":"Physical control of the Irrawaddy REE belt — ~94% of China's heavy-REE feedstock; can halt exports at will","str":"hi"},{"actor":"China","asset":"Sole processing destination + border/trade leverage; backs both junta and rebels for flow stability","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Myanmar junta (USDP)","asset":"Airpower + foreign backing; retaking mines to restore leverage — but holds shrinking territory","str":"md"},{"actor":"India / US (Pax Silica)","asset":"Alternative buyers courting KIA to break China's monopoly — nascent","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Global Witness / Chinese customs","detail":"Myanmar REE exports to China ~$773M in H1 2023; 300+ mining sites (40% rise since 2021)"},{"src":"KIO rare-earth mining regulation (2025)","detail":"Rebel political wing now taxing + regulating mining as a governance/revenue function"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"China","channel":"REE processing monopoly","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Myanmar supplies ~94% of China's heavy-REE imports; China controls the only processing/magnet chain — dual chokehold"},{"holder":"KIA/KIO","channel":"Resource control","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Physical control of the mines = revenue + political leverage; 2024 export halt proved the chokehold works both ways"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"both (junta + KIA)","flow":"Backs junta politically + maintains KIA ties; border closures to force peace talks; capital + mining equipment","tier":"documented","for":"~94% of its heavy-REE feedstock — flow stability over ideology"},{"actor":"🇮🇳 India","side":"KIA-courting","flow":"Pax Silica member; obtained dysprosium/terbium samples; exploring cross-border road access","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Diversify critical-minerals supply off China"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"either side","flow":"Considering working with either side of the war; Pax Silica framework","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Break China's heavy-REE monopoly"}],"mil":"The civil war and the resource war are now the same war. Since the 2021 coup, ~3.5M have been displaced and ethnic armies govern much of the north — Kachin, Shan, Rakhine, Chin, Karenni. The strategic inflection was the KIA's 2024 seizure of the Irrawaddy-basin rare-earth belt, which handed a rebel army control of the feedstock for ~94% of China's heavy-REE imports. The junta's renewed 2026 offensives on Chipwi and Pangwa are explicitly aimed at retaking those mining belts — military maneuvers sitting atop dysprosium and terbium deposits, not random front movement.","pol":"The Dec 2025-Jan 2026 elections were a junta legitimation exercise (USDP took 339 of 420 seats) that resolved none of the core democracy-deficit or ethnic-autonomy questions; for many ethnic armies, de facto independence is already the lived reality. ASEAN is split — Thailand pushing to soften toward the junta, the Philippines (2026 chair) holding the non-recognition line.","eco":"Rare earths are the economy of the war now. The KIA/KIO taxes and regulates the mines as a governance function; China applies border and trade pressure to keep feedstock flowing; and India and the US are courting the rebels to diversify supply. The environmental cost is severe — acid-leach extraction poisoning the Irrawaddy headwaters and, downstream, Thailand's rivers.","assess":"Myanmar enters at 88 (SEVERE, conflict), trend up — a top-tier theater the platform was previously missing entirely. It is the sharpest live example of the platform's whole thesis: a civil war whose trajectory is set by a resource chokehold, where a rebel army holding ~94% of China's heavy-REE feedstock has more structural leverage than most states. It plugs directly into the critical-minerals system, and the China-plays-both-sides dynamic is the purest 'flow stability over ideology' case on the board. Watch the junta's Kachin offensives and any KIA export halt — either can move global magnet/REE prices.","tier":"CRITICAL","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"China backs the junta and trades with the rebels holding the mines. Flow beats ideology.","claims":[{"claim":"China supports the junta.","who":"China — but the KIA rebels control the mines supplying ~94% of its heavy rare-earth feedstock.","evid":"China maintains ties with BOTH the junta and the KIA. The material interest overrides the political one: what matters is that the dysprosium keeps moving.","status":"DOCUMENTED — the contradiction is the policy"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Heavy Rare Earths","stage":"mine","actor":"Kachin State — Chipwi, Pangwa (KIA-controlled)","detail":"The world's largest source of HEAVY rare earths (dysprosium, terbium) — ion-adsorption clays leached in ~2,700 pits across 370+ sites. USGS logs Myanmar as a major producer (22,000t in 2025) but lists reserves as 'NA': the deposits sit in militia-controlled frontier and officially 'do not exist.' In-situ leaching poisons the water; landslides bury workers.","who":"KIA seized Chipwi/Pangwa (Oct 2024), taxes Chinese operators ~$200M/yr; UWSA runs Shan State sites","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Heavy Rare Earths","stage":"process","actor":"Trucked into Yunnan → Chinese separation plants","detail":"Almost ALL of Myanmar's output crosses into Yunnan — China controls 90%+ of global heavy-REE separation. ~2/3 of China's rare-earth imports by value come from Myanmar. The KIA's control of the mines is the one lever that has brought Beijing to the negotiating table.","who":"China (state-linked entities set up the mines and buy 100% of output)","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Heavy Rare Earths","stage":"mid","actor":"NdFeB permanent magnets (JL Mag, China Southern)","detail":"Separated oxides → sintered into heat-resistant permanent magnets, ~90% of dysprosium/terbium's value. Global Witness: even 'recycled' magnet supply gets 'contaminated' with Myanmar material because sources mix during processing. If you buy a high-grade magnet, some of it likely came from Kachin.","who":"Bosch and Nidec buy magnets from JL Mag; both claim recycled-only sourcing","tier":"alleged-credible"},{"mineral":"Heavy Rare Earths","stage":"end","actor":"EV motors · wind turbines · defense · robotics","detail":"The magnets go into EV traction motors, wind-turbine generators, missiles and robotics — demand for the Nd-Pr + Dy-Tb magnet pairs is projected to grow ~7x to ~$70B by 2040. Western EVs indirectly depend on Kachin ore refined in China. India (IREL) is now testing KIA ore samples as an alternative — the one crack in the monopoly.","who":"The energy transition runs on conflict minerals it cannot officially trace","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["china-refine"],"history":{"roots":"Myanmar's war is the violent unraveling of a 2021 coup. After decades of military dictatorship, a democratic opening (2011-2021) let Aung San Suu Kyi's party win landslides — until the army (Tatmadaw) seized power again in Feb 2021. This time the resistance fought back: a nationwide civil war pitting the junta against ethnic armed organizations (some fighting since independence in 1948) and new pro-democracy militias (PDFs). The junta is losing ground, especially in the resource-rich borderlands where ethnic armies control the jade, rare-earth, and drug economies. The roots: a military that has never accepted civilian rule, and a patchwork of ethnic nations never fully integrated into the Burman-dominated state.","timeline":[{"date":"1948","e":"Independence from Britain; ethnic insurgencies begin almost immediately."},{"date":"1962","e":"Military coup begins decades of junta rule."},{"date":"2011","e":"Gradual democratic opening begins."},{"date":"2015","e":"Aung San Suu Kyi's NLD wins elections."},{"date":"2017","e":"Rohingya genocide drives ~700,000 into Bangladesh."},{"date":"1 Feb 2021","e":"Military coup; mass protests met with lethal force."},{"date":"2021","e":"Armed resistance (PDFs) forms; nationwide civil war."},{"date":"2023-24","e":"'Operation 1027' — ethnic alliances seize vast territory from the junta."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Junta on the defensive; ethnic armies control the rare-earth/jade borderlands."},{"date":"14-15 Mar 2026","e":"MNDAA attacks its own ally the TNLA and seizes Kutkai — the Three Brotherhood Alliance shatters; analysts read it as realignment toward Beijing."},{"date":"Jul 2026","e":"The junta signs memoranda across all four of Xi's Global Initiatives — losing territory and gaining a patron simultaneously."}],"actors":[{"name":"Tatmadaw (junta / SAC)","bio":"The military that has ruled Myanmar for most of its independent history; seized power in 2021 and is now losing the civil war."},{"name":"KIA (Kachin Independence Army)","bio":"One of the oldest ethnic armies; controls heavy-rare-earth mining that feeds China's magnet industry — taxes it heavily."},{"name":"NUG / PDFs","bio":"The parallel civilian government and its allied People's Defense Forces born from the anti-coup resistance."},{"name":"China","bio":"Buys the borderlands' rare earths, hedges between the junta and ethnic armies to protect its mineral supply and BRI corridors."}],"bridge":"Thant Myint-U's warning — that Myanmar's crisis is a collapse of the state idea itself, not a democracy-vs-junta morality play — is the frame the rare-earth economy proves: the borderlands never belonged to the Burman center, and now their militias tax the minerals of the global energy transition while the junta loses the interior. The KIA's Kachin mines financing both resistance and Chinese magnets is the platform's purest case of conflict and commerce fused; the India-IREL probe is the first crack in the monopoly and a Watchlist off-ramp. Whatever state emerges will be negotiated between armed nations, not restored from Naypyidaw. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: the junta is losing territory while signing memoranda across all four of Xi's Global Initiatives, and the MNDAA turned on its own ally under Chinese pressure. The British-drawn frontier states are still negotiating their terms of entry into a Burmese state — now with Beijing as the arbiter London once was.","sources":[{"work":"The Hidden History of Burma: Race, Capitalism, and the Crisis of Democracy","author":"Thant Myint-U","year":"2019","note":"The Burmese historian-statesman's structural account — the state idea's collapse beneath the transition story."},{"work":"UN Independent Fact-Finding Mission on Myanmar report","author":"UN HRC","year":"2018","note":"The primary record of the Rohingya genocide."},{"work":"Global Witness heavy-rare-earth investigations","author":"Global Witness","year":"2022-26","note":"Primary supply-chain tracing: Kachin pits to Chinese magnet-makers."}]},"human":{"dead":75000,"disp":3500000,"famine":false,"aid":"junta-obstructed","note":"Post-coup conflict deaths ~75k+ (ACLED-basis); 3.5M displaced; Rohingya (~1M in Bangladesh) counted under Bangladesh hosting.","est":false,"basis":"AAPP and ACLED conflict-death monitoring"},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"The state idea's collapse; borderland nations taxing the transition's minerals.","alt":"This is a federal self-determination war seven decades older than the rare-earth trade — minerals explain the KIA's budget, not its existence. And the Jul-2026 Beijing lifeline cuts against a pure resource read in the other direction too: China is now underwriting the losing centre for corridor security, not simply buying from the winning periphery.","why":"The platform agrees and says so: the history leads with 1948, the pipeline with the economy. HIGH in the layered reading."},"refs":[{"t":"The Irrawaddy via Asia Daily: MAH signs Xi's Global Initiatives","u":"https://www.theasiacable.com/p/asia-daily-july-7-2026"},{"t":"Crisis Group: Myanmar","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/asia/south-east-asia/myanmar"},{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"},{"t":"CFR · Global Conflict Tracker","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker"},{"t":"UN OHCHR · human rights reporting","u":"https://www.ohchr.org/en/countries"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"88 = civil war across most of the country with the junta losing territory while gaining a patron; scored down slightly from 2024 peak because the resistance is now fragmenting against itself.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"MNDAA-TNLA split (Mar 2026) added; junta-Beijing memoranda recorded"}],"spill":[{"n":"China","dir":"in","w":"Pressured the MNDAA to return Lashio, brokered its turn on the TNLA, and signed the junta across all four Global Initiatives. Beijing is the decisive external hand — the junta loses ground and gains a patron at once.","id":"china"},{"n":"Thailand","dir":"both","w":"The refugee flow, the scam-compound economy on the border, and the trade corridors; Bangkok manages the overflow of a war it cannot influence.","id":"thailand-cambodia"},{"n":"Bangladesh","dir":"both","w":"The Rohingya displacement from Rakhine — the Arakan Army now holds the state the refugees fled, and ARSA/RSO recruit in the Cox's Bazar camps.","id":"bangladesh"},{"n":"India","dir":"out","w":"The Kachin rare-earth belt feeds Chinese processing; instability on the northeastern frontier touches Indian border states.","id":"india"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Junta losing ground, Chinese arbitration","dir":"volatile","phase":"Escalation with external brake","up":["The junta's territorial collapse accelerates","Brotherhood Alliance fully fractures (MNDAA turned on TNLA)","Scam-economy and refugee flows destabilise borders","Rohingya return becomes impossible under Arakan Army rule"],"down":["Chinese pressure freezing front lines (it forced Lashio's return)","A federal settlement with the ethnic armed organisations","The NUG-EAO coalition holding rather than splintering","Border-state stabilisation via Thailand/China"],"fw":["conflict-curve","proxy","ethnic-security","resource-conflict"]},"macro":{"growth":"Suppressed; parallel war economy","infl":"Elevated","src":"Compiled reporting, 2026","note":"Formal GDP understates the actual flows: jade, rare earths from the Kachin belt feeding Chinese processing, scam-compound revenue on the Thai border, and cross-border trade taxed by whichever armed group holds the checkpoint. The junta signs Chinese Global Initiative memoranda while losing territory — and where it has regained ground, Chinese leverage rather than Burmese arms explains it."},"inherit":{"name":"The Frontier Areas Exclusion","est":"1886-1947","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Britain governed Burma as two systems: <b>Ministerial Burma</b>, the Bamar-majority lowlands run directly, and the <b>Frontier Areas</b> — Shan, Kachin, Chin, Karenni — left under their own chiefs with separate administration and no shared political institutions. Christian missionaries worked the hills, and the colonial army recruited heavily from minorities to police the majority. The 1947 Panglong Agreement promised the frontier peoples autonomy and, for some, the right to secede after ten years.","now":"<b>Panglong was never honoured, and every Burmese conflict since is a version of that unpaid promise.</b> The Arakan Army, the KIA, the TNLA, the MNDAA and the UWSA are frontier-state administrations with their own courts, taxation and armies — the Wa run a currency and a border regime. <b>The junta is losing territory to peoples Britain never integrated</b>, while the KIA taxes the jade and rare-earth extraction feeding Chinese processing.","change":"Beijing is now the arbiter London once was — pressuring the MNDAA to return Lashio, brokering its turn on the TNLA. <b>The NUG maintains a parallel government in exile; Myanmar does not have a state and an insurgency, it has competing administrations.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"ACLED Myanmar conflict data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}],"ctrl":{"gov":35,"contested":25,"other":40,"est":true,"src":"Composite of open-source control mapping, 2026","note":"The junta holds the central lowlands and major cities. Ethnic armed organisations administer most of the frontier states — the Arakan Army holds most of Rakhine, the UWSA runs Wa as a de facto state."},"control":{"gov":40,"contested":25,"other":35,"holders":"The junta holds the central lowlands and major cities. Ethnic armed organisations administer most of the frontier states — the Arakan Army holds nearly all of Rakhine, the UWSA runs Wa as a de facto state, and the KIA, TNLA and MNDAA hold territory in Kachin and Shan.","note":"Where the junta has regained ground, Chinese leverage rather than Burmese arms explains it — Beijing pressured the MNDAA to return Lashio.","est":true}},{"id":"south-china-sea","cat":"strategic","region":"Southeast Asia","subregion":"Southeast Asia","tclass":"conflict","name":"South China Sea — Philippines-China Gray Zone","sub":"Scarborough/Second Thomas Shoal clashes · $3.4T trade · MDT tripwire","domain":"Maritime Gray-Zone Confrontation","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"2023 (acute)","esc":71,"lat":15.2,"lon":117.8,"trend":"up","esc_history":[66,67,67,68,68,68,68,68,71,71],"cas":"No deaths yet; ramming, water cannon, military-grade lasers, injuries in resupply clashes","disp":"Fishing communities displaced from traditional grounds","flag":"🌊","note":"JUL 2026 — THE COST IS SHOWING IN PUBLIC OPINION: Asia Society polling finds Southeast Asian sentiment toward China still net positive but DECLINING, with SOUTH CHINA SEA CONFRONTATIONS driving the downturns specifically in the PHILIPPINES and VIETNAM, while Thailand and Singapore hold steady on trade and infrastructure · INDONESIA and MALAYSIA contained their own maritime disputes through diplomacy and quiet enforcement rather than confrontation — two different strategies for the same problem, with measurably different domestic results · public views show LOWER polarisation than elite assessments · TRIPWIRE UPDATE — THE SCARBOROUGH PLATFORM WAS REMOVED: the Philippine SCS task force confirmed (Jun 17) that the floating 'structure' detected on Scarborough Shoal was gone within a week of appearing, after Manila protested (Jun 9) that it could be the first step toward an island base · this VALIDATES the platform's probe-and-pause read (see Watchlist: 'Scarborough platform becomes a permanent structure' — ARMED, not tripped): Beijing tests, measures the reaction, withdraws, and returns — the Mischief Reef playbook run in slow motion rather than a single seizure · JUN-JUL ESCALATION SEQUENCE: China SANCTIONED Philippine Defense Sec. Gilberto Teodoro (Jun 11) — sanctioning a sitting allied defense minister for 'egregious conduct'; AFP leadership rallied behind him · RARE NAVAL STANDOFF (Jun 21-23): PH frigate BRP Diego Silang vs FOUR PLA warships near Scarborough, radio challenges exchanged, helicopter launched — timed to the close of Salaknib 2026 (7,000+ troops: PH/US/JP/NZ/AUS) · China's Sept-2025 'nature reserve' + May floating platform in Scarborough lagoon read as creeping-occupation pretext ('Mischief Reef playbook') · JUL 12: 10th anniversary of the PCA ruling — US, UK, Japan + ~12 states jointly demanded China comply; Beijing: the award is 'a poisoned tree' · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · SE Asia) · China's nine-dash line overlaps EEZs of Philippines, Vietnam, Malaysia, Brunei, Indonesia · flashpoints: Second Thomas Shoal (BRP Sierra Madre resupply), Scarborough Shoal (China deploying floating 'structures'), Xianbin/Sabina Reef · escalating ramming, water cannon, military-grade lasers, flares fired at PH patrol aircraft · Apr 2026: PH lab-confirmed CYANIDE in bottles alleged used by Chinese vessels (environmental warfare claim) · ~$3.4T annual trade transits; est. 11B barrels oil + 190T cu ft gas · PH 2026 ASEAN chair pushing Code of Conduct (10th anniv of 2016 arbitral ruling China rejects) · US Mutual Defense Treaty (Art. IV) = tripwire; Balikatan war games w/ Japan as full participant; Japan supplying used warships to PH","leverage":[{"actor":"China","asset":"Coast guard + maritime militia mass; island bases; nine-dash-line claim enforced by presence","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Philippines","asset":"2016 arbitral ruling (legal high ground) + US MDT tripwire + ASEAN 2026 chair + new Japan/US basing","str":"md"},{"actor":"United States","asset":"MDT Art. IV commitment; first-island-chain doctrine; Balikatan; the deterrent that caps escalation","str":"hi"},{"actor":"ASEAN claimants (Vietnam/Malaysia)","asset":"Overlapping claims + Vietnam's own Spratly buildup — dilutes a unified front","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"PACC 2016 Arbitral Tribunal","detail":"Ruled China's nine-dash-line claim has no legal basis; China rejects as 'illegal and invalid'"},{"src":"PH National Security Council (Apr 2026)","detail":"Lab-confirmed cyanide in bottles alleged deployed by Chinese vessels — environmental-warfare allegation"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"China","channel":"Sea-lane + resource control","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Controls presence over $3.4T/yr trade route + 11B bbl oil / 190T cu ft gas estimated reserves via nine-dash line"},{"holder":"Global trade","channel":"Chokepoint dependence","exp":"HIGH","detail":"~1/3 of global shipping transits; disruption ripples worldwide — the leverage cuts both ways"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"Philippines","flow":"Mutual Defense Treaty (Art. IV tripwire); Balikatan war games; EDCA base access; surveillance","tier":"documented","for":"First-island-chain containment of China"},{"actor":"🇯🇵 Japan","side":"Philippines","flow":"Supplying used warships; joined Balikatan as full participant; 'normative power' aid","tier":"documented","for":"Rule-of-law-at-sea; counter-China presence"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"claimant","flow":"Coast guard + maritime militia; island-building; water cannon/laser gray-zone coercion","tier":"documented","for":"Nine-dash-line sovereignty; sea-lane + hydrocarbon control"}],"mil":"A gray-zone confrontation engineered to stay just below the threshold of war: China uses coast guard and maritime-militia mass — ramming, water cannon, military-grade lasers, and flares fired at Philippine aircraft — rather than naval gunfire, precisely to avoid triggering the US Mutual Defense Treaty. The recurring flashpoints are the Second Thomas Shoal (where the Philippines resupplies the grounded BRP Sierra Madre) and Scarborough Shoal (where China has deployed floating 'structures' analysts fear presage an island base). The danger is miscalculation: a single death could invoke MDT Article IV.","pol":"The Philippines holds the legal high ground (the 2016 arbitral ruling China rejects) and the 2026 ASEAN chair, and is pushing to conclude a Code of Conduct on the ruling's 10th anniversary — while deepening US and Japanese security ties. ASEAN unity is the weak point: Cambodia has historically blocked consensus on China's behalf, and claimant states' overlapping claims dilute a common front.","eco":"The stakes are a ~$3.4T annual trade artery and an estimated 11 billion barrels of oil and 190 trillion cubic feet of gas. The April 2026 cyanide allegation adds an environmental-warfare dimension to the resource contest.","assess":"South China Sea enters at 68 (ELEVATED, conflict-class for the active gray-zone clashes), trend up. It belongs in the platform as the maritime counterpart to Taiwan — the same US-China first-island-chain contest, fought by coast guards instead of navies. Its defining feature is calibrated ambiguity: every actor manages escalation to stay below the MDT tripwire, which makes accidental escalation the primary risk. Watch Scarborough Shoal construction and any loss of life.","tier":"SEVERE","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Strategic","contested":{"summary":"A decade after the PCA ruling, the contest is whether law or presence defines the sea.","claims":[{"claim":"China has 'indisputable historic sovereignty' over the nine-dash line; the 2016 PCA award is 'a poisoned tree... a cynical parody of justice' (Xinhua).","who":"Beijing — rejecting the ruling wholesale while building the physical facts (bases, reserves, platforms) that make it moot.","evid":"The PCA ruled unanimously in 2016 that the nine-dash claim has no legal basis under UNCLOS — a treaty China signed. Scarborough, Second Thomas and Mischief Reef sit inside the Philippine EEZ. China refused to participate and ignores the award; ten years on (Jul 12 2026) the US, UK, Japan + ~12 states jointly demanded compliance.","status":"DOCUMENTED ruling vs DOCUMENTED defiance — law and power on parallel tracks"},{"claim":"China's Scarborough structures are 'temporary scientific research' and a 'national nature reserve.'","who":"Beijing — the environmental/scientific cover story for creeping occupation.","evid":"The 'nature reserve' (Sept 2025) and floating platforms (May 2026) follow the exact Mischief Reef playbook: huts→structures→airstrip. Manila calls it 'a clear pretext for occupation.' China removed one platform under scrutiny — the probe-and-pause pattern.","status":"CONTESTED — science framing vs occupation precedent"}]},"history":{"roots":"The South China Sea dispute is the collision between a rules-based map and a power-based one. China's nine-dash line — inherited from a 1947 Nationalist map — claims most of a sea through which a third of global shipping passes, overlapping five neighbors' EEZs. From 2013 Beijing built the facts: dredged reefs into fortress islands (Mischief, Subi, Fiery Cross), militarized them despite pledges, and deployed a coast-guard-and-militia swarm to enforce the claim daily. The 2016 PCA ruling demolished the line's legal basis; Beijing called it 'a poisoned tree' and kept building. The Philippines under Marcos chose confrontation-by-transparency — filming every ramming — and the US alliance commitment is the tripwire the whole region watches.","timeline":[{"date":"1947","e":"The nine-dash line drawn on a Nationalist Chinese map."},{"date":"1995/2012","e":"Mischief Reef, then Scarborough Shoal, taken by increments."},{"date":"2013-16","e":"Island-building: reefs become military bases."},{"date":"Jul 2016","e":"PCA rules the line has no legal basis; Beijing ignores it."},{"date":"2023-25","e":"Second Thomas Shoal water-cannon sieges; Philippine transparency campaign."},{"date":"2026","e":"China sanctions the Philippine defense secretary; 4-warship standoff at Scarborough; 'nature reserve' pretext; the PCA's 10th anniversary demands."}],"actors":[{"name":"China (CCG + militia)","bio":"The claimant enforcing by presence — gray-zone coercion calibrated below the threshold that triggers the US treaty."},{"name":"Philippines (Marcos)","bio":"The frontline state that chose exposure over acquiescence — every ramming filmed, every incursion publicized."},{"name":"United States","bio":"The mutual-defense treaty ally — the ambiguity of when Article IV triggers is the deterrence question."},{"name":"Vietnam/Malaysia/others","bio":"The quieter claimants — building their own islands while Manila absorbs the pressure."}],"bridge":"Ten years after the PCA award, the sea is the world's clearest test of whether adjudicated law constrains accumulated presence — and the running answer is Carpio's: law wins arguments, presence wins reefs, and only documented exposure narrows the gap. The Philippines' transparency campaign is jurisprudence by camera; China's 'nature reserve' at Scarborough is occupation by taxonomy. The July 2026 sequence (a defense minister sanctioned, a four-warship standoff, a fifteen-state compliance demand) shows both tracks accelerating at once — which is what makes the permanent-structure tripwire on this platform's Watchlist the region's live fuse.","sources":[{"work":"The South China Sea Dispute: Philippine Sovereign Rights and Jurisdiction in the West Philippine Sea","author":"Antonio T. Carpio","year":"2017","note":"The Philippine Supreme Court justice's case — the archipelagic view, freely published."},{"work":"PCA Award, The South China Sea Arbitration (Philippines v. China)","author":"Permanent Court of Arbitration","year":"12 Jul 2016","note":"The primary ruling — the nine-dash line's legal demolition."},{"work":"The South China Sea: The Struggle for Power in Asia","author":"Bill Hayton","year":"2014","note":"The standard narrative history of the claims' construction."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"Adjudicated law versus accumulated presence — China building the facts that make the ruling moot.","alt":"A freedom-of-navigation-overstated reading holds commercial shipping largely unimpeded, making the dispute more about sovereignty symbolism than actual trade chokehold.","why":"The militarization is documented and the EEZ incursions real; the trade-disruption claim is the weaker part, logged as such. HIGH on the law-vs-presence frame."},"refs":[{"t":"CFR Global Conflict Tracker: structure removed","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/territorial-disputes-south-china-sea"},{"t":"PCA Award (Philippines v. China, 2016)","u":"https://pca-cpa.org/en/cases/7/"},{"t":"IMF · country data and Article IV reports","u":"https://www.imf.org/en/Countries"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"71 = strategic competition, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Provenance, check-mode and escalation-derivation fields added; no substantive content change this cycle."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Grey-zone confrontation, declining regional sentiment","dir":"stalemate","phase":"Managed standoff under structural stress","up":["A collision at Second Thomas Shoal or Scarborough escalating","The Thucydides dynamic making any clash a hierarchy test","Joint PRC-Russia operations normalising a two-power presence","A US-Philippines treaty trigger being tested"],"down":["ASEAN diplomacy and the Code of Conduct negotiations","The economic interdependence that raises the cost of war for all","Philippines-Vietnam containing incidents bilaterally","Deterrence credibility holding without a shooting incident"],"fw":["thucydides","deterrence","security-dilemma","ladder"]},"spill":[{"n":"Taiwan","id":"taiwan","dir":"both","w":"Shared grey-zone toolkit and shared structural stress; the two theatres are read together by every party."},{"n":"ASEAN states","dir":"both","w":"Asia Society polling finds regional sentiment toward China declining specifically in the PHILIPPINES and VIETNAM on the back of maritime confrontations — while Indonesia and Malaysia contained their disputes quietly, with measurably different domestic results."},{"n":"Chokepoints","id":"chokepoints","dir":"out","w":"The Malacca approach and the trade volume that transits — the economic stake that makes escalation costly for everyone including Beijing."},{"n":"China","id":"china","dir":"in","w":"The claimant whose maritime confrontations are measurably eroding its own standing in the Philippines and Vietnam — a cost Beijing is currently accepting."}],"macrona":"DELIBERATELY EMPTY. This is a maritime dispute among many claimant economies, not a national economy — a single growth figure would describe none of them. The relevant economics sit on the China, Taiwan and chokepoints files, and on the Asia Society finding that confrontations are eroding Beijing's standing in the Philippines and Vietnam specifically.","inherit":{"name":"The Eleven-Dash Map","est":"1947","status":"IN FORCE","what":"The Republic of China's Ministry of the Interior published a map in 1947 marking an <b>eleven-dash line</b> enclosing most of the South China Sea, drawn largely from earlier private cartography and asserted as the war ended and Japanese occupation collapsed. The PRC inherited it and removed two dashes in the Gulf of Tonkin in 1953. <b>Both Beijing and Taipei claim the same waters on the authority of the same pre-communist map</b>, which is why the dispute cannot be reduced to Chinese communist expansionism.","now":"<b>A 2016 arbitral tribunal under UNCLOS found the line had no basis in international law; Beijing rejected the ruling and the ruling has no enforcement mechanism.</b> The claim is now pressed through grey-zone means — coast guard, militia and construction — rather than adjudication. Asia Society polling finds regional sentiment toward China declining specifically in the <b>Philippines and Vietnam</b>, while Indonesia and Malaysia contained their disputes through quiet diplomacy and enforcement.","change":"ASEAN Code of Conduct negotiations have run for over two decades without a binding instrument. <b>Two claimant strategies — confrontation and quiet containment — have produced measurably different domestic results, which is the finding this board holds.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"Asia Society · Global Public Opinion on China (Jul 2026)","u":"https://asiasociety.org/"},{"t":"AMTI (CSIS) maritime tracking","u":"https://amti.csis.org/"},{"t":"World Bank · country data and Global Economic Prospects","u":"https://data.worldbank.org/"}]},{"id":"indonesia","cat":"economic","region":"Southeast Asia","subregion":"Southeast Asia","tclass":"watch","name":"Indonesia — Nickel Superpower + Resource Nationalism","sub":"~50% of global nickel · Prabowo statism · China JV dominance","domain":"Critical-Minerals Leverage / Governance","sev":"medium","status":"stalemate","since":"2020 export ban","esc":52,"lat":-2.5,"lon":118,"trend":"ss","esc_history":[52,52,52,52,52,52,52,52,52,52],"cas":"Nov 2025 floods (China-backed hydro breaches) killed 1,000+; mining-linked environmental deaths","disp":"Mining-displaced communities (Sulawesi, Halmahera)","flag":"🇮🇩","note":"JUL 2026 — (checked) the World Bank reclassified the Philippines to upper-middle-income this month, a regional benchmark; for Indonesia itself no discrete new Papua development surfaced this cycle. The TPNPB-OPM insurgency and the Grasberg extraction context remain as logged · JUL 16 2026 — BRIN restarted the TRIGA 2000 research reactor in Bandung, strengthening radioisotope and nuclear research capacity — a modest but notable step for a state that has repeatedly deferred civil nuclear power while pursuing full-spectrum resource sovereignty · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · SE Asia) · world's #1 nickel producer (~50% of global supply) — the battery-metal chokehold · 2020 ore-export ban forced downstream processing onshore, drawing massive Chinese JV investment (Tsingshan et al.) · resource-nationalism model: capture the value chain, not just dig ore · revoked permit of China-backed Batangtoru/Sumatra hydro after Nov 2025 floods killed 1,000+ (28 firms penalized) · Prabowo govt statist tilt (Danantara sovereign fund; strategic-sector control) · Natuna EEZ friction w/ China's nine-dash incursions · stock market tumbled early 2026 on investor-confidence wobbles","leverage":[{"actor":"Indonesia","asset":"~50% of global nickel + export-ban leverage forcing onshore processing — the battery-metal spigot","str":"hi"},{"actor":"China","asset":"Dominant nickel-JV investor + processing tech (Tsingshan); controls much of the built downstream capacity","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Prabowo govt","asset":"Statist control via Danantara sovereign fund + strategic-sector nationalism","str":"md"},{"actor":"US/EV West","asset":"IRA sourcing rules push to de-risk from Chinese-controlled Indonesian nickel — buyer leverage","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Indonesia Min. of Energy/Investment","detail":"2020 nickel-ore export ban; 2026 revoked Batangtoru hydro permit + penalized 28 firms for environmental breaches after fatal floods"},{"src":"Danantara sovereign wealth fund","detail":"Prabowo-era vehicle consolidating state control over strategic sectors"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Indonesia","channel":"Nickel supply chokehold","exp":"HIGH","detail":"~50% of global nickel; export ban forces value-chain onshoring — the single biggest EV-battery-metal lever"},{"holder":"China","channel":"Processing/JV control","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Chinese firms (Tsingshan) built + control much of Indonesia's downstream nickel capacity — leverage inside the leverage"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"investment-dominant","flow":"Dominant nickel-JV capital + HPAL processing tech; hydropower (Batangtoru); Belt-and-Road infrastructure","tier":"documented","for":"Control of ~50% of global nickel / EV-battery supply chain"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US / EU","side":"de-risking buyer","flow":"IRA/CRMA sourcing rules pressuring supply chains to reduce Chinese-controlled Indonesian nickel content","tier":"documented","for":"Battery-supply-chain diversification"}],"mil":"Not a kinetic theater — the friction is economic and maritime. The only armed dimension is periodic naval friction in the Natuna EEZ, where China's nine-dash-line incursions overlap Indonesian waters and Jakarta has deployed frigates and F-16s to assert control.","pol":"Prabowo's government has tilted toward statism — the Danantara sovereign wealth fund consolidating control over strategic sectors, and a willingness to penalize even Chinese-backed investors (the Batangtoru hydro permit revocation after the November 2025 floods that killed over 1,000). The balancing act: harness Chinese capital and processing tech for the nickel build-out while resisting full dependence.","eco":"Indonesia is the battery-metal superpower — roughly half of global nickel supply, weaponized through the 2020 export ban that forced onshore processing and drew tens of billions in Chinese joint-venture investment. The strategic contradiction: the resource-nationalism model succeeded at capturing the value chain, but handed much of the downstream capacity to Chinese firms, so the West's de-risking push now targets Indonesian nickel precisely because it's Chinese-controlled.","assess":"Indonesia enters at 52 (GUARDED, strategic-watch) — the model case for why the watch class matters. No war, but it sits on the single most important EV-battery-metal chokehold on earth and is the arena where resource nationalism, Chinese supply-chain control, and Western de-risking collide. It plugs directly into the critical-minerals system alongside the DRC (cobalt) and Myanmar (rare earths). Watch the nickel price, any export-policy shift, and the Natuna friction.","tier":"GUARDED","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Economic","contested":{"summary":"Papua is the site of a decades-old dispute over both the legitimacy of Indonesian sovereignty and the scale of the violence sustaining it — conducted largely without independent observers.","claims":[{"claim":"The 1969 'Act of Free Choice' expressed Papuan self-determination.","who":"Indonesia, and the UN which accepted the result.","evid":"1,025 electors hand-picked by the Indonesian military voted unanimously to join Indonesia, under supervision, on behalf of ~800,000 people. Declassified diplomatic cables show contemporaneous Western officials privately described the process as a formality. Freeport's mining contract was signed in 1967 — TWO YEARS BEFORE the vote.","status":"DISPUTED-IN-THE-RECORD"},{"claim":"Casualty and displacement figures in Papua are exaggerated.","who":"Indonesian authorities.","evid":"Independent journalists, UN special rapporteurs and international monitors are heavily restricted from the Papuan provinces, so no verifiable count exists. Estimates of deaths since the 1960s range across an order of magnitude. Displacement from operations in Nduga, Intan Jaya and Puncak is documented by churches and local NGOs but not independently audited.","status":"UNVERIFIABLE-BY-DESIGN"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Nickel (laterite)","stage":"mine","actor":"Sulawesi & Halmahera — Morowali, Weda Bay","detail":"Indonesia produces ~60% of world nickel. The 2020 raw-ore EXPORT BAN ('hilirisasi') forced processing onshore — Prabowo calls downstreaming 'the path to national revival.' But the 2026 quota cuts bit hard: Tsingshan's Weda Bay quota collapsed from 42Mt (2025) to 12Mt (2026), -70%. Mining in Raja Ampat was halted after a biodiversity backlash revoked 4 of 5 licenses.","who":"Jakarta uses the ban + quotas to capture value; state miners ANTAM largely confined to supplying ore","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Nickel","stage":"process","actor":"Chinese-built HPAL + smelters (75% Chinese-controlled)","detail":"Chinese firms control ~75% of Indonesia's refining; Tsingshan + Jiangsu Delong alone >70%, ~$14-15B invested and 'embedded, cannot be relocated.' Tsingshan's HPAL (with GEM/CATL/Huayou) converts low-grade laterite → MHP for batteries. Powered by captive COAL — 4-5x the CO2 of Canadian nickel, the ESG stain. The May 2026 CCCI letter to Prabowo protesting taxes/quotas is the leverage fight in the open.","who":"China (Tsingshan, Huayou, CATL/Lygend) — the processing chokehold Jakarta built and now negotiates against","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Nickel","stage":"mid","actor":"MHP → nickel sulphate → NCM battery cathodes","detail":"MHP refines to nickel sulphate (>2x the value of ferronickel) then into NCM cathodes. Indonesia began exporting sulphate in 2023 — the move up the value chain. But a HORMUZ cross-link: HPAL needs sulfur, most shipped from the Middle East through the strait — the Iran war pushed smelting costs up ~$300/t and paused half of Huayou's Huafei output (cross-link: chokepoints theater).","who":"CATL + LG (Korea) build cathode/battery plants; the ecosystem Jakarta wants onshore","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Nickel","stage":"end","actor":"EV batteries · stainless steel — but IRA-excluded","detail":"~75% of nickel still goes to stainless steel; the rest to EV batteries. THE CATCH: the US Inflation Reduction Act EXCLUDES Indonesian nickel from EV tax credits precisely because of the Chinese ownership — so the world's biggest nickel producer is locked out of the biggest EV-subsidy market. And Tesla's shift toward LFP (nickel-free) batteries threatens the demand thesis.","who":"China's EV industry is the anchor buyer; the West's IRA wall keeps Indonesian nickel Chinese-bound","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["china-refine","resource-nationalism"],"history":{"roots":"Indonesia is the giant that chose leverage over alignment. The world's fourth-most-populous state and largest Muslim-majority democracy, it emerged from Suharto's three-decade dictatorship (1998) into a raucous decentralized democracy — and under Jokowi and now Prabowo, into a resource-nationalist power. The 2020 nickel export ban ('hilirisasi') forced the world's EV supply chain onshore, built a Chinese-financed refining empire, and made Indonesia the test case of whether a producer can climb the value chain by decree. Non-aligned by doctrine since Bandung (1955), it joins BRICS while buying US planes — the swing state of the Indo-Pacific, selling access to all sides.","timeline":[{"date":"1945-49","e":"Independence war from the Dutch."},{"date":"1965-66","e":"Anti-communist massacres (~500k+); Suharto's New Order begins."},{"date":"1998","e":"Suharto falls; democratization."},{"date":"2020","e":"Nickel raw-ore export ban — hilirisasi begins."},{"date":"2024","e":"Prabowo elected — the general returns as democrat."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"BRICS membership; quota cuts hit Chinese smelters; IRA exclusion bites; Raja Ampat backlash."}],"actors":[{"name":"Prabowo Subianto","bio":"The Suharto-era general turned president — 'downstreaming is national revival'; non-alignment as leverage-maximization."},{"name":"China (Tsingshan et al.)","bio":"Built ~75% of the refining Indonesia's ban created — the partner-hostage relationship both sides now renegotiate."},{"name":"The US / IRA wall","bio":"The market that excludes Chinese-controlled Indonesian nickel — the wall Jakarta wants tunneled."},{"name":"ANTAM / the state","bio":"The domestic champion largely confined to supplying ore — the gap between sovereignty rhetoric and ownership reality."}],"bridge":"Hilirisasi is Bandung's economics arriving seventy years late: the non-aligned founding promise — sovereignty meaning more than a flag — attempted through export bans and smelter decrees. The catch the platform tracks is Roosa's shadow: the 1965-66 massacres that built the New Order also built the political economy (military-business fusion, foreign-capital bargain) that Chinese refining capital now occupies; Jakarta captured the midstream's location without its ownership. The IRA wall and the quota fight are the same negotiation from both ends — whether the world's nickel superpower can convert geology into leverage that survives its partners' veto. · AND NOW, 2025-26: BRICS membership; quota cuts hit Chinese smelters; IRA exclusion bites; Raja Ampat backlash.","sources":[{"work":"A History of Modern Indonesia since c.1200","author":"M.C. Ricklefs","year":"4th ed. 2008","note":"The standard survey — from the sultanates through Reformasi."},{"work":"Pretext for Mass Murder: The September 30th Movement and Suharto's Coup d'État","author":"John Roosa","year":"2006","note":"The 1965 foundation of the New Order's political economy."},{"work":"The Killing Season: A History of the Indonesian Massacres","author":"Geoffrey Robinson","year":"2018","note":"The fullest account of 1965-66 — the suppressed founding violence."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"Leverage over alignment — hilirisasi as Bandung's economics arriving seventy years late.","alt":"A dependency reading holds that Chinese capital ownership means Indonesia captured the midstream's location without its control — sovereignty rhetoric over foreign-owned substance.","why":"The platform states both: the ban worked (location captured), the ownership didn't follow (75% Chinese). HIGH on the tension."},"refs":[{"t":"BERNAMA/ANTARA: TRIGA 2000 restart","u":"https://asean.bernama.com/news.php?id=2581466"},{"t":"Human Rights Watch · Indonesia/Papua","u":"https://www.hrw.org/asia/indonesia"},{"t":"IMF · country data and Article IV reports","u":"https://www.imf.org/en/Countries"},{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"52 = economic and financial stress, broadly static over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"TPNPB-OPM added to Groups; contested claims filled — previously a blind spot"}],"spill":[{"n":"Papua / Grasberg","dir":"in","w":"The TPNPB-OPM insurgency sits on the world's largest gold mine — the extraction thesis this platform tracks, in a theatre it flagged as its own blind spot."},{"n":"South China Sea","id":"south-china-sea","dir":"both","w":"Jakarta contained its Natuna maritime dispute through quiet diplomacy and enforcement rather than confrontation — a different strategy from Manila's, with measurably different domestic results."},{"n":"Australia / Pacific","dir":"both","w":"The maritime frontier and the West Papua question that strains the relationship."}],"inherit":{"name":"The Act of Free Choice","est":"1962-1969","status":"IN FORCE","what":"When the Netherlands withdrew from West New Guinea, the 1962 New York Agreement transferred it to Indonesia pending an act of self-determination. In 1969 Indonesia conducted the <b>Act of Free Choice</b> by selecting <b>1,025 hand-picked electors</b> from a population of roughly 800,000 and having them vote unanimously by consensus, under military supervision. <b>The UN noted the result without endorsing the method.</b>","now":"<b>The TPNPB-OPM insurgency has run continuously since, and the extraction is the reason it is not allowed to end.</b> Grasberg, the world's largest gold mine and one of its largest copper mines, sits in Papua. The province supplies a share of Indonesian export revenue wildly disproportionate to what it receives, and access for journalists and monitors is restricted. <b>This platform flagged West Papua as its own blind spot before it built the file</b> — the extraction thesis applied everywhere except the place with the largest goldmine on earth.","change":"Special autonomy laws have been passed and renewed; the province has been subdivided into additional units, which Papuan organisations read as fragmentation rather than devolution. <b>The 1969 process has never been revisited.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"IPAC · Institute for Policy Analysis of Conflict","u":"http://www.understandingconflict.org/"},{"t":"World Bank · country data and Global Economic Prospects","u":"https://data.worldbank.org/"}],"macrona":"NOT YET SOURCED at theater level. The relevant economics are provincial: Grasberg's output against Papua's share of what it generates. National Indonesian figures would obscure rather than explain that."},{"id":"thailand-cambodia","cat":"military","region":"Southeast Asia","subregion":"Southeast Asia","tclass":"watch","name":"Thailand-Cambodia — Border War Aftermath","sub":"Dec 2025 border war → fragile ceasefire · Trump-brokered · energy-deal collapse","domain":"Interstate Border Conflict","sev":"medium","status":"stalemate","since":"2025","esc":48,"lat":14.4,"lon":104,"trend":"dn","esc_history":[49,48,48,48,48,48,48,48,48,48],"cas":"Dec 2025 border war: dozens of civilian + combatant deaths across multiple provinces","disp":"Widespread civilian displacement along border provinces","flag":"🇹🇭","note":"JUL 2026 — BEIJING STEPS IN AS MEDIATOR: Xi Jinping has publicly urged Thailand and Cambodia to settle their long-running border dispute · the mediation itself is the development — China positioning as arbiter between two ASEAN members is the role the US and ASEAN mechanisms have historically claimed, and it follows the same pattern as Beijing's brokering between the MNDAA and the Myanmar junta · JUL 6 2026 — CEASEFIRE FRAYING: four Cambodian soldiers wounded by a blast during a patrol in Oddar Meanchey near the Thai border (Phnom Penh's Defence Ministry; cause under investigation, incident occurred as Cambodian troops passed a Thai patrol across a barbed-wire fence) · Cambodia has been briefing foreign envoys on a Thai border build-up — the US tariff-brokered ceasefire holds in name while both sides posture · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · SE Asia) · worst Thai-Cambodian border clashes in years: 5-day war Jul 2025 → 3-week war Dec 7-27 2025 (heavy weapons, multiple provinces, dozens dead) · 2nd ceasefire late Dec (Malaysia + US mediation; Trump pushed truce, Cambodia welcomed it) · Thailand exited 25-yr joint offshore energy-exploration deal w/ Cambodia (overlapping maritime claims now unresolved) · Cambodia rebuilding US ties (USS Cincinnati at Ream Naval Base Jan 2026; joining Trump's Board of Peace; resuming US joint exercises) despite US scam-center crackdown · China views the conflict as a threat to its Maritime Silk Road + Malacca-hedge strategy","leverage":[{"actor":"Thailand","asset":"Larger military + economy; exited the joint energy deal to pressure Cambodia; US 'key partner' designation","str":"md"},{"actor":"Cambodia","asset":"US-rapprochement (Ream base access) hedging its China dependence; Trump Board of Peace seat","str":"md"},{"actor":"United States","asset":"Ceasefire broker + courting both (Ream visit, exercises) to pull Cambodia off China","str":"md"},{"actor":"China","asset":"Belt-and-Road stakes in both; wants stability for its Malacca-hedge corridor — mediating pressure","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Malaysia/US ceasefire mediation","detail":"Brokered 28 Jul 2025 truce + late-Dec 2025 second ceasefire; 18 Cambodian soldiers returned by Thailand Dec 31"},{"src":"Thai govt","detail":"Exited 25-year joint offshore energy-exploration agreement with Cambodia post-conflict"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Thailand/Cambodia (disputed)","channel":"Offshore energy","exp":"MED","detail":"Overlapping maritime claims + collapsed 25-yr joint-exploration deal leave Gulf of Thailand gas reserves locked and contested"},{"holder":"China","channel":"BRI infrastructure","exp":"MED","detail":"Belt-and-Road stakes in both states; conflict threatens the Maritime Silk Road + Malacca-hedge corridor"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"broker / Cambodia-courting","flow":"Ceasefire mediation; USS Cincinnati at Ream Naval Base; resuming joint exercises; Board of Peace","tier":"documented","for":"Pull Cambodia off China; Southeast Asia influence vs Beijing"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"both (BRI)","flow":"Belt-and-Road investment in both; mediation pressure for stability","tier":"documented","for":"Protect Maritime Silk Road + Malacca-Dilemma hedge"},{"actor":"🇲🇾 Malaysia","side":"mediator","flow":"Co-brokered both ceasefires as ASEAN convener","tier":"documented","for":"Regional stability; ASEAN centrality"}],"mil":"Two interstate wars in one year between ASEAN neighbors: a five-day clash in July 2025 and a far larger three-week war from December 7-27, 2025, involving heavy weapons across multiple border provinces and reaching into Siem Reap, with dozens of civilian and combatant deaths and widespread displacement. A second ceasefire (Malaysia and US mediation) held into 2026, but low-intensity landmine incidents and mutual accusations keep it fragile.","pol":"The war accelerated a strategic realignment: Cambodia, long seen as China's closest ASEAN partner, is rebuilding US ties — hosting the USS Cincinnati at Ream Naval Base, joining Trump's Board of Peace, and resuming US military exercises — even through the US crackdown on Cambodian scam centers. Thailand, designated a US 'key partner,' exited its 25-year joint offshore energy deal with Cambodia, locking up contested Gulf of Thailand gas.","eco":"The economic casualty is the collapsed joint offshore energy-exploration agreement — overlapping maritime claims now leave Gulf of Thailand gas reserves contested and undeveloped. China's Belt-and-Road stakes in both states give Beijing a stability interest, since the conflict threatens its Maritime Silk Road and its Malacca-Dilemma hedging strategy.","assess":"Thailand-Cambodia enters at 48 (GUARDED, strategic-watch), trend down as the ceasefire holds. It earns a place less for the (now-paused) fighting than for what it reveals: a US-China influence contest playing out through a border war, where Washington is using the crisis to pull Cambodia off Beijing, and the collapsed energy deal is a resource dimension to watch. A ceasefire breakdown or a move on the contested Gulf gas would re-escalate it.","tier":"GUARDED","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"No significant narrative-war dimension identified in this theater at present. The main claims are broadly consistent with the evidence, or the disputes are policy disagreements rather than contested facts. This is stated affirmatively rather than left blank — the absence of an information operation is itself a finding.","claims":[]},"history":{"roots":"The Thai-Cambodian border war of 2025-26 is a century-old map dispute weaponized by two domestic crises. The border was drawn by French colonial cartographers (1907); the Preah Vihear temple ruling (ICJ 1962) never settled the surrounding land. The 2025 escalation — landmines, artillery, F-16 strikes, the worst fighting in decades — served both capitals: Cambodia's Hun Sen dynasty rallying nationalism, Thailand's fractured politics (a leaked Hun Sen-Paetongtarn call collapsed her government) needing an external enemy. A US-brokered ceasefire (Trump's tariff leverage) holds unevenly. Beneath it: Cambodia's scam-compound economy, Thai casino politics, and two militaries that profit from the frontier they fight over.","timeline":[{"date":"1907","e":"French-Siamese treaty draws the disputed border."},{"date":"1962","e":"ICJ awards Preah Vihear temple to Cambodia — the land around it stays contested."},{"date":"2008-11","e":"Temple-area clashes kill dozens."},{"date":"Jul 2025","e":"Full escalation: mines, artillery, F-16s; ~40+ dead, 300k displaced."},{"date":"Aug 2025","e":"US-brokered ceasefire (tariff leverage); Paetongtarn's government falls to the leaked call."},{"date":"2026","e":"Ceasefire holds unevenly; monitors deployed; the scam-economy dimension surfaces."}],"actors":[{"name":"Hun Sen / Hun Manet","bio":"Cambodia's dynasty — the leaked call was vintage Hun Sen statecraft; nationalism as regime maintenance."},{"name":"Thailand's military + politics","bio":"The army fights, the coalition churns — the border crisis toppled a Shinawatra government via a phone call."},{"name":"United States (Trump)","bio":"The mediator by tariff — trade leverage as ceasefire enforcement, a new model of intervention."},{"name":"The scam-compound economy","bio":"The borderland's real industry — cyber-fraud cities whose protection rackets entangle both militaries."}],"bridge":"Thongchai's 'geo-body' thesis is this war's decoder: the border being shelled is not ancient — it is the 1907 cartographic line by which Siam learned to be a modern nation, and both states' nationalisms are built on maps whose margins were never agreed. That is why a leaked phone call could topple a Thai government and why Hun Sen could convert temple stones into regime fuel. The US tariff-brokered ceasefire adds the newest layer: trade access as the hemisphere-style enforcement tool arriving in Southeast Asia, holding the line the ICJ's 1962 judgment never fully drew. · AND NOW, 2026: Ceasefire holds unevenly; monitors deployed; the scam-economy dimension surfaces.","sources":[{"work":"Siam Mapped: A History of the Geo-Body of a Nation","author":"Thongchai Winichakul","year":"1994","note":"The Thai scholar's classic — how the map made the nation, and what its edges cost."},{"work":"ICJ Judgment, Temple of Preah Vihear (Cambodia v. Thailand)","author":"International Court of Justice","year":"1962 (interpretation 2013)","note":"The primary ruling and its contested surroundings."},{"work":"A History of Cambodia","author":"David Chandler","year":"4th ed. 2007","note":"The standard Cambodian history — the dynasty politics beneath the border theater."}]},"human":{"dead":60,"disp":300000,"famine":false,"aid":"functioning","note":"The 2025 border war: ~40-60 dead, 300k displaced at peak — small against this board, enormous against the border's history.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"A colonial map dispute instrumentalized by two domestic crises — local-politics-primary, borders as pretext.","alt":"A genuine territorial-nationalist reading would take the temple claims at face value; the evidence (the leaked call's effect, timing against the Thai coalition's collapse) points otherwise.","why":"This theater is the platform's own proof it does not force extraction onto everything. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"Asia Cable · Asia Daily, Jul 2026","u":"https://www.theasiacable.com/p/asia-daily-july-20-2026"},{"t":"Asia Daily: Oddar Meanchey blast, border build-up briefings","u":"https://www.theasiacable.com/p/asia-daily-july-7-2026"},{"t":"ICG · South East Asia","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/asia/south-east-asia"},{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"48 = active armed conflict, a recorded human toll, trending DOWN over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Provenance, check-mode and escalation-derivation fields added; no substantive content change this cycle."}],"spill":[{"n":"Myanmar","id":"myanmar","dir":"in","w":"The refugee flow and the scam-compound economy on the Thai border — the overflow of a war Bangkok cannot influence."},{"n":"China","dir":"in","w":"XI JINPING has publicly urged Thailand and Cambodia to settle the border dispute — Beijing positioning as arbiter between two ASEAN members, the same brokerage role it plays with the MNDAA and the Myanmar junta.","id":"china"},{"n":"ASEAN","dir":"both","w":"The regional mechanism whose mediation China is now supplementing."}],"inherit":{"name":"The 1907 French Map","est":"1904-1907","status":"IN FORCE","what":"France, governing Cambodia as a protectorate, produced boundary maps under a 1904 treaty with Siam. <b>The map covering the Dangrek range placed the Preah Vihear temple on the Cambodian side, contrary to the watershed line the treaty itself specified.</b> Siam did not object at the time, and the ICJ ruled in 1962 that this acquiescence bound Thailand. A colonial cartographer's departure from an agreed principle became a legal border.","now":"<b>The map is still the dispute.</b> Periodic exchanges of fire around Preah Vihear and adjacent areas continue, and the ICJ returned to the question in 2013. <b>What is new is the arbiter: Xi Jinping has publicly urged both states to settle</b> — China positioning between two ASEAN members, the role Western powers and ASEAN mechanisms historically claimed.","change":"ASEAN mechanisms remain nominally seized of it. <b>Beijing's brokerage here follows the same pattern as its pressure on the MNDAA in Myanmar and its failed mediation on the Pakistan-Afghanistan border.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"ASEAN · official statements","u":"https://asean.org/"},{"t":"ACLED · conflict event data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}],"macrona":"DELIBERATELY EMPTY. This is a border dispute between two economies, not a national economic story. The relevant figures sit on each state's own trade position and on Myanmar's scam-compound economy next door."},{"id":"korea","cat":"military","region":"East Asia","subregion":"East Asia","tclass":"conflict","name":"Korean Peninsula — Nuclear Standoff + Russia Axis","sub":"NK nuclear expansion · Russia-DPRK war alliance · US-ROK intel friction","domain":"Nuclear Deterrence / Alliance Realignment","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"Armistice 1953","esc":70,"lat":38.3,"lon":127.5,"trend":"up","esc_history":[68,69,70,70,70,70,70,70,70,70],"cas":"No active combat; DPRK troops fighting/dying in Russia's Ukraine war","disp":"N/A","flag":"🇰🇷","note":"CORRECTION — THE 30,000 FIGURE NEEDS CONTEXT (31 JUL 2026): this board recorded Zelensky's report of another 30,000 DPRK troops as converting Pyongyang 'from a supplier into a scaled expeditionary participant'. ISW's assessment qualifies that materially: an estimated 15,000 already sent have performed REAR AREA FUNCTIONS, ISW has NOT observed reports of North Korean forces FIGHTING in Ukraine, and both governments appear hesitant to deploy them into occupied territory for fear of a Western response · the relationship is real and scaling, but it is a labour transfer freeing Russian personnel rather than a combat deployment — and at Russian casualty rates it buys about a month · 27 JUL 2026 — 30,000 MORE TROOPS TO RUSSIA: Zelensky says NORTH KOREA IS PREPARING TO SEND ANOTHER 30,000 TROOPS to support Russia's war — MORE THAN TWICE the number believed to be serving alongside Russian forces now · this converts the DPRK from a supplier into a scaled expeditionary participant in a European war, and it is the clearest evidence yet that the Pyongyang-Moscow relationship is a manpower alliance rather than an arms transaction · JUL 2026 — PYONGYANG ON RIMPAC: North Korea has denounced the Rim of the Pacific exercise in Hawaii, warning of 'proportional countermeasures'; KCNA accused the US and its allies of turning the Pacific into 'a battlefield permeated with the smell of gunpowder' · read with the PRC-Russia joint aerial patrol that entered South Korea's ADIZ this month, three parties are now signalling on the same theatre in the same weeks · JUL 2026 — THE ADIZ INCURSION WAS JOINT: a PRC-RUSSIAN joint strategic aerial patrol entered SOUTH KOREA'S air defence identification zone ahead of the Joint Sea-2026 naval exercise off Qingdao · the significance is the pairing, not the incursion — Seoul now faces a coordinated two-power probe rather than separate Chinese and Russian activity, which complicates every assumption about who it is deterring and with whose help · JUL 2026 — THE TWO-PATRON SIGNAL: a PRC-Russia JOINT STRATEGIC AERIAL PATROL entered South Korea's air defense identification zone, alongside the Joint Sea-2026 naval drills — Pyongyang's two great-power backers now exercise together against the alliance that contains it, converting the North's bilateral Russia alignment into a trilateral security problem for Seoul and Tokyo · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · East Asia) · NK nuclear modernization: could reach 290 warheads by 2035 (ROK est.); 'serious increase' in enrichment capacity (IAEA); Kusong enrichment site exposure sparked US-ROK intel-sharing cut · Kim's dual doctrine: 'war deterrence' (minimum 2nd-strike) + 'war strategy' (tactical-nuke threat to cap a N-S conventional war) · RUSSIA-DPRK AXIS: NK troops + arms to Russia's Ukraine war in exchange for tech/food/energy; Xi-Kim summit; PRC+Russia jointly oppose sanctions · NK tungsten exports to China up 13-fold (2026) · ROK Pres. Lee Jae Myung 'peaceful coexistence' policy vs hardening rhetoric; proposed renaming NK 'Joseon' (implicit two-state recognition) · US reduced-nuclear-umbrella concerns amid alliance transition","leverage":[{"actor":"North Korea","asset":"Nuclear arsenal (~50→290 warheads) + tactical-nuke escalation threat; troops/arms Russia needs; enrichment leverage","str":"hi"},{"actor":"United States","asset":"Extended deterrence (nuclear umbrella) + 28,500 troops + intel-sharing control over ROK","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Russia","asset":"Now DPRK's war patron — tech/energy/food for troops+shells; shields NK at UN","str":"md"},{"actor":"China","asset":"Largest NK trading partner; treats NK arsenal as geopolitical asset vs US; tungsten/mineral buyer","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"IAEA","detail":"Warned of 'serious increase' in North Korean uranium-enrichment capacity"},{"src":"ROK Defense Intelligence / Yonhap","detail":"US reduced NK-nuclear-site intel sharing with Seoul after a lawmaker exposed the Kusong enrichment site"},{"src":"US-ROK-Japan +7 (May 30 2026)","detail":"Joint statement to act against vessels in DPRK sanctions evasion (coal/iron-ore exports)"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"China + Russia","channel":"Sanctions lifeline","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Both oppose DPRK sanctions; China's NK-tungsten imports up 13-fold (2026); Russia trades tech/energy for troops+shells — the arsenal's economic oxygen"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"North Korea","flow":"War alliance: receives DPRK troops + artillery/missiles for Ukraine; supplies tech, energy, food, sanctions cover","tier":"documented","for":"Manpower + shells for the Ukraine war"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"North Korea","flow":"Largest trading partner; tungsten/mineral purchases; UN sanctions opposition; Xi-Kim summit","tier":"documented","for":"NK as a strategic buffer + geopolitical lever vs US in the Indo-Pacific"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"South Korea","flow":"Extended nuclear deterrence; 28,500 troops; trilateral (US-ROK-Japan) coordination — but cut some intel sharing","tier":"documented","for":"First-island-chain containment; alliance credibility"}],"mil":"The peninsula is escalating structurally without active combat. North Korea is expanding its nuclear arsenal — South Korean estimates project up to 290 warheads by 2035, and the IAEA warns of a serious jump in enrichment — while codifying a two-tier doctrine: 'minimum deterrence' second-strike against the US, plus a 'war strategy' that threatens tactical nuclear use to stop a North-South conventional war from escalating. The most consequential shift is external: North Korean troops are now fighting and dying in Russia's war in Ukraine.","pol":"The Russia-DPRK war alliance is the defining 2026 development — Pyongyang trades troops and artillery for Russian technology, energy, and food, while Moscow and Beijing jointly shield it from sanctions at the UN. South Korea's President Lee Jae Myung pursues 'peaceful coexistence' (even proposing to rename the North 'Joseon,' an implicit two-state recognition), but faced a hardening moment after a lawmaker's exposure of the Kusong enrichment site led Washington to curtail intelligence sharing with Seoul — a rare alliance friction.","eco":"Sanctions are the battleground: the US, ROK, Japan and seven partners target DPRK sanctions-evasion shipping, while China and Russia openly oppose the regime and keep it afloat — Chinese tungsten imports from North Korea jumped more than 13-fold in early 2026. The arsenal runs on this Sino-Russian economic oxygen.","assess":"Korea enters at 70 (ELEVATED, conflict-class), trend up. It belongs in the platform not for active fighting but as a nuclear flashpoint being reshaped by the Ukraine war — the Russia-DPRK alliance has converted a frozen standoff into a live node of the global sanctions-evasion and great-power-competition systems. The US-ROK intel-sharing friction is a warning sign about alliance cohesion. Watch DPRK warhead/enrichment milestones, any tactical-nuke test, and the depth of Russian tech transfer in return for troops.","tier":"SEVERE","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"The contest is over whether North Korea's arsenal is a bargaining chip or a permanent fact — and who must adjust