failed nuclear bargain."},{"date":"2006","e":"First nuclear test."},{"date":"2011","e":"Kim Jong Un inherits — purges, acceleration."},{"date":"2018-19","e":"Trump-Kim summits collapse at Hanoi."},{"date":"2023-","e":"Russia alliance: shells and troops for tech; enrichment 'serious increase' (IAEA)."},{"date":"2026","e":"290-warhead trajectory; constitutional nuclear status; the acceptance fight."}],"actors":[{"name":"Kim Jong Un","bio":"Third-generation ruler — the arsenal is the dynasty's insurance and the Russia alliance its first great-power leverage since the USSR."},{"name":"South Korea","bio":"The economic superpower neighbor debating its own nuclear option as US extended deterrence wobbles."},{"name":"Russia","bio":"The new patron — arms-for-tech trade that functionally endorses the arsenal and breaks the sanctions regime."},{"name":"United States / China","bio":"The deterrence anchor and the reluctant enabler — both preferring a frozen problem to any available solution."}],"bridge":"The Russia alliance is the peninsula's biggest structural change since 1953: Cumings' long thesis — that the Korean War never ended, only paused — now has Pyongyang fighting in a European war and being paid in the technology every prior diplomatic framework tried to deny it. The 290-warhead trajectory converts the old denuclearization question into an acceptance question, and Seoul's own nuclear debate is the alliance system pricing American reliability in real time. The armistice's 73rd year finds all its assumptions inverted except one: nobody can afford the war's resumption. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: a PRC-Russia ADIZ patrol and no functioning communication channel. The 1945 line drawn by two American colonels in thirty minutes is still the border, still armistice, still not peace.","sources":[{"work":"Korea's Place in the Sun: A Modern History","author":"Bruce Cumings","year":"1997 (rev. 2005)","note":"The revisionist standard — the war's origins in division, not invasion alone."},{"work":"Brothers at War: The Unending Conflict in Korea","author":"Sheila Miyoshi Jager","year":"2013","note":"The war as a 70-year continuing contest, not an event."},{"work":"IAEA DPRK safeguards reports","author":"IAEA","year":"2023-26","note":"Primary enrichment findings ('serious increase') behind this theater's assessments."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"The Cold War's last front, the arsenal as dynasty insurance, now paid in Russian technology.","alt":"A rational-deterrence reading holds Pyongyang as a stable status-quo power whose arsenal is purely defensive — not the destabilizer the escalation framing implies.","why":"The capability trajectory (290 warheads) and the Russia arms trade are documented facts; whether they're offensive or insurance is the genuine open question. HIGH on facts, both on intent."},"refs":[{"t":"ISW / Critical Threats · Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment (25 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/russian-offensive-campaign-assessment-july-25-2026"},{"t":"American Legion · Five Things to Know (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.legion.org/information-center/news/security/2026/july/five-things-to-know-july-27-2026"},{"t":"KCNA via American Legion · Five Things (Jul 20 2026)","u":"https://www.legion.org/information-center/news/security/2026/july/five-things-to-know-july-20-2026"},{"t":"AEI/ISW: Joint Sea-2026, PRC-Russia ADIZ patrol","u":"https://www.aei.org/articles/china-taiwan-update-july-10-2026/"},{"t":"IAEA DPRK reports","u":"https://www.iaea.org/newscenter/focus/dprk"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"70 = same logic as Taiwan: an armistice, not a peace, with a nuclear-armed party and no functioning communication channel.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Provenance, check-mode and escalation-derivation fields added; no substantive content change this cycle."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Armistice stand-off, joint adversary patrols","dir":"stalemate","phase":"Frozen deterrence","up":["A PRC-Russia-DPRK alignment hardening","No functioning communication channel during a crisis","RIMPAC-type exercises drawing DPRK countermeasures","The ADIZ patrols normalising"],"down":["Restored communication channels","The armistice holding as it has since 1953","Deterrence credibility maintained","Denuclearisation diplomacy (long dormant)"],"fw":["deterrence","ladder","security-dilemma","thucydides"]},"spill":[{"n":"Russia / China","id":"russia-domestic","dir":"in","w":"A PRC-Russia joint strategic aerial patrol entered South Korea's ADIZ ahead of Joint Sea-2026. The pairing is the story — Seoul now faces a coordinated two-power probe, not separate activity."},{"n":"Taiwan","id":"taiwan","dir":"both","w":"The same joint-patrol campaign and the same rising-versus-ruling-power structure; escalation in one theatre is read as intent in the other."},{"n":"United States / Pacific","dir":"both","w":"Pyongyang denounced RIMPAC, warning of 'proportional countermeasures' and calling the Pacific a battlefield permeated with the smell of gunpowder."},{"n":"China","id":"china","dir":"in","w":"Half of the joint PRC-Russia aerial patrol that entered the ADIZ, and the economic relationship that constrains how far Seoul can align against Beijing."}],"macro":{"growth":"Chip exports +151% in Q1 2026","infl":"Contained","src":"South Korean trade data via comparative analysis, 2026","note":"THE CONTRAST WITH TAIWAN IS THE ANALYSIS. South Korea had the same semiconductor surge — chip exports up 151% in Q1 — but on a diversified base: automotive, shipbuilding, petrochemicals and entertainment all globally competitive, with SAMSUNG AT ROUGHLY 20-25% OF THE KOSPI AGAINST TSMC'S 45% OF THE TAIEX. Seoul therefore carries far less single-point exposure than Taipei to either a chip-cycle reversal or a blockade. Read against the joint PRC-Russia ADIZ patrol and the DPRK troop transfers to Russia, South Korea is the more resilient economy facing the more conventional threat, and Taiwan the reverse."},"inherit":{"name":"The 38th Parallel","est":"1945","status":"IN FORCE","what":"On the night of 10 August 1945, two American colonels — Dean Rusk and Charles Bonesteel — were given <b>thirty minutes</b> to propose a line dividing Korea for the purpose of accepting Japanese surrender. Using a National Geographic map, they chose the 38th parallel because it left Seoul on the American side. <b>No Korean was consulted.</b> The Soviets accepted it. What was meant as a temporary administrative convenience became two states, a war that killed millions, and an armistice that has never become a peace treaty.","now":"<b>Eighty-one years on, the line drawn in half an hour is still the border, still an armistice, and still not peace.</b> There is no functioning communication channel during a crisis. A joint PRC-Russia aerial patrol entered South Korea's ADIZ in July 2026, and North Korea is reported to be preparing a further 30,000 troops for Russia — though ISW assesses those serve rear-area functions and buy Moscow under a month at current casualty rates.","change":"<b>Nothing structural since 1953.</b> Denuclearisation diplomacy has been dormant for years; Pyongyang denounced RIMPAC in July, warning of 'proportional countermeasures'."},"monitors":[{"t":"AEI · Korean Peninsula Update, Jul 2026","u":"https://www.aei.org/"},{"t":"38 North analysis","u":"https://www.38north.org/"}]},{"id":"kazakhstan","cat":"economic","region":"Central Asia","subregion":"Central Asia","tclass":"watch","name":"Kazakhstan — Uranium Pivot + Multi-Vector Squeeze","sub":"40% of global uranium · Russia+China reactor lock-in · 10% output cut","domain":"Critical-Minerals Leverage / Great-Power Hedging","sev":"medium","status":"stalemate","since":"2022 (post-Ukraine)","esc":46,"lat":48,"lon":66.9,"trend":"up","esc_history":[45,45,46,46,46,46,46,46,46,46],"cas":"N/A","disp":"N/A — Central Asian migrant mistreatment in Russia a friction point","flag":"🇰🇿","note":"JUL 2026 — THE CORRIDOR DIPLOMACY DEEPENS: Uzbekistan's relationship with Kazakhstan has advanced beyond strategic partnership to the level of ALLIANCE, and Tashkent signed strategic partnerships with GEORGIA and then BELARUS (8-9 Jul) · the Kazakhstan-Georgia-Belarus sequence is Central Asia building a diversified network of transport and industrial partnerships that secures ACCESS TO WESTERN ROUTES — the Middle Corridor logic, pursued state by state rather than as a bloc · Kazakhstan is described as indispensable to Uzbek transit, which makes Astana the hinge of the whole arrangement · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · Central Asia) · world's #1 uranium producer — ~40% of global supply (Kazatomprom) · 2026: cutting output ~10% (32,777→~29,697 tU), tightening a ~5% slice of global supply → upward price pressure · reactor politics = leverage map: 1st NPP to Russia's Rosatom, 2nd+3rd to China's CNNC — US 'wasn't even in the room' (no bid) · Rosatom/Uranium One divested some mine stakes under sanctions pressure; France's Orano (KATCO JV) + Macron pushing EU export boost · 'multi-vector' Tokayev doctrine: bridge between poles, reduce Russia dependence, but China now largest economic player · yuan bonds, Belt-and-Road middle corridor · sulfuric-acid supply = production bottleneck","leverage":[{"actor":"Kazakhstan (Kazatomprom)","asset":"~40% of global uranium; output-cut lever tightens world supply; multi-vector hedging","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Russia","asset":"Rosatom builds 1st NPP + owns ~26% of uranium deposits + processing/transit control + EAEU pull","str":"hi"},{"actor":"China","asset":"CNNC builds 2nd+3rd NPPs; largest economic player; BRI middle-corridor + yuan financing","str":"hi"},{"actor":"West (France/US/Canada)","asset":"Orano KATCO JV + Cameco Inkai stake; ~19% of exports — but forfeited reactor bids","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Kazatomprom","detail":"World's largest uranium producer (~40% global); announced ~10% 2026 output cut, tightening ~5% of global supply"},{"src":"Kazakhstan govt (2024 referendum)","detail":"Public voted for nuclear power; awarded 1st NPP to Rosatom, 2nd+3rd to China's CNNC; US submitted no bid"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Kazakhstan","channel":"Uranium supply","exp":"HIGH","detail":"~40% of global uranium; the output-cut decision moves world prices — the single largest nuclear-fuel lever on earth"},{"holder":"Russia + China","channel":"Reactor + processing lock-in","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Rosatom (1st NPP, ~26% of deposits) + CNNC (2nd+3rd NPPs) capture Kazakhstan's downstream nuclear future; West forfeited"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"incumbent patron","flow":"Rosatom builds 1st NPP ($14.4B, 2 VVER-1200); owns ~26% uranium deposits + Kazatomprom stake; processing/transit control; EAEU","tier":"documented","for":"Retain nuclear-sector dominance + regional leverage despite sanctions erosion"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"rising patron","flow":"CNNC to build 2nd+3rd NPPs; largest economic player; BRI middle corridor; yuan bonds/loans","tier":"documented","for":"Upstream critical-minerals control + de-dollarized financial integration"},{"actor":"🇫🇷 France / 🇺🇸 US","side":"de-risking challenger","flow":"Orano KATCO JV; Macron push for EU export boost; Wabtec locomotive deal — but no reactor bid","tier":"documented","for":"Uranium supply diversification off Russia"}],"mil":"Not a kinetic theater — Kazakhstan is a strategic-watch node defined by resource leverage and great-power hedging. The nearest security dimensions are indirect: the mistreatment of Central Asian migrant workers in Russia as a friction point, and the ever-present 'shadow of Russian leverage' as an EAEU member bordering both Russia and China.","pol":"President Tokayev's 'multi-vector' doctrine — positioning Kazakhstan as a 'middle power' bridging conflicting geopolitical poles — is being tested by infrastructure lock-in. The reactor awards are the tell: Russia's Rosatom won the first nuclear plant and China's CNNC the second and third, while the US 'wasn't even in the room' with no bid submitted. Astana has made subtle moves to reduce Russian ownership (Uranium One divested some mine stakes under sanctions pressure), but China has become the largest single economic player.","eco":"Kazakhstan is the uranium superpower — roughly 40% of global supply through Kazatomprom — and in 2026 it wielded that position by cutting output ~10%, tightening about 5% of world supply and pressuring prices upward. The strategic contest is over who controls the downstream: Russia and China locked in the reactor builds and much of the processing and financing (yuan bonds, Belt-and-Road corridor), while France's Orano and Canada's Cameco hold minority mine stakes and the West pushes to de-risk.","assess":"Kazakhstan enters at 46 (GUARDED, strategic-watch), trend up — the missing uranium node in the critical-minerals system. It's the nuclear-fuel counterpart to the DRC's cobalt, Niger's uranium, Myanmar's rare earths, and Indonesia's nickel: a stable state whose ~40% grip on global uranium gives it real leverage, now being locked into the Russian and Chinese nuclear orbit while the West forfeits the reactor contracts that determine long-term alignment. Watch Kazatomprom's output decisions (they move global prices) and any Western re-entry into the fuel cycle.","tier":"GUARDED","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Economic","contested":{"summary":"A 'multi-vector' state whose vectors were decided in the reactor bids.","claims":[{"claim":"Kazakhstan pursues a balanced 'multi-vector' foreign policy.","who":"Tokayev — hedging is more sellable domestically than alignment.","evid":"Rosatom won the first nuclear plant; China's CNNC won the second and third. The US submitted NO BID — 'wasn't even in the room.' Infrastructure lock-in outlives rhetoric.","status":"CONTESTED — the hedge is already tipping"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Uranium","stage":"mine","actor":"Kazatomprom ISR mines — Budenovskoye, Inkai","detail":"The world's #1 uranium producer — ~40%+ of global supply, 25,839 tU in 2025, targeting 27,500-29,000t in 2026. Extracted by low-cost in-situ recovery. 2025 Subsoil Code amendments force Kazatomprom's stake to ≥75% in new contracts — resource nationalism, uranium edition. Of 13 projects, 10 are JVs (Cameco, Orano, CNNC, Rosatom).","who":"Kazatomprom (state) controls all mining; but Budenovskoye's 2024-26 output is 100% committed to RUSSIA","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Uranium","stage":"process","actor":"Conversion + the enrichment chokehold","detail":"THE BOTTLENECK: Kazakhstan mines the ore but cannot enrich it. It bought conversion tech from Cameco (2020) but holds only a 10% stake in Russia's Angarsk enrichment center — access, not control. Sanctions on Russia make selling to the West hard, so Kazatomprom sits between a Russian enrichment dependency and Western buyers it struggles to reach.","who":"Russia (Rosatom) provides the enrichment Kazakhstan lacks — the dependency that defines the relationship","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Uranium","stage":"mid","actor":"The reactor contest — Rosatom vs CNNC","detail":"Whoever builds the reactors locks in decades of fuel dependency. After a 2024 referendum (71% yes), Kazakhstan gave the FIRST plant (Balkash/Ulken, 2 VVER-1200s, ~$14B) to Russia's ROSATOM — and the 2nd AND 3rd to China's CNNC, which underbid at ~$5.47B. This is the live Russia-vs-China contest for Central Asia's nuclear future, playing out on one country's grid.","who":"Russia (incumbent, Soviet-standard, finance) vs China (cheaper, 67% of Kazakh uranium already goes to China)","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Uranium","stage":"end","actor":"Reactor fuel — China, Russia, EU, Japan","detail":"~67% of China's uranium imports come from Kazakhstan; France leaned on it harder after losing Niger's ~20% share to the junta. Kazatomprom signed 2025 supply deals with Swiss Axpo, Czech ČEZ and Japan's Kansai — the multi-vector hedge. The end-buyer competition IS the geopolitics: everyone needs the fuel, and Astana sells to all sides.","who":"The multi-vector strategy — sell to China, Russia, EU and Japan at once, depend fully on none","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["china-refine","resource-nationalism"],"history":{"roots":"Kazakhstan is the multi-vector state par excellence — a resource giant (world's #1 uranium, major oil) wedged between Russia and China, selling to everyone and belonging to no one. Independent since 1991 under Nazarbayev's three-decade personalist rule, it weathered his managed succession to Tokayev, then the January 2022 unrest that Tokayev survived with CSTO help — before distancing from Moscow once Ukraine burned. Its uranium feeds China (67% of imports), its oil crosses Russia, its enrichment happens in Russia, and its new reactors are split between Rosatom and CNNC — dependency diversified into leverage. The 2025 Subsoil Code (state stake ≥75%) is resource nationalism, Astana-style: quiet, legal, total.","timeline":[{"date":"1991","e":"Independence; Nazarbayev's three-decade rule begins; Semipalatinsk nukes renounced."},{"date":"2019","e":"Managed succession to Tokayev."},{"date":"Jan 2022","e":"'Qandy Qantar' unrest; CSTO intervention; Tokayev consolidates."},{"date":"2022-","e":"Distancing from Moscow post-invasion; Middle Corridor rises."},{"date":"2024","e":"Nuclear referendum (71% yes)."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Rosatom gets plant #1, CNNC plants #2-3; Subsoil Code stake-floor; record uranium output committed multi-vector."}],"actors":[{"name":"Kassym-Jomart Tokayev","bio":"The career-diplomat president — survived 2022, distanced Moscow, and plays Russia, China, and the West against each other by design."},{"name":"Kazatomprom","bio":"The state uranium champion — world's #1 producer whose output and stake-floors move global prices."},{"name":"Russia (Rosatom)","bio":"The enrichment chokehold and reactor incumbent — the dependency Astana dilutes but can't drop."},{"name":"China (CNNC)","bio":"The rising buyer-builder — 67% of Kazakh uranium and two of three new plants; the eastern vector winning share."}],"bridge":"Kazakhstan's multi-vector method is the small-state answer to the platform's biggest pattern: sitting on the resource (uranium #1) while lacking the midstream (enrichment), it converts dependency into auction — Rosatom's plant against CNNC's two, Western utilities against Chinese offtake, the Subsoil Code's 75% floor as the house's rake. Cooley and Heathershaw's 'dictators without borders' frame adds the caveat: the same statecraft that hedges empires also exports the elite's capital and quiets its politics. January 2022 showed the model's floor; the reactor auction shows its ceiling. · AND NOW, 2025-26: Rosatom gets plant #1, CNNC plants #2-3; Subsoil Code stake-floor; record uranium output committed multi-vector.","sources":[{"work":"Dictators Without Borders: Power and Money in Central Asia","author":"Alexander Cooley & John Heathershaw","year":"2017","note":"The transnational political economy of Central Asian statehood."},{"work":"World Nuclear Association / Kazatomprom disclosures","author":"WNA; Kazatomprom","year":"2024-26","note":"Primary production and contract data behind this theater's uranium figures."},{"work":"Qandy Qantar (January 2022) documentation","author":"Human Rights Watch / Adilet","year":"2022","note":"The primary record of the unrest and its suppression."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"Multi-vector statecraft converting dependency into auction — uranium #1 without the midstream.","alt":"A Russian-orbit reading holds the multi-vector autonomy as thinner than claimed — enrichment dependence and 2022 CSTO rescue show real limits.","why":"The reactor auction (Rosatom AND CNNC AND Western supply deals) demonstrates genuine hedging; the enrichment chokehold is the real limit, stated. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"News.az · Uzbekistan strategic partnerships (Jul 2026)","u":"https://news.az/news/why-uzbekistan-elevated-relations-with-belarus-after-kazakhstan-and-georgia"},{"t":"World Nuclear Association: Kazakhstan uranium","u":"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/country-profiles/countries-g-n/kazakhstan"},{"t":"Kazatomprom investor disclosures","u":"https://www.kazatomprom.kz/en"},{"t":"Carnegie: Central Asia analysis","u":"https://carnegieendowment.org/regions/central-asia"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"46 = economic and financial stress, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Provenance, check-mode and escalation-derivation fields added; no substantive content change this cycle."}],"spill":[{"n":"Russia","id":"russia-domestic","dir":"both","w":"The historic dependency being hedged — CPC pipeline exposure against new westward routes."},{"n":"Caucasus","id":"caucasus","dir":"out","w":"The Middle Corridor — Uzbekistan's partnerships with Kazakhstan, Georgia and Belarus build transport routes that secure access to western markets around Russia. Astana is the hinge."},{"n":"China","dir":"in","w":"Belt and Road infrastructure and the eastward export option that balances the Russian one.","id":"china"}],"inherit":{"name":"Soviet Nationalities Delimitation","est":"1924-1936","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Central Asia's republics were drawn by Soviet commissions in the 1920s and 1930s, deliberately creating <b>titular nationalities with borders that left substantial minorities on the wrong side</b> — Uzbeks in Kyrgyzstan, Tajiks in Uzbekistan, Russians across northern Kazakhstan. Ethnographers built nations that had been fluid regional and clan identities, and the borders were designed to make each republic dependent on Moscow rather than viable alone. Water, rail and power grids were engineered to run through the centre.","now":"<b>Those grids and borders became international frontiers in 1991 without being redesigned.</b> Kazakhstan's northern Russian-majority regions are the demographic fact underneath its careful hedging, and the CPC pipeline dependence is the infrastructural one. <b>The corridor diplomacy this board tracks — Uzbekistan's partnership sequence with Kazakhstan, Georgia and Belarus — is an attempt to build the westward routes the Soviet design deliberately omitted.</b>","change":"The CSTO deployed once, to Kazakhstan in January 2022, and did not come when Armenia needed it. <b>Members have drawn the conclusion and are building alternatives.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"OSCE · field operations and reporting","u":"https://www.osce.org/"},{"t":"Carnegie Endowment · regional programmes","u":"https://carnegieendowment.org/"}],"macrona":"NOT YET SOURCED. The structural facts are on the card: CPC pipeline dependence against new westward corridors, with Astana as the hinge of Central Asia's diversification."},{"id":"balkans","cat":"political","region":"Europe","subregion":"Europe","tclass":"conflict","name":"Western Balkans — Serbia-Kosovo + Bosnia Fracture","sub":"Frozen Kosovo dispute · Republika Srpska secession threat · KFOR lid","domain":"Frozen Ethno-Territorial Conflict","sev":"high","status":"volatile","since":"1990s (unresolved)","esc":60,"lat":43,"lon":20.5,"trend":"up","esc_history":[58,59,60,60,60,60,60,60,60,60],"cas":"Sept 2023 Banjska clash killed a Kosovo police officer; periodic incidents","disp":"Legacy displacement; parallel-institution friction in N. Kosovo","flag":"🇽🇰","note":"JUL 2026 — RUSSIA'S REACH IS ASSESSED AS FADING: analysis published this month argues Russian destabilising influence in the WESTERN BALKANS can be significantly contained given sufficient EU political will — a notably less alarmed reading than the prevailing one · the platform logs it as a contrary signal rather than a conclusion: the same argument has been made and falsified before, and Republika Srpska's institutional defiance is a domestic project that does not require Russian sponsorship to continue · JUL 2026 — ENLARGEMENT SPLITS THE REGION: Albania and Montenegro are surging ahead on the accession path while the EU is DIVIDED on advancing SERBIA absent tangible progress — differentiated enlargement is becoming the bloc's main lever, and the reform-laggard/reform-leader gap is now the region's real fault line · Albania's PM Rama attended Macron's Coalition of the Willing summit on Ukraine (Jul 13) — small-state alignment with the Western security track as accession currency · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · Europe) · TWO linked flashpoints: (1) Serbia-Kosovo frozen conflict — Serbia won't recognize Kosovo; friction over 4 Serb-majority N. municipalities; police raids on Serb institutions spark protests; Sept 2023 Banjska armed incursion (killed a KP officer, equipment traced to Serbia) · (2) Bosnia — Republika Srpska's Dodik threatening secession; Crisis Group: 'national collapse may be closer than ever'; Dayton framework straining at 30 yrs · new Croatia-Albania-Kosovo military pact alarms Belgrade · Serbia rocked by anti-Vučić student protests (Novi Sad canopy collapse killed 16) · NATO KFOR cut 4,700→3,000-3,500 · Russia backs Serbia's non-recognition + hybrid info ops; EU accession stalled","leverage":[{"actor":"Serbia (Vučić)","asset":"Military buildup (regional lead); 'protect co-nationals' doctrine over Kosovo/Bosnia/Montenegro; Russia/China ties","str":"hi"},{"actor":"NATO (KFOR)","asset":"Peacekeeping force = the physical lid preventing escalation; the ultimate deterrent","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Kosovo (Kurti)","asset":"Centralizing control over the north; Croatia-Albania military pact; US/EU recognition","str":"md"},{"actor":"Russia","asset":"Backs Serbian non-recognition + Republika Srpska; hybrid info ops; blocks normalization","str":"md"},{"actor":"EU","asset":"Accession carrot (stalled) + dialogue mediation; economic interdependence","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"European Commission Enlargement Report","detail":"Flagged undue political influence over Kosovo judiciary + media-freedom decline (Kosovo 99th of 180, lowest in EU-Balkans)"},{"src":"NATO/KFOR","detail":"Cut peacekeepers 4,700→3,000-3,500 citing improved north security; bolstered posture after Sept 2023 Banjska clash"},{"src":"Kosovo Specialist Chambers","detail":"War-crimes prosecutions continue (Thaçi trial) alongside national courts"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"Serbia / Rep. Srpska","flow":"Political backing for non-recognition; support for Dodik secession threats; hybrid information operations","tier":"documented","for":"Block Western Balkans EU/NATO integration; keep a foothold in Europe's soft underbelly"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US / 🇪🇺 EU","side":"Kosovo / Bosnia integrity","flow":"KFOR (NATO); EU accession framework; Western Balkans Democracy and Prosperity Act (2026 NDAA)","tier":"documented","for":"Regional stability; complete the European integration project"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"Serbia","flow":"Infrastructure investment + arms sales to Serbia","tier":"documented","for":"European foothold; Belt-and-Road terminus"}],"mil":"A frozen conflict held down by force. The Serbia-Kosovo dispute centers on the four Serb-majority municipalities of northern Kosovo, where Pristina's assertions of authority trigger protests, barricades, and periodic armed incidents — most seriously the September 2023 Banjska clash, when heavily armed men crossed from Serbia, killed a Kosovo police officer, and left behind equipment traced back to Serbia. NATO's KFOR is the physical lid; its drawdown from 4,700 to ~3,000-3,500 is a calculated bet that the north has stabilized.","pol":"Two crises compound. In Bosnia, Republika Srpska's Milorad Dodik openly threatens secession, and Crisis Group warns national collapse may be closer than ever as the 30-year-old Dayton framework strains. In Serbia, President Vučić faces the largest protests in decades (sparked by the Novi Sad station-canopy collapse that killed 16), which he blames on Western intelligence. A new Croatia-Albania-Kosovo military pact alarms Belgrade, and EU accession has stalled across the region.","eco":"Economic interdependence and the EU accession carrot are the main non-military deterrents to escalation, but the accession timeline's ambiguity is eroding their pull — pushing Western Balkan states to 'assume greater agency' and, in Serbia's case, deeper toward Moscow and Beijing.","assess":"The Western Balkans enters at 60 (ELEVATED, conflict-class), trend up — the sharpest gap in the platform's Europe coverage now filled. It's a textbook frozen conflict: KFOR keeps the lid on while the EU dialogue stalls, and northern Kosovo remains the likeliest trigger for a flare-up, with Bosnia's Republika Srpska secession threat as a second fuse. The Russia dimension makes it part of the broader European-security contest with Ukraine. Watch the KFOR drawdown, Dodik's secession moves, and any repeat of a Banjska-style incident.","tier":"ELEVATED","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Political","econlev":[{"holder":"EU","channel":"Accession + funding","exp":"MED","detail":"The accession carrot is the main non-military deterrent — but its stalling is pushing Serbia toward Moscow and Beijing"}],"contested":{"summary":"A frozen conflict kept warm by an information campaign.","claims":[{"claim":"Serbia denies involvement in the Banjska incursion.","who":"Belgrade — direct attribution would trigger EU/NATO consequences.","evid":"Equipment left at the scene was traced back to Serbia. A Kosovo police officer was killed.","status":"DOCUMENTED-BUT-DENIED"},{"claim":"Vučić blames the mass protests on Western intelligence services.","who":"Vučić — externalizing a domestic legitimacy crisis (the Novi Sad canopy collapse killed 16).","evid":"No evidence presented. The protests are the largest in decades and have a clear domestic trigger.","status":"UNSUPPORTED"}]},"history":{"roots":"The Balkans carry Europe's unfinished 1990s. Yugoslavia's wars ended in externally-imposed frameworks — Dayton for Bosnia, UNSCR 1244 then supervised independence for Kosovo — that froze conflicts rather than resolving them. Serbia never accepted Kosovo's statehood; Republika Srpska's Dodik works Dayton's seams toward secession; and Russia sponsors both revisionisms as cheap leverage against EU enlargement. The 2020s brought the sharpest tests since the wars: the 2023 Banjska attack in north Kosovo, Dodik's separation laws, and Serbia's massive student-led protest movement shaking Vučić's rule from below. The region's question is whether the EU's gravitational pull outlasts the spoilers' patience.","timeline":[{"date":"1991-99","e":"Yugoslav wars: Croatia, Bosnia (Srebrenica), Kosovo — ~130,000 dead."},{"date":"1995","e":"Dayton freezes Bosnia into two entities — the seams Dodik works."},{"date":"2008","e":"Kosovo declares independence; Serbia + Russia never accept it."},{"date":"2023","e":"Banjska attack — armed Serb incursion in north Kosovo."},{"date":"2024-26","e":"Dodik's secession laws vs state crackdown on him; Serbia's student protests vs Vučić; Russian info-ops back both revisionisms."},{"date":"2023-24","e":"Banjska attack in northern Kosovo; Dodik's Republika Srpska escalates secession legislation."},{"date":"2025","e":"Dodik convicted and barred from office; RS institutions defy the ruling, testing whether Dayton's structures still bind."},{"date":"2026","e":"EU accession leverage weakens as enlargement fatigue meets Russian and Chinese alternatives — the Balkans become a bidding contest again."}],"actors":[{"name":"Aleksandar Vučić","bio":"Serbia's dominant figure — balancing EU accession theater, Russian ties, and the largest protests of his rule."},{"name":"Milorad Dodik","bio":"Republika Srpska's secessionist — convicted, defiant, and Russia's most useful man in Bosnia."},{"name":"Kosovo (Kurti)","bio":"Pressing sovereignty in the Serb-majority north; the Banjska aftermath defines the standoff."},{"name":"Russia / EU","bio":"The spoiler and the anchor — Moscow's cheap leverage against Brussels' slow enlargement."}],"bridge":"The Balkans' 2026 is Dayton's arithmetic reaching its limits: Dodik works the entity seam toward secession while Serbia's streets work Vučić's legitimacy from below — the two pressures Bieber's authoritarian-stabilitocracy model predicted would eventually collide. Russia sponsors the revisionists cheaply; the EU's gravitational pull weakens with every postponed accession. Banjska showed the hardware exists for a Kosovo escalation; the platform's read is that the region's risk is not a new 1990s but a slow secession-by-procedure that tests whether externally imposed settlements can outlive their guarantors' attention.","sources":[{"work":"The Rise of Authoritarianism in the Western Balkans","author":"Florian Bieber","year":"2020","note":"The 'stabilitocracy' model — EU-tolerated strongmen as the region's operating system."},{"work":"ICTY judgments and archives","author":"UN ICTY/IRMCT","year":"1993-2017","note":"The primary adjudicated record of the 1990s wars — the baseline every revisionism contests."},{"work":"The Balkans: Nationalism, War, and the Great Powers","author":"Misha Glenny","year":"1999 (rev. 2012)","note":"The long-arc survey of external-power entanglement."}]},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"Dayton's arithmetic reaching its limits — secession-by-procedure testing whether imposed settlements outlive their guarantors.","alt":"A stability-holds reading argues the EU pull and war-fatigue make a 1990s rerun unlikely; the risk may be overstated.","why":"Banjska showed the hardware exists; the slow-secession read is more likely than open war. MOD."},"refs":[{"t":"New Union Post · Russia's reach in the Western Balkans","u":"https://newunionpost.eu/2025/05/27/russia-interference-western-balkans-eu/"},{"t":"Balkan Insight: Monday Briefing, Jul 13 2026","u":"https://balkaninsight.com/2026/07/13/monday-briefing-from-the-balkans-july-13-2026/bi/"},{"t":"ICG · Balkans","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/europe-central-asia/balkans"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"60 = institutional or electoral instability, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Timeline brought current from 2024 (Banjska, Dodik conviction, enlargement fatigue)"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Institutional defiance, contested sovereignty","dir":"stalemate","phase":"Frozen fragmentation","up":["Republika Srpska's secessionist steps escalating","Kosovo-Serbia incidents recurring","EU enlargement fatigue removing the incentive","External sponsors amplifying division"],"down":["EU accession conditionality with credible timelines","Analysis suggesting Russian reach is containable with political will","EUFOR/KFOR deterrent presence","Economic integration outpacing political division"],"fw":["ethnic-security","conflict-curve","deterrence"]},"spill":[{"n":"Russia","id":"russia-domestic","dir":"in","w":"Destabilising influence that recent analysis argues is CONTAINABLE given EU political will — a notably less alarmed reading than the prevailing one, logged as a contrary signal."},{"n":"European Union","dir":"both","w":"Accession conditionality is the main lever; enlargement fatigue is the main risk."},{"n":"Turkey","id":"turkey","dir":"in","w":"Historic and commercial reach across the region, particularly in Bosnia and Kosovo."}],"inherit":{"name":"Millet, Versailles and Dayton","est":"1878-1995","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Ottoman rule organised subjects by <b>millet</b> — confessional community rather than territory — so identity was religious and administrative before it was national. The Congress of Berlin in 1878 and the post-1918 settlement then imposed territorial nation-states on populations organised that way, creating minorities everywhere. <b>Dayton in 1995 ended a war by formalising the same logic:</b> Bosnia was divided into entities with ethnic quotas, a rotating presidency, and veto powers for each constituent people.","now":"<b>Dayton stopped the killing and froze the cause.</b> Republika Srpska governs its own police, courts, education and revenue at entity level, and has legislated to withdraw from state institutions — secession by subtraction rather than mobilisation, which the international toolkit built for the 1990s does not grip. <b>EUFOR Althea and KFOR hold two settlements with a few thousand troops</b>, which is the cheapest deterrent on this board and the most taken for granted.","change":"EU accession conditionality is the main lever and enlargement fatigue the main risk. <b>Recent analysis argues Russian influence here is containable given political will</b> — which this board logs as a contrary signal, since the RS project does not require Russian sponsorship to continue."},"monitors":[{"t":"Balkan Insight · BIRN","u":"https://balkaninsight.com/"},{"t":"OSCE · field operations and reporting","u":"https://www.osce.org/"},{"t":"Carnegie Endowment · regional programmes","u":"https://carnegieendowment.org/"}],"macrona":"DELIBERATELY EMPTY. A regional file covering several economies has no single macro line. EU accession conditionality is the economic lever and it is described in the assessment rather than in a growth rate."},{"id":"caucasus","cat":"strategic","region":"South Caucasus","subregion":"Caucasus","tclass":"watch","name":"South Caucasus — TRIPP Corridor + Russia's Retreat","sub":"US-brokered Armenia-Azerbaijan peace · Zangezur/TRIPP · Russia+Iran squeezed out","domain":"Connectivity Geopolitics / Post-Conflict Realignment","sev":"medium","status":"declining","since":"2020 war → 2025 peace","esc":45,"lat":40.2,"lon":45.5,"trend":"dn","esc_history":[47,46,45,45,45,45,45,45,45,45],"cas":"2020 war + 2023 Karabakh exodus (100k+ Armenians fled); now de-escalating","disp":"100k+ Karabakh Armenians displaced 2023 (unresolved right of return)","flag":"🇦🇲","note":"JUL 2026 — AZERBAIJAN IS NOW SUPPLYING SYRIA WITH GAS: President ALIYEV says Azerbaijan HAS BEGUN EXPORTING NATURAL GAS TO SYRIA and is considering expanding supplies to neighbouring countries · this is a new energy vector into the post-Assad Levant — Baku filling a supply role in a country Iran previously serviced, alongside Turkish and Gulf positioning · Georgia's protests continue at day 599; Uzbekistan has established strategic partnerships across the Kazakhstan-Georgia-Belarus sequence, building transport corridors westward that route around Russia · JUL 16 — Aliyev lauds Trump as 'a man of peace' crediting him with the Armenia-Azerbaijan deal (Breitbart interview amplified on Trump's feed) — Baku cementing the Washington-brokered corridor order by flattering its broker · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · Caucasus) · Aug 8 2025: US-brokered Armenia-Azerbaijan peace (Trump/White House; initialed) · centerpiece = TRIPP (Trump Route for Int'l Peace & Prosperity), aka Zangezur Corridor — 42km across S. Armenia linking Azerbaijan to Nakhchivan; Armenia keeps sovereignty but US gets 99-yr development rights · plugs the missing gap in the Middle Corridor (China-Europe trade bypassing Russia+Iran) · Azerbaijan lifted transit ban, sent fuel/grain to Armenia via Georgia (Q1 2026 trade ~$5.75M, historic) · OSCE Minsk Group dissolved · WINNERS: US (permanent S. Caucasus presence), Turkey (gateway), Azerbaijan · LOSERS: Russia (lost traditional ally Armenia + N-S corridor), Iran (condemned as encroachment on its border) · unresolved: Karabakh Armenian right of return; Armenia constitution territorial-claim clause","leverage":[{"actor":"United States","asset":"99-yr TRIPP development rights = permanent S. Caucasus foothold at Russia's southern door + Iran-influence lever","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Azerbaijan (Aliyev)","asset":"2020 military victory + Middle Corridor expansion; 'peace already achieved' leverage over Armenia","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Turkey","asset":"Shortest overland link to Central Asia via corridor; Caucasus gateway to the West","str":"md"},{"actor":"Armenia (Pashinyan)","asset":"Westward pivot for security after Russia failed to defend it in 2020; transit-country upside","str":"md"},{"actor":"Russia + Iran","asset":"Both squeezed — condemn TRIPP as encroachment; Russia retains Georgia leverage + N-S ambitions","str":"lo"}],"gov":[{"src":"US-brokered peace declaration (Aug 8 2025)","detail":"Trump hosted Pashinyan + Aliyev at White House; peace text initialed; TRIPP connectivity deal announced"},{"src":"US-Armenia implementation framework (Jan 2026)","detail":"Published TRIPP corridor implementation plan; rail/fiber/power/gas lines across S. Armenia"},{"src":"OSCE Minsk Group","detail":"Dissolved — confirmation there is 'no longer a Karabakh problem' in the two sides' framing"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"United States","channel":"Corridor control","exp":"HIGH","detail":"99-yr development rights over TRIPP/Zangezur — controls a key Middle Corridor link for China-Europe trade bypassing Russia+Iran"},{"holder":"Middle Corridor bloc","channel":"Trans-Caspian trade","exp":"MED","detail":"Kazakhstan-Azerbaijan-Georgia-Turkey route; TRIPP plugs the missing gap, boosting throughput off the Russia route"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"broker/developer","flow":"Brokered the peace; secured 99-yr exclusive TRIPP development rights via consortium","tier":"documented","for":"Permanent S. Caucasus presence; squeeze Russia+Iran; shape a China-Europe trade artery"},{"actor":"🇹🇷 Turkey","side":"Azerbaijan","flow":"Backs Azerbaijan; gains shortest overland Central Asia link; Armenia normalization track","tier":"documented","for":"Caucasus gateway role; Turkic-world connectivity"},{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"displaced patron","flow":"Condemns TRIPP as US encroachment; lost Armenia as ally after failing to defend it in 2020","tier":"documented","for":"Salvage N-S corridor ambition + regional influence (retains Georgia leverage)"},{"actor":"🇮🇷 Iran","side":"opponent","flow":"Condemns corridor as encroachment on its northern border; threatened the project","tier":"documented","for":"Prevent being bypassed/encircled on its Armenia border"}],"mil":"The trajectory is de-escalation, not conflict — which is precisely why it's a strategic-watch theater. Three decades of Armenia-Azerbaijan war (culminating in the 2020 six-week war and the 2023 Karabakh exodus of over 100,000 Armenians) gave way to a US-brokered peace initialed in August 2025. Fears of renewed military action have receded; Aliyev now says 'peace has already been achieved.' The unresolved wound is the Karabakh Armenians' right of return, which the deal does not address.","pol":"This is a great-power realignment disguised as a peace deal. The US brokered it and secured 99-year development rights over the TRIPP/Zangezur corridor — establishing a permanent presence at Russia's southern doorstep and Iran's northern border. Armenia, abandoned by Russia in 2020, has pivoted west; Russia and Iran both condemn the corridor as encroachment. The OSCE Minsk Group's dissolution confirms the old Russian-mediated order is over.","eco":"Connectivity is the engine. TRIPP plugs the missing gap in the Trans-Caspian 'Middle Corridor,' letting goods flow from China and Central Asia through Azerbaijan into Turkey and Europe without transiting Russia or Iran. Historic first trade flows (Azerbaijani fuel and grain reaching Armenia via Georgia, ~$5.75M in Q1 2026) mark the shift from enemies to trading partners.","assess":"The South Caucasus enters at 45 (GUARDED, strategic-watch), trend down — the platform's clearest example of a de-escalating theater that still matters intensely. It earns its place not for violence but for the leverage realignment: a US-managed corridor squeezing Russia and Iran out of their own backyard, reconnecting the Middle Corridor trade route, and pulling Armenia westward. It ties to the critical-minerals/Middle-Corridor logic (Kazakhstan uranium and Caspian trade flow through here). Watch corridor construction (breaks ground 2026), the Karabakh right-of-return wound, and any Russian or Iranian move to sabotage TRIPP.","tier":"GUARDED","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Strategic","contested":{"summary":"No significant narrative-war dimension identified in this theater at present. The main claims are broadly consistent with the evidence, or the disputes are policy disagreements rather than contested facts. This is stated affirmatively rather than left blank — the absence of an information operation is itself a finding.","claims":[]},"history":{"roots":"The South Caucasus is where three empires' borderlands became three small states with unfinished wars. Armenia and Azerbaijan fought twice over Nagorno-Karabakh (1990s; 2020) before Azerbaijan's 2023 lightning offensive emptied it of Armenians entirely — 100,000 fled in a week, ending a 35-year conflict by fait accompli. Russia's peacekeepers stood aside, discrediting Moscow's guarantee and pushing Armenia west. The 2025 Washington framework and the TRIPP/Zangezur corridor now redraw the map: a US-brokered transit route stitching Azerbaijan to Turkey through Armenia — the Middle Corridor's missing link, bypassing both Russia and Iran. The region's history of being transited is becoming its leverage.","timeline":[{"date":"1988-94","e":"First Karabakh war — Armenia takes the enclave and surroundings."},{"date":"2020","e":"Second war — Azerbaijan (Turkish drones) retakes most territory."},{"date":"Sep 2023","e":"Azerbaijan's offensive; 100,000 Armenians flee Karabakh in days."},{"date":"2024","e":"Armenia freezes CSTO membership — the break with Moscow."},{"date":"2025","e":"Washington framework + TRIPP corridor agreement."},{"date":"2026","e":"Corridor construction politics; Georgia's drift complicates the western route."}],"actors":[{"name":"Azerbaijan (Aliyev)","bio":"The victor — oil-funded, Turkish-backed, converting military victory into corridor leverage."},{"name":"Armenia (Pashinyan)","bio":"The defeated pivoting west — CSTO frozen, EU/US courted, signing the corridor its public distrusts."},{"name":"Turkey","bio":"Azerbaijan's patron ('one nation, two states') and the corridor's western terminus."},{"name":"Russia / Iran","bio":"The bypassed — both lose transit leverage to TRIPP; both retain the capacity to spoil."}],"bridge":"The TRIPP corridor is the region's history inverted: a land historically partitioned by empires for transit is now selling transit as sovereignty — Armenia signing the route its public distrusts because the alternative is irrelevance between a victorious Azerbaijan and a discredited Russian guarantee. De Waal's 'Black Garden' warning — that Karabakh's grievances outlive its map — hangs over the whole arrangement: 100,000 displaced Armenians are the settlement's unpriced liability, and the corridor's construction politics (plus Georgia's drift on the western route) decide whether the Washington framework becomes architecture or artifact. · AND NOW, 2026: Corridor construction politics; Georgia's drift complicates the western route.","sources":[{"work":"Black Garden: Armenia and Azerbaijan Through Peace and War","author":"Thomas de Waal","year":"2003 (rev. 2013)","note":"The definitive Karabakh account — both societies' narratives taken seriously."},{"work":"Washington framework / TRIPP agreement texts","author":"US State Department releases","year":"2025","note":"Primary documents of the corridor architecture."},{"work":"UNHCR displacement reporting on Nagorno-Karabakh","author":"UNHCR","year":"2023-24","note":"The primary record of the exodus — the settlement's human ledger."}]},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"History inverted — a region partitioned for transit now selling transit as sovereignty via TRIPP.","alt":"A frozen-grievance reading (de Waal's Black Garden warning) holds the 100,000 displaced Armenians as an unpriced liability that could unravel the corridor settlement.","why":"The platform holds both — the corridor as the present architecture and the displacement as its standing liability. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"News.az · Uzbekistan strategic partnerships (Jul 2026)","u":"https://news.az/news/why-uzbekistan-elevated-relations-with-belarus-after-kazakhstan-and-georgia"},{"t":"Crisis Group: Caucasus","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/europe-central-asia/caucasus"}],"human":{"dead":7000,"disp":100000,"famine":false,"aid":"functioning","note":"The 2020 war killed ~7k combatants on both sides; the Sep-2023 offensive displaced ~100,000 Armenians from Nagorno-Karabakh in under a week — effectively the entire population. That displacement is the settlement's unpriced liability.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"45 = strategic competition, a recorded human toll, trending DOWN over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Provenance, check-mode and escalation-derivation fields added; no substantive content change this cycle."}],"spill":[{"n":"Georgia","id":"georgia","dir":"both","w":"Day 599 of continuous protest in Tbilisi and Batumi — a movement that can neither win concessions nor be suppressed."},{"n":"Syria","id":"syria","dir":"out","w":"Azerbaijan has BEGUN EXPORTING NATURAL GAS TO SYRIA and is considering expanding to neighbours — Baku filling a supply role Iran previously held in the post-Assad Levant."},{"n":"Kazakhstan / Central Asia","id":"kazakhstan","dir":"both","w":"The corridor architecture routing around Russia; Uzbekistan's partnership sequence runs through here."},{"n":"Turkey","id":"turkey","dir":"in","w":"Ankara's alignment with Baku and the corridor to Central Asia."}],"inherit":{"name":"Stalin's Nagorno-Karabakh Arrangement","est":"1921-1923","status":"RESOLVED BY FORCE","what":"In 1921 the Caucasian Bureau, with Stalin as Nationalities Commissar, placed <b>Nagorno-Karabakh — an Armenian-majority territory — inside Soviet Azerbaijan as an autonomous oblast</b>, and Nakhchivan as an Azerbaijani exclave beyond Armenia. It was a textbook application of the principle that made every republic dependent on the centre to manage a grievance the centre had created. Within the USSR the arrangement was suppressed rather than settled.","now":"<b>It produced a thirty-year war that ended in 2023 with Azerbaijan retaking the territory and the Armenian population leaving.</b> The military question is closed; delimitation, corridor access and diaspora politics are not. <b>The most consequential outcome for this board is Armenia's reorientation:</b> Russia declined to intervene under the CSTO, and Yerevan has since turned procurement and alignment toward France, India and the EU — the clearest case here of a security guarantee failing and a state simply changing patron.","change":"Azerbaijan has begun <b>exporting natural gas to Syria</b> and is considering neighbouring markets — energy leverage extended into the post-Assad Levant. Georgia's protests have run 599 consecutive days."},"monitors":[{"t":"Caucasus Liveuamap (24 Jul 2026)","u":"https://caucasus.liveuamap.com/"},{"t":"UNHCR: Nagorno-Karabakh displacement","u":"https://www.unhcr.org/"},{"t":"Carnegie: South Caucasus","u":"https://carnegieendowment.org/"},{"t":"OSCE · field operations and reporting","u":"https://www.osce.org/"}],"macrona":"DELIBERATELY EMPTY at regional level. The economically significant development — Azerbaijan exporting gas to Syria and considering neighbouring markets — is recorded in the file and on the Syria card."},{"id":"ethiopia","cat":"military","region":"Horn of Africa","subregion":"Horn / NE Africa","tclass":"conflict","name":"Ethiopia — Tigray/Eritrea Powder Keg + Red Sea Bid","sub":"TPLF-federal clashes · Eritrea war risk · Abiy's Assab ambition · June elections","domain":"Multi-Front Conflict Risk / Regional War","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"2020 Tigray War","esc":78,"lat":9,"lon":38.7,"trend":"up","esc_history":[72,72,72,72,72,72,72,72,72,78],"cas":"Tigray War (2020-22) killed ~600,000; Jan 2026 Tselemti/Raya clashes renewed","disp":"5.1M displaced in 2021 (record); 1M+ refugees hosted","flag":"🇪🇹","note":"MAJOR CORRECTION — THE PRETORIA DEAL IS DEAD AND THE ALIGNMENTS HAVE INVERTED (verified 31 Jul 2026). This file recorded 'sporadic armed clashes' and an intact if strained framework. The actual position: · IN EARLY MAY 2026 THE TPLF REINSTATED ITS PRE-WAR REGIONAL GOVERNMENT under DEBRETSION GEBREMICHAEL, fully sidelining the federal-backed Tigray Interim Administration. Critical Threats' assessment: the move 'effectively killed the already moribund Pretoria peace deal' — TIGRAY IS NOW OUTSIDE ANY FEDERAL CONTROL, including in the elections · FANO HAS SUSTAINED OFFENSIVES ACROSS AMHARA SINCE MARCH 2026, disrupting the federal government's organisation of the 1 JUNE elections in affected areas, and claims to have deterred a week-long ENDF offensive at DEGA DAMOT in Gojjam with fighting for the CHOKIE MOUNTAIN chain · THE INVERSION THAT MATTERS: ACLED assesses that ERITREA AND THE SUDANESE ARMED FORCES are structurally positioning to SUPPORT A TPLF CAMPAIGN TO RECAPTURE WESTERN TIGRAY, and that TPLF-ALIGNED FANO MILITIAS would launch diversionary offensives to overstretch the ENDF. Eritrea fought AGAINST the TPLF in 2020-22 and Fano fought ALONGSIDE the federal government; both have switched sides. Local ACLED sources report Fano militias in western Oromia are TRAINED BY ERITREA · WESTERN TIGRAY REMAINS OCCUPIED BY AMHARA MILITIAS, which is the territorial question underneath all of it · THE OLA/OLF-SHANE FACTION SURRENDERED IN DECEMBER 2024 — this board carried it as an active insurgency · the NATIONAL DIALOGUE launched 15 July is dismissed by the opposition coalition and OLF-OLA as a 'mock process' controlled by the Prosperity Party · 20 JUL 2026 — CORRECTION TO THIS FILE: earlier v59 notes recorded the Addis protests as being against FEDERAL forced recruitment. Africa Center reporting (20 Jul) states thousands rallied in Addis Ababa against alleged forced recruitment of civilians in Tigray BY TPLF FORCES — the attribution runs the other way. The correction is logged rather than silently overwritten because the direction of the allegation is the entire political point: it is Addis-based mobilisation against the TPLF, not against conscription by the state · ACLED records sporadic armed clashes since early 2026 between TIGRAY DEFENSE FORCES and government forces in disputed territories and along the border · ICG: federal economic restrictions leave nearly four-fifths of Tigray needing assistance · THE SEA-ACCESS QUESTION: Ethiopia depends on Djibouti for nearly all trade, which officials call expensive and vulnerable; ICG frames a package of port options (Djibouti upgrades, Berbera with Mogadishu's agreement) as the alternative to a land grab — Abiy needs a deliverable his domestic audience wants · JUN-JUL 2026 — ELECTION WON, TIGRAY SQUEEZED, CONSCRIPTION PROTESTED: the ruling Prosperity Party took a decisive electoral victory (June) · the US has IMPOSED SANCTIONS over rising tensions in Tigray · ICG reports Addis has placed ECONOMIC RESTRICTIONS ON THE REGION amid the standoff with the TPLF over who controls it — nearly FOUR-FIFTHS of Tigray's population now requires food and other assistance, a siege-by-budget rather than by army · 18 Jul: hundreds marched in Addis Ababa against what protesters call FORCED MILITARY RECRUITMENT of relatives in Tigray — 'conscription by stealth' · the pattern this file tracks holds: the imperial question is being answered through fiscal and demographic instruments now, not only military ones · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · Horn) · 3-way powder keg: federal govt vs TPLF/Tigray vs Eritrea, 3 yrs after the 600k-death Tigray War · Jan 2026: TDF moved into disputed Tselemti + took Alamata/Korem; federal drone strikes; Ethiopian Airlines cancelled Tigray flights · Abiy accuses Eritrea of a secret TPLF pact (tsimdo rapprochement underway) · Amhara Fano militias turned against federal govt; Oromia OLA insurgency ongoing · ABIY'S RED SEA BID: 'Red Sea and Ethiopia cannot remain separated' — eyeing Eritrea's Assab port (landlocked Ethiopia pays Djibouti ~$1.5B/yr); Somaliland Berbera MoU angered Somalia · SUDAN LINK: Abiy close to UAE (backs RSF); Eritrea sides with SAF+Egypt · Iran-war fuel shortages hampering ENDF · June 2026 elections = flashpoint (TPLF legal status unresolved)","leverage":[{"actor":"Federal govt (Abiy)","asset":"ENDF airpower/drones; controls budget subventions to Tigray; UAE backing; but stretched across Amhara/Oromia/Tigray fronts","str":"md"},{"actor":"TPLF/Tigray (TDF)","asset":"Undemobilized army; controls Tigray; disputed-territory claims (Welkait/Tselemti); possible Eritrea tsimdo","str":"md"},{"actor":"Eritrea (Isaias)","asset":"Occupies parts of W. Tigray; alleged TPLF/Fano/OLA backing; Assab port = Abiy's casus belli; SAF/Egypt axis","str":"md"},{"actor":"UAE","asset":"Key Abiy backer (weapons flights); but Iran-war + RSF-support strains vs Egypt/Saudi (pro-Eritrea)","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Crisis Group (Briefing 210, Feb 2026)","detail":"'Powder Keg in the Horn' — high war risk 3-way; urges back channels among Addis, Asmara, Mekelle"},{"src":"Pretoria Agreement brokers (Kenya/SA/Nigeria)","detail":"2022 deal fraying; TPLF never demobilized; brokers urged to re-engage before collapse"},{"src":"Ethiopia federal govt","detail":"Revoked TPLF's legal party status (May 2025); struggling to organize June 2026 Tigray elections"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇦🇪 UAE","side":"federal govt","flow":"Weapons flights to ENDF (paused briefly during Iran war, resumed); key Abiy backer","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Horn/Red Sea influence; counterweight — but conflicts with its RSF support in Sudan"},{"actor":"🇪🇷 Eritrea","side":"anti-TPLF / anti-federal (shifting)","flow":"Occupies W. Tigray; alleged support to Fano/OLA; tsimdo rapprochement w/ Tigray vs Abiy","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Keep Ethiopia weak/constrained; block Assab ambition"},{"actor":"🇪🇬 Egypt","side":"Eritrea axis","flow":"Aligned with Eritrea + SAF vs Ethiopia; GERD Nile-dam rivalry","tier":"documented","for":"Nile water security; contain Ethiopian regional power"}],"mil":"A three-way powder keg three years after a war that killed an estimated 600,000. In January 2026 the Tigray Defence Forces moved into the disputed Tselemti district and took Alamata and Korem, drawing federal drone strikes and flight cancellations. The federal government is simultaneously fighting Amhara Fano militias (former allies) and the Oromo Liberation Army, while accusing Eritrea of forging a secret pact with the TPLF. Crisis Group's February 2026 briefing calls it a 'powder keg' where war would be easy to start and hard to stop.","pol":"Prime Minister Abiy — a Nobel Peace laureate for the 2018 Eritrea peace — now needs a unifying cause with June 2026 elections looming, and has increasingly signaled that regaining Red Sea access could be it, eyeing Eritrea's port of Assab ('the Red Sea and Ethiopia cannot remain separated'). The TPLF's unresolved legal status is the election flashpoint: if Tigray can't vote, tensions could pass the point of no return.","eco":"Landlocked Ethiopia pays Djibouti ~$1.5B/year for port access, driving the Red Sea/Assab ambition and the Somaliland Berbera MoU that angered Somalia. The economy is under acute strain — an IMF program the government can't jeopardize, and Iran-war fuel shortages that have hampered the military itself.","assess":"Ethiopia enters at 72 (ELEVATED, conflict-class), trend up — the Horn's central powder keg, now tracked. It ties directly into the Sudan theater (Abiy-UAE-RSF vs Eritrea-SAF-Egypt), the chokepoints/Red Sea system (Assab ambition), and the external-actor web (competing Gulf patrons). The June 2026 elections and the TPLF legal-status question are the near-term fuses; the Eritrea war risk is the catastrophic tail. Watch Tselemti/Welkait, the Abiy-Isaias rhetoric, and any Assab move.","tier":"SEVERE","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Military","econlev":[{"holder":"Ethiopia","channel":"Port dependency / Assab ambition","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Landlocked Ethiopia pays Djibouti ~$1.5B/yr for port access — driving Abiy's Red Sea/Assab ambition and the Somaliland Berbera MoU that angered Somalia"},{"holder":"UAE","channel":"Weapons + finance","exp":"MED","detail":"Key Abiy backer (weapons flights) — the same patron arming the RSF next door; a UAE-financed RSF training camp reportedly sits in Benishangul-Gumuz near the GERD"}],"contested":{"summary":"The same patron arms both a government and the militia massacring its neighbor's people.","claims":[{"claim":"The UAE backs Abiy's government (weapons flights) while also arming the RSF next door.","who":"The UAE — a Horn/Red Sea position built on both sides of a genocide.","evid":"Weapons flights to the ENDF are credibly reported. A UAE-financed RSF training camp reportedly sits in Benishangul-Gumuz near the GERD. Egypt and Eritrea align against Ethiopia in the same contest.","status":"ALLEGED-CREDIBLE"},{"claim":"Abiy denies preparing a move on Eritrea's Assab port.","who":"Abiy — an election-year unifying cause is a powerful motive to deny until ready.","evid":"'The Red Sea and Ethiopia cannot remain separated' is his own line. Landlocked Ethiopia pays Djibouti ~$1.5B/yr. Crisis Group calls it a powder keg.","status":"CONTESTED"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"mine","actor":"Lega Dembi + artisanal (Tigray/Oromia/Benishangul)","detail":"Gold is Ethiopia's top mineral export and a critical hard-currency source for a state under acute IMF strain. Much is artisanal and in conflict-affected regions (Tigray, Oromia) — extraction entangled with the multi-front war.","who":"Federal state banks the FX; artisanal output leaks through conflict zones","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"process","actor":"National Bank purchase → reserves / export","detail":"The central bank buys artisanal gold to build FX reserves — but chronic under-pricing drives large volumes into SMUGGLING toward Gulf refiners, the same leak as Mali and Sudan. Gold is regime lifeline and leak at once.","who":"Smuggling networks route a large share to Dubai, bypassing the state","tier":"alleged-credible"},{"mineral":"Gold","stage":"end","actor":"Central-bank reserves · Gulf bullion market","detail":"Refined gold shores up reserves or vanishes into Dubai. For a government fighting on three fronts and negotiating an IMF program, the gold FX line is strategically load-bearing.","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Potash","stage":"mine","actor":"Danakil Depression (Afar) — Circum/others","detail":"World-class potash reserves in the Danakil. Long-stalled by infrastructure and the ~$1.5B/yr port-access problem (landlocked Ethiopia pays Djibouti), it's the resource dimension underneath Abiy's Red Sea/Assab ambition — extraction that needs a coastline Ethiopia doesn't have.","who":"The port-dependency is the bottleneck — the reason Assab is a casus belli","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Potash","stage":"end","actor":"Fertilizer → regional + global agriculture","detail":"If the port problem is solved, Danakil potash feeds fertilizer markets — which is why Red Sea access (Assab, Berbera MoU) is framed as existential, and why the resource question is inseparable from the Eritrea war risk.","tier":"documented"}],"patterns":["dubai-gold","water-leverage"],"history":{"roots":"Ethiopia's crises are the politics of an African empire — the only one to defeat a European coloniser at Adwa (1896) and escape the Scramble, but an empire nonetheless. Menelik II's conquests assembled the modern state by incorporating the Oromo, Somali, and southern peoples into an Amhara-dominated imperial core; every Ethiopian regime since — Haile Selassie's monarchy, the Derg's terror, the TPLF's ethnic-federal EPRDF, Abiy's centralizing 'Medemer' — has been an answer to the question that conquest posed: whose state is this? Ethnic federalism (1995) wrote the nations into the constitution without resolving their relationship to the center; Bahru Zewde's history shows each settlement redistributing, never dissolving, the imperial question. Eritrea's secession (1993) — after Africa's longest liberation war — took the coastline, adding the landlocked condition that now drives Abiy's Red Sea revisionism. The Tigray war (~600,000 dead, one of this century's deadliest anywhere) was the ethnic-federal question turned total.","timeline":[{"date":"1991","e":"TPLF-led rebels topple the Derg; ethnic federalism installed."},{"date":"1993","e":"Eritrea secedes, leaving Ethiopia landlocked."},{"date":"1998-2000","e":"Ethiopia-Eritrea border war (~100,000 dead)."},{"date":"2018","e":"Abiy Ahmed becomes PM, sidelines the TPLF, makes peace with Eritrea (wins a Nobel)."},{"date":"2020-22","e":"Tigray war — one of the century's deadliest (~600,000 dead)."},{"date":"2022","e":"Pretoria truce pauses the Tigray war."},{"date":"2023-25","e":"Amhara Fano insurgency; Abiy's Red Sea/Assab push alarms Eritrea."},{"date":"2026","e":"TPLF-federal tensions and Eritrea war-risk reconverge; June election a flashpoint."},{"date":"Jun 2026","e":"Prosperity Party takes a decisive electoral victory; the US imposes sanctions over rising Tigray tensions."},{"date":"Jul 2026","e":"Federal economic restrictions on Tigray leave nearly four-fifths of the population needing food aid; protests in Addis against forced recruitment of Tigrayan relatives."}],"actors":[{"name":"Abiy Ahmed","bio":"PM since 2018; Nobel laureate turned wartime leader. Centralizer pursuing Red Sea access, at odds with Tigray, the Amhara, and Eritrea."},{"name":"TPLF","bio":"The formerly dominant Tigrayan party; fought the 2020-22 war and remains in uneasy standoff with Addis."},{"name":"Eritrea (Isaias Afwerki)","bio":"Abiy's 2018 peace partner turned likely adversary; the Red Sea ambition puts them on a collision course."},{"name":"Amhara Fano","bio":"An ethnic-Amhara militia-turned-insurgency, once allied with the federal army, now fighting it."}],"bridge":"Every live Ethiopian fault line today is the imperial question unresolved: TPLF-federal standoff (which nation holds the center), the Amhara Fano insurgency (the old core demoted), Oromo insurgency (the largest nation still peripheral), and the Assab obsession (the empire's lost sea). The GERD dam — the greatest pan-Ethiopian project since Adwa — is simultaneously the state's unifying myth and the trigger for Egypt's shadow war via Fano support. Abiy's June 2026 election and the Eritrea war-risk are not new stories; they are the 1896 empire still negotiating its own composition, now with drones.","sources":[{"work":"A History of Modern Ethiopia, 1855–1991","author":"Bahru Zewde","year":"2001","note":"The standard history by Ethiopia's foremost historian — empire-building, Adwa, and the successive settlements of the national question."},{"work":"The Ethiopian Revolution: War in the Horn of Africa","author":"Gebru Tareke","year":"2009","note":"The Derg era and the armed movements (TPLF, EPLF) whose victory built the 1991 order now unraveling."},{"work":"The Struggle for Eritrea's Independence","author":"Ruth Iyob","year":"1995","note":"Africa's longest liberation war and the secession that landlocked Ethiopia — the root of the Assab question."},{"work":"War and Famine reporting / Tigray documentation","author":"Ghent University (Jan Nyssen et al.) & Ethiopia Peace Observatory (ACLED)","year":"2020-26","note":"Primary casualty and atrocity documentation for the Tigray war and current Fano/Oromo conflicts."}]},"human":{"dead":600000,"disp":4000000,"famine":false,"aid":"partial","note":"Tigray war ~600k (AU-cited estimate) is the century's deadliest single war; current Fano/Oromo conflicts add displacement across Amhara/Oromia.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"The imperial question unresolved since Adwa: which nation holds the center, and the landlocked drive for a sea.","alt":"An ethnic-federalism-design reading blames the 1995 constitution's structure itself; a personalist reading centers Abiy's centralizing ambition as the proximate destabilizer.","why":"All three converge — the platform holds the imperial frame for the deep pattern and Abiy's centralization for the current trigger. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"ACLED · TPLF's move to fully control Tigray (Jun 2026)","u":"https://acleddata.com/report/tplfs-move-fully-control-tigray-poses-growing-and-expanding-threat-horn-africa"},{"t":"Critical Threats · Africa File (28 May 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/ethiopia-fano-tplf-abiy-somalia-isis-puntland-drc-m23-rwanda-rubaya-africa-file-may-28-2026"},{"t":"BTI 2026 · Ethiopia country report","u":"https://bti-project.org/en/reports/country-report/ETH"},{"t":"EEPA · Horn of Africa situation reporting (Jul 2026)","u":"https://indepthnews.net/eepa-reports-on-tplf-public-mobilization-in-tigray/"},{"t":"Africa Center · Africa Media Review (20 Jul 2026)","u":"https://africacenter.org/daily-media-review/africa-media-review-for-july-20-2026/"},{"t":"ACLED · Conflict in the Horn of Africa","u":"https://acleddata.com/region/conflict-horn-africa"},{"t":"ICG · Seven Peace and Security Priorities for Africa in 2026","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/brf/africa/b209-seven-peace-and-security-priorities-africa-2026"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"78 = active multi-front conflict (base ~72) raised for the collapse of the Pretoria framework, a regional government operating outside federal control, sustained Fano offensives disrupting national elections, and an assessed Eritrea-TPLF-SAF alignment to retake Western Tigray. Not higher because full-scale interstate fighting has not resumed; not lower because every party that ended the last war has now changed sides. · NOTE ON THE TRAJECTORY CHART: the step in this theater's history is a CORRECTION TO THIS BOARD'S READ, not a real-world escalation — the board carried 72 while the Pretoria framework collapsed in May; the step to 78 is the correction. The flat line before it is what the platform actually recorded, and it is left visible rather than smoothed.","log":[{"v":"v60","d":"31 Jul 2026","c":"MAJOR CORRECTION: file recorded sporadic clashes under a strained but intact Pretoria framework. Verification found the framework dead since May 2026 — TPLF reinstated its pre-war government, Fano on sustained offensive since March, and Eritrea/SAF positioning behind a TPLF campaign. Escalation raised 72 → 78 and the read rebuilt. The OLA entry was also stale: the OLF-Shane faction surrendered in December 2024."},{"v":"v59","d":"24 Jul 2026","c":"CORRECTION: the Addis protest attribution was reversed. Earlier notes recorded protests against FEDERAL forced recruitment; Africa Center reporting places them against alleged TPLF forced mobilisation in Tigray. Logged in-note rather than silently overwritten."},{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Election result, US sanctions, Tigray economic restrictions, conscription protests"}],"spill":[{"n":"Somalia / Somaliland","dir":"both","w":"Addis seeks commercial sea access via Berbera, holds military agreements with Somaliland (which Israel just recognised), and the port question is the alternative to a land grab."},{"n":"Eritrea","dir":"in","w":"Ethiopia has accused Eritrea of backing armed groups; the Assab axis is a live tripwire, and Eritrean troops remain a border factor after Tigray."},{"n":"Sudan","dir":"in","w":"The war on the northwestern flank compounds an already contested border and the al-Fashaga dispute.","id":"sudan"},{"n":"Djibouti","dir":"out","w":"Near-total dependence on Djibouti for trade — expensive and, officials say, strategically vulnerable, which drives the sea-access push."},{"n":"Egypt","id":"egypt","dir":"out","w":"The GERD and the Nile allocation question — the resource issue underneath every Egyptian strategic calculation, and Addis's strongest structural card."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Pretoria dead, TPLF governing independently, Fano on the offensive","dir":"climbing","phase":"Relapse — pre-war alignments reversed","up":["A TPLF campaign to retake WESTERN TIGRAY, with Eritrean and Sudanese Armed Forces backing assessed as likely","Fano offensives across Amhara overstretching the ENDF on a second front","Eritrean-trained Fano activity in western Oromia risking mass ethnic violence","A National Dialogue the opposition and OLF-OLA both reject as staged"],"down":["A renegotiated framework that includes Eritrea — the party Pretoria left out","Resolving the Western Tigray occupation, the territorial question underneath the whole conflict","Lifting federal economic restrictions on Tigray","An inclusive dialogue with legitimacy the current process lacks"],"fw":["ethnic-security","security-dilemma","conflict-curve","proxy"]},"macro":{"growth":"7.1% (2026f) · 7.7% (2027f) · GDP $125.7B","infl":"9.4% (2026 est.)","src":"IMF / World Bank Global Economic Prospects, 2026","note":"ONE OF THE FASTEST-GROWING ECONOMIES ON THIS BOARD, DURING AN UNRESOLVED CONFLICT. World Bank attributes the outlook to monetary and financial-sector reform including exchange-rate liberalisation. Imports run 45.5% from CHINA. The contradiction the file has to hold: aggregate growth near 7% alongside federal economic restrictions leaving roughly four-fifths of Tigray dependent on aid. National growth figures do not disaggregate a region under siege."},"corrdelta":"This period's change is a CORRECTION to this board's read, not movement in the theater.","inherit":{"name":"The Neftägna-Gabbar System","est":"1880s-1890s","status":"ABOLISHED · LAND QUESTION IN FORCE","what":"Ethiopia was never European-colonised, which is why this instrument is usually left out of the conversation — and it is the reason the country is at war. <b>Menelik II's conquests of the 1880s and 1890s doubled the empire southward and eastward</b>, absorbing Oromo, Sidama, Somali and dozens of other peoples. Conquered land was allotted to <b>neftägna</b> — armed northern settlers — who held the local population as <b>gabbar</b>, tribute-paying cultivators bound to them. Amharic became the language of the state and Orthodox Christianity its idiom; southern peoples' land, language and law were subordinated inside a state that had defeated Europeans.","now":"<b>The land question the system created is the axis of every current Ethiopian conflict.</b> Oromo grievance over land alienation and political exclusion, Somali-region irredentism in the Ogaden Menelik took in 1897, and the Amhara-Tigrayan contest for the centre are all arguments about who conquered whom and what is owed. Fano, the OLA and the TPLF are fighting over the shape of that inheritance, not over ideology. <b>Western Tigray remains occupied by Amhara militias</b> — a live territorial dispute inside the same frame.","change":"The 1974 revolution abolished the land tenure system with the slogan 'land to the tiller'; the 1995 constitution built ethnic federalism explicitly to answer the conquest question, granting nations the right to self-determination up to secession. <b>Both are contested by everyone currently fighting.</b> The TPLF reinstated its own regional government in May 2026, and Eritrea — which fought the TPLF in 2020-22 — is now assessed to be backing it."}},{"id":"mozambique","cat":"energy","region":"Southern Africa","subregion":"Southern Africa","tclass":"conflict","name":"Mozambique — Cabo Delgado Gas Insurgency","sub":"ISIS-linked insurgency · $20B TotalEnergies LNG restart · Rwandan troops","domain":"Resource Insurgency / Extraction Conflict","sev":"high","status":"active","since":"2017","esc":63,"lat":-12.3,"lon":40.5,"trend":"up","esc_history":[62,62,63,63,63,63,63,63,63,63],"cas":"6,400+ killed since 2017 (ACLED); 2021 Palma attack killed ~800","disp":"1.3M+ displaced from Cabo Delgado","flag":"🇲🇿","note":"JUL 2026 — SOVEREIGN RISK 'SEVERE', AND EBOLA IS NOT THE ONLY CROSS-BORDER FLOW: the BANK OF MOZAMBIQUE warns sovereign risk remained SEVERE in 2025 as domestic public debt rose 16.5% to 474 billion meticais (~$6.5B) — the debt story running underneath the Cabo Delgado gas story · India provided humanitarian assistance to FLOOD-AFFECTED Mozambique · Mozambican nationals are among those targeted in South Africa's anti-migrant violence — the regional pole's unrest reaching Maputo's citizens · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · S. Africa) · ISIS-linked insurgency (local 'Al-Shabaab', unrelated to Somalia's) in gas-rich Cabo Delgado since 2017 — triggered by gas discovery + extraction locals feel excluded from · Jan 2026: TotalEnergies RESTARTED its $20B LNG project (force majeure lifted after 2021 Palma attack froze it ~40% built); first gas 2029; could make Mozambique a top-10 gas producer (20% of African output by 2040) · Rwandan troops (from 2021) + SADC secured the Afungi enclave after Wagner failed · insurgency persists in the north (beheadings, kidnappings); 'closed enclave' model exposes communities · ExxonMobil $30B Rovuma + ENI Coral also developing · UK+Netherlands withdrew financing Dec 2025; TotalEnergies facing French war-crimes-complicity + GBV complaints","leverage":[{"actor":"TotalEnergies + majors","asset":"$20B (Total) + $30B (Exxon Rovuma) + ENI — capital that makes Cabo Delgado strategic; enclave security","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Mozambique govt (Chapo)","asset":"Gas revenue prize (top-10 producer potential); but pressured to fund project security at its own cost","str":"md"},{"actor":"Rwanda","asset":"Troops securing Afungi = the enabler of the restart; extradition-treaty + influence payback","str":"md"},{"actor":"ISIS-linked insurgents","asset":"Persistent rural violence; can threaten (not stop) the project; feeds on extraction grievance","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"ACLED","detail":"6,400+ killed since 2017; insurgency persists despite military interventions; 'failed military strategy'"},{"src":"UN report 'Voices of Mozambique' (Jun 2026)","detail":"Documents GBV by Mozambican military + Al-Shabaab; coercion by TotalEnergies-linked staff"},{"src":"UK UKEF / Netherlands","detail":"Withdrew ~$1.15B+ project financing Dec 2025 over security/human-rights concerns"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Western majors (Total/Exxon/ENI)","channel":"LNG megaprojects","exp":"HIGH","detail":"$50B+ combined; Mozambique could supply 20% of African gas by 2040 — Europe/Asia energy-security prize driving the security calculus"},{"holder":"Rwanda","channel":"Security provision","exp":"MED","detail":"Troops securing the enclave = leverage over Mozambique + the majors; extradition/influence payback"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇷🇼 Rwanda","side":"govt/majors","flow":"~1,000+ troops securing the Afungi LNG enclave (from 2021, after Wagner failed); ongoing deployment","tier":"documented","for":"Influence, extradition treaty, standing with France/TotalEnergies + EU"},{"actor":"🇫🇷 France (TotalEnergies)","side":"extractor","flow":"$20B LNG project; funded/supported the Joint Task Force (war-crimes complicity complaint in France)","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Gas reserves; European energy-security supply"},{"actor":"SADC","side":"govt","flow":"SAMIM multinational force (2021-) alongside Rwanda + Mozambican FADM","tier":"documented","for":"Regional stability; contain jihadist spread"}],"mil":"An ISIS-linked insurgency — the local 'Al-Shabaab,' unrelated to Somalia's — has ravaged the gas-rich Cabo Delgado province since 2017, killing over 6,400 and displacing 1.3 million. The 2021 Palma attack (roughly 800 dead) froze the LNG megaprojects. After Wagner failed, Rwandan and SADC forces secured the Afungi construction enclave, enabling the January 2026 restart, but the insurgency persists in the north with beheadings and kidnappings — a 'closed enclave' model that critics say protects the gas site while exposing surrounding communities.","pol":"The conflict is inseparable from the extraction: the insurgency was triggered in part by the gas discovery and the sense among Cabo Delgado's population — over 80% below the poverty line — that they would not benefit. TotalEnergies faces French legal proceedings including a war-crimes-complicity complaint and a UN-documented gender-based-violence report implicating project-linked staff and the Mozambican military, while the UK and Netherlands withdrew financing in December 2025.","eco":"This is a pure extraction theater. TotalEnergies' $20B project (first gas 2029), ExxonMobil's $30B Rovuma, and ENI's Coral could make Mozambique a top-10 gas producer supplying 20% of African output by 2040 — a European and Asian energy-security prize. The economics drive everything: the government is pressured to fund the project's security at its own cost, and the majors' capital is what makes an impoverished province geopolitically strategic.","assess":"Mozambique enters at 63 (ELEVATED, conflict-class), trend up — the clearest extraction-conflict case in the platform. It's the textbook expression of the framework's thesis: a resource discovery generating an insurgency, foreign troops securing the enclave, foreign majors extracting the value, and the local population bearing the cost. It ties to the critical-minerals/energy logic and the external-actor web (Rwanda, France). Watch the restart's security through construction, insurgent moves toward Palma/Afungi, and the Exxon Rovuma FID.","tier":"ELEVATED","hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk13 (Jul 2026)","domain_tag":"Energy","contested":{"summary":"The gas restarted. The insurgency didn't stop. Only the enclave is safe.","claims":[{"claim":"TotalEnergies frames the LNG restart as evidence that security has been restored.","who":"TotalEnergies ($20B), the majors behind Rovuma and Coral, and a government that needs the revenue.","evid":"The Afungi ENCLAVE is secured by Rwandan and SADC troops. The insurgency persists in the north — beheadings, kidnappings continue. UK and Netherlands WITHDREW financing (Dec 2025) over security and human-rights concerns. A UN report documents GBV by the military and project-linked staff; TotalEnergies faces a French war-crimes-complicity complaint.","status":"CONTESTED — 'secure' describes the perimeter, not the province"}]},"pipeline":[{"mineral":"Natural Gas","stage":"mine","actor":"Rovuma Basin, Cabo Delgado (offshore)","detail":"World-class offshore gas discovered 2010 — the discovery that triggered the ISIS-linked insurgency, because a population 80%+ below the poverty line saw no benefit. The 2021 Palma attack (~800 dead) froze everything at ~40% built.","who":"Insurgency feeds on the extraction grievance; the gas is the war's cause","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"Natural Gas","stage":"process","actor":"Afungi LNG enclave — secured by Rwandan troops","detail":"TotalEnergies restarted its $20B project (Jan 2026, force majeure lifted) after RWANDAN + SADC troops secured the Afungi enclave (Wagner had failed). ExxonMobil's $30B Rovuma + ENI's Coral also developing. The 'closed enclave' model: protect the gas site, expose the surrounding communities.","who":"Rwanda provides enclave security = leverage over Mozambique + the majors","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"LNG","stage":"mid","actor":"Liquefied → tankers → Europe + Asia","detail":"First gas targeted 2029. Mozambique could become a top-10 gas producer supplying 20% of African output by 2040 — a European and Asian energy-security prize. UK and Netherlands WITHDREW ~$1.15B+ financing (Dec 2025) over security/rights concerns; TotalEnergies faces a French war-crimes-complicity complaint + a UN GBV report.","who":"Western majors (Total/Exxon/ENI) capture the value; the state funds the security","tier":"documented"},{"mineral":"LNG","stage":"end","actor":"European power grids · Asian utilities","detail":"The end-buyer is Europe's post-Russia gas diversification and Asian demand. The extraction lens in full: a resource discovery generating an insurgency, foreign troops securing the enclave, foreign majors extracting, the local population bearing the cost.","tier":"documented"}],"history":{"roots":"Mozambique's gas war was written in its liberation war's own textbook. Eduardo Mondlane — the anthropologist who founded FRELIMO and was assassinated by parcel bomb in 1969 — analyzed Portuguese colonialism as pure extraction: forced cotton labor, migrant miners exported to the Rand, a colony run to service others' economies. FRELIMO won in 1975, inherited that structure, and was immediately bled by Rhodesian- then apartheid-sponsored Renamo in a destabilization war (~1 million dead) that was southern Africa's punishment for hosting the ANC and defying the settler states — a war Anglophone media long miscast as civil when its engine sat in Pretoria. Peace (1992) brought donor-darling growth that repeated the colonial geometry: megaprojects and aluminum smelters for the ledger, poverty for the province — and when Rovuma's gas was found beneath Cabo Delgado, the country's most neglected, Muslim, historically trader-cosmopolitan province, the insurgency that erupted there in 2017 recruited on exactly that exclusion, whatever ISIS flag it flies.","timeline":[{"date":"1975","e":"Independence from Portugal; Frelimo's one-party rule begins."},{"date":"1977-92","e":"Civil war with Renamo — ~1M dead."},{"date":"2010","e":"Rovuma Basin gas discovered — the stakes transform."},{"date":"2017","e":"Cabo Delgado insurgency begins."},{"date":"2021","e":"Palma attack (~800 dead); Total declares force majeure; Rwanda deploys."},{"date":"2024-25","e":"Disputed election; mass protests, hundreds killed."},{"date":"2026","e":"Total restarts the $20B project; UK/Dutch financing withdrawn over rights concerns; first gas targeted 2029."}],"actors":[{"name":"Frelimo (Chapo)","bio":"The 50-year ruling party — survived the ballot crisis; the gas rents are its next lease on power."},{"name":"The insurgency (ISIS-Mozambique)","bio":"The exclusion revolt in the gas province — degraded, displaced, not defeated."},{"name":"Rwanda","bio":"The enclave's guarantor — Kagame's troops protect Total's site; security export as foreign policy."},{"name":"TotalEnergies / the majors","bio":"The $50B+ bet — restarted under a war-crimes-complicity complaint and withdrawn European financing."}],"bridge":"The Total enclave — foreign troops (Rwanda's) guarding foreign capital's gas fenced off from the province that owns it — is Mondlane's diagnosis rebuilt in steel: extraction serviced, hinterland policed. The 'hidden debts' scandal (secret loans through European banks that collapsed the economy) showed the creditor circuit's continuity; the withdrawn UK/Dutch financing and the war-crimes complaint against Total show the same circuit's newer conscience. First gas in 2029 will pose the founding question a third time: does the surplus finally build Mozambique, or does Mozambique keep building others' balance sheets? The 2024 ballot crisis says the population has stopped waiting politely for the answer.","sources":[{"work":"The Struggle for Mozambique","author":"Eduardo Mondlane","year":"1969","note":"The founder's primary analysis of Portuguese extraction — the framework his successors' gas economy now tests."},{"work":"Apartheid's Contras: An Inquiry into the Roots of War in Angola and Mozambique","author":"William Minter","year":"1994","note":"The destabilization war's external engine — Rhodesia and Pretoria's sponsorship of Renamo documented."},{"work":"'Hidden debts' investigations","author":"Kroll audit / Mozambique constitutional council & US DOJ filings","year":"2017-21","note":"Primary record of the secret-loans scandal — the creditor circuit in the democratic era."},{"work":"Cabo Delgado conflict analyses","author":"Joseph Hanlon (ed., Mozambique News Reports & Clippings) / Cabo Ligado (ACLED)","year":"2017-26","note":"The running primary chronicle of the insurgency and the gas economy's collision."}]},"human":{"dead":6000,"disp":600000,"famine":false,"aid":"enclave-shadowed","note":"Cabo Delgado ~6k dead, ~600k displaced (peak 1M) — the province the \u0000B gas enclave is fenced against.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"The gas discovery's exclusion economy produced the insurgency in the province it enriched on paper.","alt":"The insurgency's religious-network lineage (Kenyan/Tanzanian preachers) predates LNG revenue and recruitment began before major construction — ideology and local grievance may be primary, gas an accelerant and target.","why":"Timing supports accelerant; geography supports exclusion. MOD, both stated."},"refs":[{"t":"Bank of Mozambique via Africa Risk Consulting","u":"https://www.africariskconsulting.com/chronologies/weekly"},{"t":"VIF · Africa Now (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.vifindia.org/2026/july/17/Africa-Now-01-15-july-2026"},{"t":"Crisis Group: Mozambique","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/southern-africa/mozambique"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"63 = strategic competition, a recorded human toll, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Cabo Delgado insurgency added — flagged as the cleanest extraction case on the board"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Cabo Delgado insurgency beside an LNG restart","dir":"volatile","phase":"Contained insurgency, unresolved drivers","up":["Insurgent attacks resuming near the LNG zone","Rwandan/SAMIM force drawdown leaving a vacuum","Sovereign debt stress limiting state capacity","Northern grievance untouched by security gains"],"down":["Rwandan deployment sustaining the security perimeter","LNG revenue actually reaching Cabo Delgado","Addressing the exclusion the insurgency recruits on","Regional force coordination"],"fw":["resource-conflict","ethnic-security","conflict-curve"]},"spill":[{"n":"Rwanda","id":"rwanda","dir":"in","w":"Rwandan troops secure the Cabo Delgado LNG zone, paid via European energy interests — African security services sold to protect European supply."},{"n":"South Africa","id":"south-africa","dir":"in","w":"Mozambican nationals targeted in the anti-migrant violence — the regional pole exporting its crisis."},{"n":"Tanzania","dir":"both","w":"The northern border, refugee movement, and the insurgent recruitment corridor."}],"macro":{"growth":"LNG-dependent","infl":"n/a this cycle","src":"Bank of Mozambique, 2026","note":"The central bank warns sovereign risk remained SEVERE in 2025 as domestic public debt rose 16.5% to 474 billion meticais (~$6.5B). The gas story and the debt story run underneath each other — Cabo Delgado's security perimeter is being held by Rwandan troops paid via European energy interests while the state's own balance sheet deteriorates."},"inherit":{"name":"The Labour and Transit Corridors","est":"1897-1928","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Portugal ran Mozambique as a <b>service economy for its neighbours</b>. Under agreements with the Transvaal from 1897, Mozambican men were recruited in tens of thousands for South African mines, with a portion of their wages paid to Lisbon in gold. <b>Chibalo</b> — forced labour — supplied plantations and public works. The railways were built east-west from Rhodesian and South African mines to Mozambican ports, not north-south to connect Mozambicans, because the country's function was transit.","now":"<b>The geography still runs sideways.</b> The Beira and Nacala corridors serve landlocked neighbours; Cahora Bassa's power has historically flowed to South Africa; and Mozambican labour migration to South Africa continues — which is why Mozambican nationals are among those targeted in the current anti-migrant violence there. <b>Cabo Delgado's gas is the newest version of the same pattern:</b> a $20B+ project in the country's poorest province, secured by Rwandan troops paid via European energy interests, against an insurgency recruiting on exclusion from it.","change":"The Bank of Mozambique warns sovereign risk remained <b>severe</b> in 2025 as domestic public debt rose 16.5% to roughly $6.5B. The insurgency has been reduced to an estimated 300-1,000 fighters without being ended."},"monitors":[{"t":"Cabo Ligado (ACLED) conflict observatory","u":"https://acleddata.com/"},{"t":"ISS Africa · Cabo Delgado analysis","u":"https://issafrica.org/"},{"t":"ACLED · Mozambique conflict data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}]},{"id":"kashmir","cat":"military","region":"South Asia","subregion":"South Asia","tclass":"conflict","name":"India-Pakistan — Kashmir + Water War","sub":"'Armed coexistence' post-May war · Indus Treaty suspended · nuclear dyad","domain":"Nuclear Rivalry / Resource Weaponization","sev":"high","status":"volatile","since":"1947 Partition","esc":66,"lat":34,"lon":74.8,"trend":"ss","esc_history":[66,66,66,66,66,66,66,66,66,66],"hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk14 (Jul 2026)","cas":"May 2026 4-day war killed dozens both sides; Apr 2025 Pahalgam attack killed 26; Nov 2025 Delhi bombing killed 8","disp":"Kashmir civilians bear the brunt; border-state displacement","flag":"🇮🇳","note":"JUN-JUL 2026 — THE DEADLY UNREST IS IN THE PAKISTANI-ADMINISTERED HALF, OVER AN ELECTION: June was one of the DEADLIEST MONTHS OF DOMESTIC UNREST IN PAKISTANI-ADMINISTERED KASHMIR in years — clashes between security forces and civil society activists left DOZENS DEAD · the trigger was the announcement of PROVINCIAL ELECTIONS: the government failed to agree with the civil society consortium JKJAAC on the assembly's composition · THE DISPUTE IS REVEALING — JKJAAC opposes RESERVING A QUARTER OF SEATS for refugees from INDIAN-administered Kashmir residing elsewhere in Pakistan, arguing it lets national parties override local autonomy; the government says the reservation is constitutionally mandated to maintain its CLAIM over the whole of Kashmir · the platform's competing read gains a third strand: the autonomy grievance is now being fought on the PAKISTANI side too, not only the Indian — both administrations face the same demand from the governed · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · South Asia) · nuclear dyad in 'armed coexistence' (Crisis Group) after the most serious clash in decades · Apr 2025 Pahalgam attack (LeT offshoot, 26 killed) → India's May 2026 'Operation Sindoor' (strikes on 9 sites in Pakistan/PaK) → 4-day aerial war, dozens dead, both claimed victory · India SUSPENDED the 6-decade Indus Waters Treaty — Pakistan calls it an existential threat (Indus/Chenab/Jhelum feed its agriculture); potential permanent rupture · Nov 2025 Delhi vehicle bombing (8 dead) India labeled terror after 3 days · virtually no official contact; LoC skirmishes persist · China border (LAC) a 2nd front — mistrust since 2020 Galwan, slow normalization talks · Bangladesh's tilt to Pakistan post-Hasina adds an eastern front for India","leverage":[{"actor":"India","asset":"Upper-riparian control of the Indus system (Treaty suspension = water weapon); conventional military edge; 'Operation Sindoor' precedent","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Pakistan","asset":"Nuclear parity + LeT/proxy leverage in Kashmir; rising geopolitical standing; China alliance","str":"md"},{"actor":"China","asset":"LAC pressure = 2nd front pinning India; Pakistan's patron; CPEC through PaK","str":"md"},{"actor":"Water (Indus system)","asset":"90% of Pakistan's agriculture depends on the Indus rivers India controls upstream — the ultimate coercive lever","str":"hi"}],"gov":[{"src":"Crisis Group","detail":"India-Pakistan in 'armed coexistence' — little room for diplomacy, narrow margin for error; Kashmir conflict 'far from resolved'"},{"src":"Indus Waters Treaty (suspended 2025)","detail":"India withdrew from the 1960 World Bank-brokered treaty after Pahalgam; Pakistan calls it an act of war / existential threat"},{"src":"India MEA / Operation Sindoor (May 2026)","detail":"Struck 9 sites in Pakistan and Pakistan-administered Kashmir; most serious bilateral confrontation since 2019"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"India","channel":"Trans-boundary water","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Upper-riparian control of Indus/Chenab/Jhelum + Treaty suspension = leverage over ~90% of Pakistan's irrigated agriculture — a resource weapon short of war"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"Pakistan","flow":"All-weather ally; CPEC through Pakistan-administered Kashmir; LAC pressure on India as a second front","tier":"documented","for":"Contain India; secure CPEC/Belt-and-Road corridor to the Arabian Sea"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"India (tilt)","flow":"Strategic partner vs China; but urged de-escalation in May war; Pakistan standing has risen","tier":"documented","for":"India as a China counterweight in the Indo-Pacific"}],"mil":"Two nuclear-armed states in what Crisis Group calls 'armed coexistence' — a phase with little room for diplomacy and a narrow margin for error. The 2025-26 cycle was the most dangerous in decades: the April 2025 Pahalgam massacre (26 killed by a Lashkar-e-Taiba offshoot) triggered India's May 2026 'Operation Sindoor,' strikes on nine sites in Pakistan and Pakistan-administered Kashmir, escalating into a four-day aerial war with dozens dead and both sides claiming victory. LoC skirmishes persist, and the China border (LAC) remains a simmering second front since the 2020 Galwan clash.","pol":"The most consequential shift is the weaponization of water: India suspended the six-decade Indus Waters Treaty after Pahalgam, which Pakistan — 90% dependent on the Indus system for agriculture — calls an existential threat and a potential act of war. With virtually no official contact between the two, a December 2025 handshake in Dhaka was notable as the barest sign of thaw. Regionally, the post-Hasina realignment of Bangladesh toward Pakistan opens an eastern front of concern for India.","eco":"Water is the economic weapon. Beyond the Indus leverage, the rivalry disrupts everything from cricket revenue (the India-Pakistan World Cup match moved to neutral Colombo) to regional trade, and pulls both into the US-China contest — India as a Western counterweight, Pakistan rising as China's partner.","assess":"India-Pakistan/Kashmir enters at 66 (ELEVATED, conflict-class) — a nuclear flashpoint the platform was missing. Its defining 2026 feature is the shift from territorial dispute to resource weaponization: the Indus Waters Treaty suspension is a textbook leverage move, giving upper-riparian India a coercive water lever over Pakistan's agriculture short of war. It ties to the Pakistan-Afghanistan theater and the China rivalry. Watch the Treaty status (permanent rupture would be a major escalation), any repeat militant attack, and LoC/LAC flare-ups. Trend estimate — newly entered tracking.","tier":"ELEVATED","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"Two nuclear powers each own a truth about Kashmir and deny the other's entirely.","claims":[{"claim":"Kashmir is an integral, settled part of India ('atoot ang'); Article 370's abrogation normalized it; militancy is purely Pakistan-sponsored terrorism.","who":"New Delhi — the framing that forecloses negotiation and internationalization.","evid":"Militancy has genuine cross-border sponsorship (documented) AND indigenous alienation (documented — communications blackouts, detentions, demographic anxieties post-2019). Both realities coexist; each capital's narrative admits only one.","status":"CONTESTED — sponsored terror vs indigenous grievance, both real"},{"claim":"India stages false-flag attacks to justify strikes on Pakistan.","who":"Islamabad — deployed after every major attack (Pulwama, Pahalgam) to deflect sponsorship evidence.","evid":"No credible evidence supports the false-flag claims; investigations have traced major attacks to Pakistan-based groups. But the claim persists because prior Indian information operations (EU DisinfoLab findings) damaged New Delhi's credibility as a narrator.","status":"DEBUNKED claim sustained by the accuser's own credibility deficit"}]},"history":{"roots":"Kashmir is the wound of Partition that two nuclear arsenals now guard. In 1947 the Hindu maharaja of a Muslim-majority princely state acceded to India under invasion; Pakistan never accepted it, and the two states have fought three wars and a permanent proxy conflict over the line since. India's 2019 revocation of Article 370 (Kashmir's autonomy) hardened the map; Pakistan's sponsorship of militant groups sustains the fire; and the 2025 Pahalgam attack triggered the worst crisis in decades — strikes, a shot-down-jets air battle, and India suspending the Indus Waters Treaty, weaponizing water for the first time. Two doctrines now grind: India's 'new normal' of retaliation, Pakistan's nuclear shadow.","timeline":[{"date":"1947","e":"Partition; the maharaja accedes under invasion — the dispute is born."},{"date":"1947/1965/1999","e":"Three wars over the line."},{"date":"1989","e":"Insurgency era begins — indigenous + Pakistan-sponsored."},{"date":"2019","e":"Pulwama attack → Balakot strikes; Article 370 revoked."},{"date":"Apr 2025","e":"Pahalgam massacre → Operation Sindoor: strikes, air battle, IWT suspended."},{"date":"2026","e":"Gulf-brokered calm holds uneasily; water leverage remains on the table."}],"actors":[{"name":"India (Modi)","bio":"The status-quo-plus power — integration policy, retaliation doctrine, and now water leverage via the suspended treaty."},{"name":"Pakistan (army/ISI)","bio":"The revisionist sponsor — militant proxies as strategy, the nuclear umbrella as insurance, now axis-embedded."},{"name":"Kashmiris","bio":"The population both narratives claim and neither consults — alienation is the insurgency's renewable fuel."},{"name":"China","bio":"The third claimant (Aksai Chin) and Pakistan's patron — the trilateral dimension every crisis carries."}],"bridge":"The Indus suspension is the conflict's rubicon: Bose's structural read — that Kashmir is a sovereignty dispute layered on a democracy deficit — now carries a hydrological weapon on top, and Noorani's constitutional archive shows why 2019's revocation forecloses the federal solutions every earlier framework assumed. The Gulf-brokered calm holds because both arsenals make the alternative unusable, not because anything is settled; the platform reads every Pahalgam-type incident as a test of whether the 'new normal' retaliation doctrine and the nuclear shadow can keep coexisting. · AND NOW, 2026: Gulf-brokered calm holds uneasily; water leverage remains on the table.","sources":[{"work":"Kashmir: Roots of Conflict, Paths to Peace","author":"Sumantra Bose","year":"2003","note":"The standard structural account — sovereignty dispute plus democracy deficit."},{"work":"Article 370: A Constitutional History of Jammu and Kashmir","author":"A.G. Noorani","year":"2011","note":"The Indian constitutional scholar's documentary record of the autonomy the 2019 revocation ended."},{"work":"Indus Waters Treaty","author":"World Bank-brokered treaty text","year":"1960","note":"The primary text of the water architecture now suspended."}]},"patterns":["water-leverage"],"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"Sovereignty dispute layered on democracy deficit; water newly weaponized.","alt":"A purely externally-sponsored-terrorism reading (Delhi's) or purely indigenous-uprising reading (Islamabad's) — each capital's single-cause story.","why":"Evidence supports both components at once (documented sponsorship AND documented alienation); the both/and is high-confidence precisely because each single-cause reading fails the record."},"refs":[{"t":"ACLED · Asia-Pacific Overview July 2026","u":"https://acleddata.com/update/asia-pacific-overview-july-2026"},{"t":"Indus Waters Treaty (World Bank)","u":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/programs/indus-waters-treaty"},{"t":"Crisis Group: Kashmir","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/asia/south-asia/kashmir"},{"t":"ICG · South Asia","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/asia/south-asia"},{"t":"UN Security Council · India-Pakistan question","u":"https://www.un.org/securitycouncil/"}],"human":{"dead":70000,"disp":100000,"famine":false,"aid":"restricted access","note":"~70k killed since the 1989 insurgency began (estimates vary widely by source and methodology); Kashmiri Pandit displacement plus recurrent cross-LoC evacuations. The 2025 Pahalgam attack and Operation Sindoor added civilian and military deaths on both sides.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"66 = active armed conflict, a recorded human toll, broadly static over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"LeT/JeM/TRF and indigenous militancy added with both-and competing read"}],"spill":[{"n":"Pakistan","dir":"both","w":"The deadliest unrest this cycle was in PAKISTANI-administered Kashmir over provincial elections — the autonomy grievance now fought on both sides of the Line of Control.","id":"pak-afg"},{"n":"India","dir":"both","w":"The reserved-seat dispute is over refugees from Indian-administered Kashmir; LeT/JeM/TRF operations are the escalation trigger between two nuclear states.","id":"india"},{"n":"China","dir":"in","w":"The Aksai Chin and CPEC dimensions; Beijing's stake in the wider boundary question.","id":"china"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Cross-LoC militancy, dual-side unrest","dir":"volatile","phase":"Escalation risk between nuclear states","up":["An LeT/JeM/TRF attack triggering interstate escalation","The Pakistani-side election unrest spreading","The autonomy grievance on both sides hardening","A security-dilemma spiral during a crisis"],"down":["Deterrence and back-channel crisis management holding","Addressing the autonomy demand both administrations face","De-escalation mechanisms after past crises","Third-party crisis mediation"],"fw":["security-dilemma","deterrence","ladder"]},"inherit":{"name":"The Princely State Loophole","est":"1947","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Partition let the rulers of some 565 princely states choose India or Pakistan, <b>without reference to their populations</b>. Kashmir had a Hindu maharaja and a Muslim majority; his accession to India under invasion pressure in October 1947 handed a demographic question to a dynastic decision. Mountbatten's boundary award and the compressed ten-week timetable produced the Line of Control that three wars have failed to move.","now":"<b>The unresolved plebiscite promised in 1948 has never been held.</b> India revoked Article 370 in 2019 and split the territory into two Union Territories; <b>Ladakh has been governed without a legislature ever since</b>, which is the grievance a six-year cross-communal movement is now pressing. And in 2026 the deadliest unrest was on the <b>Pakistani-administered side</b>, over provincial elections and reserved seats. <b>Both administrations now face the same demand from the people they govern.</b>","change":"Nothing structural. The dispute is managed rather than addressed, between two nuclear-armed states with LeT, JeM and TRF operations as the standing escalation trigger."},"monitors":[{"t":"ACLED · conflict event data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}],"macrona":"DELIBERATELY EMPTY. A disputed territory administered in parts by two states has no national accounts of its own. The relevant macro is on the India and Pakistan-Afghanistan files."},{"id":"bangladesh","cat":"political","region":"South Asia","subregion":"South Asia","tclass":"watch","name":"Bangladesh — Post-Hasina Realignment","sub":"Feb 2026 elections · Hasina death sentence · India-out, Pakistan-in tilt","domain":"Political Transition / Regional Realignment","sev":"medium","status":"volatile","since":"2024 (Hasina ouster)","esc":50,"lat":23.8,"lon":90.4,"trend":"up","esc_history":[49,50,50,50,50,50,50,50,50,50],"hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk14 (Jul 2026)","cas":"2024 uprising killed ~1,000; anti-minority (Hindu) violence spikes; political-transition unrest","disp":"Rohingya camps (~1M) persist; minority displacement pressure","flag":"🇧🇩","note":"2026 — THE SPILLOVER IS IDEOLOGICAL, NOT TERRITORIAL: analysis frames the Pakistan-Afghanistan war as posing a LATENT RISK OF IDEOLOGICAL CONTAGION AMONG MILITANT NETWORKS rather than direct spillover, and as hampering Dhaka's plan to revive SAARC for regional integration · Bangladesh's 'Friend yes, Master no' foreign-policy line is being tested by having to balance among competing South Asian strategic narratives · the Rohingya camp situation — ARSA/RSO militarisation, funding cuts — remains the acute exposure the SAARC-revival ambition sits above · JUL 2026 — CIVIC SPACE UNDER THE INTERIM: defamation charges filed against six journalists (IFJ Asia-Pacific bulletin) — the transition that was made by mass protest is now prosecuting press criticism, the clearest test yet of whether 2024 broke the pattern or renewed it · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · South Asia) · Yunus interim govt after 2024 ouster of India-ally Sheikh Hasina · Feb 2026 nationwide elections (Awami League gone; antagonistic-to-India parties lead) · Hasina sentenced to DEATH in absentia (Int'l Crimes Tribunal, crimes against humanity); India refuses extradition — a running India-Bangladesh rupture · Dhaka rapprochement with PAKISTAN (Jaishankar handshake at Khaleda Zia funeral = rare thaw signal) · India intensifying deportation of undocumented Bangladeshis; border 'push-in' friction (BGB vs BSF) · far-right Islamist groups emboldened; anti-Hindu violence → protests in India · ~1M Rohingya still in camps","leverage":[{"actor":"Bangladesh interim/elected govt","asset":"Pivot away from India toward Pakistan/China; Hasina extradition demand; border-migration leverage","str":"md"},{"actor":"India","asset":"Hosts Hasina (extradition refusal); upper hand on trade/border/water; deportation pressure","str":"md"},{"actor":"Pakistan","asset":"Rapprochement with Dhaka = strategic gain vs India on its eastern flank","str":"md"},{"actor":"China","asset":"Investment + BRI courtship of the post-Hasina government","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Bangladesh Int'l Crimes Tribunal","detail":"Sentenced ex-PM Sheikh Hasina to death in absentia for crimes against humanity (2024 crackdown)"},{"src":"Crisis Group","detail":"Suspicion lingers between Dhaka and New Delhi post-Hasina; Feb 2026 elections a chance to reset or deepen the rupture"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇵🇰 Pakistan","side":"interim govt","flow":"Diplomatic rapprochement (rare public thaw); alignment vs shared India antagonism","tier":"documented","for":"Strategic depth on India's eastern flank"},{"actor":"🇮🇳 India","side":"anti-interim / pro-Hasina","flow":"Shelters Hasina; refuses extradition; deportation crackdown on Bangladeshis; border push-ins","tier":"documented","for":"Restore a friendly Dhaka; secure the eastern border"},{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"interim govt","flow":"Investment + BRI courtship of the new government","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Fill the vacuum left by India's lost influence"}],"mil":"Not a kinetic theater — a political-transition and realignment watch. The violence dimension is domestic and communal: the 2024 uprising that ousted Sheikh Hasina killed roughly 1,000, and anti-minority (Hindu) violence has spiked, spilling into protests and diplomatic friction with India. Border 'push-in' incidents between Bangladesh's BGB and India's BSF are the main interstate friction.","pol":"Bangladesh's realignment is the strategic story. The ouster of India-ally Hasina, her in-absentia death sentence, and India's refusal to extradite her have ruptured a once-close relationship — while Dhaka's interim government (and the parties leading the February 2026 elections) tilt toward Pakistan and China. A Jaishankar-Pakistan handshake at a Dhaka funeral was a rare thaw signal in an otherwise antagonistic regional picture.","eco":"India retains economic leverage (trade, border, shared rivers) and is using deportation of undocumented Bangladeshis as pressure, while China courts the new government with investment. The ~1M Rohingya in camps remain a standing humanitarian and security burden.","assess":"Bangladesh enters at 50 (GUARDED, strategic-watch), trend up — a realignment theater that matters for how it reshapes South Asian alignments. The post-Hasina tilt away from India toward Pakistan and China alters the regional balance and gives India a second front of concern alongside Kashmir. It ties to the Kashmir theater and the displacement system (Rohingya). Watch the February 2026 election outcome, the Hasina extradition standoff, and India-Bangladesh border friction. Trend estimate — newly entered tracking.","tier":"GUARDED","domain_tag":"Political","econlev":[{"holder":"India","channel":"Trade + border + water","exp":"MED","detail":"Retains trade/border/river leverage; using deportation of undocumented Bangladeshis as pressure on the post-Hasina government"}],"contested":{"summary":"The Rohingya crisis carries an unresolved legal determination and an unacknowledged security reality inside the camps.","claims":[{"claim":"The 2017 clearance operations did not constitute genocide.","who":"Myanmar's military authorities.","evid":"The UN Independent International Fact-Finding Mission concluded the operations were carried out with genocidal intent; The Gambia's case against Myanmar proceeds at the ICJ, which has ordered provisional measures. Over 700,000 fled in weeks.","status":"BEFORE-THE-COURT"},{"claim":"The Cox's Bazar camps are civilian humanitarian spaces.","who":"Implicit in donor and host-government framing.","evid":"ARSA and RSO operate within and around the camps, with documented assassinations of community leaders, factional fighting and reports of forced recruitment. Acknowledging militarisation would carry legal and funding consequences for all parties, so it is largely absent from official framing.","status":"UNDER-ACKNOWLEDGED"}]},"history":{"roots":"Bangladesh was born twice — in the 1971 liberation war's bloodbath, and again in the 2024 student revolution that ended Sheikh Hasina's 15-year rule. The world's most densely populated large country built an export-garment economy that lifted millions while its politics stayed a two-dynasty blood feud (Hasina's Awami League vs the BNP). Hasina's escalating autocracy fell to five weeks of student protests (~1,400 killed); Nobel laureate Muhammad Yunus took over an interim government facing an impossible brief: try the old regime, calm the streets, hold elections, manage a million Rohingya refugees, and rebalance between India (Hasina's shelter) and a courting China and Pakistan.","timeline":[{"date":"1971","e":"Liberation war from Pakistan — up to 3M dead; the founding trauma."},{"date":"1975","e":"Mujib assassinated — the dynastic feud begins."},{"date":"2009-24","e":"Hasina's 15-year rule: growth, then autocracy."},{"date":"2017","e":"~700k Rohingya flee Myanmar into Cox's Bazar."},{"date":"Aug 2024","e":"Student revolution ousts Hasina (~1,400 killed); Yunus interim government."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Hasina tried and convicted in absentia; election timeline contested; India-Dhaka friction over her extradition."}],"actors":[{"name":"Muhammad Yunus","bio":"The Nobel-laureate interim leader — legitimacy without a party, running a transition everyone contests."},{"name":"Sheikh Hasina (exiled)","bio":"The ousted ruler in India — convicted in absentia; her extradition is the India-Bangladesh fault line."},{"name":"The student movement / new parties","bio":"The revolution's owners — impatient with the transition they created."},{"name":"India vs China/Pakistan","bio":"Delhi lost its client; Beijing and Islamabad court the successor — the strategic realignment beneath the transition."}],"bridge":"The 2024 revolution reopened 1971's unfinished accounts all at once: Raghavan's point that Bangladesh's birth was an international event — decided as much in Washington, Moscow and Delhi as in Dhaka — reruns in the transition, with India sheltering Hasina, China and Pakistan courting Yunus, and the trials adjudicating the old regime under the world's gaze. The student movement's impatience is the deeper continuity: both 1971 and 2024 were made by the young against orders their elders had normalized. Whether the interim delivers elections or a new managed order decides which inheritance wins.","sources":[{"work":"1971: A Global History of the Creation of Bangladesh","author":"Srinath Raghavan","year":"2013","note":"The international making of the liberation war."},{"work":"The Blood Telegram: Nixon, Kissinger, and a Forgotten Genocide","author":"Gary J. Bass","year":"2013","note":"The primary-document account of great-power complicity in 1971."},{"work":"UN OHCHR fact-finding report on the 2024 protests","author":"UN OHCHR","year":"2025","note":"The primary casualty record (~1,400) of the revolution this theater's present begins from."}]},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"Born twice — 1971 and 2024 — the revolution reopening the old international accounts.","alt":"A managed-transition-fails reading holds the interim likely to reproduce a new dynastic order rather than deliver the democratic break the framing implies.","why":"The trials and India-friction are real; whether 2024 breaks or repeats the two-dynasty pattern is unresolved. MOD."},"refs":[{"t":"The Daily Star · what the Pakistan-Afghanistan conflict means for Bangladesh","u":"https://www.thedailystar.net/slow-reads/geopolitical-insights/news/what-the-pakistan-afghanistan-conflict-means-bangladesh-4126351"},{"t":"IFJ Asia-Pacific Regional Bulletin, Jul 2026","u":"https://www.ifj.org/media-centre/news/detail/category/newsletter/article/asia-pacific-regional-bulletin-july-2026"},{"t":"UNHCR · Rohingya emergency","u":"https://www.unhcr.org/rohingya-emergency.html"},{"t":"ICG · Bangladesh/Myanmar","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/asia/south-asia/bangladesh"},{"t":"IMF · country data and Article IV reports","u":"https://www.imf.org/en/Countries"}],"human":{"dead":1400,"disp":1000000,"famine":false,"aid":"Rohingya-underfunded","note":"The 2024 revolution killed ~1,400 (UN OHCHR). Separately Bangladesh hosts ~1M Rohingya refugees in Cox's Bazar — the displacement burden of Myanmar's genocide, funded at a fraction of need.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"50 = institutional or electoral instability, a recorded human toll, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"ARSA/RSO added; camp militarisation contested entry"}],"spill":[{"n":"Myanmar","id":"myanmar","dir":"in","w":"The Rohingya displacement from Rakhine — and the Arakan Army now holds the state the refugees fled, which makes return a negotiation with an insurgent administration."},{"n":"Pakistan / Afghanistan","id":"pak-afg","dir":"in","w":"Analysts frame the war there as a LATENT RISK OF IDEOLOGICAL CONTAGION among militant networks rather than direct spillover — and it has hampered Dhaka's SAARC revival plan."},{"n":"India","dir":"both","w":"The border, trade dependence, and the 'Friend yes, Master no' balancing line being tested.","id":"india"}],"inherit":{"name":"Partitioned Twice","est":"1947 and 1971","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Bengal was divided in 1947 on religious lines, and East Bengal became East Pakistan — <b>a province separated from its own capital by 1,600 kilometres of India</b>, sharing religion but not language, culture or economy. West Pakistan took the larger share of revenue and administration, imposed Urdu as the state language over a Bengali-speaking majority, and in 1971 responded to an election it lost with a military campaign that killed hundreds of thousands and drove ten million into India. <b>Bangladesh is the only state created by partition and then by war against the state partition created.</b>","now":"<b>The 'Friend yes, Master no' foreign policy line is the direct product of that history</b> — a state that has twice been governed from elsewhere balancing between India, China and the Gulf without alignment. The Rohingya camps at Cox's Bazar hold roughly a million people from a partition Britain made in Burma, hosted by a country made by two of its own.","change":"Dhaka's SAARC revival plan has been hampered by the Pakistan-Afghanistan war and by Indian weight. <b>The Rohingya return question is now a negotiation with the Arakan Army rather than with any state.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"World Bank · country data and Global Economic Prospects","u":"https://data.worldbank.org/"}],"macrona":"NOT YET SOURCED. The material facts are the Rohingya camp economy and the SAARC revival ambition, both described on the card."},{"id":"lakurawa","cat":"military","region":"West Africa","subregion":"Sahel Core","tclass":"conflict","name":"Lakurawa — NW Nigeria Tri-Border Insurgency","sub":"Nigeria-Niger-Benin tri-border · US airstrikes Dec 2025 · 162 killed in Kwara Feb 2026","domain":"Hybrid Jihadist-Criminal Insurgency","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"2018 (terror label 2024)","esc":74,"lat":13.1,"lon":5.2,"trend":"up","esc_history":[68,70,71,72,73,74,74,74,74,74],"hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk14 (Jul 2026)","cas":"250+ killed 2024-25 (ACLED/Amnesty); 162 killed in Woro & Nuku, Kwara State (Feb 4 2026)","disp":"Communities displaced across ~500 villages in Sokoto/Kebbi; conversion of settlements to camps","flag":"🇳🇬","note":"JUL 2026 — (checked) no discrete new incident surfaced this cycle beyond the northwest banditry sweep already logged on the Nigeria file; Lakurawa remains embedded in the Sokoto-Kebbi frontier picture, with the weakened Multinational Joint Task Force reducing coordinated pressure on the border zone it exploits · JUL 2026 — checked against the Sahel and Nigeria sweeps: the tri-border zone remains the active frontier (JNIM franchising south, Niamey-Abuja security cooperation still severed post-coup). No discrete Lakurawa-specific development surfaced this cycle — logged as verified-unchanged rather than assumed quiet · NEW THEATER (Phase 3 · Sahel) · hybrid jihadist-criminal group in the Nigeria-Niger-Benin tri-border (Sokoto, Kebbi + expanding to Niger State's Borgu axis + Kwara) · origin: emerged ~2017-18 from a merger of Malian Macina/JNIM-linked Fulani fighters + a Nigerien herder self-defense faction; invited by communities to fight bandits, then turned predatory · declared a terrorist group by Nigeria (Nov 2024/Jan 2025) · US AIRSTRIKES Dec 25 2025 (Trump-announced, Tinubu-approved) on Sokoto/Bauni Forest camps — 'ISIS terrorist scum' · Feb 4 2026: massacred 162 civilians in Woro & Nuku (Kwara) after villagers refused to let them preach · governance model = extortion/'zakat' taxation, livestock confiscation, forced religious code, suppression of rival vigilantes · leader Amir Tajudeen moves between Nigeria/Niger/Mali/Chad · ISS: cooperates with Boko Haram/JAS + ISSP/ISWAP as a 'strategic conduit'; Niger post-2023-coup tension blocks joint ops","leverage":[{"actor":"Lakurawa","asset":"Tri-border sanctuary (ungoverned Nigeria-Niger-Benin space); embedded governance over ~500 villages; Sahel reinforcement pipeline","str":"md"},{"actor":"Nigeria (Op Fansan Yamma)","asset":"Air force + JTF North West offensives; US airstrike backing; but overstretched vs Boko Haram/ISWAP/bandits","str":"md"},{"actor":"United States","asset":"AFRICOM airstrikes (Dec 2025) — signaling willingness to hit NW Nigeria directly; intel/ISR","str":"md"},{"actor":"ISSP / ISWAP / JNIM","asset":"Lakurawa as a conduit linking Sahel IS/AQ franchises toward the Nigerian littoral","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"Nigerian military (Op Fansan Yamma)","detail":"Declared Lakurawa terrorist (Jan 2025); Dec 2025 Borgu camp strike killed 46 fighters; ongoing Sokoto/Kebbi/Niger air + ground ops"},{"src":"US AFRICOM / White House","detail":"Dec 25 2025 airstrikes on Sokoto camps (Trump-announced, Tinubu-approved); Feb 2026 US military team deployment confirmed"},{"src":"ACLED / Amnesty International","detail":"Attributed 250+ deaths to Lakurawa across 2024-25; documented mass-casualty village raids"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"Nigeria (counter-terror)","flow":"AFRICOM airstrikes on Lakurawa camps (Dec 2025); military advisory team deployment (Feb 2026)","tier":"documented","for":"Prevent IS-Sahel expansion toward strategic infrastructure + the littoral"},{"actor":"🏴 ISSP / ISWAP","side":"Lakurawa (aligned)","flow":"Ideological alignment + reinforcement pipeline from the central Sahel; operating 'under the Lakurawa label'","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Extend Islamic State's reach from the Sahel toward coastal West Africa"},{"actor":"🏴 JNIM / Boko Haram (JAS)","side":"Lakurawa (cooperative)","flow":"Origin ties (Macina/JNIM) + operational cooperation with Boko Haram's JAS faction; fighter exchange from 2023","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Cross-network logistics, sanctuary, and recruitment"}],"mil":"Lakurawa is a hybrid jihadist-criminal insurgency in the Nigeria-Niger-Benin tri-border, distinct from the Boko Haram/ISWAP conflict in the northeast. It emerged around 2017-18 from a merger of Malian Macina/JNIM-linked Fulani fighters and a Nigerien herder self-defense faction, was invited by Sokoto communities to fight bandits, then turned predatory. After Nigeria declared it a terrorist group, the violence escalated sharply: the US conducted airstrikes on its Sokoto camps on Christmas Day 2025, and in February 2026 the group massacred 162 civilians in the Kwara villages of Woro and Nuku after residents refused to let it preach. It has expanded south into Niger State's Borgu axis, where its operational leadership is now reportedly based.","pol":"The group governs where the state is absent — imposing 'zakat' taxation, confiscating livestock, enforcing a rigid religious code, and suppressing rival vigilante groups across roughly 500 villages. This coercive governance-through-extraction is the core of its model. Nigerian counter-operations (Operation Fansan Yamma) intensified from late 2025, but tension with Niger since its 2023 coup blocks the cross-border cooperation needed to defeat a group whose leader, Amir Tajudeen, moves freely between Nigeria, Niger, Mali, and Chad.","eco":"Lakurawa funds itself through the extraction it frames as religious obligation — taxation, extortion, livestock and produce confiscation, and high-value kidnapping (the Kebbi deputy speaker was ransomed for ~$140,600, partly in CFA francs, showing its cross-border reach). This crime-terror nexus makes it financially self-sustaining and less dependent on external patrons than the Sahel's larger franchises.","assess":"Lakurawa enters at 74 (ELEVATED, conflict-class), trend up — a distinct and rising Sahel threat the platform needed to break out from the broader Nigeria picture. Its significance is structural: it functions as a conduit linking the central Sahel's IS and al-Qaeda franchises (ISSP, JNIM) toward the Nigerian littoral and the Gulf of Guinea, threatening to connect the Sahel and Lake Chad theaters. The Christmas 2025 US airstrikes mark a notable escalation of direct American involvement in northwest Nigeria. It ties to the Nigeria, Niger, and Benin-Togo theaters and the Sahel security-vacuum dynamic. Watch the Borgu/Kwara southward expansion, the pace of Sahel reinforcement, and Nigeria-Niger cooperation. Trend estimate — newly entered tracking.","tier":"SEVERE","domain_tag":"Military","econlev":[{"holder":"Lakurawa","channel":"'Zakat' taxation + livestock","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Governance-through-extraction across ~500 villages: coercive zakat, livestock confiscation, produce seizure; high-value kidnapping (Kebbi deputy speaker ~$140,600, partly in CFA francs) — self-financing, not patron-dependent"}],"contested":{"summary":"Whether Lakurawa is a foreign implant or a local formation determines who is responsible for it — and both governments prefer the answer that points across the border.","claims":[{"claim":"Lakurawa is a foreign group implanted from Niger and Mali.","who":"Nigerian federal authorities.","evid":"The group's emergence in Sokoto and Kebbi followed the collapse of Nigeria-Niger security cooperation after the 2023 coup, and its methods resemble Sahelian katibas. But recruitment is substantially LOCAL, drawing on Fulani pastoralist grievance inside Nigeria — researchers note the community was reportedly invited in by local actors seeking protection from banditry. Framing it as foreign relieves Abuja of responsibility for the governance vacuum that made it welcome.","status":"CONVENIENT-FOR-BOTH-STATES"}]},"history":{"roots":"Lakurawa is new; the conditions that made it are not. Nigeria's northwest was the heart of the Sokoto Caliphate — Usman dan Fodio's 1804 jihad built one of precolonial Africa's largest states, a literate, administered polity whose emirate structures Britain then ruled through (indirect rule's original template, as Mamdani shows, hardening 'native authority' into decentralized despotism). Colonial and post-colonial neglect left the rural northwest with the country's deepest poverty and thinnest state presence; the bandit economy of the 2010s — mass kidnapping, cattle rustling, mining rackets — grew in that vacuum, and Nigerian scholar Murtala Ahmed Rufa'i's fieldwork documents how communities, abandoned by the state, first hired the armed men who then enslaved them. Lakurawa is that sequence's jihadist completion: Sahelian fighters invited as protectors, converting protection into governance — taxes, courts, levies — in the very country where dan Fodio's model of religious state-building is two centuries of living memory.","timeline":[{"date":"2010s","e":"Sahelian fighters drift into the Nigeria-Niger borderlands."},{"date":"~2018","e":"Local communities in Sokoto invite armed 'protectors' against bandits."},{"date":"2023","e":"Niger coup severs Nigeria-Niger security cooperation — the opening."},{"date":"Nov 2024","e":"'Lakurawa' emerges publicly; Nigerian military acknowledges the new front."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Taxation, courts, and settlement pressure across ~500 villages in the NW tri-border; raids escalate."}],"actors":[{"name":"Lakurawa","bio":"The Sahel-origin jihadist implant in Nigeria's northwest — protection racket turned proto-governance, taxing and judging where the state doesn't reach."},{"name":"Nigerian military","bio":"Fighting a fourth front (after ISWAP, Boko Haram, bandits) with the same overstretched forces."},{"name":"The bandit economy","bio":"The northwest's mass-kidnapping industry — the labor pool and business model jihadism is absorbing."},{"name":"Niger (post-coup)","bio":"The severed partner — the border that used to be jointly policed is now the group's sanctuary line."}],"bridge":"The group's proto-governance works because it occupies a legitimacy vacuum with a familiar script: where the Nigerian state offers neither security nor justice, a tax-and-court order invoking the Sokoto inheritance finds takers — the same mechanism as al-Shabaab's courts and JNIM's zakat. The Niger coup's severing of Abuja-Niamey cooperation opened the border sanctuary; the bandit economy supplies the manpower market. Lakurawa is small, but it is the proof-of-concept that the Sahel model can be franchised into Nigeria's northwest — which is why this platform tracks it as its own theater. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: a group both governments call foreign recruits locally on both sides of a border that colonial cartography drew through one pastoral society. Whichever state disowns it, the Fulani grievance it feeds on is domestic to both.","sources":[{"work":"'I Am a Bandit': A Decade of Research in Zamfara State's Bandit Enclaves","author":"Murtala Ahmed Rufa'i (Usmanu Danfodiyo University, Sokoto)","year":"2021","note":"Nigerian fieldwork inside the bandit economy — the protection-to-predation sequence Lakurawa completes."},{"work":"Citizen and Subject: Contemporary Africa and the Legacy of Late Colonialism","author":"Mahmood Mamdani","year":"1996","note":"Indirect rule through the emirates — how 'native authority' became the decentralized despotism whose vacuum armed groups now fill."},{"work":"The Sokoto Caliphate","author":"Murray Last","year":"1967","note":"The dan Fodio state — the indigenous governance inheritance the region's jihadists invoke and invert."},{"work":"Nigeria conflict event data","author":"ACLED / Nigeria Security Tracker","year":"2024-26","note":"Primary tracking of Lakurawa's emergence and the NW tri-border violence."}]},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"The Sahel model franchised into Nigeria's northwest: jihadism grafted onto the bandit economy in the state's absence.","alt":"A pure-criminality reading holds Lakurawa as rebranded banditry with a religious veneer, not a genuine ideological implant — the group may be a protection racket first, jihadist second.","why":"The proto-governance (taxes, courts) exceeds simple banditry, but the ideological depth is unproven this early. MOD, flagged as evolving."},"refs":[{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"},{"t":"CFR · Global Conflict Tracker","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker"}],"human":{"dead":2000,"disp":150000,"famine":false,"aid":"thin state presence","note":"Northwest Nigeria tri-border violence: mass-kidnapping economy plus the newer Sahel-linked implant. Figures are indicative — the zone is poorly covered and casualties are widely under-counted.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"74 = active armed conflict, a recorded human toll, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Provenance, check-mode and escalation-derivation fields added; no substantive content change this cycle."}],"spill":[{"n":"Niger / Mali","dir":"in","w":"The group both governments call foreign; its methods resemble Sahelian katibas and the frontier is the asset."},{"n":"Nigeria","dir":"both","w":"Recruits locally on the Sokoto-Kebbi side; the collapse of Nigeria-Niger security cooperation after the 2023 coup created the vacuum it fills.","id":"nigeria"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Frontier implant, local recruitment","dir":"climbing","phase":"Emergence → escalation","up":["The Nigeria-Niger security-cooperation collapse persists","Sahelian methods spread across the border","Fulani grievance feeds recruitment","MJTF weakness reduces pressure"],"down":["Restored Nigeria-Niger cooperation","Addressing the pastoralist grievance directly","Local protection alternatives to the group","Border-zone governance"],"fw":["ethnic-security","resource-conflict"]},"macrona":"DELIBERATELY EMPTY. This is a frontier zone across the Nigeria-Niger border, not a state. Its economics are the pastoralist land and cattle economy the group taxes; the national figures that matter are on the Nigeria and Niger files.","monitors":[{"t":"ACLED / Nigeria Security Tracker","u":"https://acleddata.com/"},{"t":"CDD West Africa","u":"https://www.cddwestafrica.org/"},{"t":"ISS Africa · West Africa monitoring","u":"https://issafrica.org/"},{"t":"ACLED · Nigeria data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}]},{"id":"cameroon","cat":"military","region":"Central Africa","subregion":"Central Africa","tclass":"conflict","name":"Cameroon — Twin Insurgencies + Succession Vacuum","sub":"Far North: BH/ISWAP spillover · Anglophone war · Biya (92) succession crisis","domain":"Multi-Front Insurgency / Regime Fragility","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"2014 (BH) / 2017 (Anglophone)","esc":69,"lat":10.6,"lon":14.3,"trend":"up","esc_history":[64,65,66,67,68,68,69,69,69,69],"hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk15 (Jul 2026)","cas":"Q1 2026 Far North alone: 104 killed, 123 wounded, 128 abducted (OCHA); Mar 2025 Wulgo base attack killed 20 soldiers; Anglophone war ~6,000+ dead since 2017","disp":"500,000+ IDPs in Anglophone regions; 400,000+ refugees hosted (CAR/Nigeria/Chad)","flag":"🇨🇲","note":"JUL 2026 — (checked) the Anglophone war and the succession question remain the two variables; no discrete new development surfaced this cycle beyond the Lake Chad basin dynamics logged on the Nigeria and Chad files, where Cameroonian forces operate against Boko Haram and ISWAP · JUL 19 2026 — Armed men kidnapped seven passengers on the BAFOUSSAM-BAMENDA road — the arterial route between the Francophone west and the Anglophone northwest, where mass abduction has become a standing tax on movement rather than an exceptional event · JUL 2026 — BOTH WARS GRIND, ONE ACCOUNTABILITY CRACK: overnight Jul 6-7 more than 100 Boko Haram fighters attacked an Operation Alpha post held by the Rapid Intervention Battalion at Vreket (Koza, Mayo-Tsanaga) — the Far North remains a core Lake Chad Basin conflict zone where forces report tactical wins without eliminating the groups · ANGLOPHONE WAR NEARS ITS TENTH YEAR: the Safeguarding Health in Conflict Coalition (Jul 10) recorded 22 attacks on healthcare in the NW/SW in 2025 — down from 30 (2024) and 25 (2023), but medics still face kidnapping, assault, arrest and extortion · ACCOUNTABILITY: a Yaoundé court-martial convicted three soldiers (Feb 19) over the 2020 NGARBUH MASSACRE of 21 civilians, sentencing them to up to 10 years — rare, partial, and six years late · NEW THEATER (Phase 4 · closes the Lake Chad gap) · TWIN INSURGENCIES + a succession vacuum · (1) FAR NORTH: Boko Haram/JAS + ISWAP spillover from Nigeria/Chad — Q1 2026: 104 killed, 123 wounded, 128 abducted (OCHA); raids in Mayo-Tsanaga, Mayo-Sava, Logone-et-Chari; 9 IED incidents, 2 schools + 4 health centers attacked · Mar 2025 Wulgo base attack: 20 soldiers killed, BH SEIZED ZSU-23-4 Shilka radar-guided AA guns + now uses explosive-laden DRONES · State Dept: ISWAP is the LARGEST IS affiliate in Africa · (2) ANGLOPHONE WAR (2017-): Ambazonia separatists vs govt in NW/SW; abductions, illegal taxation, roadblocks; Dec 2025-Jan 2026 attacks killed 23 (mostly women/children) in Wowo + Gidado · (3) SUCCESSION VACUUM: Biya, 92 — world's oldest head of state, in power since 1982 — won a disputed 8th term (Oct 2025); prolonged Switzerland medical absences; NO government appointed 8 months post-election; the new VP post (auto-successor) left UNFILLED; a man was arrested for trying to air a FAKE decree appointing a govt+VP · MNJTF (Nigeria-led) eroding; Niger's 2023 coup broke regional CT cooperation","leverage":[{"actor":"Boko Haram/JAS + ISWAP","asset":"Lake Chad island sanctuaries; captured Cameroonian heavy weapons (Shilka AA guns) + armed drones; cross-border mobility","str":"md"},{"actor":"Biya regime","asset":"Total executive control (appoints PM, judges, generals, governors) — but a 92-yr-old with no successor named; France/military backing","str":"md"},{"actor":"Ambazonia separatists","asset":"NW/SW territorial contest; diaspora funding; 'ghost town' shutdowns; parallel taxation","str":"md"},{"actor":"France","asset":"Foreign Legion units regionally; French firms run the offshore oil (off the Anglophone coast) — the extraction irony","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"UN OCHA (Q1 2026)","detail":"Far North: 104 killed, 123 wounded, 128 abducted; 9 IED incidents; attacks on 2 schools and 4 health centres"},{"src":"US State Dept (Country Reports on Terrorism)","detail":"Identifies ISWAP as the LARGEST Islamic State affiliate in Africa; both BH and ISWAP are designated FTOs operating in Cameroon's Far North"},{"src":"Crisis Group","detail":"Biya's succession vacuum: no govt appointed 8 months after the election; VP post (automatic successor) left unfilled; Constitutional Council rejected opposition petitions"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"France / foreign majors","channel":"Offshore oil + mining","exp":"MED","detail":"Cameroon's oil lies off the ANGLOPHONE coast but French companies run the rigs — a core grievance fueling the separatist war; Douala/Kribi ports are Chad + CAR's Atlantic lifeline"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇫🇷 France","side":"Biya regime","flow":"Longstanding military/economic backing; Foreign Legion units regionally; French firms operate the offshore oil","tier":"documented","for":"Françafrique influence; energy + port access; regional stability"},{"actor":"🏴 ISWAP / Boko Haram","side":"insurgents","flow":"Cross-border operations from Nigeria/Chad/Niger; captured Cameroonian heavy weapons; drone attacks","tier":"documented","for":"Lake Chad caliphate project; taxation base expansion"},{"actor":"🇺🇸 US","side":"limited","flow":"Cut military aid + removed trade benefits over human-rights violations; $120M of $123M FY23 aid was health-only","tier":"documented","for":"Distancing from Biya's rights record while retaining CT interest"}],"mil":"Cameroon fights two wars at once. In the Far North, Boko Haram and ISWAP spill over from Nigeria and Chad — OCHA recorded 104 killed, 123 wounded, and 128 abducted in the first quarter of 2026 alone, with raids on villages, farms, fishing grounds, schools, and health centres. The capability gap is widening: in the March 2025 Wulgo attack, militants killed 20 Cameroonian soldiers and seized Soviet-made ZSU-23-4 Shilka radar-guided anti-aircraft guns, and they now deploy explosive-laden drones. In the Northwest and Southwest, the Anglophone separatist war grinds on with abductions, illegal taxation, roadblocks, and 'ghost town' shutdowns.","pol":"The deepest risk is the succession vacuum. President Paul Biya — 92 years old, the world's oldest head of state, in power since 1982 — won a disputed eighth term in October 2025 amid violent protests. He has made prolonged medical trips to Switzerland, has not appointed a government eight months after the election, and has left the newly created vice-presidency (whose holder would automatically assume power in a vacancy) unfilled. A man was arrested for attempting to trick state broadcasters into airing a fake decree naming a government and VP — a signal of hidden succession struggles. Regional counter-terrorism has also frayed: the Nigeria-led MNJTF's gains have eroded, and Niger's 2023 coup broke the cooperation the Lake Chad Basin depends on.","eco":"The extraction irony sits at the heart of the Anglophone war: Cameroon's oil lies off the English-speaking coast, but French companies run the rigs — a grievance about who benefits from whose resources that helped turn a lawyers' and teachers' protest into an armed rebellion. Cameroon's Douala and Kribi ports are the Atlantic lifeline for landlocked Chad and the Central African Republic, giving it regional economic weight it cannot convert into stability.","assess":"Cameroon enters at 69 (ELEVATED, conflict-class), trend up — closing the platform's Lake Chad Basin gap, which previously covered only Nigeria and Niger while the same insurgency burned across four countries. It is the clearest case of compounding fragility: two separate insurgencies, an eroding regional CT framework, and a 92-year-old president with no named successor and an unfilled vice-presidency. The armed-groups dimension is now tracked in the Groups board (ISWAP's Lake Chad taxation empire reaches here). Watch Biya's health, any VP appointment, the Far North's weapons-capability gap (Shilkas, drones), and further MNJTF erosion.","tier":"ELEVATED","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"Cameroon's Anglophone war is among the least internationally reported conflicts of its scale — and the invisibility is itself contested.","claims":[{"claim":"The Anglophone crisis is a manageable internal security matter.","who":"The Cameroonian government.","evid":"Over 6,000 deaths and more than 700,000 displaced since 2017 by NGO counts, with a school boycott that has kept a generation out of education. The government restricts access to the Northwest and Southwest; both state forces and separatist groups have been documented committing abuses against civilians. The conflict receives a fraction of the coverage of smaller crises.","status":"UNDER-REPORTED"},{"claim":"Presidential succession is orderly and constitutional.","who":"The ruling party.","evid":"Cameroon has had one president since 1982. No functioning succession mechanism has been demonstrated, and the constitutional provision for the Senate president to serve as interim leader has never been tested. Analysts across the spectrum treat the succession as the country's principal risk event.","status":"UNTESTED"}]},"history":{"roots":"Cameroon's Anglophone war began with a decolonization France ensured stayed incomplete — and a liberation war Europe erased. The UPC (Union des Populations du Cameroun), the nationalist movement demanding real independence and reunification, was banned, its leaders assassinated (Um Nyobè 1958, Moumié poisoned in Geneva 1960, Ouandié executed 1971), and its maquis crushed in a Franco-Cameroonian counterinsurgency that killed tens of thousands — a war France only officially acknowledged in 2022, after Cameroonian and French historians (Mbembe among the former) spent decades documenting it. 'Independence' was granted instead to a client regime; the Anglophone territories, a former British mandate, federated in 1961 under promises of equal standing that Yaoundé's Francophone center dismantled step by step — federation abolished 1972, common-law courts and schools Frenchified — until the 2016 lawyers' and teachers' protests met bullets and became the Ambazonia war. Biya, in power since 1982 within the same client architecture, is the arrangement's caretaker more than its author.","timeline":[{"date":"1961","e":"British and French Cameroons federate — the Anglophone bargain later broken."},{"date":"1982","e":"Paul Biya takes power — still there four decades later."},{"date":"2014-","e":"Boko Haram war reaches the Far North."},{"date":"2016-17","e":"Anglophone protests met with force; separatist war begins."},{"date":"2025","e":"Biya, 92, wins re-election amid protests and killings."},{"date":"2026","e":"Both wars grind on; the succession vacuum is the real theater."}],"actors":[{"name":"Paul Biya","bio":"President since 1982 — rules through absence, patronage, and a security state; the succession he won't arrange is the country's biggest risk."},{"name":"Ambazonia separatists","bio":"Fragmented Anglophone armed groups — school bans, kidnappings, and a war neither side can win."},{"name":"Boko Haram / ISWAP","bio":"The Lake Chad insurgency's Cameroonian front — Far North raids and forced displacement."},{"name":"France","bio":"The post-colonial patron with deep commercial stakes — quietly central to any succession outcome."}],"bridge":"The Anglophone war is the 1961 federation's broken promise collecting; the UPC's erased war is why the state answers every grievance as sedition — it was born doing so, with French doctrine and hardware. The Far North's Boko Haram front burns in the country's poorest, least-administered region, and the succession vacuum around a nonagenarian president is Françafrique's client-state model reaching its actuarial limit with no legitimate mechanism behind it. Cameroon's three crises share one root: a state whose founding bargain was with its patron, not its peoples. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: an Anglophone war of 6,000 dead and 700,000 displaced continues under a president in office since 1982 with no succession mechanism. The 1961 joining of British Southern Cameroons to French Cameroun is the seam, and it has never been sewn.","sources":[{"work":"Kamerun! Une guerre cachée aux origines de la Françafrique","author":"Thomas Deltombe, Manuel Domergue & Jacob Tatsitsa","year":"2011","note":"The buried Franco-Cameroonian war against the UPC — the founding counterinsurgency, since officially acknowledged by France (2022)."},{"work":"La naissance du maquis dans le Sud-Cameroun","author":"Achille Mbembe","year":"1996","note":"Cameroon's own leading scholar on the UPC insurgency's social roots."},{"work":"Nation of Outlaws, State of Violence: Nationalism, Grassfields Tradition, and State Building in Cameroon","author":"Meredith Terretta","year":"2013","note":"The nationalist movement from below and its suppression."},{"work":"Anglophone crisis documentation","author":"Human Rights Watch / ICG Cameroon reports","year":"2017-26","note":"Primary documentation of the Ambazonia war's abuses on both sides."}]},"human":{"dead":6500,"disp":1100000,"famine":false,"aid":"obstructed NW/SW","note":"Anglophone war ~6.5k dead, ~700k displaced + Far North Boko Haram displacement.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"A client-state founding bargain answering every grievance as sedition.","alt":"The Anglophone war is identity-and-language federalism denied — not resource-driven; the platform's own account already centers the broken 1961 promise.","why":"Agreed and stated: this runs on the governance/identity register; extraction applies only to why the patron tie outweighs reform. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"Cameroon Concord News","u":"https://www.cameroonconcordnews.com/category/headline-news/"},{"t":"Cameroon Concord: Vreket attack; SHCC healthcare report","u":"https://www.cameroonconcordnews.com/category/headline-news/"},{"t":"Human Rights Watch · Cameroon","u":"https://www.hrw.org/africa/cameroon"},{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"},{"t":"CFR · Global Conflict Tracker","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker"}],"checkmode":"CHECKED","escwhy":"69 = active armed conflict, a recorded human toll, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Ambazonia separatists added to Groups; succession and under-reporting contested entries"}],"spill":[{"n":"Nigeria / Chad / Niger","dir":"both","w":"Lake Chad basin operations against Boko Haram and ISWAP; the weakened Multinational Joint Task Force reduces coordination."},{"n":"Nigeria","dir":"in","w":"The Anglophone separatists draw diaspora funding and weapons from the Nigerian border region.","id":"nigeria"},{"n":"Equatorial Guinea / Gabon","dir":"out","w":"Anglophone refugee flows and the Gulf of Guinea maritime dimension."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Anglophone war, frozen succession","dir":"stalemate","phase":"Protracted stalemate","up":["The Anglophone separatist war continuing at attrition","The 1982-incumbent succession vacuum","Lake Chad basin operations spilling south","Diaspora funding sustaining the separatists"],"down":["A federal or devolution settlement on the Anglophone question","A managed succession","Lake Chad coordination","Ripeness after years of hurting stalemate"],"fw":["conflict-curve","ethnic-security","ripeness"]},"inherit":{"name":"The CFA Franc","est":"1945","status":"IN FORCE · CONTESTED","what":"Created by the French provisional government in December 1945 as the <b>franc des Colonies Françaises d'Afrique</b> — the franc of the French Colonies of Africa. It was not a neutral monetary tool: it was an administrative instrument for managing colonial economies in the service of Paris, imposed by requiring taxes to be paid in it and by banning the cowrie and manilla currencies that preceded it. Colonial administrators used legal sanction and physical force where the ban failed.","now":"<b>It still circulates, used by 227 million people across fourteen countries as of 2026</b>, in two zones — West and Central African — with the French Treasury and, through the euro peg, the European Central Bank retaining roles in its governance. The Tax Justice Network's assessment is that it 'still functions according to the same principles and purpose established during the colonial period'. <b>Fiscal policy is national; monetary power is outsourced.</b> The 1994 devaluation, decided at a late-night meeting in Dakar, halved the savings of millions overnight at a rate nobody affected had voted on.","change":"France agreed in May 2020 to end its engagement in the West African zone and the reserve-deposit requirement, with the replacement currency — the <b>eco</b> — due to launch. <b>The launch has now been postponed five times; the current target is July 2027.</b> Cameroon sits in the Central African zone, where no eco transition is even scheduled — the reform conversation has been almost entirely West African, leaving six countries with the original arrangement and no announced exit."},"monitors":[{"t":"ACLED · conflict event data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}],"macrona":"NOT YET SOURCED. The economically relevant structure — the CFA franc zone, oil revenue and the Anglophone regions' exclusion from it — is covered in the inheritance and assessment fields."},{"id":"somalia","cat":"military","region":"Horn of Africa","subregion":"Horn / NE Africa","tclass":"conflict","name":"Somalia — Al-Shabaab Resurgence + Security Vacuum","sub":"ATMIS withdrawal → territory retaken · ISIS-Somalia in Puntland · 2026 election crisis","domain":"Insurgency / Post-Peacekeeping Collapse","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"2006 (Al-Shabaab)","esc":78,"lat":2.05,"lon":45.3,"trend":"up","esc_history":[72,73,75,76,77,77,78,78,78,78],"hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk15 (Jul 2026)","cas":"Decades of war; Al-Shabaab attacks up 70% (68→116/month) after 2022; attacks on civilians surged 306%; 900+ children recruited, 663 abducted in a single year (UN)","disp":"~3.8M internally displaced; recurrent famine risk","flag":"🇸🇴","note":"ELECTION TALKS HAVE COLLAPSED: negotiations between the Federal Government and the anti-FGS opposition coalition broke down over long-standing disputes, making electoral disputes and political violence likely · Critical Threats notes the elections could ALSO ATTRACT FOREIGN INTERFERENCE AS RIVAL RED SEA COALITIONS VIE FOR INFLUENCE — which places Somalia's internal contest inside the same Gulf and Red Sea rivalry the chokepoints and Saudi files track, rather than beside it · JUN-JUL 2026 — A CONSTITUTIONAL CRISIS, A TERM EXTENSION, AND FIGHTING IN MOGADISHU: constitutional amendments passed by the Federal Parliament in MARCH 2026 EXTENDED PRESIDENTIAL AND PARLIAMENTARY TERMS and imposed a new electoral framework WITHOUT POLITICAL CONSENSUS · ACLED records that the extension of the president's term TRIGGERED FIGHTING IN MOGADISHU · THE SUPPORT STRUCTURE IS FRAYING SIMULTANEOUSLY: the US has TERMINATED financial and logistical support for UNSOS (the UN Support Office that sustains the AU mission), while TURKEY is EXTENDING its military deployment — Erdogan submitted a motion to the Grand National Assembly citing bilateral defence agreements and a request from Mogadishu · ICG's assessment: 'Somalia appears to be at a tipping point. Its security is fragile, its politics fractured and its outside support fraying' · ISRAEL HAS RECOGNISED SOMALILAND'S INDEPENDENCE — the only state to do so; criticised by the AU and Mogadishu, while ETHIOPIA and the UAE, which hold military agreements with Hargeisa, have not commented · JUL 2026 — DROUGHT PLUS AID WITHDRAWAL: six million people — nearly ONE THIRD of the population — face acute hunger as drought devastates livelihoods; political dysfunction and conflict hamper the response · the decisive variable is external: the US SUSPENSION OF AID has drastically reduced emergency food assistance in the pipeline (ICG). The famine risk here is being set in Washington as much as by the rains · 2026 — THE GULF OF ADEN NEXUS: al-Shabaab and the Houthis have moved from ad hoc contact to what a former Somali national security adviser calls 'a functional strategic partnership governed by mutual utility' — al-Shabaab fighters trained in Yemen on DRONES and explosives (UN Panel of Experts documented four groups of ~30 departing from the Lower Shabelle coast for Hodeidah); Houthi personnel visiting al-Shabaab's Galgala stronghold for asymmetric-warfare exchanges; JOINT FINANCIAL VENTURES IN SOMALI EXTRACTIVE INDUSTRIES now documented (Saldhig Institute) · THE MATERIAL RISK: a Houthi attack drone can cost as little as $10,000 — technology transfer would give al-Shabaab precision strike capability it could never have built alone, in a theater flanking Bab el-Mandeb · CONTEXT: as Iran's network is degraded (Hezbollah, Syria, Iraqi militias, Hamas), its most autonomous member is building its own network southward · separately, reported US withdrawal of support puts the AUSSOM peacekeeping mission at risk mid-transition · NEW THEATER (Phase 4 · Horn) · TWO SEPARATE WARS + an election crisis · (1) AL-SHABAAB (al-Qaeda's wealthiest affiliate — the benchmark JNIM is measured against): exploiting the ATMIS→AUSSOM transition vacuum. ATMIS (18,000 troops, from AMISOM 2007) completed major withdrawal mid-2025; the SNA was NOT ready. By late 2025 Al-Shabaab had RETAKEN at least 5 districts in Lower Shabelle + Middle Jubba; offensive intensified in early 2026 · attacks rose from 68→116/month (+70%) after 2022; strikes on SNA special forces (Danab/Gorgor) surged 1,764% · Mar 2025: attempted to ASSASSINATE the president with an IED on his convoy in Mogadishu · controls large rural swathes of south-central Somalia + strongholds in the northern Al-Madow mountains · (2) ISIS-SOMALIA (Puntland): a DISTINCT theater — Puntland's independent 'Hilaac' (Lightning) offensive from Dec 2024; 364 PDF operations vs ISIS through Sept 2025; US + UAE airstrikes peaked Jul-Sept 2025; conducted independently of the federal govt · Feb 2025: 1st major US military op of the new administration targeted IS-Somalia · (3) POLITICS: AUSSOM replaced ATMIS (Jan 2025, 12,626 personnel cap) but is underfunded; 2026 election crisis — Puntland+Jubaland+opposition ('Somali Future Council') reject the FGS's one-person-one-vote plan","leverage":[{"actor":"Al-Shabaab","asset":"Wealthiest AQ affiliate — extortion/taxation state; deep clan integration; intelligence penetration of the govt enabling assassinations + insider attacks","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Federal Govt (FGS)","asset":"International backing (US strikes, AUSSOM, Turkey's largest overseas base) — but no monopoly on force; depends on clan militias (Ma'awisley)","str":"lo"},{"actor":"Puntland","asset":"Autonomous military capacity — runs its own anti-ISIS campaign with US/UAE air support, independent of Mogadishu","str":"md"},{"actor":"Turkey","asset":"Largest overseas military base (Mogadishu); trains/equips Somali forces; mediated the Ethiopia-Somalia row","str":"md"},{"actor":"UAE","asset":"Puntland's longtime patron; airstrikes vs ISIS; Bosaso airport access (until Somalia cancelled UAE agreements Jan 2026 over the RSF pipeline)","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"UN Panel of Experts / Crisis Group","detail":"'Al-Shabaab remains the most significant threat to peace and security in Somalia'; ICG's 'After ATMIS: Somalia's Security Vacuum' (Jan 2026) documents the drawdown-driven collapse"},{"src":"Hiraal Institute (Feb 2026)","detail":"Attacks rose 68→116/month (+70%) post-2022; attacks on civilians +306%; strikes on SNA special forces +1,764%; 364 Puntland operations vs ISIS"},{"src":"UNSC / AUSSOM","detail":"AUSSOM replaced ATMIS Jan 2025 (up to 12,626 personnel); chronically underfunded — EU committed only $8.71M in Apr 2026"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Al-Shabaab","channel":"Taxation + extortion state","exp":"HIGH","detail":"The WEALTHIEST al-Qaeda affiliate — runs a lucrative extortion/taxation business over businesses, checkpoints, ports, and the telecoms/hawala economy. Its revenue is the benchmark against which JNIM is measured (JNIM ranks second)."},{"holder":"Turkey","channel":"Base + port/airport concessions","exp":"MED","detail":"Largest overseas Turkish base; manages Mogadishu port and airport — a foothold traded for security provision"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 US (AFRICOM)","side":"FGS + Puntland","flow":"Airstrikes vs both Al-Shabaab and IS-Somalia; killed senior leaders (Jaafar Gurey, Oct 2025); 1st major op of the new administration targeted IS-Somalia (Feb 2025)","tier":"documented","for":"Counter-terror; Red Sea/Gulf of Aden security"},{"actor":"🇹🇷 Turkey","side":"FGS","flow":"Largest overseas military base; trains/equips Somali forces; mediated the Ethiopia-Somalia Red Sea row","tier":"documented","for":"Horn influence; port/airport concessions; arms market"},{"actor":"🇦🇪 UAE","side":"Puntland","flow":"Longtime Puntland patron; airstrikes vs IS-Somalia; Bosaso airport — but Somalia CANCELLED all bilateral UAE agreements (Jan 2026) over the RSF supply pipeline","tier":"documented","for":"Red Sea/Gulf of Aden port network — the same strategy driving its Sudan intervention"},{"actor":"🇪🇹🇰🇪 Ethiopia/Kenya","side":"FGS (AUSSOM)","flow":"Troop contributors; Ethiopia's role strained by the Red Sea/Somaliland recognition row (Turkey-mediated)","tier":"documented","for":"Border security; Ethiopia's Red Sea access ambition"}],"mil":"Two distinct wars run in parallel. In the south, al-Shabaab — al-Qaeda's wealthiest affiliate — is exploiting the security vacuum left by the ATMIS withdrawal: the 18,000-strong African Union mission drew down through mid-2025, the Somali National Army was not ready to replace it, and by late 2025 the group had retaken at least five districts in Lower Shabelle and Middle Jubba. Its attack tempo rose from 68 to 116 per month, attacks on civilians surged 306%, and strikes on Somalia's US-trained special forces jumped more than seventeenfold. In March 2025 it came close to assassinating the president with an IED on his convoy. In the north, Puntland fights a separate war against ISIS-Somalia in the Bari mountains, running its own 'Hilaac' offensive with US and UAE airstrikes — independent of Mogadishu.","pol":"The state has never re-established a monopoly on force since 1991. AUSSOM replaced ATMIS in January 2025 with a 12,626-personnel cap but is chronically underfunded (the EU committed just $8.71M in April 2026). Meanwhile the 2026 election is fracturing the federation: Puntland, Jubaland, and the opposition 'Somali Future Council' reject the federal government's one-person-one-vote plan, and the political infighting is directly undermining the counterinsurgency — Jubaland's row with Mogadishu escalated to armed conflict, precluding joint operations.","eco":"Al-Shabaab's power is fiscal. It is the wealthiest al-Qaeda affiliate in the world — the benchmark against which JNIM's ~$100M Sahel economy is measured and found second — running a taxation and extortion state over businesses, checkpoints, ports, and the hawala economy. Its intelligence network penetrates government and security institutions, and its integration with clan structures makes it nearly impossible to dislodge. Turkey, meanwhile, has converted security provision into control of Mogadishu's port and airport.","assess":"Somalia enters at 78 (SEVERE, conflict-class), trend up — and it closes the platform's most conspicuous gap: al-Shabaab is the wealthiest al-Qaeda affiliate on earth, the explicit benchmark the Groups board uses to rank JNIM, yet it was untracked. Its significance is a warning the whole board should read: this is what happens when a peacekeeping force withdraws before the state can replace it. Territory declared 'liberated' was retaken within months. It also completes the UAE picture — Abu Dhabi patronizes Puntland for the same Red Sea port-network reasons it arms the RSF in Sudan, and Somalia cancelled its UAE agreements in January 2026 precisely over that pipeline. Watch the AUSSOM funding gap, the 2026 election fracture, and further al-Shabaab district seizures. Trend estimate — newly entered tracking.","tier":"SEVERE","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"Whether al-Shabaab is 'losing' has been announced for 15 years; the narrative war is over momentum itself.","claims":[{"claim":"Government offensives have al-Shabaab on the verge of defeat.","who":"Mogadishu + successive AU/US statements — the momentum narrative that sustains funding and troop commitments.","evid":"Al-Shabaab retook much of what the 2022-23 offensive captured; it taxes the economy under the government's nose and is resurgent as AUSSOM shrinks. The 'verge of defeat' claim has been falsified repeatedly since 2011.","status":"CONTESTED — momentum claims vs territorial reality"},{"claim":"Al-Shabaab is a legitimate resistance to foreign occupation and a fairer justice provider.","who":"Al-Shabaab's own information operations — the governance-legitimacy pitch that aids recruitment and taxation.","evid":"Its courts are used even by non-supporters (documented) — but so are its massacres, forced recruitment of children, and famine-weaponization. The 'fair justice' brand is real as perception, monstrous as practice.","status":"CONTESTED — governance brand vs atrocity record"}]},"history":{"roots":"Somalia's condition was manufactured by partition. The Somali people — one language, one faith, a pastoral civilization with sophisticated indigenous governance (the xeer customary-law system, clan assemblies) — were split five ways by the Scramble: British Somaliland, Italian Somalia, French Djibouti, Ethiopia's Ogaden, and Kenya's Northern Frontier District. Independence (1960) unified only two of the five, making irredentism the state's founding purpose; the 1977 Ogaden war's defeat — when the superpowers switched sides — broke the project and, by 1991, the state. What outsiders call 'anarchy' Somali scholars describe differently: Barre's dictatorship destroyed the state's legitimacy, and the clan system both fragmented the war and, in Somaliland, rebuilt peace without any foreign force — the most successful indigenous state-reconstruction in modern Africa, still unrecognized. Al-Shabaab grew from the rubble of the 2006 Ethiopian invasion (US-backed), which destroyed the Islamic Courts Union — the one indigenous order that had unified the south.","timeline":[{"date":"1991","e":"Barre regime falls; the state collapses into clan warfare."},{"date":"1993","e":"US 'Black Hawk Down' battle in Mogadishu; US withdraws."},{"date":"2006","e":"Islamic Courts Union briefly unifies the south; Ethiopia invades."},{"date":"2007","e":"African Union force (AMISOM) deploys; al-Shabaab emerges."},{"date":"2012","e":"A federal government is re-established."},{"date":"2022-24","e":"Government offensives push al-Shabaab back, then stall."},{"date":"2025","e":"ATMIS ends; the under-funded AUSSOM mission takes over."},{"date":"2026","e":"Al-Shabaab resurgent amid the security-transition vacuum; US airstrikes continue."},{"date":"2026","e":"Drought and the US aid suspension leave six million — nearly a third of the population — in acute hunger; UN monitors document an AQAP-al-Shabaab network across the Gulf of Aden."}],"actors":[{"name":"Al-Shabaab","bio":"Al-Qaeda's East Africa affiliate; controls rural southern Somalia, taxes the economy, and outlasts every foreign force."},{"name":"Federal Government (Hassan Sheikh Mohamud)","bio":"Internationally-backed but weak, dependent on AU troops and US strikes."},{"name":"AUSSOM (African Union)","bio":"The latest, under-funded AU stabilization mission; its drawdown is the immediate escalation driver."},{"name":"ISIS-Somalia","bio":"A smaller rival jihadist faction in Puntland, targeted by recent US strikes."}],"bridge":"Today's 'security transition crisis' repeats the pattern: external forces (AMISOM→ATMIS→AUSSOM) substitute for, rather than build, indigenous legitimacy — and each drawdown reveals the substitution. Al-Shabaab's taxation and courts work because they plug into the xeer expectation of order the federal state doesn't meet; Somaliland's unrecognized success and the Ogaden/NFD partitions remain the standing proof that the crisis was never Somali incapacity but the 1884 map plus repeated external decapitation of homegrown orders (1977, 2006). The famine-and-drone cycle is the partition still collecting. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: six million in acute hunger as US aid is suspended, while al-Shabaab builds a network across the Gulf of Aden with AQAP. The colonial partition into five pieces still defines the irredentist claim that keeps the region armed.","sources":[{"work":"Africa's First Democrats: Somalia's Aden A. Osman and Abdirazak H. Hussein","author":"Abdi Ismail Samatar","year":"2016","note":"Somali scholarship recovering the democratic founding era erased by the 'failed state' frame."},{"work":"Oral Poetry and Somali Nationalism","author":"Said S. Samatar","year":"1982","note":"The indigenous political culture — poetry as politics — that partition cut across."},{"work":"Somalia: State Collapse and the Threat of Terrorism","author":"Ken Menkhaus","year":"2004","note":"The mechanics of collapse and the external-intervention paradox."},{"work":"Becoming Somaliland","author":"Mark Bradbury","year":"2008","note":"The indigenous peace-building (Borama conference tradition) that rebuilt the north without foreign troops."}]},"human":{"dead":50000,"disp":3800000,"famine":true,"aid":"al-Shabaab-taxed","note":"Six million — nearly a third of the population — face acute hunger (ICG, Jul 2026) as drought compounds conflict. US aid suspension has drastically cut the emergency food pipeline. Conflict + drought compound; 3.8M displaced; famine-risk districts recurrent; aid access negotiated with the insurgency it feeds.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"rival":{"conf":"MOD","lens":"Partition plus repeated external decapitation of indigenous orders produced the vacuum al-Shabaab fills.","alt":"Clan political economy may explain more of the present than the 1884 map: al-Shabaab works partly as a cross-clan arbitration service where the federal state itself IS a clan bargain. Resources are marginal — this theater fits the extraction lens worst.","why":"Both true at different depths; structural frame for origins, clan-marketplace frame for mechanics. The lens is explicitly weakest here, stated openly. MOD."},"refs":[{"t":"Critical Threats · Africa File (26 Feb 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/drc-fardc-m23-russia-wagner-mali-burkina-niger-rsf-chad-sudan-fgs-somalia-ghana-aes-ecowas-africa-file-february-26-2026"},{"t":"ICG · Seven Peace and Security Priorities for Africa in 2026","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/brf/africa/b209-seven-peace-and-security-priorities-africa-2026"},{"t":"ACLED · Horn of Africa (UNSOS funding exit)","u":"https://acleddata.com/region/conflict-horn-africa"},{"t":"Africa Center · Africa Media Review (20 Jul 2026)","u":"https://africacenter.org/daily-media-review/africa-media-review-for-july-20-2026/"},{"t":"UK Parliament · Africa in 2026","u":"https://commonslibrary.parliament.uk/africa-in-2026-conflict-elections-and-a-new-uk-framework/"},{"t":"ICG On the Horizon Jul-Dec 2026: Horn food insecurity","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/oth/chad-cuba-horn-africa-pakistan/horizon-july-december-2026"},{"t":"ADF: Houthi technology transfer fuels violence in Somalia","u":"https://adf-magazine.com/2026/05/houthi-technology-transfer-fuels-violence-in-somalia/"}],"checkmode":"SWEPT","escwhy":"78 = an al-Shabaab offensive exploiting the ATMIS vacuum, compounded by drought and a US aid suspension that has cut the emergency food pipeline. Famine risk is the scoring driver.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Six million in acute hunger; US aid suspension effect recorded; famine flag set"}],"spill":[{"n":"Turkey","dir":"in","w":"Extending its military deployment by parliamentary motion — training, advisory and technical support under bilateral defence agreements, at Mogadishu's request. Ankara is filling the space the US is vacating.","id":"turkey"},{"n":"United States","dir":"out","w":"Terminated financial and logistical support for UNSOS, the UN office that sustains the AU mission — plus the aid suspension that cut the emergency food pipeline for six million people.","id":"us-politics"},{"n":"Somaliland / Israel","dir":"both","w":"Israel has recognised Somaliland's independence — the only state to do so. Criticised by the AU and Mogadishu; Ethiopia and the UAE, which hold military agreements with Hargeisa, have not commented.","id":"somalia"},{"n":"Ethiopia","dir":"both","w":"Addis seeks commercial sea access and has military agreements with Somaliland; ICG frames a Berbera package with Mogadishu's agreement as the alternative to a land grab.","id":"ethiopia"},{"n":"Yemen","dir":"both","w":"UN monitors document an AQAP-al-Shabaab network across the Gulf of Aden — two shores, one logistics system.","id":"yemen"},{"n":"Uganda","id":"uganda","dir":"in","w":"A long-standing troop contributor to the African Union mission — the deployment that has underwritten Mogadishu's security, now exposed as the US terminates UNSOS funding."},{"n":"Israel","id":"israel","dir":"in","w":"The only state to recognise Somaliland's independence — criticised by the AU and Mogadishu, and a direct challenge to the federation's territorial claim."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Constitutional crisis, support structure fraying","dir":"volatile","phase":"Escalation → tipping point","up":["The term-extension fighting in Mogadishu spreads","US UNSOS funding termination collapses the AU mission","al-Shabaab exploits the political-security vacuum","Somaliland recognition fractures the federation further"],"down":["Turkey filling the security gap the US vacated","Clan-militia (Macawisley) gains against al-Shabaab holding","A political consensus on the electoral framework","Federal-state relations repaired"],"fw":["conflict-curve","resource-conflict","ethnic-security"]},"macro":{"growth":"~3.7% · GDP $13.9B nominal","infl":"Low; import-price driven","src":"IMF/World Bank country data, 2026","note":"GDP PER CAPITA $798 with 63% BELOW THE POVERTY LINE and government debt at 76.7% of GDP. Imports run $9.0B against exports of $1.1B — an eight-to-one gap financed by remittances and aid, both now contracting as the US suspends assistance and terminates UNSOS funding. Ethiopia is both the largest export destination (29.7%) and the largest import source (17.6%), which is the economic weight underneath the Berbera and sea-access dispute."},"inherit":{"name":"The Five-Way Partition","est":"1884-1897","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Somali-inhabited territory was divided among <b>five administrations</b> — British Somaliland, Italian Somaliland, French Somaliland (Djibouti), the Ogaden ceded to Ethiopia in 1897, and the Northern Frontier District given to Kenya. The five points of the Somali flag's star represent them. No other African people was partitioned quite so completely.","now":"<b>The partition still defines the region's security architecture.</b> The irredentist claim it created has produced the Ogaden wars, the Kenyan frontier insurgency, and the permanent question of Somaliland — the former British territory, which functions as a state and which <b>Israel recognised in 2026, the only country to do so</b>. Al-Shabaab operates across three of the five pieces.","change":"Ethiopia seeks sea access through Berbera in the territory it was ceded the interior of; the UAE holds military agreements with Hargeisa. <b>The recognition question is now a live instrument of foreign policy rather than a frozen dispute.</b>"},"ctrl":{"gov":40,"contested":25,"other":35,"est":true,"src":"Composite of ACLED and ICG reporting, 2026","note":"FGS control is concentrated in Mogadishu and federal member state capitals. Al-Shabaab holds rural southern and central areas and taxes well beyond what it holds. Somaliland is separately administered."},"control":{"gov":35,"contested":30,"other":35,"holders":"The Federal Government holds Mogadishu and parts of the south with AU support. Al-Shabaab controls or taxes much of the rural south and centre. Somaliland governs the northwest as a de facto state; Puntland runs the northeast.","note":"Clan militias (Macawisley) drove the gains that actually held in 2022-23; several reversed when federal-clan relations soured.","est":true}},{"id":"chad","cat":"military","region":"Central Africa","subregion":"Central Africa","tclass":"conflict","name":"Chad — The RSF Corridor Turns on Its Host","sub":"UAE arms transit · RSF now striking Chad · Zaghawa fracture in Déby's own army · Saudi hedge","domain":"Proxy-Corridor State / Regime Fragility","sev":"high","status":"escalating","since":"2023 (Sudan war)","esc":76,"lat":13.5,"lon":20.9,"trend":"up","esc_history":[70,72,73,74,75,76,76,76,76,76],"hist_real":false,"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"Wk16 (Jul 2026)","cas":"Dec 2025: 2 soldiers killed by Sudan-origin drone · Jan 2026: 7 killed in RSF attack on a Chadian military camp · Feb 2026: 5 soldiers + 3 civilians killed at Tine · Mar 18 2026: drone strike on Ramadan Iftar gathering at Mabrouka (Wadi Fira) killed 16 civilians","disp":"918,760+ Sudanese refugees in eastern Chad (as of Mar 31) — one of the world's largest refugee burdens per capita","flag":"🇹🇩","note":"MAJOR GAP CLOSED — CHAD CLOSED ITS BORDER WITH SUDAN: following an RSF ATTACK ON CHADIAN FORCES INSIDE CHAD, N'Djamena closed the border — THREATENING KEY RSF SUPPLY LINES at the same time as other RSF corridors were being disrupted and the SAF was advancing · this board carried Chad as a passive corridor for RSF resupply and as the host of the refugee mass. It is now an active party that has used its own territory as leverage against the RSF, and has been attacked for it · the closure is the single most consequential thing any neighbouring state has done to the RSF's logistics · MAY-JUL 2026 — STATE OF EMERGENCY IN LAKE PROVINCE, AND THE TASK FORCE IS BREAKING: N'Djamena declared a STATE OF EMERGENCY in LAKE PROVINCE in May 2026 and ICG expects a military-led response including AIRSTRIKES and ground operations — while warning the army could become OVERSTRETCHED with limited intelligence capability · THE STRUCTURAL PROBLEM: CHAD AND NIGER'S WITHDRAWALS HAVE WEAKENED THE MULTINATIONAL JOINT TASK FORCE, once the key mechanism for coordinating against Boko Haram — sovereignty assertions have dismantled the one instrument that made the Lake Chad basin governable · ICG flags a possible RESUMPTION OF SECURITY COOPERATION WITH FRANCE to restore surveillance and intelligence — the exit being partially reversed under operational pressure · Nigerian operations in Borno could push militants toward Chadian shores; attacks may ease during the rainy season through September · 2026 — THE GARRISON STATE EXPORTS ITS BUSINESS MODEL: on 20 Apr Déby announced the deployment of 1,500 CHADIAN TROOPS TO HAITI as part of the UN-backed Gang Suppression Force — the first Chadian officers arrived 1 Apr. A state that cannot secure its own Lake Chad periphery is now selling security abroad; the subcontractor model this file describes, made literal · Déby was awarded the 2026 African Peace Prize for managing the transition and hosting Sudanese refugees — the same transition that saw eight GCAP opposition leaders sentenced to 8 years for 'rebellion' (8 May) · 26 Apr: at least 42 KILLED IN INTERETHNIC CLASHES IN WADI FIRA over a dispute about A WELL — water scarcity as a direct casualty driver · 4 May: 23 soldiers killed by Boko Haram at Barka Tolorom · ICG warns Chad risks being drawn further into Sudan's civil war · NEW THEATER (Wk16 · closes the platform's biggest structural gap — Chad was load-bearing in Sudan, Haiti, and gold-laundering but had no theater) · THE CORRIDOR: the UAE→RSF arms pipeline runs through Chad; a UAE-built airport at AMDJARASS/Amjarass (2023, officially a 'field hospital' for refugees) was found by UN investigators and journalists to be a weapons transit node · PAYMENT: Chad reportedly received ~$2B in UAE loans (incl. a $500M Abu Dhabi Fund loan at 1% over 18yr, 2024; a $200M offer in 2023) — transformative for a country with GDP/capita ~$3,000 · THE BLOWBACK — the corridor is now killing its host: RSF attacked a Chadian military camp (Jan, 7 dead); fighting at Tine spilled over (Feb, 8 dead); Chad CLOSED the Sudan border Feb 23 and deployed thousands of troops along the 1,400km frontier; a Sudan-origin drone killed 16 civilians at a Ramadan Iftar in Mabrouka (Mar 18) — Déby, in fatigues, called it 'outrageous and excessive aggression' and ordered retaliation · THE ETHNIC TRAP: the RSF is exterminating ZAGHAWA in Darfur — and the Zaghawa dominate Chad's military and security elite. Déby is Zaghawa. One officer: the war is now seen as 'a war between Zaghawa and Arabs' · Chadian Zaghawa militias DEFY government disarmament orders and fight alongside Sudanese Zaghawa in the Joint Force (SAF-aligned), using Chad as a rear base · Déby's cousin Ousmane Dillo is a Zaghawa rebel fighting the RSF in Darfur — his influence on the officer corps is a Crisis Group watch item · Hemedti's cousin Gen. Bichara Issa Djadalla is Déby's SPECIAL ADVISER · THE HEDGE: Déby flew to Saudi Arabia (May 2026) — the SAF's backer — to build a 'political firewall,' playing the two Gulf powers against each other · Egypt, Saudi Arabia and Somalia began DENYING UAE overflight (Jan 2026); Somalia cancelled all UAE agreements Jan 12; eastern Libya briefly closed al-Kufra — squeezing the alternatives and making the Chad route MORE critical · BOKO HARAM: JAS + ISWAP re-establishing a foothold in Lake province; state of emergency declared May 2026 · Déby won the 2026 African Peace Prize (Jan) for hosting refugees — while hosting the pipeline arming the force creating them","leverage":[{"actor":"UAE","asset":"~$2B in loans + political backing buying transit rights through Chadian territory — the payment that makes the corridor work","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Déby regime","asset":"Controls the transit territory both the UAE and the RSF need; can close the border (and did) — but depends on Emirati money to fund the patronage that keeps him in power","str":"md"},{"actor":"Zaghawa officer corps","asset":"Dominates Chad's military — and is watching its own kin exterminated by the force its president lets be armed. The regime's core constituency is its core vulnerability","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Saudi Arabia","asset":"The counter-patron — the SAF's backer, now courted by Déby as a firewall against total UAE dependence","str":"md"},{"actor":"RSF","asset":"Attacks Chad directly while depending on it for supply — betting Déby needs Emirati money too badly to retaliate seriously","str":"md"}],"gov":[{"src":"UN Panel of Experts","detail":"Found 'credible evidence' the UAE's Amdjarass 'field hospital' facility was used for arms transfers to the RSF"},{"src":"Crisis Group (On the Horizon, Jul-Dec 2026)","detail":"'The longer the Sudan war continues, the harder it may become for Déby to balance ties with the UAE, communal grievances and elite fragmentation. His grip on power could weaken' — though no Zaghawa figure yet commands enough support to challenge him"},{"src":"Africa Center for Strategic Studies","detail":"'Chad Risks Greater Instability from Engagement in Sudan's Conflict'; documents the $500M Abu Dhabi Fund loan (1%, 18yr) and border closure"},{"src":"Chad Presidency (Mar 18 2026)","detail":"Déby ordered the military to retaliate against any Sudanese forces attacking Chadian territory after the Mabrouka drone strike killed 16; convened emergency Defence and Security Council"},{"src":"ISW / Critical Threats (Africa File, Jan 22 2026)","detail":"Chadian security officials have criticized Déby for allowing UAE weapons transit; Egypt/Saudi/Somalia began denying UAE overflight, raising the stakes of the Chad route"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"UAE","channel":"Sovereign loans + patronage","exp":"HIGH","detail":"~$2B (incl. $500M Abu Dhabi Fund at 1%/18yr) — for a ~$3,000 GDP/capita state this is regime-defining money, and it buys transit rights. This is the purchase price of a corridor."},{"holder":"Chad (Déby)","channel":"Territorial transit rights","exp":"HIGH","detail":"The commodity Chad sells is geography — the 1,400km Darfur frontier. Adré and Amdjarass are the nodes; illicit economies have boomed since 2023."},{"holder":"Gold smuggling networks","channel":"Sudanese gold → Dubai","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Chad is a primary smuggling route for RSF-controlled Sudanese gold heading to the UAE — the loop that pays for the weapons that come back the other way"}],"external":[{"actor":"🇦🇪 UAE","side":"RSF (via Chad)","flow":"Runs the RSF arms pipeline through Chadian territory (Amdjarass airport); paid ~$2B in loans for the access; denies everything","tier":"alleged-credible","for":"Sudanese gold; Red Sea/Sahel position — Chad is the land bridge that makes the RSF resupply possible"},{"actor":"🇸🇦 Saudi Arabia","side":"Déby (hedge) / SAF","flow":"Déby's May 2026 Riyadh trip sought financial backing as a firewall; Saudi backs the SAF against the UAE's RSF","tier":"documented","for":"Counter-Emirati influence; a stable Chad that isn't wholly Abu Dhabi's"},{"actor":"🏴 RSF","side":"belligerent + client","flow":"Depends on Chadian transit for supply — while attacking Chadian military camps and killing Chadian civilians with drones","tier":"documented","for":"The supply line it cannot survive without, from a host it is simultaneously attacking"},{"actor":"🇫🇷 France","side":"Déby regime","flow":"Historic patron; military presence drawn down but influence persists","tier":"documented","for":"Sahel foothold after expulsion from Mali/Burkina/Niger"},{"actor":"🏴 Boko Haram / ISWAP","side":"insurgents","flow":"Re-establishing a foothold in Lake province after retrenchment; state of emergency declared May 2026","tier":"documented","for":"Lake Chad caliphate project — the second front Déby cannot afford"}],"mil":"Chad is being attacked by the force its own territory helps supply. Between December 2025 and March 2026 the war it was hosting came home: a Sudan-origin drone killed two Chadian soldiers in December, an RSF attack on a Chadian military camp killed seven in January, fighting at the border town of Tine killed five soldiers and three civilians in February, and on March 18 a drone struck a Ramadan Iftar gathering at Mabrouka in Wadi Fira, killing sixteen civilians. Déby appeared in military fatigues, called it 'outrageous and excessive aggression,' convened an emergency Defence and Security Council, and ordered retaliation. Chad closed its 1,400km border with Sudan in February and deployed thousands of troops — and the weapons kept moving anyway. A second front is opening in Lake province, where Boko Haram's JAS faction and ISWAP are re-establishing a foothold after months of retrenchment, prompting a state of emergency in May.","pol":"The trap is ethnic and it runs through the president's own army. The RSF is exterminating the Zaghawa in Darfur — and the Zaghawa dominate Chad's military and security elite. Déby is Zaghawa. When the RSF massacres Zaghawa communities in El Fasher, Chadian Zaghawa officers watch their own kin slaughtered by a force their president permits to be armed across his territory. One officer described the war as increasingly 'a war between Zaghawa and Arabs' — combustible language in a state defined by a delicate ethnic balance. Chadian Zaghawa militias now defy government disarmament orders and fight alongside Sudanese Zaghawa in the SAF-aligned Joint Force, using Chad as a rear base. Déby's own cousin, Ousmane Dillo, is a Zaghawa rebel fighting the RSF in Darfur. Meanwhile Hemedti's cousin, General Bichara Issa Djadalla, serves as Déby's special adviser. Crisis Group's assessment is blunt: the longer the war runs, the harder it becomes for Déby to balance Emirati money against communal grievance and elite fragmentation — and his grip could weaken, though no Zaghawa figure yet commands enough support to replace him.","eco":"Chad sells geography. The UAE built an airport at Amdjarass in 2023, officially a field hospital for refugees; UN investigators and journalists found it functioning as an arms-transfer node into RSF-held Darfur. In exchange Chad reportedly received roughly $2 billion in loans — including a $500 million Abu Dhabi Fund facility at 1% interest over eighteen years. For a country with GDP per capita around $3,000, that is regime-defining money, and it is the purchase price of a corridor. The loop closes on the other side: Chad is a primary smuggling route for RSF-controlled Sudanese gold moving to Dubai — the gold pays for the weapons that come back through the same desert. Meanwhile Chad hosts over 918,000 Sudanese refugees, one of the heaviest per-capita burdens on earth, and Déby collected the 2026 African Peace Prize in January for taking them in.","assess":"Chad enters at 76 (SEVERE, conflict-class), trend up — and it is the theater the board most needed, because Chad was already load-bearing in three places and tracked in none: the Sudan pipeline, the Haiti deployment, and the gold-laundering system all run through it. Its structural position is the platform's thesis in miniature. Déby sold transit rights to the UAE for about $2 billion, and the corridor is now killing its host: the RSF strikes Chadian camps and Chadian civilians while depending on Chadian territory to survive, betting that Déby needs Emirati money too badly to retaliate. The deeper fracture is ethnic — the force being armed across his territory is exterminating the Zaghawa, who are his own base and his own officer corps, and Zaghawa militias are already defying his disarmament orders to go fight it. The squeeze is tightening from outside too: Egypt, Saudi Arabia and Somalia have begun denying UAE overflight and eastern Libya briefly closed al-Kufra, which makes the Chad route more essential, not less. Déby's answer has been to fly to Riyadh and court the SAF's patron — playing both Gulf powers to build a firewall. Watch the Zaghawa officer corps, Ousmane Dillo's influence, the Tine border fighting, the depth of the Saudi hedge, and whether Boko Haram's Lake province revival opens a second front the regime cannot fund. This is the clearest case on the board of a state that monetized someone else's war and is now being consumed by it. Trend estimate — newly entered tracking.","tier":"SEVERE","domain_tag":"Military","contested":{"summary":"A president who won a peace prize for hosting the refugees, from a war he helps supply.","claims":[{"claim":"Chad and the UAE both deny that arms transit Chadian territory to the RSF.","who":"Déby — who cannot admit what the $2B bought — and the UAE, which needs the corridor deniable.","evid":"UN investigators found 'credible evidence' the Amdjarass 'field hospital' was an arms-transfer node. Journalists and Crisis Group document the loans. Analysts (Cameron Hudson, World Politics Review) describe Emirati financial and political support exchanged for territorial access. Chad closed the border in February 2026 and the weapons reportedly kept moving.","status":"DOCUMENTED-BUT-DENIED"},{"claim":"Déby was awarded the 2026 African Peace Prize for managing the political transition and hosting Sudanese refugees.","who":"Déby — international legitimacy is the counter-narrative to the corridor allegations.","evid":"Both are true, and that is the point. Chad genuinely hosts 918,760+ refugees at enormous cost. It also hosts the pipeline arming the force that created them. The prize does not disprove the corridor; the corridor does not erase the refugees.","status":"UNRESOLVED — both facts are real; only one is celebrated"},{"claim":"The RSF's cross-border attacks are framed as spillover or accident.","who":"The RSF, which cannot afford to lose Chadian transit, and Déby, who cannot afford to admit his patron's client is killing his people.","evid":"Four separate lethal incidents Dec 2025-Mar 2026, including a drone strike on a Ramadan Iftar that killed 16 civilians. Déby called it 'outrageous and excessive aggression' and ordered retaliation. Analysts read RSF overconfidence: a bet that Déby needs Emirati money too badly to respond.","status":"CONTESTED"}]},"history":{"roots":"Chad was colonized as an afterthought and governed as a barracks. France classified it 'Tchad utile' and 'Tchad inutile' — the cotton south worth administering, the Saharan north worth only garrisoning — a bifurcation that structured every conflict since. Marielle Debos's ethnography names the result: a state where 'living by the gun' is a profession, where power has changed hands by rebellion column (1979, 1982, 1990) rather than ballot, and where France intervened militarily again and again (against Libya in the 1980s, for Déby in 2008 and 2019) to keep its Sahel keystone in place — the garrison-state as a joint Franco-Chadian enterprise. Idriss Déby ruled for thirty years as the West's indispensable soldier (Chad's army carried the Sahel coalitions) and died as he ruled, at the front; the dynastic handover to Mahamat, blessed by Macron at the funeral, showed the arrangement outliving the man.","timeline":[{"date":"1960","e":"Independence from France; decades of rebellion and coups follow."},{"date":"1990","e":"Idriss Déby seizes power — the dynasty begins."},{"date":"2003-","e":"Darfur war spills over; Chad hosts refugees and rebels both."},{"date":"2021","e":"Idriss Déby killed at the front; son Mahamat installed."},{"date":"2023-","e":"Sudan war: ~1M refugees; UAE arms transit via Amdjarass to the RSF."},{"date":"2024","e":"French forces expelled — the last Sahel base falls."},{"date":"2025-26","e":"Mabrouka drone strike inside Chad; Zaghawa elite fractures over the RSF; border closures; Déby balances UAE money (CEPA talks) against the blowback."},{"date":"20 Apr 2026","e":"Déby announces the deployment of 1,500 Chadian troops to HAITI for the Gang Suppression Force — a state that cannot secure Lake Chad exporting security."},{"date":"26 Apr 2026","e":"At least 42 killed in interethnic clashes in Wadi Fira over a dispute about A WELL."},{"date":"8 May 2026","e":"Eight GCAP opposition leaders sentenced to eight years for 'rebellion'."}],"actors":[{"name":"Mahamat Déby","bio":"Dynastic president since 2021 — running his father's security state while the Sudan war splits his Zaghawa base and the UAE relationship buys and endangers him at once."},{"name":"UAE","bio":"Loans and trade deals in exchange for the Amdjarass corridor — the RSF's supply line runs on Chadian tolerance."},{"name":"Zaghawa networks","bio":"The cross-border kin group spanning the Déby elite and Darfur's victims — the war forces a choice the regime can't safely make."},{"name":"France (departed)","bio":"The century-long patron whose 2024 exit left the vacuum Russia and the Gulf now contest."}],"bridge":"Today Chad is what happens when the garrison-state's patron leaves and its neighbors burn: France expelled (2024), the Sahel coalitions dead, and Sudan's war pressing the exact seam colonial rule built — the Zaghawa kin-networks spanning the Déby elite and Darfur's victims, now split by the UAE's RSF corridor running through Amdjarass with Abu Dhabi's loans as rent. A state constructed as a security subcontractor is discovering it has no other business model; the Mabrouka strike and the elite fracture are the invoice arriving.","sources":[{"work":"Living by the Gun in Chad: Combatants, Impunity and State Formation","author":"Marielle Debos","year":"2016","note":"The ethnography of Chad's métier des armes — the gun as profession and the state as its market."},{"work":"Historical Dictionary of Chad","author":"Mario Azevedo & Samuel Decalo","year":"2018 (4th ed.)","note":"The utile/inutile bifurcation and the rebellion-column pattern of power transfer."},{"work":"France's wars in Chad: Military intervention and decolonization in Africa","author":"Nathaniel K. Powell","year":"2020","note":"The Franco-Chadian garrison partnership — interventions 1968-90 as the keystone's maintenance."},{"work":"UN Panel of Experts on the Sudan (Chad corridor findings)","author":"UN Security Council","year":"2023-26","note":"Primary documentation of the Amdjarass supply route and UAE flights."}]},"patterns":["water-leverage"],"rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"A garrison-state built as a security subcontractor, discovering it has no other business model as its patron leaves.","alt":"An ethnic-patronage reading centers the Zaghawa elite's internal fracture over the Sudan war as the real instability, not the France-exit or the utile/inutile structure.","why":"Both operate; the platform holds the garrison-state frame for the deep pattern and the Zaghawa split for the current fracture. HIGH."},"refs":[{"t":"Critical Threats · Africa File (26 Feb 2026)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/drc-fardc-m23-russia-wagner-mali-burkina-niger-rsf-chad-sudan-fgs-somalia-ghana-aes-ecowas-africa-file-february-26-2026"},{"t":"ICG · On the Horizon: July-December 2026","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/oth/chad-cuba-horn-africa-pakistan/horizon-july-december-2026"},{"t":"AP: Chad to deploy 1,500 troops to Haiti","u":"https://apnews.com/article/chad-haiti-gang-violence-un-suppression-force-3c0fc858eec089354342858434d9c0d1"},{"t":"ICG On the Horizon: Chad and the Sudan war","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/oth/chad-cuba-horn-africa-pakistan/horizon-july-december-2026"},{"t":"UN Panel of Experts on Sudan (Chad corridor)","u":"https://www.un.org/securitycouncil/sanctions/1591/panel-of-experts"},{"t":"Crisis Group: Chad","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/central-africa/chad"}],"human":{"dead":3000,"disp":1900000,"famine":false,"aid":"severely underfunded","note":"Hosting ~1M Sudanese refugees on top of internal displacement and Lake Chad Basin violence — among the world's largest per-capita refugee burdens, in one of its poorest states.","est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude working figure — no single authoritative running count exists for this theater"},"checkmode":"SWEPT","escwhy":"76 = active armed conflict, a recorded human toll, trending UP over the last ten weeks. Composite judgement, not a formula — see the Methodology board for the rubric and its limits.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"21 Jul 2026","c":"Haiti deployment (1,500 troops); Wadi Fira well killings; GCAP sentencing — all added to timeline"}],"spill":[{"n":"Sudan","dir":"in","w":"RSF supply routes cross Chadian territory; eastern Chad hosts the refugee mass and the ICC evidence base.","id":"sudan"},{"n":"Nigeria / Niger / Cameroon","dir":"both","w":"The Multinational Joint Task Force has been WEAKENED BY CHAD AND NIGER'S WITHDRAWALS — sovereignty assertions dismantled the mechanism that made Lake Chad basin operations coordinated. Nigerian pressure in Borno may now displace militants onto Chadian shores."},{"n":"France","dir":"in","w":"ICG flags a possible RESUMPTION of security cooperation to restore surveillance and intelligence — the exit partially reversing under operational pressure."},{"n":"Haiti","dir":"out","w":"1,500 troops pledged to the Gang Suppression Force while a state of emergency runs in Lake province.","id":"haiti"}],"escalation":{"rung":"State of emergency, task force broken","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation → overstretch","up":["Boko Haram exploits the weakened MJTF","The army overstretched with limited intelligence","Sudan's war spilling across the eastern border","Lake Province emergency widening"],"down":["Resuming French surveillance cooperation (under consideration)","Rebuilding the multinational task force","Rainy-season attack lull through September","Regional coordination restored"],"fw":["resource-conflict","conflict-curve"]},"macro":{"growth":"Oil-dependent","infl":"Moderate","src":"Compiled reporting, 2026","note":"Oil revenue against a state of emergency in Lake province, a weakened Multinational Joint Task Force, and 1,500 troops pledged to Haiti. Chad exports security it cannot fund domestically — the clearest case on this board of a state monetising its army because it has little else to monetise."},"inherit":{"name":"The CFA Franc","est":"1945","status":"IN FORCE · CONTESTED","what":"Created by the French provisional government in December 1945 as the <b>franc des Colonies Françaises d'Afrique</b> — the franc of the French Colonies of Africa. It was not a neutral monetary tool: it was an administrative instrument for managing colonial economies in the service of Paris, imposed by requiring taxes to be paid in it and by banning the cowrie and manilla currencies that preceded it. Colonial administrators used legal sanction and physical force where the ban failed.","now":"<b>It still circulates, used by 227 million people across fourteen countries as of 2026</b>, in two zones — West and Central African — with the French Treasury and, through the euro peg, the European Central Bank retaining roles in its governance. The Tax Justice Network's assessment is that it 'still functions according to the same principles and purpose established during the colonial period'. <b>Fiscal policy is national; monetary power is 