y in the outcome.","econlev":[{"holder":"Donors and IMF","channel":"Aid and balance-of-payments support","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Among the world's most aid-dependent states, in a foreign exchange crisis severe enough to produce petroleum shortages affecting basic transport."},{"holder":"Burundi","channel":"Coffee and tea exports","exp":"MODERATE","detail":"The only substantial foreign-currency earners, in a landlocked economy dependent on the Tanzanian corridor."},{"holder":"Kinshasa","channel":"Military deployment","exp":"MODERATE","detail":"Burundi supplies soldiers it cannot afford to a war on another state's territory — an expeditionary commitment during a currency emergency."}],"external":[{"actor":"🇨🇩 DRC (Kinshasa)","side":"Ally","flow":"Burundian troops fight alongside the Congolese army and Wazalendo militias against M23 and the Twirwaneho","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇷🇼 Rwanda","side":"Adversary","flow":"Border closed over mutual accusations of backing each other's rebel movements; the Uvira offensive was assessed as partly aimed at removing Burundi from the war","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇪🇺 European Union","side":"Donor","flow":"Direct budget support suspended after the 2015 crisis, with partial re-engagement since 2020","tier":"confirmed"}],"gov":[{"src":"Critical Threats / CTP","detail":"Assessed that M23 and Rwanda launched the Uvira offensive at least partly to knock the Burundian military out of the war"},{"src":"UN Group of Experts on the DRC","detail":"Has reported on both Burundian and Rwandan allegations of cross-border rebel sponsorship, with varying confirmation"},{"src":"IOM","detail":"Displacement tracking at Rugombo and other Cibitoke transit sites receiving families fleeing the DRC"}],"rival":{"conf":"MODERATE","lens":"A poor frontline state conscripted into a resource war it has no stake in, paying in soldiers and currency for a Congolese government's survival.","alt":"Burundi has its own security rationale: RED-Tabara and other Burundian rebel groups operate from eastern DRC, and Bujumbura accuses Kigali of backing them. Its deployment protects a genuine interest, not merely Kinshasa's.","why":"MODERATE because both are true and the weighting is unclear. The rebel-sanctuary argument is real and documented; so is the fact that Burundi is bearing costs wildly out of proportion to its capacity."},"contested":{"summary":"Burundi and Rwanda accuse each other of sponsoring rebel movements, with limited independent verification available for either claim.","claims":[{"claim":"Rwanda backs Burundian rebel groups including RED-Tabara.","who":"The Government of Burundi.","evid":"Burundi has closed its border with Rwanda over the allegation and attributed attacks to Rwandan-backed forces. Kigali denies it and counter-accuses Bujumbura of hosting the FDLR. Independent verification is thin; the UN Group of Experts has reported on both sets of allegations with varying degrees of confirmation.","status":"MUTUALLY-ALLEGED"}]},"history":{"roots":"Belgian rule governed Burundi through the same Hutu-Tutsi administrative categories imposed on Rwanda, producing a post-independence sequence of coups and mass killings — including the 1972 genocide of Hutu elites and the 1993 assassination of the first elected Hutu president, which began a twelve-year civil war.","timeline":[{"date":"1962","e":"Independence from Belgian trusteeship with the ethnic administrative categories intact."},{"date":"1972","e":"Mass killing of Hutu elites by the Tutsi-dominated state; estimates run into the hundreds of thousands."},{"date":"1993","e":"Assassination of President Melchior Ndadaye, the first elected Hutu head of state, triggers civil war."},{"date":"2000-2005","e":"Arusha Accords and transition; CNDD-FDD enters government."},{"date":"2015","e":"Nkurunziza's third-term bid kills hundreds and drives over 400,000 into exile; the EU suspends direct budget support."},{"date":"2020","e":"Ndayishimiye succeeds Nkurunziza; partial international re-engagement."},{"date":"2023-2025","e":"Burundian troops deploy to eastern DRC alongside the Congolese army; the Rwanda border closes over mutual rebel-sponsorship accusations."},{"date":"Dec 2025","e":"Uvira falls to M23 — 400 killed, 200,000 displaced; Congolese and Burundian troops retreat into Burundi."},{"date":"2026","e":"Foreign exchange crisis and severe petroleum shortages while the deployment continues."}],"actors":[{"name":"Évariste Ndayishimiye","bio":"President since 2020, sustaining an expeditionary war during a currency and fuel crisis."},{"name":"FDNB","bio":"Deployed against M23 and the Twirwaneho in South Kivu, alongside Congolese forces and Wazalendo militias."},{"name":"RED-Tabara","bio":"Burundian rebel movement operating from eastern DRC, which Bujumbura says Rwanda backs — Kigali denies it."}],"bridge":"Belgium imposed the same ethnic administration here as in Rwanda and got the mirror outcome — a Tutsi-dominated state killing Hutu elites rather than the reverse. Both countries have spent sixty years fighting the categories a colonial census created. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: Burundi fights Rwanda's proxy in a third country's war, receives the refugees it produces, and cannot buy fuel.","sources":[{"t":"Critical Threats · Deadlock in the DRC","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/deadlock-in-the-drc-rwanda-m23-fdlr-doha-africa-file"},{"t":"ICG · Burundi","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/great-lakes/burundi"}]},"refs":[{"t":"Critical Threats · Deadlock in the DRC (Rwanda/M23/FDLR/Doha)","u":"https://www.criticalthreats.org/analysis/deadlock-in-the-drc-rwanda-m23-fdlr-doha-africa-file"},{"t":"Al Jazeera · Uvira offensive and Burundi","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2025/12/9/drc-accuses-rwanda-of-peace-deal-violations-as-m23-advances-in-the-east"},{"t":"ICG · Great Lakes","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/great-lakes"},{"t":"IOM · displacement tracking, Cibitoke transit sites","u":"https://www.iom.int/countries/burundi"},{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"}],"spill":[{"n":"DRC","dir":"both","w":"Troops fight alongside the Congolese army; Burundian rebel groups including RED-Tabara operate from Congolese territory. Both directions at once.","id":"drc"},{"n":"Rwanda","dir":"both","w":"Border closed over mutual accusations of backing each other's rebels. The Uvira offensive was assessed as partly aimed at removing Burundi from the war.","id":"rwanda"},{"n":"Tanzania","dir":"out","w":"Refugee flows and the main trade corridor to the coast — the lifeline during the fuel crisis."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Expeditionary war during economic collapse","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation → overexposure","up":["M23/Rwanda offensives aimed at knocking Burundi out","The forex and fuel crisis undercutting the deployment","RED-Tabara/FDLR mutual-sponsorship accusations with Rwanda","M23 at the border after Uvira"],"down":["Withdrawal from the DRC deployment it cannot afford","A Rwanda-Burundi de-escalation on rebel sponsorship","Economic stabilisation via the Tanzania corridor","A wider Great Lakes settlement"],"fw":["ethnic-security","security-dilemma","proxy"]},"escwhy":"66 = expeditionary belligerent under acute economic stress (base ~60) raised for sustained deployment against a better-armed adversary on Congolese soil, M23 positioned at the border after Uvira, mutual rebel-sponsorship accusations with Rwanda, and a foreign-exchange and fuel crisis severe enough to affect basic transport. Not higher because there is no fighting inside Burundi and the state retains control of its own territory; not lower because it is the participant least able to absorb the costs it is currently carrying. · TRAJECTORY IS RECONSTRUCTED, NOT RECORDED: this theater was added to the board on 22 Jul 2026, so it has no observed ten-period history. The chart is a backward estimate of where the score would have sat and is marked EST accordingly — it should not be read as this platform having tracked the trend.","macro":{"growth":"Constrained by crisis","infl":"Foreign-exchange and fuel crisis","src":"Reporting, 2026","note":"A forex crisis severe enough to produce petroleum shortages affecting basic transport, while sustaining an expeditionary deployment in eastern DRC. Coffee and tea are the only substantial foreign-currency earners, and the Tanzania corridor is the lifeline."},"hist_real":false,"since":"1993","inherit":{"name":"The Same Belgian Identity Cards, Mirror Outcome","est":"1933","status":"ABOLISHED · CONSEQUENCES IN FORCE","what":"Belgium applied the identical census and identity-card system it used in Rwanda, fixing Hutu, Tutsi and Twa as permanent administrative categories and governing through a Tutsi-dominated aristocracy. <b>The two territories were run as one administrative unit — Ruanda-Urundi — by the same officials with the same theory.</b>","now":"<b>The mirror is the point.</b> Rwanda's post-independence violence ran Hutu-majority state against Tutsi; Burundi's ran Tutsi-dominated state against Hutu — the 1972 killings of Hutu elites, the 1993 assassination of the first elected Hutu president, a twelve-year civil war. Same instrument, inverted outcome, which is the strongest available evidence that the categories were the cause rather than any inherent relationship between the groups. <b>Today Burundi fights Rwanda's proxy in the DRC while both accuse each other of hosting the other's rebels.</b>","change":"The Arusha Accords built ethnic quotas into the constitution as an explicit answer. <b>Burundi is now sustaining an expeditionary war during a foreign-exchange and fuel crisis</b> — the participant least able to bear the costs it is carrying."},"monitors":[{"t":"ACLED · conflict event data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}]},{"region":"Africa","tclass":"conflict","domain_tag":"Military","trend":"flat","as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"24 Jul 2026","checkmode":"CHECKED","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"24 Jul 2026","c":"Theater created. CAR was referenced across the Sudan, Chad and Rwanda files as a Wagner/Africa Corps node but had no file of its own — a whole conflict the board could not see."}],"id":"car","cat":"military","subregion":"Central Africa","name":"Central African Republic","sub":"A Russian security contract with a state attached","flag":"🇨🇫","lat":6.61,"lon":20.94,"esc":64,"sev":"elevated","status":"ongoing","tier":"ELEVATED","domain":"Resource-for-Security / Wagner Governance","esc_history":[62,63,63,64,64,63,64,64,64,64],"note":"2026 — THE PROTOTYPE FOR THE MODEL THE PLATFORM TRACKS EVERYWHERE ELSE: CAR is where the Russian security-for-minerals arrangement was first proven at national scale. Wagner — now Africa Corps under Russian state control — provides regime protection for President TOUADÉRA in exchange for mining concessions (gold at Ndassima, diamonds, timber). Touadéra removed presidential term limits by December 2023 referendum, clearing the way for indefinite rule under Russian guarantee · the December 2025 elections returned him amid the post-electoral assessment the AU Peace and Security Council is now conducting · the 2019 Khartoum peace agreement is largely hollow; the CPC rebel coalition retains rural presence while the state and its Russian contractors hold the capital and the mines · Sudan's war on the northern border adds refugee flows and arms","leverage":[{"actor":"Touadéra government","asset":"Mining concessions — gold, diamonds, timber — traded for regime survival","str":"md"},{"actor":"Russia (Africa Corps)","asset":"The security guarantee that keeps the government in office, priced in mineral rights","str":"hi"},{"actor":"CPC rebels","asset":"Rural territorial presence and the ability to threaten the roads","str":"md"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Russia","channel":"Mining concessions for security","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Africa Corps is paid in resource rights rather than cash — the Ndassima gold mine the clearest case. The state's protection is financed by selling the state's minerals: the exact model the Mali and Mozambique files track, proven here first."},{"holder":"Touadéra government","channel":"Customs and concession revenue","exp":"MODERATE","detail":"What the state collects beyond the Russian arrangement, in the territory it controls."}],"external":[{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"Regime guarantor","flow":"Africa Corps provides protection and training; paid in mining concessions; CAR is the template for the Sahel deployments","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇫🇷 France","side":"Withdrawn former patron","flow":"Ended its military presence as Russian influence displaced it — the reversal the Sahel later repeated","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇷🇼 Rwanda","side":"Troop contributor","flow":"Rwandan forces deployed alongside the arrangement — the security-export business again","tier":"confirmed"}],"gov":[{"src":"UN Panel of Experts on CAR","detail":"Documented Wagner/Africa Corps abuses and the resource-for-security arrangement"},{"src":"AU Peace and Security Council","detail":"Conducting a post-electoral assessment following the December 2025 polls"},{"src":"The Sentry / investigative reporting","detail":"Traced the Ndassima gold operation and the financial structure of the Russian presence"}],"mil":"State security forces (FACA) rebuilt with Russian training; Africa Corps as the decisive force; the CPC rebel coalition in the interior; assorted ex-Séléka and anti-balaka remnants.","pol":"Touadéra, re-elected December 2025 after removing term limits by 2023 referendum, governing under Russian security guarantee.","eco":"Gold (Ndassima), diamonds, timber, and subsistence agriculture — one of the world's poorest states sitting on the resources that pay for its protection.","assess":"THE ORIGINAL. Every resource-for-security arrangement this platform tracks in Mali, Burkina, Libya and Mozambique was prototyped here. CAR is where Russia proved that a government could be kept in office indefinitely in exchange for the right to extract — France displaced, term limits removed, elections held under guarantee. It is the least-watched theater with the most explanatory power for the model running across the continent.","rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"A captured state — sovereignty leased to a foreign security contractor in exchange for the extraction rights that fund the lease. The government governs at Moscow's pleasure and pays in minerals.","alt":"A weak state that made a rational choice: facing rebel coalitions that nearly took the capital in 2021, Touadéra took the security guarantee available when France would not provide one. The arrangement is exploitative but it is also the reason the state exists.","why":"Held at HIGH because the resource-for-security structure is thoroughly documented (UN Panel, The Sentry) and openly operating. The 'rational choice' reading explains why CAR accepted it, not whether it is extractive — it plainly is."},"contested":{"summary":"CAR's Russian arrangement is disputed less on the facts than on the framing — partnership or capture.","claims":[{"claim":"The Russian presence is a sovereign partnership freely chosen by CAR.","who":"The Touadéra government and Moscow.","evid":"CAR did request the deployment, and it did stabilise the capital after 2021. But the UN Panel of Experts and investigative reporting document mining concessions granted in exchange, abuses by the contractors, and the removal of term limits under the arrangement — which makes 'partnership' an incomplete description of a relationship in which one party guarantees the other's hold on power and is paid in the country's resources.","status":"PARTNERSHIP-IS-INCOMPLETE"}]},"human":{"dead":6000,"disp":1100000,"famine":false,"est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude. The 2013-16 civil war killed several thousand; displacement runs to roughly a fifth of the population across IDPs and refugees.","note":"Hosts and produces refugees across the Sudan, Chad and DRC borders."},"history":{"roots":"A French colony (Oubangui-Chari) whose extractive administration built almost no state; independence in 1960 was followed by Bokassa's self-coronation as emperor, decades of coups, and the collapse into the 2013 Séléka/anti-balaka civil war along Muslim-Christian lines that neither side has resolved.","timeline":[{"date":"1960","e":"Independence from France with minimal state infrastructure inherited from a purely extractive colonial administration."},{"date":"1976-1979","e":"Jean-Bédel Bokassa crowns himself emperor in a French-funded ceremony; deposed with French help."},{"date":"2013","e":"The mostly-Muslim Séléka coalition takes Bangui; anti-balaka militias form in response; the civil war kills thousands along sectarian lines."},{"date":"2018","e":"Russian Wagner personnel arrive as military instructors — the beginning of the arrangement."},{"date":"2021","e":"Rebel coalition nearly takes Bangui; Wagner and Rwandan troops repel it, cementing the Russian role."},{"date":"Jul 2023","e":"Referendum removes presidential term limits, clearing the way for indefinite Touadéra rule under Russian guarantee."},{"date":"Dec 2025","e":"Touadéra re-elected; the AU opens a post-electoral assessment."},{"date":"2026","e":"Africa Corps consolidates the former Wagner structure under Russian state control; the Ndassima gold operation continues; Sudan's war adds border pressure."}],"actors":[{"name":"Faustin-Archange Touadéra","bio":"President since 2016, re-elected 2025 after removing term limits; governs under Russian security guarantee."},{"name":"Africa Corps (ex-Wagner)","bio":"The decisive military force and the regime's guarantor, paid in mining concessions."},{"name":"CPC (Coalition of Patriots for Change)","bio":"The rebel coalition that nearly took Bangui in 2021; retains rural presence."}],"bridge":"French colonialism built an extractive shell with no state inside it, and every ruler since has borrowed foreign force to hold a centre the periphery never accepted — Bokassa's France, Touadéra's Russia. · AND NOW, 2026: the CAR model — regime protection for mining rights, term limits removed under guarantee — is the template Russia exported to the Sahel, which makes this forgotten country the most instructive theater on the continent.","sources":[{"t":"UN Panel of Experts on the CAR","u":"https://www.un.org/securitycouncil/sanctions/2127/panel-of-experts"},{"t":"The Sentry · CAR investigations","u":"https://thesentry.org/"}]},"refs":[{"t":"UN Panel of Experts on the CAR","u":"https://www.un.org/securitycouncil/sanctions/2127/panel-of-experts"},{"t":"ICG · Central African Republic","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/central-africa/central-african-republic"},{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"},{"t":"CFR · Global Conflict Tracker","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker"}],"escwhy":"64 = lower-intensity armed conflict (base) with a captured-state political structure, continued displacement of ~20% of the population, and a documented foreign security arrangement. Not higher because large-scale fighting has subsided since 2021; not lower because the state's sovereignty is materially compromised. · TRAJECTORY IS RECONSTRUCTED, NOT RECORDED: this theater was added to the board on 24 Jul 2026, so it has no observed ten-period history. The chart is a backward estimate of where the score would have sat and is marked EST accordingly — it should not be read as this platform having tracked the trend.","spill":[{"n":"Sudan","dir":"in","w":"Sudan's war drives refugees and arms across the northern border; RSF-linked movement in the frontier zone.","id":"sudan"},{"n":"Russia","dir":"in","w":"Africa Corps regime guarantee, paid in mining concessions — the template later exported to Mali, Burkina and Libya.","id":"russia-domestic"},{"n":"Chad / DRC","dir":"both","w":"Shared borders with refugee flows both directions; the CPC's rear areas and cross-border trade."},{"n":"Rwanda","dir":"in","w":"Rwandan troops deployed alongside the arrangement — the security-export business operating here too.","id":"rwanda"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Captured state under Russian guarantee","dir":"stalemate","phase":"Frozen under external force","up":["The CPC rebellion resurging in the interior","Sudan's war spilling across the northern border","The Russian extraction arrangement deepening","Anti-balaka/CPC communal violence reigniting"],"down":["A genuine political settlement beyond the hollow Khartoum deal","Reducing the resource-for-security dependence","Reconciliation the Russian guns have not bought","Regional stabilisation of the borders"],"fw":["resource-conflict","proxy","ethnic-security"]},"hist_real":false,"since":"2013","inherit":{"name":"The CFA Franc","est":"1945","status":"IN FORCE · CONTESTED","what":"Created by the French provisional government in December 1945 as the <b>franc des Colonies Françaises d'Afrique</b> — the franc of the French Colonies of Africa. It was not a neutral monetary tool: it was an administrative instrument for managing colonial economies in the service of Paris, imposed by requiring taxes to be paid in it and by banning the cowrie and manilla currencies that preceded it. Colonial administrators used legal sanction and physical force where the ban failed.","now":"<b>It still circulates, used by 227 million people across fourteen countries as of 2026</b>, in two zones — West and Central African — with the French Treasury and, through the euro peg, the European Central Bank retaining roles in its governance. The Tax Justice Network's assessment is that it 'still functions according to the same principles and purpose established during the colonial period'. <b>Fiscal policy is national; monetary power is outsourced.</b> The 1994 devaluation, decided at a late-night meeting in Dakar, halved the savings of millions overnight at a rate nobody affected had voted on.","change":"France agreed in May 2020 to end its engagement in the West African zone and the reserve-deposit requirement, with the replacement currency — the <b>eco</b> — due to launch. <b>The launch has now been postponed five times; the current target is July 2027.</b> CAR is in the Central African zone, so the gold and diamonds paid to Africa Corps leave a country whose currency is guaranteed by the state whose forces Russia displaced."},"monitors":[{"t":"The Sentry · Russia/Wagner in CAR","u":"https://thesentry.org/"},{"t":"ACLED · CAR conflict data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}],"macrona":"NOT YET SOURCED. The economy is substantially the resource-for-security arrangement itself — gold, diamonds and timber traded for regime protection — which the inheritance and econlev fields describe directly."},{"region":"Africa","tclass":"conflict","domain_tag":"Military","trend":"up","as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"24 Jul 2026","checkmode":"CHECKED","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"24 Jul 2026","c":"Theater created. South Sudan was referenced across the Sudan and Uganda files as a spillover node but had no file of its own — despite having slid back into civil war in early 2026."}],"id":"south-sudan","cat":"military","subregion":"East Africa","name":"South Sudan","sub":"The peace deal is dead and the war is back","flag":"🇸🇸","lat":6.88,"lon":31.31,"esc":80,"sev":"high","status":"escalating","tier":"SEVERE","domain":"Civil War / Succession Struggle","esc_history":[62,64,66,70,74,76,78,79,80,80],"note":"2026 — BACK TO CIVIL WAR: the 2018 peace agreement that ended the 2013-18 war is DEAD. In MARCH 2025 President SALVA KIIR (Dinka) placed First Vice President RIEK MACHAR (Nuer) under HOUSE ARREST and charged him with TREASON and crimes against humanity; Machar's SPLM-IO formally declared the R-ARCSS DEFUNCT · the NUER WHITE ARMY overran the SSPDF barracks at NASIR (Upper Nile) in March 2025; government and UGANDAN airstrikes followed, and a UN helicopter extraction was shot down, killing an SSPDF general and dozens · on 27 JAN 2026 the SSPDF launched OPERATION ENDURING PEACE — a large-scale offensive in JONGLEI; a UN commission warns the country is moving to FULL-SCALE WAR · the trigger is succession: Kiir is ailing, and the crackdown is read as clearing rivals before a fight to replace him · elections, postponed from 2015 → 2023 → Dec 2026, may not hold · THE SUDAN LINK IS DIRECT: the RSF's Jan 2026 push into Blue Nile was launched from South Sudanese territory, and Juba's oil — its only real revenue — flows through pipelines running north through war-torn Sudan","leverage":[{"actor":"Kiir government (SPLM)","asset":"Control of the oil revenue and the state security apparatus, backed by Uganda","str":"md"},{"actor":"Machar / SPLM-IO","asset":"Nuer mobilisation and the White Army's territorial reach in the northeast","str":"md"},{"actor":"Uganda","asset":"The external force that has repeatedly kept Kiir in power","str":"hi"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"South Sudan","channel":"Oil exports through Sudan","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Oil is nearly the entire state revenue — and the pipelines run north through Sudan's war zone. Juba's economy is hostage to a conflict on its border; damage to the pipeline or a Sudanese decision to choke it collapses the budget."},{"holder":"Kiir government","channel":"Oil-backed patronage","exp":"HIGH","detail":"The revenue funds the patronage network that holds the SPLM together — which is why the succession fight is also a fight over who controls the oil money."}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇬 Uganda","side":"Backs Kiir","flow":"Ugandan forces conducted airstrikes supporting the SSPDF at Nasir; Kampala has repeatedly intervened to keep Kiir in power since 2013","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇸🇩 Sudan (SAF)","side":"Historic Machar tie / pipeline leverage","flow":"Burhan pressures Juba to distance itself from the RSF; the RSF's Blue Nile push came from South Sudanese soil; the oil pipeline is the shared dependency","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇪🇺 European Union / IGAD","side":"Mediators","flow":"ICG's Watch List urges EU action to halt regional spillover; IGAD's mediation has not restored the unity government","tier":"confirmed"}],"gov":[{"src":"UN Commission on Human Rights in South Sudan","detail":"Warned in March 2026 that the peace deal is collapsing toward full-scale war"},{"src":"International Crisis Group","detail":"'South Sudan is sliding back into civil war' — attributes it to Kiir abandoning the 2018 agreement for a military strategy"},{"src":"CFR Global Conflict Tracker","detail":"Documents Machar's arrest, the White Army offensive, and the derailed transition"}],"mil":"SSPDF (Kiir's national army, Dinka-dominated) with allied Agwelek militia and Ugandan air support; against the SPLM-IO and the Nuer White Army, an ethnic community militia loosely aligned with Machar. Fighting concentrated in Upper Nile and Jonglei.","pol":"Kiir has ruled since independence in 2011 with no elections ever held; Machar arrested and on trial; the unity government dismantled. Succession jockeying around Kiir's health is the engine.","eco":"Oil is nearly the entire economy and it flows through Sudan — one of the world's poorest and most oil-dependent states, with revenue hostage to a neighbour's war.","assess":"The 2018 agreement was a tactical compromise forced on Kiir by regional pressure rather than a reconciliation, and when the regional balance shifted he abandoned it. The country is back to the Dinka-Nuer fault line it was born with, now sharpened by a succession vacuum around Kiir's health and welded to Sudan's war through oil pipelines and RSF movement across the border. It is the newest full-scale war on this board and among the most likely to worsen.","rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"An elite succession struggle wearing an ethnic mask — Kiir clearing rivals before a fight to replace him, mobilising Dinka-Nuer identity to fund a contest that is really about who controls the oil patronage.","alt":"A genuine ethno-political conflict with deep roots: the Dinka-Nuer cleavage predates Kiir and Machar, the White Army mobilises autonomously on Nuer grievance, and local violence runs largely independent of the two leaders' rivalry.","why":"Held at HIGH for the elite-succession reading because the 2025-26 escalation tracks Kiir's health and his moves against rivals precisely. But the alternative is not wrong — the communal violence has its own logic, which is why the war is so hard to switch off from the top."},"contested":{"summary":"Responsibility for the collapse of the peace deal is disputed along the same lines as the war itself.","claims":[{"claim":"Machar and the SPLM-IO broke the peace by backing the Nasir uprising.","who":"The Kiir government.","evid":"Juba accuses the SPLM-IO of supporting the Nuer White Army's assault on Nasir and charged Machar with treason on that basis. But the White Army mobilises substantially on its own, and independent analysts including ICG read Kiir's crackdown — the house arrest, the arrests of opposition figures — as the proximate cause of the collapse, connected to his succession manoeuvring rather than to an IO offensive.","status":"CONTESTED-AND-SELF-SERVING"}]},"human":{"dead":400000,"disp":4000000,"famine":true,"est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude. The 2013-18 civil war is estimated at ~400,000 excess deaths (LSHTM-type modelling); displacement combines refugees and IDPs. The 2025-26 relapse is adding to both and is not separately counted here.","note":"The renewed war is disrupting humanitarian access; South Sudan also hosts returnees and refugees from Sudan's war on its northern border."},"history":{"roots":"Sudan's British-Egyptian condominium governed the Christian-animist south separately from the Arab-Muslim north, then handed both to Khartoum at independence — producing two civil wars and, in 2011, the world's newest state. Independence did not resolve the internal Dinka-Nuer contest for control of it, which erupted into civil war within two years.","timeline":[{"date":"2011","e":"Independence — 98.3% voted to secede from Sudan; no national elections have been held since."},{"date":"2013","e":"Kiir-Machar power struggle erupts into civil war along Dinka-Nuer lines; Uganda backs Kiir, Sudan favours Machar."},{"date":"2018","e":"The Revitalized Agreement (R-ARCSS) ends the war after ~400,000 dead and half the population displaced; Machar returns as First Vice President."},{"date":"Mar 2025","e":"Kiir places Machar under house arrest and charges him with treason; the Nuer White Army overruns the SSPDF barracks at Nasir; a UN helicopter is shot down."},{"date":"Late 2025","e":"Fighting escalates in Jonglei; the SPLM-IO declares the peace agreement defunct."},{"date":"27 Jan 2026","e":"The SSPDF launches Operation Enduring Peace, a large-scale offensive in Jonglei."},{"date":"Mar 2026","e":"A UN commission warns the country is moving toward full-scale war."},{"date":"Dec 2026","e":"Elections — postponed since 2015 — are scheduled but may not hold."}],"actors":[{"name":"Salva Kiir Mayardit","bio":"President since independence (2011), Dinka; abandoned the 2018 peace deal and jailed his deputy. Ailing health drives the succession struggle."},{"name":"Riek Machar","bio":"First Vice President and SPLM-IO leader, Nuer; under arrest and on trial for treason, which dismantled the unity government."},{"name":"Nuer White Army","bio":"An ethnic Nuer community militia loosely aligned with Machar; overran Nasir and drives the northeastern fighting largely autonomously."}],"bridge":"Britain ruled the south as a separate, neglected appendage of Sudan and then left it inside a state it never accepted — two civil wars later it became its own country, and inherited the Dinka-Nuer contest colonialism had never required anyone to resolve. · AND NOW, 2026: the peace deal is dead, Machar is in a cell, the White Army is in the field, and the oil that funds the whole fight flows out through the war next door in Sudan.","sources":[{"t":"International Crisis Group · Halting South Sudan's Slide into War","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/cmt/africa/south-sudan/halting-south-sudans-slide-war"},{"t":"CFR · Global Conflict Tracker: South Sudan","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/civil-war-south-sudan"}]},"refs":[{"t":"International Crisis Group · South Sudan","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/africa/east-and-horn-africa/south-sudan"},{"t":"CFR · Global Conflict Tracker: South Sudan","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker/conflict/civil-war-south-sudan"},{"t":"UN Commission on Human Rights in South Sudan","u":"https://www.ohchr.org/en/hr-bodies/hrc/co-h-south-sudan/index"},{"t":"International Crisis Group · CrisisWatch","u":"https://www.crisisgroup.org/crisiswatch"},{"t":"CFR · Global Conflict Tracker","u":"https://www.cfr.org/global-conflict-tracker"}],"escwhy":"80 = active civil war (base ~75) with a collapsed peace agreement, a large-scale government offensive under way, direct entanglement in Sudan's war, and famine conditions in conflict zones. Not higher because full-scale nationwide fighting has not yet resumed at 2013-18 intensity; the trajectory is upward. · TRAJECTORY IS RECONSTRUCTED, NOT RECORDED: this theater was added to the board on 24 Jul 2026, so it has no observed ten-period history. The chart is a backward estimate of where the score would have sat and is marked EST accordingly — it should not be read as this platform having tracked the trend.","spill":[{"n":"Sudan","dir":"both","w":"The RSF's Jan 2026 Blue Nile push was launched FROM South Sudanese territory; Juba's oil pipeline runs north through Sudan's war; Burhan pressures Kiir to distance himself from the RSF. Each war is the other's rear area.","id":"sudan"},{"n":"Uganda","dir":"in","w":"Ugandan forces conducted airstrikes backing the SSPDF at Nasir; Kampala has repeatedly intervened to keep Kiir in power since 2013.","id":"uganda"},{"n":"Ethiopia / Kenya","dir":"out","w":"Refugee flows and the cross-border Nuer and other communities; regional mediation through IGAD."},{"n":"DRC","dir":"out","w":"Part of the wider Central African instability belt; arms and fighters move across the porous borders.","id":"drc"}],"escalation":{"rung":"Peace deal dead, government offensive under way","dir":"climbing","phase":"Emergence → escalation (relapse)","up":["Operation Enduring Peace widens in Jonglei","A battle for Malakal triggering communal massacre","Kiir's succession vacuum sharpening the elite fight","The White Army acting autonomously of any ceasefire"],"down":["A mutually hurting stalemate the exhausted 2018 war produced once","Machar's release restoring a negotiating counterpart","IGAD/EU pressure rebuilding the unity government","Decoupling from Sudan's war and the oil-pipeline hostage dynamic"],"fw":["ethnic-security","conflict-curve","proxy"]},"macro":{"growth":"4.1% (2026f) · GDP $6.0B nominal","infl":"65.7% (2025f)","src":"IMF / national data via compiled statistics, 2026","note":"GDP per capita $369 — 188th in the world — with 76% below the poverty line. Exports run 50.2% to CHINA and 28.7% to SINGAPORE; imports 33.2% from UGANDA. This is the fiscal reality behind the relapse into civil war: a state with almost no revenue base outside oil that must move through Sudan's war zone, inflation near 66%, and a succession fight over who controls what little there is."},"hist_real":false,"since":"2013","inherit":{"name":"The Southern Policy and Closed Districts","est":"1922-1946","status":"REVERSED · CONSEQUENCES IN FORCE","what":"Britain administered southern Sudan separately under the <b>Closed Districts Ordinance</b>: northerners required permits to enter, Arabic and Islam were discouraged, missionaries ran what education existed, and almost no investment was made. The stated intent was protection from the slave trade; the effect was that when Britain abruptly reversed course in 1946 and handed the south to Khartoum, <b>the south entered independence with almost no educated administrators and no stake in the state it had been placed inside.</b>","now":"<b>That deficit has never been closed.</b> Two civil wars and independence in 2011 produced a state with almost no revenue base outside oil — which must flow north through Sudan's war zone — GDP per capita of $369, 76% below the poverty line, and inflation near 66%. The 2018 peace agreement required merging government and opposition forces into one national army and it never happened, so the security sector remains a patronage system rather than an institution. <b>The war restarted in 2025.</b>","change":"Elections have been postponed from 2015 to 2023 to December 2026 and may not hold. <b>Machar is jailed, the Nuer White Army moves independently of anyone, and Eritrea and the SAF are positioning behind a related campaign next door.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"ACLED · South Sudan conflict data","u":"https://acleddata.com/"}],"ctrl":{"gov":60,"contested":25,"other":15,"est":true,"src":"Composite of UN and open-source reporting, 2026","note":"SSPDF holds Juba and the oil regions. SPLM-IO and the Nuer White Army contest Upper Nile and Jonglei; Operation Enduring Peace is the current government offensive."}},{"id":"china","cat":"strategic","region":"Asia","subregion":"East Asia","tclass":"pressure","name":"China — Powerful and Vulnerable at Once","sub":"The most-referenced actor on this board, and the one it could not see","flag":"🇨🇳","lat":39.9,"lon":116.4,"esc":68,"sev":"elevated","status":"ongoing","tier":"ELEVATED","domain":"Systemic Power / Structural Economic Strain","domain_tag":"Strategic","trend":"flat","esc_history":[64,65,65,66,66,67,67,68,68,68],"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"26 Jul 2026","checkmode":"CHECKED","note":"JUL 2026 — A RAIL-SEA CORRIDOR TO AFRICA: China has officially launched its FIRST INTERMODAL RAIL-SEA TRADE CORRIDOR connecting XINJIANG WITH AFRICA under the Belt and Road Initiative · the significance for this board is the routing — an inland western province connected directly to African markets, which builds an export channel that does not depend on the contested eastern maritime approaches this platform tracks · JUL 2026 — THE GROWTH NUMBER AND THE DOCTRINE: Q2 GDP growth came in at 4.3% — a THREE-AND-A-HALF-YEAR LOW, below the 4.5-5% target band; H1 cumulative 4.7% · the July POLITBURO discussed ACCELERATING BOND ISSUANCE for infrastructure stimulus (FT), and on 13 JULY the State Council unveiled the '15TH FIVE-YEAR PLAN FOR EXPANDING CONSUMPTION' — the FIRST TIME consumption has been written into a national five-year plan · YOUTH UNEMPLOYMENT NEAR 20%, feeding the 'rùn' emigration culture · on 30 JUN-1 JUL Xi cast the Party as a force for global prosperity 'BACKED BY A STRONG MILITARY', signalling ambition for China to stand at the fore of global affairs · THE DOCTRINE NAMES THIS PLATFORM'S OWN FRAMEWORK: Xi's stated strategy is to avoid TWO TRAPS — the MIDDLE-INCOME TRAP (requiring ~5% annual growth) and the THUCYDIDES TRAP (avoiding direct conflict with the United States). He raised the second by name with Trump at the 14 May summit. The framework on the Frameworks board is not an analyst's imposition here; it is Beijing's own vocabulary · elite politics are tightly managed ahead of the 2027 PARTY CONGRESS and Xi's expected fourth term as General Secretary (2027-2032); purges in the military-industrial complex have intensified","leverage":[{"actor":"China (state)","asset":"Manufacturing and supply-chain dominance — the capacity other economies cannot replace at speed","str":"hi"},{"actor":"China (state)","asset":"Creditor position and Belt-and-Road infrastructure across Africa, Central Asia and Southeast Asia","str":"hi"},{"actor":"China (state)","asset":"Mediation as leverage — brokering in Myanmar, Thailand-Cambodia and Pakistan-Afghanistan buys standing the West is losing","str":"md"},{"actor":"Beijing's counterparties","asset":"Market access and the hedging space that lets third states play Washington against Beijing","str":"md"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"China","channel":"Supply-chain and processing chokeholds","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Rare-earth processing, battery and cleantech manufacturing, and pharmaceutical inputs — including more clinical trials now conducted than in the United States. The leverage is not raw material but the refining and assembly step other economies did not build."},{"holder":"China","channel":"Creditor and infrastructure position","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Belt-and-Road lending across the theaters on this board — Uganda, Kazakhstan, Pakistan, Myanmar — which converts into political standing less conditioned than Western aid."},{"holder":"China (internal)","channel":"Property debt and local-government finance","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Property triage prevents crisis rather than resolving it; local debt is rolled over and supported by central transfers. Analysts frame the base case as the economy 'muddling through' — contingent on Xi's health holding, US competition staying bounded, and local finances not collapsing."},{"holder":"China (internal)","channel":"Consumption weakness","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Growth is increasingly state-directed — adding output without commensurate productivity gains. The consumption five-year plan is the first formal admission that demand is the binding constraint."}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 United States","side":"Systemic rival","flow":"The Thucydides counterparty. The 14 May summit produced no agreement on Taiwan or Iran; tariff and technology competition continues","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇷🇺 Russia","side":"Aligned partner","flow":"Joint Sea-2026 naval exercise off Qingdao (6-13 Jul), a joint Pacific patrol, and a joint aerial patrol that entered South Korea's ADIZ","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇲🇲 Myanmar junta","side":"Client under pressure","flow":"Pressured the MNDAA to return Lashio and brokered its turn on the TNLA; the junta has signed across all four Global Initiatives","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇪🇺 Europe","side":"Hedging counterparties","flow":"Xi hosting France, Ireland and South Korea with Canada and Germany expected — Beijing betting that third-state hedging tilts its way","tier":"confirmed"}],"gov":[{"src":"National Bureau of Statistics (via reporting)","detail":"Q2 2026 GDP growth 4.3%, a three-and-a-half-year low; H1 cumulative 4.7%, below the 4.5-5% target band"},{"src":"State Council","detail":"15th Five-Year Plan for Expanding Consumption published 13 July 2026 — first inclusion of consumption in a national five-year plan"},{"src":"Asia Society Policy Institute","detail":"Assesses the core paradox as dynamism-versus-control, with 'resilience in select sectors, but fragility in the broader system'"}],"mil":"The PLA, undergoing intensified purges in the military-industrial complex while conducting joint operations with Russia — Joint Sea-2026 off Qingdao, a follow-on Pacific patrol, and a joint aerial patrol into South Korea's ADIZ. Grey-zone pressure is the standing instrument in the Taiwan Strait and South China Sea rather than open force.","pol":"Xi Jinping's governance model — Party control, national security, technological self-reliance — with elite politics tightly managed ahead of the 2027 Party Congress and an expected fourth term as General Secretary (2027-2032). The 15th Five-Year Plan (2026-2030) carries his personal imprint.","eco":"The world's second-largest economy, expanding but with growth increasingly state-directed. Q2 at 4.3%, youth unemployment near 20%, a persistent property debt crisis, industrial overcapacity, and a trade surplus large enough to provoke protectionist responses across its export markets.","assess":"China appeared 27 times across other files on this board before it had a file of its own — mediating in Myanmar and Thailand-Cambodia, financing Uganda and Kazakhstan, patrolling with Russia off Korea, and standing as the Thucydides counterparty in three Pacific theaters. The asymmetry mattered: without a China file, every Chinese action read as strategy rather than as a choice made under constraint. The constraints are real. A 4.3% quarter, roughly 20% youth unemployment, unresolved property debt and a managed succession are the conditions under which Beijing's external moves are actually being taken.","rival":{"conf":"MODERATE","lens":"A rising power converting manufacturing dominance and creditor position into political standing — buying mediator roles where the West has spent its credibility, and building the supply-chain chokeholds that make coercion cheap.","alt":"A system managing serious internal fragility whose external assertiveness is substantially defensive: growth below target, youth unemployment near 20%, property debt unresolved, and a leadership prioritising control precisely because it fears the erosion of it. On this reading Beijing's outreach is about absorbing overcapacity and building hedging partners against Western pressure, not projecting confidence.","why":"Held at MODERATE deliberately. The capability build-out is documented and real; so is the economic strain. Which one drives Chinese behaviour is the single most consequential open question on this board, and analysts who are confident either way have usually chosen a conclusion first."},"contested":{"summary":"Chinese economic data and strategic intent are both contested — and the disputes are structurally similar, in that every party quoting a figure has a position it supports.","claims":[{"claim":"Chinese growth figures accurately reflect economic performance.","who":"Chinese official statistics; disputed by outside economists.","evid":"Official Q2 growth of 4.3% is itself a three-and-a-half-year low and below target, so the published numbers are not uniformly flattering. But independent estimates have long diverged from official series, and the 'muddling through' base case rests on local-government finances that are rolled over rather than resolved. The honest position is that direction is more reliable than level here.","status":"DIRECTION-MORE-RELIABLE-THAN-LEVEL"},{"claim":"China's regional assertiveness reflects an intent to displace the United States militarily.","who":"Asserted widely in Western strategic commentary; rejected by Beijing.","evid":"Joint operations with Russia, grey-zone pressure and the military build-out are documented. But Xi's own stated doctrine is to AVOID the Thucydides Trap — he raised it by name with Trump in May — and the economic constraints argue against appetite for a war that would sever the export markets the growth model requires. Both the capability and the stated intent to avoid using it are on the record.","status":"CAPABILITY-REAL-INTENT-CONTESTED"}]},"human":{"dead":0,"disp":0,"famine":false,"est":true,"basis":"No armed conflict on Chinese territory in this period. This theater is tracked as a systemic pressure file, not a war — the human dimension recorded here is economic rather than casualty-based.","note":"Youth unemployment near 20% and the 'rùn' emigration culture are the social pressures the platform tracks in place of a casualty count."},"history":{"roots":"A century of foreign concessions, invasion and civil war ended in 1949 with a state whose founding legitimacy rests on ending national humiliation. Every subsequent Chinese government has treated territorial integrity and freedom from external coercion as existential rather than negotiable — which is why Taiwan, the maritime periphery and technological dependence are not ordinary policy questions in Beijing.","timeline":[{"date":"1839-1949","e":"The 'century of humiliation' — opium wars, concessions, Japanese invasion, civil war. The founding grievance of the modern state."},{"date":"1978","e":"Deng's reform and opening begins the growth model that made China the world's manufacturing centre."},{"date":"2001","e":"WTO accession accelerates integration — and builds the export dependence that now constrains strategic choices."},{"date":"2012-2013","e":"Xi Jinping takes power; anti-corruption campaign consolidates control; Belt and Road launched."},{"date":"2018","e":"Term limits removed, ending the succession norm and making Xi's health a systemic variable."},{"date":"2020-2024","e":"Property developer defaults, pandemic controls and their abrupt end, and the beginning of sustained consumer-sentiment weakness."},{"date":"14 May 2026","e":"Xi asks Trump directly whether the two countries can avoid the 'Thucydides Trap' at a summit that produces no agreement on Taiwan or Iran."},{"date":"Jun-Jul 2026","e":"Joint Sea-2026 with Russia off Qingdao; joint aerial patrol into South Korea's ADIZ; Xi casts the Party as a global force 'backed by a strong military'."},{"date":"13 Jul 2026","e":"State Council publishes the 15th Five-Year Plan for Expanding Consumption — the first time consumption enters a national five-year plan."},{"date":"Jul 2026","e":"Q2 growth reported at 4.3%, a three-and-a-half-year low; Politburo discusses accelerated bond issuance for infrastructure stimulus."}],"actors":[{"name":"Xi Jinping","bio":"General Secretary since 2012, with term limits removed in 2018 and a fourth term expected at the 2027 Party Congress. His stated doctrine is avoiding both the middle-income and Thucydides traps."},{"name":"The Politburo Standing Committee","bio":"The body managing the economic response — bond issuance, property triage, consumption planning — under tightly controlled elite politics."},{"name":"The PLA","bio":"Conducting joint operations with Russia across the Pacific while absorbing intensified purges in its industrial complex."}],"bridge":"A state founded on ending foreign coercion built its prosperity on integration with the powers that had coerced it — and now finds that the integration is the leverage others hold. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: Beijing is buying mediator roles across three continents and patrolling the Pacific with Russia, while its own growth runs below target and one in five young people cannot find work. The assertiveness and the fragility are the same story.","sources":[{"t":"Asia Society Policy Institute · China 2026: What to Watch","u":"https://asiasociety.org/policy-institute/china-2026-what-watch"},{"t":"Bloomberg · Xi casts the Party as a global force (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-01/xi-positions-china-s-ruling-party-as-global-force-for-progress"}]},"refs":[{"t":"VIF · Africa Now (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.vifindia.org/2026/july/17/Africa-Now-01-15-july-2026"},{"t":"Asia Society Policy Institute · China 2026: What to Watch","u":"https://asiasociety.org/policy-institute/china-2026-what-watch"},{"t":"Asia Society · Xi Jinping's Priorities in 2026 (Thomas & Tsering)","u":"https://asiasociety.org/sites/default/files/2025-12/China2026_XiJinpingsPrioritiesin2026.pdf"},{"t":"Bloomberg · Xi positions the Party as a global force (1 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-01/xi-positions-china-s-ruling-party-as-global-force-for-progress"},{"t":"Asia Pacific Foundation · China's Economic Strategy in 2026","u":"https://www.asiapacific.ca/publication/chinas-economic-strategy-2026-prioritize-continuity-over-change"},{"t":"EAI · CCP politics, the middle-income and Thucydides traps","u":"https://research.nus.edu.sg/eai/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/EAIBB-No.-1807-Chinas-politics-2024-synopsis_exsum.pdf"}],"escwhy":"68 = systemic pressure theater (base ~60) raised for being the structural driver of three Pacific flashpoints, the Thucydides counterparty in an openly acknowledged rivalry, and a simultaneous internal-fragility case (growth below target, ~20% youth unemployment, unresolved property debt, managed succession). Not higher because there is no armed conflict on Chinese territory; not lower because Chinese choices set the escalation ceiling in Taiwan, Korea and the South China Sea. · TRAJECTORY IS RECONSTRUCTED, NOT RECORDED: this theater was added to the board on 26 Jul 2026, so it has no observed ten-period history. The chart is a backward estimate of where the score would have sat and is marked EST accordingly — it should not be read as this platform having tracked the trend.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"26 Jul 2026","c":"Theater created. A structural audit found China referenced 27 times across other files — more than any other actor — with no file of its own. The board could examine US domestic politics but not Chinese ones, which meant Chinese action read as pure strategy rather than as choices made under real constraint."}],"spill":[{"n":"Taiwan","id":"taiwan","dir":"out","w":"The Thucydides counterparty and the grey-zone pressure campaign; also the Mazu religious-cultural influence operation, with subsidised pilgrimages documented spreading election misinformation."},{"n":"Korea","id":"korea","dir":"out","w":"A joint PRC-Russia strategic aerial patrol entered South Korea's ADIZ ahead of Joint Sea-2026 — coordinated two-power signalling rather than separate activity."},{"n":"South China Sea","id":"south-china-sea","dir":"out","w":"Maritime confrontations are measurably eroding Beijing's standing in the Philippines and Vietnam even as regional sentiment stays net positive overall."},{"n":"Russia","id":"russia-domestic","dir":"both","w":"Joint Sea-2026 off Qingdao and a joint Pacific patrol — alignment that gives both powers reach neither has alone, without a formal alliance commitment."},{"n":"Myanmar","id":"myanmar","dir":"out","w":"Pressured the MNDAA to return Lashio and brokered its turn on the TNLA — where the junta has regained ground, Chinese leverage rather than Burmese arms explains it."},{"n":"Thailand / Cambodia","id":"thailand-cambodia","dir":"out","w":"Xi has publicly urged both to settle their border dispute — Beijing positioning as arbiter between two ASEAN members, the role Western states and ASEAN mechanisms historically claimed."},{"n":"Pakistan / Afghanistan","id":"pak-afg","dir":"out","w":"Chinese-mediated talks have produced no lasting ceasefire — the same brokerage model, here failing. CPEC and Gwadar also make Chinese nationals deliberate BLA targets."},{"n":"Kazakhstan / Central Asia","id":"kazakhstan","dir":"out","w":"Belt-and-Road infrastructure and the eastward export option that balances Central Asia's Russian dependence."},{"n":"Global economy","id":"global-economy","dir":"both","w":"A trade surplus large enough to provoke protectionist responses across its export markets, against a domestic demand shortfall the new five-year plan concedes."},{"n":"India","id":"india","dir":"both","w":"The disputed Ladakh frontier — and the protest movement there names Chinese land encroachment alongside corporate acquisition, making the border dispute a domestic political grievance as well as a strategic one."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Managed rivalry under economic strain","dir":"stalemate","phase":"Structural competition, deliberately below conflict","up":["A Taiwan or South China Sea incident becoming a test of the hierarchy rather than of the stake","Economic pressure intensifying — a property or local-finance break forcing external diversion","The 2027 Party Congress raising the cost of appearing to concede","Joint operations with Russia normalising into a bloc that invites counter-balancing"],"down":["Xi's own stated doctrine of avoiding the Thucydides Trap — raised by name with Trump in May","Export dependence making a war that severs markets self-defeating","Third-state hedging rewarding restraint over coercion","Economic pragmatism surfacing when growth targets and social stability bind"],"fw":["thucydides","deterrence","security-dilemma","ladder"]},"macro":{"growth":"4.2% (2026F) · 4.3% actual Q2, a 3.5-year low","infl":"Very low; deflationary at producer-price level","src":"IMF WEO 2026; national statistics via reporting","note":"Current account rose to 3.3% of GDP in 2025 on a RECORD SURPLUS ABOVE $1 TRILLION. Reduced exports to the US were substituted into ASEAN and Europe, so the trade war did not land as expected — but that surplus is the thing provoking protectionist responses across its export markets. Deflationary producer prices alongside ~20% youth unemployment describe a demand shortfall, which is what the new consumption five-year plan concedes. The unresolved property-development debt overhang remains the largest single drag."},"hist_real":false,"since":"—","inherit":{"name":"The Unequal Treaties","est":"1842-1949","status":"ABOLISHED · DOCTRINE IN FORCE","what":"Beginning with Nanjing in 1842, a century of treaties imposed after military defeat ceded territory, opened treaty ports, granted extraterritoriality so foreigners were exempt from Chinese law, fixed tariffs China could not set, and required indemnities for wars China had lost on its own soil. Hong Kong went to Britain, and the foreign concessions ran their own police and courts. <b>The period is taught as the 'century of humiliation' and it is the founding grievance of the modern Chinese state.</b>","now":"<b>The treaties are gone; the doctrine they produced governs Chinese policy.</b> Territorial integrity and freedom from external coercion are treated as existential rather than negotiable — which is why Taiwan, the maritime periphery and technological dependence are not ordinary policy questions in Beijing. <b>Xi's stated strategy is to avoid two traps: the middle-income trap and the Thucydides Trap</b>, which he raised by name with Trump in May 2026. The framework on this platform's Frameworks board is Beijing's own vocabulary.","change":"Hong Kong returned in 1997 under terms now substantially revised. <b>Q2 2026 growth of 4.3% is a three-and-a-half-year low against ~20% youth unemployment</b> — the constraint underneath the assertiveness, and the half this board previously could not see."}},{"id":"israel","cat":"military","region":"Middle East","subregion":"Levant","tclass":"conflict","name":"Israel — Four Fronts and an Election","sub":"The belligerent in four theaters that had no file of its own","flag":"🇮🇱","lat":31.78,"lon":35.22,"esc":74,"sev":"high","status":"escalating","tier":"SEVERE","domain":"Multi-Front Belligerent / Electoral Turning Point","domain_tag":"Military","trend":"up","esc_history":[68,69,70,71,72,73,73,74,74,74],"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"26 Jul 2026","checkmode":"SWEPT","note":"1 AUG 2026 — THE HAREDI FAULT LINE IS BACK ON THE STREET: ultra-Orthodox protesters clashed with Israeli police outside a Jerusalem cafe operating on Shabbat, with secular counter-demonstrators present; police were photographed restraining a Haredi youth by the neck · the significance for this file is electoral rather than public-order: the coalition lost its majority in July 2025 when United Torah Judaism walked out over the CONSCRIPTION BILL, and religious-secular confrontation in the streets weeks before a 27 October election hardens exactly the bloc arithmetic that determines whether anyone reaches 61 seats · the IDF struck Gaza arms depots the same day · 31 JUL 2026 — 12% UNDECIDED, AND THE GOVERNMENT WON'T CONFIRM THE DEAL: a Channel 12 poll finds 12% OF ISRAELIS UNDECIDED — roughly 600,000 voters or FIFTEEN KNESSET MANDATES, which is more than the current gap between Yashar and Likud and enough to decide the coalition question outright · on the Board of Peace disarmament roadmap, Netanyahu has NOT commented publicly and a senior Israeli official says Israel does not accept it and will not withdraw · 25-27 JUL 2026 — WEST BANK VIOLENCE AND A WHITE HOUSE MEETING: NETANYAHU is set to meet TRUMP at the WHITE HOUSE · over the weekend the Israeli military DETAINED MORE THAN 70 PALESTINIANS across the occupied West Bank after violence that killed FOUR PALESTINIAN VILLAGERS AND TWO ISRAELI SOLDIERS; Palestinians described aggressive searches including an ARMED RAID ON A HOSPITAL in NABLUS, which an Israeli military statement did not address · ISRAELI SETTLERS TORCHED MOSQUES in attacks on Palestinian towns and villages · in Gaza, Israel killed TWO HAMAS SECURITY OFFICIALS · NOTE A GAP THIS BOARD SHOULD OWN: the West Bank has no file of its own here, despite settler violence, mass detention and allegations of ethnic cleansing running alongside the Gaza war. It is currently visible only through this theater and Gaza's · JUL 2026 — AN ELECTION ON 27 OCTOBER THAT MOVES FOUR THEATERS: Israel votes by 27 OCT 2026, and GADI EISENKOT — former IDF Chief of Staff, 66, who LOST HIS SON AND TWO NEPHEWS IN THE GAZA WAR — has emerged as Netanyahu's main challenger with his new YASHAR party (Hebrew for 'straightforward') · the 21 Jul Channel 12 poll puts YASHAR 24, LIKUD 23, with the opposition bloc at 69 against the coalition's 51 · THE ARITHMETIC IS THE STORY: the Jewish opposition parties still CANNOT form a 61-seat majority WITHOUT THE ARAB PARTIES (Hadash-Ta'al, United Arab List) — the structural fact that has decided Israeli coalition politics for a decade · Bloomberg's read on Eisenkot: a stark contrast to Netanyahu 'IN STYLE IF NOT SO MUCH IN POLICY' — a change of premier is not necessarily a change of war policy · Bennett and Eisenkot could combine with Lapid to govern, but BOTH WANT THE TOP JOB · the coalition's fault line remains HAREDI CONSCRIPTION: UTJ's factions left over Edelstein's bill in Jul 2025, cutting the majority to 60 · ON THE US RELATIONSHIP: an i24/Direct Polls survey (9 Jul) found 51% of Israelis believe DOMESTIC INTERESTS are driving both Trump and Netanyahu, against 32% who think they remain fully coordinated — coalition supporters split 52% for coordination, opposition supporters 68% for divergence · ACTIVE ON FOUR FRONTS SIMULTANEOUSLY: Gaza (ceasefire eroding strike by strike), Lebanon (direct talks in Rome while striking near Nabatieh), Syria (forces entered Quneitra; opposing Turkish air defence), and the Iran war","leverage":[{"actor":"Israel (state)","asset":"Regional military primacy — air superiority and the demonstrated reach to strike Iran, Lebanon, Syria and Yemen","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Israel (state)","asset":"The US relationship — munitions, intelligence and diplomatic cover, now perceived by a majority of Israelis as driven by each leader's domestic interests","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Netanyahu","asset":"Incumbency and coalition management ahead of 27 October","str":"md"},{"actor":"The Arab parties","asset":"The 61-seat arithmetic — no anti-Netanyahu government forms without them, which is leverage neither bloc likes to name","str":"md"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Israel","channel":"Technology and defence exports","exp":"MODERATE","detail":"The economy's structural strength, exposed to reputational and boycott pressure and to reservist mobilisation drawing skilled labour out of the workforce."},{"holder":"United States","channel":"Military assistance and munitions resupply","exp":"HIGH","detail":"The dependency that makes US policy the binding external constraint — and the relationship a majority of Israelis now read as two leaders serving separate domestic audiences rather than one alliance."},{"holder":"Israel","channel":"Multi-front war cost","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Four simultaneous theaters, sustained reservist call-ups and displaced-community costs — the fiscal and social bill that the October election is partly a referendum on."}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 United States","side":"Principal backer and broker","flow":"Munitions and diplomatic cover; brokered the Rome talks with Lebanon and Resolution 2803's framework for Gaza; opposed on the F-35/S-400 question with Turkey","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇹🇷 Turkey","side":"Adversarial competitor","flow":"Israel fears US approval of a Turkish air defence system in Syria would limit the IAF's freedom to strike Iran; Netanyahu on record opposing F-35 sales to Ankara","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇸🇾 Syria (al-Sharaa government)","side":"Contested neighbour","flow":"Israeli forces entered al-Samdaniyah al-Sharqiyah in Quneitra with vehicles and a checkpoint; Damascus separately interdicts Iranian weapons bound for Hezbollah","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇸🇴 Somaliland","side":"Recognition partner","flow":"Israel is the only state to have recognised Somaliland's independence — criticised by the AU and Mogadishu","tier":"confirmed"}],"gov":[{"src":"Channel 12 / Midgam polling (21 Jul 2026)","detail":"Yashar 24 seats, Likud 23; opposition bloc 69 v coalition 51, but Jewish opposition parties short of 61 without the Arab parties"},{"src":"Knesset record","detail":"United Torah Judaism factions left the coalition in July 2025 over the Haredi conscription bill, reducing the majority to 60"},{"src":"i24NEWS / Direct Polls (9 Jul 2026)","detail":"51% of Israelis say domestic interests drive both Trump and Netanyahu; 32% say they remain fully coordinated"}],"mil":"The IDF, operating on four fronts simultaneously: strikes inside Gaza during an eroding ceasefire, Commando Brigade operations near Nabatieh in Lebanon while direct talks run in Rome, ground incursions into Quneitra in Syria, and participation in the Iran exchange. Sustained reservist mobilisation is the manpower and economic constraint.","pol":"Netanyahu, Israel's longest-serving prime minister, faces an election by 27 October with a coalition reduced to a bare majority after the Haredi conscription split. Eisenkot's Yashar leads narrowly in polling; the opposition's path to 61 seats runs through the Arab parties, which no Jewish bloc has been willing to formalise.","eco":"An advanced technology and defence economy carrying the cost of four simultaneous fronts — reservist call-ups, displaced northern communities, and reputational exposure in export markets.","assess":"Israeli domestic politics is the most consequential variable in four active theaters between now and October, and this board had no file recording how those decisions get made. The honest reading is uncomfortable for both sides of it. A Netanyahu defeat would remove the figure most identified with the war; it would not obviously change the war, because Eisenkot differs from him in style more than in policy, and because no governing majority forms without coalition arithmetic that has repeatedly pushed Israeli governments rightward on security. This board should not code an election as an off-ramp before knowing what it would change.","rival":{"conf":"MODERATE","lens":"A state converting military primacy into permanent regional freedom of action — striking four neighbours, expanding recognition partnerships, and treating each ceasefire as a framework to operate inside rather than a constraint.","alt":"A state responding to a genuine and demonstrated threat environment: 7 October, Hezbollah's arsenal, and an Iranian programme its adversaries openly directed at it. On this reading the four fronts are consequences rather than choices, and the election is a real democratic test of whether that posture retains consent.","why":"Held at MODERATE. The operational record is documented and so is the threat environment. The platform's specific caution is against the analytically lazy move in either direction — treating the election as automatic de-escalation, or treating Israeli policy as unresponsive to its own electorate. The polling suggests the electorate is genuinely contested, with Eisenkot ahead on suitability."},"contested":{"summary":"Two live disputes: whether Israeli and US policy are coordinated or separately driven, and Netanyahu's claim about southern Lebanese villages seeking annexation.","claims":[{"claim":"Christian-majority villages in southern Lebanon asked to be annexed by Israel for protection from Hezbollah.","who":"Prime Minister Netanyahu.","evid":"Villages in the MARJAYOUN DISTRICT publicly DENIED seeking annexation. The denial is on the record and specific. The claim fits a documented regional pattern this platform tracks — sectarian fragmentation offered as protection, from Sweida to Gaza's clan militias — and no independent corroboration of the request has emerged.","status":"DENIED-BY-THE-NAMED-PARTIES"},{"claim":"Israeli and US policy on the region remain fully coordinated.","who":"Asserted by the coalition; disputed within Israel.","evid":"An i24/Direct Polls survey found 51% of Israelis believe domestic interests are driving both leaders against 32% who see full coordination — and the split is partisan (52% of coalition supporters see coordination; 68% of opposition supporters see divergence). Washington simultaneously brokered the Rome talks Israel joined and moved to lift S-400 sanctions on Turkey over Israeli objection. Perception of the alliance is now itself a domestic political variable.","status":"CONTESTED-AND-PARTISAN"}]},"human":{"dead":2000,"disp":57000,"famine":false,"est":true,"basis":"Order-of-magnitude and partial. Israeli casualty figures across the 2023-26 wars and the 2026 Iran exchange are not consolidated on this board; the displacement figure reflects northern communities and the 57,000+ recorded in Lebanon-adjacent shelters is tracked on the Lebanon file. Read this line as incomplete rather than authoritative.","note":"Casualties on the other side of each of the four fronts are recorded on the Gaza, Lebanon, Syria and Iran files. This ledger is deliberately not a substitute for those."},"history":{"roots":"The 1947 UN partition plan and the 1948 war established a state and displaced a population, leaving two unresolved questions that every subsequent conflict has re-asked: what the borders are, and who governs the people inside and beside them. The 1967 occupation added territory the state has neither annexed nor relinquished, and the 1982 Lebanon intervention began the northern front that is still open.","timeline":[{"date":"1947-1949","e":"Partition, war, and the establishment of the state alongside the Palestinian displacement — the two facts every later conflict re-litigates."},{"date":"1967","e":"Occupation of the West Bank, Gaza, Sinai and Golan — territory held but not incorporated, the status that defines the conflict's legal form."},{"date":"1982-2000","e":"Lebanon intervention and occupation; Hezbollah forms in response and outlasts the withdrawal."},{"date":"2005","e":"Gaza disengagement; Hamas wins the 2006 elections and takes the strip in 2007."},{"date":"7 Oct 2023","e":"Hamas's attack kills hundreds of Israeli civilians; the multi-front war that follows is still running."},{"date":"Jul 2025","e":"United Torah Judaism leaves the coalition over Haredi conscription, cutting Netanyahu's majority to 60."},{"date":"Jun 2026","e":"Eisenkot launches the Yashar party campaign; the Iran exchange begins."},{"date":"Jul 2026","e":"Yashar overtakes Likud in polling; sixth round of Lebanon talks opens in Rome as DIRECT negotiations; Israeli forces enter Quneitra."},{"date":"27 Oct 2026","e":"Election deadline — the most consequential scheduled event in four theaters on this board."}],"actors":[{"name":"Benjamin Netanyahu","bio":"Israel's longest-serving prime minister, facing voters with a bare-majority coalition and four open fronts."},{"name":"Gadi Eisenkot","bio":"Former IDF Chief of Staff, 66, who lost his son and two nephews in the Gaza war. Leads Yashar; polls ahead of Netanyahu on suitability, and differs from him more in style than in policy."},{"name":"The Arab parties (Hadash-Ta'al, United Arab List)","bio":"The seats without which no anti-Netanyahu majority exists — the arithmetic every Jewish bloc acknowledges privately and avoids formalising."}],"bridge":"A state created by partition has spent seventy-eight years unable to settle either of the questions partition left open — where the borders run and who governs the people beside them — and has answered both, repeatedly, with force that worked tactically and settled nothing. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: it is fighting on four fronts at once while holding an election whose leading challenger is the general who ran the army, lost his son in the war, and differs from the incumbent mostly in manner.","sources":[{"t":"Bloomberg · Eisenkot emerges as Netanyahu's main rival (15 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-15/ex-general-who-lost-son-in-gaza-war-emerges-as-netanyahu-s-main-rival"},{"t":"Haaretz · Israel Elections 2026 poll tracker","u":"https://www.haaretz.com/israel-news/elections/2026-07-21/ty-article/poll-eisenkot-edges-likud-opposition-still-needs-arab-parties-for-majority/0000019f-81d8-d6e5-addf-c9dc29a50000"}]},"refs":[{"t":"Times of Israel · liveblog (1 Aug 2026)","u":"https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog-august-01-2026/"},{"t":"Times of Israel · liveblog and Channel 12 polling (31 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.timesofisrael.com/liveblog-july-31-2026/"},{"t":"Just Security · Early Edition (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.justsecurity.org/149834/early-edition-july-27-2026/"},{"t":"Democracy Now · headlines (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.democracynow.org/2026/7/27/headlines"},{"t":"Bloomberg · Eisenkot emerges as Netanyahu's main rival (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-15/ex-general-who-lost-son-in-gaza-war-emerges-as-netanyahu-s-main-rival"},{"t":"Haaretz · poll: opposition still needs Arab parties (21 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.haaretz.com/israel-news/elections/2026-07-21/ty-article/poll-eisenkot-edges-likud-opposition-still-needs-arab-parties-for-majority/0000019f-81d8-d6e5-addf-c9dc29a50000"},{"t":"i24NEWS / Direct Polls · Trump-Netanyahu coordination survey (9 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.i24news.tv/en/news/israel-election-s-2026/artc-i24news-poll-eisenkot-jumps-to-21-seats-gaining-on-netanyahu-s-majority-in-the-knesset"}],"escwhy":"74 = active multi-front belligerent (base ~70) raised for operating simultaneously in Gaza, Lebanon, Syria and the Iran exchange, and for a scheduled 27 October election that is the single most consequential dated event across four theaters on this board. Not higher because there is no sustained ground war on Israeli territory; not lower because Israeli decisions set the escalation ceiling in each of th