 at a twenty-year high, mass detention including 3,244 held without charge, and de facto annexation advancing through E1 and 13 new settlements — with the minister holding West Bank authority also closing the banking channels that keep the Palestinian Authority solvent."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Four simultaneous fronts, election in October","dir":"volatile","phase":"Escalation with a scheduled political decision point","up":["Lebanon talks collapsing and strikes resuming at scale","The Gaza ceasefire eroding below the framework until it fails outright","A Turkish air defence system in Syria colliding with IAF operations","Election-season incentives rewarding demonstrated toughness on four fronts"],"down":["The Rome direct-talks format producing a redeployment timetable","A change of government — though Eisenkot differs 'in style if not so much in policy', so this is not automatic","US pressure on the Gaza and Lebanon frameworks it brokered","The cumulative fiscal and reservist cost of four fronts becoming electorally decisive"],"fw":["deterrence","ripeness","proxy","security-dilemma"]},"macro":{"growth":"Carrying four simultaneous fronts","infl":"War-cost pressure","src":"Reporting, 2026","note":"An advanced technology and defence economy absorbing sustained reservist mobilisation, displaced northern communities and four-front operational costs — a bill that is now an electoral issue ahead of the 27 October vote."},"hist_real":false,"since":"1948","inherit":{"name":"The Mandate for Palestine","est":"1917-1948","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Britain committed in 1917 to facilitating 'a national home for the Jewish people' in Palestine while stating nothing should prejudice the rights of 'existing non-Jewish communities', and the 1922 League Mandate made that binding. It governed by promising incompatible things — immigration and land purchase against Arab political rights — suppressed the 1936-39 revolt by force, then restricted Jewish immigration in the 1939 White Paper as European Jews were being destroyed. <b>Britain referred the problem to the UN in 1947 and left.</b>","now":"<b>Israel exists because the mandate ended without an agreed settlement, and its security posture is shaped by what the mandate never resolved.</b> The 1947 partition was accepted by the Jewish Agency and rejected by Arab leaderships; the war established a state, displaced roughly 700,000 Palestinians, and produced no agreed frontier. 1967 added territory held but not incorporated. <b>Israel is now operating on four fronts simultaneously and holds an election on 27 October</b> in which the arithmetic still requires the Arab parties for any anti-incumbent majority — the mandate's demographic question, inside the Knesset.","change":"Oslo's five-year interim arrangement is in its thirty-first year. <b>The US has removed Syria's terror listing and is lifting S-400 sanctions on Turkey over Israeli objection</b>, and a majority of Israelis now read the American relationship as two leaders serving separate domestic audiences."}},{"id":"india","cat":"political","region":"Asia","subregion":"South Asia","tclass":"pressure","name":"India — The Street Wins One","sub":"A nuclear power on two contested borders, and the first mass movement to make Modi concede","flag":"🇮🇳","lat":28.61,"lon":77.21,"esc":62,"sev":"elevated","status":"escalating","tier":"ELEVATED","domain":"Democratic Contestation / Border Autonomy","domain_tag":"Political","trend":"up","esc_history":[54,55,56,57,58,59,60,61,62,62],"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"27 Jul 2026","checkmode":"CHECKED","note":"31 JUL 2026 — THE MOVEMENT GETS ITS CHRONICLER: Arundhati Roy has characterised the youth-led movement as 'COCKROACH DEMOCRACY' · the naming matters for the file: a movement that adopted a mocking label is having it converted into a description of a political method, which is how protest movements acquire durability beyond a single demand · 27 JUL 2026 — CONFIRMED: EDUCATION MINISTER DHARMENDRA PRADHAN HAS RESIGNED after a week of mass protests · the concession this file recorded as reported is now executed. The Cockroach Janta Party achieved a cabinet resignation through street mobilisation — the first of the Modi era · 27 JUL 2026 — MODI CONCEDED: the government has ACCEPTED THE KEY DEMANDS of the youth-led COCKROACH JANTA PARTY movement, INCLUDING THE RESIGNATION OF EDUCATION MINISTER DHARMENDRA PRADHAN, per Bloomberg reporting today · the movement — which ironically named itself to MOCK the ruling Bharatiya Janata Party — mobilised over EXAM PAPER LEAKS and corruption in the examination system · THE SEQUENCE: on 20 JUL more than 10,000 marched on parliament and were met with TEAR GAS AND BATON CHARGES, with dozens injured on both sides; numbers had been swelled by anger at the FORCIBLE HOSPITALISATION of 59-year-old activist SONAM WANGCHUK during a hunger strike; by that Sunday an estimated 25,000 had gathered, the largest youth-led demonstration in years; thousands then camped in Delhi through 22-23 Jul defying new restrictions · New Lines' read: 'fear has begun to loosen its grip on a significant section of the Indian public — the fear of taking to the streets, of questioning the government and, above all, of criticizing Prime Minister Narendra Modi' · THE LADAKH THREAD RUNS UNDERNEATH IT: protests there have run since 2021 demanding STATEHOOD and inclusion in the SIXTH SCHEDULE (tribal autonomy over land, water, forests) — promised in the BJP's own 2019 manifesto and not delivered. Ladakh has been a Union Territory WITHOUT A LEGISLATURE since 2019. The September 2025 unrest in Leh killed FOUR and injured 87 · NOTE WHAT THE LADAKH MOVEMENT IS: the Buddhist-majority LEH APEX BODY and the Muslim-majority KARGIL DEMOCRATIC ALLIANCE are working TOGETHER — cross-communal organising in a region that is 47% Muslim, 40% Buddhist and 80% tribal, on a border facing both China and Pakistan","leverage":[{"actor":"Government of India","asset":"Central control of Ladakh as a Union Territory with no legislature — direct rule over a border region since 2019","str":"hi"},{"actor":"The youth movement (CJP)","asset":"Street mobilisation that has now successfully forced a cabinet resignation — a demonstrated capability, which changes the next negotiation","str":"md"},{"actor":"Leh Apex Body / Kargil Democratic Alliance","asset":"Cross-communal unity that denies the centre its usual wedge, sustained across six years","str":"md"},{"actor":"Corporations and the centre","asset":"Land access in Ladakh — what the Sixth Schedule would restrict, and what the Pashmina March was called to highlight","str":"hi"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Central government","channel":"Land and resource access in Ladakh","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Sixth Schedule inclusion would empower District Councils over land, water, agriculture and forests — which is precisely why it has not been granted. Wangchuk's 2024 Pashmina March was called to highlight land encroachment by corporations and by China, naming both at once."},{"holder":"India","channel":"Scale and market access","exp":"HIGH","detail":"The economic weight that makes India a hedging partner courted by Washington, Moscow and the Gulf simultaneously, and that underwrites its refusal to align."},{"holder":"Students and youth","channel":"The examination system","exp":"MODERATE","detail":"Exam paper leaks are not a peripheral grievance — competitive examinations are the principal mobility mechanism in Indian public life, which is why corruption in them produced the largest youth mobilisation in years."}],"external":[{"actor":"🇨🇳 China","side":"Border adversary","flow":"The Ladakh frontier is at the heart of India-China tensions; land encroachment there is named by the protest movement itself","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇵🇰 Pakistan","side":"Adversary","flow":"Kashmir and the Line of Control; Ladakh borders both states, and the Kargil district faces Pakistan directly","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇪🇬 Egypt","side":"Partner","flow":"Egypt reaffirmed its strategic partnership with India during National Day celebrations in New Delhi — part of a documented deepening of India-Africa trade and defence ties","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇧🇩 Bangladesh","side":"Neighbour under strain","flow":"Dhaka's 'Friend yes, Master no' line is calibrated against Indian weight; the SAARC revival Bangladesh wants runs through Delhi","tier":"confirmed"}],"gov":[{"src":"Bloomberg (27 Jul 2026)","detail":"Government accepted the movement's key demands including Education Minister Dharmendra Pradhan's resignation"},{"src":"Al Jazeera / AFP (20 Jul 2026)","detail":"More than 10,000 marched on parliament; police used tear gas and baton charges"},{"src":"Ladakh protest record (2021-2026)","detail":"Four deaths and 87 injuries; demands are statehood, Sixth Schedule inclusion, a Ladakh Public Service Commission, two parliamentary seats and local job reservation"}],"mil":"Not an internal armed conflict. Security response is police and paramilitary: tear gas and baton charges in Delhi, additional armed-forces deployment in Leh during the September 2025 unrest. The military dimension of this file sits on its borders — the Line of Control with Pakistan and the disputed Ladakh frontier with China.","pol":"Modi's BJP government has, for the first time, conceded to a mass street movement — accepting the Cockroach Janta Party's demands including a cabinet resignation. Ladakh remains under direct central rule as a Union Territory without a legislature, six years after a manifesto promise of Sixth Schedule protection.","eco":"The world's most populous country and a major economy whose scale allows it to hedge between Washington, Moscow and the Gulf without aligning. Domestically the binding grievance is opportunity — the examination system that allocates it, and the land rights that a border region says are being taken.","assess":"Mass mobilisation against this government has been attempted before and absorbed. What is different is that in July 2026 it worked — a cabinet minister resigned because students would not leave the street. Two things follow. A demonstrated capability changes every subsequent negotiation, including Ladakh's, refused for six years on demands the BJP put in its own 2019 manifesto. But a government that concedes on an education minister is not thereby conceding on a border territory's autonomy, and the Ladakh demand touches land, resources and a frontier with China. The cross-communal structure of the Ladakh movement — Buddhist-majority and Muslim-majority bodies organising together for six years — is the detail most likely to be underestimated.","rival":{"conf":"MODERATE","lens":"A centralising state holding a strategically-placed border region under direct rule to keep land and resource decisions out of local hands — the Sixth Schedule refused precisely because it would work.","alt":"A government managing a genuinely difficult security frontier: Ladakh faces two nuclear-armed adversaries, and devolving authority over land and infrastructure in a live border dispute carries real strategic cost. On this reading the delay is caution rather than extraction, and the 2026 concession on education shows the government does respond when the stake is not territorial.","why":"Held at MODERATE. The extraction reading is supported by the movement naming corporate land encroachment itself, and by a manifesto promise unhonoured for six years. The security reading is not manufactured — the frontier is real and contested. What the July concession newly tests is which constraint was actually binding."},"contested":{"summary":"The Ladakh movement's character is disputed — organic grievance or engineered conspiracy — and the dispute is itself an information operation this board can document.","claims":[{"claim":"The Ladakh protest movement is a conspiracy rather than a genuine local demand.","who":"Advanced by BJP-aligned media and online networks after the September 2025 unrest.","evid":"The demands — statehood, Sixth Schedule inclusion, a public service commission, parliamentary seats, local job reservation — have been consistent and public since 2021, and Sixth Schedule inclusion was in the BJP's own 2019 manifesto. The movement is led by two established bodies, the Leh Apex Body and the Kargil Democratic Alliance. Documented conduct includes six years of talks, marches and hunger strikes before any violence. The conspiracy framing appeared after the unrest, not before it.","status":"CONTRADICTED-BY-THE-RECORD"},{"claim":"The July 2026 concession shows the government responds to peaceful pressure.","who":"Implied by the government's acceptance of the CJP demands.","evid":"The concession followed tear gas, baton charges, dozens of injuries, a forcible hospitalisation of a hunger striker, and 25,000 people in the street — not peaceful petitioning. The Ladakh movement pursued exactly the peaceful route for six years and obtained nothing. The honest reading is that the government responded to scale and disruption, which is a lesson the next movement will draw.","status":"TRUE-BUT-MISLEADING"}]},"human":{"dead":4,"disp":0,"famine":false,"est":false,"basis":"Counted. Four deaths and 87 injuries in the September 2025 Leh unrest, per protest coordinators and authorities. The July 2026 Delhi clashes produced dozens of injuries on both sides with no reported fatalities.","note":"This is a political-contestation file, not an armed conflict. Casualties on India's borders are recorded on the Kashmir and Pakistan-Afghanistan files."},"history":{"roots":"British India was partitioned in 1947 into states whose borders were drawn in weeks and are still contested by force — Kashmir most of all. The republic that emerged built a federal structure meant to hold extraordinary diversity, and the recurring question since has been how much autonomy the centre will tolerate at the periphery. Ladakh, split from Jammu and Kashmir in 2019, is the newest instance of the oldest argument.","timeline":[{"date":"1947","e":"Partition; the Kashmir war begins a dispute that remains unresolved across three subsequent wars."},{"date":"1962","e":"The India-China war leaves the Ladakh frontier disputed — the border the protest movement now says is being encroached."},{"date":"2019","e":"Article 370 revoked; Ladakh separated from Jammu and Kashmir as a Union Territory WITHOUT a legislature. The BJP manifesto promises Sixth Schedule protection."},{"date":"2021","e":"Ladakh protests begin; the Leh Apex Body and Kargil Democratic Alliance form and start organising together across the Buddhist-Muslim divide."},{"date":"Mar-Sep 2024","e":"Wangchuk's 21-day fast; the Pashmina March against land encroachment by corporations and China is blocked under Section 144; a 1,000km padyatra from Leh to Delhi."},{"date":"Sep 2025","e":"Gen-Z protest wave reaches Leh — the BJP office is torched, four protesters killed, 87 injured."},{"date":"18-20 Jul 2026","e":"Wangchuk forcibly hospitalised during a hunger strike; 10,000+ march on parliament and are met with tear gas and baton charges; 25,000 gather by Sunday."},{"date":"22-23 Jul 2026","e":"Thousands camp in Delhi defying new restrictions — one of the biggest challenges the Modi government has faced."},{"date":"27 Jul 2026","e":"The government accepts the movement's key demands, including Education Minister Dharmendra Pradhan's resignation."}],"actors":[{"name":"Narendra Modi","bio":"Prime Minister since 2014, who has just made the first major concession of his tenure to a street movement."},{"name":"Cockroach Janta Party","bio":"The youth-led movement, ironically named to mock the ruling BJP, that forced a cabinet resignation over examination corruption."},{"name":"Sonam Wangchuk","bio":"Ladakhi activist, 59, repeatedly on hunger strike for Sixth Schedule protection; his forcible hospitalisation swelled the July mobilisation."},{"name":"Leh Apex Body & Kargil Democratic Alliance","bio":"Buddhist-majority and Muslim-majority organisations that have organised together since 2020-21 — the cross-communal structure the centre has not been able to split."}],"bridge":"A partition drawn in weeks left borders that three wars have not settled, and a republic that has argued ever since about how much self-rule the periphery may have. Ladakh — carved out in 2019 and governed without a legislature since — is that argument in its newest form, on a frontier facing two nuclear-armed neighbours. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: students in Delhi made this government blink for the first time, and the Ladakhis who spent six years asking politely are watching what that lesson was.","sources":[{"t":"New Lines Magazine · The protests gripping India","u":"https://newlinesmag.com/argument/the-protests-gripping-india-and-rattling-modi/"},{"t":"Al Jazeera · Bloodiest day: Gen-Z protest wave hits Ladakh","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/amp/news/2025/9/25/bloodiest-day-how-gen-z-protest-wave-hit-indias-ladakh-killing-four"}]},"refs":[{"t":"Democracy Now (Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.democracynow.org/2026/7/30/headlines"},{"t":"Democracy Now · headlines (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.democracynow.org/2026/7/27/headlines"},{"t":"Bloomberg · Inside Modi's decision to cave to the 'Cockroach' protesters (27 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-07-27/inside-modi-s-decision-to-cave-to-india-s-cockroach-protesters"},{"t":"Al Jazeera · Police attack Cockroach activists as thousands march (20 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/20/police-attack-cockroach-activists-as-thousands-march-on-indian-parliament"},{"t":"New Lines Magazine · The protests gripping India — and rattling Modi","u":"https://newlinesmag.com/argument/the-protests-gripping-india-and-rattling-modi/"},{"t":"Washington Post · capital sit-in as police crack down (23 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2026/07/23/india-cockroach-party-youth-movement-protest-modi/bf898b40-8667-11f1-9cec-0fb26676f07e_story.html"}],"escwhy":"62 = political-contestation theater (base ~55) raised for lethal unrest in Ladakh (4 dead, 87 injured), a nationwide movement that forced a cabinet resignation, and two live nuclear-armed border disputes adjacent to the autonomy demand. Not higher because there is no armed insurgency in the theaters covered here and the July confrontation ended in concession rather than escalation; not lower because the Ladakh demand touches a contested frontier with China. · TRAJECTORY IS RECONSTRUCTED, NOT RECORDED: this theater was added to the board on 27 Jul 2026, so it has no observed ten-period history. The chart is a backward estimate of where the score would have sat and is marked EST accordingly — it should not be read as this platform having tracked the trend.","log":[{"v":"v59","d":"27 Jul 2026","c":"Theater created. A structural audit found India referenced six times across other files with no file of its own — a nuclear power adjacent to the Kashmir and Pakistan-Afghanistan theaters, during the largest youth mobilisation against its government in years."}],"spill":[{"n":"Kashmir","id":"kashmir","dir":"both","w":"The Line of Control and the reserved-seat dispute over refugees from Indian-administered Kashmir; the autonomy grievance is now being pressed on both sides of the line and inside Ladakh as well."},{"n":"Pakistan","id":"pak-afg","dir":"both","w":"Kargil district faces Pakistan directly; LeT/JeM/TRF operations across the LoC are the standing escalation trigger between two nuclear states."},{"n":"China","id":"china","dir":"both","w":"The disputed Ladakh frontier is at the heart of India-China tensions — and the protest movement's own Pashmina March named Chinese encroachment alongside corporate land acquisition."},{"n":"Bangladesh","id":"bangladesh","dir":"out","w":"Dhaka's 'Friend yes, Master no' posture is calibrated against Indian weight; the SAARC revival Bangladesh wants requires Delhi's participation."},{"n":"Egypt","id":"egypt","dir":"both","w":"Strategic partnership reaffirmed in New Delhi — part of a documented deepening of India-Africa trade, defence and development ties."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Mass movement that just won a cabinet resignation","dir":"volatile","phase":"Contestation — first successful concession changes the next round","up":["The Ladakh demand escalating on the lesson that disruption works where petitioning did not","Further security crackdowns producing deaths as in Leh, September 2025","The China frontier dispute intersecting with the autonomy demand","Delegitimisation campaigns hardening the movement rather than dispersing it"],"down":["The 27 July concession establishing that demands can be met without further escalation","Granting Sixth Schedule protection — already promised in the BJP's own 2019 manifesto","Restoring a legislature to Ladakh, ending direct central rule","The cross-communal structure of the Ladakh bodies keeping the movement non-sectarian"],"fw":["conflict-curve","ripeness","resource-conflict"]},"macro":{"growth":"6.3% (2026F) · 6.5% (2027F), fiscal-year basis","infl":"Contained","src":"IMF World Economic Outlook, January 2026","note":"The fastest-growing major economy on this board, and the scale that lets Delhi hedge between Washington, Moscow and the Gulf without aligning. Growth is not the binding domestic constraint — opportunity distribution is, which is what the examination-corruption movement mobilised on and what the Ladakh land-rights demand is about."},"hist_real":false,"since":"1947","inherit":{"name":"The Radcliffe Line and Princely Accession","est":"1947","status":"IN FORCE","what":"Cyril Radcliffe, who had never been to India, was given <b>five weeks</b> to partition Punjab and Bengal for a population of 400 million, working from outdated census maps. The award was published two days after independence. Between 10 and 20 million people were displaced and estimates of the dead run from several hundred thousand to two million. <b>Separately, 565 princely states were allowed to choose a dominion without reference to their populations</b> — the loophole that produced Kashmir.","now":"<b>The partition's logic — that political identity maps onto religious community — remains the central argument in Indian politics.</b> Citizenship legislation, the status of Kashmir, and the question of who counts as fully Indian are all continuations of it. Ladakh, carved out in 2019 and governed <b>without a legislature ever since</b>, is the newest instance of the oldest federal question: how much self-rule the centre will tolerate at the periphery.","change":"In July 2026 a youth movement forced the first cabinet resignation of the Modi era. <b>The Ladakhi movement that has asked politely for six years is watching what that lesson was</b> — and its cross-communal structure, Buddhist and Muslim bodies organising together, denies the centre the communal wedge partition normally supplies."}},{"id":"west-bank","cat":"military","region":"Middle East","subregion":"Levant","tclass":"conflict","name":"West Bank — Annexation Without the Word","sub":"The slower war beside Gaza, and the one this board could not see","flag":"🇵🇸","lat":31.95,"lon":35.23,"esc":79,"sev":"high","status":"escalating","tier":"SEVERE","domain":"Occupation / De Facto Annexation","domain_tag":"Military","trend":"up","esc_history":[70,71,72,74,75,76,77,78,79,79],"as_of":"1 Aug 2026","tracked_since":"28 Jul 2026","checkmode":"CHECKED","note":"30-31 JUL 2026 — THE UN AND PBS BOTH REPORT ESCALATION: UN reporting on Israeli settler violence in the occupied West Bank continues, and PBS records settlers ESCALATING ATTACKS on Palestinians · a US Congressman, Rep. Ro Khanna, was DETAINED BY ISRAELI SETTLERS — an incident notable less for its severity than for what it establishes about who exercises authority on the ground in Area C · 24-27 JUL 2026 — A GUNFIGHT, A MILITARY OPERATION, AND TWO MOSQUES BURNED: on Friday 24 Jul a crowd of Israeli settlers, some armed, approached the Palestinian village of TAL (Tell) southwest of NABLUS; FOUR PALESTINIANS AND TWO ISRAELI SOLDIERS were killed — one soldier a security coordinator for the nearby HAVAT GILAD settlement · Netanyahu ordered a WIDE-SCALE MILITARY OPERATION across the West Bank; HUNDREDS OF PALESTINIANS were detained, with an armed raid reported on a hospital in Nablus · settlers then attacked villages, SETTING FIRE TO TWO MOSQUES IN QUSRA and scrawling 'Revenge Benayahu' after the killed reservist · THE BASELINE THIS SITS ON: OCHA records 1,111 PALESTINIANS KILLED in the West Bank and East Jerusalem — AT LEAST 243 OF THEM CHILDREN — with at least 65 killed since the start of 2026. OCHA documented MORE THAN 1,800 SETTLER ATTACKS IN 2025 causing casualties or property damage across about 280 communities — AN AVERAGE OF FIVE A DAY, THE HIGHEST RATE SINCE RECORDS BEGAN IN 2006 — and attacks are UP MORE THAN 50% in the first six months of 2026 · as of 1 JUL 2026, 9,299 PALESTINIANS ARE IN ISRAELI JAILS, OF WHOM 3,244 ARE HELD WITHOUT CHARGE · THE SETTLEMENT TRACK RUNS IN PARALLEL: 13 new settlements approved 3 Jul; 763 units at Eli; Peace Now reports settler OUTPOSTS NOW CONTROL NEARLY A FIFTH OF THE WEST BANK; the E1 PLAN — 3,400+ units between East Jerusalem and Ma'ale Adumim, about 3% of the territory — has been revived, and it severs West Bank contiguity around Jerusalem · SMOTRICH, the minister with West Bank authority, says the settlement 'revolution' has only just begun","leverage":[{"actor":"Israeli government (Smotrich portfolio)","asset":"Planning authority, demolition orders, and control of Area C land allocation — the administrative machinery of de facto annexation","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Settler movement and outposts","asset":"Physical presence on the ground, now controlling nearly a fifth of the territory by Peace Now's count","str":"hi"},{"actor":"Israel (fiscal)","asset":"Withholding Palestinian Authority clearance revenues and blocking correspondent banking — the ability to collapse the PA administratively","str":"hi"},{"actor":"European states","asset":"Trade, arms and tech relations, and the option to exclude settlement goods or the OPT from bilateral agreements — leverage held and largely unused","str":"md"}],"econlev":[{"holder":"Israel","channel":"PA fiscal strangulation","exp":"HIGH","detail":"The Palestinian Authority's 2026 deficit is forecast at $4 billion, with total debt around $15.4 billion by late 2025 — including $2.7 billion owed to its own public sector employees. Two Israeli banks, Hapoalim and Discount, are halting services to Palestinian banks after Smotrich declined to sign the indemnity waivers that let them process transactions. Reporting describes this as deliberate: collapse the PA, expand direct control, and in Smotrich's own framing encourage emigration."},{"holder":"Israel","channel":"Land, water and permit control","exp":"HIGH","detail":"Building permits in Area C are almost never granted to Palestinians, so construction proceeds without them and is then demolished as unlicensed — as at Anza on 27 July. Water allocation and access to agricultural land operate on the same logic."},{"holder":"Settlement economy","channel":"Construction and tourism development","exp":"MODERATE","detail":"Over 50,000 new housing units and 98 new settlements advanced since December 2022, alongside a government programme to develop tourism in the territory — the civilian infrastructure that makes reversal progressively harder."}],"external":[{"actor":"🇺🇸 United States","side":"Principal backer","flow":"Diplomatic cover at the Security Council; sanctions on violent settlers imposed and then rescinded under the current administration","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇪🇺 European Union / UK","side":"Critics with unused leverage","flow":"Bilateral trade, arms and tech relations that Chatham House and others argue could exclude the OPT or ban settlement goods — largely not exercised","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇯🇴 Jordan","side":"Custodial and demographic stake","flow":"Custodianship of Jerusalem's holy sites and the state most exposed to any mass displacement eastward","tier":"confirmed"},{"actor":"🇺🇳 UN OCHA / OHCHR","side":"Monitors","flow":"The casualty, displacement and settler-attack record this file is built on","tier":"confirmed"}],"gov":[{"src":"UN OCHA","detail":"1,111 Palestinians killed in the West Bank and East Jerusalem including at least 243 children; 1,800+ settler attacks in 2025 across ~280 communities, five a day, the highest since records began in 2006"},{"src":"Israeli Prison Service figures (via reporting)","detail":"9,299 Palestinians held as of 1 July 2026, of whom 3,244 are in administrative detention without charge"},{"src":"Peace Now / Kerem Navot","detail":"'Annus Mirabilis' report concludes the government advanced de facto annexation 2023-2025; settler outposts now control nearly a fifth of the West Bank"},{"src":"UN OHCHR","detail":"States that policy and legislative actions appear aimed at emptying areas of the West Bank of Palestinians and consolidating annexation"}],"mil":"Israeli military operations across the territory — the 'wide-scale' operation ordered after the Tal killings involved storming towns and villages and detaining hundreds. Alongside it, an armed settler movement whose outposts function as forward positions; UN reporting describes escalating settler violence occurring with the acquiescence, support, and in some cases participation of Israeli security forces. Palestinian armed activity is fragmented and localised, concentrated around Nablus and Jenin.","pol":"Administered under Israeli military law for Palestinians and Israeli civil law for settlers, with Bezalel Smotrich holding the ministerial authority over civil administration. A July 2025 Knesset motion to apply sovereignty passed as a non-binding vote. The Palestinian Authority governs fragments of the territory while its finances are being closed off.","eco":"A permit-controlled economy: Palestinian construction and agriculture in Area C proceed largely without licences and face demolition, while settlement construction, industry and now tourism development are state-supported. The PA's own solvency is contingent on Israeli decisions about clearance revenues and banking access.","assess":"Israel has not formally annexed the West Bank, because formal annexation would trigger a defined international response. What is documented instead is the same outcome assembled administratively: outposts holding nearly a fifth of the land, E1 planning that would sever contiguity around Jerusalem, permits denied and structures demolished, and settler violence at five incidents a day that prosecutors rarely charge. The mechanism is a division of labour — the state supplies planning and permits while an armed civilian movement does the displacing, which preserves a deniability the state would lose if soldiers did it.","rival":{"conf":"HIGH","lens":"A state annexing territory by administrative increment while outsourcing the violence that clears it — land seizure, permit denial, demolition and a settler movement operating with acquiescence, assembled so that no single step constitutes the declaration that would force a response.","alt":"A security occupation responding to real armed attacks: the Tal gunfight killed two Israeli soldiers, Palestinian militancy around Nablus and Jenin is genuine, and the Israeli government's position is that the territory is disputed rather than occupied and that operations target militants rather than civilians.","why":"Held at HIGH for the annexation reading because the documentary base is unusually strong and largely Israeli: Peace Now and Kerem Navot on settlement advance, Israeli prison figures on detention without charge, B'Tselem on the legal structure, alongside UN OCHA's incident record. The security rationale explains specific operations. It does not explain 98 new settlements, E1, or a tourism development programme."},"contested":{"summary":"Two disputes: how the Israeli government characterises settler violence, and the legal status of the territory itself.","claims":[{"claim":"Settler violence is the work of '150 or so juvenile delinquents'.","who":"Prime Minister Netanyahu, July 2026.","evid":"UN OCHA documented more than 1,800 settler attacks causing casualties or property damage in 2025 alone, across about 280 communities — an average of five a day and the highest rate since records began in 2006, with attacks up more than 50% in the first half of 2026. Hundreds of Israelis participated in the Qusra-area attacks that burned two mosques. The scale described is not consistent with the number given.","status":"CONTRADICTED-BY-THE-INCIDENT-RECORD"},{"claim":"The West Bank is disputed territory rather than occupied territory.","who":"The Government of Israel.","evid":"The ICJ, the UN Security Council and the overwhelming majority of states treat the territory as occupied under the Fourth Geneva Convention, which prohibits transferring the occupier's civilian population into it. Israel disputes the Convention's applicability and points to the absence of a recognised prior sovereign. The legal argument is genuinely made rather than invented, and it is close to universally rejected — both facts belong in the record.","status":"ISRAELI-POSITION-NEAR-UNIVERSALLY-REJECTED"}]},"human":{"dead":1111,"disp":1500,"famine":false,"est":false,"basis":"Counted, per UN OCHA — 1,111 Palestinians killed in the West Bank and East Jerusalem including at least 243 children, with at least 65 in 2026. Displacement figure is the 1,500+ recorded in the first ten weeks of 2026 alone and is therefore a substantial undercount of the cumulative total.","note":"9,299 Palestinians are held in Israeli jails as of 1 July 2026, of whom 3,244 are in administrative detention without charge. Israeli deaths from West Bank attacks are recorded on the Israel file."},"history":{"roots":"The territory was captured in 1967 and has been held ever since without being annexed or relinquished — a status Israel calls disputed and almost every other state calls occupied. Oslo divided it into Areas A, B and C in the 1990s as a five-year interim arrangement pending final-status talks that never concluded; Area C, about 60% of the land and containing almost all its agricultural and development space, remained under full Israeli control and is where the settlement enterprise has grown ever since.","timeline":[{"date":"1967","e":"Israel captures the West Bank; military administration begins and settlement construction follows within months."},{"date":"1993-1995","e":"Oslo Accords divide the territory into Areas A, B and C as a five-year interim step. Area C — 60% of the land — stays under full Israeli control."},{"date":"2002-","e":"Construction of the separation barrier, routed substantially inside the West Bank rather than along the 1949 line."},{"date":"Dec 2022","e":"Netanyahu's coalition takes office with a pledge to legalise outposts and expand settlement funding; Smotrich receives authority over the civil administration."},{"date":"Jul 2025","e":"The Knesset passes a non-binding motion to apply Israeli sovereignty to the territory; E1 planning resumes after a four-year pause."},{"date":"2025","e":"OCHA documents 1,800+ settler attacks across ~280 communities — five a day, the highest rate since records began in 2006."},{"date":"H1 2026","e":"Settler attacks up more than 50%; 1,500+ Palestinians displaced in the first ten weeks; 13 new settlements approved in July."},{"date":"24-27 Jul 2026","e":"Four Palestinians and two Israeli soldiers killed at Tal; a wide-scale military operation detains hundreds; settlers burn two mosques at Qusra."}],"actors":[{"name":"Bezalel Smotrich","bio":"Finance minister with ministerial authority over the West Bank civil administration; declines the banking indemnity waivers that keep Palestinian banks connected, and says the settlement 'revolution' has only begun."},{"name":"The settler movement","bio":"Over 500,000 Israelis in West Bank settlements excluding East Jerusalem, alongside roughly three million Palestinians. Outposts now hold nearly a fifth of the territory."},{"name":"The Palestinian Authority","bio":"Governs fragments of the territory with a forecast $4 billion deficit and roughly $15.4 billion in debt, including $2.7 billion owed to its own employees."}],"bridge":"A territory taken in 1967 was never annexed and never released, and an interim division meant to last five years has governed it for thirty — with the 60% designated Area C becoming the space where the permanent facts were built. · AND NOW, JUL 2026: outposts hold a fifth of the land, E1 is severing the last contiguity around Jerusalem, and a finance minister is closing the banking channels that keep the Palestinian Authority alive. The word annexation has still not been used.","sources":[{"t":"UN OCHA · occupied Palestinian territory","u":"https://www.ochaopt.org/"},{"t":"Peace Now / Kerem Navot · Annus Mirabilis","u":"https://peacenow.org.il/en"}]},"refs":[{"t":"Democracy Now · headlines (30 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.democracynow.org/2026/7/30/headlines"},{"t":"PBS NewsHour (30 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.pbs.org/newshour/show/july-30-2026-pbs-news-hour-full-episode"},{"t":"Al Jazeera · Israeli crackdown intensifies, settlers cause mayhem (26 Jul 2026)","u":"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/7/26/israeli-crackdown-in-occupied-west-bank-intensifies-settlers-cause-mayhem"},{"t":"Chatham House · Israel's accelerating de facto annexation (Apr 2026)","u":"https://www.chathamhouse.org/2026/04/israels-accelerating-de-facto-annexation-west-bank-has-dangerous-implications"},{"t":"FMEP · Settlement & Annexation Report (9 Jul 2026)","u":"https://fmep.org/resource/settlement-annexation-report-july-9-2026/"}],"escwhy":"79 = sustained lethal occupation violence (base ~72) raised for a documented five-a-day settler attack rate at a twenty-year high and rising 50% year on year, mass detention including 3,244 without charge, active de facto annexation via E1 and 13 new settlements, and a deliberate fiscal campaign to collapse the Palestinian Authority. Not higher than Gaza's active war because there is no sustained bombardment or siege-induced famine; not lower because the displacement mechanism here is continuous rather than episodic. · TRAJECTORY IS RECONSTRUCTED, NOT RECORDED: this theater was added to the board on 28 Jul 2026, so it has no observed ten-period history. The chart is a backward estimate of where the score would have sat and is marked EST accordingly — it should not be read as this platform having tracked the trend.","log":[{"v":"v60","d":"28 Jul 2026","c":"Theater created. This board flagged its own gap on 28 July — the West Bank was visible only through the Israel and Gaza files despite settler violence at record levels, mass detention, and documented de facto annexation. Building it was the correction."}],"spill":[{"n":"Israel","id":"israel","dir":"both","w":"The occupying power and the theater where the decisions are made — including an October election in which settlement expansion is a core constituency issue for the incumbent."},{"n":"Gaza","id":"gaza","dir":"both","w":"The other half of the occupied Palestinian territory. Attention and international pressure concentrated on Gaza's war is part of what has made the West Bank's slower process possible."},{"n":"Jordan","dir":"out","w":"Custodian of Jerusalem's holy sites and the state most exposed to any displacement eastward — the scenario its stability planning has treated as existential since 1970."},{"n":"Lebanon","id":"lebanon","dir":"out","w":"The annexation-by-request claim Netanyahu made about southern Lebanese villages, which Marjayoun denied, is the same sectarian-fragmentation template applied across a different border."}],"escalation":{"rung":"Record settler violence during active de facto annexation","dir":"climbing","phase":"Escalation — continuous displacement, not episodic","up":["Settler attacks up more than 50% year on year from an already record 2025 base","E1 construction severing West Bank contiguity around Jerusalem","Smotrich's banking blockade forcing Palestinian Authority collapse and direct Israeli control","Election-season incentives rewarding settlement expansion with the incumbent's base"],"down":["European states exercising trade, arms and tech leverage they currently hold unused","Prosecution of settler violence — currently rare enough that UN monitors cite impunity as a driver","Restoring Palestinian Authority fiscal viability, which requires an Israeli ministerial signature","The October election — though this board has recorded why that is not automatically an off-ramp"],"fw":["ethnic-security","resource-conflict","conflict-curve","ripeness"]},"macro":{"growth":"PA facing engineered insolvency","infl":"Permit-constrained economy","src":"Reporting; Chatham House, 2026","note":"The Palestinian Authority's 2026 deficit is forecast at $4 BILLION against total debt near $15.4 BILLION, INCLUDING $2.7 BILLION OWED TO ITS OWN PUBLIC SECTOR EMPLOYEES. Two Israeli banks are halting services to Palestinian banks after the finance minister declined to sign the indemnity waivers that permit them — reporting describes this as deliberate: collapse the Authority, expand direct control. This is fiscal policy used as a territorial instrument, and it is the clearest case on this board of a budget line functioning as a weapon."},"hist_real":false,"since":"1967","inherit":{"name":"The Mandate for Palestine","est":"1917-1948","status":"IN FORCE","what":"The <b>Balfour Declaration of 1917</b> committed Britain to facilitating 'a national home for the Jewish people' in Palestine while stating that nothing should prejudice 'the civil and religious rights of existing non-Jewish communities' — a population then roughly 90% Arab, referred to only by what they were not. The 1922 League of Nations Mandate incorporated that text as binding. Britain then governed by promising incompatible things: Zionist immigration and land purchase against Arab political rights, a contradiction it managed by force during the 1936-39 revolt and never resolved. It referred the problem to the UN in 1947 and left in 1948.","now":"<b>Both of the mandate's unanswered questions are the live conflicts on this board:</b> where the borders run, and who governs the people inside and beside them. The 1947 partition plan was accepted by the Jewish Agency and rejected by Arab leaderships; the war that followed established a state, displaced roughly 700,000 Palestinians, and left no agreed frontier. 1967 added territory held but not incorporated — a status Israel calls disputed and almost every other state calls occupied. The West Bank is where the borders question is being answered without being asked: <b>outposts now control nearly a fifth of the territory, E1 would sever contiguity around Jerusalem, and settler attacks run at five a day — the highest rate since records began in 2006.</b> Formal annexation would trigger a defined international response; administrative annexation does not.","change":"Oslo created an interim division meant to last five years and now in its thirty-first. <b>Resolution 2803 and the Board of Peace are the current attempt at the governance half of the question; nothing currently addresses the borders half.</b>"},"monitors":[{"t":"UN OCHA · occupied Palestinian territory data","u":"https://www.ochaopt.org/"}],"ctrl":{"gov":0,"contested":40,"other":60,"est":true,"src":"Oslo Area A/B/C framework and Peace Now outpost mapping, 2026","note":"Area C — about 60% — remains under full Israeli civil and security control; settler outposts hold nearly a fifth of the territory. 'Contested' covers Areas A and B, where the Palestinian Authority has partial or full civil authority but Israeli forces operate at will."},"control":{"gov":0,"contested":22,"other":78,"holders":"Area A (PA civil and security control) is roughly 18% of the territory and Area B (PA civil, Israeli security) about 22%. Area C — roughly 60% — is under full Israeli control, and settler outposts hold nearly a fifth of the whole.","note":"Oslo's division was meant to last five years and is in its thirty-first. E1 construction would sever contiguity around Jerusalem.","est":false}}];
