tion plays","catalyst":"JUL 2026 — China's Nov-2026 export-control pause expiry + Jan-1-2027 Pentagon ban on Chinese-origin REEs approach; Myanmar heavy-REE supply runs through KIA-taxed mines into Chinese separation. The mine-to-magnet Western gap remains the structural short."},{"id":"nickel","commodity":"Nickel","icon":"⬡","color":"#5b9bd5","use":"EV batteries (high-nickel cathodes), stainless steel","chokehold":"Indonesia ~50% of global supply; export ban forced onshore processing, largely Chinese-controlled","theaters":["indonesia"],"signal":"Indonesian export-policy shifts + Prabowo statism move nickel prices; Western IRA de-risking targets Chinese-controlled Indonesian supply","vectors":"Nickel miners, battery-metal ETFs, EV-cathode supply chain; Indonesia policy + Chinese-JV control are the swing factors"},{"id":"energy","commodity":"Oil & Gas / Chokepoints","icon":"◊","color":"#d4b24a","use":"Global energy supply; the master variable","chokehold":"Hormuz (~20-25% of seaborne oil), Bab el-Mandeb; Venezuela (largest reserves, US-administered); Mozambique LNG (top-10 potential)","theaters":["iran-us","chokepoints","venezuela","mozambique"],"signal":"Any sustained Hormuz closure OR a credible all-Gulf-export threat = the oil-spike scenario. Watch tanker insurance rates, SPR-release signals, and whether Saudi/UAE east-west pipelines can bypass the strait.","vectors":"Energy majors, oil/tanker/shipping equities, LNG developers, war-risk-sensitive insurers; Hormuz is the fastest-moving price trigger","catalyst":"JUL 16 2026 — IRGC threatens to choke off ALL regional oil & gas (not just Hormuz); US striking Iran around-the-clock as convoy protection; Iran hitting Gulf US bases. A simultaneous Hormuz + Bab-el-Mandeb + Gulf-loadport disruption is now the tail risk being priced. War-premium live; tanker war-risk insurance spiking."},{"id":"gold","commodity":"Gold","icon":"◉","color":"#d4b24a","use":"Safe-haven, conflict-finance, sanctions-evasion store of value","chokehold":"African conflict gold (Sudan/Sahel) laundered via UAE; Russia using gold to fund the Ukraine war","theaters":["sudan","mali","geopolitical"],"signal":"Record central-bank gold accumulation is the de-dollarization/trust gauge. Watch for any UAE-conduit sanction (would disrupt physical flows) and safe-haven flows on each Iran-war escalation.","vectors":"Gold miners, bullion ETFs, safe-haven allocation; rises with systemic geopolitical stress across the whole platform","catalyst":"JUL 2026 — EU banned Sudanese gold (Jul 13) but NOT the UAE conduit; war-premium + record central-bank buying keep gold elevated. The Dubai laundering circuit (Sudan/DRC/Sahel) stays open — sanctions hit the periphery, not the refiner. Gold remains the system-distrust hedge as the Iran war escalates."},{"id":"defense","commodity":"Defense & Drones","icon":"⚔","color":"#e2564e","use":"Arms demand driven by every conflict theater","chokehold":"Turkey (Baykar drones), US/EU primes, China volume arms; the Iranian drone ecosystem on the other side","theaters":["ukraine","iran-us","sudan","kashmir","korea"],"signal":"Air-defense + counter-UAS + munitions replenishment is the demand center as three theaters (Iran, Ukraine, Red Sea) consume interceptors. Watch the NDAA resolution and Patriot-tech-transfer follow-through.","vectors":"Defense primes, drone/UAV makers, missile-defense; broad conflict escalation across the platform is the tailwind","catalyst":"JUL 13-16 2026 — US to SHARE Patriot air-defense TECHNOLOGY with Ukraine (not just batteries); Blanche AG hearing opens; NDAA ($1.15T) blocked 50-46 over the unauthorized Iran war. Around-the-clock Iran strikes = live munitions burn + air-defense demand. The Saudi axis diversifies to non-NATO suppliers (Korean/Ukrainian)."}];
const GROUPS = [{"id":"anti-hamas-forces","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Documented anti-Hamas insurgency reporting (2024-2026); UN Security Council Report on Gaza governance","name":"Popular Forces & anti-Hamas clans","full":"Yasser Abu Shabab's Popular Forces · Fatah-aligned and clan militias","affil":"PALESTINIAN · anti-Hamas · externally backed","icon":"◆","color":"#c78a4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The armed alternative Israel is building, in the territory it controls","theaters":["gaza","israel"],"mil":"An insurgency against Hamas inside Gaza that grew from 2024, operating principally in Israeli-controlled areas. Composed of the Popular Forces under Yasser Abu Shabab, Fatah-aligned groups and non-aligned clans, with reported Israeli support and alleged UAE backing. Hamas has killed several of its commanders; the movement has lost leaders in turn.","log":"Operates from within the roughly 64% of Gaza under Israeli military control as of April 2026 — which is both its protection and the limit of its reach.","eco":"Aid diversion, control of crossing-adjacent commerce, and external sponsorship.","gvn":"CONTESTS GOVERNANCE WITHOUT PROVIDING IT. These formations hold ground and levy, but none administers services at the scale Hamas did, and none is recognised by the National Committee for the Administration of Gaza — the technocratic body established in January under Board of Peace oversight as the sole legitimate transitional authority. The result is a governance vacuum with several armed claimants and one unarmed legitimate one.","assess":"Resolution 2803's roadmap requires Hamas to decommission and cease all military, policing and administrative activity. But disarmament that removes only Hamas leaves clan militias and externally-backed formations armed in the same territory — and ACLED forecasts continued localised clashes between Hamas and rival groups regardless of the ceasefire. Sponsoring an armed alternative to a group you are simultaneously demanding disarm is a coherent tactic and an incoherent endstate."}, {"id":"ogrf-transnistria","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"OSCE Mission to Moldova reporting; UN General Assembly resolutions on the withdrawal of foreign forces from Moldova — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"OGRF & Transnistrian forces","full":"Operational Group of Russian Forces · Transnistrian security structures","affil":"RUSSIAN · breakaway administration","icon":"⚑","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The garrison Moldova cannot remove and Russia cannot reinforce","theaters":["moldova","ukraine"],"mil":"A residual Russian contingent guarding the Cobasna ammunition depot — one of Europe's largest Soviet-era stockpiles — alongside Transnistrian security forces. Militarily static, but the depot's condition and the garrison's isolation are the variables: Ukraine's control of the eastern approach means Moscow cannot resupply or extract it.","log":"Isolated. Rotation and resupply run through Ukrainian or Moldovan territory, both closed — which makes this the most cut-off Russian deployment anywhere.","eco":"Transnistria's economy runs on subsidised Russian gas delivered through Moldova and on metals and manufacturing exports that go largely to the EU — a breakaway sustained by one patron and banked by the bloc it rejects.","gvn":"A FULLY ADMINISTERED UNRECOGNISED STATE. Runs its own currency, customs, courts, conscription and schooling, with a functioning bureaucracy after three decades. It is the template for what M23 and the RSF parallel administration are building toward — and evidence that unrecognised administrations can persist indefinitely when a patron pays and no one forces the question.","assess":"Nothing changed inside Transnistria; everything changed around it. Chișinău's formalised CIS exit, EU accession progress and the closure of Russian resupply have converted a stalemate into a slow squeeze, without a shot fired. Watch the gas subsidy: it is the mechanism, not the garrison."}, {"id":"rs-institutions","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUFOR Althea reporting; EU enlargement assessments; ICG Balkans","name":"Republika Srpska structures","full":"RS police, reserve formations and institutional apparatus","affil":"BOSNIAN SERB · entity-level","icon":"▣","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"Secession by legislation, not by mobilisation","theaters":["balkans"],"mil":"Not an insurgency. Entity-level police with reserve capacity and paramilitary-adjacent formations, against EUFOR Althea's residual deterrent presence. The threat here is institutional withdrawal from the Bosnian state rather than armed confrontation — which is precisely why the international toolkit designed for the 1990s does not grip.","log":"Entity budgets, Serbian and Russian political backing, and control of RS-level administration.","eco":"Entity-level revenue, public enterprise patronage and energy links to Serbia and Russia.","gvn":"GOVERNS A HALF-STATE AND IS TRYING TO LEAVE. Runs police, courts, education, health and revenue at entity level under Dayton, and has legislated to withdraw from state-level institutions. This is the inverse of the armed groups elsewhere on this board: it does not need to seize governance because it already holds it, and its project is subtraction rather than capture.","assess":"Recent analysis argues Russian destabilising influence here is containable given EU political will — which this board logs as a contrary signal. The RS project does not require Russian sponsorship to continue, and treating it as Russian-driven misreads a domestic secession programme with an external cheerleader."}, {"id":"caucasus-forces","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"SIPRI arms transfers database; OSCE record; ICG Caucasus reporting — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"Azerbaijani & Armenian forces","full":"National armed forces of the South Caucasus states","affil":"STATE · South Caucasus","icon":"⚑","color":"#5a8dc7","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"A settled military question and an unsettled political one","theaters":["caucasus","turkey"],"mil":"Azerbaijan holds decisive conventional superiority after Nagorno-Karabakh, built substantially on Turkish and Israeli systems; Armenia is rearming and reorienting procurement away from Russia after Moscow's non-intervention. The fighting question is resolved; the border delimitation, corridor access and diaspora politics are not.","log":"Azerbaijani hydrocarbon revenue and Turkish alignment; Armenian diversification toward France, India and the EU.","eco":"Azerbaijan now EXPORTS NATURAL GAS TO SYRIA and is considering expanding to neighbours — energy leverage extended into the post-Assad Levant, which is a new vector this board tracks.","assess":"A decisive military outcome ended a thirty-year conflict, and the extraction reading applies to the victor's energy position rather than to the war itself. Armenia's turn away from Russia after Moscow declined to intervene is the clearest instance on this board of a security guarantee failing and a state simply changing patron."}, {"id":"csto","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"CSTO official record; SIPRI; Carnegie Politika","name":"CSTO","full":"Collective Security Treaty Organization","affil":"RUSSIAN-LED · treaty alliance","icon":"◈","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"WATCH","strength":"Deployed once, and the deployment is why members now hedge","theaters":["kazakhstan","russia-domestic","belarus"],"mil":"A Russian-led mutual defence treaty whose only significant operational deployment was to Kazakhstan in January 2022. Armenia has effectively frozen its participation after Moscow declined to act over Nagorno-Karabakh; Central Asian members maintain membership while building alternatives.","log":"Russian basing, training and equipment supply — all constrained by the demands of the Ukraine war.","eco":"Not a revenue actor; the relevant economics are the arms and energy dependencies membership has historically implied.","gvn":"AN ALLIANCE WITHOUT AN ENFORCEMENT RECORD. Its founding promise is intervention on request; the one time a member invoked it against internal unrest it worked, and the one time a member needed it against an external adversary it did not come. Members have drawn the obvious conclusion — Uzbekistan's partnership sequence with Kazakhstan, Georgia and Belarus builds westward corridors that route around the guarantor.","assess":"Central Asia's corridor diplomacy and Armenia's reorientation are both responses to the same finding: the alliance protects regimes from their populations more reliably than it protects states from adversaries."}, {"id":"eufor-kfor","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUFOR Althea and KFOR mission reporting; NATO records","name":"EUFOR Althea & KFOR","full":"EU and NATO stabilisation forces in the Western Balkans","affil":"EU / NATO · peace support","icon":"○","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"WATCH","strength":"Small, symbolic, and the reason nothing has restarted","theaters":["balkans","nato-flank"],"mil":"Residual multinational forces in Bosnia and Kosovo, reinforced during periods of tension. Their function is deterrence by presence rather than capability — neither is sized to fight, and both exist so that any escalation would require attacking an EU or NATO deployment.","log":"Member-state contributions on rotation; reserve forces held over the horizon.","eco":"Not applicable — externally funded stabilisation deployments.","assess":"A few thousand troops have held two post-conflict settlements for two decades at a fraction of the cost of the wars they replaced. The risk is that the arrangement's success makes it look unnecessary — enlargement fatigue and drawdown pressure are the variables to watch, not a resurgence of 1990s-style mobilisation."}, {"id":"hezbollah","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UNIFIL ceasefire-violation reporting; Rome talks coverage (Jul 2026); ICG Lebanon","name":"Hezbollah","full":"Hizb Allah · Lebanese Shia movement, militia and party","affil":"LEBANESE · Iran-aligned","icon":"⚔","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Degraded militarily, undefeated politically — and the subject of the Rome talks","theaters":["lebanon","israel","iran-us","syria"],"mil":"Substantially degraded by the 2024-26 wars — leadership decapitated, arsenal reduced, and the Syrian resupply corridor now interdicted by the al-Sharaa government that replaced its guarantor. Israeli operations continue against its operatives near Nabatieh even as direct talks run in Rome. Retains the capacity to reconstitute if the supply line reopens, which is precisely what the disarmament question is about.","log":"Historically the Damascus land bridge from Iran, now cut by Syrian interdiction; residual smuggling through the Lebanon-Syria frontier and a financial network operating outside the Lebanese banking system.","eco":"Iranian funding, its own financial institutions, and control of import channels — alongside a reconstruction fund that pays out where the Lebanese state cannot.","gvn":"A PARALLEL WELFARE STATE, AND THAT IS THE OBSTACLE. Runs schools, clinics, reconstruction payments and a financial network independent of Lebanon's collapsed banking system, alongside ministerial representation in government. Disarmament is therefore not a security transaction but a political one — the weapons are attached to a constituency that receives services no other institution provides, and no party at the Rome table has offered to replace them.","assess":"Hezbollah is the subject of the Israel-Lebanon negotiations, a principal casualty of the Iran war, and the reason Damascus interdicting Iranian weapons matters — yet it had no file here. The analytical point its absence obscured: military degradation has not produced political defeat, because the organisation's hold rests on service provision in a state that provides almost none. Any disarmament that does not answer who pays for the clinics is a plan for the group's reconstitution."}, {"id":"settler-outposts","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN OCHA incident record (1,800+ attacks, 2025); Peace Now / Kerem Navot outpost mapping; OHCHR statements","name":"Settler outpost network","full":"West Bank settler militias · hilltop outposts · settlement security squads","affil":"ISRAELI · settler movement","icon":"◆","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Holds nearly a fifth of the West Bank — and is rarely prosecuted","theaters":["west-bank","israel"],"mil":"An armed civilian movement operating from hilltop outposts, with settlement security squads that are state-armed and in some cases formally coordinated with the military — one of the two Israelis killed at Tal was a settlement security coordinator. UN reporting describes escalating settler violence occurring with the acquiescence, support, and in some cases participation of Israeli security forces. OCHA documented more than 1,800 attacks causing casualties or property damage in 2025 across about 280 communities, five a day and the highest rate since records began in 2006, rising more than 50% in the first half of 2026.","log":"State-supplied weapons through settlement security squads, road access unavailable to Palestinians, and outposts that are retroactively legalised rather than removed.","eco":"Land itself is the asset. Peace Now assesses that outposts now control nearly a fifth of the West Bank; over 50,000 new housing units and 98 new settlements have been advanced since December 2022, alongside a state tourism development programme.","gvn":"GOVERNS THROUGH THE STATE, NOT AGAINST IT. Outposts function as forward settlement, then receive retroactive legalisation, state-supplied weapons through settlement security squads, road access, planning permission and utilities. This is the inverse of every other entry here — an armed movement whose governance is provided BY the state whose deniability it preserves.","assess":"This is the actor that makes de facto annexation work without a declaration: the state supplies the planning, permits and demolition orders while an armed civilian movement does the displacing, which preserves deniability the state would lose if soldiers did it. Impunity is the enabling condition — UN monitors record that settlers are rarely prosecuted and soldiers seldom convicted. Netanyahu's characterisation of this as '150 or so juvenile delinquents' is contradicted by his own territory's incident record."}, {"id":"nablus-jenin-brigades","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"UN OCHA occupied Palestinian territory incident record; OHCHR reporting on West Bank operations (2026) — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"Nablus & Jenin brigades","full":"Localised Palestinian armed formations · refugee-camp based","affil":"PALESTINIAN · fragmented local","icon":"⚔","color":"#5a8dc7","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"Fragmented, local, and outside anyone's command","theaters":["west-bank"],"mil":"Loose armed formations rooted in the Nablus and Jenin refugee camps, drawing fighters across factional lines — Fatah-linked, Islamic Jihad and unaffiliated — which is why they answer neither to the Palestinian Authority nor reliably to any faction leadership. Small arms and improvised devices; the target set is Israeli military raids and settler incursions rather than sustained offensive capability.","log":"Dense camp terrain, local support, and weapons circulating through a market the PA cannot police in areas it does not control.","eco":"Not a resource-financed actor — community-supported, which is also what makes suppression by raid self-defeating.","assess":"These formations grew precisely where the Palestinian Authority stopped functioning as a security provider or a political horizon — and the fiscal campaign now closing the PA's banking channels will widen that space rather than narrow it. Israel's own strategy of collapsing the Authority produces the armed fragmentation its raids are then mounted against."}, {"id":"idf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Operational reporting across Gaza, Lebanon, Syria and the Iran exchange (Jul 2026); UNIFIL ceasefire-violation reporting","name":"IDF","full":"Israel Defense Forces","affil":"ISRAELI STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#4a7dc7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Operating on four fronts at once — and the reason each has a ceiling","theaters":["israel","gaza","lebanon","syria","iran-us","west-bank"],"mil":"Conducting simultaneous operations in four theaters: strikes inside Gaza during an eroding ceasefire, Commando Brigade actions near Nabatieh in Lebanon while direct talks run in Rome, ground incursions into Quneitra in Syria, and participation in the Iran exchange. Regional air superiority and demonstrated long-range reach are the standing capability; sustained reservist mobilisation is the constraint.","log":"US munitions resupply and intelligence sharing — the dependency that makes Washington the binding external limit on tempo.","eco":"Funded by the Israeli state budget with substantial US military assistance; the cost of four fronts, reservist call-ups and displaced northern communities is now an electoral issue ahead of the 27 October vote.","assess":"No escalation read on Gaza, Lebanon, Syria or Iran is complete without this force, because in each the IDF holds the initiative and the question is restraint rather than capability. Its political direction is decided on 27 October — though the leading challenger is the general who commanded it, and differs from the incumbent more in style than in policy."}, {"id":"pla","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Joint Sea-2026 reporting (Jul 2026); ADIZ incursion reporting; Asia Society Policy Institute","name":"PLA","full":"People's Liberation Army","affil":"CHINESE STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Grey-zone pressure as doctrine — and purges as the internal story","theaters":["china","taiwan","south-china-sea","korea"],"mil":"Conducted Joint Sea-2026 with Russia off Qingdao (6-13 Jul) followed by a joint Pacific patrol, and a joint strategic aerial patrol that entered South Korea's ADIZ. Standing instruments in the Taiwan Strait and South China Sea are grey-zone — pressure below the threshold of force. Intensified purges are running through the military-industrial complex.","log":"Domestic industrial base and shipbuilding capacity; the Russian partnership provides reach without the commitment of a formal alliance.","eco":"Funded from a budget under strain from a 4.3% growth quarter, unresolved property debt and local-government finances — the constraint most often left out of capability assessments.","assess":"Xi's declared doctrine is avoiding the Thucydides Trap — raised by name with Trump in May — while the force conducts joint operations that normalise a two-power presence across the Pacific. This platform holds both on the record: what the PLA can do, and what Beijing says it is for."}, {"id":"indian-forces","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Ladakh unrest reporting (Sep 2025); ACLED South Asia; ICG","name":"Indian security forces","full":"Indian Armed Forces · paramilitary · police","affil":"INDIAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Two nuclear borders and a domestic policing problem it keeps escalating","theaters":["india","kashmir"],"mil":"Deployed on the Line of Control with Pakistan and the disputed Ladakh frontier with China, alongside counter-militancy operations in Kashmir. Domestically the response to protest has been police and paramilitary — tear gas and baton charges in Delhi in July 2026, and additional armed-forces deployment during the September 2025 Leh unrest that killed four and injured 87.","log":"State budget and domestic production; the Ladakh frontier is supplied over some of the most difficult terrain any army holds.","eco":"Not a resource-financed actor — but the Ladakh autonomy demand it polices is substantially about land, water and forest rights the Sixth Schedule would devolve.","assess":"The counterparty to LeT, JeM and TRF on one side of the Line of Control, and to unarmed protesters on the other side of the Ladakh question. The July 2026 concession in Delhi came after tear gas rather than instead of it — which is the lesson this force has now taught the next movement."}, {"id":"sspdf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"CFR Global Conflict Tracker; ICG; UN Commission on Human Rights in South Sudan","name":"SSPDF","full":"South Sudan People's Defence Forces","affil":"SOUTH SUDANESE STATE · Dinka-dominated","icon":"⚑","color":"#4a7dc7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Kiir's army, and the instrument of the succession crackdown","theaters":["south-sudan"],"mil":"The national army under President Kiir, Dinka-dominated, backed by allied Agwelek militia and Ugandan air support. Launched Operation Enduring Peace in Jonglei in January 2026 and conducted the Nasir operations. Never unified with opposition forces as the 2018 deal required — security-sector reform was never implemented.","log":"Oil-funded state patronage, Ugandan air support, and the arms that unification was meant to consolidate but never did.","eco":"Funded by the oil revenue that is nearly the entire state budget — which is why control of the army and control of the oil money are the same fight.","gvn":"THE ARMY IS THE PATRONAGE SYSTEM. Funded by oil revenue that constitutes nearly the entire state budget, which is why control of the army and control of the oil money are the same contest. The 2018 agreement required merging government and opposition forces into one national army; that never happened, and the unreformed security sector is precisely why the peace could be abandoned from the top.","assess":"The 2018 deal required merging government and opposition forces into one army; it never happened. The SSPDF stayed Kiir's instrument, and in 2025-26 it became the tool for dismantling the unity government — the unreformed security sector is exactly why the peace could be abandoned from the top."}, {"id":"splm-io","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"CFR Global Conflict Tracker; ICG; UN reporting","name":"SPLM-IO","full":"Sudan People's Liberation Movement-in-Opposition","affil":"SOUTH SUDANESE · opposition · Nuer-aligned","icon":"⚔","color":"#c77d4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Machar's movement — decapitated but not dissolved","theaters":["south-sudan"],"mil":"The main opposition force under Riek Machar, drawn substantially from Nuer constituencies. With Machar under arrest and on trial for treason, its command is disrupted, but it formally declared the 2018 agreement defunct and its allied forces remain in the field in Upper Nile and Jonglei.","log":"Nuer community mobilisation, the loose alliance with the White Army, and whatever external sympathy Sudan's SAF historically offered Machar.","eco":"Not a resource-financed force — dependent on community support and control of contested rural areas.","gvn":"AN OPPOSITION WITHOUT AN ADMINISTRATION. Holds constituencies rather than territory it governs, and with Machar jailed it has no figure able to negotiate on its behalf. Removing the leader dismantled the unity government without dismantling the movement — which makes the war harder to stop rather than easier.","assess":"Jailing Machar dismantled the unity government but did not end his movement — it removed the one figure who could negotiate on its behalf, which may make the war harder to stop rather than easier. An opposition without a leader to arrest is an opposition without a leader to make peace."}, {"id":"nuer-white-army","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"CFR; ICG; 2025 Nasir clashes and 2026 Jonglei offensive reporting","name":"Nuer White Army","full":"Nuer White Army · ethnic community militia","affil":"SOUTH SUDANESE · Nuer communal","icon":"◆","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The militia that broke Nasir — and moves on its own","theaters":["south-sudan"],"mil":"An ethnic Nuer community militia, loosely aligned with Machar's SPLM-IO but mobilising substantially on its own. Overran the SSPDF barracks at Nasir in March 2025 and drives much of the northeastern fighting. Named for the white ash fighters use against insects.","log":"Autonomous cattle-camp mobilisation, community weapons, and intimate knowledge of the Upper Nile terrain.","eco":"Cattle, local resources, and the contested grazing and oil-adjacent territory of Upper Nile.","gvn":"MOBILISES WITHOUT GOVERNING. Authority derives from cattle-camp and age-set structures rather than any administrative apparatus, which is why it answers to no faction leadership reliably and why a ceasefire signed above it may not bind it. The autonomous communal layer is what makes South Sudan's war resistant to elite bargains.","assess":"The White Army is loosely aligned with Machar but not controlled by him — which is why arresting him did not stop the war and why a ceasefire signed at the top may not bind the fighters in the field. It is the autonomous communal violence that makes South Sudan's war so resistant to elite bargains, and the reason a battle for Malakal could trigger massacres beyond anyone's control."}, {"id":"eritrea-forces","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ACLED assessment of Eritrean positioning behind a TPLF western Tigray campaign (Jun 2026); local ACLED sources on Eritrean training of Fano militias","name":"Eritrean forces","full":"Eritrean Defence Forces · cross-border factor","affil":"ERITREAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#c74a4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The border army that fought inside Tigray — and did not fully leave","theaters":["ethiopia"],"mil":"Eritrean troops fought alongside the Ethiopian federal army during the 2020-22 Tigray war, implicated by UN investigators in massacres and mass rape. The Pretoria agreement did not include Asmara, and Eritrean forces or proxies remained a factor on the border. Addis has separately accused Asmara of backing armed groups inside Ethiopia; Asmara's own posture is opaque by design.","log":"The militarised Eritrea-Ethiopia frontier, Asmara's total mobilisation system, and cross-border proxy relationships.","eco":"Not a resource actor here — a security and territorial one; the Assab port question is the strategic prize.","assess":"The agreement that ended the Tigray war did not bind Eritrea, which is why the platform's Assab-axis tripwire watches this border: a shooting incident here would reopen the war the paper closed, with a nuclear-free but heavily militarised neighbour that answers to no ceasefire it did not sign."}, {"id":"tdf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ACLED (TPLF-government clashes 2026); Africa Center for Strategic Studies","name":"Tigray Defense Forces","full":"TDF · armed wing of the TPLF","affil":"ETHIOPIAN · Tigrayan","icon":"⚔","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"The force Pretoria was meant to disarm — and did not","theaters":["ethiopia"],"mil":"The military wing of the TPLF, which fought the 2020-22 war to a negotiated end at Pretoria. Disarmament was never completed; in 2026 ACLED records SPORADIC ARMED CLASHES between TDF and government forces in disputed territories and along the border. The TPLF has since fractured into rival factions contesting the interim administration.","log":"Post-Pretoria force structure never fully dismantled; the disputed Western Tigray territories; a region under federal economic restriction.","eco":"A region under federal economic pressure with four-fifths of the population needing aid — the fiscal instrument replacing the military one.","gvn":"CONTESTED INTERIM AUTHORITY. Operates alongside a Tigray interim administration that has fractured into rival factions, under federal economic restriction leaving most of the region dependent on aid. Governance capacity is the variable that determines whether Tigray's next phase is politics or war.","assess":"Pretoria stopped the shooting without completing the demobilisation, leaving an intact armed force inside a fractured movement, in disputed territory, under economic siege. The platform treats Tigray as the most likely site of Ethiopia's next open war precisely because the TDF still exists."}, {"id":"boko-haram-lake","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ICG On the Horizon (Jul-Dec 2026); ACLED Lake Chad","name":"Boko Haram (Lake Chad)","full":"JAS / ISWAP factions on the Chadian shore","affil":"NIGERIAN-ORIGIN · cross-border","icon":"☠","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The reason N'Djamena declared a state of emergency in Lake province","theaters":["chad","nigeria","cameroon","niger"],"mil":"The Lake Chad factions of Boko Haram (JAS) and ISWAP operating on Chad's islands and shore. Killed 23 Chadian soldiers at Barka Tolorom in May 2026; N'Djamena declared a STATE OF EMERGENCY in LAKE PROVINCE in May and is mounting an airstrike-and-ground response. The weakened Multinational Joint Task Force — after Chad and Niger's withdrawals — has removed the coordination that once contained them.","log":"The lake's islands and reed channels as sanctuary; cross-border movement between four states; fish and pepper trade taxation.","eco":"Taxation of the lake economy — fishing, farming, smuggling — and abduction.","gvn":"TAXES THE CHADIAN SHORE. Levies fishing, farming and the island smuggling economy across Lake Chad's Chadian waters, with rudimentary adjudication where it holds ground. The state of emergency in Lake province is a response to a revenue base the collapsed Multinational Joint Task Force can no longer pressure across borders.","assess":"Chad and Niger left the Multinational Joint Task Force, the one mechanism that made the Lake Chad basin governable across borders. The insurgency exploits exactly the seams the withdrawals reopened — and N'Djamena is now considering resuming French cooperation to restore the surveillance it gave up."}, {"id":"fact-ccmsr","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"UN Panel of Experts on Libya (southern Libya sanctuary); ICG Chad country reporting — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"FACT & northern rebels","full":"Front for Change and Concord in Chad · CCMSR","affil":"CHADIAN · northern rebel","icon":"⚔","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The rebellion that killed a president","theaters":["chad","libya"],"mil":"Northern Chadian rebel movements based partly in southern Libya. FACT's 2021 offensive killed President Idriss Déby at the front, bringing his son Mahamat to power. The groups retain rear areas in Libya's ungoverned south, tied to the Haftar-Russia logistics geography.","log":"Southern Libya sanctuary, Toubou fighter networks, and the trans-Saharan smuggling routes.","eco":"Gold, migrant smuggling and cross-border trafficking through the Chad-Libya-Sudan triangle.","gvn":"EXTRACTS, DOES NOT ADMINISTER. Northern Chadian rebel formations operating from southern Libyan sanctuary, financed through gold, migrant smuggling and trans-Saharan trafficking. No civil structure — which is why their 2021 offensive could kill a president without producing a government, and why Chad's succession passed to the son rather than to them.","assess":"The dynastic succession's original threat. Mahamat Déby rules because his father died fighting these groups; their Libyan sanctuary ties Chad's stability to the Haftar-Russia arrangement next door, and to whatever Sudan's war does to the shared frontier."}, {"id":"africa-corps-car","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Panel of Experts on CAR; The Sentry","name":"Africa Corps (CAR)","full":"Russian Africa Corps · ex-Wagner · CAR deployment","affil":"RUSSIAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"The regime's guarantor, paid in gold","theaters":["car"],"mil":"The former Wagner deployment, now under Russian state control, that provides regime protection for Touadéra. Repelled the 2021 rebel assault on Bangui and has been the decisive force since. UN Panel documentation records abuses against civilians alongside the security function.","log":"Russian airlift and materiel; the Ndassima gold operation as payment; basing across the government-held zone.","eco":"PAID IN MINING CONCESSIONS — gold at Ndassima, diamonds, timber. The prototype of the resource-for-security model exported to the Sahel.","assess":"This deployment proved a government could be kept in office indefinitely in exchange for extraction rights. Everything Africa Corps does in Mali and Burkina was rehearsed here first."}, {"id":"cpc-car","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"UN Panel of Experts on the Central African Republic; MINUSCA reporting; ICG CAR — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"CPC rebels","full":"Coalition of Patriots for Change · ex-Séléka and allied factions","affil":"CENTRAL AFRICAN · rebel coalition","icon":"◆","color":"#c77d4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Nearly took the capital in 2021","theaters":["car"],"mil":"A coalition of armed groups, many drawn from the former mostly-Muslim Séléka, that launched the 2021 offensive nearly reaching Bangui before Russian and Rwandan forces repelled it. Retains rural territorial presence across the interior; the 2019 Khartoum agreement that was meant to demobilise it is largely hollow.","log":"Rural base areas, cross-border rear zones in Chad and Sudan, and control of interior roads and mining sites.","eco":"Diamonds, gold, cattle and roadblock taxation in the territory it holds.","gvn":"TAXES THE ROADS. Holds rural territory across the CAR interior with roadblock levies, control of mining sites and cattle taxation, but little administration — the 2019 Khartoum agreement that was meant to demobilise it is hollow, and its presence is extractive rather than governmental.","assess":"The armed expression of the question CAR has never answered: who belongs to the nation. Built from the Séléka that the anti-balaka mobilised against, it is the unresolved 2013 war in coalition form — and the reason Touadéra needed a foreign guarantor at all."}, {"id":"anti-balaka","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"UN Panel of Experts on the Central African Republic; ICC proceedings on anti-balaka commanders; MINUSCA — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"Anti-balaka","full":"Anti-balaka militias","affil":"CENTRAL AFRICAN · mostly-Christian communal","icon":"◇","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"CPC's communal mirror","theaters":["car"],"mil":"Mostly-Christian and animist communal militias formed in 2013 against the Séléka, responsible for reciprocal massacres of Muslim civilians. Fragmented and localised, some elements later co-opted by the state, others still operating autonomously.","log":"Village-level mobilisation, local weapons, and periodic informal alignment with state forces.","eco":"Local levies, cattle, and control of village-level trade.","assess":"The other half of CAR's sectarian fracture. The Séléka/anti-balaka war of 2013-16 was never resolved, only frozen under Russian guns — and the communal militias remain the evidence that the Russian arrangement bought stability without buying reconciliation."}, {"id":"macawisley","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ACLED; ICG Horn of Africa","name":"Macawisley","full":"Macawisley · clan-based community uprising militias","affil":"SOMALI · clan self-defence","icon":"✧","color":"#5a8dc7","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Drove the 2022-23 gains that actually held against al-Shabaab","theaters":["somalia"],"mil":"Clan-based community militias — named for the macawis sarong fighters wore — whose 2022-23 risings in Hirshabelle and Galmudug produced the most significant territorial losses al-Shabaab has suffered in a decade. When federal-clan relations soured, several of the gains reversed.","log":"Clan mobilisation, federal weapons where relations allow, and intimate local knowledge of al-Shabaab's tax networks.","eco":"Community resources and the reclaimed tax base of liberated districts.","assess":"The gains against al-Shabaab that held came from clan militias defending their own districts — not AMISOM, ATMIS, US airstrikes or the AU mission. Somalia's security is a function of clan settlement, the one variable external missions cannot supply."}, {"id":"rada-444","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Panel of Experts on Libya; ICG","name":"Tripoli brigades","full":"RADA (SDF) · 444 Brigade · Stability Support Apparatus","affil":"LIBYAN WESTERN · rival state-aligned","icon":"▣","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The capital held by competing payrolls that periodically fight","theaters":["libya"],"mil":"The distinct armed groups nominally under the Tripoli government that actually control it: the Special Deterrence Force (RADA), the 444 Brigade, and the Stability Support Apparatus (SSA). They periodically FIGHT EACH OTHER for control of ministries and revenue nodes — the August 2025 and earlier clashes killed dozens in the capital.","log":"State salaries, captured stocks, and control of the institutions that fund rivals.","eco":"CAPTURE OF STATE INSTITUTIONS — central bank access, letters of credit, fuel-subsidy arbitrage, and the EU-funded migrant-interception economy.","assess":"These brigades do not build a state by making war; they make war to capture the state's revenue functions. Naming them separately from 'GNU-aligned militias' shows the western government is not a chain of command but a set of competing payrolls under one flag."}, {"id":"rdf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts on the DRC; US Treasury sanctions (Mar 2026)","name":"Rwandan Defence Force","full":"RDF · Rwanda’s national army","affil":"RWANDAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#4a9dc7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"The most effective conventional force in the Great Lakes — and a sanctioned one","theaters":["rwanda","drc","mozambique"],"mil":"Small, professional, and decisive. Documented by successive UN Groups of Experts as providing troops, equipment and command support to M23 in eastern DRC — denied by Kigali. Sanctioned by the US in March 2026 on that basis. Simultaneously a leading UN peacekeeping contributor and a PAID security provider in Mozambique’s Cabo Delgado and CAR.","log":"Cross-border resupply into eastern DRC; airlift and logistics for expeditionary deployments; Rwandan defence budget plus the revenue of the security-export business.","eco":"Its DRC operations coincide with mineral re-exports — coltan, tin, tungsten, gold — that exceed plausible Rwandan production. The UN Panel’s central finding: export volumes track M23 territorial control.","gvn":"SELLS GOVERNANCE ABROAD WHILE BEING SANCTIONED FOR ITS ABSENCE NEXT DOOR. Rwanda's army provides paid security in Mozambique's LNG zone and CAR — functioning as a service export — while under US sanctions for backing a rebellion in the DRC whose territorial gains track Rwandan mineral export figures. The same institution is both the product and the problem.","assess":"The RDF is punished for causing insecurity in one country and paid to provide it in two others. It is the instrument of the contradiction the Rwanda file holds: a real security mandate, a documented proxy war, and a mineral trade, executed by one of Africa’s most capable militaries."}, {"id":"updf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts on the DRC; ICJ reparations ruling; Operation Shujaa reporting","name":"UPDF","full":"Uganda People’s Defence Force","affil":"UGANDAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Deployed against M23, in support of M23, and against the ADF — simultaneously","theaters":["uganda","drc","somalia"],"mil":"Large and experienced, with deployments in Somalia (AU mission), South Sudan and eastern DRC. Runs Operation Shujaa against the Ugandan-origin ADF jointly with Congolese forces — while UN experts document Ugandan support to M23, and while Ugandan troops ALSO blocked M23’s Lubero advance. The ICJ has ordered Uganda to pay the DRC reparations for its earlier conduct.","log":"Gold that exits through Kampala, Western training and equipment bought by peacekeeping contributions, and control of Lake Victoria and cross-border routes.","eco":"Ugandan gold exports far exceed domestic production; UN experts trace the surplus to eastern DRC. The UPDF’s presence in the east is inseparable from that trade.","assess":"One army holds three incompatible-looking positions at once, and they have so far been compatible because one man manages them. The UPDF is where Museveni’s balancing act becomes operational — and where the succession would first show strain."}, {"id":"fdnb","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts on the DRC; Critical Threats / CTP (Uvira offensive)","name":"FDNB","full":"Burundi National Defence Force","affil":"BURUNDIAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"An expeditionary war its treasury cannot afford","theaters":["burundi","drc"],"mil":"Deployed in eastern DRC alongside the Congolese army and Wazalendo militias, and engaged directly against the M23-aligned Twirwaneho at Minembwe. Also a troop contributor in Somalia. As Uvira fell, hundreds of Burundian and Congolese troops retreated into Burundi.","log":"State supply strained by a foreign exchange and fuel crisis; the deployment runs on a budget in emergency.","eco":"Almost no resource base — Burundi is the least diversified state in the theatre. Its contribution is manpower, which is precisely what is bankrupting it.","assess":"The FDNB fights a better-armed adversary on another state’s territory during a currency and fuel emergency. CTP assesses the Uvira offensive was launched partly to knock it out of the war — and it nearly did."}, {"id":"red-tabara","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts on the DRC; ICG Great Lakes","name":"RED-Tabara","full":"Résistance pour un État de droit au Burundi","affil":"BURUNDIAN · rebel · DRC-based","icon":"✧","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The reason Bujumbura and Kigali accuse each other","theaters":["burundi","drc"],"mil":"The most active Burundian rebel movement, operating from bases in eastern DRC. Bujumbura accuses Rwanda of backing it — the mirror of Kigali’s accusation that Burundi hosts the FDLR. Has claimed attacks inside Burundi, including near the capital.","log":"Congolese highland sanctuary and the South Kivu border; alleged external sponsorship that both sides trade as accusation.","eco":"Local support and cross-border movement; not a resource-financed force.","assess":"RED-Tabara is to Burundi what the FDLR is to Rwanda — an exile armed group on Congolese soil that each government cites to justify its posture toward the other. The symmetry is the point: the DRC is where both states’ domestic insurgencies are hosted, which is why the Great Lakes war cannot be settled two-by-two."}, {"id":"isis-remnant","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"CFR Global Conflict Tracker · camps transferred to government control, Jan 2026","name":"Islamic State remnants","full":"IS · Syria and Iraq residual networks","affil":"TRANSNATIONAL · Islamic State","icon":"☠","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The camps changed hands in January 2026 — the detainee problem did not","theaters":["syria","iraq"],"mil":"Territorially defeated in 2019 but operating as insurgent cells across the Syrian Badiya and northern Iraq, attacking security forces, oil infrastructure and civilians. Its principal asset is not military: roughly 10,000 fighters and tens of thousands of family members remain in SDF-run detention at al-Hol, Roj and associated prisons, with repatriation stalled because states will not take their nationals back.","log":"Desert cells, sleeper networks in liberated areas, and prison-break operations aimed at the detention camps.","eco":"Extortion, kidnapping, and residual cash reserves moved through the Al-Karrar network in Somalia and other regional offices.","assess":"CORRECTED, JAN 2026: the detention camps holding tens of thousands of ISIS detainees and family members passed to Syrian government control when Damascus captured the northeast. The custodian changed; the problem did not. No state will repatriate its nationals, no legal process exists, and responsibility now sits with a transitional government whose own institutional capacity is unproven and whose forces were until recently an al-Qaeda affiliate. Al-Hol is still where the counter-ISIS campaign's political failure is physically stored — it simply has a new landlord."}, {"id":"assad-remnants","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Documented record of the 2025 Syrian coastal massacres (SOHR, SNHR and Reuters investigation casualty ranges); UN Commission of Inquiry on Syria","name":"Assad-era networks","full":"Former regime security apparatus · Alawite-linked militias · captagon networks","affil":"SYRIAN · former regime","icon":"○","color":"#7d5ac7","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The coastal killings ran for a year, and the state was among the perpetrators","theaters":["syria","lebanon"],"mil":"Residual networks from the fallen government: intelligence-service personnel, Alawite-linked local militias in the coastal governorates, and the smuggling structures built around captagon production. Coastal violence in 2025 involved these networks and produced the transition's worst sectarian episode.","log":"Local community bases in Latakia and Tartus, Lebanese border smuggling routes, and Iranian residual contacts.","eco":"CAPTAGON — a multi-billion-dollar industrial drug trade the old government ran as state policy. Dismantling it removes the transition's biggest illicit revenue pool and the coastal economy's employer of last resort simultaneously.","assess":"The March 2025 coastal violence killed an estimated 1,000-2,000 people, the majority civilians — SOHR records 1,659 civilians and 259 Alawite militants against 273 security forces; SNHR records 639+ civilians and disarmed fighters killed by government-aligned forces. Reuters documented 1,479 Alawites killed in three days, characterised largely by direct targeting of civilians. THE PERPETRATOR LIST INCLUDES THE STATE: Syrian Armed Forces, the General Security Service, Syrian National Army units including the Hamza and Sultan Suleiman Shah Divisions, the Turkistan Islamic Party, and Saraya Ansar al-Sunnah. Gunmen went to homes and asked whether residents were Alawite or Sunni. THE VIOLENCE HAS CONTINUED PAST THE MAIN PHASE, not stopped with it — and this is the file's clearest evidence that al-Sharaa's government either cannot control its own coalition or does not intend to."}, {"id":"ukr-partisans","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ISW/Critical Threats Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment (25 Jul 2026); Atesh operational reporting (Jul 2026)","name":"Occupied-territory resistance","full":"Ukrainian partisan networks · Atesh · Crimean and southern cells","affil":"UKRAINIAN · occupied territories","icon":"✧","color":"#4a9dc7","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"Operating inside the Russian army \u2014 not only behind its lines","theaters":["ukraine"],"mil":"Clandestine networks operating inside Russian-occupied Ukraine — including the Atesh movement, which recruits among Crimean Tatars and Russian soldiers — conducting reconnaissance for strikes, sabotage of rail and fuel infrastructure, and targeted attacks on collaborationist officials. Occupation authorities respond with filtration, detention and deportation.","log":"Local knowledge, encrypted communication, and targeting data passed to Ukrainian long-range fires.","eco":"Not a revenue actor — externally supported and locally staffed.","assess":"The dimension the front-line map cannot show. Russia's hold on occupied territory is a policing problem as much as a military one, and the deportation of children and filtration of adults are the instruments — which is why the ICC warrants target that programme rather than battlefield conduct."}, {"id":"wagner-legacy","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ISW/Critical Threats assessment on DPRK personnel and Russian casualty rates (25 Jul 2026); Kyiv Post on recruitment raids targeting debtors and ex-prisoners (Jun 2026)","name":"Russian irregular formations","full":"Akhmat · Storm-Z penal units · absorbed Wagner structures","affil":"RUSSIAN · state-directed irregulars","icon":"▲","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The manpower system underneath the official one","theaters":["ukraine","russia-domestic"],"mil":"The layer beneath Russia's regular order of battle: Kadyrov's Akhmat formations, penal assault units recruited from prisons, volunteer battalions raised by regions and corporations, and the remnants of Wagner absorbed after the 2023 mutiny. These units absorb the casualties that would otherwise force a politically costly general mobilisation.","log":"Prison recruitment, regional quotas, and corporate sponsorship of units — a distributed system designed to keep losses off the federal ledger.","eco":"Enlistment bonuses and death payments that exceed regional annual wages by multiples — which is the recruitment mechanism, concentrated in Russia's poorest republics.","gvn":"RECRUITMENT IS THE GOVERNANCE FUNCTION. Enlistment bonuses and death payments set at multiples of regional annual wages, concentrated in Buryatia, Dagestan and Tuva — an administrative system for converting the poverty of the internal periphery into manpower for the centre's war. The imperial extraction relationship this platform tracks abroad, running inward.","assess":"Moscow buys soldiers from Buryatia, Dagestan and Tuva at prices that only work where alternatives are absent. The periphery pays the imperial centre's war in bodies — the same relationship the Russia-domestic file describes in fiscal terms."}, {"id":"tpnpb-opm","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"Human Rights Watch Indonesia/Papua reporting; IPAC (Institute for Policy Analysis of Conflict); ACLED — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"TPNPB-OPM","full":"West Papua National Liberation Army · Free Papua Movement","affil":"PAPUAN · INDONESIAN state · separatist","icon":"✧","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Fighting the world's largest gold mine","theaters":["indonesia"],"mil":"The armed wing of the Free Papua Movement, waging a low-intensity insurgency in Indonesia's Papuan provinces since the 1960s. Its tactics include attacks on security forces, migrant workers and infrastructure, and high-profile hostage-taking — a New Zealand pilot was held for over a year. Indonesia has designated it a terrorist organisation and deployed heavy force; access for journalists and independent monitors is severely restricted, so casualty figures are contested and displacement is undercounted.","log":"Highland terrain, porous Papua New Guinea border, and weapons bought or captured from Indonesian forces.","eco":"THE POINT OF THE WAR. Papua hosts the GRASBERG COMPLEX — among the largest gold and copper deposits on earth, operated by Freeport-McMoRan with the Indonesian state now the majority holder. Papua is simultaneously Indonesia's most resource-rich and poorest region. Nickel, timber and palm oil expansion drive further dispossession.","assess":"A platform arguing that extraction drives conflict, which tracked fifty-five theaters without listing the insurgency fighting over the world's biggest goldmine, had a blind spot shaped exactly like its own thesis — in Asia rather than Africa. The 1969 'Act of Free Choice', in which 1,025 hand-picked electors voted unanimously to join Indonesia under military supervision, is the decolonisation fraud at the root; Freeport's contract predates it by two years."}, {"id":"is-sinai","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"UN Analytical Support and Sanctions Monitoring Team reports on ISIL affiliates; Egyptian Armed Forces operational statements — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"IS-Sinai","full":"Wilayat Sinai · formerly Ansar Bayt al-Maqdis","affil":"EGYPTIAN · Islamic State province","icon":"☠","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"Degraded, not eliminated — and unobservable","theaters":["egypt","gaza"],"mil":"Egypt's Islamic State affiliate in NORTH SINAI, formed as Ansar Bayt al-Maqdis and pledged to IS in 2014. Responsible for the 2015 Metrojet airliner bombing and the 2017 al-Rawda mosque massacre that killed over 300 worshippers. Egypt's counter-insurgency has substantially degraded it, at the cost of mass displacement, home demolitions and a near-total INFORMATION BLACKOUT across North Sinai. Tribal militias, notably the Tarabin, were armed against it.","log":"Tunnels and smuggling routes along the Gaza frontier; local recruitment among marginalised Sinai Bedouin.","eco":"Smuggling taxation — the same tunnel economy Egypt's Gaza policy is built around controlling.","assess":"Journalists and independent monitors are effectively barred from North Sinai, so both Cairo's success claims and the true civilian cost are unauditable. This platform logs the group AND the blackout: an insurgency in an information vacuum is a governance fact as much as a security one, and it sits directly on the Gaza border that Egypt's entire regional position depends on."}, {"id":"arsa-rso","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ARSA organisational record (leader Ataullah abu Ammar Jununi held as POW since Mar 2025, succeeded by Ustad Khalid; Tatmadaw alignment from 2024); ULA/AA statements on Maungdaw (Dec 2024); ICCT Perspectives on Terrorism","name":"ARSA & RSO","full":"Arakan Rohingya Salvation Army · Rohingya Solidarity Organisation","affil":"ROHINGYA · Myanmar/Bangladesh","icon":"◆","color":"#c77d4a","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"Now fighting alongside the army that drove them out — 5,000+ with their leader in custody","theaters":["bangladesh","myanmar"],"mil":"ARSA (founded 2013, ~5,000+) and the RSO operate from the Cox's Bazar camps and the Bangladesh-Myanmar border. ARSA's founder ATAULLAH ABU AMMAR JUNUNI HAS BEEN HELD AS A PRISONER SINCE MARCH 2025, with Ustad Khalid now leading. The two groups fought each other directly at Konarpara in 2023 and are now listed as allies.","log":"The camps themselves as recruitment base, cross-border movement, and criminal networks in the camp economy.","eco":"Extortion within the camps, and involvement in the methamphetamine trade transiting from Myanmar.","assess":"ARSA HAS BEEN ALIGNED WITH THE TATMADAW SINCE 2024 — the military that conducted the 2017 genocide against the Rohingya is now the partner of the principal Rohingya armed group, because both oppose the ARAKAN ARMY, which took Maungdaw and now administers the state the refugees would return to. When the ULA/AA captured the last junta border outpost in December 2024 it accused RSO, ARSA and ARA fighters of fleeing alongside junta forces. THE CONSEQUENCE FOR THE ONE MILLION PEOPLE IN THE CAMPS: their armed representatives have made themselves enemies of whoever governs Rakhine, which forecloses negotiated return regardless of what Dhaka or the UN arrange."}, {"id":"jamaica-gangs","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"UNODC homicide and firearms trafficking data; InSight Crime Caribbean reporting — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"Jamaican gang networks","full":"Klansman, One Order and successor 'posses'","affil":"JAMAICAN · criminal-political lineage","icon":"●","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"WATCH","strength":"Among the highest homicide rates on earth","theaters":["jamaica"],"mil":"Organised criminal networks descended from the 'garrison' system in which Kingston constituencies were controlled by armed enforcers tied to the two main political parties from the 1970s. Successive states of emergency and the 2010 Tivoli Gardens incursion — which killed over 70 people while security forces sought Christopher 'Dudus' Coke — mark the state's approach.","log":"Weapons trafficked overwhelmingly FROM THE UNITED STATES — the same Florida pipeline that arms Haiti's gangs.","eco":"Lottery-scamming targeting US victims, extortion, and drug transhipment on the cocaine route north.","assess":"The Caribbean's shared structure, visible twice on this board. Jamaica and Haiti both host armed networks with political parentage, financed by trafficking to the US and armed by weapons FROM the US — a hemispheric system in which the same country is the primary market, the primary arms source, and the primary author of the interdiction policy."}, {"id":"fano","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Critical Threats Africa File (28 May 2026); ACLED Ethiopia analysis (Jun 2026); EEPA situation reporting (Jul 2026)","name":"Fano","full":"Fano · Amhara popular militia","affil":"ETHIOPIAN · Amhara","icon":"⚔","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Fought for Addis, then against it","theaters":["ethiopia"],"mil":"An AMHARA popular militia — not a single organisation but a name shared by many local formations with no unified command, which is what makes it hard to defeat and impossible to negotiate with. Fano fought ALONGSIDE the federal army against the TPLF in the Tigray war, then turned on the government in 2023 when Addis moved to dissolve regional special forces. Since then Amhara region has been under recurrent states of emergency and drone strikes by its own government.","log":"Community mobilisation, weapons retained from the Tigray war, and the absence of a command structure to decapitate.","eco":"Minimal external financing — a grievance insurgency rather than a resource one.","assess":"Amhara forces were essential to the federal victory in Tigray and were then asked to disarm; they refused, and the state that armed them now bombs them. It is the clearest Ethiopian case of the pattern this board tracks from Burkina to Kinshasa: the auxiliary you raise for one war becomes the problem in the next."}, {"id":"ola","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ACLED Ethiopia analysis (Jun 2026) — records the OLA/OLF-Shane faction's surrender to the government in December 2024; BTI 2026 country report","name":"OLA","full":"Oromo Liberation Army","affil":"ETHIOPIAN · Oromo","icon":"▲","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The OLF-Shane faction surrendered in Dec 2024 \u2014 the insurgency did not end with it","theaters":["ethiopia"],"mil":"The armed wing that split from the Oromo Liberation Front when the OLF returned to legal politics in 2018, designated a terrorist organisation by Ethiopia's parliament in 2021. It operates across OROMIA — the country's largest and most populous region, and Abiy Ahmed's own — fighting for Oromo self-determination against a government that came to power on the back of Oromo protest.","log":"Rural western and southern Oromia, community support in contested districts, and periodic reported contact with other insurgent movements.","eco":"Local levies; among the least resource-financed actors on this board.","assess":"The revolution that put an Oromo prime minister in office did not settle the Oromo question — it relocated it. An armed movement fighting the first Oromo leader of Ethiopia is the sharpest available evidence that the country's crisis is constitutional rather than ethnic: the dispute is over what the federation IS, not over who holds the chair."}, {"id":"tplf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ACLED report on TPLF control of Tigray (Jun 2026); Critical Threats Africa File (28 May 2026); BTI 2026 Ethiopia country report","name":"TPLF","full":"Tigray People's Liberation Front","affil":"ETHIOPIAN · Tigrayan","icon":"◆","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Ruled Ethiopia for 27 years · now split against itself","theaters":["ethiopia"],"mil":"Dominant party of Ethiopia's ruling coalition from 1991 to 2018, then belligerent in the 2020-22 Tigray war that killed an estimated several hundred thousand people. The November 2022 PRETORIA AGREEMENT ended the fighting but left disarmament, the status of WESTERN TIGRAY and the return of displaced people unresolved. The movement has since SPLIT INTO RIVAL FACTIONS contesting control of the interim administration — a fracture Addis has been accused of exploiting.","log":"Post-Pretoria demobilisation incomplete; the force structure was never fully dismantled.","eco":"A region under federal ECONOMIC RESTRICTIONS with roughly four-fifths of its population needing food assistance — the fiscal instrument replacing the military one.","assess":"Pretoria stopped the shooting without answering any of the questions the shooting was about. With the TPLF fractured, Eritrean troops still a factor on the border, and Addis applying economic pressure, this platform's Watchlist treats Tigray as the most likely site of Ethiopia's next open conflict rather than a post-conflict zone."}, {"id":"is-somalia","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Monitoring Team reporting on the Al-Karrar office; Africa Center militant Islamic groups survey (2025)","name":"Islamic State in Somalia","full":"IS-Somalia · Al-Karrar office","affil":"SOMALI · Islamic State","icon":"☠","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Small in numbers · outsized in the global IS financial system","theaters":["somalia"],"mil":"A few hundred fighters based in the BARI MOUNTAINS of PUNTLAND, far smaller than al-Shabaab and in open conflict with it. Puntland's forces have run sustained offensives against its mountain strongholds. Its significance is not military: the AL-KARRAR regional office based here has been assessed by UN monitors as a FINANCIAL AND COORDINATION HUB for Islamic State affiliates across Africa.","log":"Coastal smuggling routes, foreign fighters arriving through Puntland's ports, and extortion of Bosaso businesses.","eco":"MONEY MOVEMENT ITSELF is the function — taxing local business to fund affiliates elsewhere on the continent.","assess":"The reason a few hundred fighters matter. If Al-Karrar routes finance to IS affiliates in the Sahel, the Congo basin and Mozambique, then Puntland is not a peripheral front — it is a node, and the platform's Africa-wide jihadist map has a treasury in northeastern Somalia."}, {"id":"aswj-clan","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ACLED Somalia data; Ahlu Sunna Waljama'a organisational record (Sufi paramilitary, ~5,000, Dhusamareb-based, opposed to al-Shabaab and in conflict with the Federal Government since 2021)","name":"ASWJ & clan militias","full":"Ahlu Sunna Wal Jama'a · macawisley community risings","affil":"SOMALI · Sufi and clan-based","icon":"✧","color":"#5a8dc7","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The local forces that actually rolled al-Shabaab back","theaters":["somalia"],"mil":"AHLU SUNNA WAL JAMA'A is a SUFI paramilitary movement formed to resist al-Shabaab's destruction of Sufi shrines, at times allied with and at times fighting the federal government over control of Galmudug. Alongside it, the MACAWISLEY — clan-based community militias — drove the 2022-23 risings that produced the most significant territorial losses al-Shabaab has suffered in a decade.","log":"Clan mobilisation, government weapons where relations allow, and intimate local knowledge.","eco":"Local resources; these are community forces, not enterprises.","assess":"The gains against al-Shabaab that actually held came from CLAN MILITIAS defending their own districts, not from AMISOM, ATMIS or airstrikes. And when federal politics soured clan relations, those gains reversed. Somalia's security is a function of clan settlement, which is exactly the variable external missions cannot supply."}, {"id":"ambazonia","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"Human Rights Watch Cameroon reporting; ICG Cameroon country page; ACLED Cameroon data — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"Ambazonia separatists","full":"Ambazonia Defence Forces · ADF, IG, AGovC and other factions","affil":"CAMEROONIAN · Anglophone","icon":"◇","color":"#4a9dc7","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Fragmented beyond negotiation · 700,000+ displaced","theaters":["cameroon"],"mil":"Armed groups fighting for independence of Cameroon's ANGLOPHONE Northwest and Southwest regions as 'Ambazonia', following the 2016-17 lawyers' and teachers' strikes and the state's violent response. They are DEEPLY FRAGMENTED — the Ambazonia Defence Forces, the Interim Government's forces, the Ambazonia Governing Council and numerous local commanders with no unified command — which has made both military defeat and negotiated settlement elusive. Tactics include enforced 'ghost town' lockdowns and a SCHOOL BOYCOTT that has kept a generation of Anglophone children out of education, enforced by attacks on schools and teachers.","log":"Diaspora financing from North America and Europe, weapons from Nigeria's border region, and forest terrain.","eco":"Diaspora remittances and local levies — one of the most externally-financed insurgencies on this board.","assess":"The Anglophone regions were British-administered Southern Cameroons joined to French Cameroun in 1961 under terms Anglophones have contested ever since. This is a partition dispute inherited directly from decolonisation, fought sixty years later — and the platform's Cameroon file treats the succession question as the trigger that could make it far worse."}, {"id":"aswj-mozambique","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Soufan Center IntelBrief (May 2026); GNET research (Jul 2026); ACLED actor profile: Islamic State Mozambique","name":"Ansar al-Sunna (Cabo Delgado)","full":"Ahlu Sunnah Wa-Jama · ISIS-Mozambique · locally 'al-Shabaab'","affil":"MOZAMBICAN · Islamic State affiliate","icon":"☠","color":"#c74a4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Reduced to an estimated 300-1,000 fighters — and sitting beside $20B+ of gas","theaters":["mozambique"],"mil":"Emerged in Cabo Delgado in 2017, pledged allegiance to IS in 2018 and was recognised as a distinct province in May 2022, having previously operated under the Islamic State Central Africa Province umbrella alongside the ADF in the DRC. At its April 2021 peak it held Mocímboa da Praia and its assault on Palma forced the suspension of a $20 billion LNG project. Rwandan and Mozambican forces retook the port in 2021. Counteroffensives have reduced it to an estimated 300-1,000 fighters, and it has drawn foreign fighters from Tanzania, Kenya, Somalia, the DRC, Rwanda and South Africa.","log":"Coastal and forest terrain, ruby- and timber-smuggling routes, and cross-border movement into Tanzania.","eco":"Illicit trade in rubies, timber and heroin transiting the coast — and the province holds one of the largest gas discoveries of the century, which is the reason foreign troops arrived.","assess":"Locally called 'al-Shabaab' but entirely unconnected to the al-Qaeda affiliate in Somalia — this board previously carried it as TWO SEPARATE ACTORS for that reason, an error corrected here. The substantive point: Africa Corps (then Wagner) exited Mozambique after heavy casualties and failure to contain it, and Rwandan troops paid via European energy interests hold the perimeter instead. The counter-insurgency and the grievance share a source — exclusion from the gas economy is what the insurgency recruits on.","gvn":"RAIDS RATHER THAN ADMINISTERS. Unlike JNIM or al-Shabaab in Somalia, this group has built no durable taxation or judicial apparatus — its territorial peak lasted months, not years, and its resilience comes from dispersal and local recruitment rather than from governing anything. That distinction matters: an insurgency that does not administer cannot be displaced by out-administering it, which is why the security perimeter around the LNG zone contains it without ending it."}, {"id":"hamas","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Security Council Report monthly forecast on Resolution 2803 (Jul 2026); Board of Peace disarmament roadmap (30 Jul 2026); ACLED Middle East Overview July 2026","name":"Hamas","full":"Harakat al-Muqawama al-Islamiyya · Izz ad-Din al-Qassam Brigades","affil":"PALESTINIAN · Muslim Brotherhood lineage","icon":"⚔","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Degraded militarily, undefeated politically","theaters":["gaza"],"mil":"Founded 1987, won the 2006 Palestinian legislative election, took sole control of Gaza in 2007. Its armed wing has been severely degraded since October 2023, but it retains the capacity to police, tax and reconstitute in areas the IDF leaves. Under Resolution 2803 it is required to DISARM — and REFUSES, on the grounds that Israel is itself in breach through continued strikes and aid restrictions.","log":"Domestic manufacture, tunnels, smuggled components; external political backing from Qatar and Turkey rather than sustained resupply.","eco":"Taxation of goods entering Gaza, control of aid distribution where it persists, and diaspora finance.","gvn":"GOVERNED, AND IS BEING ASKED TO STOP. Ran ministries, police, courts and taxation in Gaza for seventeen years. Resolution 2803's disarmament demand is inseparable from the governance question: no party has produced an administration to replace it, which is why the Board of Peace supervises a vacuum.","assess":"Resolution 2803's Board of Peace and International Stabilization Force exist to supervise a decommissioning Hamas will not perform, and no external actor will forcibly disarm it. Every 'day after' plan is downstream of this refusal — which is the platform's point that the governance question, not the military one, is unresolved."}, {"id":"pij-gaza","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ACLED Middle East Overview July 2026; UN Security Council Report on Resolution 2803 implementation","name":"PIJ & smaller factions","full":"Palestinian Islamic Jihad · PFLP · local clan militias","affil":"PALESTINIAN · mixed","icon":"◆","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The actors no framework accounts for","theaters":["gaza"],"mil":"Palestinian Islamic Jihad is the second-largest armed faction and more directly Iran-aligned than Hamas, with no governing responsibility to moderate it. Alongside it operate the PFLP and, increasingly, LOCAL CLAN MILITIAS — some armed with Israeli acquiescence as counterweights to Hamas, notably the group around Yasser Abu Shabab in Rafah.","log":"PIJ receives Iranian support; clan militias have been supplied locally, in some cases from seized aid.","eco":"Aid diversion and control of distribution points — which is why aid access is a military as well as humanitarian question.","assess":"The clan militias are the detail that breaks the two-party frame. Arming local factions against Hamas creates exactly the fragmented armed landscape Libya and Haiti demonstrate — a decision taken for tactical reasons whose structural consequence is a Gaza with many guns and no single address."}, {"id":"ttp","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UNAMA civilian harm report (12 May 2026); CSCR analysis of TTP expansion into Balochistan (Jul 2026); Small Wars Journal on the TTP-BLA nexus (May 2026)","name":"TTP","full":"Tehrik-i-Taliban Pakistan","affil":"PAKISTANI · Afghan Taliban-sheltered","icon":"⚔","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Opening a second front in Balochistan \u2014 no longer contained to KP","theaters":["pak-afg"],"mil":"A Pakistani jihadist coalition seeking to overthrow the Pakistani state, resurgent since the Afghan Taliban's 2021 return gave it sanctuary and captured NATO weapons. Its attacks on Pakistani security forces are the principal driver of Islamabad's cross-border strikes into Afghanistan and of the 2026 fighting.","log":"Afghan territory as sanctuary, US-origin equipment abandoned in 2021, and recruitment in the tribal districts.","eco":"Extortion, kidnapping and taxation in the frontier areas.","assess":"Pakistan's security establishment cultivated the Afghan Taliban for strategic depth; the Afghan Taliban now shelters the movement attacking Pakistan and will not expel it. This is the platform's blowback thesis with a documented chain of custody."}, {"id":"bla-baloch","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"2026 Balochistan attacks record (Jan-Feb offensive, 10-12 districts); Diplomatic Insight on the TTP-BLA nexus (Feb 2026); The Diplomat on Operation Shaban (Jul 2026)","name":"BLA & Baloch groups","full":"Baloch Liberation Army · BLF · Majeed Brigade","affil":"PAKISTANI BALOCH · separatist","icon":"✧","color":"#4a9dc7","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Struck 10-12 districts simultaneously in the Jan-Feb 2026 offensive","theaters":["pak-afg","kashmir"],"mil":"Baloch separatist organisations fighting for independence from Pakistan, with the BLA's Majeed Brigade conducting high-profile suicide operations including the 2025 Jaffar Express hijacking. Three attacks in Balochistan since 6 July 2026 killed 42 army and police personnel; security forces reported killing 54 militants.","log":"Mountainous terrain, Afghan and Iranian border areas, local recruitment among a population that considers itself colonised.","eco":"Minimal — among the least resource-financed insurgencies on this board, which is itself the point.","assess":"Gwadar port and CPEC run through Balochistan, and Baloch groups frame the corridor as extraction without consent — mineral and port wealth exported while the province stays Pakistan's poorest. This is the platform's extraction thesis articulated by the insurgents themselves."}, {"id":"iea-taliban","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UNAMA (12 May 2026); CFR Global Conflict Tracker; Pakistani defence ministry 'open war' declaration (27 Feb 2026)","name":"Islamic Emirate (Taliban)","full":"Islamic Emirate of Afghanistan","affil":"AFGHAN STATE · unrecognised","icon":"⚑","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Governs a country, recognised by almost no one","theaters":["pak-afg"],"mil":"The de facto government of Afghanistan since 2021, now fighting its former sponsor Pakistan across the DURAND LINE it has never recognised, while suppressing ISKP internally. It refuses to expel the TTP.","log":"Captured US-supplied equipment, customs revenue, and expanding trade with China, Russia and Central Asia.","eco":"Customs and mining concessions — including lithium and copper talks with Chinese firms — plus a partial narcotics ban that raised prices for existing stockpiles.","gvn":"THE STATE. Governs Afghanistan with a functioning revenue service, customs regime and centralised appointments; collections have reportedly exceeded those of the republic it replaced. Its constraints are external — frozen reserves and non-recognition — rather than administrative. It is also now conducting cross-border air operations, which is a capability no insurgency has and only a state does.","assess":"The border it will not recognise is the border its neighbour insists on. The Durand Line has been the region's unfinished colonial business since 1893, and it is now the axis of an interstate war between a nuclear state and an unrecognised emirate."}, {"id":"let-jem","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"UN Security Council 1267 Committee sanctions list (LeT, JeM); Indian and Pakistani official statements — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"LeT, JeM & TRF","full":"Lashkar-e-Taiba · Jaish-e-Mohammed · The Resistance Front","affil":"PAKISTAN-LINKED · anti-India","icon":"⚔","color":"#c74a4a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"The groups that can start a war between nuclear states","theaters":["kashmir"],"mil":"Lashkar-e-Taiba and Jaish-e-Mohammed are UN-designated organisations with documented historical links to Pakistan's security establishment; THE RESISTANCE FRONT emerged as a rebranded front designed to appear indigenous. The 2025 PAHALGAM attack triggered India's Operation Sindoor and the most serious India-Pakistan exchange in decades.","log":"Cross-LoC infiltration, and — per Indian and Western assessments — sanctuary and historical sponsorship inside Pakistan, which Islamabad denies.","eco":"Charitable-front financing and state-adjacent patronage rather than territorial revenue.","assess":"These are the only actors on this board whose single operation can move two nuclear-armed states to exchange fire within days. The platform's Kashmir Competing Read holds HIGH confidence on the both/and: documented sponsorship AND documented indigenous alienation — each capital's single-cause story fails the record."}, {"id":"kashmir-indigenous","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"ACLED South Asia data; UN OHCHR reporting on Kashmir; ICG South Asia — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"Indigenous Kashmiri militancy","full":"Hizbul Mujahideen and successor networks","affil":"KASHMIRI · indigenous","icon":"◇","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"Diminished in arms, undiminished in grievance","theaters":["kashmir"],"mil":"Hizbul Mujahideen and smaller successor networks represent the indigenous strand of Kashmiri militancy, substantially degraded since 2019 by Indian security operations and the constitutional changes that followed the abrogation of Article 370. Recruitment has fallen; alienation has not.","log":"Local, and increasingly thin — which is why Pakistan-linked groups dominate operationally.","eco":"Negligible.","assess":"Their decline is what makes the Competing Read necessary. As indigenous capability falls and externally-linked capability persists, Delhi's 'it is all sponsored' argument gets stronger operationally while its 'there is no grievance' argument gets weaker politically. Both things are becoming more true at once."}, {"id":"eln","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"FIP 'El Deterioro de la Seguridad Marca el Inicio de 2026'; ICG Colombia; UN Verification Mission for Colombia","name":"ELN","full":"Ejército de Liberación Nacional","affil":"COLOMBIAN · Guevarist · cross-border","icon":"⚔","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"6,810 members and rising \u2014 fronts that ignore their own national ceasefires","theaters":["colombia","venezuela"],"mil":"Founded 1964 by radical priests and students, the ELN refused the 2016 peace accord the FARC signed and is now Colombia's largest remaining guerrilla force. It operates on BOTH SIDES OF THE VENEZUELAN BORDER, with substantial presence in Venezuelan territory that Caracas has tolerated. Its federated structure — semi-autonomous fronts — makes negotiated demobilisation structurally harder than the FARC's centralised command.","log":"Cross-border sanctuary in Venezuela, local recruitment, and captured or trafficked weapons.","eco":"COCA taxation, ILLEGAL GOLD MINING — now rivalling cocaine as a revenue source — extortion, and control of the Catatumbo and Aráuca corridors.","gvn":"GOVERNS THE BORDER ZONES. Taxes coca cultivation, illegal mining and fuel smuggling across Catatumbo and the Venezuelan frontier, adjudicates disputes and regulates community conduct in areas the Colombian state does not reach. The drone strike on Tibú airport signals a shift from taxing a territory to contesting the state's control of its transport nodes.","assess":"The reason 'post-conflict Colombia' was always premature. The Truth Commission's finding applies exactly here: the war persists where its returns survive. Gold and coca fund a movement whose federated structure means no single signature can end it."}, {"id":"farc-dissidents","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"FIP security assessment (2026); ACLED 'Total Peace paradox' report; ICG Colombia","name":"FARC dissidents","full":"Estado Mayor Central · Segunda Marquetalia","affil":"COLOMBIAN · accord refusers and returnees","icon":"◆","color":"#c77d4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"~3,000 in Segunda Marquetalia, with EMC/CNEB up almost a quarter","theaters":["colombia","venezuela"],"mil":"Two main blocs: the ESTADO MAYOR CENTRAL under 'Iván Mordisco', which never demobilised, and the SEGUNDA MARQUETALIA under 'Iván Márquez', formed by commanders who signed the 2016 accord and then returned to arms. They fight each other as well as the state and the ELN, contesting the territories the FARC vacated.","log":"Former FARC networks, Venezuelan border sanctuary, and cartel supply relationships.","eco":"Coca cultivation and processing, illegal mining, and the Pacific trafficking corridors.","assess":"The strongest evidence for the platform's read that Colombia's war is sustained by its economics rather than its politics. Men who signed a peace agreement went back to war not over ideology but because the territories they left were worth returning to."}, {"id":"clan-del-golfo","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"FIP security assessment (2026) — 9,840 active members, up 30%; InSight Crime; ACLED","name":"Clan del Golfo","full":"Autodefensas Gaitanistas de Colombia","affil":"COLOMBIAN · paramilitary successor","icon":"◇","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"9,840 active members, up 30% \u2014 the largest surge under Total Peace","theaters":["colombia"],"mil":"The largest of Colombia's neo-paramilitary organisations, descended from the AUC blocs that formally demobilised in the 2000s. It exercises armed governance across Antioquia, Córdoba and the Uraba region — including 'armed strikes' that shut down entire departments on command.","log":"Former paramilitary networks, corrupt state links, and the Urábaa-to-Caribbean smuggling geography.","eco":"COCAINE EXPORT above all, plus illegal gold, extortion, and land control — the AUC's original business model with a new name.","assess":"Two demobilisations, two reconstitutions. The FARC's produced dissidents; the AUC's produced this. Colombia's recurring lesson is that disarming a structure without dismantling its economy just changes the letterhead."}, {"id":"rus-forces","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ISW/Critical Threats Russian Offensive Campaign Assessment (25 Jul 2026); Kyiv Post reporting on forced recruitment raids (Jun 2026)","name":"Russian forces & auxiliaries","full":"Russian Armed Forces · Rosgvardia · Chechen units · Storm-Z","affil":"RUSSIAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Attritional advance, operational stalemate","theaters":["ukraine","russia-domestic"],"mil":"Regular forces plus a layered auxiliary system: Rosgvardia internal troops, Chechen Akhmat units under Kadyrov, penal 'Storm-Z' assault formations, and the remnants of the Wagner structure absorbed after 2023. It took Pokrovsk and Myrnohrad in early 2026 and has since failed to convert tactical gains into operational advance — independent assessments describe exploitation becoming 'progressively more difficult'.","log":"North Korean munitions and troops, Iranian drone designs now domestically produced, and a war economy running at unsustainable fiscal cost.","eco":"Hydrocarbon revenue under a sanctions ceiling, plus seized Ukrainian industry and grain in occupied territory.","gvn":"OCCUPATION ADMINISTRATION IS THE WAR AIM, NOT ITS BYPRODUCT. In occupied Ukraine the apparatus is filtration camps, forced passportisation, replacement of school curricula, installed local officials, and the transfer of children into Russian families and institutions. THIS IS WHY THE ICC WARRANTS TARGET THE DEPORTATION PROGRAMME RATHER THAN BATTLEFIELD CONDUCT — the conduct that meets the legal threshold is administrative. Territory is being converted into population, and a ceasefire along current lines would freeze that conversion in place.","assess":"The auxiliary layer is the tell. A state that must recruit convicts, subcontract to a republic's personal army, and import munitions from Pyongyang is winning ground at a price its budget records even when its rhetoric does not."}, {"id":"ukr-forces","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ISW/Critical Threats campaign assessments (Jul 2026); Ukrainian General Staff operational reporting","name":"Ukrainian forces","full":"ZSU · territorial defence · drone units","affil":"UKRAINIAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#4a9dc7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Holding at attrition with a technological edge","theaters":["ukraine"],"mil":"Regular armed forces, territorial defence brigades, and a distinctive layer of specialised DRONE UNITS that have made Ukraine the world's leading practitioner of cheap precision strike — including deep strikes into Russian refining and airbase infrastructure. Manpower, not materiel, is now the binding constraint.","log":"Western systems, and increasingly a domestic drone and missile industry — the Patriot technology-sharing arrangement was a shift toward co-production rather than donation.","eco":"Financed externally: EU facilities, G7 ERA loans and the IMF's $8.1B EFF cover a $136.5B four-year gap.","gvn":"A STATE GOVERNING UNDER MARTIAL LAW AND EXTERNAL FINANCE. Mobilisation, wartime budgeting and suspended elections, with the reconstruction terms set abroad through EU facilities, G7 ERA loans and IMF programmes. The sovereignty paradox this board tracks: the state fighting for its independence has its fiscal decisions increasingly made by the creditors funding the fight.","assess":"Ukraine's survival depends on financing whose terms are set abroad, and the debt and reconstruction architecture being negotiated now will shape its economic sovereignty for a generation. Fighting for independence while mortgaging the peace is the condition, not a criticism of it."}, {"id":"lna","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Security Council Panel of Experts on Libya (Mar 2026); UNSMIL briefings (Apr 2026); Soufan Center IntelBrief; CFR Global Conflict Tracker","name":"LNA / Haftar","full":"Libyan National Army · Haftar family","affil":"LIBYAN EASTERN · Russia/UAE/Egypt-backed","icon":"⚔","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"A family command absorbing Russian assets redeployed out of Syria","theaters":["libya"],"mil":"Khalifa Haftar's eastern coalition, increasingly run as a family enterprise with sons Saddam and Khalid in senior command. Backed by Russia — Africa Corps uses eastern Libya as its logistics hub for the Sahel — and historically by the UAE and Egypt. Controls the OIL CRESCENT terminals.","log":"Russian airlift through eastern bases, Emirati funding, Egyptian border cooperation.","eco":"OIL TERMINAL CONTROL as political leverage — the ability to shut exports is the LNA's principal instrument against Tripoli — plus smuggling and reconstruction contracts.","assess":"Libya's partition is functionally administered. Haftar's succession planning matters more than any election roadmap: an armed family business with foreign patrons is not a faction awaiting a settlement, it is a parallel state arranging its inheritance."}, {"id":"gnu-militias","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Panel of Experts on Libya (Mar 2026) — al-Katiba 55 and migrant detention; UNSMIL; GIS Reports on militia 'partners'","name":"GNU-aligned militias","full":"Tripoli militia coalitions · SSA · 444 Brigade · RADA","affil":"LIBYAN WESTERN · Turkey-aligned","icon":"◆","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The capital is held by competing payrolls","theaters":["libya"],"mil":"A shifting set of armed groups nominally under the Tripoli government — the Stability Support Apparatus, the 444 Brigade, the Special Deterrence Force (RADA) and others — which periodically fight EACH OTHER for control of state institutions and revenue nodes. Turkey provides the western government's external military backing.","log":"State salaries, Turkish support, and control of ministries that fund rivals.","eco":"CAPTURE OF STATE INSTITUTIONS: central bank access, letters of credit, fuel subsidy arbitrage and the coastal migrant-interception economy the EU funds.","assess":"The most literal case on this board of Tilly's argument inverted. These groups do not build a state by making war; they make war to capture the state's revenue functions. Libya's institutions were never destroyed — they were divided as assets."}, {"id":"pmf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Joint US-Saudi airstrikes on PMF facilities (29 Jul 2026, Reuters/Jerusalem Post); Iraqi Ministerial Council for National Security emergency session","name":"PMF / Hashd al-Shaabi","full":"Popular Mobilisation Forces","affil":"IRAQI STATE-INCORPORATED · Iran-aligned factions","icon":"⚑","color":"#b8863d","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"On the state payroll, outside state command","theaters":["iraq","iran-us"],"mil":"An umbrella of militias formed against Islamic State in 2014 and subsequently INCORPORATED INTO THE IRAQI STATE with legal status and budget lines. Its Iran-aligned components — Kataib Hezbollah, Asaib Ahl al-Haq, Harakat Hezbollah al-Nujaba — have struck US positions and act on Tehran's strategic guidance, while Baghdad pays their salaries. The 2026 war has constrained rather than unleashed them.","log":"Iraqi state budget, Iranian training and weapons, and cross-border logistics into Syria.","eco":"Budget allocations, checkpoint economies, control of border crossings and reconstruction contracting — institutional capture as a revenue model.","assess":"THE STRUCTURE THE 2003 INVASION BUILT. Iraq pays, arms and legitimises forces that answer partly to another government. This is what the platform means by subdivided sovereignty: not a state too weak to control its territory, but a state whose own institutions contain a rival's instruments."}, {"id":"cjng-sinaloa","prov":"STRUCTURAL","src":"InSight Crime organisational profiles; UNODC; US Treasury OFAC designations — organisational record established; current-cycle status not event-verified","name":"CJNG & Sinaloa factions","full":"Cartel Jalisco Nueva Generación · Sinaloa (Chapitos / Mayitos)","affil":"MEXICAN · criminal","icon":"◆","color":"#c74a4a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Two systems, and one of them is at civil war","theaters":["mexico"],"mil":"CJNG is the most territorially expansive Mexican organisation, fielding armoured vehicles, drones with explosives and anti-aircraft weapons. The Sinaloa federation has been in open internal war since the 2024 capture of Ismael 'El Mayo' Zambada, with the CHAPITOS and MAYITOS factions fighting across Sinaloa state — violence that followed a US law-enforcement operation.","log":"Precursor chemicals from Asia, weapons trafficked SOUTH FROM THE UNITED STATES, and drone components bought commercially.","eco":"FENTANYL and methamphetamine above all, plus extortion of agriculture, mining and transport — and increasingly control of legitimate sectors as tax bases.","gvn":"TAXES WHAT IT DOES NOT OWN. Levies businesses, agriculture and migration routes across areas of control, adjudicates disputes, and in places enforces order more reliably than local police — while the internal war between Sinaloa factions has degraded that capacity and turned parts of the state into contested extraction rather than governed territory.","assess":"The platform's Mexico Competing Read holds this at HIGH confidence as criminal-market fragmentation rather than extraction politics — and the Sinaloa split proves it: a decapitation produced a civil war, exactly as the fragmentation literature predicts. ~400,000 homicides and 100,000+ disappeared since 2006, a casualty scale exceeding most declared wars and classified as crime."}, {"id":"houthis","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Panel of Experts; Grundberg briefings","name":"Houthis / Ansar Allah","full":"Ansar Allah · Zaidi Shia movement","affil":"YEMENI · Iran-aligned · US-designated FTO","icon":"⚔","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Hold Sana'a and most of Yemen's population","theaters":["yemen","chokepoints","saudi"],"mil":"A ZAIDI SHIA movement from the northern highlands that took Sana'a in 2014 and now governs most of western Yemen — where most Yemenis live. Part of the Iran-aligned 'axis of resistance'; hundreds of attacks on Israel and Red Sea shipping since October 2023. On 28 March 2026 they fired missiles at southern Israel, their first strike since the US-Israel war with Iran began, and in July broke the four-year Saudi truce by hitting ABHA AIRPORT.","log":"Iranian components and training, domestic assembly, and a drone industry cheap enough to be expendable — attack drones reportedly cost as little as $10,000.","eco":"Taxation of Sana'a and the ports, a parallel CENTRAL BANK with its own currency, fuel imports, and now extraction ventures with al-Shabaab documented by UN monitors.","gvn":"A STATE IN ALL BUT RECOGNITION. Runs ministries, courts, taxation, conscription and a security service across northern Yemen, with its own central bank arrangements and salary system. This is why the group can declare and sustain a blockade of Saudi Arabia — it has the institutional depth to conduct foreign policy.","assess":"As Hezbollah, the Iraqi militias and Hamas were degraded, the Houthis became Iran's most autonomous partner — and started building their own network southward into Somalia. Grundberg's warning is the right frame: the danger is Yemen being drawn INTO the regional war, not merely reflecting it."}, {"id":"stc-dissolved","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"STC dissolution reporting Jan 2026","name":"STC (dissolved)","full":"Southern Transitional Council · UAE-backed separatists","affil":"YEMENI SOUTHERN · UAE-backed","icon":"◇","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"WATCH","strength":"Reached for independence and lost everything in five weeks","theaters":["yemen","uae-gulf"],"mil":"A southern separatist coalition founded 2017, UAE-backed, with the ~13,000-strong SECURITY BELT as its core. On 2 DEC 2025 it launched a large offensive across southern Yemen, seizing Hadhramaut including Seiyun and Tarim, and announced plans for an INDEPENDENCE REFERENDUM within two years. A Saudi-backed government counteroffensive with airstrikes retook Hadhramaut; on 7 JAN 2026 government forces entered Aden as STC resistance collapsed. Leader AIDARUS AL-ZOUBAIDI was expelled from the Presidential Leadership Council, CHARGED WITH TREASON, and fled to the UAE. On 9 JAN the STC ANNOUNCED ITS DISSOLUTION.","log":"Was UAE-supplied — and the UAE WITHDREW ITS REMAINING FORCES FROM YEMEN in January 2026.","eco":"Aden's port and customs, and a bid for the Masila oil fields that triggered the tribal confrontation.","assess":"A separatist project existed exactly as long as Abu Dhabi funded it; when Riyadh pushed back, the UAE folded its hand and left. Southern secessionism did not die — STC demonstrators were shot in Ataq in February — but its institutional vehicle was dismantled in five weeks. Proxy status is not sovereignty."}, {"id":"aqap","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Monitoring Team (Feb 2026)","name":"AQAP","full":"Al-Qaeda in the Arabian Peninsula","affil":"YEMENI · al-Qaeda global leadership","icon":"☠","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"2,000-3,000 fighters · now the global network's centre of gravity","theaters":["yemen","somalia"],"mil":"Under SAAD BEN ATEF AL-AWLAKI, UN monitors assess AQAP is 'increasingly asserting its ideological and operational leadership of the GLOBAL al-Qaeda network' — the franchise has become the headquarters. Most active in Abyan, Shabwa, Marib and Hadramout. The external-attack threat is judged 'increasing' but 'opportunistic'.","log":"Local tribal ties in the southern and eastern governorates; a 'covert pragmatic relationship' with the Houthis despite sectarian opposition.","eco":"Smuggling, kidnapping and taxation in the remote governorates.","assess":"UN monitors document an AQAP-AL-SHABAAB ALLIANCE sustaining 'a logistical and operational network across the GULF OF ADEN'. Read with the Somalia entries, this is the same finding from the other shore: two al-Qaeda affiliates and one Iranian-aligned movement are all building capability across the same water — which is the strait this platform tracks."}, {"id":"plc-irg","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"PLC reporting 2026","name":"PLC / government forces","full":"Presidential Leadership Council · internationally recognised government","affil":"YEMENI · Saudi-backed","icon":"⚑","color":"#5a8dc7","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Reunified the south by force — and inherited its grievances","theaters":["yemen","saudi"],"mil":"An eight-member council formed in 2022 from Saudi- and UAE-backed figures, nominally governing non-Houthi Yemen from Saudi Arabia. Analysts call it fractured. In Dec 2025-Jan 2026 it destroyed the STC's bid with Saudi air support, expelled STC ministers, and is now trying to unify factions and consolidate command — while the Houthis, unaffected by all of it, broke the Saudi truce in July.","log":"Saudi funding, airpower and political cover; component forces with distinct loyalties.","eco":"The recognised central bank, oil exports where they function, and donor finance.","assess":"It won the south and cannot hold the north. The PLC's consolidation is real but it was achieved by Saudi airstrikes against a fellow anti-Houthi faction — which is the anti-Houthi coalition's core problem in one sentence."}, {"id":"hadhramaut-tribes","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Masila deployment reporting Nov 2025","name":"Hadhramaut tribal forces","full":"Hadhramaut Tribal Alliance · Hadhramaut Protection Forces","affil":"YEMENI · Saudi-aligned tribal","icon":"●","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"Deployed to the oil fields before the shooting started","theaters":["yemen"],"mil":"A Saudi-backed tribal alliance under AMR BIN HABRISH whose military wing, the Hadhramaut Protection Forces, deployed around the MASILA OIL FIELDS operated by PetroMasila — Yemen's largest oil company — on 29 November 2025, days before the STC offensive, to 'defend national resources from external interference'.","log":"Tribal mobilisation with Saudi backing.","eco":"THE OIL. Hadhramaut holds Yemen's principal producing fields; whoever holds them holds the only substantial export revenue in the country.","assess":"The tribal deployment to the oil fields PRECEDED the offensive by three days — the resource was positioned over before the political fight began. The clearest illustration in this theater that Yemen's war is fought over revenue geography, whatever the flags say."}, {"id":"hts-gov","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA Syria actor mapping","name":"HTS / transitional government","full":"Hay'at Tahrir al-Sham · al-Sharaa government","affil":"SYRIAN · former al-Qaeda affiliate","icon":"⚑","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"The insurgency that became the state","theaters":["syria"],"mil":"Ahmed al-Sharaa's movement, formerly Jabhat al-Nusra and formerly al-Qaeda's Syrian branch, which took Damascus in Dec 2024 and now forms the transitional government. It is attempting to absorb other factions into a national army, has interdicted Iranian weapons shipments bound for HEZBOLLAH, and is negotiating with the SDF over integration.","log":"Captured Syrian army stocks, Turkish tolerance, and Gulf financing.","eco":"Customs, the reconstruction economy, and control of what remains of state revenue.","assess":"Either a jihadist movement genuinely became a governing project, or the rebrand is tactical and the 2025 sectarian massacres were preview rather than aberration. The Competing Read holds both at MOD confidence; the treatment of Alawites, Druze and Kurds is the deciding evidence, and it is still accumulating."}, {"id":"sdf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"CFR Global Conflict Tracker (Jan 2026 offensive and 30 Jan transfer agreement); EUAA Syria actor mapping","name":"SDF","full":"Syrian Democratic Forces · YPG/YPJ-led","affil":"SYRIAN KURDISH-LED · multi-ethnic · US-partnered","icon":"✧","color":"#4a9dc7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"LOST the northeast in January 2026 — now negotiating from inside, not across the table","theaters":["syria","turkey"],"mil":"A Kurdish-led coalition built around the YPG and the women's YPJ, incorporating the Syriac Military Council, Arab tribal militias (al-Sanadid of the Shammar, Deir ez-Zor Military Council, Baggara and other tribal forces) and local military councils. Washington's partner against Islamic State; Ankara's declared enemy through the YPG's PKK lineage. Now negotiating integration terms with Damascus.","log":"US support, captured stocks, and the northeast's own manufacturing.","eco":"SYRIA'S OIL AND WHEAT. The northeast holds the majority of both — which is the SDF's actual negotiating position with Damascus, and the reason integration talks are really revenue talks.","gvn":"ADMINISTERED, THEN TRANSFERRED. Ran the Autonomous Administration of North and East Syria — councils, schools, courts, oil revenue and the detention camps — until the January 2026 agreement handed administrative control to Damascus. What happens to that apparatus is the unresolved question of the Syrian transition.","assess":"STATUS CHANGED, JAN 2026 — THIS ENTRY IS CORRECTED. Government forces captured roughly 80% of northeast Syria including the ISIS detention camps; Turkey backed Damascus; and under a US-mediated agreement on 30 January the SDF agreed to TRANSFER ADMINISTRATIVE CONTROL to the central government. The three-way squeeze resolved — against the SDF. What remains is whether integration means Kurdish rights inside a Syrian state or the dismantling of an autonomy project, and whether Arab tribal components accept the outcome. The oil and wheat that were its negotiating position are now Damascus's."}, {"id":"sna","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA; UN abuse documentation","name":"SNA","full":"Syrian National Army · Turkish-backed factions","affil":"SYRIAN · TURKEY-backed","icon":"◆","color":"#c77d4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Ankara's instrument in the north","theaters":["syria","turkey"],"mil":"A coalition of Turkish-backed factions holding the northern border strip, used repeatedly against the SDF. Documented by UN bodies for abuses in the areas it administers, including against Kurdish civilians. Its integration into the new national army is one of the transition's hardest problems.","log":"Turkish salaries, weapons, and operational direction.","eco":"Border crossings, olive and agricultural revenue from Afrin, and checkpoint taxation.","assess":"The test of whether the new Syria is a state or a set of patronage networks under one flag. If Damascus can absorb the SNA it can absorb anyone; if Turkey retains a parallel command, the transition has a foreign veto inside its own army."}, {"id":"sweida-daraa","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA; 2025 southern violence reporting","name":"Sweida & Dar'a factions","full":"Druze self-defence · southern local factions","affil":"SYRIAN · Druze and southern communal","icon":"◇","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The communities that armed themselves against the new order","theaters":["syria","lebanon"],"mil":"Local factions in SWEIDA (predominantly DRUZE) and DAR'A that mobilised during and after the transition, in several cases fighting government-aligned forces. The 2025 sectarian violence in the south — and Israeli strikes framed as protecting the Druze — made this the transition's most internationalised internal fault line.","log":"Local weapons, community mobilisation, and in Sweida's case an explicit external patron claim.","eco":"Local trade and the Jordanian border economy, including captagon routes the new government says it is dismantling.","assess":"Where the transition's legitimacy is actually being tested. A government that cannot protect minorities produces minorities that arm themselves, which produces foreign patrons who claim to protect them — the exact sequence that dismembered Syria the first time."}, {"id":"viv-ansanm","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Panel of Experts; US FTO designation","name":"Viv Ansanm","full":"Viv Ansanm gang coalition · G9 and G-Pep","affil":"HAITIAN · criminal-political","icon":"◆","color":"#c74a4a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Holds 70-90% of Port-au-Prince","theaters":["haiti"],"mil":"The coalition formed in 2023 uniting the previously warring G9 (led by Jimmy 'Barbecue' Chérizier) and G-Pep federations. It controls most of the capital, has attacked prisons, the airport and the port, and forced the prime minister's resignation in 2024. The US designated it a terrorist organisation. Internal fighting resumed within the coalition in 2025-26 — the alliance is not stable.","log":"Weapons overwhelmingly trafficked FROM THE UNITED STATES, principally Florida; ammunition resupply is the coalition's binding constraint.","eco":"Port and road TAXATION, kidnapping-for-ransom, extortion of businesses and aid convoys — a war economy that funds itself from the movement of everything into and out of the capital.","gvn":"GOVERNS BY NEIGHBOURHOOD. Coalition control over most of Port-au-Prince includes checkpoint taxation, protection levies on businesses and utilities, and dispute adjudication in areas the state has vacated. Where the state is absent the gang is not competing with government — it is the only government present.","assess":"Haiti's gangs were armed by political and business elites; the platform's Competing Read holds it MOD whether they are still instruments or now self-sustaining. The 2025-26 infighting suggests genuine autonomy; the continuing elite links suggest otherwise. Either way, the entity that governs Port-au-Prince is this one."}, {"id":"gsf-haiti","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UNSC Res 2793; deployment reporting","name":"Gang Suppression Force","full":"GSF · UNSC Resolution 2793","affil":"MULTINATIONAL · UN-authorised","icon":"⚑","color":"#5a8dc7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"~1,000 deployed of 5,550 authorised","theaters":["haiti","chad"],"mil":"Authorised at 5,550 personnel; roughly 1,000 are actually on the ground. First CHADIAN officers arrived 1 April 2026 — Chad has pledged 1,500 troops — and Mongolian force commander Erdenebat Batsuuri arrived 14 May. The Dominican Republic has ruled out contributing troops. It replaced the Kenyan-led MSS mission.","log":"Voluntary contributions, chronically under-funded and under-staffed.","eco":"Not a revenue actor — but its under-resourcing is a funding decision made in donor capitals.","assess":"This platform's Watchlist tripwire asked whether the GSF would launch a real offensive or stall under 1,500 troops. It stalled. Meanwhile a UN report finds MORE THAN 60% OF CASUALTIES occur during anti-gang operations — the intervention is a leading cause of the toll it exists to reduce."}, {"id":"vectus","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"HRW (1,243 killed in 141 operations)","name":"Vectus Global","full":"Vectus Global · Erik Prince private military company","affil":"UNITED STATES · private contractor","icon":"▣","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Drone strikes under an unpublished contract","theaters":["haiti"],"mil":"The private military company led by ERIK PRINCE, founder of Blackwater, contracted by Haiti's transitional government. It has struck gang targets using EXPLOSIVE-LADEN QUADCOPTERS in densely populated neighbourhoods. HRW documented at least 1,243 people killed in 141 drone operations between March 2025 and January 2026, INCLUDING 17 CHILDREN, some appearing unlawful and possibly amounting to extrajudicial killings.","log":"Commercial drone airframes and contractor personnel; the contract with the Haitian government HAS NEVER BEEN MADE PUBLIC.","eco":"Paid by a transitional government that cannot fund its own police.","assess":"A foreign private company conducts lethal strikes in a capital city under a contract no citizen has seen, for a government no one elected, with no public accountability mechanism — and the Secretary-General has warned the Vectus-supported Special Security Task Force may be listed next year for child casualties. This is what security outsourcing looks like at its end state."}, {"id":"hnp","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Secretary-General child-violations reporting","name":"Haitian National Police","full":"Police Nationale d'Haïti · Special Security Task Force","affil":"HAITIAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Outgunned, and now facing a UN listing","theaters":["haiti"],"mil":"The state's primary security institution, chronically under-resourced against better-armed gangs, and operating alongside the Vectus-supported Special Security Task Force. The UN Secretary-General has warned that the HNP and the Task Force SHOULD BE LISTED next year among parties committing grave violations against children if their record repeats.","log":"Donor-funded equipment, an arms embargo that constrains it more than the gangs, and heavy attrition.","eco":"State salaries where they are paid.","assess":"The gangs Gran Grif, Kraze Barye and 400 Mawozo were added to the Secretary-General's child-violations annexes this year. The state's own police are one report away from the same list. When the distinction between the armed groups and the security forces stops being visible in the human-rights record, the state has lost the argument that it is different in kind."}, {"id":"tatmadaw","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ICG; territorial control reporting 2026","name":"Tatmadaw / SAC","full":"Myanmar Armed Forces · State Administration Council","affil":"MYANMAR STATE · junta","icon":"⚑","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Losing territory, gaining a patron","theaters":["myanmar"],"mil":"Min Aung Hlaing's junta, holding the dry-zone core, Naypyidaw and the major cities while losing the peripheries. Overstretched and suffering desertion since Operation 1027; it compensates with AIRPOWER, striking Sagaing and Karenni relentlessly. It has lost Chin State's last base (2025) and 14 of Rakhine's 17 townships.","log":"Air force, Russian and Chinese aircraft, conscription by force — and, since July 2026, memoranda on all four of Xi's Global Initiatives.","eco":"Jade, gas (Shwe pipeline), and the Kyaukphyu corridor — revenue streams that require Chinese buyers and Chinese protection.","assess":"The junta is losing ground and gaining a patron simultaneously. Beijing pressured the MNDAA to return Lashio and now underwrites Naypyidaw — which is why the Watchlist carries a tripwire designed to falsify this file's own 'junta is losing' read."}, {"id":"arakan-army","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"HRW; Fortify Rights","name":"Arakan Army","full":"Arakan Army · Rakhine/Arakanese nationalist force","affil":"MYANMAR · Rakhine ethnic","icon":"⚔","color":"#4a9dc7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"50,000+ · holds 14 of Rakhine's 17 townships","theaters":["myanmar"],"mil":"The most successful ethnic armed organisation of the war. It has effectively taken Rakhine State — the junta clings to the capital Sittwe and the Chinese-backed KYAUKPHYU deep-sea port — and now fights well beyond it, into Ayeyarwady, Bago and Magway alongside local PDFs. Part of the Three Brotherhood Alliance.","log":"Captured junta stocks, the Bangladesh frontier, and revenue from the territory it administers.","eco":"Taxation of a state it now governs, plus the leverage of sitting astride Kyaukphyu — China's Indian Ocean outlet.","gvn":"BUILDING A GOVERNMENT. Administers most of Rakhine through its United League of Arakan political wing — courts, police, taxation, health and education departments. This is what makes Rohingya return a negotiation with an insurgent administration rather than with Naypyidaw.","assess":"The AA is the war's most effective liberation force AND, per HRW and Fortify Rights, has killed Rohingya civilians, burned villages and conscripted men in areas it controls. A platform that credits anti-junta resistance without recording this would be doing exactly what it criticises others for."}, {"id":"mndaa","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Kutkai reporting Mar 2026","name":"MNDAA","full":"Myanmar National Democratic Alliance Army · Kokang","affil":"MYANMAR · Kokang (ethnic Chinese)","icon":"◆","color":"#c74a4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"6,000+ · broke the alliance it helped build","theaters":["myanmar"],"mil":"Kokang force that seized LAUKKAI in January 2024, forcing a regional commander's surrender — the Tatmadaw's most humiliating defeat. Then, under Chinese pressure, it handed LASHIO back to the junta. On 14-15 MARCH 2026 it ATTACKED ITS OWN ALLY THE TNLA and took KUTKAI, detaining fighters and seizing trade gates — shattering the Three Brotherhood Alliance. It now holds a ceasefire with the junta.","log":"The China border, and Beijing's tolerance — which is the actual supply line.","eco":"Border trade gates, casinos and the Kokang crossing economy.","assess":"An ethnic army that beat the Myanmar military, then returned a captured city on Beijing's instruction, then turned its guns on its closest ally in a move analysts read as realignment toward China. The Brotherhood Alliance was not defeated — it was bought apart."}, {"id":"tnla","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Kutkai reporting Mar 2026","name":"TNLA","full":"Ta'ang National Liberation Army","affil":"MYANMAR · Ta'ang/Palaung ethnic","icon":"▲","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"~10,000 · attacked by its former ally","theaters":["myanmar"],"mil":"Ta'ang nationalist force founded 1992, HQ Namhsan, grown from 800 fighters in 2011 to around 10,000. A Brotherhood Alliance and Northern Alliance member that took ground in Operation 1027 — and in March 2026 lost Kutkai not to the junta but to the MNDAA, after the MNDAA restricted fuel and goods into its territory. Now holds a ceasefire with the junta while fighting a former ally.","log":"Northern Shan highlands; supply now squeezed by the MNDAA's control of crossings.","eco":"Tea, taxation, and the Shan trade corridors.","gvn":"GOVERNS TA'ANG AREAS. Operates administration, taxation and dispute resolution in the territory it holds in northern Shan. Its position collapsed into open conflict with the MNDAA — its own Brotherhood Alliance partner — in March 2026 under Chinese pressure, which is what happens when an armed group's governance depends on a patron with interests on both sides.","assess":"Proof that Myanmar's war has stopped being binary. The TNLA now has ceasefires with the government it took up arms against and open war with the ally it fought beside — the resistance is fragmenting into its component nationalisms exactly as the junta stabilises."}, {"id":"kia","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Bhamo and rare-earth belt reporting","name":"KIA","full":"Kachin Independence Army","affil":"MYANMAR · Kachin ethnic","icon":"✧","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Sits on the rare-earth belt","theaters":["myanmar"],"mil":"One of the oldest ethnic armies in the country, pressing junta battalions around BHAMO and holding much of Kachin State. Northern Alliance member; allied with the NUG's PDFs.","log":"The China frontier and long-established base areas.","eco":"THE RARE EARTHS. Kachin's heavy rare-earth mines feed Chinese processing — the KIA's control of that belt gives it a revenue stream and gives Beijing a reason to deal with it directly, whatever Naypyidaw says. Also jade.","gvn":"ADMINISTERS AND TAXES THE SUPPLY CHAIN. The Kachin Independence Organisation runs civil administration, courts, schools and taxation across the territory the KIA holds — and levies the JADE AND RARE-EARTH extraction that feeds Chinese processing. This is the detail that matters beyond Myanmar: part of the global rare-earth chain runs through territory administered by an armed group in a civil war, which is a supply-chain exposure no buyer has priced.","assess":"The clearest link between Myanmar's war and the minerals system this platform tracks. Chinese magnet supply chains run through territory held by an armed group in open revolt against a government China is simultaneously underwriting."}, {"id":"uwsa","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ICG; Wa SAD reporting","name":"UWSA","full":"United Wa State Army","affil":"MYANMAR · Wa ethnic · Chinese-equipped","icon":"▣","color":"#b8863d","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"~30,000 · the best-armed non-state force in Asia","theaters":["myanmar"],"mil":"Around 30,000 troops with modern Chinese weapons — reportedly including man-portable air defence and armoured vehicles — governing the WA SELF-ADMINISTERED DIVISION as a de facto state with its own administration, schools and currency circulation. It has largely stayed OUT of the civil war while everyone else fights.","log":"Direct Chinese supply; territorial contiguity with Yunnan.","eco":"Mining, agriculture, and a long history in the narcotics economy; now a substantial legitimate trade sector too.","gvn":"A DE FACTO STATE. The Wa region operates its own administration, army, currency circulation and border regime with Chinese infrastructure and, effectively, Chinese economic integration. It is the clearest case on this board of an armed group that stopped being an insurgency and became a polity.","assess":"The end-state every other ethnic army is fighting toward, achieved decades ago and preserved by not fighting. The Wa demonstrate that Myanmar's real settlement was never federalism — it was armed autonomy tolerated by Beijing."}, {"id":"pdf-nug","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Sagaing resistance reporting","name":"PDFs / NUG","full":"People's Defence Forces · National Unity Government","affil":"MYANMAR · multi-ethnic anti-coup","icon":"⚑","color":"#7d5ac7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Hundreds of local units · Sagaing is their heartland","theaters":["myanmar"],"mil":"The armed wing of the parallel National Unity Government — hundreds of locally raised People's Defence Forces, overwhelmingly BAMAR-MAJORITY young people who took up arms after the 2021 coup. SAGAING remains the resistance heartland under relentless airstrikes. PDFs fight alongside the AA in Ayeyarwady and Magway, the KIA in the north, and Karenni forces besieging Loikaw.","log":"Locally made weapons, 3D-printed components, ethnic-army training and diaspora funding.","eco":"Diaspora remittances and local contribution — the least resource-financed force in this war.","gvn":"A PARALLEL GOVERNMENT, NOT A MILITIA. The National Unity Government maintains ministries, a civil service in exile, revenue-raising through bond issues and diaspora taxation, and coordination with ethnic armed organisations that have their own administrations. Myanmar does not have a state and an insurgency; it has competing administrations, which is why 'restoring order' has no agreed meaning.","assess":"A mass armed movement with almost no resource base, fighting on donations and improvised weapons, sustained for five years by political conviction. Any framework that reduces conflict to who controls what mineral has to account for Sagaing — and this one says so."}, {"id":"pna-junta-militias","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Junta auxiliary reporting","name":"PNA & junta militias","full":"Pa-O National Army · Pyusawhti · Border Guard Forces","affil":"MYANMAR · pro-junta auxiliaries","icon":"●","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The state's ethnic proxies","theaters":["myanmar"],"mil":"The PA-O NATIONAL ARMY guards the Pa-O Self-Administered Zone in southern Shan and has actively RECRUITED FOR THE TATMADAW since the coup. Alongside it sit the Pyusawhti militias and assorted Border Guard Forces — locally raised, state-armed, and implicated in violence against resistance-aligned communities.","log":"State weapons and local recruitment in the zones they administer.","eco":"Local levies and control of the administered zones.","assess":"Every side in this war has ethnic auxiliaries, including the junta. The framing of 'ethnic armies versus the military' obscures that several ethnic organisations chose the state — which is what a genuinely multi-national conflict looks like from inside."}, {"id":"nw-bandits","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Amnesty International (725 villages); ACLED","name":"Northwest bandit syndicates","full":"Zamfara/Katsina/Sokoto armed groups · Turji, Dogo Gidé, Sububu and others","affil":"NIGERIAN · CRIMINAL · Hausa and Fulani","icon":"◆","color":"#c77d4a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"20,000-30,000 fighters · 725 villages under their control","theaters":["nigeria","lakurawa"],"mil":"Not one organisation but dozens of competing gangs under named kachallas — BELLO TURJI, DOGO GIDÉ, HALILU SUBUBU SENO, DAN KARAMI, ALI KACHALLA and others. Estimates put 20,000-30,000 armed bandits across the northwest, ONE THIRD OF THEM IN ZAMFARA, which was Nigeria's deadliest state in 2024. They fight the state, the vigilantes and EACH OTHER — inter-group clashes are among the most frequent incident types. Both HAUSA and FULANI militias operate in the space.","log":"Motorcycles, forest camps in the Rugu and Kamuku reserves, and weapons flowing from the Sahel and from corrupt supply.","eco":"A GOVERNANCE ECONOMY, not just robbery: Amnesty documents 725 VILLAGES ACROSS 13 LGAs under bandit control where residents are TAXED under threat of death, abduction or eviction — people have been killed for refusing to pay. Plus mass-abduction ransom and illegal gold mining.","assess":"This is criminal enterprise with proto-governance, not ideology — Muslim communities are its principal victims, and its perpetrators are Muslim. It is the largest source of violent deaths in Nigeria and the least ideological, which is exactly why the platform's Nigeria Competing Read insists the four fronts be kept analytically separate."}, {"id":"ansaru","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA actor mapping","name":"Ansaru & Mahmuda","full":"Jama'atu Ansaril Muslimina fi Biladis-Sudan · Mahmuda group","affil":"NIGERIAN · al-Qaeda-aligned","icon":"⚔","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Al-Qaeda's Nigerian foothold — in the northwest, not the northeast","theaters":["nigeria"],"mil":"A Boko Haram splinter that broke away in 2012 over the killing of Muslim civilians and aligned with AL-QAEDA rather than Islamic State. It operates in the NORTHWEST and North-Central — Kaduna, Niger and Kogi states — not the northeastern theatre, and EUAA identifies armed conflict between Ansaru, the MAHMUDA group and state forces as a distinct conflict strand. Specialises in kidnapping foreigners and high-value targets.","log":"Kainji forest complex and the Niger-state corridor; links westward toward JNIM's expanding zone.","eco":"Ransom kidnapping and taxation of the areas it holds.","assess":"The bridge between two wars. Ansaru's al-Qaeda alignment and northwestern geography put it exactly where JNIM's southward expansion is heading — if the Sahelian and Nigerian jihadist theatres merge, this is the seam they merge along."}, {"id":"jnim-nigeria","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"JNIM component-unit reporting","name":"JNIM Nigerian Brigade","full":"Katiba Nigeria · JNIM's Nigerian unit","affil":"AL-QAEDA · Sahel-to-Nigeria expansion","icon":"▲","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The Sahel war arriving in Nigeria under its own flag","theaters":["nigeria","lakurawa","niger"],"mil":"JNIM maintains a designated NIGERIAN BRIGADE among its component units — formal organisational recognition that its war now extends into Nigeria rather than merely spilling over. It operates alongside the Lakurawa implant on the Nigeria-Niger frontier.","log":"The Sokoto-Kebbi-Niger border corridor and the same routes Lakurawa uses.","eco":"Cattle levies, transit taxation and the pastoral economy of the frontier.","assess":"Ends the argument about whether the Sahel insurgency is 'coming south'. Al-Qaeda's Sahelian coalition has stood up a named Nigerian formation — the coastal-expansion thesis this platform tracks in Benin and Togo has a Nigerian chapter too."}, {"id":"ipob-esn","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA; Imo State reporting","name":"IPOB / ESN","full":"Indigenous People of Biafra · Eastern Security Network","affil":"NIGERIAN IGBO · SEPARATIST","icon":"✧","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The southeast's secessionist front","theaters":["nigeria"],"mil":"The ESN is the armed wing of the proscribed INDIGENOUS PEOPLE OF BIAFRA, which seeks secession of the Southeast and parts of the South-South. Formed in 2020 nominally to protect Igbo farmers from herder attacks, it now fights Nigerian security forces directly; EUAA classes ESN-state conflict as a distinct armed conflict strand. Its 'sit-at-home' enforcement has shut down the region's economy on Mondays for years.","log":"Southeastern forests, diaspora financing, and local recruitment.","eco":"Diaspora remittance funding and local levies — the most externally-financed of Nigeria's insurgencies.","assess":"This is the Biafra question — unresolved since 1970 and the war that killed a million people — returning in armed form. It has nothing to do with jihadism, banditry or minerals: it is a sovereignty and memory conflict, and it is the clearest evidence that Nigeria's crises share a state rather than a cause."}, {"id":"askarawa","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ACLED; Zamfara CPG reporting","name":"Askarawa & vigilantes","full":"Zamfara Community Protection Guards · Vigilante Group of Nigeria · CJTF","affil":"NIGERIAN · STATE-SANCTIONED","icon":"⚑","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The state's answer to a force it cannot defeat","theaters":["nigeria"],"mil":"State-backed community forces: the ASKARAWA (Zamfara Community Protection Guards) were re-formed in early 2024 after an earlier iteration was BANNED OVER ITS OWN VIOLENCE, with training added to prevent a repeat. Alongside them sit the Vigilante Group of Nigeria and, in the northeast, the Civilian Joint Task Force. ACLED codes Zamfara communal militias as parties to the majority of violence-against-civilians incidents in the state.","log":"State-issued and locally-sourced weapons; community recruitment.","eco":"Checkpoints and protection levies in the communities they hold.","assess":"The same trade Burkina made with the VDP and Kinshasa made with the Wazalendo: manpower now, an armed constituency later. A vigilante force banned for atrocities and then re-formed is a state admitting it has no alternative."}, {"id":"herder-farmer","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA; ACLED communal violence data","name":"Herder-farmer militias","full":"Middle Belt communal militias · Plateau, Benue, Nasarawa","affil":"NIGERIAN · COMMUNAL","icon":"●","color":"#a8763d","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The conflict most often mislabelled","theaters":["nigeria"],"mil":"Armed communal militias of FARMERS and HERDERS across the North-Central, North-West and South-South. EUAA treats these as distinct armed conflicts in their own right. The violence is reciprocal — both farming communities and pastoralist communities field militias and both suffer massacres. In 2026 a Fulani group in Plateau State alleged a rival militia was using DRONES.","log":"Local weapons markets, community mobilisation, and increasingly commercial drones.","eco":"LAND AND WATER. Desertification pushes herders south into farmland as farmland expands north — the resource conflict is the substance, ethnicity and religion the identity through which it is fought.","assess":"The front most consistently misreported abroad as religious persecution. It is a climate-and-land conflict fought between communities that happen to differ in faith and livelihood — ACLED's data does not support a one-directional persecution narrative, which is precisely what the platform's InfoOps board tracks about how this violence is framed."}, {"id":"us-nigeria-op","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Joint operation reporting May 2026","name":"US intervention force","full":"US-Nigeria joint operation (from May 2026)","affil":"UNITED STATES · external","icon":"▣","color":"#4a7dc7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"SEALs and airstrikes — ~175 militants killed in the first three days","theaters":["nigeria"],"mil":"On 16 MAY 2026 the US and Nigeria began joint operations against ISWAP and Boko Haram in the northeast — special forces raids (US Navy SEALs) plus multiple rounds of airstrikes. ISWAP senior leader ABU-BILAL AL-MINUKI was killed with several other commanders; Nigerian authorities counted ~175 militants killed by 19 May. Reported force ratio: roughly two dozen US personnel against three dozen fighters in the initial raid. No US casualties reported.","log":"US air and ISR assets, Nigerian basing, and a narrow northeastern focus.","eco":"Not a revenue actor — but the intervention arrived alongside US interest in Nigerian critical minerals and a public 'Christian genocide' justification the data does not support.","assess":"Operationally it targets the jihadist northeast — which is real, and which the Nigerian state has failed to defeat for fifteen years. But the humanitarian framing used to sell it describes a persecution pattern ACLED's numbers contradict, and the northwest banditry that kills more Nigerians is not the target. The platform logs the operation as effective against ISWAP and the justification as a separate question."}, {"id":"ansar-dine","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Africa Center for Strategic Studies; ICC warrant","name":"Ansar Dine","full":"Ansar Dine · Iyad Ag Ghali · JNIM's core","affil":"MALIAN TUAREG · al-Qaeda","icon":"⚔","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"The command that now runs JNIM","theaters":["mali","burkina","niger"],"mil":"Founded by IYAD AG GHALI, a TUAREG from northern Mali and a veteran of the 1990s rebellions who turned to Salafism. Ansar Dine was one of four groups that merged into JNIM in 2017; after the deaths of the AQIM-Sahara and al-Mourabitoun leaders, its command absorbed their operations — so Ag Ghali is now JNIM's emir and its dominant faction. The ICC has issued an ARREST WARRANT for him for war crimes and crimes against humanity. Second-in-command: Sedane Ag Hita. HQ: Tinzaouaten, on the Algerian border.","log":"The Algeria-Mali frontier and the old Tuareg smuggling routes Ag Ghali has known since the 1990s.","eco":"Kidnap ransom, transit taxation and gold sites along the northern routes.","assess":"The single most consequential fact about JNIM: its emir is a TUAREG NATIONALIST who became a jihadist, not a foreign import. The insurgency's leadership is Malian, its grievances predate the Salafism, and treating it as al-Qaeda's franchise misses that the franchise came second."}, {"id":"katiba-macina","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Africa Center for Strategic Studies","name":"Katiba Macina / FLM","full":"Macina Liberation Front · Amadou Koufa","affil":"MALIAN FULANI-ASSOCIATED · JNIM","icon":"▲","color":"#c77d4a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"~3,000 fighters · the engine of central Mali's violence","theaters":["mali","burkina"],"mil":"Led by AMADOU KOUFA, operating across central Mali and northern Burkina Faso. Its name invokes the 19th-century FULANI MACINA EMPIRE — a deliberate claim to a pre-colonial political order. It runs the sieges and blockades that now strangle Bamako's supply routes. Offshoot: Katiba Serma under Abu Jalil al Fulani.","log":"Inland Niger Delta and the central Mali pastoral zone; recruitment through herder communities under pressure.","eco":"Cattle taxation, blockade tolls, and control of the roads into central Mali.","gvn":"GOVERNS CENTRAL MALI THROUGH A LAND DISPUTE. Applies sharia adjudication, collects zakat, regulates markets and schooling across the inner Niger delta — built on Fulani pastoralist grievance over land access, herding corridors and state predation. THE LABEL OBSCURES THE MECHANISM: this is a pastoralist land conflict with a jihadist vocabulary, which is why counter-terrorism operations against it keep producing more of it.","assess":"Fulani are disproportionately represented among Sahelian militant fighters — but the Africa Center is explicit that FLM is NOT an exclusively Fulani group and that most Fulani reject Koufa. The PERCEPTION that it is Fulani has driven ethnic profiling and reprisal killings by state forces and militias, which Koufa then exploits for recruitment. This is the mechanism the platform's Burkina and Mali Competing Reads name: the counter-insurgency manufactures the insurgency's next cohort."}, {"id":"fla","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"2026 Mali offensives reporting; RFI","name":"FLA / Azawad Liberation Front","full":"Front de Libération de l'Azawad · Alghabass Ag Intalla","affil":"MALIAN TUAREG · SECULAR SEPARATIST","icon":"✧","color":"#4a9dc7","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Claimed Kidal and parts of Gao in the April 2026 offensive","theaters":["mali"],"mil":"The principal vehicle for TUAREG SECESSIONIST ambition, uniting surviving elements of the MNLA, the High Council for the Unity of Azawad and parts of the Arab Movement of Azawad under ALGHABASS AG INTALLA and Bilal Ag Acherif. It is SECULAR, frames its struggle in nationalist rather than religious terms, and professes adherence to international humanitarian law. Its objective is independence or substantial autonomy for Azawad. In APRIL 2026 it began COORDINATING OPERATIONS WITH JNIM — joint attacks across Bourem, Kati, Sévaré, Senou and Mopti in the largest offensive since the 2012 rebellion.","log":"Northern Mali's desert space and the Algerian frontier; long-standing community networks.","eco":"Northern trade routes and the political economy of Kidal.","assess":"A secular nationalist movement and a Salafi-jihadist coalition have opposite end-states — they fought each other in 2012-13 — and are now attacking together because both want Bamako's army gone first. The junta's principal achievement has been to unite the two things that were never united."}, {"id":"africa-corps","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Global Centre for R2P; Bellingcat","name":"Africa Corps","full":"Russian Africa Corps (formerly Wagner Group)","affil":"RUSSIAN STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#8a4a4a","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"Moscow's direct instrument — and a documented atrocity actor","theaters":["mali","burkina","niger","libya"],"mil":"Wagner's successor, now RUSSIAN STATE-CONTROLLED under the defence ministry; commanders include Andrey Averyanov and Vladimir Selivyorstov. It fights alongside FAMa in Mali and has been documented by the Global Centre for R2P committing possible WAR CRIMES AND CRIMES AGAINST HUMANITY — summary executions, indiscriminate airstrikes, rape and sexual violence, pillaging and torture. Bellingcat and Jeune Afrique documented the FIRST CONFIRMED USE OF RUSSIAN CLUSTER MUNITIONS in Mali. It lost a Mi-35 helicopter with its crew in the 2026 offensives.","log":"Russian military airlift, drones and air support; a small deployed force relative to its influence.","eco":"PAID IN MINING CONCESSIONS AND GOLD, not cash — the model this platform tracks: security services rendered against resource rights, so the junta's protection is financed by the sale of the thing it is protecting.","assess":"Sovereignty rhetoric with a foreign army inside it. The AES states expelled France and the US on anti-colonial grounds, then contracted a European power that takes payment in minerals and answers to no Malian court."}, {"id":"vdp","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Global Centre for R2P","name":"VDP","full":"Volontaires pour la défense de la patrie · Burkina Faso","affil":"BURKINABÈ STATE MILITIA · Mossi-majority","icon":"◆","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Recruitment drive sought 50,000 — drew 90,000+ applications","theaters":["burkina"],"mil":"A STATE PARAMILITARY created by decree in 2020 under Kaboré and expanded aggressively by Traoré after his 2022 coup. Drawn largely from KOGLWEOGO and DOZO self-defence traditions, and predominantly MOSSI. The Global Centre for R2P records VDP units implicated in GRAVE CRIMES ALONG ETHNIC LINES; analysts document extortion, looting, land seizure, extrajudicial killings, ethnic cleansing against Fulani — and in places, deals with the jihadists they were raised to fight.","log":"State-issued weapons, local recruitment, minimal training relative to the army.","eco":"Local extraction: checkpoints, land seizure and control of gold sites in the zones they hold.","gvn":"THE STATE OUTSOURCING VIOLENCE TO CIVILIANS. Volunteers for the Defence of the Homeland are state-armed and state-trained but not state-disciplined — recruited locally, deployed against neighbours, and implicated in communal reprisals the government can decline to own. Structurally identical to the settler outpost entry on the West Bank file: arm a civilian movement, keep the deniability.","assess":"The counter-insurgency has produced a second insurgency problem. VDP units carve out QUASI-INDEPENDENT ENCLAVES with only nominal loyalty to Ouagadougou — 'mini statelets' — and their search for collaborators fuels the communal tension jihadists recruit on. Burkina armed 90,000 people to defeat an insurgency and created a parallel armed order it does not command."}, {"id":"dozo","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Africa Center for Strategic Studies","name":"Dozo militias","full":"Dozo · traditional hunter-warrior brotherhoods","affil":"MALIAN/BURKINABÈ · pre-colonial institution","icon":"●","color":"#a8763d","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"One of the three pillars the Malian junta rests on","theaters":["mali","burkina","cote-divoire"],"mil":"Hunter-warrior brotherhoods with PRE-COLONIAL roots across Mande West Africa, historically responsible for community protection and initiated through their own hierarchies. Analysts count the Dozo as one of THREE PILLARS of the Malian junta's security — alongside the ~25,000-strong FAMa and Africa Corps. Dozo formations have been implicated in massacres of Fulani civilians.","log":"Community-based, self-equipping, with state tolerance and periodic supply.","eco":"Hunting, artisanal mining and local levies.","gvn":"PRE-COLONIAL AUTHORITY, MILITARISED. Traditional Mande hunter societies with customary initiation, adjudication and community sanction long predating the modern state — now armed as counter-insurgency auxiliaries. JNIM's campaign against them this month targets the manpower base directly, and because Dozo formations have been implicated in massacres of Fulani civilians, each round feeds the communal recruitment both sides run on.","assess":"The state is outsourcing security to an institution older than the state itself — which works tactically and corrodes strategically: when a pre-colonial brotherhood becomes a counter-insurgency force, communal massacre becomes state policy by proxy."}, {"id":"msa-gatia","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Africa Center for Strategic Studies","name":"MSA & GATIA","full":"Mouvement pour le Salut de l'Azawad · GATIA","affil":"MALIAN TUAREG · PRO-BAMAKO","icon":"◇","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"~3,000 and ~1,000 fighters — Tuareg against Tuareg","theaters":["mali","niger"],"mil":"Two MONO-ETHNIC TUAREG formations LOYAL TO BAMAKO: the MSA drawn from the DAOUSSAHAK community (~3,000) and GATIA from the IMGHAD (~1,000). They fight both the jihadists and, at times, the separatist FLA — which is also Tuareg.","log":"Community recruitment in the Gao and Ménaka regions; state and occasionally French-era support.","eco":"Livestock and control of local trade corridors.","assess":"The clearest refutation of 'the Tuareg want independence'. Tuareg society is stratified — Imghad and Daoussahak communities have their own reasons to oppose the Ifoghas-led separatist project — and Bamako has used those internal divisions as a security instrument for a decade. The war within the war."}, {"id":"saf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA actor mapping; ACLED","name":"SAF","full":"Sudanese Armed Forces","affil":"SUDANESE STATE · Burhan","icon":"⚑","color":"#5a8dc7","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"The state army — fighting with the old regime's militias inside it","theaters":["sudan"],"mil":"Sudan's regular army under Abdel Fattah al-Burhan, holding Khartoum, the north and the east. Its momentum stalled in mid-2025 as the war moved toward RSF strongholds in Darfur and West Kordofan; the fall of EL FASHER in October 2025 after an 18-month siege was its worst reversal. It retains the decisive asset the RSF lacks: an air force, used for strikes across Darfur and Kordofan.","log":"State arsenals, Egyptian and Iranian-supplied drones, and an air arm. Command over its own coalition is loose — allied militias have clashed with each other.","eco":"Control of the central state, the banking system, and Port Sudan — the customs and remittance economy. Gold revenue it does NOT control is the RSF's counterweight.","assess":"THE TELL: to hold the line the SAF has increasingly relied on ISLAMIST MILITIAS ORIGINALLY MOBILISED UNDER THE BASHIR REGIME. The revolution of 2019 removed Bashir; the war is restoring the networks that sustained him. Whoever wins, that constituency returns armed."}, {"id":"jfasm","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA; El Fasher siege reporting","name":"Joint Force (JFASM)","full":"Joint Force of Armed Struggle Movements","affil":"DARFURI REBELS · now SAF-allied","icon":"◈","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Held El Fasher for 18 months","theaters":["sudan"],"mil":"A coalition of DARFURI rebel movements that signed the 2020 Juba Peace Agreement — principally JEM and SLM-Minnawi under Minni Arko Minnawi, plus smaller factions. It stayed NEUTRAL for the war's first year, then declared against the RSF and allied with the SAF in early 2024. It was the backbone of the 18-month defence of El Fasher, repulsing dozens of assaults until the city fell in October 2025. Since then its forces have been redeployed to other fronts; in January 2026 it claimed the recapture of Jargeira in North Darfur.","log":"Darfuri recruitment base, SAF coordination, and the Chad border corridor.","eco":"Juba Agreement entitlements and territorial revenue in the areas it holds.","assess":"Former rebels against Khartoum now dying for it. The Juba Peace Agreement converted insurgents into stakeholders, and the war converted stakeholders into the state's most effective infantry — an argument for peace agreements working, and for what they cost the communities that signed them."}, {"id":"splmn-hilu","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA; Kordofan reporting 2026","name":"SPLM-N (al-Hilu)","full":"Sudan People's Liberation Movement-North · al-Hilu faction","affil":"NUBA MOUNTAINS · RSF-allied","icon":"▲","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Besieging Dilling and Kadugli","theaters":["sudan"],"mil":"A SUDANESE movement rooted in the NUBA MOUNTAINS and South Kordofan, holding territory it has governed autonomously since the 1980s. Neutral early in the war, it is now aligned with the RSF: as of 2026 the fiercest fighting is in Kordofan where SPLM-N forces besiege the SAF-held cities of DILLING and KADUGLI. In January 2026 it joined an RSF push into Blue Nile launched from SOUTH SUDANESE territory.","log":"Long-established Nuba base areas, cross-border routes through South Sudan, and RSF coordination.","eco":"Subsistence and local taxation in the Nuba areas; the alliance supplies the heavier materiel.","gvn":"THE OLDEST ADMINISTRATION IN SUDAN'S WAR. Has governed the Nuba Mountains for decades through its own civil authority — courts, schools, land allocation — with a secular self-determination platform distinct from both Khartoum and the RSF. It is now besieging Dilling and Kadugli alongside the RSF, which is a tactical alignment rather than a shared project.","assess":"The war has re-activated Sudan's OLDEST unresolved question — the periphery's status — by giving its most durable rebel movement a powerful new patron. A Nuba movement fighting for self-determination since before either belligerent existed is now a subcontractor in someone else's war."}, {"id":"sudan-shield","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA; Gezira defection reporting","name":"Sudan Shield Forces","full":"Sudan Shield Forces · Abu Aqla Kaikal","affil":"SUDANESE MILITIA · defected to SAF","icon":"◆","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The defection that changed Gezira","theaters":["sudan"],"mil":"A militia led by ABU AQLA KAIKAL, originally aligned with the RSF in Gezira state, which DEFECTED TO THE SAF — a switch that helped unravel RSF control of the farming heartland. Formally incorporated into SAF operations from April 2026, including the Blue Nile campaign. Its camps have been hit by RSF drone strikes in the Butana plains.","log":"Gezira and Butana networks; now SAF-supplied.","eco":"Agricultural Gezira — Sudan's breadbasket — and the tax that comes with holding it.","assess":"Proof that Sudan's war is fought by units whose allegiance is transactional, not ideological. Kaikal's defection moved a front line; nothing about the cause changed."}, {"id":"slm-aw","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA; Jebel Marra reporting Jan 2026","name":"SLM-AW","full":"Sudan Liberation Movement · Abdel Wahid al-Nur","affil":"DARFURI · NEUTRAL","icon":"○","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"WATCH","strength":"The faction that refused both sides — and governs anyway","theaters":["sudan"],"mil":"A DARFURI movement, historically Fur-based, that refused to sign the Juba Peace Agreement and has stayed OUT of the SAF-RSF war. In January 2026 it ordered Sudanese state symbols replaced with its own across the 'Liberated Areas' it controls in Central and North Darfur — declaring administrative sovereignty without joining the fight.","log":"Jebel Marra highlands — the terrain that has kept it independent for two decades.","eco":"Local administration and taxation in the areas it holds.","gvn":"PROTECTS RATHER THAN TAXES. Abdul Wahid's faction holds Jebel Marra and has refused every peace agreement since 2006 on the grounds that they legitimise the perpetrators. It functions less as a revenue authority than as a civilian-protection presence for displaced Fur communities — which is why it has endured without the extraction base every other Darfur actor relies on.","assess":"The most interesting actor on the board precisely because it does nothing. While two armies destroy the country contesting the centre, SLM-AW is quietly building a third answer in the mountains: not victory, not surrender — exit."}, {"id":"tribal-militias","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA (241 armed actors); ACLED","name":"Tribal militias","full":"Zaghawa Council · Dar al-Hamar · Misseriya and others","affil":"SUDANESE COMMUNITIES · SPLIT","icon":"✧","color":"#c77d4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"The layer where allegiance fractures","theaters":["sudan"],"mil":"Community militias that mostly operate as home-region defence forces while supplying one side or the other. The ZAGHAWA COUNCIL helped hold North Darfur for the SAF early on. The RSF recruits heavily from the MISSERIYA. The DAR AL-HAMAR militia of Kordofan has ELEMENTS FIGHTING ON BOTH SIDES — and has historic tensions with the Misseriya. Clashes have occurred BETWEEN SAF-aligned groups.","log":"Local, communal, and cheap — which is why both belligerents rely on them.","eco":"Livestock, land, and control of local routes; the grievances predate the war and will outlast it.","assess":"This is where 'who is fighting whom' stops being answerable at the level of two generals. Sudan's war runs on roughly 241 identified armed actors; allegiance splits by FACTION and sometimes by VILLAGE, not by movement. Any settlement negotiated between Burhan and Hemedti addresses a fraction of the men with guns."}, {"id":"rsf-parallel","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"RSF declaration Apr 2025; UK sanctions Jul 2026","name":"Government of Peace and Unity","full":"RSF parallel government (from April 2025)","affil":"RSF POLITICAL WING","icon":"▣","color":"#e06a5f","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"The partition, formalised","theaters":["sudan"],"mil":"The RSF's parallel administration, declared in April 2025, claiming to govern the territory it holds — most of Darfur and parts of Kordofan. It gives the RSF's conquests an administrative face: ministries, appointments, and the machinery of a rival state.","log":"Runs on the same UAE-linked gold corridor and Chadian and Libyan supply routes that arm the RSF.","eco":"DARFUR'S GOLD. The parallel government's viability rests on exporting it — which is why the UK's July 2026 sanctions on gold and finance networks target the structure rather than the commanders.","gvn":"A DECLARED STATE WITHOUT A SOCIETY. The Government of Peace and Unity has ministries, appointed officials and a claimed territory financed by Darfur gold. What it does not have is service provision, consent, or a population that stayed — which is why its formation reads as a partition instrument rather than an administration.","assess":"Sudan is no longer heading toward partition; it has a second government with a treasury, a territory and a foreign patron. Recognition is the only variable left, and El Obeid is the next city that would harden the line."}, {"id":"fdlr","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts; HRW; ICG (2026)","name":"FDLR","full":"Forces démocratiques de libération du Rwanda","affil":"RWANDAN origin · Hutu","icon":"⚔","color":"#c77d4a","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Rwanda's stated casus belli — and now Kinshasa's co-belligerent","theaters":["drc","rwanda","burundi"],"mil":"A largely RWANDAN HUTU armed group based on Congolese soil, some of whose leaders took part in the 1994 genocide in Rwanda (HRW). Its presence is Rwanda's PRIMARY STATED JUSTIFICATION for backing M23. Sanctioned under UNSC Resolution 1533. Operationally it now fights ALONGSIDE the Congolese army and Wazalendo militias against M23 — UN Group of Experts reporting confirms joint FARDC-FDLR-Wazalendo lines around Kibirizi and Bwito, which Kinshasa denies.","log":"Entrenched in North Kivu's Virunga fringe; conducts incursions into M23-held areas. Sustained by local taxation and alliance with Nyatura and Wazalendo formations.","eco":"Artisanal mining, charcoal from Virunga National Park, and roadblock taxation — the low-margin end of the same extraction economy M23 monetises at scale.","gvn":"THIRTY YEARS ON SOMEONE ELSE'S TERRITORY. Has sustained itself on Congolese soil since 1994 through local taxation, mining and cross-border trade rather than any external sponsor — which is why it has outlasted every operation mounted against it. It is Kigali's stated justification for intervention, and Kinshasa's co-belligerent, at the same time.","assess":"If FDLR did not exist, Rwanda would need another justification; because it does exist, and because Kinshasa fights beside it, Kigali's security argument cannot be dismissed as pretext alone. The platform's Competing Read on the DRC rests here: minerals finance the war (HIGH confidence), but this group is why the security logic is real too."}, {"id":"adf","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UNSC Res 1533; Shujaa operation reporting","name":"ADF / ISCAP","full":"Allied Democratic Forces / Islamic State Central Africa Province","affil":"UGANDAN origin · IS-affiliated","icon":"☠","color":"#b83d3d","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"The only IS-branded force in the Congo basin — and it strikes Uganda too","theaters":["drc"],"mil":"Founded in UGANDA in the 1990s as an anti-Museveni Islamist insurgency, pushed into eastern DRC, and formally recognised by Islamic State as its Central Africa Province in 2019. Responsible for repeated mass-casualty massacres of Congolese civilians in North Kivu and Ituri, and has intensified attacks INSIDE UGANDA. Sanctioned under UNSC Resolution 1533. Ugandan and Congolese forces run joint operations (Shujaa) against it, with limited effect.","log":"Dispersed cells in the Rwenzori forests across the DRC-Uganda border; the frontier itself is the logistics asset.","eco":"Gold, timber, and abduction-for-labour. IS affiliation brings branding and some finance, but the group predates and exceeds it — the money is local.","assess":"The group most often mis-framed. It is NOT a Rwanda-DRC issue and not primarily a minerals story: it is a Ugandan insurgency exported to Congolese territory that has since found an IS franchise. Counting it in the M23 conflict confuses two separate wars sharing one map."}, {"id":"codeco","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Joint Human Rights Office; ACLED Ituri","name":"CODECO","full":"Coopérative pour le développement du Congo","affil":"CONGOLESE · Lendu","icon":"◆","color":"#8a6db8","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Ituri's deadliest actor for civilians","theaters":["drc"],"mil":"A CONGOLESE Lendu-community militia network in Ituri, framing itself as protector of Lendu farmers against Hema pastoralist militias. Fragmented into competing factions. Attacks on displacement sites have killed hundreds. With FARDC redeployed to the M23 front, CODECO has expanded in the vacuum.","log":"Rural Ituri base; local recruitment; no external state patron identified.","eco":"Ituri's ARTISANAL GOLD — the province is one of Africa's richest goldfields and CODECO taxes pits and routes directly.","gvn":"ETHNIC AUTHORITY, NOT GOVERNMENT. Claims to defend Lendu communities and levies gold sites and roads in Ituri, but operates as a fragmented set of factions with no unified command or civil structure. The distinction matters for policy: there is no counterpart to negotiate with, which is why demobilisation efforts keep failing here.","assess":"A purely internal conflict lineage — the Hema-Lendu antagonism runs to the colonial administration's ethnic classification and the 1999-2003 Ituri war. No foreign sponsor is needed to explain it, which is exactly why it tests the platform's external-actor lens."}, {"id":"zaire","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Joint Human Rights Office; ACLED Ituri","name":"Zaire militia","full":"Zaire / Force de résistance patriotique","affil":"CONGOLESE · Hema","icon":"◇","color":"#c7a54a","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"CODECO's mirror image","theaters":["drc"],"mil":"A CONGOLESE HEMA-aligned militia in Ituri formed in direct opposition to CODECO. The two groups' reciprocal massacres define Ituri's violence; both claim community self-defence.","log":"Local Ituri networks; periodic informal cooperation with elements of the state security forces.","eco":"Gold sites and cattle; the same Ituri goldfields CODECO contests.","assess":"Together with CODECO it demonstrates the platform's own caution: two Congolese communal militias, no foreign patron, fighting over land and gold — a conflict the extraction lens explains only partially and the identity lens explains better."}, {"id":"wazalendo","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"HRW abuse documentation; UN Group of Experts","name":"Wazalendo","full":"Wazalendo (‘patriots’) — pro-government militia coalition","affil":"CONGOLESE · state-aligned","icon":"⚑","color":"#5aa86e","tier":"SEVERE","strength":"Kinshasa's irregular army — and an abuse problem","theaters":["drc"],"mil":"A loose coalition of CONGOLESE Mai-Mai and community self-defence militias fighting alongside the FARDC against M23. Formerly labelled rebels, rebranded as 'patriots' after the state armed them. HRW documents WIDESPREAD ABUSES against civilians; after the army's retreat they took control of several South Kivu localities in their own right.","log":"State-supplied weapons plus captured stocks; fights beside FARDC, Burundian troops and, per UN experts, FDLR.","eco":"Roadblocks, mining-site taxation and protection payments — the militia economy the state has licensed rather than replaced.","gvn":"COMMUNITY SELF-DEFENCE, NOT GOVERNMENT. Locally raised militias fighting alongside Congolese forces, with authority derived from the communities that raised them rather than from any administrative structure. Their legitimacy is local and their coordination is weak — which is both why they persist and why they cannot hold territory as a state would.","assess":"The clearest case of a government solving a security problem by creating a governance one. Arming community militias bought manpower against M23 and produced a network of armed actors Kinshasa does not command — the Haiti pattern in a different register."}, {"id":"twirwaneho","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts; Minembwe reporting Jun 2026","name":"Twirwaneho","full":"Twirwaneho — Banyamulenge self-defence movement","affil":"CONGOLESE · Banyamulenge Tutsi · M23-aligned","icon":"✧","color":"#4a9dc7","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"Holds Minembwe against a three-army offensive","theaters":["drc"],"mil":"A CONGOLESE BANYAMULENGE (Tutsi) armed movement in the South Kivu highlands, formed to defend a community whose Congolese citizenship has been contested for decades. UN experts confirm collaboration with M23. In June 2026 the FARDC, BURUNDIAN army (FDNB) and Wazalendo launched an offensive to encircle Minembwe; Twirwaneho counterattacked around 19 June under commander Charles Sematama, held the town centre, and reportedly captured BELGIAN MERCENARIES fighting on the government side.","log":"Highland terrain, community base, and M23 alignment for materiel.","eco":"Cattle economy and highland trade routes rather than minerals — among the least extraction-driven actors in the war.","gvn":"COMMUNITY SELF-DEFENCE ON A CITIZENSHIP QUESTION. Banyamulenge fighters in the South Kivu highlands, whose grievance is contested Congolese citizenship rather than territory or resources. That makes it the hardest actor on the DRC file to resolve militarily — no settlement that leaves the citizenship question open removes the reason it exists.","assess":"The citizenship question in armed form. This is the Banyamulenge answer to decades of contested belonging — and the presence of foreign mercenaries on the other side shows how thoroughly the 'local militia' framing has broken down."}, {"id":"nyatura","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts; North Kivu monitoring","name":"Nyatura","full":"Nyatura — Congolese Hutu militia network","affil":"CONGOLESE · Hutu · FDLR-aligned","icon":"▲","color":"#a8763d","tier":"ELEVATED","strength":"The FDLR's Congolese partner","theaters":["drc"],"mil":"CONGOLESE HUTU militias in North Kivu, aligned with the Rwandan-origin FDLR. Multiple factions with shifting names and leaders.","log":"North Kivu highlands; shares territory and supply with FDLR formations.","eco":"Local taxation, charcoal, small mining sites.","assess":"Shows how the 1994 spillover became permanent Congolese politics: a Congolese community militia defined by alliance with a Rwandan exile force, three decades on."}, {"id":"foreign-forces","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts; SAMIDRC withdrawal reporting","name":"Foreign forces in the DRC","full":"State armies, missions and contractors on Congolese soil","affil":"MULTI-STATE","icon":"⚑","color":"#c74a7d","tier":"CRITICAL","strength":"At least five foreign militaries and two contractor sets","theaters":["drc"],"mil":"RWANDA (RDF): documented by successive UN Groups of Experts as providing troops, equipment and command support to M23 — denied by Kigali. BURUNDI (FDNB): deployed in support of the Congolese army, engaged directly against Twirwaneho at Minembwe. UGANDA (UPDF): joint operations against the ADF. SAMIDRC (South Africa, Malawi, Tanzania): SADC mission deployed Dec 2023, suffered fatalities at Goma including 14 South Africans, since withdrawn. MONUSCO: UN peacekeeping mission, winding down but still present. PRIVATE MILITARY COMPANIES: Romanian and other contractors employed by Kinshasa; BELGIAN mercenaries reportedly captured by Twirwaneho in June 2026.","log":"Cross-border resupply on both sides; Kigali and Bujumbura sustain opposing coalitions across the same frontier.","eco":"Rwanda's mineral exports rose in step with M23's territorial control — the UN Panel's central finding, and the platform's evidence for financing (HIGH confidence).","assess":"Eastern Congo hosts a Rwandan-backed rebellion, a Rwandan-origin exile army fighting for Kinshasa, a Ugandan-origin jihadist franchise, Burundian troops, a withdrawn SADC mission, a departing UN force, and European mercenaries on contract — all inside one country's borders. Sovereignty here is not weak; it is subdivided."}, {"id": "al-shabaab", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Monitoring Team (AQAP-al-Shabaab Gulf of Aden network, Feb 2026); ACLED attack data; ICG Horn of Africa","name": "Al-Shabaab", "full": "Harakat al-Shabaab al-Mujahideen", "affil": "al-Qaeda", "icon": "🏴", "color": "#b83d3d", "tier": "CRITICAL", "strength": "The WEALTHIEST al-Qaeda affiliate on earth — the benchmark", "theaters": ["somalia"], "mil": "Al-Qaeda's strongest and most successful affiliate. Goal: destroy the Federal Government, expel foreign forces, and build a 'Greater Somalia' uniting ethnic Somalis across East Africa under strict Islamic rule. It is exploiting the ATMIS withdrawal vacuum — retook at least 5 districts in Lower Shabelle and Middle Jubba by late 2025, with the offensive intensifying into 2026. Attack tempo rose from 68 to 116 per month (+70%); attacks on civilians surged 306%; attacks on the US-trained Danab and Gorgor special forces surged 1,764%. Attempted to assassinate the president by IED in March 2025. Controls large rural swathes of south-central Somalia plus strongholds in the northern Al-Madow mountains, and mounts cross-border raids into Kenya and Ethiopia.", "log": "Deep clan integration makes it nearly impossible to dislodge from areas where it has roots. Its intelligence network PENETRATES Somali government and security institutions — enabling targeted assassinations and insider attacks, and meaning offensives are often known in advance. Controls key routes across south-central Somalia. When peacekeepers vacate a position, it reoccupies within weeks.", "eco": "THE BENCHMARK. Al-Shabaab is the wealthiest al-Qaeda affiliate in the world — the standard against which every other group's economy is measured (JNIM ranks second). It runs a genuine taxation and extortion STATE: levies on businesses, checkpoints, ports, agricultural produce, and the telecoms/hawala money-transfer economy that Somalia runs on. It taxes both the formal and informal economy, including businesses in government-held Mogadishu — meaning the state's own commercial base pays it. This is why counterinsurgency fails: it is not funded by patrons who can be cut off, but by the economy itself.", "gvn":"THE MOST DEVELOPED NON-STATE FISCAL SYSTEM ON THIS BOARD. Runs assessed taxation with receipts, collects zakat and customs at ports and roads it does not control, and operates SHARIA COURTS that Somalis — including businesspeople in government-held Mogadishu — use because they are faster and less corrupt than the state's. It is the wealthiest al-Qaeda affiliate on earth because it built a revenue service, not because it holds ground.","assess": "Al-Shabaab is the proof-of-concept for every other group on this board: a jihadist organization that became a fiscal state, taxing the very economy the government depends on. It also delivers the platform's clearest strategic warning — when ATMIS drew down before the Somali army could replace it, 'liberated' territory fell within months. That is a lesson with direct bearing on every theater where a foreign force is holding a line."}, {"id": "jnim", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Africa Center for Strategic Studies (strength ~10,000, 2026); ICC warrant for Ag Ghali; 2026 Mali offensives reporting","name": "JNIM", "full": "Jama'at Nusrat al-Islam wal-Muslimin", "affil": "al-Qaeda", "icon": "🏴", "color": "#4a9d5f", "tier": "CRITICAL", "strength": "~6,000 fighters — largest militant force in the Sahel", "theaters": ["mali", "burkina", "niger", "benin-togo", "cote-divoire", "ghana"], "mil": "Formed 2017 from the merger of Ansar Dine, Katibat Macina, Al-Mourabitoun, Ansarul Islam, and AQIM's Sahara branch. Doubled its attack tempo in 2025 vs 2024. Burkina Faso alone recorded 280+ JNIM attacks in H1 2025. Structured in three tiers — central leadership, regional commanders, local area commanders — anchored by a Majlis al-Shura that sets strategy and approves high-value operations. Now pressing southward toward the Gulf of Guinea littoral: a new senior leader (Sekou Muslimu) was appointed to expand into Benin, Côte d'Ivoire, Ghana, Niger, and Togo, with a separate emir for Benin. Carried out its first documented attack in Nigeria (Oct 2025, Kwara State).", "log": "A 'franchise model' — local autonomy under central strategic oversight, adapting taxation and recruitment to regional conditions. Mines double as logistics hubs: recruitment grounds, weapons-storage sites, and staging points. Cross-border mobility across the Mali-Burkina-Niger tri-border and now into the coastal states. Sanctioned commanders coordinate specific functions (Sidan ag Hitta ran Western-hostage negotiations; Jafar Dicko oversaw Burkina kidnappings via Ansarul Islam).", "eco": "A war economy worth roughly $100M+/yr — the wealthiest al-Qaeda affiliate after al-Shabaab. FOUR PILLARS: (1) ARTISANAL GOLD — the most profitable sector. JNIM doesn't dig; it controls the chokepoints (road to the mine, road from it, the riverbank where ore is washed, the market where it's sold) and levies a toll at each. Sites under its influence produce up to 725kg/yr (~$34M), exported to the UAE, Turkey, and Switzerland. Over 2,000 miners work the N'Abaw goldfields in Mali. It undercuts pro-government militias on price, building legitimacy. (2) KIDNAPPING — 845 of 1,100 recorded Sahel abductions (2017-23). Local businessmen fetch $3,000-5,000; foreigners can command millions (a member of Dubai's ruling family reportedly yielded $20M+). Kidnappings spike on first entering an area — to intimidate, gather intelligence, and remove local authority figures — then shift to forced recruitment of skilled workers (doctors, nurses). (3) CATTLE RUSTLING — men on motorbikes force Fulani herders to surrender livestock; the cattle are trucked to markets in Ghana, Mali, or Senegal and blended into legitimate stock. A single cow sells for ~$346 in a country where annual GDP per capita is under $3,000. (4) MONEY LAUNDERING + ECONOMIC WARFARE — lends to merchants, underwrites shops, uses formal banking; assassinates those who interfere with its investments. Systematically attacks foreign (esp. Chinese-run) mining sites — Narena, Morila — as a racketeering campaign to extort operators and delegitimize the state. Notably, little evidence of crypto adoption, unlike ISWAP.", "gvn":"GOVERNS BY CONTRACT. Negotiates local accords with communities — security in exchange for zakat, dispute adjudication, and rules on schooling and behaviour — and enforces them by blockade rather than occupation. Strangling Malian fuel corridors is the same instrument applied at national scale: it does not need to hold Bamako to set terms for it.","assess": "JNIM is the platform's most economically sophisticated non-state actor and the clearest proof of the extraction thesis: it does not need to own the gold, only to tax every chokepoint between the ore and the market. Its southward push toward the Gulf of Guinea is the single most important trend line in West Africa — it threatens to convert the Sahel's landlocked insurgency into a coastal one with access to ports."}, {"id": "iswap", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"US-Nigeria joint operation reporting (May 2026, al-Minuki killed); EUAA conflict actor mapping; ACLED","name": "ISWAP", "full": "Islamic State West Africa Province", "affil": "Islamic State", "icon": "🏴", "color": "#c74545", "tier": "CRITICAL", "strength": "Largest IS affiliate in Africa (US State Dept)", "theaters": ["nigeria", "cameroon", "niger", "lakurawa"], "mil": "Formed March 2015 when Boko Haram's Shekau pledged allegiance to IS; split from him over indiscriminate violence, defeated his faction in 2021, and absorbed many of his fighters. Carried out 500+ attacks in Borno State between January and October 2025. Drone attacks rose from 1 (2024) to 7 (2025). Reinforced its Gargash and Sambisa bases with ~100 additional trained fighters each and elevated Sambisa to a formal IS administrative province headed by a wali — a shift toward territorial governance. Active cells in Kogi, Zamfara, Sokoto, and Katsina states, plus Cameroon's Far North, Niger's Diffa, and Chad's Lac province.", "log": "Controls many of the hundreds of islands in Lake Chad — the rear base, logistics hub, and staging ground for attacks across all four basin countries. Captures and redeploys government heavy weapons (IS pays reward bounties for equipment seized from the Nigerian military). Rising leadership weight inside global IS: after the death of IS deputy Abu Khadija (Iraq, Mar 2025), West Africa's al-Furqan office head took a more prominent role, possibly heading IS's General Directorate of Provinces — meaning West Africa is now a command center, not a periphery.", "eco": "THE SIGNATURE STATISTIC: ISWAP's taxation take is roughly $191 MILLION — against Borno State's entire 2024 revenue of $18.4 million. The jihadist group out-taxes the government it fights by more than 10 to 1. And that figure excludes revenue from elsewhere in Nigeria, kidnapping proceeds, and IS bounty payments for captured military equipment. Broader analyses put total taxation revenue well over $100M/yr, sustaining operations across Niger, Chad, Mali, and Burkina Faso — effectively making Nigeria the financial hub of the wider IS network. The model: taxation of farming, fishing, herding, and trade routes; some services provided free (water, boats, basic healthcare) to build legitimacy — but rates are arbitrary and set at leaders' discretion, 'creating opportunities for abuse.' A fisherman in ISWAP waters without proof of payment can be flogged to death; a fish dealer who refuses loses his shipment, his life, or is abducted; herders face arbitrary cattle taxes that can wipe them out. Entire communities have been massacred for non-payment. ISWAP has experimented with cryptocurrency (Tether). US sanctions have targeted its financing architecture — including Lagos and Kano bureaux de change — and a joint US-Nigerian operation killed Abu-Bilal al-Minuki, the architect of ISWAP's financing.", "gvn":"TAXES AND ADJUDICATES. Operates a levy system on fishing, farming and trade around Lake Chad, provides dispute resolution and some services, and has at points been described by residents as more predictable than the Nigerian state — which is the recruitment mechanism the 'Christian genocide' framing entirely misses.","assess": "ISWAP is the most important counter-example to the idea that jihadist groups are merely destructive: it is a functioning parallel state with a revenue base ten times its government rival's. That fiscal asymmetry — not battlefield strength — is why it cannot be dislodged. It is also the connective tissue of the Lake Chad Basin, operating across Nigeria, Cameroon, Niger, and Chad simultaneously, which is precisely why the platform now tracks Cameroon."}, {"id": "boko-haram", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA conflict actor mapping; ACLED Lake Chad data; Chad Barka Tolorom attack reporting (May 2026)","name": "Boko Haram (JAS)", "full": "Jama'atu Ahlis Sunna Lidda'awati wal-Jihad", "affil": "Independent (ex-IS)", "icon": "🏴", "color": "#8c4a9d", "tier": "SEVERE", "strength": "Diminished vs ISWAP but resurgent in capability", "theaters": ["nigeria", "cameroon", "lakurawa"], "mil": "The original Lake Chad insurgency. Lost the intra-jihadist war to ISWAP (Shekau killed himself in May 2021 during an ISWAP assault) but remains lethal and is closing the capability gap: in the March 2025 Wulgo attack on a Cameroonian base it killed 20 soldiers and SEIZED Soviet-made ZSU-23-4 Shilka radar-guided anti-aircraft guns, and it now employs explosive-laden drones. Distinguished from ISWAP by indiscriminate violence against Muslim civilians — the very issue that caused the 2016 split.", "log": "Operates from Lake Chad and Sambisa remnant areas; cross-border raids into Cameroon's Far North (Mayo-Tsanaga, Mayo-Sava, Logone-et-Chari). Captured government heavy weaponry is now a meaningful part of its arsenal, and drone acquisition marks a step-change. Cooperates opportunistically with Lakurawa in the northwest.", "eco": "Kidnap-for-ransom is the core earner — a Boko Haram-linked group netted ~$500,000 for a single judge. Nigeria's abduction economy is vast: 4,722 people taken in 997 incidents between July 2024 and June 2025, with ~$1.66M in ransoms paid and 762 victims killed. Also relies on raiding, cattle theft, and extortion of farming and fishing communities. Less sophisticated than ISWAP's taxation state — more predatory, less governmental — which is why it commands less territory but remains violent.", "gvn":"TAXES THE LAKE. Levies fishing, farming, pepper and the smuggling routes across the Lake Chad islands, with sharia adjudication in areas it holds. The Multinational Joint Task Force's collapse after Chad and Niger withdrew removed the one mechanism that pressured this revenue base across all four borders at once.","assess": "Boko Haram is the cautionary tale about 'defeated' groups: militarily eclipsed by ISWAP, yet it just seized radar-guided anti-aircraft systems and fields attack drones. Its resurgence is the clearest driver of the new Cameroon theater."}, {"id": "issp", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Africa Center for Strategic Studies; ACLED Sahel data; 2026 Mali offensives reporting","name": "ISSP / IS-Sahel", "full": "Islamic State Sahel Province", "affil": "Islamic State", "icon": "🏴", "color": "#c74545", "tier": "SEVERE", "strength": "JNIM's principal rival in the Sahel", "theaters": ["niger", "mali", "burkina", "lakurawa"], "mil": "The Islamic State's Sahel arm and JNIM's direct competitor — the two fight each other as well as the states. Concentrated in the Mali-Niger-Burkina tri-border. Increasingly linked to the northwest Nigeria corridor: the UN reports IS-Sahel fighters operating in Nigeria's rear zones and working to connect with ISWAP.", "log": "Tri-border sanctuary; rear bases now extending into northwest Nigeria via the Lakurawa corridor. The strategic objective is a contiguous IS space linking the Sahel to the Lake Chad Basin.", "eco": "Shifted toward high-value foreign abductions to raise cash — in early 2025 it conducted or sponsored the kidnapping of six foreign nationals, including an Austrian woman in Agadez and four Moroccan truck drivers in Burkina Faso. ACLED assesses the shift is 'driven by the group's need for resources, given that foreigners command higher ransom fees than local abductees.' Also taxes gold sites and trade routes in its zones, competing directly with JNIM for the same revenue base.", "gvn":"EXTRACTS WHERE IT CANNOT ADMINISTER. Taxes livestock, gold sites and transit in the Mali-Niger-Burkina tri-border, but with markedly less governance apparatus than JNIM — it competes on coercion rather than on service, which is one reason the two groups' relationship is hostile. Tillabéri's 1,200+ fatalities in 2025 reflect that competition.","assess": "ISSP matters most as the other half of a two-front jihadist competition: JNIM and ISSP fight each other for the same taxation base, which sometimes slows both — but their rivalry is also what drives expansion into new territory, including the Nigerian northwest."}, {"id": "lakurawa", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Amnesty International; ACLED Zamfara/Kebbi/Sokoto data; Nigerian security reporting (174 arrests Jan 2025)","name": "Lakurawa", "full": "Lakurawa (NW Nigeria tri-border)", "affil": "Hybrid jihadist-criminal", "icon": "🏴", "color": "#d4884a", "tier": "ELEVATED", "strength": "Tri-border hybrid; the ISSP-ISWAP conduit", "theaters": ["lakurawa", "nigeria", "niger", "benin-togo"], "mil": "Emerged ~2017-18 from a merger of Malian Macina/JNIM-linked Fulani fighters and a Nigerien herder self-defense faction — invited by Sokoto communities to fight bandits, then turned predatory. Declared a terrorist group by Nigeria (Nov 2024/Jan 2025). Hit by US airstrikes on Christmas Day 2025 (Sokoto/Bauni Forest camps). Massacred 162 civilians in Woro and Nuku, Kwara State (Feb 2026), after villagers refused to let it preach. Expanding south into Niger State's Borgu axis. Leader Amir Tajudeen moves between Nigeria, Niger, Mali, and Chad.", "log": "Sanctuary in the ungoverned Nigeria-Niger-Benin tri-border. ISS assesses it functions as a STRATEGIC CONDUIT — the link connecting the Sahel's IS/AQ franchises (ISSP, JNIM) toward Nigeria and the Gulf of Guinea. Cooperates with both Boko Haram/JAS and ISWAP. Niger's post-2023-coup tension with Nigeria blocks the joint operations needed to close the corridor.", "eco": "Governance-through-extraction across roughly 500 villages: coercive 'zakat' taxation, livestock confiscation, produce seizure, and a forced religious code, with rival vigilantes suppressed. High-value kidnapping demonstrates cross-border financial reach — the Kebbi deputy speaker was ransomed for ~$140,600, partly paid in CFA francs. The crime-terror nexus makes it self-financing and less dependent on external patrons than the larger franchises.", "assess": "Lakurawa's significance is structural, not its size: it is the seam. If the Sahel's jihadist economy and Lake Chad's taxation state ever fully connect, Lakurawa is the corridor through which it happens — which is why US airstrikes came to northwest Nigeria."}, {"id": "rsf", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"EUAA actor mapping; UK sanctions on gold and finance networks (Jul 2026); ICC deputy prosecutor statement (Jul 2026); UN genocide determination on El Fasher","name": "RSF", "full": "Rapid Support Forces", "affil": "State-backed paramilitary", "icon": "⚔", "color": "#d43d3d", "tier": "CRITICAL", "strength": "Genocide-designated; controls Darfur + besieging Kordofan", "theaters": ["sudan"], "mil": "Descended from the Janjaweed militias that killed as many as 400,000 in Darfur two decades ago. The US State Department has FORMALLY DETERMINED the RSF is committing genocide — murdering men and boys and systematically raping women and girls on ethnic grounds. El Fasher fell in October 2025 with ~6,000 killed in the first three days. The identical siege is now closing on El Obeid, where ~500,000 civilians are trapped. Fields drones supplied via its patron network.", "log": "THE UAE PIPELINE — the platform's best-documented external-supply chain. Arms flow from UAE bases to Amdjarass in Chad (officially a 'field hospital'; UN investigators found 'credible evidence' it was an arms-transfer front), then into RSF-held Sudan. Chad allows the transit in exchange for ~$2B in Emirati loans (2023-24). Alternative routes run through Libya (al-Kufra) and, increasingly, Ethiopia. In January 2026 Egypt, Saudi Arabia, and Somalia began denying the UAE overflight permission, squeezing the network and making Chad more vital.", "eco": "GOLD. Sudan is one of Africa's largest gold producers and most of its gold flows to Dubai — the UAE officially imported 39 tons in 2022, with perhaps another 60 tons smuggled via Chad, Egypt, and Ethiopia. The war has only increased the smuggling, letting the RSF (and the SAF) trade gold for weapons. This is the purest gold-for-guns economy on the platform, and it is why the UAE's patronage persists despite the genocide determination.", "gvn":"PARALLEL STATE. Declared a rival government with its own ministries and appointed officials, financed by Darfur gold moved through the UAE corridor. Taxation is extraction at checkpoints and mines rather than assessment; there is no meaningful service provision, which is why its rule generates displacement rather than consent.","assess": "The RSF is the clearest case in the platform of an armed group functioning as the delivery mechanism for someone else's extraction: it fights, and the gold flows to Dubai. Senator Sanders has now named the UAE directly as having 'financed and enabled this genocide for years' — while it remains a US 'major defense partner.'"}, {"id": "m23", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts; US sanctions on the RDF (Mar 2026); Washington Accords and Doha framework reporting","name": "M23 / AFC", "full": "March 23 Movement / Alliance Fleuve Congo", "affil": "Rwanda-backed", "icon": "⚔", "color": "#4a7dc7", "tier": "SEVERE", "strength": "Holds Goma + Bukavu; Rwanda-equipped", "theaters": ["drc"], "mil": "Rwanda-backed rebel coalition that seized Goma and Bukavu in early 2025 — the most significant territorial conquest in the eastern DRC in a generation. Under US pressure it fully withdrew from Uvira in South Kivu, but the Washington and Doha peace processes remain fragile and the African Union is trying to reassert a facilitation role.", "log": "Direct Rwandan Defence Force support (troops, equipment, command) — documented repeatedly by UN Group of Experts panels despite Kigali's denials. Supply lines run across the Rwandan border.", "eco": "COLTAN AND COBALT. The eastern DRC holds the world's critical-minerals prize, and M23's territorial control sits atop it. Minerals are smuggled through Rwanda and enter global supply chains laundered as Rwandan-origin — the mechanism by which a country with modest deposits became a significant coltan exporter. This is the extraction thesis in its most literal form: the conflict is the mechanism, the minerals are the point, and the end user is the global electronics and EV supply chain.", "gvn":"ADMINISTERS. Appoints its own governors, mayors and police in Goma, Bukavu and the territories it holds, collects taxes and customs at border posts and mining sites, and has run parallel civil administration long enough that residents deal with it rather than Kinshasa. A UN report highlights the AFC/M23 alliance's growing POLITICAL as well as military influence — it is consolidating a government, not just a front line.","assess": "M23 is the DRC theater's engine and the platform's most direct link between an armed group and the critical-minerals system. Every EV battery and smartphone touches this war somewhere upstream."}, ];
const BLOCS = [{"id":"maritime-alliance","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Saudi defence ministry announcement via CNN (30 Jul 2026)","name":"The Maritime Defense Alliance","icon":"⚓","color":"#5aa8a8","what":"Fourteen states have backed a SAUDI-PROPOSED alliance to protect international shipping routes and global trade — announced 30 July, as the Houthi blockade, Red Sea tanker strikes and Hormuz interdiction ran simultaneously. It is the first multilateral security architecture this crisis has produced.","sideA":"Saudi Arabia and the 13 states backing the proposal — principally littoral and trade-dependent economies bearing the cost of disrupted transit.","sideB":"Not a counter-bloc so much as a set of actors the alliance is designed to route around: Iran and Ansar Allah as interdictors, and the US-led coalition whose enforcement is separate and unilateral — CENTCOM has redirected 24 vessels, disabled two and boarded two under its own restrictions.","contest":"WHOSE ORDER GOVERNS THE STRAITS. Washington is enforcing shipping restrictions to pressure Iran; Riyadh is assembling a coalition to keep trade moving. Those aims overlap but are not identical, and the alliance is notably NOT American-led — a state under blockade choosing to build the multilateral response rather than shelter under an existing one.","faultline":"Whether it can act without US assets. Fourteen political backers is not fourteen navies, and the alliance has no announced command structure, rules of engagement or basing. It also has no answer to GPS spoofing, which degrades navigation for members and non-members alike with no attribution available.","theaters":["saudi","chokepoints","yemen","iran-us","uae-gulf","egypt"]}, {"id": "aes-ecowas", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ECOWAS and AES official statements; ICG West Africa reporting (2026)","name": "AES vs ECOWAS", "icon": "⬡", "color": "#d4884a", "what": "The Sahel's rupture with West Africa's regional order", "sideA": "AES (Alliance of Sahel States): Mali, Burkina Faso, Niger — military juntas, exited ECOWAS (Jan 2025), formed a mutual-defense confederation", "sideB": "ECOWAS: Nigeria, Ghana, Senegal, Côte d'Ivoire + the coastal states — plus France/EU backing", "contest": "Sovereignty and security orientation. The AES states expelled French forces, turned to Russia's Africa Corps, and rejected ECOWAS's democratic-restoration demands. ECOWAS lost its leverage — sanctions failed, and the juntas consolidated.", "faultline": "The jihadist insurgency doesn't respect the split: JNIM and ISSP operate across both blocs and are now pushing south into ECOWAS coastal states (Benin, Togo, Côte d'Ivoire, Ghana). The rupture destroyed the cross-border cooperation needed to fight them — Niger's coup broke joint operations with Nigeria, which is precisely why the Lakurawa corridor stays open. · INFORMATION WAR: the fault line is now being exploited by coordinated coup-disinformation campaigns targeting Côte d'Ivoire — the region's key French-aligned economy — fabricating a military takeover to manufacture the appearance of AES-style rupture. Ivorian authorities call it 'a deliberate and coordinated disinformation campaign'; analysts describe a broader push promoting coups across West Africa. Attribution unresolved.", "theaters": ["mali", "burkina", "niger", "benin-togo", "lakurawa", "nigeria", "ghana", "cote-divoire"]}, {"id": "gulf-rivalry", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ICG Gulf States analysis; Chatham House MENA programme (2026)","name": "The Gulf Rivalry", "icon": "⬡", "color": "#c74545", "what": "UAE vs Qatar/Saudi/Turkey/Egypt — fought through African proxies", "sideA": "UAE: backs the RSF (Sudan), Abiy's Ethiopia, Haftar's Libya; buys the gold", "sideB": "Saudi Arabia, Egypt, Turkey, Qatar, Iran: back Sudan's SAF; Egypt aligns with Eritrea vs Ethiopia", "contest": "Red Sea and Horn primacy, ports, gold, and agricultural land. The UAE has become the single most important foreign power keeping the RSF in the fight — arms via Amdjarass (Chad) and Libya — while Egypt, Saudi Arabia, and Turkey back the army. Egypt's rivalry with Ethiopia over the GERD dam pulls the Nile into the same contest.", "faultline": "Sudan is the battlefield, but the fault line runs through Chad (which takes ~$2B in UAE loans to permit the arms transit), Libya (the al-Kufra route), and Ethiopia (a UAE-financed RSF training camp near the GERD, per Reuters). In January 2026, Egypt, Saudi Arabia, and Somalia began denying the UAE overflight rights — an escalation of the proxy war into airspace denial. Somalia CANCELLED all bilateral UAE agreements (Jan 2026) over the RSF pipeline — while the UAE simultaneously patronizes Puntland with airstrikes. The same Red Sea port strategy drives both.", "theaters": ["sudan", "ethiopia", "chokepoints", "iran-us", "somalia"]}, {"id": "russia-africa", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Panel of Experts (CAR, Mali, Libya); The Sentry investigations (2026)","name": "Russia's Africa Corps", "icon": "⬡", "color": "#8c4a9d", "what": "The post-Wagner state takeover of Russian influence in Africa", "sideA": "Russia (Africa Corps, MoD-controlled): Mali, Burkina, Niger, CAR, Libya", "sideB": "France (expelled), the EU, the US — the displaced Western security architecture", "contest": "Regime survival services in exchange for mining concessions and diplomatic alignment. Russia offers what the West won't: no governance conditions. In return it takes gold and mineral rights and gains UN votes.", "faultline": "The security results are poor — jihadist violence rose after Russian deployment in Mali and Burkina — but the political bargain holds because it's about regime survival, not counter-terrorism. Meanwhile Russia's DPRK alliance (troops and shells for the Ukraine war) shows the same logic operating globally: patrons who ask no questions.", "theaters": ["mali", "burkina", "niger", "ukraine", "korea"]}, {"id": "minerals-cold-war", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"UN Group of Experts on the DRC; EU critical raw materials MoU reporting (2026)","name": "The Minerals Cold War", "icon": "⬡", "color": "#4a9d5f", "what": "China's processing monopoly vs the Western de-risking bloc", "sideA": "China: ~90% of rare-earth processing, dominant in DRC cobalt (CMOC), Indonesian nickel JVs, Kazakh reactors (CNNC); buys Myanmar's heavy-REE feedstock", "sideB": "US/EU/G7 ('Pax Silica'): IRA/CRMA de-risking, the DRC EGC/Trafigura deal, Kazakh/Ukrainian minerals agreements, Middle Corridor investment", "contest": "Not the mines — the PROCESSING and the chokepoints. China's leverage is that it refines what everyone else digs. The Western counter is to build parallel supply chains, but it has forfeited key ground: the US 'wasn't even in the room' for Kazakhstan's reactor bids, and Indonesia's export ban handed downstream capacity largely to Chinese firms.", "faultline": "Every conflict theater with a mineral is a front: Myanmar (KIA controls ~94% of China's heavy-REE feedstock), DRC (M23 sits on the coltan), Indonesia (nickel), Kazakhstan (uranium), Ukraine (the minerals deal). The Caucasus TRIPP corridor is the connectivity front — a US-controlled route bypassing Russia and Iran.", "theaters": ["myanmar", "drc", "indonesia", "kazakhstan", "taiwan", "caucasus", "ukraine", "geopolitical"]}, {"id": "donroe", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"US policy statements and regional reporting; PBS/CFR coverage of hemispheric doctrine (2026)","name": "The Donroe Doctrine", "icon": "⬡", "color": "#e0a030", "what": "US hemispheric reassertion — Venezuela, Cuba, Colombia, Haiti", "sideA": "US: captured Maduro (Jan 2026), administers Venezuelan oil, sanctions Cuba, architected Haiti's GSF, aligns with Colombia's incoming Espriella government", "sideB": "China, Russia, Cuba, Nicaragua — plus Petro's outgoing Colombia; China/Russia/Pakistan abstained on the Haiti mandate", "contest": "Hemispheric primacy and resources — Venezuelan oil now flows through an opaque Qatari account under US administration; Cuba is being strangled into 100+ market reforms.", "faultline": "The tell is Haiti: the US architected and financed the Gang Suppression Force but kept it AFRICAN-LED by design — Chad (1,500), Benin (up to 2,000), Bangladesh, Mongolia — stabilizing the hemisphere without US boots. The same Chad simultaneously hosts the UAE's RSF arms pipeline. Colombia's incoming government will close its Havana and Managua embassies, tightening the ring.", "theaters": ["venezuela", "cuba", "colombia", "haiti", "mexico", "jamaica"]}, {"id": "sanctions-bloc", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"OFAC and EU sanctions listings; UK Sudan gold and finance designations (Jul 2026); EU Parliament RSF resolution","name": "The Sanctions Bloc", "icon": "⬡", "color": "#5b9bd5", "what": "Iran-Russia-DPRK-Venezuela-Cuba: the mutual-survival network", "sideA": "Iran, Russia, North Korea, Venezuela, Cuba — sanctioned states trading with each other", "sideB": "The US-led sanctions architecture + the G7 financial system", "contest": "Survival outside the dollar system. Russia supplies DPRK with tech/energy/food for troops and shells; Iran supplied Russia with drones; DPRK's tungsten exports to China rose 13-fold; Venezuela's oil moves through opaque channels.", "faultline": "The Hormuz crisis is the sharpest edge — Iran is enforcing a transit regime (IRGC authorization, 'maritime service fees') that would be a permanent revenue stream and a permanent lever. New Supreme Leader Mojtaba Khamenei has enshrined 'the lever of closing the Strait of Hormuz' as strategic doctrine. Sanctions-evasion gold (Russia, Sudan) and DPRK coal move through the same shadow networks.", "theaters": ["iran-us", "ukraine", "korea", "venezuela", "cuba", "chokepoints", "sudan"]}, {"id": "indo-pacific", "prov":"VERIFIED","src":"Joint Sea-2026 and ADIZ incursion reporting (Jul 2026); Asia Society regional opinion polling; IISS Military Balance","name": "First Island Chain", "icon": "⬡", "color": "#4a7dc7", "what": "US alliance system vs Chinese expansion in the Western Pacific", "sideA": "US, Japan, ROK, Philippines, Australia, Taiwan — treaty alliances + AUKUS", "sideB": "China (+ Russia and DPRK as the northern anchor)", "contest": "Taiwan (~90% of advanced chips) and the South China Sea ($3.4T trade artery). China's gray-zone tactics — coast guards, water cannon, ramming — are calibrated to stay below the US Mutual Defense Treaty tripwire.", "faultline": "The alliance is showing strain: the US curtailed intelligence sharing with Seoul after a lawmaker exposed the Kusong enrichment site, and the Russia-DPRK war alliance (troops for tech) has converted a frozen Korean standoff into a live node of the Ukraine war. Myanmar's rare earths flow into the same Chinese supply chain that the Taiwan contest is ultimately about.", "theaters": ["taiwan", "south-china-sea", "korea", "myanmar", "indonesia"]},{"id":"saudi-axis","prov":"VERIFIED","src":"ICG Gulf States analysis; Houthi blockade and Red Sea tanker strike reporting (Jul 2026)","name":"The Saudi-Led Axis","icon":"☪","color":"#4cc98a","what":"Saudi Arabia + Qatar + Turkey + Pakistan + Egypt — a post-Iran-war security bloc meeting at quarterly FM level (Cairo, Jun 21 2026), dubbed an 'Islamic NATO' in Israeli reports. Pakistan's nuclear arsenal underpins it via the Saudi-Pakistani defense framework; Turkey brings NATO's second army and the KAAN fighter (Saudi SAMI co-invested); Qatar brings money and mediation; Egypt brings 438k troops.","contest":"Twin objectives that don't fully cohere: contain IRAN (and reclaim influence in Syria/Lebanon from its proxies) while also capping ISRAEL's 'military adventurism' — the Doha strike convinced Gulf capitals that 'we could be next.' Members split on whether to normalize with Israel or brace against it.","faultline":"The UAE is conspicuously EXCLUDED — Riyadh-Abu Dhabi mistrust now has institutional form, with both competing for the same post-oil investment. The deeper premise (Chatham House, May 2026): Gulf states 'should no longer rely on America for security guarantees' — and China won't fill the vacancy. The axis is what hedging looks like when the umbrella wobbles.","theaters":["saudi","turkey","pak-afg","egypt","uae-gulf","iran-us","syria","lebanon"]}];
