f GDP over two and a half years \u2014 and roughly two-thirds of it is deficit-financed</b>. Rearmament is not paid for out of growth. It is borrowed, which means today\u2019s military build-out is tomorrow\u2019s debt-service line.</div></div></div><div class="dx-grid"><div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">READING THIS BOARD — TIC, CHOKEHOLDS, AND WHAT THE RATINGS MEAN</div><div class="dx-d"><b>TIC</b> — Treasury International Capital, the US Treasury’s monthly series on foreign holdings of American securities. It is the standard source for “who holds US debt”, and it has one distortion worth knowing: <b>holdings are booked where the custodian sits, not where the owner does.</b> That is why Belgium, Luxembourg and the UK appear far larger than their economies suggest — they host the custody banks. Treat country lines as approximate.</div><div class="dx-d"><b>CHOKEHOLD vs DEPENDENCE — direction is the whole point.</b> A chokehold is something you control that someone else needs. A dependence is something you need that someone else controls. The same trade relationship is both, read from opposite ends, and this board labels which end each entry describes.</div><div class="dx-d"><b>HIGH · MODERATE · LOW</b> apply the same three tests as the Leverage board: can the target substitute, who bleeds first, and how fast does it bite. A lever rates HIGH only when the target has no near-term alternative <i>and</i> the holder can absorb the cost of using it.</div><div class="dx-d"><b>IDX</b> is the theater’s escalation index (0–100) carried through from its file, so economic leverage can be read against how hot the conflict actually is. The derivation for each number is on the theater card.</div><div class="dx-d"><b>The unpulled counter-lever</b> appears throughout this board. Economic leverage that exists and is not being exercised is recorded deliberately — a map showing only leverage in use would imply every actor is already doing what it can, which is exactly the assumption