function openHuman(){
  const w=DB.filter(function(d){return d.human;});
  var tD=0,tX=0;
  w.forEach(function(d){tD+=(d.human.dead||0);tX+=(d.human.disp||0);});
  const fmt=function(n){return n>=1e6?(n/1e6).toFixed(1)+'M':n>=1e3?Math.round(n/1e3)+'k':''+n;};
  const rows=w.sort(function(a,b){return (b.human.dead||0)-(a.human.dead||0);}).map(function(d){
    const h=d.human;
    return '<div class="hm-row" onclick="closeOverlay();pick(\''+d.id+'\')">'
      +'<div class="hm-th">'+d.flag+' '+d.name.split('\u2014')[0].trim()+'</div>'
      +'<div class="hm-nums"><span class="hm-d">\u2020 '+fmt(h.dead||0)+''+(h.est?'<span class="hm-est">~est</span>':'')+'</span><span class="hm-x">\u2924 '+fmt(h.disp||0)+'</span>'+(h.famine?'<span class="hm-f">\u26a0 FAMINE</span>':'')+(h.aid?'<span class="hm-a">AID: '+h.aid+'</span>':'')+'</div>'
      +'<div class="hm-basis">'+(h.basis||'')+'</div><div class="hm-note">'+h.note+'</div></div>';
  }).join('');
  board('\u2695 HUMAN COST LEDGER','',
    '<div class="intro-note" style="margin-bottom:14px">The price in one view: deaths and displacement per tracked conflict, famine and aid-access flags where they apply. Figures are the file\u2019s working numbers \u2014 scope noted per theater (a war\u2019s round vs its total), sourced in each theater\u2019s record. <b>Board totals: \u2020 '+fmt(tD)+' dead \u00b7 \u2924 '+fmt(tX)+' displaced</b> across '+w.length+' theaters. The extraction lens\u2019s bottom line is paid here.</div>'
    +'<div class="dx-grid">'
    +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">WHAT \u201cDEAD\u201d ACTUALLY COUNTS \u2014 READ THIS BEFORE COMPARING NUMBERS</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>These figures are not measuring the same thing, and comparing them directly is the most common error made with this kind of table.</b></div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>DIRECT DEATHS</b> \u2014 people killed by violence: shot, bombed, executed. This is what most conflict counts report, and what organisations like ACLED compile from incident reporting.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>EXCESS DEATHS</b> \u2014 everyone who died who would not have died without the war, including from hunger, untreated illness, collapsed maternity care and cholera. Derived from mortality surveys and modelling, not from counting bodies. <b>The DRC\u2019s ~6 million is this kind of number</b>, covering the eastern wars since 1996 \u2014 it is not six million killings, and reading it that way misunderstands both the figure and the war. Most of that toll is disease and starvation caused by displacement.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>Why the platform keeps both:</b> excess mortality is the honest measure of what a war costs a society, and direct counts are the honest measure of what armed actors did. Dropping either one distorts a different thing. Each row here is labelled in its own theater record with which basis applies and what period it covers \u2014 a war\u2019s current round or its cumulative total.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>Almost every number here is an undercount.</b> In active war zones the reporting infrastructure is the first casualty. Where a figure is modelled rather than counted this board marks it <b>~est</b>, and the theater record states the basis.</div>'
    +'</div>'
    +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">FAMINE IS A TECHNICAL THRESHOLD, NOT AN ADJECTIVE</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d">Food crises are classified on the <b>IPC</b> scale (Integrated Food Security Phase Classification), a five-step framework used by the UN and major agencies. <b>Phase 1</b> minimal \u00b7 <b>Phase 2</b> stressed \u00b7 <b>Phase 3</b> crisis \u00b7 <b>Phase 4</b> emergency \u00b7 <b>Phase 5</b> catastrophe/famine.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>Declaring famine requires all three thresholds at once</b> in the same area: at least 20% of households facing extreme food shortage, at least 30% of children acutely malnourished, and at least two deaths per 10,000 people per day from starvation or hunger-related disease.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>This is why \u201cno famine declared\u201d means much less than it sounds.</b> Phase 4 is emergency \u2014 people are dying of hunger \u2014 and a declaration also requires data collectors to have access, which besieging forces routinely deny. <b>The absence of a famine declaration is frequently evidence of blocked access rather than of adequate food.</b> Where this board flags famine it says which authority declared it and for which area.</div>'
    +'</div>'
    +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">DISPLACEMENT AND AID ACCESS</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>IDP</b> (internally displaced person) \u2014 forced from home but still inside their own country, with no international legal protection regime and usually dependent on the government that failed to protect them. <b>REFUGEE</b> \u2014 crossed a border, and acquires protections under the 1951 Convention. The distinction is legal, not humanitarian: the same family changes category by crossing a line.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d">Displacement figures here are typically <b>cumulative and double-counted across rounds</b> \u2014 a household displaced four times may appear four times in the underlying data. Read them as a measure of disruption, not as a headcount of distinct people.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>AID ACCESS flags</b> mark where humanitarian operations are obstructed \u2014 by siege, bureaucratic denial of visas and permits, attacks on convoys, or the security situation. It is the variable that converts a food crisis into a famine, which is why this board tracks it beside the casualty numbers rather than separately.</div>'
    +'</div>'
    +'</div>'+rows);
}
