     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>QUOTA</b> \u2014 each member\u2019s capital subscription, which sets its voting power AND its normal borrowing ceiling. <b>Access as a % of quota is the dependency measure</b>: 100% is a standard drawing, 391% (Benin) means the Fund has waived its own limits. High multiples signal a state that cannot fund itself any other way.</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>OUTSTANDING vs COMMITTED</b> \u2014 outstanding is money actually drawn and still owed. Committed is the headline programme size, much of which may never be disbursed if reviews fail. This board labels which is which.</div>'
   +'</div>'
   +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">THE INSTRUMENTS</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>ECF</b> Extended Credit Facility \u2014 concessional, zero-interest, for low-income states. <b>EFF</b> Extended Fund Facility \u2014 market-rate, longer structural reform. <b>SBA</b> Stand-By \u2014 short-term crisis. <b>RCF/RFI</b> \u2014 rapid emergency, minimal conditions.</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>RSF</b> Resilience and Sustainability Facility \u2014 the climate window, opened 2022. Longer maturities, but <b>only available to states already in another IMF programme</b>: climate money gated behind conditionality.</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>PCI</b> Policy Coordination Instrument \u2014 surveillance and conditions with NO money attached. <b>FCL</b> Flexible Credit Line \u2014 a pre-approved credit line for states judged strong enough to need no conditions at all. Morocco holds the only African FCL; Rwanda holds a PCI. The two ends of the same ladder.</div>'
   +'</div>'
   +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">THE RISK LABELS</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d">Distress classes come from the joint IMF-World Bank <b>Debt Sustainability Analysis</b>, which rates each low-income country <b>LOW \u00b7 MODERATE \u00b7 HIGH \u00b7 IN DEBT DISTRESS</b>, and separately judges the debt <b>sustainable</b> or <b>unsustainable</b>.</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>The label is a forecast, not a fact.</b> It rests on growth and export projections made by the same institutions that lend. Ethiopia is rated unsustainable while its programme continues; Kenya is high-risk with no programme at all. Where the platform disagrees with a rating it says so rather than deferring.</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>MARKET</b> on this board means the state borrows commercially rather than from the Fund \u2014 not that it is safe.</div>'
   +'</div>'
   +'<div class="dx-box dx-warn"><div class="dx-t">WHERE THIS DATA IS WEAK</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>Chinese figures are commitments, not debt.</b> The CLA Database tracks signed loans, not disbursements or repayments \u2014 totals overstate what is actually owed. Most \u2018debt trap\u2019 arguments rest on this conflation.</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>US Treasury holdings are custody data.</b> Belgium\u2019s position is Euroclear; Luxembourg, Cayman and Ireland are fund domiciles, not sovereign wealth. Chinese holdings held through Brussels do not appear on China\u2019s line \u2014 the real position is higher than the table shows.</div>'
     +'<div class="dx-d"><b>DSA classes are dated.</b> The published list runs to Sep 2025; some states have moved since. <b>Hidden debt is real</b> \u2014 Mozambique 2016 and Senegal 2024 were both discovered only after a change of government, which means any figure here could be understated by the same mechanism.</div>'
   +'</div>'
    +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">IMF vs WORLD BANK \u2014 THE DIFFERENCE PEOPLE GET WRONG</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d">They are separate institutions with separate jobs, created together at Bretton Woods in 1944 and permanently confused with each other since.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>THE IMF lends you money to stay solvent.</b> It is the emergency lender of last resort for governments that cannot pay their foreign bills \u2014 import invoices, debt service, currency defence. The money goes to the treasury or central bank, arrives in tranches, and each tranche is released only if the previous conditions were met. That release schedule IS the leverage: the Fund does not enforce policy, it withholds the next payment.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>THE WORLD BANK lends you money to build things.</b> Roads, power, health systems, irrigation \u2014 long-horizon project and programme finance. It splits into two windows. <b>IDA</b> serves the poorest states: grants and near-zero-interest credits over 30-40 years. <b>IBRD</b> serves middle-income states at close to market rates. Which window a country qualifies for is set by income per head, so <b>graduating out of IDA is a promotion that makes borrowing more expensive.</b></div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>Why they act as one anyway:</b> the Bank generally will not lend into a macroeconomic mess, so its money often waits on an IMF programme being in place. In practice the Fund sets the terms and the Bank funds the projects inside them \u2014 which is why borrowers experience two institutions as a single condition.</div>'
    +'</div>'
    +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">US TREASURY DEBT \u2014 AND THE ASYMMETRY THIS BOARD EXISTS TO SHOW</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>What a Treasury security actually is:</b> an IOU from the US government. <b>Bills</b> mature in under a year, <b>notes</b> in two to ten, <b>bonds</b> in twenty to thirty. You buy it at auction, collect interest, get the face value back at maturity. Foreign central banks hold them as reserves because they are the deepest and most liquid instrument in existence.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>The US borrows in the currency it issues.</b> That single fact removes the failure mode every state on this board faces. A Ghanaian or Zambian government owes dollars it cannot print, so a currency slide raises the real cost of debt it already contracted. Washington owes dollars it can create. Its constraint is inflation and politics, not solvency.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>Nobody supervises the borrower.</b> There is no programme, no review mission, no tranche schedule, no conditions on US spending. Foreign holders are <b>buyers, not supervisors</b> \u2014 they can sell, which moves the price, but they cannot demand a wage freeze or a subsidy cut. The often-repeated idea that a large holder could coerce Washington by dumping bonds mostly describes a loss the seller would absorb first.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>THE POINT OF PUTTING THESE SIDE BY SIDE:</b> the same architecture that lends the United States trillions on no conditions attaches public-sector hiring freezes and subsidy removal to a few hundred million dollars for a low-income state. The difference is not moral and it is not really about creditworthiness \u2014 it is about who issues the currency the debt is written in. That is the structural fact, and it is why this board opens with US holdings rather than African ones.</div>'
    +'</div>'
    +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">THE MECHANICS \u2014 WHAT THE NUMBERS MEAN</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>COUPON</b> the fixed interest a bond pays, set when it is issued and unchanging. <b>YIELD</b> what a buyer actually earns at today\u2019s price \u2014 it moves <i>opposite</i> to price. When you read that a country\u2019s yields spiked, it means the price of its existing debt fell because buyers now demand more compensation to hold it.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>SPREAD</b> the gap between a country\u2019s yield and the US Treasury yield at the same maturity, quoted in <b>basis points</b> (100bp = 1%). This is the market\u2019s price on that government\u2019s risk, measured against the borrower it treats as risk-free. A spread above roughly 1,000bp is generally read as shut out of markets \u2014 the state can no longer refinance and must go to the Fund.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>DEFAULT</b> missing a scheduled payment. <b>DEBT DISTRESS</b> the broader condition of being unable to service debt without relief \u2014 a state can be in distress for years without defaulting, by cutting everything else instead. That is what the four-in-five statistic below is describing.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>HAIRCUT</b> how much face value creditors give up. <b>NPV REDUCTION</b> the honest measure of relief \u2014 whether the present value of what you owe actually fell. <b>A \u201clight\u201d restructuring reschedules payments without reducing them:</b> the headline says relief, the borrower still owes the same money later, and distress recurs. Watch for NPV, not for the announcement.</div>'
    +'</div>'
    +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">THE RESTRUCTURING MACHINERY \u2014 AND WHY IT JAMS</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>PARIS CLUB</b> an informal group of about 22 mostly-Western government creditors, meeting since 1956 to coordinate bilateral relief. For fifty years it was where sovereign debt got restructured. <b>It no longer holds the majority of the claims.</b></div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>CHINA</b> has lent sub-Saharan Africa more than <b>\u0024800 billion over two decades</b>, eclipsing the Paris Club, and is not a member of it. Its participation in relief is selective and case-by-case, which is the single largest reason coordinated restructuring stalls.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>G20 COMMON FRAMEWORK</b> created in 2020 precisely to bring China and other non-Paris creditors into one table. Widely criticised as slow and shallow \u2014 years per case, against a borrower that needs financing now.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>COMPARABILITY OF TREATMENT</b> the rule that no creditor class should do better than another. <b>THE SEQUENCING FLAW:</b> official creditors negotiate first and privately, and private creditors are shown nothing until the terms are confirmed. World Bank analysis finds this makes shallow restructurings more likely \u2014 official creditors will not commit to deep relief without assurance the private side will match, so everyone gives less and the country returns to distress.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>PRIVATE CREDITORS</b> \u2014 Eurobond holders, banks, commodity traders \u2014 now hold a rising share, up roughly 15% between 2010 and 2021, faster than in any other developing region. More creditor types means more parties who must all agree.</div>'
    +'</div>'
    +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">RESOURCE-BACKED LOANS \u2014 THE INSTRUMENT THIS PLATFORM TRACKS EVERYWHERE</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d">A loan secured against future commodity output \u2014 oil, cobalt, cocoa, gold \u2014 rather than against the government\u2019s general credit. Repayment is taken in cargoes, or from an escrow account the exports flow through.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>Why it changes everything:</b> the money comes off the top, before the budget ever sees it. A conventional loan competes with schools and clinics inside a finance ministry\u2019s allocation decisions. A resource-backed loan is paid before that decision is made. The state keeps the sovereignty and loses the revenue.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>It is also frequently invisible.</b> These deals sit outside standard debt reporting, which is how <b>hidden debt</b> happens \u2014 Mozambique 2016 and Senegal 2024 were both discovered only after a change of government. Congo-Brazzaville\u2019s undisclosed Chinese borrowing stalled its own IMF talks. Any total on this board may be understated by exactly this mechanism.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>The same structure appears on the security files:</b> Africa Corps paid in mining concessions in Mali and CAR is a resource-backed loan with soldiers instead of cash. The platform treats them as one instrument because the borrower\u2019s position is identical \u2014 the asset is pledged before the revenue arrives.</div>'
    +'</div>'
    +'<div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">2026 \u2014 WHAT THE BORROWERS ARE NOW DOING ABOUT IT</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>The defining statistic:</b> four out of five African governments now spend more on debt service than on health or on education. In 2025 nearly <b>one in every five dollars of public revenue</b> went to debt payments. African external debt passed <b>\u00241 trillion in 2024</b>, up from around \u0024500 billion in 2020 \u2014 roughly doubling in four years.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>The framing has shifted from access to terms.</b> African finance ministers arrived at the 2026 IMF-World Bank Spring Meetings, in African Business\u2019s phrase, <i>not as supplicants but as negotiators</i> \u2014 arguing the issue is no longer whether finance is available but on what conditions it is offered.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>Three collective vehicles now exist where none did:</b> the <b>Lom\u00e9 Declaration</b> from the AU\u2019s Pan-African Debt Conference (May 2025); the <b>Common African Position on Debt</b> adopted at the AU Heads of State Summit in February 2026; and UNCTAD\u2019s <b>Borrowers\u2019 Platform</b>, launched at the 2026 Spring Meetings to coordinate indebted states the way creditors have always coordinated. Whether they change any term is untested \u2014 but the asymmetry they target is the one this board documents.</div>'
      +'<div class="dx-d"><b>Read the counter-case too.</b> Zambia defaulted in 2020 and has since completed a restructuring; Kenya cut deficits and stabilised inflation without a programme; C\u00f4te d\u2019Ivoire has held infrastructure-led growth. Supporters of conditional lending argue the requirements protect borrower and lender alike. Critics point to Ghana\u2019s post-2022 public-sector hiring freeze and wage controls, and to contested findings linking 1980s-90s structural adjustment to worse health outcomes. This board holds both: the conditions are real, and so are the cases where they worked.</div>'
