const FRAMEWORKS = [{"id":"ladder","name":"Escalation Ladder","who":"Herman Kahn (RAND, 1965)","short":"Conflict as discrete, climbable rungs from crisis to total war.","def":"Kahn modelled escalation as a ladder of distinct, recognisable steps — 44 rungs in his original — from ostensible crisis through 'don't-rock-the-boat' signalling, to nuclear thresholds. Each rung is a deliberate choice, and both sides can read where they are, which makes the ladder a communication device as much as a warning.","predicts":"That escalation proceeds in identifiable, often reversible steps rather than one plunge; that thresholds (especially the first use of a new weapon class or the first strike on a new target type) matter more than incremental intensity; and that actors signal resolve by visibly climbing.","limits":"Assumes rational, legible, symmetrical actors who share the same ladder. Fails where one side does not recognise the rungs, where escalation is driven by autonomous sub-state actors, or where the 'ladder' is actually a slope with no stable steps. Cold-War-shaped: less suited to civil wars and proxy conflicts.","fits":["iran-us","korea","taiwan","nato-flank","kashmir","china"]},{"id":"conflict-curve","name":"Conflict Curve / Phase Model","who":"Michael Lund; adopted by UN, ICG, SIPRI","short":"Conflict as a life-cycle: latent → emergence → escalation → stalemate → de-escalation → resolution.","def":"The curve maps a conflict's intensity over time through recognised phases. Prevention targets the pre-escalation phases; peacebuilding targets the descent. The model's value is diagnostic — it forces the question 'what phase is this in?' before asking what to do about it.","predicts":"That interventions must match the phase — mediation works in stalemate, not at the peak of escalation; that conflicts can re-enter escalation from a stalemate rather than descending; and that 'post-conflict' is a phase with its own relapse risk, not an end state.","limits":"Tidy curves rarely match messy reality — many conflicts oscillate or run multiple phases at once in different regions. Risks implying a natural progression toward resolution that is not guaranteed. Says what phase, not why it moves.","fits":["south-sudan","drc","ethiopia","myanmar","colombia","syria","india","west-bank"]},{"id":"security-dilemma","name":"Security Dilemma / Spiral Model","who":"Herbert Butterfield; John Herz; Robert Jervis (1978)","short":"Defensive moves by one side are read as offensive by the other, spiralling both toward war neither wanted.","def":"When one actor arms or manoeuvres for its own security, a rival cannot be certain the intent is defensive, and responds in kind — leaving both less secure. Jervis distinguished the spiral model (escalation from fear) from the deterrence model (escalation from unmet aggression); mistaking one for the other prescribes exactly the wrong policy.","predicts":"That escalation can occur between actors with no aggressive intent; that reassurance, not just deterrence, can de-escalate; and that offence-defence balance and the distinguishability of weapons determine how vicious the spiral gets.","limits":"Can excuse genuine aggression by reframing it as fear. Hard to apply where one actor really is a revisionist. Requires reading intent, which is exactly what actors cannot verify — the same problem it describes.","fits":["nato-flank","kashmir","korea","taiwan","rwanda","ethiopia","china","israel"]},{"id":"ripeness","name":"Ripeness Theory","who":"I. William Zartman (1985, 2000)","short":"Conflicts become resolvable only at a 'mutually hurting stalemate' — when both sides see no way to win and the status quo hurts.","def":"Zartman argued negotiation succeeds not when parties are exhausted but when they perceive a mutually hurting stalemate coupled with a way out — a sense that escalation is futile and a settlement is imaginable. Ripeness is about perception, not objective conditions: a stalemate that both sides feel is more decisive than one an analyst measures.","predicts":"That mediation fails until the moment is ripe; that a 'way out' must be visible alongside the pain; and that outside powers can sometimes manufacture ripeness by making the stalemate hurt more (sanctions, arms cutoffs) or the exit clearer.","limits":"Nearly tautological — ripeness is often identified only after a settlement succeeds. Cannot reliably predict when a stalemate will be felt as hurting. Some conflicts stay unripe for decades while civilians pay.","fits":["ukraine","gaza","lebanon","chokepoints","colombia","israel","india","west-bank"]},{"id":"proxy","name":"Proxy War Dynamics","who":"Karl Deutsch; contemporary work by Idean Salehyan, Andrew Mumford","short":"External sponsors fight through local forces — lowering their own costs but losing control of the escalation.","def":"A proxy relationship lets a patron pursue aims at arm's length, but the principal-agent problem is severe: the local force has its own aims, and the sponsor cannot fully control when it escalates. Escalation risk comes from the agent dragging the principal, or from two patrons colliding through their clients.","predicts":"That proxy conflicts escalate through the agent's incentives, not just the patron's; that they are hard to end because multiple sponsors must align; and that 'controlled' escalation frequently isn't.","limits":"The patron-proxy line is often blurry — many 'proxies' are autonomous actors with sponsors, not instruments. Overstates external agency and can erase local drivers entirely.","fits":["drc","sudan","yemen","syria","lebanon","ukraine","haiti","israel"]},{"id":"resource-conflict","name":"Resource / Political-Economy of War","who":"Paul Collier & Anke Hoeffler; David Keen; Philippe Le Billon","short":"War as an economic system — sustained by who profits from it, not only by grievance.","def":"This literature shifts the question from 'why do people rebel?' to 'what makes war viable and profitable?' Lootable resources (alluvial diamonds, artisanal gold, cobalt) finance and prolong conflict; some actors have a positive interest in continued war. Keen's 'economic functions of violence' names war as an alternative economic order, not a breakdown of one.","predicts":"That conflicts over lootable, decentralised resources last longer; that peace threatens the war-economy's beneficiaries, who spoil it; and that cutting the revenue (not just the fighting) is the lever.","limits":"Can reduce genuine political and identity grievances to greed. The 'greed vs grievance' framing is now seen as a false binary — most wars are both. Correlation of resources and war length is contested.","fits":["drc","sudan","car","mali","libya","mozambique","myanmar","india","west-bank"]},{"id":"ethnic-security","name":"Ethnic Security Dilemma / Fear Under Anarchy","who":"Barry Posen (1993); Chaim Kaufmann","short":"When a state collapses, ethnic groups arm for safety — and the act of arming triggers the war each feared.","def":"Posen applied the security dilemma to collapsing multi-ethnic states: as central authority fails, groups face emerging anarchy and must provide their own security, but mobilisation looks offensive to neighbours. Kaufmann added that once ethnic war begins, populations hard-en into mutually threatening blocs, making the war hard to end without separation.","predicts":"That state collapse or contested succession is the trigger, not ancient hatred; that first-mover advantages create pressure to strike pre-emptively; and that intermixed populations plus offensive advantage is the most dangerous combination.","limits":"Can naturalise ethnic mobilisation as inevitable when it is usually engineered by elites. Kaufmann's separation prescription is ethically and empirically contested. Understates the role of deliberate provocation.","fits":["south-sudan","ethiopia","drc","syria","lebanon","burundi","rwanda","west-bank"]},{"id":"deterrence","name":"Deterrence Theory","who":"Thomas Schelling (1960, 1966); Glenn Snyder","short":"Escalation prevented by credibly threatening costs that outweigh any gain — the whole edifice rests on credibility.","def":"Schelling reframed strategy as bargaining: deterrence works by manipulating a rival's expectations of cost, and credibility — not capability alone — is the currency. The 'threat that leaves something to chance' and the deliberate ceding of control (a tripwire, a commitment) can strengthen a deterrent by making escalation appear automatic.","predicts":"That escalation occurs when deterrence loses credibility, when commitments are ambiguous, or when a challenger believes the defender will not follow through; and that clear red lines can prevent war — or cause it, if crossed and unenforced.","limits":"Assumes a rational cost-calculating adversary. Fails against actors who value the stake more than the cost, or who cannot be communicated with. Red-line credibility, once spent, is hard to rebuild — and every unenforced line teaches the next challenger.","fits":["iran-us","korea","taiwan","nato-flank","chokepoints","china","israel"]},{"id":"thucydides","name":"Thucydides Trap","who":"Graham Allison (2012, 2017); after Thucydides, 431 BC","short":"When a rising power threatens to displace a ruling one, the structural stress makes war dangerously likely — though not inevitable.","def":"Allison drew from Thucydides' account of Athens' rise alarming Sparta into the Peloponnesian War, arguing that a rapid shift in the balance of power between a rising and an established state generates structural stress that repeatedly ends in war. His Harvard study found war in 12 of 16 historical cases. The mechanism is not any single decision but the interaction of the riser's growing entitlement and the ruler's fear — which turns ordinary frictions (a third-party crisis, an ally's provocation) into triggers neither side fully controls.","predicts":"That US-China friction is driven by structure, not just personalities or policies; that flashpoints like Taiwan are dangerous less for their own stakes than for what a clash there signals about the hierarchy; and that avoiding war requires deliberate statecraft against the structural current, not a natural equilibrium.","limits":"Allison himself stresses the trap is escapable — 4 of 16 cases avoided war — so 'inevitability' overstates it. Critics note the 16-case set is selectively assembled, that nuclear deterrence changes the calculus fundamentally, and that deep economic interdependence between the US and China has no historical analogue. The framing can also become self-fulfilling if leaders act as though war is fated.","fits":["taiwan","korea","south-china-sea","chokepoints","china"]}];
  const MIC = [{"k":"PRODUCERS","n":"Russia · Rostec & United Shipbuilding","v":"$31.2B combined, +23%","d":"The two Russian entries in the SIPRI Top 100 grew 23% despite sanctions-driven component shortages — domestic war demand more than offset the collapse in export revenue. SIPRI flags a SKILLED LABOUR SHORTAGE as the constraint that could slow production and limit innovation, while cautioning that the sector has repeatedly outperformed predictions of its collapse.","t":["russia-domestic","ukraine"]},{"k":"PRODUCERS","n":"United States","v":"Largest producer and exporter","d":"The dominant supplier, and the one whose limits surfaced this cycle: officials warned the president about DWINDLING MUNITIONS STOCKPILES during the Iran campaign, and CENTCOM recommended halting strikes around Hormuz because the campaign had reached the limit of its effectiveness. Production capacity, not political will, became the binding constraint on a war the US was winning tactically.","t":["iran-us","us-politics","chokepoints"]},{"k":"PRODUCERS","n":"Italy","v":"+157% arms exports","d":"The sharpest single-country rise in the 2021-25 period. Read alongside Europe's rearmament and the vacuum left by Russia's export collapse, this is what supply-chain reallocation looks like in practice — middle-tier European producers absorbing demand that used to go to Moscow.","t":["nato-flank","libya"]},{"k":"PRODUCERS","n":"China","v":"Top-tier exporter; Xinjiang-Africa corridor opened","d":"Among the top exporters that together account for roughly 70% of global transfers, and now building export logistics that bypass contested maritime routes — the first intermodal rail-sea corridor connecting Xinjiang to Africa launched this month under the Belt and Road Initiative.","t":["china","pak-afg","myanmar"]},{"k":"PRODUCERS","n":"India","v":"Shifting to domestic manufacture","d":"Historically the largest single customer for Russian arms, now moving procurement toward indigenous production. The shift is both an industrial policy and a hedge: it removes the dependency that sanctions on Moscow turned into a strategic vulnerability.","t":["india","kashmir"]},{"k":"THE EXPORT REALIGNMENT","n":"Russia’s export share collapse","v":"21% → 6.8%","d":"Russia's share of global arms exports fell roughly 64% across 2021-2025. Sanctions are part of it; so is the fact that everything Russia produces is being consumed by its own war. The customers this displaced — India above all — did not simply switch supplier, they moved toward building their own, which makes the loss structural rather than cyclical.","t":["russia-domestic","india"]},{"k":"THE EXPORT REALIGNMENT","n":"Concentration at the top","v":"~70% from a handful of states","d":"The top exporters — the US, Russia, France and China — account for around 70% of major arms transfers. For most states on this board, 'diversifying suppliers' means choosing between four sellers who are also the five permanent members' core, which is why arms dependency and diplomatic alignment are so hard to separate.","t":["geopolitical"]},{"k":"THE EXPORT REALIGNMENT","n":"Asia-Oceania contraction","v":"$130B, −1.2%","d":"The only world region where Top 100 arms revenues fell. Notable because it runs against the global direction of travel and against the assumption that Pacific tension automatically means Pacific arms growth.","t":["taiwan","south-china-sea","korea"]},{"k":"WHO SUPPLIES THE WARS ON THIS BOARD","n":"Sahel · Africa Corps for concessions","v":"Payment in mining rights","d":"Russia supplies security services to Mali, Burkina, CAR and Libya and is paid in mineral concessions rather than cash. This is arms transfer as resource extraction — the supplier takes an equity position in the thing being defended. An ambush on Russian-aligned forces in Mali this month is the first hard test of whether the model delivers.","t":["mali","burkina","car","libya","russia-domestic"]},{"k":"WHO SUPPLIES THE WARS ON THIS BOARD","n":"Sudan · the UAE gold-for-arms corridor","v":"Named in an EU Parliament resolution","d":"The European Parliament called for RSF terrorist designation and for sanctions on individuals AND on a UAE-based company — the first time a European institution has named the corporate financing rather than only the commanders. The UK's July sanctions targeted the same revenue structure.","t":["sudan","uae-gulf"]},{"k":"WHO SUPPLIES THE WARS ON THIS BOARD","n":"Ukraine · DPRK manpower, not just munitions","v":"+30,000 troops reported","d":"North Korea is preparing to send another 30,000 troops, more than doubling the current deployment. The Pyongyang-Moscow relationship has moved from an arms transaction to a manpower alliance — which defers the mobilisation and fiscal constraints this platform had listed as limits on the Russian war economy.","t":["ukraine","korea","russia-domestic"]},{"k":"WHO SUPPLIES THE WARS ON THIS BOARD","n":"Israel · US resupply as the binding limit","v":"Munitions and intelligence","d":"The IDF operates on four fronts; the constraint is American resupply, which makes US policy the practical ceiling on Israeli tempo. A majority of Israelis now read the relationship as two leaders serving separate domestic audiences rather than one alliance.","t":["israel","gaza","lebanon","iran-us"]},{"k":"WHO SUPPLIES THE WARS ON THIS BOARD","n":"Turkey · the S-400/F-35 unlock","v":"2020 sanctions being lifted","d":"Washington is lifting the sanctions imposed over Ankara's Russian S-400 purchase, reopening the F-35 question against Congressional resistance, Israeli objection and Pentagon concern that the S-400 could collect F-35 intelligence for Moscow. A single procurement decision from 2019 is still setting three countries' options in 2026.","t":["turkey","israel","syria"]}];
  const PLUMB = [{"k":"THE SHADOW FLEET","n":"Scale","v":"1,300–1,900+ vessels · ~20% of global oil capacity","d":"Not an organisation — a distributed ecosystem of ageing tankers, shell-company owners, non-Western insurers, low-oversight flag registries and commodity traders willing to handle sanctioned cargo. It moves Russian crude, Iranian petroleum, North Korean coal and Venezuelan heavy crude, and by 2026 it is structurally embedded in global energy logistics rather than parasitic on it.","t":["chokepoints","iran-us","russia-domestic","venezuela"]},{"k":"THE SHADOW FLEET","n":"The jurisdictional division of labour","v":"Each hub does one job","d":"HONG KONG for corporate ownership layering and financial routing · MARSHALL ISLANDS, PANAMA and LIBERIA for flags of convenience · UAE and TÜRKIYE for front companies and financial facilitation · DUBAI'S JAFZA FREE ZONE for the commodity-trading intermediaries · UAE, OMAN and the COMOROS for single-ship owning companies. No single jurisdiction hosts the whole chain, which is precisely what makes it hard to sanction.","t":["uae-gulf","turkey","china","chokepoints"]},{"k":"THE SHADOW FLEET","n":"Tradecraft","v":"AIS off, flags rotated, STS transfers","d":"Transponders disabled, names and flags changed repeatedly, ship-to-ship transfers in unmonitored waters, falsified documentation. Detection now relies on cross-referencing AIS gaps against satellite radar imagery to find vessel clusters where no transponder traffic exists.","t":["chokepoints","yemen"]},{"k":"HOW THE MONEY MOVES WHEN BANKS CANNOT","n":"Currency exchange houses","v":"OFAC designated Amin Exchange, May 2026","d":"THE KEY MECHANISM MOST COVERAGE MISSES. When sanctioned banks lose SWIFT and correspondent banking access, exchange houses perform the same settlement functions through less regulated channels. OFAC's May 2026 action named Amin Exchange as a major conduit for sanctioned Iranian banks, petrochemical exporters and the National Iranian Oil Company. Cutting a country off from the formal system does not stop settlement — it moves settlement somewhere with less visibility.","t":["iran-us","uae-gulf"]},{"k":"HOW THE MONEY MOVES WHEN BANKS CANNOT","n":"Shadow banking and third-party settlement","v":"Chinese-routed settlement for Iranian oil","d":"Reporting describes China sustaining Iranian exports through both a shadow fleet and shadow-banking settlement that bypasses dollar clearing. The pattern generalises: sanctions push trade into currencies and institutions the sanctioning state does not supervise, which is a transfer of visibility rather than a reduction in volume.","t":["china","iran-us"]},{"k":"HOW THE MONEY MOVES WHEN BANKS CANNOT","n":"The next adaptation","v":"Decentralised payments · AI-assisted AIS spoofing","d":"Operators are adopting decentralised payment systems and AI-assisted manipulation of vessel tracking data, which forces enforcement toward satellite surveillance and behavioural analytics. Each enforcement generation produces the evasion generation that answers it.","t":["chokepoints","china"]},{"k":"THE ENABLER LAYER — WHERE THE LEVERAGE ACTUALLY SITS","n":"Insurers, registries, traders, port states","v":"The unsanctioned middle","d":"Analysts assessing 2026 enforcement conclude the West's most effective tools ARE NOT NAVAL. Targeting the enabler network — insurers, flag registries, port state controllers, financiers and the commodity traders who handle sanctioned oil — through secondary sanctions raises operating costs across the whole ecosystem without requiring sustained naval deployment. This is the same finding the Sanctions board reaches from the other direction: the gaps are where the policy is.","t":["chokepoints","uae-gulf","geopolitical"]},{"k":"THE ENABLER LAYER — WHERE THE LEVERAGE ACTUALLY SITS","n":"Sanctioning ports, not just ships","v":"EU proposal, 2026","d":"The EU has proposed sanctioning PORTS IN THIRD COUNTRIES that frustrate its restrictive measures — a significant escalation in method, because it prices the entire jurisdiction rather than chasing individual hulls that re-flag within weeks.","t":["chokepoints","india","turkey"]},{"k":"THE ENABLER LAYER — WHERE THE LEVERAGE ACTUALLY SITS","n":"Industry preference for clear listings","v":"P&I Clubs and BIMCO","d":"The International Group of P&I Clubs and BIMCO have generally supported coordinated G7 vessel listings, because ambiguity is more expensive to the compliant industry than restriction is. Smaller shipping firms and traders carry higher per-transaction compliance costs with each tranche — so enforcement consolidates the legitimate market while the grey one keeps operating.","t":["chokepoints","global-economy"]},{"k":"ENFORCEMENT THIS CYCLE","n":"G7 · Évian, 16 June 2026","v":"~200 vessels designated","d":"Canada announced a $4.3B Ukraine package — $2B military and a $2.3B infrastructure loan repaid from interest on frozen Russian assets in Europe — alongside sanctions on roughly 200 shadow-fleet vessels and associated individuals and entities. European G7 members announced in parallel; the US position at the summit was described as more ambiguous.","t":["ukraine","russia-domestic","nato-flank"]},{"k":"ENFORCEMENT THIS CYCLE","n":"Interdictions","v":"27 ships stopped · Touska seized · Kiwala boarded","d":"CENTCOM reported stopping 27 ships sailing to or from Iran as of April 2026; the US seized the Iranian-flagged Touska in the Gulf of Oman on 19 April after it ignored warnings for six hours. In the Baltic on 14 February, the Swedish Coast Guard boarded the Palau-flagged Kiwala carrying 23,000 tonnes of Russian crude with its AIS disabled.","t":["iran-us","chokepoints","nato-flank"]},{"k":"ENFORCEMENT THIS CYCLE","n":"An allegation worth watching","v":"Prediction-market positioning before seizures","d":"Senate Banking Committee Democrats alleged in January 2026 that individuals with advance knowledge of US sanctions enforcement traded on prediction markets — contracts on whether the US would seize Iranian oil showed anomalous volume and directional positioning roughly 18 hours before the January seizures were announced. A records request to Kalshi and other forecasting markets was issued in February and remains outstanding; neither Treasury nor Justice has confirmed an investigation. UNPROVEN — logged because it connects the commodity, the enforcement decision and the financial market in a way nothing else on this board does.","t":["us-politics","iran-us","global-economy"]}];
