      +'<b>TIER 2 \u00b7 EVENT-DATA AND MONITORING PROJECTS.</b> ACLED for coded conflict events, the Critical Threats Project and ISW for daily campaign assessments, Crisis Group for country analysis, UKMTO for maritime incidents, IPC for food-security classification, SIPRI for arms transfers, InSight Crime for organised-crime structures. These convert raw reporting into structured, comparable records.<br><br>'
      +'<b>TIER 3 \u00b7 OSINT, AND HOW THIS BOARD ACTUALLY USES IT.</b> Much of the fastest ground-level reporting on active fronts originates on social platforms \u2014 local journalists, community accounts, combatant spokesmen and flight and vessel trackers. <b>This board does not source directly from those posts.</b> It takes them through monitors who geolocate, cross-reference and attribute them first: a CTP Congo War Security Review entry naming a village capture typically rests on eight to thirty individual posts, checked against Congolese outlets and satellite or flight-tracking data before it is written down. <b>The verification is the product; the post is the raw material.</b><br><br>'
