  document.getElementById('ovScroll').innerHTML=`
    <div class="inv-lead">Not a map of all diplomacy \u2014 only the structural alignments that actually explain the conflicts on this board. Each bloc shows who is on each side, what is genuinely being contested, and the fault line where it breaks. These are the \u201cwhy\u201d behind the external-actor flows tracked in every theater.</div>
    <div class="dx-grid"><div class="dx-box"><div class="dx-t">BLOC, ALLIANCE, ALIGNMENT — THREE DIFFERENT THINGS</div><div class="dx-d"><b>ALLIANCE</b> — a treaty commitment with a defined obligation, usually mutual defence. Rare, and rarer still that it is tested. <b>BLOC</b> — a pattern of states acting together on a set of questions, which may rest on no treaty at all. <b>ALIGNMENT</b> — a state leaning one way on one issue while hedging on others, which describes most of the world most of the time.</div><div class="dx-d">This board tracks blocs rather than alliances because <b>blocs predict behaviour and alliances mostly describe paperwork.</b> The states supplying Sudan’s war owe each other nothing on paper.</div><div class="dx-d"><b>FAULT LINE</b> — the question on which a bloc breaks. Every entry here names one, because a bloc holds only as long as its members’ interests overlap, and the interesting moment is always where they stop. A bloc with no identifiable fault line is usually one that has not yet been tested.</div><div class="dx-d"><b>Hedging is the norm, not the exception.</b> Most states on this board are courted by more than one pole and take from all of them — Gulf capital, Chinese infrastructure, Western security cooperation. Reading the world as two camps misses that the majority are deliberately in neither, and that this is a strategy rather than indecision.</div></div></div>
    ${BLOCS.map(b=>`
    <div class="blc-card">
      <div class="blc-name">${b.icon} ${b.name}</div>
      <div class="blc-what">${b.what.toUpperCase()}</div>
